"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-a-library-has-b0d18804","Math G3: Finding and estimating the results of addition and subtraction - Set 1","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-2-1 Finding and estimating the results of addition and subtraction of whole numbers up to three digits, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. solving mathematical problems including real- life applications with one or two steps using addition and subtraction of whole numbers up to three digits, and explaining their solution.","A library has 300 books on a shelf. If 192 books are added, how many books are on the shelf now?","492 books","300 books","192 books","108 books","492 books","To find the total number of books on the shelf, we need to add 300 and 192. 300 + 100 = 400, then 400 + 90 = 490, then 490 + 2 = 492.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-david-has-180-88511bad","Math G3: Finding and estimating the results of addition and subtraction - Set 1","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-2-1 Finding and estimating the results of addition and subtraction of whole numbers up to three digits, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. solving mathematical problems including real- life applications with one or two steps using addition and subtraction of whole numbers up to three digits, and explaining their solution.","David has 180 pencils in his case. He buys 219 more pencils. How many pencils does David have now?","399 pencils","180 pencils","219 pencils","599 pencils","399 pencils","To find the total number of pencils David has, we need to add 180 and 219. 180 + 200 = 380, then 380 + 10 = 390, then 390 + 9 = 399.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-emily-has-240-9a3efd97","Math G3: Finding and estimating the results of addition and subtraction - Set 1","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-2-1 Finding and estimating the results of addition and subtraction of whole numbers up to three digits, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Adding and Subtracting whole numbers up to three digits (with and without regrouping) by using place value strategies.","Emily has 240 buttons in her jar. She gives 168 buttons to her sister. How many buttons does Emily have left?","72 buttons","240 buttons","168 buttons","408 buttons","72 buttons","To find the number of buttons Emily has left, we need to subtract 168 from 240. 240 - 100 = 140, then 140 - 60 = 80, then 80 - 8 = 72.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-a-bookshelf-has-6db43c11","Math G3: Finding and estimating the results of addition and subtraction - Set 1","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-2-1 Finding and estimating the results of addition and subtraction of whole numbers up to three digits, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Adding and Subtracting whole numbers up to three digits (with and without regrouping) by using place value strategies.","A bookshelf has 250 books on it. If 175 books are removed, how many books are left on the bookshelf?","75 books","250 books","175 books","375 books","75 books","To find the number of books left, we need to subtract 175 from 250. 250 - 100 = 150, then 150 - 50 = 100, then 100 - 25 = 75.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-tom-has-147-3b7f3812","Math G3: Finding and estimating the results of addition and subtraction - Set 1","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-2-1 Finding and estimating the results of addition and subtraction of whole numbers up to three digits, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. solving mathematical problems including real- life applications with one or two steps using addition and subtraction of whole numbers up to three digits, and explaining their solution.","Tom has 147 pencils in his pencil case. His friend gives him 119 more pencils. How many pencils does Tom have now?","266","256","276","286","266","The calculation gives 266; meta-label options were replaced with numeric distractors.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-ashley-has-180-34401c4a","Math G3: Finding and estimating the results of addition and subtraction - Set 1","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-2-1 Finding and estimating the results of addition and subtraction of whole numbers up to three digits, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. solving mathematical problems including real- life applications with one or two steps using addition and subtraction of whole numbers up to three digits, and explaining their solution.","Ashley has 180 stickers in her collection. Her mom buys her 122 more stickers. How many stickers does Ashley have now?","302 stickers","180 stickers","122 stickers","402 stickers","302 stickers","To find the total number of stickers Ashley has, we need to add 180 and 122. 180 + 100 = 280, then 280 + 20 = 300, then 300 + 2 = 302.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-a-toy-car-dd5a8b8d","Math G3: Finding and estimating the results of addition and subtraction - Set 1","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-2-1 Finding and estimating the results of addition and subtraction of whole numbers up to three digits, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. solving mathematical problems including real- life applications with one or two steps using addition and subtraction of whole numbers up to three digits, and explaining their solution.","A toy car track is 150 meters long. If it is extended by 127 meters, how long is the track now?","277 meters","150 meters","127 meters","377 meters","277 meters","To find the total length of the track, we need to add 150 and 127. 150 + 100 = 250, then 250 + 20 = 270, then 270 + 7 = 277.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-a-basket-contains-f393d5c3","Math G3: Finding and estimating the results of addition and subtraction - Set 1","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-2-1 Finding and estimating the results of addition and subtraction of whole numbers up to three digits, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Adding and Subtracting whole numbers up to three digits (with and without regrouping) by using place value strategies.","A basket contains 240 apples. If 98 apples are taken out, how many apples are left in the basket?","142 apples","240 apples","98 apples","338 apples","142 apples","To find the number of apples left, we need to subtract 98 from 240. 240 - 100 = 140, then 140 + 2 = 142.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-sarah-has-300-6a3ae7d6","Math G3: Finding and estimating the results of addition and subtraction - Set 1","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-2-1 Finding and estimating the results of addition and subtraction of whole numbers up to three digits, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Adding and Subtracting whole numbers up to three digits (with and without regrouping) by using place value strategies.","Sarah has 300 crayons in her box. She gives 127 crayons to her brother. How many crayons does Sarah have left?","173 crayons","127 crayons","300 crayons","427 crayons","173 crayons","To find the number of crayons Sarah has left, we need to subtract 127 from 300. 300 - 100 = 200, then 200 - 20 = 180, then 180 - 7 = 173.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-ben-has-270-786cbd93","Math G3: Finding and estimating the results of addition and subtraction - Set 1","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-2-1 Finding and estimating the results of addition and subtraction of whole numbers up to three digits, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Adding and Subtracting whole numbers up to three digits (with and without regrouping) by using place value strategies.","Ben has 270 marbles in his bag. He loses 139 marbles. How many marbles does Ben have left?","131 marbles","270 marbles","139 marbles","409 marbles","131 marbles","To find the number of marbles Ben has left, we need to subtract 139 from 270. 270 - 100 = 170, then 170 - 30 = 140, then 140 - 9 = 131.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-a-library-has-fc7faf55","Math G3: Finding and estimating the results of addition and subtraction - Set 2","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-2-1 Finding and estimating the results of addition and subtraction of whole numbers up to three digits, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. solving mathematical problems including real- life applications with one or two steps using addition and subtraction of whole numbers up to three digits, and explaining their solution.","A library has 817 books on a shelf. If they add 192 more books, about how many books will the shelf have now?","About 900 books","About 1000 books","About 1100 books","About 1200 books","About 1000 books","The library started with 817 books. If they add 192 more books, they will have about 817 + 192 = 1009 books, which is about 1000 books.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-a-boy-has-804cf388","Math G3: Finding and estimating the results of addition and subtraction - Set 2","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-2-1 Finding and estimating the results of addition and subtraction of whole numbers up to three digits, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. solving mathematical problems including real- life applications with one or two steps using addition and subtraction of whole numbers up to three digits, and explaining their solution.","A boy has 654 marbles in his pocket. If he loses 278 marbles, about how many marbles will he have left?","About 300 marbles","About 350 marbles","About 400 marbles","About 450 marbles","About 400 marbles","The boy started with 654 marbles. If he loses 278 marbles, he will have about 654 - 278 = 376 marbles left, which is about 400 marbles.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-a-water-tank-2367d5b5","Math G3: Finding and estimating the results of addition and subtraction - Set 2","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-2-1 Finding and estimating the results of addition and subtraction of whole numbers up to three digits, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. solving mathematical problems including real- life applications with one or two steps using addition and subtraction of whole numbers up to three digits, and explaining their solution.","A water tank can hold 945 liters of water. If 378 liters of water are already in the tank, about how much more water can be added?","About 500 liters","About 600 liters","About 700 liters","About 800 liters","About 600 liters","The water tank can hold 945 liters. If 378 liters are already in the tank, about 945 - 378 = 567 liters can be added, which is about 600 liters.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-a-bakery-has-1c22657f","Math G3: Finding and estimating the results of addition and subtraction - Set 2","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-2-1 Finding and estimating the results of addition and subtraction of whole numbers up to three digits, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. solving mathematical problems including real- life applications with one or two steps using addition and subtraction of whole numbers up to three digits, and explaining their solution.","A bakery has 753 cupcakes to sell. If they sell 219 cupcakes, about how many cupcakes will they have left?","About 500 cupcakes","About 550 cupcakes","About 600 cupcakes","About 650 cupcakes","About 550 cupcakes","The bakery started with 753 cupcakes. If they sell 219 cupcakes, they will have about 753 - 219 = 534 cupcakes left, which is about 550 cupcakes.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-tom-has-457-7ae55262","Math G3: Finding and estimating the results of addition and subtraction - Set 2","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-2-1 Finding and estimating the results of addition and subtraction of whole numbers up to three digits, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. solving mathematical problems including real- life applications with one or two steps using addition and subtraction of whole numbers up to three digits, and explaining their solution.","Tom has 457 pencils in his pencil case. If he gives 127 pencils to his friend, about how many pencils will Tom have left?","About 300 pencils","About 400 pencils","About 500 pencils","About 600 pencils","About 300 pencils","Tom started with 457 pencils. If he gives 127 away, he will have about 457 - 127 = 330 pencils left, which is about 300 pencils.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-a-bookshelf-has-f9bb95af","Math G3: Finding and estimating the results of addition and subtraction - Set 2","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-2-1 Finding and estimating the results of addition and subtraction of whole numbers up to three digits, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. solving mathematical problems including real- life applications with one or two steps using addition and subtraction of whole numbers up to three digits, and explaining their solution.","A bookshelf has 245 books on it. If 117 more books are added, about how many books will the bookshelf have now?","About 300 books","About 350 books","About 400 books","About 450 books","About 350 books","The bookshelf started with 245 books. If 117 more books are added, it will have about 245 + 117 = 362 books, which is about 350 books.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-a-store-has-82896667","Math G3: Finding and estimating the results of addition and subtraction - Set 2","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-2-1 Finding and estimating the results of addition and subtraction of whole numbers up to three digits, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. solving mathematical problems including real- life applications with one or two steps using addition and subtraction of whole numbers up to three digits, and explaining their solution.","A store has 983 toys to sell. If they sell 421 toys, about how many toys will they have left?","About 500 toys","About 550 toys","About 600 toys","About 650 toys","About 550 toys","The store started with 983 toys. If they sell 421 toys, they will have about 983 - 421 = 562 toys left, which is about 550 toys.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-a-group-of-e636853c","Math G3: Finding and estimating the results of addition and subtraction - Set 2","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-2-1 Finding and estimating the results of addition and subtraction of whole numbers up to three digits, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. solving mathematical problems including real- life applications with one or two steps using addition and subtraction of whole numbers up to three digits, and explaining their solution.","A group of friends want to share some candy. If they have 942 pieces of candy and they want to share it equally among 4 friends, about how many pieces of candy will each friend get?","About 200 pieces","About 250 pieces","About 300 pieces","About 350 pieces","About 250 pieces","If the friends have 942 pieces of candy and they want to share it equally among 4 friends, each friend will get about 942 / 4 = 235.5 pieces, which is about 250 pieces.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-a-girl-has-9cd9a2db","Math G3: Finding and estimating the results of addition and subtraction - Set 2","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-2-1 Finding and estimating the results of addition and subtraction of whole numbers up to three digits, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. solving mathematical problems including real- life applications with one or two steps using addition and subtraction of whole numbers up to three digits, and explaining their solution.","A girl has 749 stickers in her sticker book. If she gives 317 stickers to her sister, about how many stickers will she have left?","About 400 stickers","About 450 stickers","About 500 stickers","About 550 stickers","About 400 stickers","The girl started with 749 stickers. If she gives 317 stickers to her sister, she will have about 749 - 317 = 432 stickers left, which is about 400 stickers.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-a-farmer-has-8bd9d4f1","Math G3: Finding and estimating the results of addition and subtraction - Set 2","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-2-1 Finding and estimating the results of addition and subtraction of whole numbers up to three digits, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. solving mathematical problems including real- life applications with one or two steps using addition and subtraction of whole numbers up to three digits, and explaining their solution.","A farmer has 936 apples in his orchard. If he picks 482 apples, about how many apples will he have left?","About 400 apples","About 450 apples","About 500 apples","About 550 apples","About 450 apples","The farmer started with 936 apples. If he picks 482 apples, he will have about 936 - 482 = 454 apples left, which is about 450 apples.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-a-toy-car-621e9937","Math G3: Finding and estimating the results of addition and subtraction - Set 3","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-2-1 Finding and estimating the results of addition and subtraction of whole numbers up to three digits, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. solving mathematical problems including real- life applications with one or two steps using addition and subtraction of whole numbers up to three digits, and explaining their solution.","A toy car track is 120 inches long. If it is extended by 42 inches, how long is the track now?","78 inches","120 inches","162 inches","200 inches","162 inches","To find the new length of the track, we need to add the length of the extension to the original length. 120 + 42 = 162.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-a-bookshelf-has-c577a13e","Math G3: Finding and estimating the results of addition and subtraction - Set 3","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-2-1 Finding and estimating the results of addition and subtraction of whole numbers up to three digits, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. solving mathematical problems including real- life applications with one or two steps using addition and subtraction of whole numbers up to three digits, and explaining their solution.","A bookshelf has 150 books on it. If 35 more books are added, how many books are on the bookshelf now?","115 books","150 books","185 books","200 books","185 books","To find the total number of books, we need to add the number of new books to the number of books already on the bookshelf. 150 + 35 = 185.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-ashley-has-189-2eeaed8d","Math G3: Finding and estimating the results of addition and subtraction - Set 3","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-2-1 Finding and estimating the results of addition and subtraction of whole numbers up to three digits, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Adding and Subtracting whole numbers up to three digits (with and without regrouping) by using place value strategies.","Ashley has 189 stickers in her collection. She gives 39 stickers to her brother. How many stickers does Ashley have left?","150 stickers","189 stickers","228 stickers","250 stickers","150 stickers","To find out how many stickers Ashley has left, we need to subtract the number of stickers she gave away from the total number of stickers she had. 189 - 39 = 150.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-emily-has-135-1a8be373","Math G3: Finding and estimating the results of addition and subtraction - Set 3","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-2-1 Finding and estimating the results of addition and subtraction of whole numbers up to three digits, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. solving mathematical problems including real- life applications with one or two steps using addition and subtraction of whole numbers up to three digits, and explaining their solution.","Emily has 135 buttons in her jar. She adds 25 more buttons to the jar. How many buttons does Emily have now?","110 buttons","135 buttons","160 buttons","200 buttons","160 buttons","To find the total number of buttons Emily has, we need to add the number of new buttons to the number of buttons she already had. 135 + 25 = 160.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-a-library-has-ed2ef42f","Math G3: Finding and estimating the results of addition and subtraction - Set 3","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-2-1 Finding and estimating the results of addition and subtraction of whole numbers up to three digits, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Adding and Subtracting whole numbers up to three digits (with and without regrouping) by using place value strategies.","A library has 270 books on a shelf. If 90 books are removed from the shelf, how many books are left on the shelf?","180 books","200 books","270 books","300 books","180 books","To find out how many books are left, we need to subtract the number of books removed from the total number of books. 270 - 90 = 180.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-a-water-bottle-fd6502e2","Math G3: Finding and estimating the results of addition and subtraction - Set 3","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-2-1 Finding and estimating the results of addition and subtraction of whole numbers up to three digits, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. solving mathematical problems including real- life applications with one or two steps using addition and subtraction of whole numbers up to three digits, and explaining their solution.","A water bottle can hold 240 ounces of water. If 75 ounces of water are already in the bottle, how much more water can be added?","165 ounces","175 ounces","185 ounces","195 ounces","165 ounces","To find out how much more water can be added, we need to subtract the amount of water already in the bottle from the total capacity. 240 - 75 = 165.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-tom-has-147-20b24f66","Math G3: Finding and estimating the results of addition and subtraction - Set 3","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-2-1 Finding and estimating the results of addition and subtraction of whole numbers up to three digits, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Adding and Subtracting whole numbers up to three digits (with and without regrouping) by using place value strategies.","Tom has 147 pencils in his pencil case. He gives 27 pencils to his friend. How many pencils does Tom have left?","120 pencils","147 pencils","174 pencils","200 pencils","120 pencils","To find out how many pencils Tom has left, we need to subtract the number of pencils he gave away from the total number of pencils he had. 147 - 27 = 120.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-david-has-198-17376acd","Math G3: Finding and estimating the results of addition and subtraction - Set 3","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-2-1 Finding and estimating the results of addition and subtraction of whole numbers up to three digits, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. solving mathematical problems including real- life applications with one or two steps using addition and subtraction of whole numbers up to three digits, and explaining their solution.","David has 198 pennies in his piggy bank. He adds 22 more pennies to the bank. How many pennies does David have now?","176 pennies","198 pennies","220 pennies","250 pennies","220 pennies","To find the total number of pennies David has, we need to add the number of new pennies to the number of pennies he already had. 198 + 22 = 220.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-ben-has-216-1855fdaa","Math G3: Finding and estimating the results of addition and subtraction - Set 3","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-2-1 Finding and estimating the results of addition and subtraction of whole numbers up to three digits, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Adding and Subtracting whole numbers up to three digits (with and without regrouping) by using place value strategies.","Ben has 216 marbles in his bag. He loses 48 marbles down a drain. How many marbles does Ben have left?","168 marbles","200 marbles","216 marbles","250 marbles","168 marbles","To find out how many marbles Ben has left, we need to subtract the number of marbles he lost from the total number of marbles he had. 216 - 48 = 168.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-sarah-has-95-c16a7a5b","Math G3: Finding and estimating the results of addition and subtraction - Set 3","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-2-1 Finding and estimating the results of addition and subtraction of whole numbers up to three digits, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. solving mathematical problems including real- life applications with one or two steps using addition and subtraction of whole numbers up to three digits, and explaining their solution.","Sarah has 95 crayons in her box. She finds 17 more crayons under her desk. How many crayons does Sarah have now?","78 crayons","95 crayons","112 crayons","125 crayons","112 crayons","To find the total number of crayons Sarah has, we need to add the number of crayons she found to the number of crayons she already had. 95 + 17 = 112.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-a-bag-has-6cd9044f","Math G3: Understanding fractions - Set 1","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-1-2 Understanding fractions, representing, reading, writing, comparing, and ordering them.","2. Reading and writing fractions (with numerators or denominators not exceeding 12).","A bag has 9 marbles. If 3 marbles are added, what fraction of the marbles is 3?","3/12 of the marbles","3/9 of the marbles","9/12 of the marbles","3/3 of the marbles","3/12 of the marbles","After adding marbles, there are 12 marbles total. The fraction is 3/12.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-a-toy-box-4c7e4def","Math G3: Understanding fractions - Set 1","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-1-2 Understanding fractions, representing, reading, writing, comparing, and ordering them.","2. Reading and writing fractions (with numerators or denominators not exceeding 12).","A toy box has 9 toys. If 3 toys are added, what fraction of the toys is 3?","3/12 of the toys","3/9 of the toys","9/12 of the toys","3/3 of the toys","3/12 of the toys","After adding toys, there are 12 toys total. The fraction is 3/12.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-a-pencil-has-18413a14","Math G3: Understanding fractions - Set 1","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-1-2 Understanding fractions, representing, reading, writing, comparing, and ordering them.","1. Distinguishing fractions (with numerators or denominators not exceeding 12) as parts of a whole or as parts of a set, and represent them using models and diagrams.","A pencil has 12 parts. If 4 parts are colored, what fraction of the pencil is colored?","1/3 of the pencil","2/3 of the pencil","3/12 of the pencil","4/12 of the pencil","2/3 of the pencil","Since 4 parts are colored, the fraction of the pencil that is colored is 4 out of 12, which is 1/3.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-a-pizza-has-f498ca66","Math G3: Understanding fractions - Set 1","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-1-2 Understanding fractions, representing, reading, writing, comparing, and ordering them.","2. Reading and writing fractions (with numerators or denominators not exceeding 12).","A pizza has 8 slices. If 2 slices are eaten, what fraction of the pizza is left?","1/2 of the pizza","2/8 of the pizza","6/8 of the pizza","7/8 of the pizza","6/8 of the pizza","Since 2 slices were eaten, 6 slices are left. The fraction of the pizza left is 6 out of 8, which is 6/8.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-a-bookshelf-has-5564fd47","Math G3: Understanding fractions - Set 1","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-1-2 Understanding fractions, representing, reading, writing, comparing, and ordering them.","2. Reading and writing fractions (with numerators or denominators not exceeding 12).","A bookshelf has 12 books. If 4 books are taken out, what fraction of the books are left?","1/3 of the books","2/3 of the books","3/4 of the books","4/12 of the books","2/3 of the books","Since 4 books were taken out, 8 books are left. The fraction of the books left is 8 out of 12, which is 2/3.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-a-cake-has-971acee6","Math G3: Understanding fractions - Set 1","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-1-2 Understanding fractions, representing, reading, writing, comparing, and ordering them.","2. Reading and writing fractions (with numerators or denominators not exceeding 12).","A cake has 8 slices. If 2 slices are eaten, what fraction of the cake is eaten?","1/2 of the cake","2/8 of the cake","6/8 of the cake","7/8 of the cake","2/8 of the cake","Since 2 slices are eaten, the fraction of the cake that is eaten is 2 out of 8, which is 1/4.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-a-box-has-a7b6f92d","Math G3: Understanding fractions - Set 1","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-1-2 Understanding fractions, representing, reading, writing, comparing, and ordering them.","2. Reading and writing fractions (with numerators or denominators not exceeding 12).","A box has 9 crayons. If 3 crayons are taken out, what fraction of the crayons are left?","1/3 of the crayons","2/3 of the crayons","3/9 of the crayons","6/9 of the crayons","2/3 of the crayons","Since 3 crayons are taken out, 6 crayons are left. The fraction of the crayons left is 6 out of 9, which is 2/3.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-a-toy-car-8eff5051","Math G3: Understanding fractions - Set 1","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-1-2 Understanding fractions, representing, reading, writing, comparing, and ordering them.","2. Reading and writing fractions (with numerators or denominators not exceeding 12).","A toy car has 12 wheels. If 4 wheels are missing, what fraction of the wheels are missing?","1/3 of the wheels","2/3 of the wheels","3/12 of the wheels","4/12 of the wheels","2/3 of the wheels","Since 4 wheels are missing, the fraction of the wheels that are missing is 4 out of 12, which is 1/3.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-a-book-has-00111757","Math G3: Understanding fractions - Set 1","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-1-2 Understanding fractions, representing, reading, writing, comparing, and ordering them.","2. Reading and writing fractions (with numerators or denominators not exceeding 12).","A book has 8 pages. If 2 pages are turned, what fraction of the pages are turned?","1/2 of the pages","2/8 of the pages","6/8 of the pages","7/8 of the pages","2/8 of the pages","Since 2 pages are turned, the fraction of the pages that are turned is 2 out of 8, which is 1/4.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-a-basket-has-26bf97be","Math G3: Understanding fractions - Set 1","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-1-2 Understanding fractions, representing, reading, writing, comparing, and ordering them.","2. Reading and writing fractions (with numerators or denominators not exceeding 12).","A basket has 6 apples. If 2 apples are eaten, what fraction of the apples is left?","1/2 of the apples","2/6 of the apples","4/6 of the apples","5/6 of the apples","4/6 of the apples","Since 2 apples were eaten, 4 apples are left. The fraction of the apples left is 4 out of 6, which is 2/3.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-is-six-twelfths-fc0cdf18","Math G3: Understanding fractions - Set 2","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-1-2 Understanding fractions, representing, reading, writing, comparing, and ordering them.","2. Reading and writing fractions (with numerators or denominators not exceeding 12).","What is six twelfths written as a fraction in simplest form?","one half","one quarter","one sixth","one second","one half","Six twelfths simplifies to one half because both numbers can be divided by 6.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-is-eight-twelfths-e45aad80","Math G3: Understanding fractions - Set 2","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-2-1 Finding and estimating the results of addition and subtraction of whole numbers up to three digits, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. solving mathematical problems including real- life applications with one or two steps using addition and subtraction of whole numbers up to three digits, and explaining their solution.","What is eight twelfths written as a fraction in simplest form?","two thirds","one half","one quarter","three quarters","two thirds","Eight twelfths simplifies to two thirds because both numbers can be divided by 4.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-is-nine-twelfths-7e842187","Math G3: Understanding fractions - Set 2","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-2-1 Finding and estimating the results of addition and subtraction of whole numbers up to three digits, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. solving mathematical problems including real- life applications with one or two steps using addition and subtraction of whole numbers up to three digits, and explaining their solution.","What is nine twelfths written as a fraction in simplest form?","three quarters","two thirds","one half","one quarter","three quarters","Nine twelfths simplifies to three quarters because both numbers can be divided by 3.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-is-two-sixths-e0d5a85a","Math G3: Understanding fractions - Set 2","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-1-2 Understanding fractions, representing, reading, writing, comparing, and ordering them.","2. Reading and writing fractions (with numerators or denominators not exceeding 12).","What is two sixths written as a fraction in simplest form?","one third","one half","one quarter","one sixth","one third","Two sixths simplifies by dividing the numerator and denominator by 2, giving one third.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-is-two-thirds-5fba0f41","Math G3: Understanding fractions - Set 2","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-1-2 Understanding fractions, representing, reading, writing, comparing, and ordering them.","2. Reading and writing fractions (with numerators or denominators not exceeding 12).","What is two thirds written as a fraction?","1/2","1/4","2/3","3/4","2/3","Two thirds is written as 2/3.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-is-three-fourths-15cfb075","Math G3: Understanding fractions - Set 2","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-1-2 Understanding fractions, representing, reading, writing, comparing, and ordering them.","2. Reading and writing fractions (with numerators or denominators not exceeding 12).","What is three fourths written as a fraction?","1/4","1/2","3/4","2/3","3/4","Three fourths is written as 3/4.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-is-four-eighths-afe7162b","Math G3: Understanding fractions - Set 2","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-1-2 Understanding fractions, representing, reading, writing, comparing, and ordering them.","2. Reading and writing fractions (with numerators or denominators not exceeding 12).","What is four eighths written as a fraction in simplest form?","one half","one quarter","one sixth","one second","one half","Four eighths simplifies to one half because both numbers can be divided by 4.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-is-one-quarter-6eecb9b7","Math G3: Understanding fractions - Set 2","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-1-2 Understanding fractions, representing, reading, writing, comparing, and ordering them.","2. Reading and writing fractions (with numerators or denominators not exceeding 12).","What is one quarter written as a fraction?","1/2","2/3","3/4","1/4","1/4","One quarter is written as 1/4.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-is-three-sixths-f4e94ba9","Math G3: Understanding fractions - Set 2","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-2-1 Finding and estimating the results of addition and subtraction of whole numbers up to three digits, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. solving mathematical problems including real- life applications with one or two steps using addition and subtraction of whole numbers up to three digits, and explaining their solution.","What is three sixths written as a fraction in simplest form?","one half","one quarter","two thirds","one sixth","one half","Three sixths simplifies to one half because both numbers can be divided by 3.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-is-one-half-f4959010","Math G3: Understanding fractions - Set 2","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-1-2 Understanding fractions, representing, reading, writing, comparing, and ordering them.","2. Reading and writing fractions (with numerators or denominators not exceeding 12).","What is one half written as a fraction?","1/2","1/3","2/3","1/4","1/2","One half is written as 1/2.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-fraction-is-greater-9ca149be","Math G3: Understanding fractions - Set 3","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-1-2 Understanding fractions, representing, reading, writing, comparing, and ordering them.","1. Distinguishing fractions (with numerators or denominators not exceeding 12) as parts of a whole or as parts of a set, and represent them using models and diagrams.","Which fraction is greater than 3/4?","3/4 is greater than 1/2","1/2 is greater than 3/4","3/4 is greater than 1/3","1/3 is greater than 3/4","3/4 is greater than 1/2","3/4 is greater than 1/2 because 3/4 is a larger portion of a whole.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-fraction-is-less-d528876e","Math G3: Understanding fractions - Set 3","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-1-2 Understanding fractions, representing, reading, writing, comparing, and ordering them.","2. Reading and writing fractions (with numerators or denominators not exceeding 12).","Which fraction is less than 1/3?","1/3 is less than 1/2","1/2 is less than 1/3","1/3 is less than 1/4","1/4 is less than 1/3","1/4 is less than 1/3","Among the options, 1/4 is less than 1/3.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-fraction-is-equal-e30e06fa","Math G3: Understanding fractions - Set 3","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-1-2 Understanding fractions, representing, reading, writing, comparing, and ordering them.","1. Distinguishing fractions (with numerators or denominators not exceeding 12) as parts of a whole or as parts of a set, and represent them using models and diagrams.","Which fraction is equal to 1/2?","1/3 is equal to 1/2","1/2 is equal to 1/4","1/2 is equal to 2/4","2/4 is not equal to 1/2","1/2 is equal to 2/4","1/2 is equal to 2/4 because both fractions represent the same portion of a whole.","","",""
