"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-fraction-is-equal-d3f33152","Math G3: Understanding fractions - Set 3","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-1-2 Understanding fractions, representing, reading, writing, comparing, and ordering them.","1. Distinguishing fractions (with numerators or denominators not exceeding 12) as parts of a whole or as parts of a set, and represent them using models and diagrams.","Which fraction is equal to 2/4?","2/4 is equal to 1/2","1/2 is equal to 3/4","2/4 is equal to 1/4","1/4 is not equal to 2/4","2/4 is equal to 1/2","2/4 is equal to 1/2 because both fractions represent the same portion of a whole.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-fraction-is-greater-d6ac7319","Math G3: Understanding fractions - Set 3","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-1-2 Understanding fractions, representing, reading, writing, comparing, and ordering them.","1. Distinguishing fractions (with numerators or denominators not exceeding 12) as parts of a whole or as parts of a set, and represent them using models and diagrams.","Which fraction is greater than 2/3?","2/3 is greater than 1/2","1/2 is greater than 2/3","2/3 is greater than 1/4","1/4 is greater than 2/3","2/3 is greater than 1/2","2/3 is greater than 1/2 because 2/3 is a larger portion of a whole.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-fraction-is-equal-56396e56","Math G3: Understanding fractions - Set 3","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-1-2 Understanding fractions, representing, reading, writing, comparing, and ordering them.","1. Distinguishing fractions (with numerators or denominators not exceeding 12) as parts of a whole or as parts of a set, and represent them using models and diagrams.","Which fraction is equal to 1/4?","1/4 is equal to 1/2","1/2 is equal to 2/4","1/4 is equal to 3/12","3/12 is not equal to 1/4","1/4 is equal to 3/12","1/4 is equal to 3/12 because both fractions represent the same portion of a whole.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-fraction-is-less-a9591c61","Math G3: Understanding fractions - Set 3","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-1-2 Understanding fractions, representing, reading, writing, comparing, and ordering them.","1. Distinguishing fractions (with numerators or denominators not exceeding 12) as parts of a whole or as parts of a set, and represent them using models and diagrams.","Which fraction is less than 1/4?","1/4 is less than 1/2","1/2 is less than 1/4","1/4 is less than 1/3","1/3 is less than 1/4","1/4 is less than 1/3","1/4 is less than 1/3 because 1/4 is a smaller portion of a whole.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-fraction-is-greater-e2262faf","Math G3: Understanding fractions - Set 3","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-1-2 Understanding fractions, representing, reading, writing, comparing, and ordering them.","2. Reading and writing fractions (with numerators or denominators not exceeding 12).","Which fraction is greater than 1/3?","1/3 is greater than 1/4","1/4 is greater than 1/3","1/3 is greater than 1/2","1/2 is greater than 1/3","1/2 is greater than 1/3","Among the options, 1/2 is greater than 1/3.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-fraction-is-same-a10e210f","Math G3: Understanding fractions - Set 4","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-1-2 Understanding fractions, representing, reading, writing, comparing, and ordering them.","2. Reading and writing fractions (with numerators or denominators not exceeding 12).","Which fraction is the same as four eighths?","two quarters","one half","three sixths","two fourths","one half","Four eighths is equal to one half because if you cut a cake into eight pieces and you eat four of them, it is the same as cutting the cake into two pieces and eating one of them.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-is-another-way-df5d1457","Math G3: Understanding fractions - Set 4","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-1-2 Understanding fractions, representing, reading, writing, comparing, and ordering them.","2. Reading and writing fractions (with numerators or denominators not exceeding 12).","What is another way to write one sixth?","two twelfths","three eighteenths","four twenty-fourths","one twelfth","two twelfths","One sixth is the same as two twelfths because if you have one group of six and you want to make groups of twelve, you will have two groups of twelve.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-fraction-is-equal-456db13f","Math G3: Understanding fractions - Set 4","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-1-2 Understanding fractions, representing, reading, writing, comparing, and ordering them.","2. Reading and writing fractions (with numerators or denominators not exceeding 12).","Which fraction is equal to two fourths?","one half","three sixths","four eighths","six twelfths","one half","Two fourths is equal to one half because if you have two groups of four and you want to make groups of two, you will have one group of two.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-is-an-equivalent-b6497e9e","Math G3: Understanding fractions - Set 4","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-1-2 Understanding fractions, representing, reading, writing, comparing, and ordering them.","2. Reading and writing fractions (with numerators or denominators not exceeding 12).","What is an equivalent fraction for six twelfths?","one half","one quarter","two thirds","one third","one half","Six twelfths is the same as one half because if you cut a pizza into twelve pieces and you eat six of them, it is the same as cutting the pizza into two pieces and eating one of them.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-is-an-equivalent-9ec51fce","Math G3: Understanding fractions - Set 4","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-1-2 Understanding fractions, representing, reading, writing, comparing, and ordering them.","2. Reading and writing fractions (with numerators or denominators not exceeding 12).","What is an equivalent fraction for three sixths?","one half","two thirds","four eighths","five twelfths","one half","Three sixths is the same as one half because if you cut a pizza into six pieces and you eat three of them, it is the same as cutting the pizza into two pieces and eating one of them.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-fraction-is-same-5a69c06a","Math G3: Understanding fractions - Set 4","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-1-2 Understanding fractions, representing, reading, writing, comparing, and ordering them.","2. Reading and writing fractions (with numerators or denominators not exceeding 12).","Which fraction is the same as one quarter?","two eighths","three sixteenths","four sixteenths","one eighth","two eighths","One quarter is equal to two eighths because if you cut a cake into eight pieces and you eat two of them, it is the same as cutting the cake into four pieces and eating one of them.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-fraction-is-equal-5bffd1f4","Math G3: Understanding fractions - Set 4","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-1-2 Understanding fractions, representing, reading, writing, comparing, and ordering them.","2. Reading and writing fractions (with numerators or denominators not exceeding 12).","Which fraction is equal to three fourths?","nine twelfths","six eighths","two thirds","five sixths","nine twelfths","Three fourths is equal to nine twelfths because if you have three groups of four and you want to make groups of twelve, you will have nine groups of twelve.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-is-another-way-3c6ae00b","Math G3: Understanding fractions - Set 4","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-1-2 Understanding fractions, representing, reading, writing, comparing, and ordering them.","2. Reading and writing fractions (with numerators or denominators not exceeding 12).","What is another way to write two thirds?","four sixths","three fourths","six eighths","eight twelfths","four sixths","Two thirds is the same as four sixths because if you have two groups of three and you want to make groups of six, you will have four groups of six.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-fraction-is-equal-ff350f47","Math G3: Understanding fractions - Set 4","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-1-2 Understanding fractions, representing, reading, writing, comparing, and ordering them.","2. Reading and writing fractions (with numerators or denominators not exceeding 12).","Which fraction is equal to three fourths?","two thirds","six eighths","nine twelfths","five sixths","six eighths","Three fourths is equal to six eighths because if you have three groups of four and you want to make groups of eight, you will have six groups of eight.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-is-an-equivalent-51a7f190","Math G3: Understanding fractions - Set 4","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-1-2 Understanding fractions, representing, reading, writing, comparing, and ordering them.","4. Distinguishing and finding equivalent fractions for a given fraction (with numerators or denominators not exceeding 12).","What is an equivalent fraction for one half?","one fourth","three sixths","two sixths","five eighths","three sixths","One half is equivalent to three sixths because 1/2 and 3/6 represent the same amount.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-if-a-book-2bc45729","Math G3: Understanding the operations of multiplication and division - Set 1","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-2-2 Understanding the operations of multiplication and division, representing them using models, forming fact family, finding their results, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. Finding multiplication facts up to (10 أ— 10) and the corresponding division facts.","If a book costs $5 and you want to buy 3 books, how much will you need to pay in total?","You will need to pay $12","You will need to pay $15","You will need to pay $10","You will need to pay $8","You will need to pay $15","Since each book costs $5 and you want to buy 3 books, we can find the total by adding $5 + $5 + $5, which equals $15.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-if-it-takes-b86145e0","Math G3: Understanding the operations of multiplication and division - Set 1","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-2-2 Understanding the operations of multiplication and division, representing them using models, forming fact family, finding their results, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. Finding multiplication facts up to (10 أ— 10) and the corresponding division facts.","If it takes 4 rows of 6 chairs to seat all the students, how many students are there in total?","There are 20 students","There are 24 students","There are 16 students","There are 10 students","There are 24 students","Since there are 4 rows of 6 chairs, we can find the total by adding 6 + 6 + 6 + 6, which equals 24.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-if-you-have-0167eed0","Math G3: Understanding the operations of multiplication and division - Set 1","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-2-2 Understanding the operations of multiplication and division, representing them using models, forming fact family, finding their results, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","2. Distinguishing division as equal sharing and constructing division facts related to multiplication facts up to (10 أ— 10) by using models and drawings.","If you have 9 groups of 3 stickers, how many stickers do you have in total?","You have 24 stickers","You have 27 stickers","You have 20 stickers","You have 30 stickers","You have 27 stickers","Since you have 9 groups of 3 stickers, we can find the total by adding 3 + 3 + 3 + 3 + 3 + 3 + 3 + 3 + 3, which equals 27.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-if-a-toy-1cba2293","Math G3: Understanding the operations of multiplication and division - Set 1","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-2-2 Understanding the operations of multiplication and division, representing them using models, forming fact family, finding their results, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. Finding multiplication facts up to (10 أ— 10) and the corresponding division facts.","If a toy requires 6 batteries, and you want to buy batteries for 4 toys, how many batteries will you need in total?","You will need 20 batteries","You will need 24 batteries","You will need 16 batteries","You will need 10 batteries","You will need 24 batteries","Since each toy requires 6 batteries and you want to buy batteries for 4 toys, we can find the total by adding 6 + 6 + 6 + 6, which equals 24.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-a-bookshelf-has-60371012","Math G3: Understanding the operations of multiplication and division - Set 1","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-2-2 Understanding the operations of multiplication and division, representing them using models, forming fact family, finding their results, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. Finding multiplication facts up to (10 أ— 10) and the corresponding division facts.","A bookshelf has 5 shelves, and each shelf can hold 6 books. How many books can the bookshelf hold in total?","It can hold 10 books","It can hold 25 books","It can hold 30 books","It can hold 20 books","It can hold 30 books","Since the bookshelf has 5 shelves and each shelf can hold 6 books, we can find the total by adding 6 + 6 + 6 + 6 + 6, which equals 30.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-if-you-have-2f19c9c7","Math G3: Understanding the operations of multiplication and division - Set 1","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-2-2 Understanding the operations of multiplication and division, representing them using models, forming fact family, finding their results, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. Finding multiplication facts up to (10 أ— 10) and the corresponding division facts.","If you have 5 boxes of pencils, and each box contains 4 pencils, how many pencils do you have in total?","You have 15 pencils","You have 20 pencils","You have 12 pencils","You have 10 pencils","You have 20 pencils","Since you have 5 boxes and each box contains 4 pencils, we can find the total by adding 4 + 4 + 4 + 4 + 4, which equals 20.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-if-sara-has-331988b1","Math G3: Understanding the operations of multiplication and division - Set 1","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-2-2 Understanding the operations of multiplication and division, representing them using models, forming fact family, finding their results, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. Finding multiplication facts up to (10 أ— 10) and the corresponding division facts.","If Sara has 3 groups of 4 pencils, how many pencils does Sara have in total?","She has 10 pencils","She has 12 pencils","She has 6 pencils","She has 8 pencils","She has 12 pencils","Since Sara has 3 groups of 4 pencils, we can find the total by adding 4 + 4 + 4, which equals 12.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-if-a-toy-f18b0ad7","Math G3: Understanding the operations of multiplication and division - Set 1","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-2-2 Understanding the operations of multiplication and division, representing them using models, forming fact family, finding their results, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. Finding multiplication facts up to (10 أ— 10) and the corresponding division facts.","If a toy car track is 3 blocks long and you want to make 5 tracks, how many blocks will you need in total?","You will need 12 blocks","You will need 15 blocks","You will need 10 blocks","You will need 8 blocks","You will need 15 blocks","Since each track is 3 blocks long and you want to make 5 tracks, we can find the total by adding 3 + 3 + 3 + 3 + 3, which equals 15.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-a-farmer-has-5d21b851","Math G3: Understanding the operations of multiplication and division - Set 1","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-2-2 Understanding the operations of multiplication and division, representing them using models, forming fact family, finding their results, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. Finding multiplication facts up to (10 أ— 10) and the corresponding division facts.","A farmer has 6 baskets of apples, and each basket contains 8 apples. How many apples does the farmer have in total?","The farmer has 40 apples","The farmer has 48 apples","The farmer has 30 apples","The farmer has 20 apples","The farmer has 48 apples","Since the farmer has 6 baskets and each basket contains 8 apples, we can find the total by adding 8 + 8 + 8 + 8 + 8 + 8, which equals 48.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-if-you-have-c8206477","Math G3: Understanding the operations of multiplication and division - Set 1","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-2-2 Understanding the operations of multiplication and division, representing them using models, forming fact family, finding their results, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","2. Distinguishing division as equal sharing and constructing division facts related to multiplication facts up to (10 أ— 10) by using models and drawings.","If you have 2 groups of 9 crayons, how many crayons do you have in total?","You have 11 crayons","You have 18 crayons","You have 16 crayons","You have 10 crayons","You have 18 crayons","Since you have 2 groups of 9 crayons, we can find the total by adding 9 + 9, which equals 18.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-a-group-of-f53b7545","Math G3: Understanding the operations of multiplication and division - Set 2","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-2-2 Understanding the operations of multiplication and division, representing them using models, forming fact family, finding their results, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. Finding multiplication facts up to (10 أ— 10) and the corresponding division facts.","A group of friends want to share some candy equally. If they have 18 pieces of candy and there are 3 friends, how many pieces of candy will each friend get?","Each friend will get 6 pieces of candy","5 pieces","7 pieces","18 pieces","Each friend will get 6 pieces of candy","18 ÷ 3 = 6 pieces of candy.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-if-you-have-21c5ee2d","Math G3: Understanding the operations of multiplication and division - Set 2","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-2-2 Understanding the operations of multiplication and division, representing them using models, forming fact family, finding their results, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. Finding multiplication facts up to (10 أ— 10) and the corresponding division facts.","If you have 16 pencils and you want to put them in boxes of 2 pencils each, how many boxes can you make?","We can make 6 boxes","We can make 7 boxes","We can make 8 boxes","We can make 9 boxes","We can make 8 boxes","Because 16 divided by 2 equals 8, so we can make 8 boxes with 2 pencils in each box.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-a-library-has-a4f76e06","Math G3: Understanding the operations of multiplication and division - Set 2","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-2-2 Understanding the operations of multiplication and division, representing them using models, forming fact family, finding their results, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. Finding multiplication facts up to (10 أ— 10) and the corresponding division facts.","A library has 24 books to be packed in boxes of 6 books each. How many boxes can be filled?","We can fill 3 boxes","We can fill 4 boxes","We can fill 5 boxes","We can fill 6 boxes","We can fill 4 boxes","Because 24 divided by 6 equals 4, so we can fill 4 boxes with 6 books in each box.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-if-you-have-7da68dc2","Math G3: Understanding the operations of multiplication and division - Set 2","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-2-2 Understanding the operations of multiplication and division, representing them using models, forming fact family, finding their results, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. Finding multiplication facts up to (10 أ— 10) and the corresponding division facts.","If you have 12 toys and you want to put them in bags of 2 toys each, how many bags can you make?","We can make 4 bags","We can make 5 bags","We can make 6 bags","We can make 8 bags","We can make 6 bags","Because 12 divided by 2 equals 6, so we can make 6 bags with 2 toys in each bag.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-if-you-have-58739730","Math G3: Understanding the operations of multiplication and division - Set 2","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-2-2 Understanding the operations of multiplication and division, representing them using models, forming fact family, finding their results, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. Finding multiplication facts up to (10 أ— 10) and the corresponding division facts.","If you have 12 pencils and you want to put them in boxes of 4 pencils each, how many boxes can you make?","We can make 2 boxes","We can make 3 boxes","We can make 4 boxes","We can make 6 boxes","We can make 3 boxes","Because 12 divided by 4 equals 3, so we can make 3 boxes with 4 pencils in each box.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-if-you-have-a96c1c0b","Math G3: Understanding the operations of multiplication and division - Set 2","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-2-2 Understanding the operations of multiplication and division, representing them using models, forming fact family, finding their results, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. Finding multiplication facts up to (10 أ— 10) and the corresponding division facts.","If you have 15 crayons and you want to put them in bags of 5 crayons each, how many bags can you make?","We can make 2 bags","We can make 3 bags","We can make 4 bags","We can make 5 bags","We can make 3 bags","Because 15 divided by 5 equals 3, so we can make 3 bags with 5 crayons in each bag.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-a-toy-car-411b21ee","Math G3: Understanding the operations of multiplication and division - Set 2","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-2-2 Understanding the operations of multiplication and division, representing them using models, forming fact family, finding their results, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. Finding multiplication facts up to (10 أ— 10) and the corresponding division facts.","A toy car track is 24 feet long, and it is to be divided into parts that are 4 feet long each. How many parts can the track be divided into?","We can divide it into 4 parts","We can divide it into 5 parts","We can divide it into 6 parts","We can divide it into 8 parts","We can divide it into 6 parts","Because 24 divided by 4 equals 6, so the track can be divided into 6 parts that are 4 feet long each.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-if-you-have-ec6ea3a3","Math G3: Understanding the operations of multiplication and division - Set 2","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-2-2 Understanding the operations of multiplication and division, representing them using models, forming fact family, finding their results, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. Finding multiplication facts up to (10 أ— 10) and the corresponding division facts.","If you have 20 buttons and you want to put them in jars of 5 buttons each, how many jars can you fill?","We can fill 3 jars","We can fill 4 jars","We can fill 5 jars","We can fill 6 jars","We can fill 4 jars","Because 20 divided by 5 equals 4, so we can fill 4 jars with 5 buttons in each jar.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-a-group-of-1b3b1b87","Math G3: Understanding the operations of multiplication and division - Set 2","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-2-2 Understanding the operations of multiplication and division, representing them using models, forming fact family, finding their results, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. Finding multiplication facts up to (10 أ— 10) and the corresponding division facts.","A group of students want to share some markers equally. If they have 20 markers and there are 5 students, how many markers will each student get?","Each student will get 2 markers","Each student will get 3 markers","Each student will get 4 markers","Each student will get 6 markers","Each student will get 4 markers","Because 20 divided by 5 equals 4, so each student will get 4 markers.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-a-bookshelf-has-f41912db","Math G3: Understanding the operations of multiplication and division - Set 2","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-2-2 Understanding the operations of multiplication and division, representing them using models, forming fact family, finding their results, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. Finding multiplication facts up to (10 أ— 10) and the corresponding division facts.","A bookshelf has 18 books on it, and the books are to be packed in bags of 3 books each. How many bags can be filled?","We can fill 5 bags","We can fill 6 bags","We can fill 7 bags","We can fill 9 bags","We can fill 6 bags","Because 18 divided by 3 equals 6, so we can fill 6 bags with 3 books in each bag.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-a-bookshelf-has-21b9ddd5","Math G3: Understanding the operations of multiplication and division - Set 3","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-2-2 Understanding the operations of multiplication and division, representing them using models, forming fact family, finding their results, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. Finding multiplication facts up to (10 أ— 10) and the corresponding division facts.","A bookshelf has 5 shelves, and each shelf can hold 6 books. How many books can the bookshelf hold in total?","20 books","25 books","26 books","30 books","30 books","To find the total number of books the bookshelf can hold, we multiply the number of shelves by the number of books each shelf can hold, which is 5 x 6 = 30.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-if-i-have-7fd59a49","Math G3: Understanding the operations of multiplication and division - Set 3","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-2-2-1 Distinguishing the characteristics of four operations, and using them to find the results and verify their correctness.","4. Distinguishing the distributive property of multiplication over addition to find the result of multiplying two numbers of 1-digit mentally.","If I have 8 rows of 3 chairs, how many chairs do I have in total?","20 chairs","21 chairs","24 chairs","25 chairs","24 chairs","Since we have 8 rows of 3 chairs, we can multiply 8 by 3 to find the total number of chairs, which is 24.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-if-i-have-6b6f9eeb","Math G3: Understanding the operations of multiplication and division - Set 3","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-2-2-1 Distinguishing the characteristics of four operations, and using them to find the results and verify their correctness.","4. Distinguishing the distributive property of multiplication over addition to find the result of multiplying two numbers of 1-digit mentally.","If I have 9 groups of 5 pencils, how many pencils do I have in total?","40 pencils","45 pencils","50 pencils","55 pencils","45 pencils","Since we have 9 groups of 5 pencils, we can multiply 9 by 5 to find the total number of pencils, which is 45.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-a-library-has-6a80caad","Math G3: Understanding the operations of multiplication and division - Set 3","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-2-2 Understanding the operations of multiplication and division, representing them using models, forming fact family, finding their results, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. Finding multiplication facts up to (10 أ— 10) and the corresponding division facts.","A library has 8 shelves, and each shelf can hold 9 books. How many books can the library hold in total?","64 books","72 books","80 books","90 books","72 books","To find the total number of books the library can hold, we multiply the number of shelves by the number of books each shelf can hold, which is 8 x 9 = 72.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-if-i-have-54bdfedf","Math G3: Understanding the operations of multiplication and division - Set 3","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-2-2 Understanding the operations of multiplication and division, representing them using models, forming fact family, finding their results, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. Finding multiplication facts up to (10 أ— 10) and the corresponding division facts.","If I have 9 boxes of crayons, and each box contains 8 crayons, how many crayons do I have in total?","64 crayons","72 crayons","80 crayons","90 crayons","72 crayons","To find the total number of crayons, we multiply the number of boxes by the number of crayons in each box, which is 9 x 8 = 72.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-a-farmer-has-e450da47","Math G3: Understanding the operations of multiplication and division - Set 3","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-2-2-1 Distinguishing the characteristics of four operations, and using them to find the results and verify their correctness.","4. Distinguishing the distributive property of multiplication over addition to find the result of multiplying two numbers of 1-digit mentally.","A farmer has 6 baskets of apples, with 5 apples in each basket. How many apples does the farmer have in total?","20 apples","25 apples","28 apples","30 apples","30 apples","To find the total number of apples, we multiply the number of baskets by the number of apples in each basket, which is 6 x 5 = 30.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-if-i-have-f003f838","Math G3: Understanding the operations of multiplication and division - Set 3","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-2-2-1 Distinguishing the characteristics of four operations, and using them to find the results and verify their correctness.","4. Distinguishing the distributive property of multiplication over addition to find the result of multiplying two numbers of 1-digit mentally.","If I have 6 boxes of markers, with 8 markers in each box, how many markers do I have in total?","40 markers","45 markers","48 markers","50 markers","48 markers","To find the total number of markers, we multiply the number of boxes by the number of markers in each box, which is 6 x 8 = 48.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-a-toy-car-7015aee3","Math G3: Understanding the operations of multiplication and division - Set 3","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-2-2 Understanding the operations of multiplication and division, representing them using models, forming fact family, finding their results, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. Finding multiplication facts up to (10 أ— 10) and the corresponding division facts.","A toy car track is 4 blocks long, and we want to make 6 tracks. How many blocks will we need in total?","20 blocks","22 blocks","24 blocks","26 blocks","24 blocks","To find the total number of blocks needed, we multiply the number of tracks by the number of blocks per track, which is 6 x 4 = 24.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-if-i-have-68c3623d","Math G3: Understanding the operations of multiplication and division - Set 3","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-2-2-1 Distinguishing the characteristics of four operations, and using them to find the results and verify their correctness.","4. Distinguishing the distributive property of multiplication over addition to find the result of multiplying two numbers of 1-digit mentally.","If I have 3 groups of 4 pencils, how many pencils do I have in total?","12 pencils","10 pencils","8 pencils","6 pencils","12 pencils","Since we have 3 groups of 4 pencils, we can multiply 3 by 4 to find the total number of pencils, which is 12.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-a-bakery-has-456290c9","Math G3: Understanding the operations of multiplication and division - Set 3","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-2-2-1 Distinguishing the characteristics of four operations, and using them to find the results and verify their correctness.","4. Distinguishing the distributive property of multiplication over addition to find the result of multiplying two numbers of 1-digit mentally.","A bakery has 5 trays of cookies, with 6 cookies on each tray. How many cookies does the bakery have in total?","25 cookies","28 cookies","30 cookies","32 cookies","30 cookies","To find the total number of cookies, we multiply the number of trays by the number of cookies on each tray, which is 5 x 6 = 30.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-a-bakery-is-d0bc2648","Math G3: Understanding the operations of multiplication and division - Set 4","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","true","2026-07-12T12:21:48.152Z","3-4-1-2-2 Understanding the operations of multiplication and division, representing them using models, forming fact family, finding their results, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. Finding multiplication facts up to (10 أ— 10) and the corresponding division facts.","A bakery is making cookies for a party. They need to package 18 cookies into boxes of 3 cookies each. How many boxes can they make?","5 boxes","6 boxes","4 boxes","3 boxes","6 boxes","The bakery needs to package 18 cookies into boxes of 3 cookies each, so we divide 18 by 3 to get 6 boxes.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-a-water-bottle-3c286432","Math G3: Understanding the operations of multiplication and division - Set 4","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","true","2026-07-12T12:21:48.152Z","3-4-1-2-2 Understanding the operations of multiplication and division, representing them using models, forming fact family, finding their results, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. Finding multiplication facts up to (10 أ— 10) and the corresponding division facts.","A water bottle can hold 12 ounces of water. If 4 ounces of water are already in the bottle, how many more ounces can be added?","8 ounces","10 ounces","6 ounces","4 ounces","8 ounces","The water bottle can hold 12 ounces of water and 4 ounces are already in the bottle, so we subtract 4 from 12 to get 8 ounces.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-a-group-of-dcad9699","Math G3: Understanding the operations of multiplication and division - Set 4","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","true","2026-07-12T12:21:48.152Z","3-4-1-2-2 Understanding the operations of multiplication and division, representing them using models, forming fact family, finding their results, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. Finding multiplication facts up to (10 أ— 10) and the corresponding division facts.","A group of friends want to share some candy equally. If they have 24 pieces of candy and there are 4 friends, how many pieces of candy will each friend get?","Each friend will get 6 pieces of candy","5 pieces","7 pieces","24 pieces","Each friend will get 6 pieces of candy","24 ÷ 4 = 6 pieces of candy.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-a-bookshelf-has-c873158d","Math G3: Understanding the operations of multiplication and division - Set 4","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","true","2026-07-12T12:21:48.152Z","3-4-1-2-2 Understanding the operations of multiplication and division, representing them using models, forming fact family, finding their results, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. Finding multiplication facts up to (10 أ— 10) and the corresponding division facts.","A bookshelf has 5 shelves, and each shelf can hold 6 books. How many books can the bookshelf hold in total?","20 books","25 books","26 books","30 books","30 books","The bookshelf has 5 shelves, and each shelf can hold 6 books, so we multiply 5 x 6 to get 30 books.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-a-toy-car-2db9dea5","Math G3: Understanding the operations of multiplication and division - Set 4","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","true","2026-07-12T12:21:48.152Z","3-4-1-2-2 Understanding the operations of multiplication and division, representing them using models, forming fact family, finding their results, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. Finding multiplication facts up to (10 أ— 10) and the corresponding division facts.","A toy car track is 12 feet long. If it is divided into sections of 4 feet each, how many sections are there?","2 sections","3 sections","4 sections","6 sections","3 sections","The toy car track is 12 feet long and it is divided into sections of 4 feet each, so we divide 12 by 4 to get 3 sections.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-ashley-has-9-7cbfa5f1","Math G3: Understanding the operations of multiplication and division - Set 4","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","true","2026-07-12T12:21:48.152Z","3-4-1-2-2 Understanding the operations of multiplication and division, representing them using models, forming fact family, finding their results, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. Finding multiplication facts up to (10 أ— 10) and the corresponding division facts.","Ashley has 9 pencils and she wants to share them equally among 3 of her friends. How many pencils will each friend get?","2 pencils","3 pencils","1 pencil","4 pencils","3 pencils","Ashley has 9 pencils and she wants to share them equally among 3 friends, so we divide 9 by 3 to get 3 pencils.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-if-sara-has-fe27ec2b","Math G3: Understanding the operations of multiplication and division - Set 4","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","true","2026-07-12T12:21:48.152Z","3-4-1-2-2 Understanding the operations of multiplication and division, representing them using models, forming fact family, finding their results, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. Finding multiplication facts up to (10 أ— 10) and the corresponding division facts.","If Sara has 12 crayons and she wants to put them into boxes of 4 crayons each, how many boxes can she make?","2 boxes","3 boxes","4 boxes","6 boxes","3 boxes","Sara has 12 crayons and she wants to put them into boxes of 4 crayons each, so we divide 12 by 4 to get 3 boxes.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-if-a-book-fc5d3b85","Math G3: Understanding the operations of multiplication and division - Set 4","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","true","2026-07-12T12:21:48.152Z","3-4-1-2-2 Understanding the operations of multiplication and division, representing them using models, forming fact family, finding their results, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. Finding multiplication facts up to (10 أ— 10) and the corresponding division facts.","If a book costs $12 and you have a $4 discount coupon, how much will you pay for the book?","$8","$10","$12","$16","$8","The book costs $12 and you have a $4 discount coupon, so we subtract 4 from 12 to get $8.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-tom-has-3-730bf48a","Math G3: Understanding the operations of multiplication and division - Set 4","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","true","2026-07-12T12:21:48.152Z","3-4-1-2-2 Understanding the operations of multiplication and division, representing them using models, forming fact family, finding their results, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. Finding multiplication facts up to (10 أ— 10) and the corresponding division facts.","Tom has 3 groups of 4 pencils. How many pencils does Tom have in total?","Tom has 12 pencils","Tom has 7 pencils","Tom has 8 pencils","Tom has 16 pencils","Tom has 12 pencils","3 groups of 4 pencils equals 3 x 4 = 12 pencils.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-if-it-takes-99d9805d","Math G3: Understanding the operations of multiplication and division - Set 4","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","true","2026-07-12T12:21:48.152Z","3-4-1-2-2 Understanding the operations of multiplication and division, representing them using models, forming fact family, finding their results, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. Finding multiplication facts up to (10 أ— 10) and the corresponding division facts.","If it takes 5 apples to make a pie and you have 15 apples, how many pies can you make?","1 pie","2 pies","3 pies","5 pies","3 pies","It takes 5 apples to make a pie and you have 15 apples, so we divide 15 by 5 to get 3 pies.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-if-a-square-106b7ac7","Math G3: Understanding the perimeter and area of a shape - Set 2","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-3-2-1 Understanding the perimeter and area of a shape, estimating, and finding them.","3. Finding the area of a square and a rectangle by using a grid of squares.","If a square has a side length of 4 units, what is its area?","8 square units","12 square units","16 square units","20 square units","16 square units","The area of a square is found by multiplying the side length by itself. So, 4 units x 4 units = 16 square units.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-a-triangle-has-73eca89d","Math G3: Understanding the perimeter and area of a shape - Set 2","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-3-2-1 Understanding the perimeter and area of a shape, estimating, and finding them.","3. Finding the area of a square and a rectangle by using a grid of squares.","A triangle has a base of 5 units and a height of 2 units. What is its area?","4 square units","5 square units","6 square units","10 square units","5 square units","The area of a triangle is found by multiplying the base by the height and dividing by 2. So, (5 units x 2 units) / 2 = 5 square units.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-a-rectangle-has-975dedc2","Math G3: Understanding the perimeter and area of a shape - Set 2","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-3-2-1 Understanding the perimeter and area of a shape, estimating, and finding them.","3. Finding the area of a square and a rectangle by using a grid of squares.","A rectangle has an area of 12 square units. If its length is 4 units, what is its width?","2 units","3 units","4 units","6 units","3 units","To find the width, we divide the area by the length. So, 12 square units / 4 units = 3 units.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-is-area-of-7ca9a89c","Math G3: Understanding the perimeter and area of a shape - Set 2","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-3-2-1 Understanding the perimeter and area of a shape, estimating, and finding them.","3. Finding the area of a square and a rectangle by using a grid of squares.","What is the area of a square with a side length of 5 units?","20 square units","24 square units","25 square units","30 square units","25 square units","The area of a square is found by multiplying the side length by itself. So, 5 units x 5 units = 25 square units.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-a-square-has-48200a2d","Math G3: Understanding the perimeter and area of a shape - Set 2","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-3-2-1 Understanding the perimeter and area of a shape, estimating, and finding them.","3. Finding the area of a square and a rectangle by using a grid of squares.","A square has an area of 9 square units. What is the length of its side?","2 units","3 units","4 units","5 units","3 units","Since it's a square, all sides are equal. The area of a square is side length squared. So, if the area is 9 square units, the side length is the square root of 9, which is 3 units.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-is-area-of-bd259d9a","Math G3: Understanding the perimeter and area of a shape - Set 2","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-3-2-1 Understanding the perimeter and area of a shape, estimating, and finding them.","3. Finding the area of a square and a rectangle by using a grid of squares.","What is the area of a rectangle that is 3 squares long and 2 squares wide?","5 squares","6 squares","8 squares","10 squares","6 squares","To find the area of a rectangle, we multiply the length by the width. In this case, 3 squares x 2 squares = 6 squares.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-a-rectangle-has-397876fd","Math G3: Understanding the perimeter and area of a shape - Set 2","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-3-2-1 Understanding the perimeter and area of a shape, estimating, and finding them.","3. Finding the area of a square and a rectangle by using a grid of squares.","A rectangle has an area of 15 square units. If its width is 3 units, what is its length?","4 units","5 units","6 units","8 units","5 units","To find the length, we divide the area by the width. So, 15 square units / 3 units = 5 units.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-is-area-of-9c49bde1","Math G3: Understanding the perimeter and area of a shape - Set 2","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-3-2-1 Understanding the perimeter and area of a shape, estimating, and finding them.","3. Finding the area of a square and a rectangle by using a grid of squares.","What is the area of a rectangle that is 6 units long and 1 unit wide?","5 square units","6 square units","7 square units","8 square units","6 square units","To find the area of a rectangle, we multiply the length by the width. In this case, 6 units x 1 unit = 6 square units.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-is-area-of-691f0a74","Math G3: Understanding the perimeter and area of a shape - Set 2","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-3-2-1 Understanding the perimeter and area of a shape, estimating, and finding them.","3. Finding the area of a square and a rectangle by using a grid of squares.","What is the area of a triangle with a base of 3 units and a height of 4 units?","3 square units","4 square units","6 square units","12 square units","6 square units","The area of a triangle is found by multiplying the base by the height and dividing by 2. So, (3 units x 4 units) / 2 = 6 square units.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-a-bookshelf-is-69a70278","Math G3: Understanding the perimeter and area of a shape - Set 2","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-3-2-1 Understanding the perimeter and area of a shape, estimating, and finding them.","3. Finding the area of a square and a rectangle by using a grid of squares.","A bookshelf is 5 feet long and 2 feet wide. What is its area?","5 square feet","7 square feet","10 square feet","12 square feet","10 square feet","To find the area of a rectangle, we multiply the length by the width. In this case, 5 feet x 2 feet = 10 square feet.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-a-square-has-880cb35f","Math G3: Understanding the perimeter and area of a shape - Set 3","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-3-2-1 Understanding the perimeter and area of a shape, estimating, and finding them.","3. Finding the area of a square and a rectangle by using a grid of squares.","A square has 25 squares in it. How many rows and columns of squares does it have?","4 rows and 4 columns","5 rows and 5 columns","6 rows and 6 columns","7 rows and 7 columns","5 rows and 5 columns","Since the area of the square is 25 square units, and it is a square, we find the number of rows and columns by finding the number that multiplied by itself gives 25. That number is 5.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-a-square-has-4a965ead","Math G3: Understanding the perimeter and area of a shape - Set 3","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-3-2-1 Understanding the perimeter and area of a shape, estimating, and finding them.","3. Finding the area of a square and a rectangle by using a grid of squares.","A square has 9 squares in it. How many rows and columns of squares does it have?","3 rows and 3 columns","2 rows and 4 columns","4 rows and 2 columns","5 rows and 1 column","3 rows and 3 columns","Since the area of the square is 9 square units, and it is a square, we find the number of rows and columns by finding the number that multiplied by itself gives 9. That number is 3.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-is-area-of-9f52188c","Math G3: Understanding the perimeter and area of a shape - Set 3","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-3-2-1 Understanding the perimeter and area of a shape, estimating, and finding them.","3. Finding the area of a square and a rectangle by using a grid of squares.","What is the area of a rectangle with 5 rows and 3 columns of squares?","12 square units","15 square units","18 square units","20 square units","15 square units","The area of the rectangle is found by multiplying the number of rows by the number of columns. So, 5 rows x 3 columns = 15 squares.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-is-area-of-558f444c","Math G3: Understanding the perimeter and area of a shape - Set 3","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-3-2-1 Understanding the perimeter and area of a shape, estimating, and finding them.","3. Finding the area of a square and a rectangle by using a grid of squares.","What is the area of a square that has 4 rows and 4 columns of squares?","16 square units","12 square units","8 square units","20 square units","16 square units","The area of a square is found by counting the total number of squares inside it. Since this square has 4 rows and 4 columns, we count 4 x 4 = 16 squares.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-if-a-rectangle-d8e5773f","Math G3: Understanding the perimeter and area of a shape - Set 3","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-3-2-1 Understanding the perimeter and area of a shape, estimating, and finding them.","3. Finding the area of a square and a rectangle by using a grid of squares.","If a rectangle has 3 rows and 5 columns of squares, what is its area?","12 square units","15 square units","20 square units","18 square units","15 square units","To find the area of a rectangle, we multiply the number of rows by the number of columns. So, 3 rows x 5 columns = 15 squares.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-if-a-square-564127ca","Math G3: Understanding the perimeter and area of a shape - Set 3","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-3-2-1 Understanding the perimeter and area of a shape, estimating, and finding them.","3. Finding the area of a square and a rectangle by using a grid of squares.","If a square has an area of 16 square units, how many squares are in each row?","2 squares","4 squares","6 squares","8 squares","4 squares","Since the area of the square is 16 square units, and it is a square, we find the number of squares in each row by finding the number that multiplied by itself gives 16. That number is 4.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-a-rectangle-has-3cd4e99c","Math G3: Understanding the perimeter and area of a shape - Set 3","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-3-2-1 Understanding the perimeter and area of a shape, estimating, and finding them.","3. Finding the area of a square and a rectangle by using a grid of squares.","A rectangle has an area of 24 square units. If it has 4 rows, how many columns does it have?","4 columns","5 columns","6 columns","8 columns","6 columns","To find the number of columns, we divide the area by the number of rows. So, 24 square units / 4 rows = 6 columns.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-is-area-of-ac07f727","Math G3: Understanding the perimeter and area of a shape - Set 3","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-3-2-1 Understanding the perimeter and area of a shape, estimating, and finding them.","3. Finding the area of a square and a rectangle by using a grid of squares.","What is the area of a square with 6 rows and 6 columns of squares?","30 square units","32 square units","36 square units","40 square units","36 square units","The area of the square is found by multiplying the number of rows by the number of columns. So, 6 rows x 6 columns = 36 squares.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-is-area-of-2e09b892","Math G3: Understanding the perimeter and area of a shape - Set 3","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-3-2-1 Understanding the perimeter and area of a shape, estimating, and finding them.","3. Finding the area of a square and a rectangle by using a grid of squares.","What is the area of a rectangle with 2 rows and 6 columns of squares?","8 square units","10 square units","12 square units","14 square units","12 square units","The area of the rectangle is found by multiplying the number of rows by the number of columns. So, 2 rows x 6 columns = 12 squares.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-if-a-rectangle-19ea8c09","Math G3: Understanding the perimeter and area of a shape - Set 3","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-3-2-1 Understanding the perimeter and area of a shape, estimating, and finding them.","3. Finding the area of a square and a rectangle by using a grid of squares.","If a rectangle has an area of 20 square units and 4 rows, how many columns does it have?","4 columns","5 columns","6 columns","8 columns","5 columns","To find the number of columns, we divide the area by the number of rows. So, 20 square units / 4 rows = 5 columns.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-is-place-value-4248a4dd","Math G3: Understanding the place value of numbers up to four digits - Set 1","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-1-1 Understanding the place value of numbers up to four digits and using it to represent numbers, read, write, compare, order, and round them.","4. Comparing numbers up to four digits using symbols (>, <, =) and ordering them in ascending and descending order.","What is the place value of the digit 3 in the number 3542?","Ones place","Tens place","Hundreds place","Thousands place","Hundreds place","The digit 3 is in the hundreds place because it is the third digit from the right in the number 3542.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-is-place-value-02d98065","Math G3: Understanding the place value of numbers up to four digits - Set 1","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-1-1 Understanding the place value of numbers up to four digits and using it to represent numbers, read, write, compare, order, and round them.","4. Comparing numbers up to four digits using symbols (>, <, =) and ordering them in ascending and descending order.","What is the place value of the digit 7 in the number 7890?","Ones place","Tens place","Hundreds place","Thousands place","Ones place","The digit 7 is in the ones place because it is the rightmost digit in the number 7890.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-round-number-456-c75d73f5","Math G3: Understanding the place value of numbers up to four digits - Set 1","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-1-1 Understanding the place value of numbers up to four digits and using it to represent numbers, read, write, compare, order, and round them.","1. Identifying the place value of a digit in whole number up to four digits, representing numbers using models, diagrams, number line, and rounding them to the nearest ten, hundred, or thousand.","Round the number 456 to the nearest hundred","400","500","600","700","500","Since 456 is closer to 500 than 400, we round it to the nearest hundred, which is 500.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-if-we-have-9dd57fcf","Math G3: Understanding the place value of numbers up to four digits - Set 1","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-1-1 Understanding the place value of numbers up to four digits and using it to represent numbers, read, write, compare, order, and round them.","1. Identifying the place value of a digit in whole number up to four digits, representing numbers using models, diagrams, number line, and rounding them to the nearest ten, hundred, or thousand.","If we have the number 432, what is the place value of the digit 4?","Tens place","Hundreds place","Thousands place","Ten thousands place","Hundreds place","The digit 4 is in the hundreds place because it is the leftmost digit in the number 432.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-if-we-have-a58a5802","Math G3: Understanding the place value of numbers up to four digits - Set 1","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-1-1 Understanding the place value of numbers up to four digits and using it to represent numbers, read, write, compare, order, and round them.","4. Comparing numbers up to four digits using symbols (>, <, =) and ordering them in ascending and descending order.","If we have the number 987, what is the place value of the digit 9?","Ones place","Tens place","Hundreds place","Thousands place","Hundreds place","The digit 9 is in the hundreds place because it is the leftmost digit in the number 987.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-round-number-1456-ef15ef0a","Math G3: Understanding the place value of numbers up to four digits - Set 1","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-1-1 Understanding the place value of numbers up to four digits and using it to represent numbers, read, write, compare, order, and round them.","1. Identifying the place value of a digit in whole number up to four digits, representing numbers using models, diagrams, number line, and rounding them to the nearest ten, hundred, or thousand.","Round the number 1456 to the nearest thousand","1000","2000","3000","4000","1000","Since 1456 is closer to 1000 than 2000, we round it to the nearest thousand, which is 1000.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-if-we-have-2cffe0ed","Math G3: Understanding the place value of numbers up to four digits - Set 1","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-1-1 Understanding the place value of numbers up to four digits and using it to represent numbers, read, write, compare, order, and round them.","4. Comparing numbers up to four digits using symbols (>, <, =) and ordering them in ascending and descending order.","If we have the number 1234, what is the place value of the digit 1?","Ones place","Tens place","Hundreds place","Thousands place","Thousands place","The digit 1 is in the thousands place because it is the leftmost digit in the number 1234.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-is-place-value-cfe69519","Math G3: Understanding the place value of numbers up to four digits - Set 1","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-1-1 Understanding the place value of numbers up to four digits and using it to represent numbers, read, write, compare, order, and round them.","4. Comparing numbers up to four digits using symbols (>, <, =) and ordering them in ascending and descending order.","What is the place value of the digit 5 in the number 5421?","Ones place","Tens place","Hundreds place","Thousands place","Ones place","The digit 5 is in the ones place because it is the rightmost digit in the number 5421.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-is-place-value-6c54f0c0","Math G3: Understanding the place value of numbers up to four digits - Set 1","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-1-1 Understanding the place value of numbers up to four digits and using it to represent numbers, read, write, compare, order, and round them.","4. Comparing numbers up to four digits using symbols (>, <, =) and ordering them in ascending and descending order.","What is the place value of the digit 9 in the number 9456?","Ones place","Tens place","Hundreds place","Thousands place","Hundreds place","The digit 9 is in the hundreds place because it is the third digit from the right in the number 9456.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-round-number-234-999ee74c","Math G3: Understanding the place value of numbers up to four digits - Set 1","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-1-1 Understanding the place value of numbers up to four digits and using it to represent numbers, read, write, compare, order, and round them.","1. Identifying the place value of a digit in whole number up to four digits, representing numbers using models, diagrams, number line, and rounding them to the nearest ten, hundred, or thousand.","Round the number 234 to the nearest ten","230","240","250","260","230","Since 234 is closer to 230 than 240, we round it to the nearest ten, which is 230.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-is-standard-form-45614bd8","Math G3: Understanding the place value of numbers up to four digits - Set 2","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-1-1 Understanding the place value of numbers up to four digits and using it to represent numbers, read, write, compare, order, and round them.","3. Counting numbers in ascending, descending, and jumping of (two, five, ten, hundred, and thousands), and determining even and odd numbers.","What is the standard form of the number four thousand one hundred twenty five?","4,125","4135","four thousand one hundred twenty six","four thousand one hundred","4,125","The standard form of a number is the way we write it with digits, and for this number, it is 4,125.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-is-standard-form-16140678","Math G3: Understanding the place value of numbers up to four digits - Set 2","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-1-1 Understanding the place value of numbers up to four digits and using it to represent numbers, read, write, compare, order, and round them.","3. Counting numbers in ascending, descending, and jumping of (two, five, ten, hundred, and thousands), and determining even and odd numbers.","What is the standard form of the number two thousand five hundred?","2,500","2510","two thousand five hundred and one","twenty five hundred","2,500","The standard form of a number is the way we write it with digits, and for this number, it is 2,500.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-is-verbal-form-18fec784","Math G3: Understanding the place value of numbers up to four digits - Set 2","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-1-1 Understanding the place value of numbers up to four digits and using it to represent numbers, read, write, compare, order, and round them.","3. Counting numbers in ascending, descending, and jumping of (two, five, ten, hundred, and thousands), and determining even and odd numbers.","What is the verbal form of the number one thousand two hundred?","one thousand two hundred","one thousand two hundred and one","one thousand two","one thousand","one thousand two hundred","The verbal form of a number is the way we say it, and for this number, it is one thousand two hundred.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-is-standard-form-60232b80","Math G3: Understanding the place value of numbers up to four digits - Set 2","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-1-1 Understanding the place value of numbers up to four digits and using it to represent numbers, read, write, compare, order, and round them.","3. Counting numbers in ascending, descending, and jumping of (two, five, ten, hundred, and thousands), and determining even and odd numbers.","What is the standard form of the number two thousand one hundred twenty five?","2,125","2135","two thousand one hundred twenty six","two thousand one hundred","2,125","The standard form of a number is the way we write it with digits, and for this number, it is 2,125.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-is-standard-form-2e3a25c6","Math G3: Understanding the place value of numbers up to four digits - Set 2","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-1-1 Understanding the place value of numbers up to four digits and using it to represent numbers, read, write, compare, order, and round them.","3. Counting numbers in ascending, descending, and jumping of (two, five, ten, hundred, and thousands), and determining even and odd numbers.","What is the standard form of the number three thousand five hundred twenty?","3,520","3530","three thousand five hundred twenty one","three thousand five hundred","3,520","The standard form of a number is the way we write it with digits, and for this number, it is 3,520.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-is-expanded-form-cb00cebd","Math G3: Understanding the place value of numbers up to four digits - Set 2","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-1-1 Understanding the place value of numbers up to four digits and using it to represent numbers, read, write, compare, order, and round them.","3. Counting numbers in ascending, descending, and jumping of (two, five, ten, hundred, and thousands), and determining even and odd numbers.","What is the expanded form of the number one thousand two hundred fifty?","1000 + 200 + 50","1000 + 200 + 5","1000 + 20 + 250","1000 + 200 + 500","1000 + 200 + 50","The expanded form of a number shows the value of each digit, and for this number, it is 1000 + 200 + 50.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-is-expanded-form-2673835c","Math G3: Understanding the place value of numbers up to four digits - Set 2","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-1-1 Understanding the place value of numbers up to four digits and using it to represent numbers, read, write, compare, order, and round them.","3. Counting numbers in ascending, descending, and jumping of (two, five, ten, hundred, and thousands), and determining even and odd numbers.","What is the expanded form of the number one thousand eight hundred seventy five?","1000 + 800 + 70 + 5","1000 + 800 + 75","1000 + 80 + 700 + 5","1000 + 800 + 7 + 5","1000 + 800 + 70 + 5","The expanded form of a number shows the value of each digit, and for this number, it is 1000 + 800 + 70 + 5.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-is-verbal-form-058debe2","Math G3: Understanding the place value of numbers up to four digits - Set 2","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-1-1 Understanding the place value of numbers up to four digits and using it to represent numbers, read, write, compare, order, and round them.","2. Reading and writing numbers up to four digits in standard, verbal, and expanded forms.","What is the verbal form of the number four thousand?","four thousand","four thousand and one","fourty thousand","four thousand four","four thousand","The verbal form of a number is the way we say it, and for this number, it is four thousand.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-is-expanded-form-46a6502f","Math G3: Understanding the place value of numbers up to four digits - Set 2","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-1-1 Understanding the place value of numbers up to four digits and using it to represent numbers, read, write, compare, order, and round them.","2. Reading and writing numbers up to four digits in standard, verbal, and expanded forms.","What is the expanded form of the number three thousand four hundred fifty?","3000 + 400 + 50","3000 + 400 + 5","3000 + 40 + 50","3000 + 400 + 500","3000 + 400 + 50","The expanded form of a number shows the value of each digit, and for this number, it is 3000 + 400 + 50.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-is-verbal-form-51f5de79","Math G3: Understanding the place value of numbers up to four digits - Set 2","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-1-1 Understanding the place value of numbers up to four digits and using it to represent numbers, read, write, compare, order, and round them.","3. Counting numbers in ascending, descending, and jumping of (two, five, ten, hundred, and thousands), and determining even and odd numbers.","What is the verbal form of the number two thousand four hundred?","two thousand four hundred","two thousand four hundred and one","two thousand four","two thousand","two thousand four hundred","The verbal form of a number is the way we say it, and for this number, it is two thousand four hundred.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-number-is-even-dca96a3c","Math G3: Understanding the place value of numbers up to four digits - Set 3","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-1-1 Understanding the place value of numbers up to four digits and using it to represent numbers, read, write, compare, order, and round them.","3. Counting numbers in ascending, descending, and jumping of (two, five, ten, hundred, and thousands), and determining even and odd numbers.","Which number is even: 31, 32, 33, or 34?","31","32","33","34","32","Even numbers always end in 0, 2, 4, 6, or 8.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-number-is-odd-5e231a05","Math G3: Understanding the place value of numbers up to four digits - Set 3","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-1-1 Understanding the place value of numbers up to four digits and using it to represent numbers, read, write, compare, order, and round them.","3. Counting numbers in ascending, descending, and jumping of (two, five, ten, hundred, and thousands), and determining even and odd numbers.","Which number is odd: 14, 15, 16, or 17?","14","15","16","17","17","Odd numbers always end in 1, 3, 5, 7, or 9.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-count-by-hundreds-025bfc63","Math G3: Understanding the place value of numbers up to four digits - Set 3","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-1-1 Understanding the place value of numbers up to four digits and using it to represent numbers, read, write, compare, order, and round them.","3. Counting numbers in ascending, descending, and jumping of (two, five, ten, hundred, and thousands), and determining even and odd numbers.","Count by hundreds from 300 to 500.","300, 400, 500","200, 300, 400","400, 500, 600","500, 600, 700","300, 400, 500","Counting by hundreds means adding 100 each time.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-count-by-tens-83df0363","Math G3: Understanding the place value of numbers up to four digits - Set 3","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-1-1 Understanding the place value of numbers up to four digits and using it to represent numbers, read, write, compare, order, and round them.","3. Counting numbers in ascending, descending, and jumping of (two, five, ten, hundred, and thousands), and determining even and odd numbers.","Count by tens from 50 to 70.","50, 60, 70","40, 50, 60","60, 70, 80","70, 80, 90","50, 60, 70","Counting by tens means adding 10 each time.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-number-is-even-20da0b69","Math G3: Understanding the place value of numbers up to four digits - Set 3","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-1-1 Understanding the place value of numbers up to four digits and using it to represent numbers, read, write, compare, order, and round them.","3. Counting numbers in ascending, descending, and jumping of (two, five, ten, hundred, and thousands), and determining even and odd numbers.","Which number is even: 23, 24, 25, or 26?","23","24","25","26","24","Even numbers always end in 0, 2, 4, 6, or 8.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-number-is-even-185d77dd","Math G3: Understanding the place value of numbers up to four digits - Set 3","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-1-1 Understanding the place value of numbers up to four digits and using it to represent numbers, read, write, compare, order, and round them.","3. Counting numbers in ascending, descending, and jumping of (two, five, ten, hundred, and thousands), and determining even and odd numbers.","Which number is even: 27, 28, 29, or 30?","27","28","29","30","30","Even numbers always end in 0, 2, 4, 6, or 8.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-count-by-hundreds-b6907ec0","Math G3: Understanding the place value of numbers up to four digits - Set 3","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-1-1 Understanding the place value of numbers up to four digits and using it to represent numbers, read, write, compare, order, and round them.","3. Counting numbers in ascending, descending, and jumping of (two, five, ten, hundred, and thousands), and determining even and odd numbers.","Count by hundreds from 500 to 700.","500, 600, 700","600, 700, 800","700, 800, 900","800, 900, 1000","500, 600, 700","Counting by hundreds means adding 100 each time.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-number-is-odd-69ddfc56","Math G3: Understanding the place value of numbers up to four digits - Set 3","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-1-1 Understanding the place value of numbers up to four digits and using it to represent numbers, read, write, compare, order, and round them.","3. Counting numbers in ascending, descending, and jumping of (two, five, ten, hundred, and thousands), and determining even and odd numbers.","Which number is odd: 48, 49, 50, or 51?","48","49","50","51","51","Odd numbers always end in 1, 3, 5, 7, or 9.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-count-by-tens-7b88f2f0","Math G3: Understanding the place value of numbers up to four digits - Set 3","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-1-1 Understanding the place value of numbers up to four digits and using it to represent numbers, read, write, compare, order, and round them.","3. Counting numbers in ascending, descending, and jumping of (two, five, ten, hundred, and thousands), and determining even and odd numbers.","Count by tens from 80 to 100.","80, 90, 100","90, 100, 110","100, 110, 120","110, 120, 130","80, 90, 100","Counting by tens means adding 10 each time.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-count-by-fives-e76f4766","Math G3: Understanding the place value of numbers up to four digits - Set 3","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-1-1 Understanding the place value of numbers up to four digits and using it to represent numbers, read, write, compare, order, and round them.","3. Counting numbers in ascending, descending, and jumping of (two, five, ten, hundred, and thousands), and determining even and odd numbers.","Count by fives from 45 to 65.","45, 50, 55","50, 55, 60","55, 60, 65","60, 65, 70","55, 60, 65","Counting by fives means adding 5 each time.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-number-is-less-877a3d1a","Math G3: Understanding the place value of numbers up to four digits - Set 4","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-1-1 Understanding the place value of numbers up to four digits and using it to represent numbers, read, write, compare, order, and round them.","4. Comparing numbers up to four digits using symbols (>, <, =) and ordering them in ascending and descending order.","Which number is less than 234?","230","240","220","200","220","220 is less than 234 because it has a smaller tens digit.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-number-is-equal-c4762f61","Math G3: Understanding the place value of numbers up to four digits - Set 4","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-1-1 Understanding the place value of numbers up to four digits and using it to represent numbers, read, write, compare, order, and round them.","4. Comparing numbers up to four digits using symbols (>, <, =) and ordering them in ascending and descending order.","Which number is equal to 567?","567","675","756","765","567","567 is equal to 567 because they have the same digits in the same place value.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-number-is-greater-a34c9485","Math G3: Understanding the place value of numbers up to four digits - Set 4","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-1-1 Understanding the place value of numbers up to four digits and using it to represent numbers, read, write, compare, order, and round them.","4. Comparing numbers up to four digits using symbols (>, <, =) and ordering them in ascending and descending order.","Which number is greater than 345?","200","400","300","250","400","400 is greater than 345 because it has the same hundreds and tens digits, but a larger ones digit.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-number-is-less-a7bdf134","Math G3: Understanding the place value of numbers up to four digits - Set 4","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-1-1 Understanding the place value of numbers up to four digits and using it to represent numbers, read, write, compare, order, and round them.","4. Comparing numbers up to four digits using symbols (>, <, =) and ordering them in ascending and descending order.","Which is the greatest number less than 678?","670","650","600","580","670","670 is less than 678 and is the greatest of the listed choices.","","",""
