"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-shape-has-four-e8ad1d26","Math G3: Understanding the properties of two-dimensional and three-di - Set 3","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-3-1-1 Understanding the properties of two-dimensional and three-dimensional geometric shapes, and using them to classify and compare them.","3. Describing and classifying three-dimensional geometric shapes (cube, sphere, cone, cylinder, pyramid, and rectangular prism), and comparing them based on the number of faces and shapes, the number of vertices, and the number of edges.","Which shape has four triangular faces that meet at the top?","A rectangular prism","A cone","A pyramid","A sphere","A pyramid","A pyramid has four triangular faces that meet at the top, which makes it different from other three-dimensional shapes.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-shape-is-round-37879497","Math G3: Understanding the properties of two-dimensional and three-di - Set 3","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-3-1-1 Understanding the properties of two-dimensional and three-dimensional geometric shapes, and using them to classify and compare them.","3. Describing and classifying three-dimensional geometric shapes (cube, sphere, cone, cylinder, pyramid, and rectangular prism), and comparing them based on the number of faces and shapes, the number of vertices, and the number of edges.","Which shape is round and has no edges or faces?","A rectangular prism","A cone","A sphere","A pyramid","A sphere","A sphere is a round shape with no edges or faces, which makes it unique among three-dimensional shapes.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-shape-has-a-f4ef389a","Math G3: Understanding the properties of two-dimensional and three-di - Set 3","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-3-1-1 Understanding the properties of two-dimensional and three-dimensional geometric shapes, and using them to classify and compare them.","3. Describing and classifying three-dimensional geometric shapes (cube, sphere, cone, cylinder, pyramid, and rectangular prism), and comparing them based on the number of faces and shapes, the number of vertices, and the number of edges.","What shape has a circular base and gets smaller as it goes up?","A cylinder","A cone","A pyramid","A cube","A cone","A cone has a circular base and gets smaller as it goes up, which makes it different from other three-dimensional shapes.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-shape-has-six-ea65a1eb","Math G3: Understanding the properties of two-dimensional and three-di - Set 3","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-3-1-1 Understanding the properties of two-dimensional and three-dimensional geometric shapes, and using them to classify and compare them.","3. Describing and classifying three-dimensional geometric shapes (cube, sphere, cone, cylinder, pyramid, and rectangular prism), and comparing them based on the number of faces and shapes, the number of vertices, and the number of edges.","Which shape has six rectangular faces?","A cube","A cone","A rectangular prism","A sphere","A rectangular prism","A rectangular prism has six rectangular faces, which makes it different from other three-dimensional shapes.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-a-group-of-5c279544","Multiplication & Division: Models & Fact Families - Math (Grade 3)","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-2-2 Understanding the operations of multiplication and division, representing them using models, forming fact family, finding their results, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. Finding multiplication facts up to (10 أ— 10) and the corresponding division facts.","A group of friends want to share some candy equally. If they have 24 pieces of candy and there are 4 friends, how many pieces of candy will each friend get?","Each friend will get 6 pieces of candy","5 pieces","7 pieces","24 pieces","Each friend will get 6 pieces of candy","24 ÷ 4 = 6 pieces of candy.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-a-basket-contains-dc4f2ed1","Multiplication & Division: Models & Fact Families - Math (Grade 3)","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-2-2 Understanding the operations of multiplication and division, representing them using models, forming fact family, finding their results, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. Finding multiplication facts up to (10 أ— 10) and the corresponding division facts.","A basket contains 12 apples. If 4 apples are eaten, how many apples are left in the basket?","There are 8 apples left","There are 10 apples left","There are 12 apples left","There are 16 apples left","There are 8 apples left","To find the number of apples left, we subtract the number of apples eaten from the total number of apples. 12 - 4 = 8.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-if-a-toy-c6ed357c","Multiplication & Division: Models & Fact Families - Math (Grade 3)","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-2-2 Understanding the operations of multiplication and division, representing them using models, forming fact family, finding their results, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. Finding multiplication facts up to (10 أ— 10) and the corresponding division facts.","If a toy car costs $6 and you have a $5 bill, how much more money do you need to buy the toy car?","You need $1 more","You need $2 more","You need $3 more","You need $4 more","You need $1 more","To find out how much more money you need, we subtract the amount of money you have from the cost of the toy car. $6 - $5 = $1.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-a-pencil-is-3d3c539a","Multiplication & Division: Models & Fact Families - Math (Grade 3)","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-2-2 Understanding the operations of multiplication and division, representing them using models, forming fact family, finding their results, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. Finding multiplication facts up to (10 أ— 10) and the corresponding division facts.","A pencil is 15 cm long. If it is divided into 5 parts, how long is each part?","Each part is 2 cm long","Each part is 3 cm long","Each part is 5 cm long","Each part is 7 cm long","Each part is 3 cm long","To find the length of each part, we divide the total length of the pencil by the number of parts. 15 ÷ 5 = 3.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-if-you-have-e5c4d136","Multiplication & Division: Models & Fact Families - Math (Grade 3)","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-2-2 Understanding the operations of multiplication and division, representing them using models, forming fact family, finding their results, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. Finding multiplication facts up to (10 أ— 10) and the corresponding division facts.","If you have 9 pencils and you want to put them into boxes that hold 3 pencils each, how many boxes can you fill?","You can fill 2 boxes","You can fill 3 boxes","You can fill 4 boxes","You can fill 6 boxes","You can fill 3 boxes","To find the number of boxes you can fill, we divide the total number of pencils by the number of pencils each box can hold. 9 ÷ 3 = 3.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-a-toy-box-ef456245","Multiplication & Division: Models & Fact Families - Math (Grade 3)","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-2-2 Understanding the operations of multiplication and division, representing them using models, forming fact family, finding their results, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. Finding multiplication facts up to (10 أ— 10) and the corresponding division facts.","A toy box contains 18 toys. If 6 toys are taken out, how many toys are left in the box?","There are 12 toys left","There are 14 toys left","There are 16 toys left","There are 18 toys left","There are 12 toys left","To find the number of toys left, we subtract the number of toys taken out from the total number of toys. 18 - 6 = 12.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-if-a-book-939fcf35","Multiplication & Division: Models & Fact Families - Math (Grade 3)","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-2-2 Understanding the operations of multiplication and division, representing them using models, forming fact family, finding their results, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. Finding multiplication facts up to (10 أ— 10) and the corresponding division facts.","If a book costs $8 and you have a $10 bill, how much change will you get?","You will get $2 change","You will get $4 change","You will get $6 change","You will get $8 change","You will get $2 change","To find the change, we subtract the cost of the book from the amount of money you have. $10 - $8 = $2.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-tom-has-6-531ad6f0","Multiplication & Division: Models & Fact Families - Math (Grade 3)","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-2-2 Understanding the operations of multiplication and division, representing them using models, forming fact family, finding their results, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. Finding multiplication facts up to (10 أ— 10) and the corresponding division facts.","Tom has 6 groups of 4 pencils. How many pencils does Tom have in total?","Tom has 24 pencils","Tom has 10 pencils","Tom has 20 pencils","Tom has 28 pencils","Tom has 24 pencils","6 groups of 4 pencils equals 6 x 4 = 24 pencils.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-a-bookshelf-has-13738162","Multiplication & Division: Models & Fact Families - Math (Grade 3)","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-2-2 Understanding the operations of multiplication and division, representing them using models, forming fact family, finding their results, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. Finding multiplication facts up to (10 أ— 10) and the corresponding division facts.","A bookshelf has 5 shelves, and each shelf can hold 3 books. How many books can the bookshelf hold in total?","The bookshelf can hold 10 books","The bookshelf can hold 12 books","The bookshelf can hold 15 books","The bookshelf can hold 20 books","The bookshelf can hold 15 books","To find the total number of books the bookshelf can hold, we multiply the number of shelves by the number of books each shelf can hold. 5 x 3 = 15.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-if-sara-has-e5e4a5f7","Multiplication & Division: Models & Fact Families - Math (Grade 3)","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-2-2 Understanding the operations of multiplication and division, representing them using models, forming fact family, finding their results, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. Finding multiplication facts up to (10 أ— 10) and the corresponding division facts.","If Sara has 18 crayons and she gives 2 crayons to her friend, how many crayons does Sara have left?","Sara has 16 crayons","Sara has 18 crayons","Sara has 20 crayons","Sara has 22 crayons","Sara has 16 crayons","To find the number of crayons Sara has left, we subtract the number of crayons she gave away from the total number of crayons she had. 18 - 2 = 16.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-of-is-expanded-6b41812c","Place Value: Four-Digit Numbers - Math (Grade 3)","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-1-1 Understanding the place value of numbers up to four digits and using it to represent numbers, read, write, compare, order, and round them.","2. Reading and writing numbers up to four digits in standard, verbal, and expanded forms.","What is the expanded form of 9,100?","9,000 + 100","9,000 + 10","900 + 100","9,100 + 100","9,000 + 100","The expanded form of 9,100 is 9,000 + 100.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-of-is-standard-8182554b","Place Value: Four-Digit Numbers - Math (Grade 3)","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-1-1 Understanding the place value of numbers up to four digits and using it to represent numbers, read, write, compare, order, and round them.","2. Reading and writing numbers up to four digits in standard, verbal, and expanded forms.","What is the standard form of three thousand nine hundred?","3,900","390","39,000","3,090","3,900","Three thousand nine hundred is written 3,900 in standard form.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-is-verbal-form-c46af117","Place Value: Four-Digit Numbers - Math (Grade 3)","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-1-1 Understanding the place value of numbers up to four digits and using it to represent numbers, read, write, compare, order, and round them.","3. Counting numbers in ascending, descending, and jumping of (two, five, ten, hundred, and thousands), and determining even and odd numbers.","What is the word form of 8,200?","Eight thousand two hundred","Eight hundred twenty","Eighty-two thousand","Eight thousand twenty","Eight thousand two hundred","The word form of 8,200 is eight thousand two hundred.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-of-is-verbal-af0ec008","Place Value: Four-Digit Numbers - Math (Grade 3)","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-1-1 Understanding the place value of numbers up to four digits and using it to represent numbers, read, write, compare, order, and round them.","1. Identifying the place value of a digit in whole number up to four digits, representing numbers using models, diagrams, number line, and rounding them to the nearest ten, hundred, or thousand.","What is the word form of 1,400?","One thousand four hundred","One hundred forty","Fourteen thousand","One thousand forty","One thousand four hundred","The word form of 1,400 is one thousand four hundred.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-is-verbal-form-099b1d00","Place Value: Four-Digit Numbers - Math (Grade 3)","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-1-1 Understanding the place value of numbers up to four digits and using it to represent numbers, read, write, compare, order, and round them.","3. Counting numbers in ascending, descending, and jumping of (two, five, ten, hundred, and thousands), and determining even and odd numbers.","What is the word form of 6,405?","Six thousand four hundred five","Six thousand forty-five","Six hundred forty-five","Sixty-four thousand five","Six thousand four hundred five","The word form of 6,405 is six thousand four hundred five.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-of-is-standard-a4054d7f","Place Value: Four-Digit Numbers - Math (Grade 3)","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-1-1 Understanding the place value of numbers up to four digits and using it to represent numbers, read, write, compare, order, and round them.","3. Counting numbers in ascending, descending, and jumping of (two, five, ten, hundred, and thousands), and determining even and odd numbers.","What is the word form of 3,200?","Three thousand two hundred","Three hundred twenty","Thirty-two thousand","Three thousand twenty","Three thousand two hundred","The word form of 3,200 is three thousand two hundred.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-is-expanded-form-9b56988d","Place Value: Four-Digit Numbers - Math (Grade 3)","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-1-1 Understanding the place value of numbers up to four digits and using it to represent numbers, read, write, compare, order, and round them.","2. Reading and writing numbers up to four digits in standard, verbal, and expanded forms.","What is the expanded form of 5,600?","5,000 + 600","5,000 + 60","500 + 600","5,600 + 100","5,000 + 600","The expanded form of 5,600 is 5,000 + 600.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-is-expanded-form-09c2ed08","Place Value: Four-Digit Numbers - Math (Grade 3)","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-1-1 Understanding the place value of numbers up to four digits and using it to represent numbers, read, write, compare, order, and round them.","3. Counting numbers in ascending, descending, and jumping of (two, five, ten, hundred, and thousands), and determining even and odd numbers.","What is the word form of 4,753?","Four thousand seven hundred fifty-three","Four thousand five hundred seventy-three","Four hundred seventy-five","Forty-seven thousand fifty-three","Four thousand seven hundred fifty-three","The word form of 4,753 is four thousand seven hundred fifty-three.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-of-is-expanded-212e9bda","Place Value: Four-Digit Numbers - Math (Grade 3)","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-1-1 Understanding the place value of numbers up to four digits and using it to represent numbers, read, write, compare, order, and round them.","2. Reading and writing numbers up to four digits in standard, verbal, and expanded forms.","What is the expanded form of 2,100?","2,000 + 100","2,000 + 10","200 + 100","2,100 + 100","2,000 + 100","The number 2,100 has 2 thousands and 1 hundred, so its expanded form is 2,000 + 100.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-is-standard-form-d72ad6c4","Place Value: Four-Digit Numbers - Math (Grade 3)","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-1-1 Understanding the place value of numbers up to four digits and using it to represent numbers, read, write, compare, order, and round them.","2. Reading and writing numbers up to four digits in standard, verbal, and expanded forms.","What is the standard form of seven thousand eight hundred?","7,800","780","78,000","7,080","7,800","Seven thousand eight hundred is written 7,800 in standard form.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-number-is-greater-4c55ba21","Place Value: Four-Digit Numbers - Math (Grade 3) - Set 2","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-1-1 Understanding the place value of numbers up to four digits and using it to represent numbers, read, write, compare, order, and round them.","4. Comparing numbers up to four digits using symbols (>, <, =) and ordering them in ascending and descending order.","Which is the smallest number greater than 1,500?","1,800","2,000","1,500","1,400","1,800","1,800 is greater than 1,500 and is the smallest listed number that satisfies the condition.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-number-is-greater-c99a99d4","Place Value: Four-Digit Numbers - Math (Grade 3) - Set 2","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-1-1 Understanding the place value of numbers up to four digits and using it to represent numbers, read, write, compare, order, and round them.","4. Comparing numbers up to four digits using symbols (>, <, =) and ordering them in ascending and descending order.","Which is the greatest number?","2,500","2,800","2,400","2,000","2,800","2,800 is greater than all the other listed numbers.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-number-is-equal-9d9de9f7","Place Value: Four-Digit Numbers - Math (Grade 3) - Set 2","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-1-1 Understanding the place value of numbers up to four digits and using it to represent numbers, read, write, compare, order, and round them.","4. Comparing numbers up to four digits using symbols (>, <, =) and ordering them in ascending and descending order.","Which number is equal to 3,000?","3,500","3,000","2,000","4,000","3,000","3,000 is equal to 3,000 because they have the same digits and the same place value.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-number-is-equal-1b58b079","Place Value: Four-Digit Numbers - Math (Grade 3) - Set 2","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-1-1 Understanding the place value of numbers up to four digits and using it to represent numbers, read, write, compare, order, and round them.","4. Comparing numbers up to four digits using symbols (>, <, =) and ordering them in ascending and descending order.","Which number is equal to 1,200?","1,000","1,200","1,500","1,800","1,200","1,200 is equal to 1,200 because they have the same digits and the same place value.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-number-is-less-4ad67766","Place Value: Four-Digit Numbers - Math (Grade 3) - Set 2","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-1-1 Understanding the place value of numbers up to four digits and using it to represent numbers, read, write, compare, order, and round them.","4. Comparing numbers up to four digits using symbols (>, <, =) and ordering them in ascending and descending order.","Which is the greatest number less than 1,000?","900","1,000","500","1,200","900","900 is less than 1,000 and is greater than the other listed number below 1,000.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-number-is-less-aff5dd21","Place Value: Four-Digit Numbers - Math (Grade 3) - Set 2","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-1-1 Understanding the place value of numbers up to four digits and using it to represent numbers, read, write, compare, order, and round them.","1. Identifying the place value of a digit in whole number up to four digits, representing numbers using models, diagrams, number line, and rounding them to the nearest ten, hundred, or thousand.","Which number is less than 3,000?","2,500","3,000","2,000","4,000","2,000","2,000 is less than 3,000 because it has fewer digits and the digit in the thousands place is smaller.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-number-is-greater-b460289e","Place Value: Four-Digit Numbers - Math (Grade 3) - Set 2","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-1-1 Understanding the place value of numbers up to four digits and using it to represent numbers, read, write, compare, order, and round them.","1. Identifying the place value of a digit in whole number up to four digits, representing numbers using models, diagrams, number line, and rounding them to the nearest ten, hundred, or thousand.","Which number is greater than 1,000?","900","1,000","1,200","500","1,200","1,200 is greater than 1,000 because it has more digits and the digit in the thousands place is larger.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-number-is-equal-1df41842","Place Value: Four-Digit Numbers - Math (Grade 3) - Set 2","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-1-1 Understanding the place value of numbers up to four digits and using it to represent numbers, read, write, compare, order, and round them.","4. Comparing numbers up to four digits using symbols (>, <, =) and ordering them in ascending and descending order.","Which number is equal to 2,500?","2,400","2,500","2,600","2,800","2,500","2,500 is equal to 2,500 because they have the same digits and the same place value.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-number-is-greater-e312ddeb","Place Value: Four-Digit Numbers - Math (Grade 3) - Set 2","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-1-1 Understanding the place value of numbers up to four digits and using it to represent numbers, read, write, compare, order, and round them.","1. Identifying the place value of a digit in whole number up to four digits, representing numbers using models, diagrams, number line, and rounding them to the nearest ten, hundred, or thousand.","Which number is greater than 4,000?","3,000","4,000","4,500","5,000","5,000","5,000 is greater than 4,000 because it has more digits and the digit in the thousands place is larger.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-number-is-less-05156a99","Place Value: Four-Digit Numbers - Math (Grade 3) - Set 2","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-1-1-1 Understanding the place value of numbers up to four digits and using it to represent numbers, read, write, compare, order, and round them.","4. Comparing numbers up to four digits using symbols (>, <, =) and ordering them in ascending and descending order.","Which number is less than 2,500?","2,800","2,500","2,600","2,400","2,400","2,400 is the only listed number less than 2,500.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-a-class-has-a7565293","Time: Measurement, Estimation & Problem Solving - Math (Grade 3)","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-3-2-4 Estimating and measuring time using appropriate units of time, reading and writing time, and solving mathematical problems including time applications.","1. Selecting the suitable units of time (seconds, minutes, hours) to estimate the duration of an event.","A class has 45 minutes of recess. What unit of time should the teacher use to tell the students how long they have?","Seconds","Minutes","Hours","Days","Minutes","The teacher should use minutes because 45 minutes is a short amount of time.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-a-basketball-game-3ad5349f","Time: Measurement, Estimation & Problem Solving - Math (Grade 3)","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-3-2-4 Estimating and measuring time using appropriate units of time, reading and writing time, and solving mathematical problems including time applications.","1. Selecting the suitable units of time (seconds, minutes, hours) to estimate the duration of an event.","A basketball game is 4 quarters long, each quarter lasting 12 minutes. What unit of time should the coach use to tell the players how long each quarter is?","Seconds","Minutes","Hours","Days","Minutes","The coach should use minutes because 12 minutes is a short amount of time.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-a-school-day-6a4128ce","Time: Measurement, Estimation & Problem Solving - Math (Grade 3)","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-3-2-4 Estimating and measuring time using appropriate units of time, reading and writing time, and solving mathematical problems including time applications.","1. Selecting the suitable units of time (seconds, minutes, hours) to estimate the duration of an event.","A school day is 8 hours long. What unit of time is best to use to measure the school day's length?","Seconds","Minutes","Hours","Days","Hours","Hours are the best unit of time to use because 8 hours is a longer amount of time.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-a-child-has-2dfd783f","Time: Measurement, Estimation & Problem Solving - Math (Grade 3)","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-3-2-4 Estimating and measuring time using appropriate units of time, reading and writing time, and solving mathematical problems including time applications.","1. Selecting the suitable units of time (seconds, minutes, hours) to estimate the duration of an event.","A child has 5 minutes to finish their homework. What unit of time should they use to measure the time?","Seconds","Minutes","Hours","Days","Minutes","The child should use minutes because 5 minutes is a short amount of time.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-a-concert-is-b2fc18ea","Time: Measurement, Estimation & Problem Solving - Math (Grade 3)","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-3-2-4 Estimating and measuring time using appropriate units of time, reading and writing time, and solving mathematical problems including time applications.","1. Selecting the suitable units of time (seconds, minutes, hours) to estimate the duration of an event.","A concert is 2 hours and 30 minutes long. What unit of time is best to use to measure the concert's length?","Seconds","Minutes","Hours","Days","Hours","Hours are the best unit of time to use because 2 hours and 30 minutes is a longer amount of time.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-a-child-has-113ee57d","Time: Measurement, Estimation & Problem Solving - Math (Grade 3)","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-3-2-4 Estimating and measuring time using appropriate units of time, reading and writing time, and solving mathematical problems including time applications.","1. Selecting the suitable units of time (seconds, minutes, hours) to estimate the duration of an event.","A child has 10 seconds to finish their task. What unit of time should they use to measure the time?","Seconds","Minutes","Hours","Days","Seconds","The child should use seconds because 10 seconds is a short amount of time.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-a-song-is-95136a35","Time: Measurement, Estimation & Problem Solving - Math (Grade 3)","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-3-2-4 Estimating and measuring time using appropriate units of time, reading and writing time, and solving mathematical problems including time applications.","1. Selecting the suitable units of time (seconds, minutes, hours) to estimate the duration of an event.","A song is 3 minutes long. What unit of time is best to use to measure the song's length?","Seconds","Minutes","Hours","Days","Minutes","Minutes are the best unit of time to use because 3 minutes is a short amount of time.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-a-movie-is-74bfff7a","Time: Measurement, Estimation & Problem Solving - Math (Grade 3)","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-3-2-4 Estimating and measuring time using appropriate units of time, reading and writing time, and solving mathematical problems including time applications.","1. Selecting the suitable units of time (seconds, minutes, hours) to estimate the duration of an event.","A movie is 2 hours long. What unit of time is best to use to measure the movie's length?","Seconds","Minutes","Hours","Days","Hours","Hours are the best unit of time to use because 2 hours is a longer amount of time.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-a-car-ride-8a94555c","Time: Measurement, Estimation & Problem Solving - Math (Grade 3)","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-3-2-4 Estimating and measuring time using appropriate units of time, reading and writing time, and solving mathematical problems including time applications.","1. Selecting the suitable units of time (seconds, minutes, hours) to estimate the duration of an event.","A car ride is 1 hour and 15 minutes long. What unit of time is best to use to measure the car ride's length?","Seconds","Minutes","Hours","Days","Hours","Hours are the best unit of time to use because 1 hour and 15 minutes is a longer amount of time.","","",""
"math-g3-3-4-2-1-1-tommy-has-30-2d87b5cb","Time: Measurement, Estimation & Problem Solving - Math (Grade 3)","Math","3","Assessment for Grade 3 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","3-4-3-2-4 Estimating and measuring time using appropriate units of time, reading and writing time, and solving mathematical problems including time applications.","1. Selecting the suitable units of time (seconds, minutes, hours) to estimate the duration of an event.","Tommy has 30 seconds to finish his task. What unit of time should he use to measure the time?","Seconds","Minutes","Hours","Days","Seconds","Tommy should use seconds because 30 seconds is a short amount of time.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-result-of-3476c08d","Math G6: Adding and subtracting whole numbers up - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-5 Performing the four operations on Proper fractions and mixed numbers and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications","3. Multiplying and dividing Proper fractions, and writing the result in simplest form.","What is the result of multiplying 654 by 9?","5,886","5,298","6,474","58,860","5,886","Multiply 654 by 9: 654 x 9 = 5,886.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-result-of-02cd1389","Math G6: Adding and subtracting whole numbers up - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-5 Performing the four operations on Proper fractions and mixed numbers and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications","3. Multiplying and dividing Proper fractions, and writing the result in simplest form.","What is the result of multiplying 135 by 6?","810","729","891","8,100","810","Multiply 135 by 6: 135 x 6 = 810.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-product-of-bdefcb8f","Math G6: Adding and subtracting whole numbers up - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-1 Adding and subtracting whole numbers up to seven digits, multiplying numbers up to three digits, dividing numbers of up to four digits by numbers of up to two digits, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Adding and subtracting numbers up to seven digits, with and without regrouping.","What is the product of 482 and 4?","1,928","1,736","2,120","19,280","1,928","Multiply 482 by 4: 482 x 4 = 1,928.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-result-of-f815e817","Math G6: Adding and subtracting whole numbers up - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-5 Performing the four operations on Proper fractions and mixed numbers and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications","3. Multiplying and dividing Proper fractions, and writing the result in simplest form.","What is the result of multiplying 963 by 2?","1,926","1,734","2,118","19,260","1,926","Multiply 963 by 2: 963 x 2 = 1,926.","","",""
