"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-result-of-834486b9","Math G6: Adding and subtracting whole numbers up - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-1-2 Distinguishing Proper fractions, mixed numbers, and improper fractions, representing, reading, writing, comparing, ordering, and rounding them.","2. Finding equivalent fractions, converting them to their simplest form, and rounding them to the nearest whole, half, or one.","What is 4321 ÷ 18, rounded to the nearest whole number?","240","216","264","2,400","240","4,321 ÷ 18 = 240.06, which rounds to 240.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-result-of-d70bfa1e","Math G6: Adding and subtracting whole numbers up - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-1-2 Distinguishing Proper fractions, mixed numbers, and improper fractions, representing, reading, writing, comparing, ordering, and rounding them.","2. Finding equivalent fractions, converting them to their simplest form, and rounding them to the nearest whole, half, or one.","What is 1234 ÷ 17, rounded to the nearest whole number?","73","66","80","730","73","1,234 ÷ 17 = 72.59, which rounds to 73.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-result-of-5cd3fadc","Math G6: Adding and subtracting whole numbers up - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-1-2 Distinguishing Proper fractions, mixed numbers, and improper fractions, representing, reading, writing, comparing, ordering, and rounding them.","2. Finding equivalent fractions, converting them to their simplest form, and rounding them to the nearest whole, half, or one.","What is 6789 ÷ 29, rounded to the nearest whole number?","234","211","257","2,340","234","6,789 ÷ 29 = 234.10, which rounds to 234.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-result-of-0187eaa8","Math G6: Adding and subtracting whole numbers up - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-1-2 Distinguishing Proper fractions, mixed numbers, and improper fractions, representing, reading, writing, comparing, ordering, and rounding them.","2. Finding equivalent fractions, converting them to their simplest form, and rounding them to the nearest whole, half, or one.","What is 1234 ÷ 34, rounded to the nearest whole number?","36","33","39","360","36","1,234 ÷ 34 = 36.29, which rounds to 36.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-result-of-b7248e7f","Math G6: Adding and subtracting whole numbers up - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-1-2 Distinguishing Proper fractions, mixed numbers, and improper fractions, representing, reading, writing, comparing, ordering, and rounding them.","2. Finding equivalent fractions, converting them to their simplest form, and rounding them to the nearest whole, half, or one.","What is 5432 ÷ 19, rounded to the nearest whole number?","286","258","314","2,860","286","5,432 ÷ 19 = 285.89, which rounds to 286.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-result-of-4e77ee84","Math G6: Adding and subtracting whole numbers up - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-1-2 Distinguishing Proper fractions, mixed numbers, and improper fractions, representing, reading, writing, comparing, ordering, and rounding them.","2. Finding equivalent fractions, converting them to their simplest form, and rounding them to the nearest whole, half, or one.","What is 4321 ÷ 17, rounded to the nearest whole number?","254","229","279","2,540","254","4,321 ÷ 17 = 254.18, which rounds to 254.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-pizza-parlor-4f638586","Math G6: Adding and subtracting whole numbers up - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-1 Adding and subtracting whole numbers up to seven digits, multiplying numbers up to three digits, dividing numbers of up to four digits by numbers of up to two digits, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Adding and subtracting numbers up to seven digits, with and without regrouping.","A pizza parlor sells large pizzas for $15 each. If a customer orders 3 large pizzas, how much will they pay in total?","$45","$50","$55","$60","$45","To find the total amount the customer will pay, we need to multiply the price of one pizza by the number of pizzas ordered.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-rectangular-garden-c4333014","Math G6: Adding and subtracting whole numbers up - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-3-3 Understanding volume and surface area and their units, distinguishing the formulas for a right rectangular prism, and applying them to find volume and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Estimating volume and measuring it by using the appropriate metric unit (ml3, cm3, and m3).","A rectangular garden measures 15 meters by 8 meters. What is its area?","120 square meters","1200 square meters","12000 square meters","120000 square meters","120 square meters","To find the area of the garden, we need to multiply the length by the width.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-bookshelf-has-fef3512d","Math G6: Adding and subtracting whole numbers up - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-1 Adding and subtracting whole numbers up to seven digits, multiplying numbers up to three digits, dividing numbers of up to four digits by numbers of up to two digits, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Adding and subtracting numbers up to seven digits, with and without regrouping.","A bookshelf has 5 shelves, and each shelf can hold 8 books. If the bookshelf is currently empty, how many books can be placed on it in total?","40 books","30 books","45 books","50 books","40 books","Multiply shelves by books per shelf: 5 x 8 = 40 books.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-group-of-9ac5d162","Math G6: Adding and subtracting whole numbers up - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-1 Adding and subtracting whole numbers up to seven digits, multiplying numbers up to three digits, dividing numbers of up to four digits by numbers of up to two digits, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Adding and subtracting numbers up to seven digits, with and without regrouping.","A group of students want to buy a book that costs $25. If they have a 10% discount coupon, how much will they pay for the book?","$22.50","$22.75","$23.00","$23.50","$22.50","To find the amount they will pay for the book, we need to calculate 10% of the original price and subtract it from the original price.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-bakery-sells-d656bfd0","Math G6: Adding and subtracting whole numbers up - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-1 Adding and subtracting whole numbers up to seven digits, multiplying numbers up to three digits, dividing numbers of up to four digits by numbers of up to two digits, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Adding and subtracting numbers up to seven digits, with and without regrouping.","A bakery sells 250 loaves of bread per day. If they operate 7 days a week, how many loaves of bread do they sell in a week?","1200 loaves","1400 loaves","1500 loaves","1700 loaves","1500 loaves","To find the total number of loaves of bread sold in a week, we need to multiply the number of loaves sold per day by the number of days in a week.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-bicycle-travels-3e143cea","Math G6: Adding and subtracting whole numbers up - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-1 Adding and subtracting whole numbers up to seven digits, multiplying numbers up to three digits, dividing numbers of up to four digits by numbers of up to two digits, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Adding and subtracting numbers up to seven digits, with and without regrouping.","A bicycle travels 18 kilometers in 3 hours. How many kilometers does it travel per hour?","5 kilometers per hour","6 kilometers per hour","7 kilometers per hour","8 kilometers per hour","6 kilometers per hour","To find the speed of the bicycle, we need to divide the distance traveled by the time taken.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-group-of-fc7c516c","Math G6: Adding and subtracting whole numbers up - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-1 Adding and subtracting whole numbers up to seven digits, multiplying numbers up to three digits, dividing numbers of up to four digits by numbers of up to two digits, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Adding and subtracting numbers up to seven digits, with and without regrouping.","A group of friends want to share some money equally. If they have $480 and there are 8 friends, how much money will each friend get?","$60","$64","$65","$68","$60","To find the amount of money each friend will get, we need to divide the total amount of money by the number of friends.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-car-travels-02945b43","Math G6: Adding and subtracting whole numbers up - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-1 Adding and subtracting whole numbers up to seven digits, multiplying numbers up to three digits, dividing numbers of up to four digits by numbers of up to two digits, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Adding and subtracting numbers up to seven digits, with and without regrouping.","A car travels 250 miles in 5 hours. How many miles does it travel per hour?","50 miles per hour","40 miles per hour","60 miles per hour","250 miles per hour","50 miles per hour","250 ÷ 5 = 50 miles per hour.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-tom-has-4567-9d284792","Math G6: Adding and subtracting whole numbers up - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-1 Adding and subtracting whole numbers up to seven digits, multiplying numbers up to three digits, dividing numbers of up to four digits by numbers of up to two digits, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Adding and subtracting numbers up to seven digits, with and without regrouping.","Tom has 4567 pencils in his pencil case. He gives 123 pencils to his friend. How many pencils does Tom have left?","Tom has 4344 pencils left.","Tom has 3434 pencils left.","Tom has 4345 pencils left.","Tom has 3435 pencils left.","Tom has 4344 pencils left.","To find the number of pencils Tom has left, we need to subtract 123 from 4567.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-water-tank-cb4d07ad","Math G6: Adding and subtracting whole numbers up - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-1 Adding and subtracting whole numbers up to seven digits, multiplying numbers up to three digits, dividing numbers of up to four digits by numbers of up to two digits, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Adding and subtracting numbers up to seven digits, with and without regrouping.","A water tank can hold 1200 liters of water. If 300 liters of water are already in the tank, what percentage of the tank is filled?","25% of the tank is filled.","30%","20%","300%","25% of the tank is filled.","300 ÷ 1200 × 100 = 25%.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-store-has-5dffd544","Math G6: Adding and subtracting whole numbers up to seven digits - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-1 Adding and subtracting whole numbers up to seven digits, multiplying numbers up to three digits, dividing numbers of up to four digits by numbers of up to two digits, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Adding and subtracting numbers up to seven digits, with and without regrouping.","A store has 945,678 boxes of cereal. If they sell 378,912 boxes, how many boxes of cereal are left?","566,766 boxes","1,324,590 boxes","945,678 boxes","378,912 boxes","566,766 boxes","To find the number of boxes left, subtract the number of boxes sold from the total number of boxes. 945,678 - 378,912 = 566,766.","/image-source/math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-store-has-5dffd544.png","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-bookshelf-has-4d81e32b","Math G6: Adding and subtracting whole numbers up to seven digits - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-2 Distinguishing the factors and multiples of a number, representing and finding them, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","4. Factoring a number into its prime factors, and using prime factorization to find the greatest common divisor and the least common multiple of two  numbers or more.","A bookshelf has 456,789 books on it. If 234,567 more books are added, how many books are on the bookshelf now?","691,356 books","691,356,789 books","691,356,000 books","456,789,567 books","691,356 books","To find the total number of books, add 456,789 and 234,567. 456,789 + 234,567 = 691,356.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-library-has-f4aeec03","Math G6: Adding and subtracting whole numbers up to seven digits - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-1 Adding and subtracting whole numbers up to seven digits, multiplying numbers up to three digits, dividing numbers of up to four digits by numbers of up to two digits, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Adding and subtracting numbers up to seven digits, with and without regrouping.","A library has 123,456 books on its shelves. If 78,901 books are checked out, how many books are left on the shelves?","44,555 books","202,357 books","123,456 books","78,901 books","44,555 books","To find the number of books left, subtract the number of books checked out from the total number of books. 123,456 - 78,901 = 44,555.","/image-source/math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-library-has-f4aeec03.png","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-student-has-d182e320","Math G6: Adding and subtracting whole numbers up to seven digits - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-1 Adding and subtracting whole numbers up to seven digits, multiplying numbers up to three digits, dividing numbers of up to four digits by numbers of up to two digits, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Adding and subtracting numbers up to seven digits, with and without regrouping.","A student has 987,654 pencils in their pencil case. If they give 543,210 pencils to their friend, how many pencils are left?","444,444 pencils","987,654 pencils","543,210 pencils","444,444,000 pencils","444,444 pencils","To find the number of pencils left, subtract the number of pencils given away from the total number of pencils. 987,654 - 543,210 = 444,444.","/image-source/math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-student-has-d182e320.png","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-school-has-195f0464","Math G6: Adding and subtracting whole numbers up to seven digits - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-1 Adding and subtracting whole numbers up to seven digits, multiplying numbers up to three digits, dividing numbers of up to four digits by numbers of up to two digits, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Adding and subtracting numbers up to seven digits, with and without regrouping.","A school has 654,321 students. If 278,901 students are in the morning session, how many students are in the afternoon session?","375,420 students","654,321 students","278,901 students","932,222 students","375,420 students","To find the number of students in the afternoon session, subtract the number of students in the morning session from the total number of students. 654,321 - 278,901 = 375,420.","/image-source/math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-school-has-195f0464.png","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-bakery-sells-c87be483","Math G6: Adding and subtracting whole numbers up to seven digits - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-1 Adding and subtracting whole numbers up to seven digits, multiplying numbers up to three digits, dividing numbers of up to four digits by numbers of up to two digits, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Adding and subtracting numbers up to seven digits, with and without regrouping.","A bakery sells 456,789 loaves of bread per day. If they sell 234,567 loaves in the morning, how many loaves do they sell in the afternoon?","222,222 loaves","456,789 loaves","234,567 loaves","222,222,000 loaves","222,222 loaves","To find the number of loaves sold in the afternoon, subtract the number of loaves sold in the morning from the total number of loaves sold per day. 456,789 - 234,567 = 222,222.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-group-of-3a3472f8","Math G6: Adding and subtracting whole numbers up to seven digits - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-1 Adding and subtracting whole numbers up to seven digits, multiplying numbers up to three digits, dividing numbers of up to four digits by numbers of up to two digits, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Adding and subtracting numbers up to seven digits, with and without regrouping.","A group of friends want to share some candy equally. If they have 945 pieces of candy and there are 15 friends, how many pieces of candy will each friend get?","Each friend will get 63 pieces of candy","62 pieces","64 pieces","945 pieces","Each friend will get 63 pieces of candy","945 ÷ 15 = 63 pieces of candy.","/image-source/math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-group-of-3a3472f8.png","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-city-has-f93baf69","Math G6: Adding and subtracting whole numbers up to seven digits - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-1 Adding and subtracting whole numbers up to seven digits, multiplying numbers up to three digits, dividing numbers of up to four digits by numbers of up to two digits, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Adding and subtracting numbers up to seven digits, with and without regrouping.","A city has a population of 765,432 people. If 321,098 people move to a different city, what is the new population?","444,334 people","765,432 people","321,098 people","1,086,530 people","444,334 people","To find the new population, subtract the number of people who moved away from the original population. 765,432 - 321,098 = 444,334.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-water-bottle-893e761a","Math G6: Adding and subtracting whole numbers up to seven digits - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-1 Adding and subtracting whole numbers up to seven digits, multiplying numbers up to three digits, dividing numbers of up to four digits by numbers of up to two digits, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Adding and subtracting numbers up to seven digits, with and without regrouping.","A water bottle can hold 999,999 milliliters of water. If 555,555 milliliters of water are already in the bottle, how much more water can be added?","444,444 milliliters","999,999 milliliters","555,555 milliliters","1,555,554 milliliters","444,444 milliliters","To find out how much more water can be added, subtract the amount of water already in the bottle from the bottle's capacity. 999,999 - 555,555 = 444,444.","/image-source/math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-water-bottle-893e761a.png","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-water-tank-4a602828","Math G6: Adding and subtracting whole numbers up to seven digits - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-1 Adding and subtracting whole numbers up to seven digits, multiplying numbers up to three digits, dividing numbers of up to four digits by numbers of up to two digits, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Adding and subtracting numbers up to seven digits, with and without regrouping.","A water tank can hold 456,000 liters of water. If 187,000 liters of water are already in the tank, how much more water can be added?","269,000 liters","269,001 liters","456,000 liters","187,000 liters","269,000 liters","To find out how much more water can be added, subtract the amount of water already in the tank from the tank's capacity. 456,000 - 187,000 = 269,000.","/image-source/math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-water-tank-4a602828.png","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-type-of-graph-182bacd5","Math G6: Collecting real-word quantitative and qualitative data - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-4-1-1 Collecting real-word quantitative and qualitative data, organizing, representing, reading, and interpreting them.","2. Reading and interpreting data represented by dot plots, line graphs, bar graphs, and pie charts.","Which type of graph is best suited to show the total number of students in the school?","A bar graph to compare the number of students in each grade level","A pie chart to show the total number of students in the school","A line graph to show the trend of student enrollment over time","A scatter plot to show the relationship between student age and grade level","A pie chart to show the total number of students in the school","A pie chart is best suited to show the total number of students in the school because it allows for the visualization of the proportion of different categories.","/image-source/math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-type-of-graph-182bacd5.png","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-type-of-graph-5aa1bbe2","Math G6: Collecting real-word quantitative and qualitative data - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-4-1-1 Collecting real-word quantitative and qualitative data, organizing, representing, reading, and interpreting them.","2. Reading and interpreting data represented by dot plots, line graphs, bar graphs, and pie charts.","Which type of graph is best suited to show the number of students in each grade level?","A bar graph to compare the number of students in each grade level","A pie chart to show the total number of students in the school","A line graph to show the trend of student enrollment over time","A scatter plot to show the relationship between student age and grade level","A bar graph to compare the number of students in each grade level","A bar graph is best suited to compare the number of students in each grade level because it allows for easy comparison of categorical data.","/image-source/math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-type-of-graph-5aa1bbe2.png","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-type-of-chart-4c355059","Math G6: Collecting real-word quantitative and qualitative data - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-4-1-1 Collecting real-word quantitative and qualitative data, organizing, representing, reading, and interpreting them.","2. Reading and interpreting data represented by dot plots, line graphs, bar graphs, and pie charts.","What type of chart is used to compare the number of boys and girls in a class?","A histogram to show the distribution of student heights","A box plot to compare the average scores of different classes","A bar graph to compare the number of boys and girls in a class","A line graph to show the trend of student enrollment over time","A bar graph to compare the number of boys and girls in a class","A bar graph is used to compare the number of boys and girls in a class because it allows for easy comparison of categorical data.","/image-source/math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-type-of-chart-4c355059.png","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-type-of-graph-9e70e2b6","Math G6: Collecting real-word quantitative and qualitative data - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-4-1-1 Collecting real-word quantitative and qualitative data, organizing, representing, reading, and interpreting them.","2. Reading and interpreting data represented by dot plots, line graphs, bar graphs, and pie charts.","Which type of graph is best suited to show the average temperature in different months?","A scatter plot to show the relationship between temperature and precipitation","A line graph to show the trend of temperature over time","A bar graph to compare the average temperature in different months","A pie chart to show the total number of days above 20 degrees C","A bar graph to compare the average temperature in different months","A bar graph is best suited to compare the average temperature in different months because it allows for easy comparison of categorical data.","/image-source/math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-type-of-graph-9e70e2b6.png","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-type-of-chart-13693585","Math G6: Collecting real-word quantitative and qualitative data - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-4-1-1 Collecting real-word quantitative and qualitative data, organizing, representing, reading, and interpreting them.","2. Reading and interpreting data represented by dot plots, line graphs, bar graphs, and pie charts.","What type of chart is used to compare the average scores of different subjects?","A histogram to show the distribution of student heights","A box plot to compare the average scores of different subjects","A bar graph to compare the number of boys and girls in a class","A line graph to show the trend of student enrollment over time","A box plot to compare the average scores of different subjects","A box plot is used to compare the average scores of different subjects because it allows for the visualization of the distribution of data and the comparison of medians.","/image-source/math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-type-of-chart-13693585.png","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-type-of-chart-81646db7","Math G6: Collecting real-word quantitative and qualitative data - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-4-1-1 Collecting real-word quantitative and qualitative data, organizing, representing, reading, and interpreting them.","2. Reading and interpreting data represented by dot plots, line graphs, bar graphs, and pie charts.","What type of chart is used to show the relationship between the number of hours studied and the test score?","A histogram to show the distribution of student heights","A box plot to compare the average scores of different classes","A scatter plot to show the relationship between hours studied and test score","A line graph to show the trend of student enrollment over time","A scatter plot to show the relationship between hours studied and test score","A scatter plot is used to show the relationship between the number of hours studied and the test score because it allows for the visualization of a correlation between two variables.","/image-source/math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-type-of-chart-81646db7.png","",""
