"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-type-of-chart-797f82d2","Math G6: Collecting real-word quantitative and qualitative data - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-4-1-1 Collecting real-word quantitative and qualitative data, organizing, representing, reading, and interpreting them.","2. Reading and interpreting data represented by dot plots, line graphs, bar graphs, and pie charts.","What type of chart is used to compare the average scores of different classes?","A histogram to show the distribution of student heights","A box plot to compare the average scores of different classes","A bar graph to compare the number of boys and girls in a class","A line graph to show the trend of student enrollment over time","A box plot to compare the average scores of different classes","A box plot is used to compare the average scores of different classes because it allows for the visualization of the distribution of data and the comparison of medians.","/image-source/math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-type-of-chart-797f82d2.png","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-type-of-graph-3f3da206","Math G6: Collecting real-word quantitative and qualitative data - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-4-1-1 Collecting real-word quantitative and qualitative data, organizing, representing, reading, and interpreting them.","2. Reading and interpreting data represented by dot plots, line graphs, bar graphs, and pie charts.","Which type of graph is best suited to show the number of students who prefer different sports?","A line graph to show the trend of student enrollment over time","A bar graph to compare the number of students who prefer different sports","A scatter plot to show the relationship between student age and sport preference","A pie chart to show the total number of students in the school","A bar graph to compare the number of students who prefer different sports","A bar graph is best suited to compare the number of students who prefer different sports because it allows for easy comparison of categorical data.","/image-source/math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-type-of-graph-3f3da206.png","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-type-of-chart-c64ab52a","Math G6: Collecting real-word quantitative and qualitative data - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-4-1-1 Collecting real-word quantitative and qualitative data, organizing, representing, reading, and interpreting them.","2. Reading and interpreting data represented by dot plots, line graphs, bar graphs, and pie charts.","What type of chart is used to show the relationship between the number of books read and the student's reading level?","A histogram to show the distribution of student heights","A box plot to compare the average scores of different classes","A scatter plot to show the relationship between books read and reading level","A line graph to show the trend of student enrollment over time","A scatter plot to show the relationship between books read and reading level","A scatter plot is used to show the relationship between the number of books read and the student's reading level because it allows for the visualization of a correlation between two variables.","/image-source/math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-type-of-chart-c64ab52a.png","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-solve-x-x-635034a5","Math G6: Distinguishing and writing algebraic expressions, finding - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-2-2-1 Distinguishing and writing algebraic expressions, finding their values, solving simple linear equations, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","2. Distinguishing and writing simple one-step linear equations,  and solving them mentally and by using models.","Solve for x: x + 4 = 11","x = 5","x = 6","x = 7","x = 8","x = 7","To solve for x, we need to isolate x on one side of the equation. We can do this by subtracting 4 from both sides of the equation, which gives us x = 7.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-solve-x-x-f9aa2923","Math G6: Distinguishing and writing algebraic expressions, finding - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-2-2-1 Distinguishing and writing algebraic expressions, finding their values, solving simple linear equations, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","2. Distinguishing and writing simple one-step linear equations,  and solving them mentally and by using models.","Solve for x: x + 2 = 9","x = 7","x = 8","x = 9","x = 10","x = 7","To solve for x, we need to isolate x on one side of the equation. We can do this by subtracting 2 from both sides of the equation, which gives us x = 7.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-solve-x-2x-d69cd087","Math G6: Distinguishing and writing algebraic expressions, finding - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-2-2-1 Distinguishing and writing algebraic expressions, finding their values, solving simple linear equations, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3.Solving mathematical problems including real-life applications of algebraic expressions and simple linear equations, and explaining their solutions.","Solve for x: 2x + 5 = 11","x = 2","x = 3","x = 4","x = 6","x = 3","To solve for x, we need to isolate x on one side of the equation. We can do this by subtracting 5 from both sides of the equation, which gives us 2x = 6. Then, we can divide both sides by 2 to get x = 3.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-solve-x-2x-2d0608f9","Math G6: Distinguishing and writing algebraic expressions, finding - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-2-2-1 Distinguishing and writing algebraic expressions, finding their values, solving simple linear equations, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3.Solving mathematical problems including real-life applications of algebraic expressions and simple linear equations, and explaining their solutions.","Solve for x: 2x + 2 = 12","x = 4","x = 5","x = 6","x = 8","x = 5","To solve for x, we need to isolate x on one side of the equation. We can do this by subtracting 2 from both sides of the equation, which gives us 2x = 10. Then, we can divide both sides by 2 to get x = 5.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-solve-x-x-6e8a51f6","Math G6: Distinguishing and writing algebraic expressions, finding - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-2-2-1 Distinguishing and writing algebraic expressions, finding their values, solving simple linear equations, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","2. Distinguishing and writing simple one-step linear equations,  and solving them mentally and by using models.","Solve for x: x - 1 = 8","x = 7","x = 8","x = 9","x = 10","x = 9","To solve for x, we need to isolate x on one side of the equation. We can do this by adding 1 to both sides of the equation, which gives us x = 9.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-solve-x-x-7c4758fa","Math G6: Distinguishing and writing algebraic expressions, finding - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-2-2-1 Distinguishing and writing algebraic expressions, finding their values, solving simple linear equations, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","2. Distinguishing and writing simple one-step linear equations,  and solving them mentally and by using models.","Solve for x: x - 2 = 5","x = 5","x = 6","x = 7","x = 8","x = 7","To solve for x, we need to isolate x on one side of the equation. We can do this by adding 2 to both sides of the equation, which gives us x = 7.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-solve-x-4x-a6169512","Math G6: Distinguishing and writing algebraic expressions, finding - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-2-2-1 Distinguishing and writing algebraic expressions, finding their values, solving simple linear equations, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","2. Distinguishing and writing simple one-step linear equations,  and solving them mentally and by using models.","Solve for x: 4x = 24","x = 4","x = 5","x = 6","x = 8","x = 6","To solve for x, we need to isolate x on one side of the equation. We can do this by dividing both sides of the equation by 4, which gives us x = 6.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-solve-x-3x-06b73a9e","Math G6: Distinguishing and writing algebraic expressions, finding - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-2-2-1 Distinguishing and writing algebraic expressions, finding their values, solving simple linear equations, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","2. Distinguishing and writing simple one-step linear equations,  and solving them mentally and by using models.","Solve for x: 3x = 15","x = 3","x = 5","x = 6","x = 8","x = 5","To solve for x, we need to isolate x on one side of the equation. We can do this by dividing both sides of the equation by 3, which gives us x = 5.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-solve-x-x-3e759487","Math G6: Distinguishing and writing algebraic expressions, finding - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-2-2-1 Distinguishing and writing algebraic expressions, finding their values, solving simple linear equations, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","2. Distinguishing and writing simple one-step linear equations,  and solving them mentally and by using models.","Solve for x: x - 3 = 7","x = 4","x = 5","x = 10","x = 11","x = 10","To solve for x, we need to isolate x on one side of the equation. We can do this by adding 3 to both sides of the equation, which gives us x = 10.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-solve-x-5x-e542c8ae","Math G6: Distinguishing and writing algebraic expressions, finding - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-2-2-1 Distinguishing and writing algebraic expressions, finding their values, solving simple linear equations, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","2. Distinguishing and writing simple one-step linear equations,  and solving them mentally and by using models.","Solve for x: 5x = 25","x = 3","x = 5","x = 6","x = 8","x = 5","To solve for x, we need to isolate x on one side of the equation. We can do this by dividing both sides of the equation by 5, which gives us x = 5.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-expression-represents-phrase-2ce7ac83","Math G6: Distinguishing and writing algebraic expressions - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-3 Understanding powers of whole numbers, representing and finding them, finding values of numerical expressions included it, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Distinguishing a power of a whole number (with a whole-number exponent) as repeated multiplication and finding its value.","Which expression represents the phrase '5 more than 3 times a number'?","3 times a number minus 5","3 times a number plus 5","5 times a number plus 3","5 times a number minus 3","3 times a number plus 5","The phrase '5 more than' indicates addition, and '3 times a number' indicates multiplication. So, the correct expression is 3x + 5, where x represents the number.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-of-expressions-has-99d308ac","Math G6: Distinguishing and writing algebraic expressions - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-2-2-1 Distinguishing and writing algebraic expressions, finding their values, solving simple linear equations, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","2. Distinguishing and writing simple one-step linear equations,  and solving them mentally and by using models.","Which of the following expressions has a value of 12 when x is 4?","2x + 4","x + 8","3x - 4","x - 4","2x + 4","To find the correct expression, we need to substitute x with 4 in each option and find the one that equals 12. So, 2*4 + 4 equals 12.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-of-is-an-da8c96da","Math G6: Distinguishing and writing algebraic expressions - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-2-2-1 Distinguishing and writing algebraic expressions, finding their values, solving simple linear equations, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Distinguishing algebraic expressions, writing them by using parentheses, and finding their values by applying the order of operations.","Which of the following is an example of an algebraic expression?","A number sentence with an equal sign","A mathematical phrase with variables and constants","A diagram with shapes and colors","A chart with numbers and pictures","A mathematical phrase with variables and constants","An algebraic expression is a mathematical phrase that contains variables, constants, and mathematical operations. It does not have an equal sign.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-expression-represents-phrase-a4eac041","Math G6: Distinguishing and writing algebraic expressions - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-3 Understanding powers of whole numbers, representing and finding them, finding values of numerical expressions included it, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Distinguishing a power of a whole number (with a whole-number exponent) as repeated multiplication and finding its value.","Which expression represents the phrase '2 less than 5 times a number'?","5 times a number minus 2","5 times a number plus 2","2 times a number minus 5","2 times a number plus 5","5 times a number minus 2","The phrase '2 less than' indicates subtraction, and '5 times a number' indicates multiplication. So, the correct expression is 5x - 2, where x represents the number.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-value-of-5e7df17e","Math G6: Distinguishing and writing algebraic expressions - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-3 Understanding powers of whole numbers, representing and finding them, finding values of numerical expressions included it, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Distinguishing a power of a whole number (with a whole-number exponent) as repeated multiplication and finding its value.","What is the value of the expression 2(x + 3) when x is 2?","10","7","8","12","10","Substitute x = 2: 2(2 + 3) = 2 x 5 = 10.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-value-of-8d47f235","Math G6: Distinguishing and writing algebraic expressions - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-2-2-1 Distinguishing and writing algebraic expressions, finding their values, solving simple linear equations, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Distinguishing algebraic expressions, writing them by using parentheses, and finding their values by applying the order of operations.","What is the value of the expression 2x + 5 when x is 3?","2 times 3 plus 5","11","2 times 5 plus 3","5 times 3 plus 2","11","To find the value of the expression, we need to substitute x with 3 and follow the order of operations. So, 2x + 5 becomes 2*3 + 5, which equals 6 + 5, and that equals 11.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-value-of-de4d2ae8","Math G6: Distinguishing and writing algebraic expressions - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-3 Understanding powers of whole numbers, representing and finding them, finding values of numerical expressions included it, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Distinguishing a power of a whole number (with a whole-number exponent) as repeated multiplication and finding its value.","What is the value of the expression x - 2 when x is 7?","7 minus 2","5","2 minus 7","7 plus 2","5","To find the value of the expression, we need to substitute x with 7. So, x - 2 becomes 7 - 2, which equals 5.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-of-is-an-243ce837","Math G6: Distinguishing and writing algebraic expressions - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-2-2-1 Distinguishing and writing algebraic expressions, finding their values, solving simple linear equations, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Distinguishing algebraic expressions, writing them by using parentheses, and finding their values by applying the order of operations.","Which of the following is an example of an algebraic expression with parentheses?","2x + 5","x - 3","2(x + 3)","x/2","2(x + 3)","An algebraic expression with parentheses is a mathematical phrase that contains variables, constants, and mathematical operations, and also has parentheses to group parts of the expression. The expression 2(x + 3) is an example of this.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-value-of-94dc24cd","Math G6: Distinguishing and writing algebraic expressions - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-3 Understanding powers of whole numbers, representing and finding them, finding values of numerical expressions included it, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Distinguishing a power of a whole number (with a whole-number exponent) as repeated multiplication and finding its value.","What is the value of the expression x/2 + 3 when x is 10?","8","5","2","15","8","Substitute x = 10: 10/2 + 3 = 5 + 3 = 8.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-value-of-f70b377d","Math G6: Distinguishing and writing algebraic expressions - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-2-2-1 Distinguishing and writing algebraic expressions, finding their values, solving simple linear equations, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Distinguishing algebraic expressions, writing them by using parentheses, and finding their values by applying the order of operations.","What is the value of the expression 4x - 2 when x is 2?","4 times 2 minus 2","6","4 times 2 plus 2","2 times 4 minus 2","6","To find the value of the expression, we need to substitute x with 2 and follow the order of operations. So, 4x - 2 becomes 4*2 - 2, which equals 8 - 2, and that equals 6.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-value-of-6d2ee680","Math G6: Distinguishing and writing algebraic expressions - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-2-2-1 Distinguishing and writing algebraic expressions, finding their values, solving simple linear equations, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3.Solving mathematical problems including real-life applications of algebraic expressions and simple linear equations, and explaining their solutions.","What is the value of x in the equation x = 15 - 3?","x = 10","x = 11","x = 12","x = 18","x = 12","To find the value of x, we need to evaluate the expression 15 - 3, so x = 12.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-equation-represents-8f82b3a3","Math G6: Distinguishing and writing algebraic expressions - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-2 Distinguishing the factors and multiples of a number, representing and finding them, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","4. Factoring a number into its prime factors, and using prime factorization to find the greatest common divisor and the least common multiple of two  numbers or more.","What is the equation that represents the statement '5 more than a number is 11'?","x + 5 = 11","x - 5 = 11","5x = 11","x = 5 + 11","x + 5 = 11","The statement '5 more than a number is 11' can be translated into the equation x + 5 = 11, where x is the unknown number.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-value-of-ae77361f","Math G6: Distinguishing and writing algebraic expressions - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-2-2-1 Distinguishing and writing algebraic expressions, finding their values, solving simple linear equations, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3.Solving mathematical problems including real-life applications of algebraic expressions and simple linear equations, and explaining their solutions.","What is the value of x in the equation 2x = 6?","x = 2","x = 3","x = 4","x = 5","x = 3","To find the value of x, we need to divide both sides of the equation by 2, so x = 6 / 2 = 3.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-equation-represents-0276a5a0","Math G6: Distinguishing and writing algebraic expressions - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-2-2-1 Distinguishing and writing algebraic expressions, finding their values, solving simple linear equations, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3.Solving mathematical problems including real-life applications of algebraic expressions and simple linear equations, and explaining their solutions.","What is the equation that represents the statement 'a number minus 2 is 5'?","x - 2 = 5","x + 2 = 5","2x = 5","x = 5 - 2","x - 2 = 5","The statement 'a number minus 2 is 5' can be translated into the equation x - 2 = 5, where x is the unknown number.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-value-of-9e26391e","Math G6: Distinguishing and writing algebraic expressions - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-2-2-1 Distinguishing and writing algebraic expressions, finding their values, solving simple linear equations, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3.Solving mathematical problems including real-life applications of algebraic expressions and simple linear equations, and explaining their solutions.","What is the value of x in the equation x = 24 / 4?","x = 4","x = 5","x = 6","x = 8","x = 6","To find the value of x, we need to evaluate the expression 24 / 4, so x = 6.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-solve-t-in-60ef2e8c","Math G6: Distinguishing and writing algebraic expressions - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-2-2-1 Distinguishing and writing algebraic expressions, finding their values, solving simple linear equations, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3.Solving mathematical problems including real-life applications of algebraic expressions and simple linear equations, and explaining their solutions.","Solve for t in the equation t + 4 = 11","t = 5","t = 6","t = 7","t = 9","t = 7","To solve for t, we need to subtract 4 from both sides of the equation, so t = 11 - 4 = 7.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-solve-y-in-e05417d9","Math G6: Distinguishing and writing algebraic expressions - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-2-2-1 Distinguishing and writing algebraic expressions, finding their values, solving simple linear equations, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3.Solving mathematical problems including real-life applications of algebraic expressions and simple linear equations, and explaining their solutions.","Solve for y in the equation 3y = 18","y = 5","y = 6","y = 7","y = 9","y = 6","To solve for y, we need to divide both sides of the equation by 3, so y = 18 / 3 = 6.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-solve-w-in-1afdf6e4","Math G6: Distinguishing and writing algebraic expressions - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-2-2-1 Distinguishing and writing algebraic expressions, finding their values, solving simple linear equations, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3.Solving mathematical problems including real-life applications of algebraic expressions and simple linear equations, and explaining their solutions.","Solve for w in the equation 4w = 20","w = 4","w = 5","w = 6","w = 8","w = 5","To solve for w, we need to divide both sides of the equation by 4, so w = 20 / 4 = 5.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-solve-y-in-ef27c210","Math G6: Distinguishing and writing algebraic expressions - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-2-2-1 Distinguishing and writing algebraic expressions, finding their values, solving simple linear equations, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3.Solving mathematical problems including real-life applications of algebraic expressions and simple linear equations, and explaining their solutions.","Solve for y in the equation y + 2 = 7","y = 5","y = 6","y = 7","y = 9","y = 5","To solve for y, we need to subtract 2 from both sides of the equation, so y = 7 - 2 = 5.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-solve-z-in-18e823d8","Math G6: Distinguishing and writing algebraic expressions - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-2-2-1 Distinguishing and writing algebraic expressions, finding their values, solving simple linear equations, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3.Solving mathematical problems including real-life applications of algebraic expressions and simple linear equations, and explaining their solutions.","Solve for z in the equation z - 1 = 9","z = 8","z = 9","z = 10","z = 11","z = 10","To solve for z, we need to add 1 to both sides of the equation, so z = 9 + 1 = 10.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-library-has-de554613","Math G6: Distinguishing and writing algebraic expressions - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-2-2-1 Distinguishing and writing algebraic expressions, finding their values, solving simple linear equations, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3.Solving mathematical problems including real-life applications of algebraic expressions and simple linear equations, and explaining their solutions.","A library has 15 shelves, and each shelf can hold 20 books. If the library is currently empty, how many books can be placed on the shelves in total?","The library can hold 250 books in total","The library can hold 300 books in total","The library can hold 200 books in total","The library can hold 400 books in total","The library can hold 300 books in total","To find the total number of books the library can hold, we multiply the number of shelves by the number of books each shelf can hold, which is 15 * 20 = 300 books.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-bakery-is-5035ef39","Math G6: Distinguishing and writing algebraic expressions - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-2-2-1 Distinguishing and writing algebraic expressions, finding their values, solving simple linear equations, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3.Solving mathematical problems including real-life applications of algebraic expressions and simple linear equations, and explaining their solutions.","A bakery is having a sale on bread. A loaf of bread normally costs $2.50, but it's on sale for 15% off. How much will you pay for a loaf of bread during the sale?","You will pay $2.13 for a loaf of bread during the sale","You will pay $2.25 for a loaf of bread during the sale","You will pay $2.38 for a loaf of bread during the sale","You will pay $2.50 for a loaf of bread during the sale","You will pay $2.13 for a loaf of bread during the sale","To find the sale price, we need to calculate 15% of the original price, which is $2.50 * 0.15 = $0.38. Then, we subtract the discount from the original price, which is $2.50 - $0.38 = $2.12.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-ashley-is-planning-ed6079d1","Math G6: Distinguishing and writing algebraic expressions - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-2-2-1 Distinguishing and writing algebraic expressions, finding their values, solving simple linear equations, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3.Solving mathematical problems including real-life applications of algebraic expressions and simple linear equations, and explaining their solutions.","Ashley is planning a road trip that is 240 miles long. If her car gets 30 miles per gallon, how many gallons of gas will she need for the trip?","Ashley will need 8 gallons of gas for the trip","Ashley will need 10 gallons of gas for the trip","Ashley will need 12 gallons of gas for the trip","Ashley will need 6 gallons of gas for the trip","Ashley will need 8 gallons of gas for the trip","To find out how many gallons of gas Ashley will need, we divide the total distance of the trip by her car's miles per gallon, which is 240 / 30 = 8 gallons.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-group-of-bfc72c95","Math G6: Distinguishing and writing algebraic expressions - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-2-2-1 Distinguishing and writing algebraic expressions, finding their values, solving simple linear equations, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3.Solving mathematical problems including real-life applications of algebraic expressions and simple linear equations, and explaining their solutions.","A group of friends want to share some candy equally. If they have 48 pieces of candy and there are 8 friends, how many pieces of candy will each friend get?","Each friend will get 6 pieces of candy","5 pieces","7 pieces","48 pieces","Each friend will get 6 pieces of candy","48 ÷ 8 = 6 pieces of candy.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-water-tank-dfc8aabe","Math G6: Distinguishing and writing algebraic expressions - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-2-2-1 Distinguishing and writing algebraic expressions, finding their values, solving simple linear equations, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3.Solving mathematical problems including real-life applications of algebraic expressions and simple linear equations, and explaining their solutions.","A water tank can hold 1200 gallons of water. If 300 gallons of water are already in the tank, what percentage of the tank is filled?","The tank is 25% filled","The tank is 30% filled","The tank is 20% filled","The tank is 40% filled","The tank is 25% filled","To find the percentage of the tank that is filled, we divide the amount of water in the tank by the total capacity and multiply by 100, which is (300 / 1200) * 100 = 25%.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-bike-is-0ed10c06","Math G6: Distinguishing and writing algebraic expressions - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-2-2-1 Distinguishing and writing algebraic expressions, finding their values, solving simple linear equations, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3.Solving mathematical problems including real-life applications of algebraic expressions and simple linear equations, and explaining their solutions.","A bike is on sale for 20% off its original price of $80. How much will you pay for the bike during the sale?","You will pay $64 for the bike during the sale","You will pay $70 for the bike during the sale","You will pay $65 for the bike during the sale","You will pay $75 for the bike during the sale","You will pay $64 for the bike during the sale","To find the sale price, we need to calculate 20% of the original price, which is $80 * 0.20 = $16. Then, we subtract the discount from the original price, which is $80 - $16 = $64.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-tom-has-been-32cf61be","Math G6: Distinguishing and writing algebraic expressions - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-2-2-1 Distinguishing and writing algebraic expressions, finding their values, solving simple linear equations, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3.Solving mathematical problems including real-life applications of algebraic expressions and simple linear equations, and explaining their solutions.","Tom has been saving money for a new bike and has $120 in his savings account. He wants to buy a bike that costs $180. If Tom's parents give him an allowance of $5 per week, how many weeks will it take him to have enough money to buy the bike?","Tom will need 12 weeks to save enough money","Tom will need 10 weeks to save enough money","Tom will need 8 weeks to save enough money","Tom will need 6 weeks to save enough money","Tom will need 12 weeks to save enough money","To find out how many weeks it will take Tom to save enough money, we need to subtract the amount he already has from the price of the bike, which is $180 - $120 = $60. Then, we divide the amount he needs by his weekly allowance, which is $60 / $5 = 12 weeks.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-bookshelf-has-7e0cf096","Math G6: Distinguishing and writing algebraic expressions - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-2-2-1 Distinguishing and writing algebraic expressions, finding their values, solving simple linear equations, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3.Solving mathematical problems including real-life applications of algebraic expressions and simple linear equations, and explaining their solutions.","A bookshelf has 5 shelves, and each shelf can hold 8 books. If the bookshelf is currently empty, how many books can be placed on it in total?","40 books","30 books","45 books","50 books","40 books","Multiply shelves by books per shelf: 5 x 8 = 40 books.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-pencil-is-30cd3339","Math G6: Distinguishing and writing algebraic expressions - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-7 Estimating the results of the four arithmetic operations on whole numbers, Proper fractions, and decimal fractions, and using mental calculation strategies to find results, and verifying the plausibility and accuracy of the results.","4. Using mental calculation to find the results of multiplying and dividing decimal fractions by multiples of (10, 100, 1000).","A pencil is 15 cm long. If it is divided into 5 parts, how long is each part?","Each part is 3 cm long","Each part is 4 cm long","Each part is 5 cm long","Each part is 2 cm long","Each part is 3 cm long","To find the length of each part, we divide the total length of the pencil by the number of parts, which is 15 / 5 = 3 cm.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-bakery-sells-b2c4bb98","Math G6: Distinguishing and writing algebraic expressions - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-2-2-1 Distinguishing and writing algebraic expressions, finding their values, solving simple linear equations, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3.Solving mathematical problems including real-life applications of algebraic expressions and simple linear equations, and explaining their solutions.","A bakery sells 250 loaves of bread per day. If they make a profit of $0.50 per loaf, how much profit will they make in a day?","The bakery will make $125 in profit per day","The bakery will make $100 in profit per day","The bakery will make $150 in profit per day","The bakery will make $200 in profit per day","The bakery will make $125 in profit per day","To find the total profit, we multiply the number of loaves sold by the profit per loaf, which is 250 * $0.50 = $125.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-type-of-line-53877bb7","Math G6: Distinguishing basic geometric concepts, identifying types - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-1-1 Distinguishing basic geometric concepts, identifying types of angles, relationships between lines, and relationships between angles.","1. Distinguishing points, lines, rays, and line segments, and identifying them on geometric figures.","What type of line is always the same distance apart from another line?","A line that touches another line at two points","A line that touches another line at one point","A line that is always the same distance apart from another line","A line that never touches another line","A line that is always the same distance apart from another line","A parallel line is always the same distance apart from another line.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-type-of-line-18b7de1a","Math G6: Distinguishing basic geometric concepts, identifying types - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-1-1 Distinguishing basic geometric concepts, identifying types of angles, relationships between lines, and relationships between angles.","1. Distinguishing points, lines, rays, and line segments, and identifying them on geometric figures.","What type of line intersects with another line at a single point?","A line that never touches another line","A line that touches another line at two points","A line that touches another line at one point","A line that is always the same distance apart from another line","A line that touches another line at one point","An intersecting line touches another line at a single point.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-type-of-angle-b3d4954c","Math G6: Distinguishing basic geometric concepts, identifying types - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-1-1 Distinguishing basic geometric concepts, identifying types of angles, relationships between lines, and relationships between angles.","2. Distinguishing angles (right, acute, obtuse, and straight), and estimating and finding their measures.","Which type of angle is less than 90 degrees?","A right angle","An acute angle","An obtuse angle","A straight angle","An acute angle","An acute angle is less than 90 degrees.","","",""
