"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-term-an-2612823e","Math G6: Distinguishing basic geometric concepts - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-1-1 Distinguishing basic geometric concepts, identifying types of angles, relationships between lines, and relationships between angles.","2. Distinguishing angles (right, acute, obtuse, and straight), and estimating and finding their measures.","What is the term for an angle that measures exactly 180 degrees?","Right angle","Acute angle","Obtuse angle","Straight angle","Straight angle","A straight angle measures exactly 180 degrees.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-type-of-angle-42f663e5","Math G6: Distinguishing basic geometric concepts - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-1-1 Distinguishing basic geometric concepts, identifying types of angles, relationships between lines, and relationships between angles.","2. Distinguishing angles (right, acute, obtuse, and straight), and estimating and finding their measures.","What type of angle is formed when two lines intersect to create a 120-degree angle?","Acute angle","Right angle","Obtuse angle","Straight angle","Obtuse angle","An obtuse angle is formed when two lines intersect to create an angle greater than 90 degrees, such as 120 degrees.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-type-of-angle-5893e5ca","Math G6: Distinguishing basic geometric concepts - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-1-1 Distinguishing basic geometric concepts, identifying types of angles, relationships between lines, and relationships between angles.","2. Distinguishing angles (right, acute, obtuse, and straight), and estimating and finding their measures.","What type of angle is formed when two lines intersect to create an angle less than 90 degrees?","Right angle","Obtuse angle","Straight angle","Acute angle","Acute angle","An acute angle is formed when two lines intersect to create an angle less than 90 degrees.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-of-angles-is-7d881e4d","Math G6: Distinguishing basic geometric concepts - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-1-1 Distinguishing basic geometric concepts, identifying types of angles, relationships between lines, and relationships between angles.","2. Distinguishing angles (right, acute, obtuse, and straight), and estimating and finding their measures.","Which of the following angles is greater than 90 degrees but less than 180 degrees?","Acute angle","Right angle","Obtuse angle","Straight angle","Obtuse angle","An obtuse angle is greater than 90 degrees but less than 180 degrees.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-term-point-4f626919","Math G6: Distinguishing basic geometric concepts - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-1-1 Distinguishing basic geometric concepts, identifying types of angles, relationships between lines, and relationships between angles.","1. Distinguishing points, lines, rays, and line segments, and identifying them on geometric figures.","What is the term for the point where two lines intersect?","Vertex","Intersection point","Endpoint","Midpoint","Intersection point","The intersection point is the point where two lines cross each other.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-type-of-angle-1d965786","Math G6: Distinguishing basic geometric concepts - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-1-1 Distinguishing basic geometric concepts, identifying types of angles, relationships between lines, and relationships between angles.","2. Distinguishing angles (right, acute, obtuse, and straight), and estimating and finding their measures.","What type of angle is formed when two perpendicular lines intersect?","Acute angle","Right angle","Obtuse angle","Straight angle","Right angle","A right angle is formed when two perpendicular lines intersect, and it measures 90 degrees.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-relationship-two-dba59b29","Math G6: Distinguishing basic geometric concepts - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-1-1 Distinguishing basic geometric concepts, identifying types of angles, relationships between lines, and relationships between angles.","3. Distinguishing intersecting, parallel, and perpendicular lines, and identifying them on geometric figures.","What is the relationship between two lines that form a 90-degree angle?","The lines are parallel","The lines are intersecting","The lines are perpendicular","The lines are skewed","The lines are perpendicular","When two lines form a 90-degree angle, they are called perpendicular lines.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-of-is-an-d17cfdc2","Math G6: Distinguishing basic geometric concepts - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-1-1 Distinguishing basic geometric concepts, identifying types of angles, relationships between lines, and relationships between angles.","3. Distinguishing intersecting, parallel, and perpendicular lines, and identifying them on geometric figures.","Which of the following is an example of parallel lines?","Two lines that cross each other","Two lines that never touch","Two lines that are at an angle","Two lines that are curved","Two lines that never touch","Parallel lines are lines that never touch or intersect, no matter how far they are extended.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-type-of-lines-1ce81ccb","Math G6: Distinguishing basic geometric concepts - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-1-1 Distinguishing basic geometric concepts, identifying types of angles, relationships between lines, and relationships between angles.","3. Distinguishing intersecting, parallel, and perpendicular lines, and identifying them on geometric figures.","What type of lines are formed when two lines cross each other?","Intersecting lines","Parallel lines","Perpendicular lines","Skewed lines","Intersecting lines","When two lines cross each other, they are called intersecting lines.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-of-is-an-e75600a8","Math G6: Distinguishing basic geometric concepts - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-1-1 Distinguishing basic geometric concepts, identifying types of angles, relationships between lines, and relationships between angles.","3. Distinguishing intersecting, parallel, and perpendicular lines, and identifying them on geometric figures.","Which of the following is an example of perpendicular lines?","The sides of a triangle","The edges of a circle","The lines of a grid","The lines of a graph","The lines of a grid","The lines of a grid are perpendicular to each other, forming right angles where they intersect.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-type-of-lines-61712b9f","Math G6: Distinguishing basic geometric concepts - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-1-1 Distinguishing basic geometric concepts, identifying types of angles, relationships between lines, and relationships between angles.","3. Distinguishing intersecting, parallel, and perpendicular lines, and identifying them on geometric figures.","What type of lines are formed when two lines are at a 90-degree angle to each other?","Intersecting lines","Parallel lines","Perpendicular lines","Skewed lines","Perpendicular lines","When two lines are at a 90-degree angle to each other, they are called perpendicular lines.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-term-lines-d5465d97","Math G6: Distinguishing basic geometric concepts - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-1-1 Distinguishing basic geometric concepts, identifying types of angles, relationships between lines, and relationships between angles.","3. Distinguishing intersecting, parallel, and perpendicular lines, and identifying them on geometric figures.","What is the term for lines that never touch or intersect?","Intersecting lines","Parallel lines","Perpendicular lines","Skewed lines","Parallel lines","Parallel lines are lines that never touch or intersect, no matter how far they are extended.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-of-is-not-259f94a2","Math G6: Distinguishing basic geometric concepts - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-1-1 Distinguishing basic geometric concepts, identifying types of angles, relationships between lines, and relationships between angles.","3. Distinguishing intersecting, parallel, and perpendicular lines, and identifying them on geometric figures.","Which of the following is NOT an example of intersecting lines?","Two lines that cross each other","Two lines that are at an angle","Two lines that never touch","Two lines that are curved","Two lines that never touch","Intersecting lines are lines that cross each other, so lines that never touch are not intersecting lines.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-of-is-a-dc339c3a","Math G6: Distinguishing basic geometric concepts - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-1-1 Distinguishing basic geometric concepts, identifying types of angles, relationships between lines, and relationships between angles.","3. Distinguishing intersecting, parallel, and perpendicular lines, and identifying them on geometric figures.","Which of the following is a characteristic of parallel lines?","They intersect at a point","They are at an angle to each other","They never touch or intersect","They are curved","They never touch or intersect","Parallel lines are lines that never touch or intersect, no matter how far they are extended.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-if-measure-of-6d3edab4","Math G6: Distinguishing basic geometric concepts - Set 4","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-1-1 Distinguishing basic geometric concepts, identifying types of angles, relationships between lines, and relationships between angles.","4. Distinguishing vertically opposite, adjacent, complementary, and supplementary angles, identifying them on geometric figures, and using them to find the measures of unknown angles.","If the measure of one angle is 60 degrees and it is complementary to another angle, what is the measure of the other angle?","30 degrees","60 degrees","90 degrees","120 degrees","30 degrees","Complementary angles add up to 90 degrees, so if one angle is 60 degrees, the other angle must be 30 degrees.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-if-two-angles-51db8aab","Math G6: Distinguishing basic geometric concepts - Set 4","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-1-1 Distinguishing basic geometric concepts, identifying types of angles, relationships between lines, and relationships between angles.","4. Distinguishing vertically opposite, adjacent, complementary, and supplementary angles, identifying them on geometric figures, and using them to find the measures of unknown angles.","If two angles are adjacent and one angle measures 45 degrees, what is the measure of the other angle if they are also complementary?","45 degrees","90 degrees","135 degrees","225 degrees","45 degrees","Complementary angles add up to 90 degrees, and since one angle is 45 degrees, the other angle must also be 45 degrees to add up to 90 degrees.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-type-of-angle-37f5ac9a","Math G6: Distinguishing basic geometric concepts - Set 4","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-1-1 Distinguishing basic geometric concepts, identifying types of angles, relationships between lines, and relationships between angles.","4. Distinguishing vertically opposite, adjacent, complementary, and supplementary angles, identifying them on geometric figures, and using them to find the measures of unknown angles.","What type of angle is formed when two lines intersect and the angles are directly across from each other?","Adjacent angles","Complementary angles","Supplementary angles","Vertically opposite angles","Vertically opposite angles","Vertically opposite angles are formed when two lines intersect and the angles are directly across from each other.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-relationship-two-f8f8cd97","Math G6: Distinguishing basic geometric concepts - Set 4","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-1-1 Distinguishing basic geometric concepts, identifying types of angles, relationships between lines, and relationships between angles.","4. Distinguishing vertically opposite, adjacent, complementary, and supplementary angles, identifying them on geometric figures, and using them to find the measures of unknown angles.","What is the relationship between two angles that add up to 180 degrees?","Complementary angles","Supplementary angles","Adjacent angles","Vertically opposite angles","Supplementary angles","Supplementary angles are two angles whose measures add up to 180 degrees.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-type-of-angle-f7a454cd","Math G6: Distinguishing basic geometric concepts - Set 4","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-1-1 Distinguishing basic geometric concepts, identifying types of angles, relationships between lines, and relationships between angles.","4. Distinguishing vertically opposite, adjacent, complementary, and supplementary angles, identifying them on geometric figures, and using them to find the measures of unknown angles.","What type of angle is formed when two lines intersect and the angles share a common vertex and side?","Adjacent angles","Complementary angles","Supplementary angles","Corresponding angles","Adjacent angles","Adjacent angles are formed when two lines intersect and the angles share a common vertex and side.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-if-two-angles-3d9179d8","Math G6: Distinguishing basic geometric concepts - Set 4","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-1-1 Distinguishing basic geometric concepts, identifying types of angles, relationships between lines, and relationships between angles.","4. Distinguishing vertically opposite, adjacent, complementary, and supplementary angles, identifying them on geometric figures, and using them to find the measures of unknown angles.","If two angles are supplementary and one angle measures 120 degrees, what is the measure of the other angle?","60 degrees","90 degrees","120 degrees","150 degrees","60 degrees","Supplementary angles add up to 180 degrees, so if one angle is 120 degrees, the other angle must be 60 degrees.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-measure-of-49ff47d1","Math G6: Distinguishing basic geometric concepts - Set 4","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-1-1 Distinguishing basic geometric concepts, identifying types of angles, relationships between lines, and relationships between angles.","4. Distinguishing vertically opposite, adjacent, complementary, and supplementary angles, identifying them on geometric figures, and using them to find the measures of unknown angles.","What is the measure of the other angle if one angle is 30 degrees and they are complementary?","30 degrees","60 degrees","90 degrees","120 degrees","60 degrees","Complementary angles add up to 90 degrees, so if one angle is 30 degrees, the other angle must be 60 degrees.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-if-two-angles-b4b189eb","Math G6: Distinguishing basic geometric concepts - Set 4","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-1-1 Distinguishing basic geometric concepts, identifying types of angles, relationships between lines, and relationships between angles.","4. Distinguishing vertically opposite, adjacent, complementary, and supplementary angles, identifying them on geometric figures, and using them to find the measures of unknown angles.","If two angles are vertically opposite and one angle measures 75 degrees, what is the measure of the other angle?","75 degrees","90 degrees","105 degrees","120 degrees","75 degrees","Vertically opposite angles are equal in measure, so if one angle is 75 degrees, the other angle must also be 75 degrees.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-type-of-angle-1f7792ab","Math G6: Distinguishing basic geometric concepts - Set 4","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-1-1 Distinguishing basic geometric concepts, identifying types of angles, relationships between lines, and relationships between angles.","4. Distinguishing vertically opposite, adjacent, complementary, and supplementary angles, identifying them on geometric figures, and using them to find the measures of unknown angles.","What type of angle is formed when two lines intersect and the angles do not share a common side or vertex?","Adjacent angles","Complementary angles","Supplementary angles","Vertically opposite angles","Vertically opposite angles","Vertically opposite angles are formed when two lines intersect and the angles do not share a common side or vertex.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-if-two-angles-1d403dcc","Math G6: Distinguishing basic geometric concepts - Set 4","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-1-1 Distinguishing basic geometric concepts, identifying types of angles, relationships between lines, and relationships between angles.","4. Distinguishing vertically opposite, adjacent, complementary, and supplementary angles, identifying them on geometric figures, and using them to find the measures of unknown angles.","If two angles are supplementary and one angle measures 90 degrees, what is the measure of the other angle?","0 degrees","45 degrees","90 degrees","180 degrees","90 degrees","Supplementary angles add up to 180 degrees, so if one angle is 90 degrees, the other angle must also be 90 degrees.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-formula-perimeter-7fd9109f","Math G6: Distinguishing formulas for the perimeter and area of two-di - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-3-2 Distinguishing formulas for the perimeter and area of two-dimensional shapes, using them to find perimeter and area, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Distinguishing formulas for the perimeters of the rectangle, square, and circle, and using them to find the perimeter.","What is the formula for the perimeter of a circle?","Perimeter equals the diameter","Perimeter equals the radius times 2","Perimeter equals pi times the diameter","Perimeter equals the sum of all the sides","Perimeter equals pi times the diameter","The formula for the perimeter (or circumference) of a circle is C = ?d, where d is the diameter.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-perimeter-of-462d6c67","Math G6: Distinguishing formulas for the perimeter and area of two-di - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-3-2 Distinguishing formulas for the perimeter and area of two-dimensional shapes, using them to find perimeter and area, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Distinguishing formulas for the perimeters of the rectangle, square, and circle, and using them to find the perimeter.","What is the perimeter of a rectangle with a length of 8 cm and a width of 5 cm?","18 cm","20 cm","22 cm","26 cm","26 cm","Using the formula P = 2l + 2w, we get P = 2 * 8 + 2 * 5 = 16 + 10 = 26 cm.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-circular-garden-e68e6db7","Math G6: Distinguishing formulas for the perimeter and area of two-di - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-3-2 Distinguishing formulas for the perimeter and area of two-dimensional shapes, using them to find perimeter and area, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Distinguishing formulas for the perimeters of the rectangle, square, and circle, and using them to find the perimeter.","A circular garden has a diameter of 14 meters. What is its perimeter?","14 meters","28 meters","43.96 meters","56 meters","43.96 meters","Using the formula C = ?d, we get C = 3.14 * 14 = 43.96 meters.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-perimeter-of-79073c24","Math G6: Distinguishing formulas for the perimeter and area of two-di - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-3-1 Distinguishing relationships between units of length, mass, capacity, and time, and using them to convert between units.","1. Identifying the appropriate unit of length, distinguishing relationships between metric units of length (cm, mm), (m, cm), (km, m), and using them to convert between units.","What is the perimeter of a square with sides of 5 cm?","10 cm","15 cm","20 cm","25 cm","20 cm","Since all sides of a square are equal, the perimeter is 4 times the side length, so 4 * 5 = 20 cm.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-formula-perimeter-05375ae2","Math G6: Distinguishing formulas for the perimeter and area of two-di - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-3-2 Distinguishing formulas for the perimeter and area of two-dimensional shapes, using them to find perimeter and area, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Distinguishing formulas for the perimeters of the rectangle, square, and circle, and using them to find the perimeter.","What is the formula for the perimeter of a rectangle?","Perimeter equals the sum of all the sides","Perimeter equals length times width","Perimeter equals 2 times the length plus 2 times the width","Perimeter equals length plus width","Perimeter equals 2 times the length plus 2 times the width","The formula for the perimeter of a rectangle is P = 2l + 2w, where l is the length and w is the width.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-square-picture-2f4898ce","Math G6: Distinguishing formulas for the perimeter and area of two-di - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-3-2 Distinguishing formulas for the perimeter and area of two-dimensional shapes, using them to find perimeter and area, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Distinguishing formulas for the perimeters of the rectangle, square, and circle, and using them to find the perimeter.","A square picture frame has a side length of 12 inches. What is its perimeter?","24 inches","36 inches","40 inches","48 inches","48 inches","Since all sides of a square are equal, the perimeter is 4 times the side length, so 4 * 12 = 48 inches.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-circular-pond-5bcf20e9","Math G6: Distinguishing formulas for the perimeter and area of two-di - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-3-2 Distinguishing formulas for the perimeter and area of two-dimensional shapes, using them to find perimeter and area, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Distinguishing formulas for the perimeters of the rectangle, square, and circle, and using them to find the perimeter.","A circular pond has a radius of 7 meters. What is its perimeter?","14 meters","21.98 meters","28 meters","43.96 meters","43.96 meters","First, find the diameter by multiplying the radius by 2: d = 2 * 7 = 14 meters. Then, using the formula C = ?d, we get C = 3.14 * 14 = 43.96 meters.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-perimeter-of-268eaa31","Math G6: Distinguishing formulas for the perimeter and area of two-di - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-3-2 Distinguishing formulas for the perimeter and area of two-dimensional shapes, using them to find perimeter and area, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Distinguishing formulas for the perimeters of the rectangle, square, and circle, and using them to find the perimeter.","What is the perimeter of a rectangle with a length of 15 cm and a width of 8 cm?","30 cm","34 cm","38 cm","46 cm","46 cm","Using the formula P = 2l + 2w, we get P = 2 * 15 + 2 * 8 = 30 + 16 = 46 cm.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-square-book-97852136","Math G6: Distinguishing formulas for the perimeter and area of two-di - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-3-2 Distinguishing formulas for the perimeter and area of two-dimensional shapes, using them to find perimeter and area, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Distinguishing formulas for the perimeters of the rectangle, square, and circle, and using them to find the perimeter.","A square book has a side length of 9 inches. What is its perimeter?","18 inches","27 inches","36 inches","45 inches","36 inches","Since all sides of a square are equal, the perimeter is 4 times the side length, so 4 * 9 = 36 inches.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-circular-clock-45a7365f","Math G6: Distinguishing formulas for the perimeter and area of two-di - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-3-2 Distinguishing formulas for the perimeter and area of two-dimensional shapes, using them to find perimeter and area, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Distinguishing formulas for the perimeters of the rectangle, square, and circle, and using them to find the perimeter.","A circular clock has a diameter of 20 cm. What is its perimeter?","20 cm","31.4 cm","40 cm","62.8 cm","62.8 cm","Using the formula C = ?d, we get C = 3.14 * 20 = 62.8 cm.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-area-of-1947c2dc","Math G6: Distinguishing formulas for the perimeter and area of two-di - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-3-2 Distinguishing formulas for the perimeter and area of two-dimensional shapes, using them to find perimeter and area, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Distinguishing formulas for the perimeters of the rectangle, square, and circle, and using them to find the perimeter.","What is the area of a rectangle with a length of 8 cm and a width of 5 cm?","30 cm^2","40 cm^2","50 cm^2","60 cm^2","40 cm^2","The area of a rectangle is calculated by multiplying the length and width. 8 cm x 5 cm = 40 cm^2.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-area-of-4dc2f96b","Math G6: Distinguishing formulas for the perimeter and area of two-di - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-3-2 Distinguishing formulas for the perimeter and area of two-dimensional shapes, using them to find perimeter and area, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","2. Distinguishing formulas for the areas of the rectangle, square, parallelogram, and triangle, and using them to calculate their areas and the areas of composite shapes formed from them.","What is the area of a parallelogram with a base of 10 cm and a height of 4 cm?","20 cm^2","30 cm^2","40 cm^2","50 cm^2","40 cm^2","The area of a parallelogram is calculated by multiplying the base and height. 10 cm x 4 cm = 40 cm^2.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-formula-area-2bf967bb","Math G6: Distinguishing formulas for the perimeter and area of two-di - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-3-2 Distinguishing formulas for the perimeter and area of two-dimensional shapes, using them to find perimeter and area, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Distinguishing formulas for the perimeters of the rectangle, square, and circle, and using them to find the perimeter.","What is the formula for the area of a rectangle?","Area = Length x Width","Area = Length + Width","Area = Length - Width","Area = Length / Width","Area = Length x Width","The formula for the area of a rectangle is Area = Length x Width.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-formula-area-0ae361fd","Math G6: Distinguishing formulas for the perimeter and area of two-di - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-3-2 Distinguishing formulas for the perimeter and area of two-dimensional shapes, using them to find perimeter and area, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Distinguishing formulas for the perimeters of the rectangle, square, and circle, and using them to find the perimeter.","What is the formula for the area of a square?","Area = Side x Side","Area = Side + Side","Area = Side - Side","Area = Side / Side","Area = Side x Side","The formula for the area of a square is Area = Side x Side.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-formula-area-0e92320b","Math G6: Distinguishing formulas for the perimeter and area of two-di - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-3-2 Distinguishing formulas for the perimeter and area of two-dimensional shapes, using them to find perimeter and area, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","2. Distinguishing formulas for the areas of the rectangle, square, parallelogram, and triangle, and using them to calculate their areas and the areas of composite shapes formed from them.","What is the formula for the area of a parallelogram?","Area = Base x Height","Area = Base + Height","Area = Base - Height","Area = Base / Height","Area = Base x Height","The formula for the area of a parallelogram is Area = Base x Height.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-formula-area-4b09cec7","Math G6: Distinguishing formulas for the perimeter and area of two-di - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-3-2 Distinguishing formulas for the perimeter and area of two-dimensional shapes, using them to find perimeter and area, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","2. Distinguishing formulas for the areas of the rectangle, square, parallelogram, and triangle, and using them to calculate their areas and the areas of composite shapes formed from them.","What is the formula for the area of a triangle?","Area = Base x Height","Area = 1/2 x Base x Height","Area = Base + Height","Area = Base / Height","Area = 1/2 x Base x Height","The area of a triangle is found by multiplying the base by the height and then dividing by 2.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-area-of-d35a36ad","Math G6: Distinguishing formulas for the perimeter and area of two-di - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-3-2 Distinguishing formulas for the perimeter and area of two-dimensional shapes, using them to find perimeter and area, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","2. Distinguishing formulas for the areas of the rectangle, square, parallelogram, and triangle, and using them to calculate their areas and the areas of composite shapes formed from them.","What is the area of a composite shape made up of a rectangle and a triangle?","40 cm^2","64 cm^2","88 cm^2","100 cm^2","64 cm^2","The rectangle area is 8 cm x 5 cm = 40 cm^2. The triangle area is 1/2 x 8 cm x 6 cm = 24 cm^2. The total is 64 cm^2.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-area-of-099b06fc","Math G6: Distinguishing formulas for the perimeter and area of two-di - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-3-2 Distinguishing formulas for the perimeter and area of two-dimensional shapes, using them to find perimeter and area, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","2. Distinguishing formulas for the areas of the rectangle, square, parallelogram, and triangle, and using them to calculate their areas and the areas of composite shapes formed from them.","What is the area of a triangle with a base of 8 cm and a height of 6 cm?","18 cm^2","24 cm^2","30 cm^2","36 cm^2","24 cm^2","The area of a triangle is calculated by multiplying the base and height and then dividing by 2. (8 cm x 6 cm) / 2 = 24 cm^2.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-area-of-3798ad99","Math G6: Distinguishing formulas for the perimeter and area of two-di - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-3-2 Distinguishing formulas for the perimeter and area of two-dimensional shapes, using them to find perimeter and area, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Distinguishing formulas for the perimeters of the rectangle, square, and circle, and using them to find the perimeter.","What is the area of a square with a side length of 6 cm?","30 cm^2","36 cm^2","40 cm^2","50 cm^2","36 cm^2","The area of a square is calculated by multiplying the side length by itself. 6 cm x 6 cm = 36 cm^2.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-area-of-83273787","Math G6: Distinguishing formulas for the perimeter and area of two-di - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-3-2 Distinguishing formulas for the perimeter and area of two-dimensional shapes, using them to find perimeter and area, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","2. Distinguishing formulas for the areas of the rectangle, square, parallelogram, and triangle, and using them to calculate their areas and the areas of composite shapes formed from them.","What is the area of a composite shape made up of a square and a parallelogram?","80 cm^2","90 cm^2","100 cm^2","110 cm^2","80 cm^2","The square has area 6 cm × 6 cm = 36 cm². The parallelogram has area 11 cm × 4 cm = 44 cm². Adding the two areas gives 80 cm².","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-circular-fountain-223371cb","Math G6: Distinguishing formulas for the perimeter and area of two-di - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-3-2 Distinguishing formulas for the perimeter and area of two-dimensional shapes, using them to find perimeter and area, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Distinguishing formulas for the perimeters of the rectangle, square, and circle, and using them to find the perimeter.","A circular fountain has a diameter of 10 meters. What is the perimeter of the fountain?","The perimeter is 20 meters","The perimeter is 25 meters","The perimeter is 30 meters","The perimeter is 31.4 meters","The perimeter is 31.4 meters","Circumference = πd ≈ 3.14 × 10 = 31.4 meters.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-area-of-11f0dfb2","Math G6: Distinguishing formulas for the perimeter and area of two-di - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-3-2 Distinguishing formulas for the perimeter and area of two-dimensional shapes, using them to find perimeter and area, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","2. Distinguishing formulas for the areas of the rectangle, square, parallelogram, and triangle, and using them to calculate their areas and the areas of composite shapes formed from them.","What is the area of a triangle with a base of 6 cm and a height of 8 cm?","The area is 12 square centimeters","The area is 24 square centimeters","The area is 36 square centimeters","The area is 48 square centimeters","The area is 24 square centimeters","The area of a triangle is calculated using the formula: Area = 0.5 * base * height. So, Area = 0.5 * 6 * 8 = 24 square centimeters.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-rectangular-garden-63ab0e9a","Math G6: Distinguishing formulas for the perimeter and area of two-di - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-3-2 Distinguishing formulas for the perimeter and area of two-dimensional shapes, using them to find perimeter and area, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Distinguishing formulas for the perimeters of the rectangle, square, and circle, and using them to find the perimeter.","A rectangular garden measures 10 meters in length and 5 meters in width. What is the perimeter of the garden?","The perimeter is 20 meters","The perimeter is 30 meters","The perimeter is 40 meters","The perimeter is 50 meters","The perimeter is 30 meters","To find the perimeter, we add up all the sides of the garden. Since the garden is rectangular, we have two lengths and two widths. Perimeter = 2(length + width) = 2(10 + 5) = 2 * 15 = 30 meters.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-rectangular-room-1e626266","Math G6: Distinguishing formulas for the perimeter and area of two-di - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-3-2 Distinguishing formulas for the perimeter and area of two-dimensional shapes, using them to find perimeter and area, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Distinguishing formulas for the perimeters of the rectangle, square, and circle, and using them to find the perimeter.","A rectangular room measures 12 feet in length and 8 feet in width. What is the area of the room?","The area is 80 square feet","The area is 96 square feet","The area is 100 square feet","The area is 120 square feet","The area is 96 square feet","The area of a rectangle is calculated by multiplying its length by its width. Area = length * width = 12 * 8 = 96 square feet.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-area-of-7d587ffa","Math G6: Distinguishing formulas for the perimeter and area of two-di - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-3-2 Distinguishing formulas for the perimeter and area of two-dimensional shapes, using them to find perimeter and area, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Distinguishing formulas for the perimeters of the rectangle, square, and circle, and using them to find the perimeter.","What is the area of a circle with a radius of 4 cm?","The area is 12 square centimeters","The area is 16 square centimeters","The area is 20 square centimeters","The area is 50 square centimeters","The area is 50 square centimeters","Area = πr² ≈ 3.14 × 4² = 50.24, or about 50 square centimeters.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-circular-pizza-04097380","Math G6: Distinguishing formulas for the perimeter and area of two-di - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-3-2 Distinguishing formulas for the perimeter and area of two-dimensional shapes, using them to find perimeter and area, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Distinguishing formulas for the perimeters of the rectangle, square, and circle, and using them to find the perimeter.","A circular pizza has a diameter of 14 inches. What is the area of the pizza?","The area is 49 square inches","The area is 77 square inches","The area is 121 square inches","The area is 154 square inches","The area is 154 square inches","The radius is 14 ÷ 2 = 7 inches. Area = πr² ≈ 3.14 × 7² = 153.86, or about 154 square inches.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-rectangular-bookshelf-8709fb7d","Math G6: Distinguishing formulas for the perimeter and area of two-di - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-3-2 Distinguishing formulas for the perimeter and area of two-dimensional shapes, using them to find perimeter and area, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Distinguishing formulas for the perimeters of the rectangle, square, and circle, and using them to find the perimeter.","A rectangular bookshelf measures 8 feet in length, 3 feet in width, and 2 feet in height. What is the area of the shelf's top surface?","The area is 10 square feet","The area is 12 square feet","The area is 20 square feet","The area is 24 square feet","The area is 24 square feet","The area of the top surface of the bookshelf, which is a rectangle, is found by multiplying its length by its width. Area = length * width = 8 * 3 = 24 square feet.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-square-picture-468977c0","Math G6: Distinguishing formulas for the perimeter and area of two-di - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-3-2 Distinguishing formulas for the perimeter and area of two-dimensional shapes, using them to find perimeter and area, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Distinguishing formulas for the perimeters of the rectangle, square, and circle, and using them to find the perimeter.","A square picture frame has a side length of 8 inches. What is the perimeter of the frame?","The perimeter is 16 inches","The perimeter is 24 inches","The perimeter is 32 inches","The perimeter is 40 inches","The perimeter is 32 inches","Since the frame is square, all sides are equal. The perimeter of a square is the sum of all its sides, which is 4 times the length of one side. Perimeter = 4 * side length = 4 * 8 = 32 inches.","","",""
