"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-best-unit-839fc87e","Math G6: Distinguishing relationships between units of length - Set 3 T1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-3-1 Distinguishing relationships between units of length, mass, capacity, and time, and using them to convert between units.","3. Identifying the appropriate unit of capacity, distinguishing relationships between metric units of capacity (l, ml), and using them to convert between units.","What is the best unit to use when measuring the amount of juice in a carton?","milliliter","kilogram","liter","gram","liter","A carton of juice usually contains a large amount of liquid, so the liter is a more suitable unit of measurement.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-small-cup-33608b37","Math G6: Distinguishing relationships between units of length - Set 3 T1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-3-1 Distinguishing relationships between units of length, mass, capacity, and time, and using them to convert between units.","3. Identifying the appropriate unit of capacity, distinguishing relationships between metric units of capacity (l, ml), and using them to convert between units.","A small cup of water contains 250 milliliters. How many liters is that?","0.25 liters","2.5 liters","25 liters","250 liters","0.25 liters","Since there are 1000 milliliters in 1 liter, 250 milliliters is equal to 0.25 liters.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-bucket-of-3300ad5e","Math G6: Distinguishing relationships between units of length - Set 3 T1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-3-1 Distinguishing relationships between units of length, mass, capacity, and time, and using them to convert between units.","3. Identifying the appropriate unit of capacity, distinguishing relationships between metric units of capacity (l, ml), and using them to convert between units.","A bucket of paint contains 3 liters of paint. How many milliliters is that?","300 milliliters","3000 milliliters","30 milliliters","30000 milliliters","3000 milliliters","Since there are 1000 milliliters in 1 liter, 3 liters is equal to 3 * 1000 = 3000 milliliters.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-if-a-juice-4999bcef","Math G6: Distinguishing relationships between units of length - Set 3 T1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-3-1 Distinguishing relationships between units of length, mass, capacity, and time, and using them to convert between units.","3. Identifying the appropriate unit of capacity, distinguishing relationships between metric units of capacity (l, ml), and using them to convert between units.","If a juice box contains 200 milliliters of juice, how many liters is that?","0.2 liters","2 liters","20 liters","200 liters","0.2 liters","Since there are 1000 milliliters in 1 liter, 200 milliliters is equal to 0.2 liters.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-if-a-bottle-6a2eba1f","Math G6: Distinguishing relationships between units of length - Set 3 T1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-3-1 Distinguishing relationships between units of length, mass, capacity, and time, and using them to convert between units.","3. Identifying the appropriate unit of capacity, distinguishing relationships between metric units of capacity (l, ml), and using them to convert between units.","If a bottle of soda contains 1.5 liters, how many milliliters is that?","150 milliliters","1500 milliliters","15 milliliters","15000 milliliters","1500 milliliters","Since there are 1000 milliliters in 1 liter, 1.5 liters is equal to 1.5 * 1000 = 1500 milliliters.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-best-unit-a0416373","Math G6: Distinguishing relationships between units of length - Set 3 T1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-3-1 Distinguishing relationships between units of length, mass, capacity, and time, and using them to convert between units.","3. Identifying the appropriate unit of capacity, distinguishing relationships between metric units of capacity (l, ml), and using them to convert between units.","What is the best unit to use when measuring the amount of oil in a car engine?","liter","milliliter","kilogram","gram","liter","A car engine usually contains a large amount of oil, so the liter is a more suitable unit of measurement.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-if-a-container-bf14558d","Math G6: Distinguishing relationships between units of length - Set 3 T1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-3-1 Distinguishing relationships between units of length, mass, capacity, and time, and using them to convert between units.","3. Identifying the appropriate unit of capacity, distinguishing relationships between metric units of capacity (l, ml), and using them to convert between units.","If a container can hold 5000 milliliters of liquid, how many liters is that?","5 liters","0.5 liters","50 liters","0.05 liters","5 liters","Since there are 1000 milliliters in 1 liter, 5000 milliliters is equal to 5 liters.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-unit-would-you-56ec3345","Math G6: Distinguishing relationships between units of length - Set 3 T1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-3-1 Distinguishing relationships between units of length, mass, capacity, and time, and using them to convert between units.","3. Identifying the appropriate unit of capacity, distinguishing relationships between metric units of capacity (l, ml), and using them to convert between units.","What unit would you use to measure the amount of water in a small bottle?","kilogram","liter","meter","gram","liter","A liter is a unit of capacity, which is used to measure the amount of liquid in a container, such as a small bottle of water.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-if-a-recipe-ca2146f8","Math G6: Distinguishing relationships between units of length - Set 3 T1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-3-1 Distinguishing relationships between units of length, mass, capacity, and time, and using them to convert between units.","3. Identifying the appropriate unit of capacity, distinguishing relationships between metric units of capacity (l, ml), and using them to convert between units.","If a recipe calls for 500 milliliters of juice, how many liters is that?","0.05 liters","0.5 liters","5 liters","50 liters","0.5 liters","Since there are 1000 milliliters in 1 liter, 500 milliliters is equal to 0.5 liters.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-if-a-water-a879cda1","Math G6: Distinguishing relationships between units of length - Set 3 T1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-3-1 Distinguishing relationships between units of length, mass, capacity, and time, and using them to convert between units.","3. Identifying the appropriate unit of capacity, distinguishing relationships between metric units of capacity (l, ml), and using them to convert between units.","If a water tank can hold 2000 liters of water, how many milliliters is that?","2,000,000 mL","200,000 mL","20,000 mL","2,000 mL","2,000,000 mL","Because 1 L = 1,000 mL, 2,000 L = 2,000 × 1,000 = 2,000,000 mL.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-recipe-calls-646cbdc1","Math G6: Distinguishing relationships between units of length - Set 4","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-3-1 Distinguishing relationships between units of length, mass, capacity, and time, and using them to convert between units.","4. Distinguishing relationships between units of time (second, minute), (minute, hour), (hour, day), (day, week), (month, year), and using them to convert between units.","A recipe calls for 30 minutes of baking time. How many seconds is that?","1800 seconds","3000 seconds","3600 seconds","4200 seconds","1800 seconds","There are 60 seconds in one minute, so 30 minutes is 30 * 60 seconds.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-how-many-hours-eb65f34c","Math G6: Distinguishing relationships between units of length - Set 4","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-3-1 Distinguishing relationships between units of length, mass, capacity, and time, and using them to convert between units.","4. Distinguishing relationships between units of time (second, minute), (minute, hour), (hour, day), (day, week), (month, year), and using them to convert between units.","How many hours are in a day?","12 hours","20 hours","24 hours","36 hours","24 hours","A standard day has 24 hours, from midnight to midnight.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-if-it-takes-c03bf364","Math G6: Distinguishing relationships between units of length - Set 4","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-3-1 Distinguishing relationships between units of length, mass, capacity, and time, and using them to convert between units.","4. Distinguishing relationships between units of time (second, minute), (minute, hour), (hour, day), (day, week), (month, year), and using them to convert between units.","If it takes 4 hours to drive to a city, and you leave at 8:00 AM, what time will you arrive?","10:00 AM","12:00 PM","4:00 PM","8:00 PM","12:00 PM","Adding 4 hours to the departure time gives the arrival time.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-how-many-minutes-404007f4","Math G6: Distinguishing relationships between units of length - Set 4","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-3-1 Distinguishing relationships between units of length, mass, capacity, and time, and using them to convert between units.","4. Distinguishing relationships between units of time (second, minute), (minute, hour), (hour, day), (day, week), (month, year), and using them to convert between units.","How many minutes are in 2 hours?","60 minutes","90 minutes","120 minutes","180 minutes","120 minutes","Since there are 60 minutes in one hour, there are 2 * 60 minutes in 2 hours.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-school-day-5f9a10e6","Math G6: Distinguishing relationships between units of length - Set 4","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-3-1 Distinguishing relationships between units of length, mass, capacity, and time, and using them to convert between units.","4. Distinguishing relationships between units of time (second, minute), (minute, hour), (hour, day), (day, week), (month, year), and using them to convert between units.","A school day is 6 hours long. How many minutes is that?","300 minutes","360 minutes","420 minutes","480 minutes","360 minutes","Since there are 60 minutes in an hour, a 6-hour school day is 6 * 60 minutes.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-if-a-tv-998e43a2","Math G6: Distinguishing relationships between units of length - Set 4","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-3-1 Distinguishing relationships between units of length, mass, capacity, and time, and using them to convert between units.","3. Identifying the appropriate unit of capacity, distinguishing relationships between metric units of capacity (l, ml), and using them to convert between units.","If a TV show starts at 7:00 PM and lasts for 30 minutes, what time will it end?","6:30 PM","7:30 PM","7:45 PM","8:00 PM","7:30 PM","Adding 30 minutes to the start time gives the end time.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-how-many-days-76612493","Math G6: Distinguishing relationships between units of length - Set 4","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-3-1 Distinguishing relationships between units of length, mass, capacity, and time, and using them to convert between units.","3. Identifying the appropriate unit of capacity, distinguishing relationships between metric units of capacity (l, ml), and using them to convert between units.","How many days are in a week?","5 days","6 days","7 days","10 days","7 days","A standard week has 7 days, from Sunday to Saturday.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-how-many-minutes-8f63a510","Math G6: Distinguishing relationships between units of length - Set 4","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-3-1 Distinguishing relationships between units of length, mass, capacity, and time, and using them to convert between units.","4. Distinguishing relationships between units of time (second, minute), (minute, hour), (hour, day), (day, week), (month, year), and using them to convert between units.","How many minutes are in one hour?","30 minutes","45 minutes","60 minutes","90 minutes","60 minutes","There are 60 seconds in one minute and 60 minutes in one hour.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-if-a-movie-6d6384e4","Math G6: Distinguishing relationships between units of length - Set 4","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-3-1 Distinguishing relationships between units of length, mass, capacity, and time, and using them to convert between units.","4. Distinguishing relationships between units of time (second, minute), (minute, hour), (hour, day), (day, week), (month, year), and using them to convert between units.","If a movie starts at 3:00 PM and lasts for 2 hours, what time will it end?","1:00 PM","3:00 PM","5:00 PM","7:00 PM","5:00 PM","Adding 2 hours to the start time gives the end time.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-if-a-project-afb49f9a","Math G6: Distinguishing relationships between units of length - Set 4","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-3-1 Distinguishing relationships between units of length, mass, capacity, and time, and using them to convert between units.","4. Distinguishing relationships between units of time (second, minute), (minute, hour), (hour, day), (day, week), (month, year), and using them to convert between units.","If a project takes 3 hours to complete, and you start at 9:00 AM, what time will you finish?","10:00 AM","11:00 AM","12:00 PM","1:00 PM","12:00 PM","Adding 3 hours to the start time gives the finish time.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-are-first-four-64747ed9","Math G6: Distinguishing the factors and multiples of a number - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-7 Estimating the results of the four arithmetic operations on whole numbers, Proper fractions, and decimal fractions, and using mental calculation strategies to find results, and verifying the plausibility and accuracy of the results.","4. Using mental calculation to find the results of multiplying and dividing decimal fractions by multiples of (10, 100, 1000).","What are the first four multiples of the number 3?","3, 6, 9, 12","2, 4, 6, 8","1, 2, 3, 4","5, 10, 15, 20","3, 6, 9, 12","The multiples of a number are the products of that number and the counting numbers. So, the first four multiples of 3 are 3 x 1 = 3, 3 x 2 = 6, 3 x 3 = 9, and 3 x 4 = 12.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-are-first-three-3bb5cb1e","Math G6: Distinguishing the factors and multiples of a number - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-2 Distinguishing the factors and multiples of a number, representing and finding them, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Determining the factors of a number and representing them using models and diagrams.","What are the first three multiples of the number 2?","2, 4, 6","1, 3, 5","4, 6, 8","10, 12, 14","2, 4, 6","The multiples of a number are the products of that number and the counting numbers. So, the first three multiples of 2 are 2 x 1 = 2, 2 x 2 = 4, and 2 x 3 = 6.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-of-is-a-7fa43a27","Math G6: Distinguishing the factors and multiples of a number - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-2 Distinguishing the factors and multiples of a number, representing and finding them, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","5. Solving mathematical problems including real-life applications of the greatest common divisor and the least common multiple, and explaining their solutions.","Which of the following is a multiple of 9?","36","45","63","72","36","To find the multiples of a number, we multiply that number by the counting numbers. Since 9 x 4 = 36, 36 is a multiple of 9.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-next-multiple-9130e39e","Math G6: Distinguishing the factors and multiples of a number - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-2 Distinguishing the factors and multiples of a number, representing and finding them, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","5. Solving mathematical problems including real-life applications of the greatest common divisor and the least common multiple, and explaining their solutions.","What is the next multiple of 6 after 30?","36","38","40","42","36","The multiples of 6 are the products of 6 and the counting numbers. So, the multiples of 6 are 6, 12, 18, 24, 30, 36, and so on.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-number-has-12-3603d8e3","Math G6: Distinguishing the factors and multiples of a number - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-2 Distinguishing the factors and multiples of a number, representing and finding them, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Determining the factors of a number and representing them using models and diagrams.","Which number has 12 as a multiple?","2","3","4","6","3","A number has another number as a multiple if the first number can be multiplied by an integer to get the second number. Since 3 x 4 = 12, 3 has 12 as a multiple.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-are-first-two-155f9e28","Math G6: Distinguishing the factors and multiples of a number - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-2 Distinguishing the factors and multiples of a number, representing and finding them, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","4. Factoring a number into its prime factors, and using prime factorization to find the greatest common divisor and the least common multiple of two  numbers or more.","What are the first two multiples of the number 8?","8, 16","4, 6","10, 12","14, 16","8, 16","The multiples of a number are the products of that number and the counting numbers. So, the first two multiples of 8 are 8 x 1 = 8 and 8 x 2 = 16.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-of-numbers-are-b0f4c3bc","Math G6: Distinguishing the factors and multiples of a number - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-7 Estimating the results of the four arithmetic operations on whole numbers, Proper fractions, and decimal fractions, and using mental calculation strategies to find results, and verifying the plausibility and accuracy of the results.","4. Using mental calculation to find the results of multiplying and dividing decimal fractions by multiples of (10, 100, 1000).","Which of the following numbers are multiples of 5?","10, 15, 20","7, 11, 13","2, 4, 6","8, 12, 16","10, 15, 20","To find the multiples of a number, we multiply that number by the counting numbers. So, the multiples of 5 are 5 x 1 = 5, 5 x 2 = 10, 5 x 3 = 15, and 5 x 4 = 20.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-of-numbers-are-00ce76b9","Math G6: Distinguishing the factors and multiples of a number - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-7 Estimating the results of the four arithmetic operations on whole numbers, Proper fractions, and decimal fractions, and using mental calculation strategies to find results, and verifying the plausibility and accuracy of the results.","4. Using mental calculation to find the results of multiplying and dividing decimal fractions by multiples of (10, 100, 1000).","Which of the following numbers are multiples of 3?","9, 12, 15","7, 11, 13","2, 4, 6","8, 10, 14","9, 12, 15","To find the multiples of a number, we multiply that number by the counting numbers. So, the multiples of 3 are 3 x 1 = 3, 3 x 2 = 6, 3 x 3 = 9, 3 x 4 = 12, and 3 x 5 = 15.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-next-multiple-28e7246a","Math G6: Distinguishing the factors and multiples of a number - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-2 Distinguishing the factors and multiples of a number, representing and finding them, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","5. Solving mathematical problems including real-life applications of the greatest common divisor and the least common multiple, and explaining their solutions.","What is the next multiple of 4 after 24?","28","30","32","36","28","The multiples of 4 are the products of 4 and the counting numbers. So, the multiples of 4 are 4, 8, 12, 16, 20, 24, 28, and so on.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-number-has-6-3b94c03d","Math G6: Distinguishing the factors and multiples of a number - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-2 Distinguishing the factors and multiples of a number, representing and finding them, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Determining the factors of a number and representing them using models and diagrams.","Which number multiplied by 2 equals 6?","1","2","3","6","3","Because 3 × 2 = 6, the correct answer is 3.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-gcd-of-cd37789f","Math G6: Distinguishing the factors and multiples of a number - Set 5","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-2 Distinguishing the factors and multiples of a number, representing and finding them, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","5. Solving mathematical problems including real-life applications of the greatest common divisor and the least common multiple, and explaining their solutions.","What is the GCD of 20 and 30?","The GCD is 10 because it is the largest number that divides both 20 and 30 without leaving a remainder.","The GCD is 1 because it is the smallest number that divides both 20 and 30 without leaving a remainder.","The GCD is 5 because it is the largest number that divides both 20 and 30 without leaving a remainder.","The GCD is 15 because it is the largest number that divides both 20 and 30 without leaving a remainder.","The GCD is 10 because it is the largest number that divides both 20 and 30 without leaving a remainder.","The greatest common divisor (GCD) of 20 and 30 is 10 because it is the largest number that divides both 20 and 30 without leaving a remainder.","","",""
