"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-48-multiplied-6f3e795a","Math G6: Estimating the results of the four - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-7 Estimating the results of the four arithmetic operations on whole numbers, Proper fractions, and decimal fractions, and using mental calculation strategies to find results, and verifying the plausibility and accuracy of the results.","1 Estimating the results of the four arithmetic operations on whole numbers, Proper fractions, mixed numbers, and decimal Proper by using rounding.","What is 48 multiplied by 10?","400","48","480","4,800","480","Multiplying 48 by 10 gives us 480.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-27-divided-4f8f0cb0","Math G6: Estimating the results of the four - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-7 Estimating the results of the four arithmetic operations on whole numbers, Proper fractions, and decimal fractions, and using mental calculation strategies to find results, and verifying the plausibility and accuracy of the results.","1 Estimating the results of the four arithmetic operations on whole numbers, Proper fractions, mixed numbers, and decimal Proper by using rounding.","What is 27 divided by 100?","0.27","27","2.7","0.3","0.27","Dividing 27 by 100 gives us 0.27.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-group-of-9db7510a","Math G6: Estimating the results of the four - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-7 Estimating the results of the four arithmetic operations on whole numbers, Proper fractions, and decimal fractions, and using mental calculation strategies to find results, and verifying the plausibility and accuracy of the results.","1 Estimating the results of the four arithmetic operations on whole numbers, Proper fractions, mixed numbers, and decimal Proper by using rounding.","A group of friends want to share some candy equally. If they have 48 pieces of candy and there are 8 friends, how many pieces of candy will each friend get?","Each friend will get 6 pieces of candy","5 pieces","7 pieces","48 pieces","Each friend will get 6 pieces of candy","48 ÷ 8 = 6 pieces of candy.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-if-a-pizza-5b25ad8c","Math G6: Estimating the results of the four - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-7 Estimating the results of the four arithmetic operations on whole numbers, Proper fractions, and decimal fractions, and using mental calculation strategies to find results, and verifying the plausibility and accuracy of the results.","1 Estimating the results of the four arithmetic operations on whole numbers, Proper fractions, mixed numbers, and decimal Proper by using rounding.","If a pizza has a diameter of 14 inches and is cut into 8 equal slices, what is the area of one slice in square inches?","19.25 square inches","7.5 square inches","14 square inches","38.5 square inches","19.25 square inches","The radius is 7 inches. The pizza area is about 3.14 x 7^2 = 153.86 square inches. One of 8 slices is about 19.25 square inches.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-if-a-water-9c4dc037","Math G6: Estimating the results of the four - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-7 Estimating the results of the four arithmetic operations on whole numbers, Proper fractions, and decimal fractions, and using mental calculation strategies to find results, and verifying the plausibility and accuracy of the results.","1 Estimating the results of the four arithmetic operations on whole numbers, Proper fractions, mixed numbers, and decimal Proper by using rounding.","If a water bottle can hold 2 liters of water and is currently 1/4 full, how many more liters of water can be added to the bottle?","1.5 liters","1.75 liters","0.5 liters","2 liters","1.5 liters","The bottle is 1/4 full, so 3/4 is empty. 3/4 of 2 liters is 1.5 liters.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-if-a-pencil-51416529","Math G6: Estimating the results of the four - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-7 Estimating the results of the four arithmetic operations on whole numbers, Proper fractions, and decimal fractions, and using mental calculation strategies to find results, and verifying the plausibility and accuracy of the results.","1 Estimating the results of the four arithmetic operations on whole numbers, Proper fractions, mixed numbers, and decimal Proper by using rounding.","If a pencil is 15 cm long and is divided into 5 parts, how long is each part?","3 cm","4 cm","5 cm","10 cm","3 cm","15 cm divided into 5 equal parts gives 15 ÷ 5 = 3 cm per part.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-if-a-book-e6d92ab9","Math G6: Estimating the results of the four - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-1-2 Understanding the properties of two-dimensional and three-dimensional geometric shapes and using them to identify and classify the shapes.","1. Distinguishing polygons, classifying them into regular and irregular polygons, and naming them.","If a book costs $18 and a pencil costs $0.50, what is the total cost of 3 books and 2 pencils?","$55.00","$56.00","$56.50","$57.00","$55.00","Three books cost 3 x $18 = $54. Two pencils cost 2 x $0.50 = $1. The total is $54 + $1 = $55.00.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-if-sally-has-c17e2761","Math G6: Estimating the results of the four - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-7 Estimating the results of the four arithmetic operations on whole numbers, Proper fractions, and decimal fractions, and using mental calculation strategies to find results, and verifying the plausibility and accuracy of the results.","1 Estimating the results of the four arithmetic operations on whole numbers, Proper fractions, mixed numbers, and decimal Proper by using rounding.","If Sally has 18 pencils and she gives 1/4 of them to her friend, how many pencils does Sally have left?","13.5 pencils","15 pencils","14 pencils","4.5 pencils","13.5 pencils","One fourth of 18 is 4.5. 18 - 4.5 = 13.5 pencils.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-bookshelf-has-72079da9","Math G6: Estimating the results of the four - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-7 Estimating the results of the four arithmetic operations on whole numbers, Proper fractions, and decimal fractions, and using mental calculation strategies to find results, and verifying the plausibility and accuracy of the results.","1 Estimating the results of the four arithmetic operations on whole numbers, Proper fractions, mixed numbers, and decimal Proper by using rounding.","A bookshelf has 5 shelves, and each shelf can hold 8 books. If the bookshelf is currently empty, how many books can be placed on it in total?","40 books","30 books","45 books","50 books","40 books","Multiply shelves by books per shelf: 5 x 8 = 40 books.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-if-a-rectangular-9e64b7c1","Math G6: Estimating the results of the four - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-3-3 Understanding volume and surface area and their units, distinguishing the formulas for a right rectangular prism, and applying them to find volume and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Estimating volume and measuring it by using the appropriate metric unit (ml3, cm3, and m3).","If a rectangular garden measures 12 meters by 8 meters, what is its area in square meters?","96 square meters","98 square meters","100 square meters","102 square meters","96 square meters","To find the area of the garden, we need to multiply its length by its width. 12 meters * 8 meters = 96 square meters.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-bakery-sells-a09f02d9","Math G6: Estimating the results of the four - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-7 Estimating the results of the four arithmetic operations on whole numbers, Proper fractions, and decimal fractions, and using mental calculation strategies to find results, and verifying the plausibility and accuracy of the results.","1 Estimating the results of the four arithmetic operations on whole numbers, Proper fractions, mixed numbers, and decimal Proper by using rounding.","A bakery sells a total of 480 loaves of bread per day. If they sell an equal number of whole wheat and white bread, how many loaves of each type are sold per day?","240 whole wheat loaves and 240 white bread loaves","216","264","2,400","240 whole wheat loaves and 240 white bread loaves","To find the number of loaves of each type sold per day, we need to divide the total number of loaves sold by 2. 480 / 2 = 240 loaves of each type.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-if-a-water-0cc00899","Math G6: Estimating the results of the four - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-7 Estimating the results of the four arithmetic operations on whole numbers, Proper fractions, and decimal fractions, and using mental calculation strategies to find results, and verifying the plausibility and accuracy of the results.","1 Estimating the results of the four arithmetic operations on whole numbers, Proper fractions, mixed numbers, and decimal Proper by using rounding.","If a water tank can hold 240 gallons of water and 1/3 of it is already filled, how many more gallons of water can be added to the tank?","160 gallons","100 gallons","80 gallons","120 gallons","160 gallons","If 1/3 is filled, 2/3 is empty. 2/3 of 240 is 160 gallons.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-bike-costs-afa1b8dc","Math G6: Performing the four arithmetic operations on - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-6 Performing the four arithmetic operations on decimal fractions, numbers and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Adding and subtracting decimal fractions up to a thousand place.","A bike costs $45.50. If you pay with a $50 bill, how much change will you get?","$4.50","$5.00","$4.00","$5.50","$4.50","Subtract the cost from the amount paid: $50.00 - $45.50 = $4.50.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-group-of-a60a43f1","Math G6: Performing the four arithmetic operations on - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-6 Performing the four arithmetic operations on decimal fractions, numbers and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Adding and subtracting decimal fractions up to a thousand place.","A group of friends want to share some money equally. If they have $32.50 and there are 5 friends, how much money will each friend get?","$6.50","$5.50","$7.00","$8.50","$6.50","The total is $6.50; duplicate equivalent currency options were replaced.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-pencil-is-356d9325","Math G6: Performing the four arithmetic operations on - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-6 Performing the four arithmetic operations on decimal fractions, numbers and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","2. Multiplying and dividing decimal fractions up to the hundredths place.","A pencil is 15.2 cm long. If it is divided into 5 parts, how long is each part?","3.04 cm","3.04","3.04 cm long","3.04 is correct","3.04 cm","To find the length of each part, we need to divide the total length of the pencil by the number of parts. 15.2 cm / 5 = 3.04 cm.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-water-bottle-6b166da9","Math G6: Performing the four arithmetic operations on - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-6 Performing the four arithmetic operations on decimal fractions, numbers and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Adding and subtracting decimal fractions up to a thousand place.","A water bottle can hold 2.5 liters of water. If it already has 1.8 liters of water in it, how much more water can be added?","0.7 liters","0.7","0.7 liters of water","0.7 is correct","0.7 liters","To find out how much more water can be added, we need to subtract the amount of water already in the bottle from the total capacity. 2.5 liters - 1.8 liters = 0.7 liters.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-bookshelf-has-a410b0ae","Math G6: Performing the four arithmetic operations on - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-6 Performing the four arithmetic operations on decimal fractions, numbers and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Adding and subtracting decimal fractions up to a thousand place.","A bookshelf has 5 shelves, and each shelf can hold 3.2 boxes of books. If the bookshelf is currently empty, how many boxes of books can it hold in total?","16","15.2","18","20","16","Multiply shelves by boxes per shelf: 5 x 3.2 = 16.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-tom-has-12-3724199d","Math G6: Performing the four arithmetic operations on - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-6 Performing the four arithmetic operations on decimal fractions, numbers and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Adding and subtracting decimal fractions up to a thousand place.","Tom has $12.50 in his piggy bank. He adds $8.25 to it. How much money does Tom have now?","$20.75","$20.25","$20.75 is correct","$20.00","$20.75","To find the total amount of money Tom has now, we need to add $12.50 and $8.25. $12.50 + $8.25 = $20.75.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-group-of-d37c8d48","Math G6: Performing the four arithmetic operations on - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-6 Performing the four arithmetic operations on decimal fractions, numbers and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Adding and subtracting decimal fractions up to a thousand place.","A group of friends want to share some candy equally. If they have 24.8 pieces of candy and there are 4 friends, how many pieces of candy will each friend get?","6.2 pieces","6.2","6.2 pieces of candy","6.2 is correct","6.2 pieces","To find out how many pieces of candy each friend will get, we need to divide the total number of pieces by the number of friends. 24.8 pieces / 4 friends = 6.2 pieces.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-water-tank-87b9ea02","Math G6: Performing the four arithmetic operations on - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-6 Performing the four arithmetic operations on decimal fractions, numbers and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Adding and subtracting decimal fractions up to a thousand place.","A water tank can hold 15.6 liters of water. If it already has 9.2 liters of water in it, how much more water can be added?","6.4 liters","6.4","6.4 liters of water","6.4 is correct","6.4 liters","To find out how much more water can be added, we need to subtract the amount of water already in the tank from the total capacity. 15.6 liters - 9.2 liters = 6.4 liters.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-book-costs-2d66c725","Math G6: Performing the four arithmetic operations on - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-7 Estimating the results of the four arithmetic operations on whole numbers, Proper fractions, and decimal fractions, and using mental calculation strategies to find results, and verifying the plausibility and accuracy of the results.","3. Applying the distributive property to multiply a two-digit number by a one-digit number mentally.","A book costs $12.75. If you buy 3 books, how much will you pay in total?","$38.25","$38.25 is correct","$38.25 is the correct answer","$38.25 is the total cost","$38.25","To find the total cost of the books, we need to multiply the cost of one book by the number of books. $12.75 * 3 = $38.25.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-rectangular-garden-d8569a48","Math G6: Performing the four arithmetic operations on - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-3-3 Understanding volume and surface area and their units, distinguishing the formulas for a right rectangular prism, and applying them to find volume and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Estimating volume and measuring it by using the appropriate metric unit (ml3, cm3, and m3).","A rectangular garden measures 8.5 meters by 3.2 meters. What is its area?","27.2 square meters","27.2","27.2 square meters is correct","27","27.2 square meters","To find the area of the garden, we need to multiply the length by the width. 8.5 meters * 3.2 meters = 27.2 square meters.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-bookshelf-has-faec4cc6","Math G6: Performing the four arithmetic operations on - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-6 Performing the four arithmetic operations on decimal fractions, numbers and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Adding and subtracting decimal fractions up to a thousand place.","A bookshelf has 5 shelves, and each shelf can hold 3 boxes of books. If the bookshelf is currently empty, how many boxes of books can be placed on it in total?","10 boxes","15 boxes","20 boxes","25 boxes","15 boxes","To find out how many boxes of books can be placed on the bookshelf, we need to multiply the number of shelves by the number of boxes each shelf can hold. 5 shelves * 3 boxes/shelf = 15 boxes.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-book-costs-5c76935e","Math G6: Performing the four arithmetic operations on - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-6 Performing the four arithmetic operations on decimal fractions, numbers and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Adding and subtracting decimal fractions up to a thousand place.","A book costs $15. If you have a 20% discount coupon, how much will you pay for the book?","$12.00","$12.50","$15.00","$17.50","$12.00","The stated calculation gives $12.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-group-of-cf12ce6c","Math G6: Performing the four arithmetic operations on - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-6 Performing the four arithmetic operations on decimal fractions, numbers and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Adding and subtracting decimal fractions up to a thousand place.","A group of friends want to go to a movie that costs $12 per ticket. If they have $60 to spend, how many tickets can they buy?","4 tickets","5 tickets","6 tickets","8 tickets","5 tickets","To find out how many tickets they can buy, we need to divide the amount of money they have by the cost of each ticket. $60 / $12 = 5 tickets.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-pencil-is-842b79be","Math G6: Performing the four arithmetic operations on - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-6 Performing the four arithmetic operations on decimal fractions, numbers and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Adding and subtracting decimal fractions up to a thousand place.","A pencil is on sale for $0.50. If you have a 10% discount coupon, how much will you pay for the pencil?","$0.40","$0.45","$0.50","$0.55","$0.45","To find out how much you will pay for the pencil, we need to calculate the discount and subtract it from the original price. 10% of $0.50 = $0.05, so the discount is $0.05. $0.50 - $0.05 = $0.45.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-group-of-5c459de1","Math G6: Performing the four arithmetic operations on - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-6 Performing the four arithmetic operations on decimal fractions, numbers and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Adding and subtracting decimal fractions up to a thousand place.","A group of friends want to share some candy equally. If they have 48 pieces of candy and there are 8 friends, how many pieces of candy will each friend get?","Each friend will get 6 pieces of candy","5 pieces","7 pieces","48 pieces","Each friend will get 6 pieces of candy","48 ÷ 8 = 6 pieces of candy.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-bakery-is-64e34c46","Math G6: Performing the four arithmetic operations on - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-6 Performing the four arithmetic operations on decimal fractions, numbers and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Adding and subtracting decimal fractions up to a thousand place.","A bakery is having a sale on bread. A loaf of bread normally costs $2.50, but it is on sale for $1.75. What is the discount on the bread?","$0.75","$0.50","$0.25","$1.00","$0.75","To find out the discount on the bread, we need to subtract the sale price from the original price. $2.50 - $1.75 = $0.75.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-tom-has-12-cbd14389","Math G6: Performing the four arithmetic operations on - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-6 Performing the four arithmetic operations on decimal fractions, numbers and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Adding and subtracting decimal fractions up to a thousand place.","Tom has $12.50 in his piggy bank. He wants to buy a toy that costs $8.25. How much money will he have left?","$4.25","$4.50","$4.75","$5.00","$4.25","To find out how much money Tom will have left, we need to subtract the cost of the toy from the amount of money he has. $12.50 - $8.25 = $4.25.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-box-of-5d765476","Math G6: Performing the four arithmetic operations on - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-6 Performing the four arithmetic operations on decimal fractions, numbers and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Adding and subtracting decimal fractions up to a thousand place.","A box of crayons weighs 0.25 kg. If you have 4 boxes of crayons, what is the total weight?","1 kg","0.75 kg","0.5 kg","1.25 kg","1 kg","0.25 kg x 4 = 1 kg.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-water-tank-ecdcfb27","Math G6: Performing the four arithmetic operations on - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-6 Performing the four arithmetic operations on decimal fractions, numbers and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Adding and subtracting decimal fractions up to a thousand place.","A water tank can hold 1200 liters of water. If 750 liters of water are already in the tank, what percentage of the tank is filled?","62.5% of the tank is filled.","67%","57%","75%","62.5% of the tank is filled.","750 ÷ 1200 × 100 = 62.5%, so the tank is 62.5% full.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-group-of-d25fd127","Math G6: Performing the four arithmetic operations on - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-6 Performing the four arithmetic operations on decimal fractions, numbers and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Adding and subtracting decimal fractions up to a thousand place.","A group of friends want to share some money equally. If they have $120 and there are 8 friends, how much money will each friend get?","$10","$12","$15","$16","$15","To find out how much money each friend will get, we need to divide the total amount of money by the number of friends. $120 / 8 friends = $15/friend.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-recipe-making-8771f10e","Math G6: Performing the four operations on Proper fractions and mixed - Set 5","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-5 Performing the four operations on Proper fractions and mixed numbers and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications","5. Solving mathematical problems up to three steps that including real-life applications on the four operations on fractions and mixed numbers, and explaining their solutions.","A recipe for making cookies calls for 2/3 cup of sugar. If you want to make half a batch of cookies, how much sugar do you need?","1/3 cup of sugar","1/2 cup of sugar","2/3 cup of sugar","1 cup of sugar","1/3 cup of sugar","One out of three equal parts is 1/3.","/image-source/math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-recipe-making-8771f10e.png","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-bakery-sells-639b5ac0","Math G6: Performing the four operations on Proper fractions and mixed - Set 5","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-5 Performing the four operations on Proper fractions and mixed numbers and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications","5. Solving mathematical problems up to three steps that including real-life applications on the four operations on fractions and mixed numbers, and explaining their solutions.","A bakery sells 2/3 of its bread in the morning. If it sells 18 loaves of bread in the morning, how many loaves of bread does the bakery have in total?","18 loaves","24 loaves","27 loaves","36 loaves","27 loaves","If 2/3 of the bread is sold, then the whole bakery is 18 * (3/2) = 27 loaves.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-group-of-c69aba18","Math G6: Performing the four operations on Proper fractions and mixed - Set 5","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-5 Performing the four operations on Proper fractions and mixed numbers and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications","5. Solving mathematical problems up to three steps that including real-life applications on the four operations on fractions and mixed numbers, and explaining their solutions.","A group of friends want to share some candy equally. If they have 3/4 of a bag of candy and there are 4 friends, how much candy will each friend get?","1/4 of the candy","3/16 of the candy","3/4 of the candy","1 bag of candy","3/16 of the candy","If they have 3/4 of a bag of candy and there are 4 friends, each friend will get 3/4 / 4 = 3/16 of the candy.","/image-source/math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-group-of-c69aba18.png","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-farmer-has-f5428301","Math G6: Performing the four operations on Proper fractions and mixed - Set 5","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-5 Performing the four operations on Proper fractions and mixed numbers and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications","5. Solving mathematical problems up to three steps that including real-life applications on the four operations on fractions and mixed numbers, and explaining their solutions.","A farmer has 2/3 of a field planted with wheat. If 1/3 of the field is planted with corn, what fraction of the field is not planted with wheat or corn?","0 of the field","1/3 of the field","1/2 of the field","1 field","0 of the field","The wheat and corn together cover 2/3 + 1/3 = 1 whole field, so 0 of the field is not planted with wheat or corn.","/image-source/math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-farmer-has-f5428301.png","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-box-of-739c4929","Math G6: Performing the four operations on Proper fractions and mixed - Set 5","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-5 Performing the four operations on Proper fractions and mixed numbers and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications","5. Solving mathematical problems up to three steps that including real-life applications on the four operations on fractions and mixed numbers, and explaining their solutions.","A box of crayons weighs 3/4 of a pound. If a pencil weighs 1/4 of a pound, how much do the crayons and pencil weigh together?","1/2 pound","3/4 pound","1 pound","1 1/4 pounds","1 pound","The described amount is 1 pound.","/image-source/math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-box-of-739c4929.png","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-book-costs-56f832a9","Math G6: Performing the four operations on Proper fractions and mixed - Set 5","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-5 Performing the four operations on Proper fractions and mixed numbers and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications","5. Solving mathematical problems up to three steps that including real-life applications on the four operations on fractions and mixed numbers, and explaining their solutions.","A book costs $2.50. If you pay 1/4 of the cost, how much do you pay?","$0.50","$0.625","$1.25","$2.50","$0.625","One fourth of $2.50 is $2.50 x 1/4 = $0.625.","/image-source/math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-book-costs-56f832a9.png","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-recipe-making-891bf6b8","Math G6: Performing the four operations on Proper fractions and mixed - Set 5","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-5 Performing the four operations on Proper fractions and mixed numbers and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications","5. Solving mathematical problems up to three steps that including real-life applications on the four operations on fractions and mixed numbers, and explaining their solutions.","A recipe for making a cake calls for 2/3 cup of milk. If you want to make half a batch of cake, how much milk do you need?","1/3 cup of milk","1/2 cup of milk","2/3 cup of milk","1 cup of milk","1/3 cup of milk","One out of three equal parts is 1/3.","/image-source/math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-recipe-making-891bf6b8.png","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-bookshelf-has-fef20470","Math G6: Performing the four operations on Proper fractions and mixed - Set 5","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-5 Performing the four operations on Proper fractions and mixed numbers and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications","5. Solving mathematical problems up to three steps that including real-life applications on the four operations on fractions and mixed numbers, and explaining their solutions.","A bookshelf has 3/4 of its space filled with books. If 1/4 of the space is filled with 12 books, how many books are on the bookshelf in total?","18 books","24 books","30 books","36 books","30 books","If 1/4 of the space is filled with 12 books, then the whole bookshelf is filled with 12 * 4 = 48 books.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-tom-has-3-8764f2f0","Math G6: Performing the four operations on Proper fractions and mixed - Set 5","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-5 Performing the four operations on Proper fractions and mixed numbers and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications","5. Solving mathematical problems up to three steps that including real-life applications on the four operations on fractions and mixed numbers, and explaining their solutions.","Tom has 3/4 of a pizza left. His friend gives him 1/4 of a pizza. What fraction of a pizza does Tom have now?","1 whole pizza","3/4 of a pizza","5/8 of a pizza","7/8 of a pizza","1 whole pizza","Tom had 3/4 of a pizza and got 1/4 of a pizza, so he now has 3/4 + 1/4 = 4/4 = 1 whole pizza.","/image-source/math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-tom-has-3-8764f2f0.png","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-water-tank-ac7d44ce","Math G6: Performing the four operations on Proper fractions and mixed - Set 5","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-5 Performing the four operations on Proper fractions and mixed numbers and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications","5. Solving mathematical problems up to three steps that including real-life applications on the four operations on fractions and mixed numbers, and explaining their solutions.","A water tank can hold 3/4 of a tank full of water. If 1/4 of the tank is already filled, how much more water can be added?","1/4 of the tank","1/2 of the tank","3/4 of the tank","1 tank","1/2 of the tank","One out of two equal parts is 1/2.","/image-source/math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-water-tank-ac7d44ce.png","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-recipe-requires-7f894939","Math G6: Performing the four operations on Proper - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-5 Performing the four operations on Proper fractions and mixed numbers and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications","5. Solving mathematical problems up to three steps that including real-life applications on the four operations on fractions and mixed numbers, and explaining their solutions.","A recipe requires 2 1/3 cups of sugar. If we already have 1 2/3 cups of sugar, how much more sugar do we need?","2/3 cup","1/3 cup","1 cup","1 2/3 cups","2/3 cup","2 1/3 - 1 2/3 = 7/3 - 5/3 = 2/3 cup.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-tom-has-3-9b61a7c3","Math G6: Performing the four operations on Proper - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-5 Performing the four operations on Proper fractions and mixed numbers and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications","5. Solving mathematical problems up to three steps that including real-life applications on the four operations on fractions and mixed numbers, and explaining their solutions.","Tom has 3 1/4 cups of flour. His friend gives him 2 1/2 cups of flour. How much flour does Tom have now?","Tom has 5 1/2 cups of flour.","Tom has 5 3/4 cups of flour.","Tom has 6 1/4 cups of flour.","Tom has 6 3/4 cups of flour.","Tom has 5 3/4 cups of flour.","To find the total amount of flour, we need to add 3 1/4 and 2 1/2. First, we add the whole numbers: 3 + 2 = 5. Then, we add the fractions: 1/4 + 1/2 = 3/4. Now, we need to combine the whole number and the fraction: 5 3/4.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-bookshelf-has-4191561d","Math G6: Performing the four operations on Proper - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-5 Performing the four operations on Proper fractions and mixed numbers and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications","5. Solving mathematical problems up to three steps that including real-life applications on the four operations on fractions and mixed numbers, and explaining their solutions.","A bookshelf has 2 3/4 meters of space on one shelf. If we add 1 1/2 meters of space from another shelf, how much space do we have now?","4 1/4 meters","4 1/2 meters","3 1/4 meters","5 meters","4 1/4 meters","2 3/4 + 1 1/2 = 2 3/4 + 1 2/4 = 4 1/4 meters.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-3-4-6f5c0577","Math G6: Performing the four operations on Proper - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-6 Performing the four arithmetic operations on decimal fractions, numbers and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","2. Multiplying and dividing decimal fractions up to the hundredths place.","What is 3/4 divided by 2/3?","1 1/8","1 1/4","2 1/4","1","1 1/8","3/4 ÷ 2/3 = 1 1/8.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-3-4-058895ed","Math G6: Performing the four operations on Proper - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-5 Performing the four operations on Proper fractions and mixed numbers and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications","5. Solving mathematical problems up to three steps that including real-life applications on the four operations on fractions and mixed numbers, and explaining their solutions.","What is 3/4 multiplied by 2/5?","3/10","1/4","1/3","3/4","3/10","The correct simplified fraction is 3/10.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-2-3-4c15eb35","Math G6: Performing the four operations on Proper - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-6 Performing the four arithmetic operations on decimal fractions, numbers and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","2. Multiplying and dividing decimal fractions up to the hundredths place.","What is 2/3 divided by 1/6?","4","5","8","3","4","2/3 ÷ 1/6 = 4.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-1-2-60a3768c","Math G6: Performing the four operations on Proper - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-5 Performing the four operations on Proper fractions and mixed numbers and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications","5. Solving mathematical problems up to three steps that including real-life applications on the four operations on fractions and mixed numbers, and explaining their solutions.","What is 1/2 multiplied by 3/4?","3/8","1/4","1/3","3/4","3/8","The correct simplified fraction is 3/8.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-3-5-b1ec4fe7","Math G6: Performing the four operations on Proper - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-5 Performing the four operations on Proper fractions and mixed numbers and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications","5. Solving mathematical problems up to three steps that including real-life applications on the four operations on fractions and mixed numbers, and explaining their solutions.","What is 3/5 multiplied by 2/3?","2/5","1/4","1/3","3/4","2/5","The correct simplified fraction is 2/5.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-1-4-dd0581ca","Math G6: Performing the four operations on Proper - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-5 Performing the four operations on Proper fractions and mixed numbers and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications","5. Solving mathematical problems up to three steps that including real-life applications on the four operations on fractions and mixed numbers, and explaining their solutions.","What is 1/4 multiplied by 2/3?","1/6","1/4","1/3","3/4","1/6","The correct simplified fraction is 1/6.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-2-3-02cca341","Math G6: Performing the four operations on Proper - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-5 Performing the four operations on Proper fractions and mixed numbers and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications","5. Solving mathematical problems up to three steps that including real-life applications on the four operations on fractions and mixed numbers, and explaining their solutions.","What is 2/3 multiplied by 3/4?","1/2","1/4","1/3","3/4","1/2","The correct simplified fraction is 1/2.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-1-2-3a7c6e9a","Math G6: Performing the four operations on Proper - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-6 Performing the four arithmetic operations on decimal fractions, numbers and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","2. Multiplying and dividing decimal fractions up to the hundredths place.","What is 1/2 divided by 3/4?","2/3","1/4","1/3","3/4","2/3","The correct simplified fraction is 2/3.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-2-5-088df157","Math G6: Performing the four operations on Proper - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-6 Performing the four arithmetic operations on decimal fractions, numbers and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","2. Multiplying and dividing decimal fractions up to the hundredths place.","What is 2/5 divided by 3/4?","8/15","1/4","1/3","3/4","8/15","The correct simplified fraction is 8/15.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-1-3-67ff637c","Math G6: Performing the four operations on Proper - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-6 Performing the four arithmetic operations on decimal fractions, numbers and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","2. Multiplying and dividing decimal fractions up to the hundredths place.","What is 1/3 divided by 2/5?","5/6","1/4","1/3","3/4","5/6","The correct simplified fraction is 5/6.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-water-tank-572b8d20","Math G6: Performing the four operations on Proper - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-5 Performing the four operations on Proper fractions and mixed numbers and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications","5. Solving mathematical problems up to three steps that including real-life applications on the four operations on fractions and mixed numbers, and explaining their solutions.","A water tank can hold 3 3/4 gallons of water. If 2 1/4 gallons of water are already in the tank, how much more water can be added?","1 1/2 gallons","2 1/4 gallons","1 gallon","2 gallons","1 1/2 gallons","3 3/4 - 2 1/4 = 1 1/2 gallons.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-pizza-has-2ed7db68","Math G6: Performing the four operations on Proper - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-5 Performing the four operations on Proper fractions and mixed numbers and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications","5. Solving mathematical problems up to three steps that including real-life applications on the four operations on fractions and mixed numbers, and explaining their solutions.","A pizza has a diameter of 14 inches. If it is cut into 8 equal slices, how many slices will you get if you divide the pizza into 1/8th slices?","8 slices","16 slices","32 slices","64 slices","8 slices","A whole pizza divided into eighths has 8 slices.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-tom-has-3-06b7356a","Math G6: Performing the four operations on Proper - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-1-2 Distinguishing Proper fractions, mixed numbers, and improper fractions, representing, reading, writing, comparing, ordering, and rounding them.","2. Finding equivalent fractions, converting them to their simplest form, and rounding them to the nearest whole, half, or one.","Tom has 3 1/2 pounds of apples. He wants to package them in bags that hold 1/4 pound each. How many bags can he fill?","He can fill 12 bags","He can fill 13 bags","He can fill 14 bags","He can fill 15 bags","He can fill 14 bags","To find the number of bags, we need to divide 3 1/2 by 1/4. First, convert 3 1/2 to an improper fraction: 7/2. Then, multiply 7/2 by 4 to get 28. Finally, divide 28 by 1/4, which is the same as multiplying 28 by 4. This gives us 112/4, which simplifies to 28. Since we can't fill a fraction of a bag, we round down to 14 bags.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-water-tank-416daece","Math G6: Performing the four operations on Proper - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-5 Performing the four operations on Proper fractions and mixed numbers and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications","5. Solving mathematical problems up to three steps that including real-life applications on the four operations on fractions and mixed numbers, and explaining their solutions.","A water tank can hold 2 3/4 gallons of water. If 1 1/4 gallons of water are already in the tank, how much more water can be added?","1 1/2 gallons","2 1/4 gallons","1 gallon","2 gallons","1 1/2 gallons","2 3/4 - 1 1/4 = 1 1/2 gallons.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-box-of-c11d89e9","Math G6: Performing the four operations on Proper - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-5 Performing the four operations on Proper fractions and mixed numbers and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications","5. Solving mathematical problems up to three steps that including real-life applications on the four operations on fractions and mixed numbers, and explaining their solutions.","A box of cereal weighs 2 3/4 pounds. If you eat 1/4 of the cereal, how much will you have left?","2 1/16 pounds","2 1/4 pounds","2 pounds","1 3/4 pounds","2 1/16 pounds","If 1/4 is eaten, 3/4 remains. 2 3/4 x 3/4 = 11/4 x 3/4 = 33/16 = 2 1/16 pounds.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-bookshelf-has-262e348e","Math G6: Performing the four operations on Proper - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-5 Performing the four operations on Proper fractions and mixed numbers and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications","5. Solving mathematical problems up to three steps that including real-life applications on the four operations on fractions and mixed numbers, and explaining their solutions.","A bookshelf has 5 shelves, and each shelf can hold 2 1/3 boxes. If the bookshelf is currently empty, how many boxes can it hold in total?","11 2/3 boxes","12 boxes","10 boxes","13 1/3 boxes","11 2/3 boxes","5 x 2 1/3 = 5 x 7/3 = 35/3 = 11 2/3 boxes.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-recipe-making-59db9a07","Math G6: Performing the four operations on Proper - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-5 Performing the four operations on Proper fractions and mixed numbers and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications","5. Solving mathematical problems up to three steps that including real-life applications on the four operations on fractions and mixed numbers, and explaining their solutions.","A recipe for making cookies calls for 2 3/4 cups of flour. If you want to make half a batch, how much flour will you need?","1 3/8 cups","1 1/2 cups","1 1/4 cups","2 cups","1 3/8 cups","Half of 2 3/4 is 11/4 x 1/2 = 11/8 = 1 3/8 cups.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-pizza-has-924600fe","Math G6: Performing the four operations on Proper - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-5 Performing the four operations on Proper fractions and mixed numbers and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications","5. Solving mathematical problems up to three steps that including real-life applications on the four operations on fractions and mixed numbers, and explaining their solutions.","A pizza has a diameter of 12 inches. If it is cut into 6 equal slices, how many slices will you get if you divide the pizza into 1/6th slices?","6 slices","12 slices","18 slices","24 slices","6 slices","A whole pizza divided into sixths has 6 slices.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-recipe-making-20838b7a","Math G6: Performing the four operations on Proper - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-5 Performing the four operations on Proper fractions and mixed numbers and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications","5. Solving mathematical problems up to three steps that including real-life applications on the four operations on fractions and mixed numbers, and explaining their solutions.","A recipe for making pancakes calls for 2 1/4 cups of flour. If you want to make 1/4 of the recipe, how much flour will you need?","9/16 cup","3/4 cup","1/2 cup","1 cup","9/16 cup","One fourth of 2 1/4 is 9/4 x 1/4 = 9/16 cup.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-book-costs-4c745269","Math G6: Performing the four operations on Proper - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-5 Performing the four operations on Proper fractions and mixed numbers and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications","5. Solving mathematical problems up to three steps that including real-life applications on the four operations on fractions and mixed numbers, and explaining their solutions.","A book costs $4 1/2. If you pay with a $10 bill, how much change will you get?","$3","$3 1/2","$5","$5 1/2","$5 1/2","To find the change, we need to subtract 4 1/2 from 10. First, convert 4 1/2 to an improper fraction: 9/2. Then, convert 10 to an improper fraction: 20/2. Now, subtract 9/2 from 20/2 to get 11/2, which simplifies to 5 1/2.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-bakery-sells-64b33651","Math G6: Understanding and finding ratios, rates, percentages - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-4 Understanding and finding ratios, rates, percentages, and proportions, distinguishing between them, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. Distinguishing and solving proportions, and identifying proportional quantities.","A bakery sells 240 cupcakes in a day. If they sell 12% of the cupcakes at a discount, how many cupcakes are sold at a discount?","24 cupcakes","28 cupcakes","30 cupcakes","32 cupcakes","24 cupcakes","To find the number of cupcakes sold at a discount, we need to multiply the total number of cupcakes by the percentage off.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-tom-has-15-000e24f1","Math G6: Understanding and finding ratios, rates, percentages - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-4 Understanding and finding ratios, rates, percentages, and proportions, distinguishing between them, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. Distinguishing and solving proportions, and identifying proportional quantities.","Tom has 15 pencils in his pencil case. If he gives 3 pencils to his friend, what percentage of pencils does Tom have left?","75% of the pencils","25% of the pencils","50% of the pencils","100% of the pencils","25% of the pencils","To find the percentage of pencils Tom has left, we need to divide the number of pencils he has left by the total number of pencils and multiply by 100.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-student-scored-300e6752","Math G6: Understanding and finding ratios, rates, percentages - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-4 Understanding and finding ratios, rates, percentages, and proportions, distinguishing between them, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. Distinguishing and solving proportions, and identifying proportional quantities.","A student scored 85% on a test. If the test had 20 questions, how many questions did the student answer correctly?","17 questions","18 questions","19 questions","20 questions","17 questions","To find the number of questions the student answered correctly, we need to multiply the total number of questions by the percentage scored.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-shirt-is-935d502c","Math G6: Understanding and finding ratios, rates, percentages - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-4 Understanding and finding ratios, rates, percentages, and proportions, distinguishing between them, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. Distinguishing and solving proportions, and identifying proportional quantities.","A shirt is on sale for 20% off. If the original price is $40, how much will you pay for the shirt?","$32.00","$36.00","$38.00","$40.00","$32.00","To find the sale price, we need to multiply the original price by the percentage off and subtract the result from the original price.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-book-costs-bd0730d0","Math G6: Understanding and finding ratios, rates, percentages - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-4 Understanding and finding ratios, rates, percentages, and proportions, distinguishing between them, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. Distinguishing and solving proportions, and identifying proportional quantities.","A book costs $25. If it is on sale for 15% off, how much will you pay for the book?","$21.25","$22.50","$23.75","$25.00","$21.25","To find the sale price, we need to multiply the original price by the percentage off and subtract the result from the original price.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-school-has-1ed91c7c","Math G6: Understanding and finding ratios, rates, percentages - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-7 Estimating the results of the four arithmetic operations on whole numbers, Proper fractions, and decimal fractions, and using mental calculation strategies to find results, and verifying the plausibility and accuracy of the results.","4. Using mental calculation to find the results of multiplying and dividing decimal fractions by multiples of (10, 100, 1000).","A school has 300 students. If 20% of the students are in the 6th grade, how many students are in the 6th grade?","60 students","62 students","64 students","66 students","60 students","To find the number of students in the 6th grade, we need to multiply the total number of students by the percentage in the 6th grade.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-store-is-3c23d63d","Math G6: Understanding and finding ratios, rates, percentages - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-4 Understanding and finding ratios, rates, percentages, and proportions, distinguishing between them, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. Distinguishing and solving proportions, and identifying proportional quantities.","A store is having a sale where everything is 15% off. If a book costs $45, how much will you pay for the book?","$38.25","$39.75","$41.25","$45.00","$38.25","To find the sale price, we need to multiply the original price by the percentage off and subtract the result from the original price.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-student-scored-73287b40","Math G6: Understanding and finding ratios, rates, percentages - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-4 Understanding and finding ratios, rates, percentages, and proportions, distinguishing between them, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. Distinguishing and solving proportions, and identifying proportional quantities.","A student scored 90% on a test. If the test had 25 questions, how many questions did the student answer correctly?","22 questions","23 questions","24 questions","25 questions","22 questions","To find the number of questions the student answered correctly, we need to multiply the total number of questions by the percentage scored.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-water-tank-23db2d06","Math G6: Understanding and finding ratios, rates, percentages - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-4 Understanding and finding ratios, rates, percentages, and proportions, distinguishing between them, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","4. Solving mathematical problems including real-life applications that involve ratio, rate, percentage, and proportion and explaining their solutions.","A water tank can hold 1200 liters of water. If 80% of the tank is filled, how many liters of water are in the tank?","960 liters","1000 liters","1040 liters","1080 liters","960 liters","To find the number of liters of water in the tank, we need to multiply the total capacity of the tank by the percentage filled.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-store-is-535ea720","Math G6: Understanding and finding ratios, rates, percentages - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-4 Understanding and finding ratios, rates, percentages, and proportions, distinguishing between them, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. Distinguishing and solving proportions, and identifying proportional quantities.","A store is having a sale where everything is 10% off. If a toy costs $60, how much will you pay for the toy?","$54.00","$56.00","$58.00","$60.00","$54.00","To find the sale price, we need to multiply the original price by the percentage off and subtract the result from the original price.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-box-of-56ae8ff8","Math G6: Understanding and finding ratios, rates, percentages - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-4 Understanding and finding ratios, rates, percentages, and proportions, distinguishing between them, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. Distinguishing and solving proportions, and identifying proportional quantities.","A box of crayons weighs 1.2 kg. If it is 80% full, how much does it weigh?","0.96 kg","1.04 kg","0.80 kg","1.20 kg","0.96 kg","80% of 1.2 kg is 0.80 x 1.2 = 0.96 kg.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-pencil-is-b80c826f","Math G6: Understanding and finding ratios, rates, percentages - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-4 Understanding and finding ratios, rates, percentages, and proportions, distinguishing between them, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. Distinguishing and solving proportions, and identifying proportional quantities.","A pencil is on sale for 15% off its original price of $2. How much will you pay for the pencil?","$1.40","$1.50","$1.60","$1.70","$1.70","The discount is 15% of $2, which is $2 x 0.15 = $0.30. The sale price is $2 - $0.30 = $1.70.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-group-of-57c08efa","Math G6: Understanding and finding ratios, rates, percentages - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-4 Understanding and finding ratios, rates, percentages, and proportions, distinguishing between them, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. Distinguishing and solving proportions, and identifying proportional quantities.","A group of friends want to share some candy equally. If they have 48 pieces of candy and there are 8 friends, how many pieces of candy will each friend get?","Each friend will get 6 pieces of candy","5 pieces","7 pieces","48 pieces","Each friend will get 6 pieces of candy","48 ÷ 8 = 6 pieces of candy.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-water-tank-f01f3dce","Math G6: Understanding and finding ratios, rates, percentages - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-4 Understanding and finding ratios, rates, percentages, and proportions, distinguishing between them, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. Distinguishing and solving proportions, and identifying proportional quantities.","A water tank can hold 1200 liters of water. If 3/4 of the tank is already filled, how many liters of water are in the tank?","900 liters","1000 liters","1100 liters","1200 liters","900 liters","To find the amount of water in the tank, we multiply the total capacity of the tank by the fraction that is filled. 1200 × 3/4 = 900 liters.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-car-travels-885f8e1e","Math G6: Understanding and finding ratios, rates, percentages - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-4 Understanding and finding ratios, rates, percentages, and proportions, distinguishing between them, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. Distinguishing and solving proportions, and identifying proportional quantities.","A car travels 250 miles in 5 hours. What is its average speed?","25 miles per hour","50 miles per hour","75 miles per hour","100 miles per hour","50 miles per hour","To find the average speed, we divide the distance traveled by the time taken. 250 ÷ 5 = 50 miles per hour.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-bakery-sells-3412e07b","Math G6: Understanding and finding ratios, rates, percentages - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-4 Understanding and finding ratios, rates, percentages, and proportions, distinguishing between them, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. Distinguishing and solving proportions, and identifying proportional quantities.","A bakery sells 240 loaves of bread per day. What is 25% of 240?","60 loaves equivalent","25","240","300","60 loaves equivalent","25% of 240 is 0.25 x 240 = 60.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-bike-travels-6f464486","Math G6: Understanding and finding ratios, rates, percentages - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-4 Understanding and finding ratios, rates, percentages, and proportions, distinguishing between them, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. Distinguishing and solving proportions, and identifying proportional quantities.","A bike travels 180 miles in 6 hours. What is its average speed?","20 miles per hour","30 miles per hour","40 miles per hour","50 miles per hour","30 miles per hour","To find the average speed, we divide the distance traveled by the time taken. 180 ÷ 6 = 30 miles per hour.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-recipe-making-e561fb14","Math G6: Understanding and finding ratios, rates, percentages - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-4 Understanding and finding ratios, rates, percentages, and proportions, distinguishing between them, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. Distinguishing and solving proportions, and identifying proportional quantities.","A recipe for making cookies calls for a 3:2 ratio of flour to sugar. If we need 6 cups of flour, how many cups of sugar do we need?","4 cups","6 cups","8 cups","9 cups","4 cups","To find the amount of sugar needed, we multiply the amount of flour needed by the ratio of sugar to flour. 6 / 2/3 = 4 cups.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-tom-has-12-2c6b6c3f","Math G6: Understanding and finding ratios, rates, percentages - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-4 Understanding and finding ratios, rates, percentages, and proportions, distinguishing between them, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. Distinguishing and solving proportions, and identifying proportional quantities.","Tom has 12 pencils in a box and his friend has 18 pencils in a box. What is the ratio of Tom's pencils to his friend's pencils?","Tom has 2/3 as many pencils as his friend","Tom has 1/2 as many pencils as his friend","Tom has 3/4 as many pencils as his friend","Tom has the same number of pencils as his friend","Tom has 2/3 as many pencils as his friend","To find the ratio, we divide the number of Tom's pencils by the number of his friend's pencils. 12 ÷ 18 = 2/3.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-bookshelf-has-edfca177","Math G6: Understanding and finding ratios, rates, percentages - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-4 Understanding and finding ratios, rates, percentages, and proportions, distinguishing between them, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. Distinguishing and solving proportions, and identifying proportional quantities.","A bookshelf has 5 shelves, and each shelf can hold 8 books. If the bookshelf is currently empty, how many books can be placed on it in total?","40 books","30 books","45 books","50 books","40 books","Multiply shelves by books per shelf: 5 x 8 = 40 books.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-group-of-3ba82a04","Math G6: Understanding and finding ratios - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-4 Understanding and finding ratios, rates, percentages, and proportions, distinguishing between them, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. Distinguishing and solving proportions, and identifying proportional quantities.","A group of students scored an average of 80 points on a test. If the total score of the class is 1600 points, how many students are in the class?","10 students","15 students","20 students","25 students","20 students","The total score of the class is the average score multiplied by the number of students, so 80 * number of students = 1600, which means there are 1600 / 80 = 20 students.","/image-source/math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-group-of-3ba82a04.png","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-person-can-c3a438d3","Math G6: Understanding and finding ratios - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-4 Understanding and finding ratios, rates, percentages, and proportions, distinguishing between them, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. Distinguishing and solving proportions, and identifying proportional quantities.","A person can walk 3 kilometers in 1 hour. What is the ratio of distance to time?","3 to 1","1 to 3","2 to 1","1 to 2","3 to 1","The ratio of distance to time is 3:1, meaning the person walks 3 kilometers for every 1 hour.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-recipe-making-a64e4627","Math G6: Understanding and finding ratios - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-4 Understanding and finding ratios, rates, percentages, and proportions, distinguishing between them, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. Distinguishing and solving proportions, and identifying proportional quantities.","A recipe for making cookies calls for a ratio of 2 cups of flour to 1 cup of sugar. If you use 4 cups of flour, how many cups of sugar should you use?","1 cup of sugar","2 cups of sugar","3 cups of sugar","4 cups of sugar","2 cups of sugar","Since the ratio is 2:1, if you use 4 cups of flour, you should use 2 cups of sugar to maintain the ratio.","/image-source/math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-recipe-making-a64e4627.png","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-car-travels-34239324","Math G6: Understanding and finding ratios - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-4 Understanding and finding ratios, rates, percentages, and proportions, distinguishing between them, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. Distinguishing and solving proportions, and identifying proportional quantities.","A car travels 240 kilometers in 4 hours. What is the rate of the car?","60 kilometers per hour","50 kilometers per hour","40 kilometers per hour","30 kilometers per hour","60 kilometers per hour","The rate of the car is the distance traveled divided by the time taken, which is 240 km / 4 hours = 60 km/h.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-bakery-sells-57038297","Math G6: Understanding and finding ratios - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-4 Understanding and finding ratios, rates, percentages, and proportions, distinguishing between them, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. Distinguishing and solving proportions, and identifying proportional quantities.","A bakery sells 250 loaves of bread per day. If they operate 5 days a week, what is the ratio of loaves sold per day to the total number of loaves sold per week?","1 to 5","1 to 10","1 to 20","1 to 25","1 to 5","The ratio of loaves sold per day to the total number of loaves sold per week is 250:1250, which simplifies to 1:5, meaning they sell 1/5 of the weekly total per day.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-bike-travels-583d1986","Math G6: Understanding and finding ratios - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-4 Understanding and finding ratios, rates, percentages, and proportions, distinguishing between them, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. Distinguishing and solving proportions, and identifying proportional quantities.","A bike travels 20 kilometers in 2 hours. What is the rate of the bike?","10 kilometers per hour","5 kilometers per hour","20 kilometers per hour","40 kilometers per hour","10 kilometers per hour","The rate of the bike is the distance traveled divided by the time taken, which is 20 km / 2 hours = 10 km/h.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-water-tank-bc9ca91e","Math G6: Understanding and finding ratios - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-4 Understanding and finding ratios, rates, percentages, and proportions, distinguishing between them, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. Distinguishing and solving proportions, and identifying proportional quantities.","A water tank can hold 1200 liters of water. If 300 liters of water are already in the tank, what is the ratio of water in the tank to the total capacity of the tank?","1 to 4","1 to 3","1 to 2","3 to 4","1 to 4","The ratio of water in the tank to the total capacity is 300:1200, which simplifies to 1:4, meaning the tank is one-quarter full.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-group-of-22dcc239","Math G6: Understanding and finding ratios - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-4 Understanding and finding ratios, rates, percentages, and proportions, distinguishing between them, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. Distinguishing and solving proportions, and identifying proportional quantities.","A group of friends want to share some candy equally. If they have 12 pieces of candy and there are 4 friends, what is the ratio of candy to friends?","3 to 1","2 to 1","1 to 1","4 to 1","3 to 1","The ratio of candy to friends is 12:4, which simplifies to 3:1, meaning each friend gets 3 pieces of candy.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-bookshelf-has-67f6128d","Math G6: Understanding and finding ratios - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-4 Understanding and finding ratios, rates, percentages, and proportions, distinguishing between them, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. Distinguishing and solving proportions, and identifying proportional quantities.","A bookshelf has 5 shelves, and 3 of them are filled with books. What is the ratio of filled shelves to total shelves?","3 to 5","5 to 3","2 to 5","1 to 1","3 to 5","The ratio of filled shelves to total shelves is 3:5, which means for every 5 shelves, 3 are filled with books.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-farmer-has-0f8e3290","Math G6: Understanding and finding ratios - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-4 Understanding and finding ratios, rates, percentages, and proportions, distinguishing between them, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. Distinguishing and solving proportions, and identifying proportional quantities.","A farmer has 15 baskets of apples. If 3 baskets are filled with red apples and the rest are filled with green apples, what is the ratio of red apples to total apples?","1 to 5","1 to 3","3 to 15","1 to 2","1 to 5","The ratio of red apples to total apples is 3:15, which simplifies to 1:5, meaning 1/5 of the baskets are filled with red apples.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-book-costs-ce1857cb","Math G6: Understanding and finding ratios - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-4 Understanding and finding ratios, rates, percentages, and proportions, distinguishing between them, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","4. Solving mathematical problems including real-life applications that involve ratio, rate, percentage, and proportion and explaining their solutions.","A book costs $30. If it's on sale for 15% off, what is the sale price?","$25","$26","$28","$30","$25","To find the discount amount, multiply the original price by the percentage off. 15% of $30 is 0.15 x 30 = $4.50. Then subtract the discount from the original price: $30 - $4.50 = $25.50, rounded to $25.","/image-source/math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-book-costs-ce1857cb.png","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-store-is-cd914569","Math G6: Understanding and finding ratios - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-4 Understanding and finding ratios, rates, percentages, and proportions, distinguishing between them, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. Distinguishing and solving proportions, and identifying proportional quantities.","A store is having a sale where everything is 20% off. If a shirt originally costs $50, how much will it cost during the sale?","$40","$42","$44","$48","$40","To find the discount amount, multiply the original price by the percentage off. 20% of $50 is 0.20 x 50 = $10. Then subtract the discount from the original price: $50 - $10 = $40.","/image-source/math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-store-is-cd914569.png","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-bakery-sells-9c5099cb","Math G6: Understanding and finding ratios - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-4 Understanding and finding ratios, rates, percentages, and proportions, distinguishing between them, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. Distinguishing and solving proportions, and identifying proportional quantities.","A bakery sells a cake for $25. If they give a 20% discount, how much will the customer pay?","$16","$20","$22","$24","$20","After applying the stated discount, the customer pays $20.","/image-source/math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-bakery-sells-9c5099cb.png","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-bike-is-e866f24b","Math G6: Understanding and finding ratios - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-4 Understanding and finding ratios, rates, percentages, and proportions, distinguishing between them, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","4. Solving mathematical problems including real-life applications that involve ratio, rate, percentage, and proportion and explaining their solutions.","A bike is priced at $120. If it's discounted by 15%, what is the sale price?","$102","$104","$108","$112","$102","To find the discount amount, multiply the original price by the percentage off. 15% of $120 is 0.15 x 120 = $18. Then subtract the discount from the original price: $120 - $18 = $102.","/image-source/math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-bike-is-e866f24b.png","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-pair-of-3f915e8b","Math G6: Understanding and finding ratios - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-4 Understanding and finding ratios, rates, percentages, and proportions, distinguishing between them, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. Distinguishing and solving proportions, and identifying proportional quantities.","A pair of shoes costs $80. If they're on sale for 25% off, what is the sale price?","$60","$62","$65","$68","$60","To find the discount amount, multiply the original price by the percentage off. 25% of $80 is 0.25 x 80 = $20. Then subtract the discount from the original price: $80 - $20 = $60.","/image-source/math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-pair-of-3f915e8b.png","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-toy-is-8e79f16c","Math G6: Understanding and finding ratios - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-4 Understanding and finding ratios, rates, percentages, and proportions, distinguishing between them, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","4. Solving mathematical problems including real-life applications that involve ratio, rate, percentage, and proportion and explaining their solutions.","A toy is priced at $60. If it's discounted by 30%, what is the sale price?","$42","$45","$48","$52","$42","To find the discount amount, multiply the original price by the percentage off. 30% of $60 is 0.30 x 60 = $18. Then subtract the discount from the original price: $60 - $18 = $42.","/image-source/math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-toy-is-8e79f16c.png","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-store-is-e8b77b31","Math G6: Understanding and finding ratios - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-4 Understanding and finding ratios, rates, percentages, and proportions, distinguishing between them, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. Distinguishing and solving proportions, and identifying proportional quantities.","A store is having a sale where everything is 10% off. If a toy originally costs $40, how much will it cost during the sale?","$36","$38","$40","$42","$36","To find the discount amount, multiply the original price by the percentage off. 10% of $40 is 0.10 x 40 = $4. Then subtract the discount from the original price: $40 - $4 = $36.","/image-source/math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-store-is-e8b77b31.png","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-book-costs-c74aecd2","Math G6: Understanding and finding ratios - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-4 Understanding and finding ratios, rates, percentages, and proportions, distinguishing between them, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","4. Solving mathematical problems including real-life applications that involve ratio, rate, percentage, and proportion and explaining their solutions.","A book costs $40. If it's on sale for 25% off, what is the sale price?","$30","$32","$35","$38","$30","To find the discount amount, multiply the original price by the percentage off. 25% of $40 is 0.25 x 40 = $10. Then subtract the discount from the original price: $40 - $10 = $30.","/image-source/math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-book-costs-c74aecd2.png","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-shirt-is-47d3698e","Math G6: Understanding and finding ratios - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-4 Understanding and finding ratios, rates, percentages, and proportions, distinguishing between them, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. Distinguishing and solving proportions, and identifying proportional quantities.","A shirt is on sale for 15% off. If the original price is $80, what is the discount amount?","$8","$10","$12","$16","$12","15% of $80 is 0.15 × 80 = $12.","/image-source/math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-shirt-is-47d3698e.png","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-video-game-f5739ed3","Math G6: Understanding and finding ratios - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-4 Understanding and finding ratios, rates, percentages, and proportions, distinguishing between them, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","4. Solving mathematical problems including real-life applications that involve ratio, rate, percentage, and proportion and explaining their solutions.","A video game is priced at $60. If it's discounted by 20%, what is the sale price?","$48","$50","$52","$54","$48","To find the discount amount, multiply the original price by the percentage off. 20% of $60 is 0.20 x 60 = $12. Then subtract the discount from the original price: $60 - $12 = $48.","/image-source/math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-video-game-f5739ed3.png","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-decimal-fraction-is-49c23749","Math G6: Understanding and representing decimal fractions, recognizing - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-1-3 Understanding and representing decimal fractions, recognizing the place value of a digit in it, reading, writing, comparing, ordering, and rounding them, and converting between decimal fractions, Proper fractions and mixed numbers.","3. Comparing decimal fractions, ordering them in ascending and descending order, and converting between decimal fractions, Proper fractions, and mixed numbers.","Which decimal fraction is greater: 0.35 or 0.30?","0.30 is greater","0.35 is greater","They are equal","Not enough information","0.35 is greater","Since 0.35 has a larger second digit, it is greater than 0.30.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-of-decimal-fractions-7827033e","Math G6: Understanding and representing decimal fractions, recognizing - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-1-3 Understanding and representing decimal fractions, recognizing the place value of a digit in it, reading, writing, comparing, ordering, and rounding them, and converting between decimal fractions, Proper fractions and mixed numbers.","3. Comparing decimal fractions, ordering them in ascending and descending order, and converting between decimal fractions, Proper fractions, and mixed numbers.","Which of the following decimal fractions is equal to 1/2?","0.25","0.75","0.33","0.5","0.5","To convert 1/2 to a decimal, we divide the numerator by the denominator: 1 ÷ 2 = 0.50.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-decimal-fraction-is-3bc598d8","Math G6: Understanding and representing decimal fractions, recognizing - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-1-3 Understanding and representing decimal fractions, recognizing the place value of a digit in it, reading, writing, comparing, ordering, and rounding them, and converting between decimal fractions, Proper fractions and mixed numbers.","3. Comparing decimal fractions, ordering them in ascending and descending order, and converting between decimal fractions, Proper fractions, and mixed numbers.","Which decimal fraction is greater: 0.45 or 0.40?","0.40 is greater","0.45 is greater","They are equal","Not enough information","0.45 is greater","Since 0.45 has a larger second digit, it is greater than 0.40.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-decimal-fraction-is-10c80452","Math G6: Understanding and representing decimal fractions, recognizing - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-1-3 Understanding and representing decimal fractions, recognizing the place value of a digit in it, reading, writing, comparing, ordering, and rounding them, and converting between decimal fractions, Proper fractions and mixed numbers.","3. Comparing decimal fractions, ordering them in ascending and descending order, and converting between decimal fractions, Proper fractions, and mixed numbers.","Which decimal fraction is less than 0.8?","0.6","0.4","0.7","0.9","0.4","0.4 is less than 0.8, so it is a correct answer choice.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-decimal-fraction-is-8dcc4fef","Math G6: Understanding and representing decimal fractions, recognizing - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-1-3 Understanding and representing decimal fractions, recognizing the place value of a digit in it, reading, writing, comparing, ordering, and rounding them, and converting between decimal fractions, Proper fractions and mixed numbers.","3. Comparing decimal fractions, ordering them in ascending and descending order, and converting between decimal fractions, Proper fractions, and mixed numbers.","Which decimal fraction is less than 0.5?","0.6","0.4","0.7","0.8","0.4","Since 0.4 is less than 0.5, it is the correct answer.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-of-decimal-fractions-b3148965","Math G6: Understanding and representing decimal fractions, recognizing - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-1-3 Understanding and representing decimal fractions, recognizing the place value of a digit in it, reading, writing, comparing, ordering, and rounding them, and converting between decimal fractions, Proper fractions and mixed numbers.","3. Comparing decimal fractions, ordering them in ascending and descending order, and converting between decimal fractions, Proper fractions, and mixed numbers.","Which of the following decimal fractions is equal to 3/4?","0.25","0.75","0.33","0.5","0.75","To convert 3/4 to a decimal, we divide the numerator by the denominator: 3 ÷ 4 = 0.75.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-of-mixed-numbers-548dc6de","Math G6: Understanding and representing decimal fractions, recognizing - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-1-3 Understanding and representing decimal fractions, recognizing the place value of a digit in it, reading, writing, comparing, ordering, and rounding them, and converting between decimal fractions, Proper fractions and mixed numbers.","1. Distinguishing decimal fractions, representing them by using models, diagrams, and number lines, identifying the place value of digits, and rounding them to the nearest whole number or to a given place value.","Which of the following mixed numbers is equal to 1.25?","1 1/4","1 3/4","1 1/2","1 1/3","1 1/4","To convert 1.25 to a mixed number, we separate the whole number from the decimal: 1 + 0.25 = 1 1/4.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-decimal-fraction-is-78a9ff24","Math G6: Understanding and representing decimal fractions, recognizing - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-1-3 Understanding and representing decimal fractions, recognizing the place value of a digit in it, reading, writing, comparing, ordering, and rounding them, and converting between decimal fractions, Proper fractions and mixed numbers.","3. Comparing decimal fractions, ordering them in ascending and descending order, and converting between decimal fractions, Proper fractions, and mixed numbers.","Which decimal fraction is greater: 0.25 or 0.20?","0.20 is greater","0.25 is greater","They are equal","Not enough information","0.25 is greater","Since 0.25 has a larger second digit, it is greater than 0.20.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-of-decimal-fractions-9f79b1de","Math G6: Understanding and representing decimal fractions, recognizing - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-1-3 Understanding and representing decimal fractions, recognizing the place value of a digit in it, reading, writing, comparing, ordering, and rounding them, and converting between decimal fractions, Proper fractions and mixed numbers.","3. Comparing decimal fractions, ordering them in ascending and descending order, and converting between decimal fractions, Proper fractions, and mixed numbers.","Which of the following decimal fractions is equal to 3/8?","0.25","0.75","0.33","0.375","0.375","To convert 3/8 to a decimal, we divide the numerator by the denominator: 3 ÷ 8 = 0.375.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-of-mixed-numbers-40e0726f","Math G6: Understanding and representing decimal fractions, recognizing - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-1-3 Understanding and representing decimal fractions, recognizing the place value of a digit in it, reading, writing, comparing, ordering, and rounding them, and converting between decimal fractions, Proper fractions and mixed numbers.","1. Distinguishing decimal fractions, representing them by using models, diagrams, and number lines, identifying the place value of digits, and rounding them to the nearest whole number or to a given place value.","Which of the following mixed numbers is equal to 2.75?","2 1/4","2 3/4","2 1/2","2 1/3","2 3/4","To convert 2.75 to a mixed number, we separate the whole number from the decimal: 2 + 0.75 = 2 3/4.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-of-decimal-fractions-7be4e7b4","Math G6: Understanding and representing decimal fractions - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-6 Performing the four arithmetic operations on decimal fractions, numbers and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","2. Multiplying and dividing decimal fractions up to the hundredths place.","Which of the following decimal fractions is equal to 2.75?","Two point seven five","Two point seven zero five","Two point seven five zero","Two point seven","Two point seven five","The decimal fraction 2.75 is equal to two and seventy-five hundredths, which is written as Two point seven five.","/image-source/math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-of-decimal-fractions-7be4e7b4.png","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-of-decimal-fractions-a6b7bcb0","Math G6: Understanding and representing decimal fractions - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-6 Performing the four arithmetic operations on decimal fractions, numbers and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. Solving mathematical problems up to three steps that involve real-life applications of the four operations on decimal fractions and explaining their solutions.","Which of the following decimal fractions is equal to 3.5?","Three point five","Three point zero five","Three point five zero","Three point zero","Three point five","The decimal fraction 3.5 is equal to three and five tenths, which is written as Three point five.","/image-source/math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-of-decimal-fractions-a6b7bcb0.png","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-place-value-f7edc68e","Math G6: Understanding and representing decimal fractions - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-1-3 Understanding and representing decimal fractions, recognizing the place value of a digit in it, reading, writing, comparing, ordering, and rounding them, and converting between decimal fractions, Proper fractions and mixed numbers.","3. Comparing decimal fractions, ordering them in ascending and descending order, and converting between decimal fractions, Proper fractions, and mixed numbers.","What is the place value of the digit 7 in the decimal fraction 7.43?","Ones","Tenths","Hundredths","Thousands","Ones","The digit 7 is in the ones place in the decimal fraction 7.43.","/image-source/math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-place-value-f7edc68e.png","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-place-value-845838f3","Math G6: Understanding and representing decimal fractions - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-1-3 Understanding and representing decimal fractions, recognizing the place value of a digit in it, reading, writing, comparing, ordering, and rounding them, and converting between decimal fractions, Proper fractions and mixed numbers.","3. Comparing decimal fractions, ordering them in ascending and descending order, and converting between decimal fractions, Proper fractions, and mixed numbers.","What is the place value of the digit 9 in the decimal fraction 9.56?","Ones","Tenths","Hundredths","Thousands","Ones","The digit 9 is in the ones place in the decimal fraction 9.56.","/image-source/math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-place-value-845838f3.png","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-of-decimal-fractions-9e0b1d66","Math G6: Understanding and representing decimal fractions - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-7 Estimating the results of the four arithmetic operations on whole numbers, Proper fractions, and decimal fractions, and using mental calculation strategies to find results, and verifying the plausibility and accuracy of the results.","3. Applying the distributive property to multiply a two-digit number by a one-digit number mentally.","Which of the following decimal fractions is equal to 1.25?","One point two five","One point two zero five","One point two five zero","One point two","One point two five","The decimal fraction 1.25 is equal to one and twenty-five hundredths, which is written as One point two five.","/image-source/math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-of-decimal-fractions-9e0b1d66.png","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-place-value-3989d2e7","Math G6: Understanding and representing decimal fractions - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-1-3 Understanding and representing decimal fractions, recognizing the place value of a digit in it, reading, writing, comparing, ordering, and rounding them, and converting between decimal fractions, Proper fractions and mixed numbers.","3. Comparing decimal fractions, ordering them in ascending and descending order, and converting between decimal fractions, Proper fractions, and mixed numbers.","What is the place value of the digit 4 in the decimal fraction 4.27?","Tenths","Hundredths","Ones","Thousands","Hundredths","The digit 4 is in the hundredths place in the decimal fraction 4.27.","/image-source/math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-place-value-3989d2e7.png","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-decimal-fraction-6b0be2e5","Math G6: Understanding and representing decimal fractions - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-6 Performing the four arithmetic operations on decimal fractions, numbers and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","2. Multiplying and dividing decimal fractions up to the hundredths place.","What is the decimal fraction 0.25 equal to in words?","Twenty-five hundredths","Twenty-five tenths","Twenty-five ones","Twenty-five thousandths","Twenty-five hundredths","The decimal fraction 0.25 is equal to twenty-five hundredths.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-of-decimal-fractions-b040a009","Math G6: Understanding and representing decimal fractions - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-7 Estimating the results of the four arithmetic operations on whole numbers, Proper fractions, and decimal fractions, and using mental calculation strategies to find results, and verifying the plausibility and accuracy of the results.","2. Using mental calculation to find the results of multiplying and dividing a two-digit number by multiples of (10, 100, 1000).","Which of the following decimal fractions is equal to 2.9?","Two point nine","Two point nine zero","Two point nine five","Two point nine zero five","Two point nine","The decimal fraction 2.9 is equal to two and nine tenths, which is written as Two point nine.","/image-source/math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-of-decimal-fractions-b040a009.png","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-of-decimal-fractions-4f985390","Math G6: Understanding and representing decimal fractions - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-6 Performing the four arithmetic operations on decimal fractions, numbers and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. Solving mathematical problems up to three steps that involve real-life applications of the four operations on decimal fractions and explaining their solutions.","Which of the following decimal fractions is equal to 3.8?","Three point eight","Three point eight zero","Three point eight five","Three point eight zero five","Three point eight","The decimal fraction 3.8 is equal to three and eight tenths, which is written as Three point eight.","/image-source/math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-of-decimal-fractions-4f985390.png","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-decimal-fraction-f30a5489","Math G6: Understanding and representing decimal fractions - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-6 Performing the four arithmetic operations on decimal fractions, numbers and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","2. Multiplying and dividing decimal fractions up to the hundredths place.","What is the decimal fraction 0.5 equal to in words?","Five tenths","Five hundredths","Five thousandths","Five ones","Five tenths","The decimal fraction 0.5 is equal to five tenths.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-decimal-fraction-00fd2d95","Math G6: Understanding and representing decimal fractions - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-1-3 Understanding and representing decimal fractions, recognizing the place value of a digit in it, reading, writing, comparing, ordering, and rounding them, and converting between decimal fractions, Proper fractions and mixed numbers.","2. Reading decimal fractions and writing them in standard, verbal, and expanded forms.","What is the decimal fraction 0.9 written in expanded form?","nine tenths","nine hundredths","nine thousandths","nine ten-thousandths","nine tenths","The decimal fraction 0.9 can be written in expanded form as nine tenths because the 9 is in the tenths place.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-decimal-fraction-eeea8691","Math G6: Understanding and representing decimal fractions - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-1-3 Understanding and representing decimal fractions, recognizing the place value of a digit in it, reading, writing, comparing, ordering, and rounding them, and converting between decimal fractions, Proper fractions and mixed numbers.","2. Reading decimal fractions and writing them in standard, verbal, and expanded forms.","What is the decimal fraction 3.45 written in standard form?","three and forty-five hundredths","three and forty-five tenths","three point four five","three and forty-five thousandths","three and forty-five hundredths","The decimal fraction 3.45 can be written in standard form as three and forty-five hundredths because the 3 is a whole number, the 4 is in the tenths place, and the 5 is in the hundredths place.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-decimal-fraction-8eac1774","Math G6: Understanding and representing decimal fractions - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-1-3 Understanding and representing decimal fractions, recognizing the place value of a digit in it, reading, writing, comparing, ordering, and rounding them, and converting between decimal fractions, Proper fractions and mixed numbers.","1. Distinguishing decimal fractions, representing them by using models, diagrams, and number lines, identifying the place value of digits, and rounding them to the nearest whole number or to a given place value.","What is the decimal fraction 1.23 written in words?","one and twenty-three hundredths","one and twenty-three tenths","one point two three","one and twenty-three thousandths","one and twenty-three hundredths","The decimal fraction 1.23 can be read as one and twenty-three hundredths because the 1 is a whole number, the 2 is in the tenths place, and the 3 is in the hundredths place.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-decimal-fraction-f66766c2","Math G6: Understanding and representing decimal fractions - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-1-3 Understanding and representing decimal fractions, recognizing the place value of a digit in it, reading, writing, comparing, ordering, and rounding them, and converting between decimal fractions, Proper fractions and mixed numbers.","2. Reading decimal fractions and writing them in standard, verbal, and expanded forms.","What is the decimal fraction 0.92 written in expanded form?","ninety-two hundredths","ninety-two tenths","ninety-two thousandths","ninety-two ten-thousandths","ninety-two hundredths","The decimal fraction 0.92 can be written in expanded form as ninety-two hundredths because the 9 is in the tenths place and the 2 is in the hundredths place.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-decimal-fraction-635f7535","Math G6: Understanding and representing decimal fractions - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-1-3 Understanding and representing decimal fractions, recognizing the place value of a digit in it, reading, writing, comparing, ordering, and rounding them, and converting between decimal fractions, Proper fractions and mixed numbers.","2. Reading decimal fractions and writing them in standard, verbal, and expanded forms.","What is the decimal fraction 2.91 written in expanded form?","two and ninety-one hundredths","two and ninety-one tenths","two point nine one","two and ninety-one thousandths","two and ninety-one hundredths","The decimal fraction 2.91 can be written in expanded form as two and ninety-one hundredths because the 2 is a whole number, the 9 is in the tenths place, and the 1 is in the hundredths place.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-decimal-fraction-ed878a68","Math G6: Understanding and representing decimal fractions - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-1-3 Understanding and representing decimal fractions, recognizing the place value of a digit in it, reading, writing, comparing, ordering, and rounding them, and converting between decimal fractions, Proper fractions and mixed numbers.","3. Comparing decimal fractions, ordering them in ascending and descending order, and converting between decimal fractions, Proper fractions, and mixed numbers.","What is the decimal fraction 0.45 written in words?","forty-five hundredths","four hundred fifty thousandths","forty-five tenths","four and five tenths","forty-five hundredths","The decimal 0.45 has 45 in the hundredths place, so it is read as forty-five hundredths.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-decimal-fraction-6ddc2499","Math G6: Understanding and representing decimal fractions - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-1-3 Understanding and representing decimal fractions, recognizing the place value of a digit in it, reading, writing, comparing, ordering, and rounding them, and converting between decimal fractions, Proper fractions and mixed numbers.","2. Reading decimal fractions and writing them in standard, verbal, and expanded forms.","What is the decimal fraction 0.36 written in standard form?","thirty-six hundredths","thirty-six tenths","thirty-six thousandths","thirty-six ten-thousandths","thirty-six hundredths","The decimal fraction 0.36 can be written in standard form as thirty-six hundredths because the 3 is in the tenths place and the 6 is in the hundredths place.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-decimal-fraction-7dfa5267","Math G6: Understanding and representing decimal fractions - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-1-3 Understanding and representing decimal fractions, recognizing the place value of a digit in it, reading, writing, comparing, ordering, and rounding them, and converting between decimal fractions, Proper fractions and mixed numbers.","1. Distinguishing decimal fractions, representing them by using models, diagrams, and number lines, identifying the place value of digits, and rounding them to the nearest whole number or to a given place value.","What is the decimal fraction 1.75 written in words?","one and seventy-five hundredths","one and seventy-five tenths","one point seven five","one and seventy-five thousandths","one and seventy-five hundredths","The decimal fraction 1.75 can be read as one and seventy-five hundredths because the 1 is a whole number, the 7 is in the tenths place, and the 5 is in the hundredths place.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-decimal-fraction-7f890560","Math G6: Understanding and representing decimal fractions - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-1-3 Understanding and representing decimal fractions, recognizing the place value of a digit in it, reading, writing, comparing, ordering, and rounding them, and converting between decimal fractions, Proper fractions and mixed numbers.","3. Comparing decimal fractions, ordering them in ascending and descending order, and converting between decimal fractions, Proper fractions, and mixed numbers.","What is the decimal fraction 0.08 written in words?","eight hundredths","eight tenths","eight thousandths","eight ten-thousandths","eight hundredths","The decimal fraction 0.08 can be read as eight hundredths because the 0 is in the ones place and the 8 is in the hundredths place.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-decimal-fraction-287f0979","Math G6: Understanding and representing decimal fractions - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-1-3 Understanding and representing decimal fractions, recognizing the place value of a digit in it, reading, writing, comparing, ordering, and rounding them, and converting between decimal fractions, Proper fractions and mixed numbers.","2. Reading decimal fractions and writing them in standard, verbal, and expanded forms.","What is the decimal fraction 2.7 written in standard form?","two and seven tenths","two and seven hundredths","two point seven","two and seven thousandths","two and seven tenths","The decimal fraction 2.7 can be written in standard form as two and seven tenths because the 2 is a whole number and the 7 is in the tenths place.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-decimal-fraction-0499ca09","Math G6: Understanding and representing decimal fractions - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-1-3 Understanding and representing decimal fractions, recognizing the place value of a digit in it, reading, writing, comparing, ordering, and rounding them, and converting between decimal fractions, Proper fractions and mixed numbers.","1. Distinguishing decimal fractions, representing them by using models, diagrams, and number lines, identifying the place value of digits, and rounding them to the nearest whole number or to a given place value.","What is the decimal fraction equivalent of the mixed number 2 3/4?","2.25","2.5","2.75","3.25","2.75","To convert a mixed number to a decimal fraction, we convert the fraction part to decimal and add it to the whole number part. 3/4 = 0.75, so 2 3/4 = 2 + 0.75 = 2.75.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-of-decimal-fractions-cf29f05c","Math G6: Understanding and representing decimal fractions - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-1-3 Understanding and representing decimal fractions, recognizing the place value of a digit in it, reading, writing, comparing, ordering, and rounding them, and converting between decimal fractions, Proper fractions and mixed numbers.","3. Comparing decimal fractions, ordering them in ascending and descending order, and converting between decimal fractions, Proper fractions, and mixed numbers.","Which of the following decimal fractions is the largest: 0.45, 0.35, 0.5, or 0.25?","0.25","0.35","0.45","0.5","0.5","0.5 is larger than 0.45, 0.35, and 0.25.","/image-source/math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-of-decimal-fractions-cf29f05c.png","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-mixed-number-7c6ea7ad","Math G6: Understanding and representing decimal fractions - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-1-3 Understanding and representing decimal fractions, recognizing the place value of a digit in it, reading, writing, comparing, ordering, and rounding them, and converting between decimal fractions, Proper fractions and mixed numbers.","1. Distinguishing decimal fractions, representing them by using models, diagrams, and number lines, identifying the place value of digits, and rounding them to the nearest whole number or to a given place value.","What is the mixed number equivalent of the decimal fraction 1.25?","1 1/4","1 1/5","1 1/3","1 1/2","1 1/4","To convert a decimal fraction to a mixed number, we separate the whole number part from the decimal part and convert the decimal part to a fraction. 0.25 = 1/4, so 1.25 = 1 1/4.","/image-source/math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-mixed-number-7c6ea7ad.png","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-arrange-decimal-fractions-8d90285a","Math G6: Understanding and representing decimal fractions - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-1-3 Understanding and representing decimal fractions, recognizing the place value of a digit in it, reading, writing, comparing, ordering, and rounding them, and converting between decimal fractions, Proper fractions and mixed numbers.","3. Comparing decimal fractions, ordering them in ascending and descending order, and converting between decimal fractions, Proper fractions, and mixed numbers.","Arrange the decimal fractions 0.1, 0.01, and 0.001 in ascending order.","0.001, 0.01, 0.1","0.01, 0.1, 0.001","0.1, 0.01, 0.001","0.001, 0.1, 0.01","0.001, 0.01, 0.1","The decimal fractions in ascending order are 0.001, 0.01, and 0.1.","/image-source/math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-arrange-decimal-fractions-8d90285a.png","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-decimal-fraction-9bdba681","Math G6: Understanding and representing decimal fractions - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-1-3 Understanding and representing decimal fractions, recognizing the place value of a digit in it, reading, writing, comparing, ordering, and rounding them, and converting between decimal fractions, Proper fractions and mixed numbers.","3. Comparing decimal fractions, ordering them in ascending and descending order, and converting between decimal fractions, Proper fractions, and mixed numbers.","What is the decimal fraction equivalent of the proper fraction 3/5?","0.5","0.6","0.7","0.8","0.6","To convert a proper fraction to a decimal fraction, we divide the numerator by the denominator. 3/5 = 3 ÷ 5 = 0.6.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-of-is-smallest-a7e87e0d","Math G6: Understanding and representing decimal fractions - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-1-3 Understanding and representing decimal fractions, recognizing the place value of a digit in it, reading, writing, comparing, ordering, and rounding them, and converting between decimal fractions, Proper fractions and mixed numbers.","3. Comparing decimal fractions, ordering them in ascending and descending order, and converting between decimal fractions, Proper fractions, and mixed numbers.","Which of the following is the smallest decimal fraction: 0.9, 0.09, 0.99, or 0.009?","0.009","0.09","0.9","0.99","0.009","0.009 is smaller than 0.09, 0.9, and 0.99.","/image-source/math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-of-is-smallest-a7e87e0d.png","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-decimal-fraction-3b3be85a","Math G6: Understanding and representing decimal fractions - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-1-3 Understanding and representing decimal fractions, recognizing the place value of a digit in it, reading, writing, comparing, ordering, and rounding them, and converting between decimal fractions, Proper fractions and mixed numbers.","3. Comparing decimal fractions, ordering them in ascending and descending order, and converting between decimal fractions, Proper fractions, and mixed numbers.","What is the decimal fraction equivalent of the proper fraction 2/5?","0.3","0.4","0.5","0.6","0.4","To convert a proper fraction to a decimal fraction, we divide the numerator by the denominator. 2/5 = 2 ÷ 5 = 0.4.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-of-decimal-fractions-419a50fd","Math G6: Understanding and representing decimal fractions - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-1-3 Understanding and representing decimal fractions, recognizing the place value of a digit in it, reading, writing, comparing, ordering, and rounding them, and converting between decimal fractions, Proper fractions and mixed numbers.","3. Comparing decimal fractions, ordering them in ascending and descending order, and converting between decimal fractions, Proper fractions, and mixed numbers.","Which of the following decimal fractions is the smallest: 0.05, 0.5, 0.005, or 0.55?","0.005","0.05","0.5","0.55","0.005","0.005 is smaller than 0.05, 0.5, and 0.55.","/image-source/math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-of-decimal-fractions-419a50fd.png","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-arrange-decimal-fractions-801cc997","Math G6: Understanding and representing decimal fractions - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-1-3 Understanding and representing decimal fractions, recognizing the place value of a digit in it, reading, writing, comparing, ordering, and rounding them, and converting between decimal fractions, Proper fractions and mixed numbers.","3. Comparing decimal fractions, ordering them in ascending and descending order, and converting between decimal fractions, Proper fractions, and mixed numbers.","Arrange the decimal fractions 0.8, 0.08, and 0.008 in descending order.","0.8, 0.08, 0.008","0.008, 0.08, 0.8","0.08, 0.008, 0.8","0.008, 0.8, 0.08","0.8, 0.08, 0.008","The decimal fractions in descending order are 0.8, 0.08, and 0.008.","/image-source/math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-arrange-decimal-fractions-801cc997.png","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-proper-fraction-fe3aba41","Math G6: Understanding and representing decimal fractions - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-1-3 Understanding and representing decimal fractions, recognizing the place value of a digit in it, reading, writing, comparing, ordering, and rounding them, and converting between decimal fractions, Proper fractions and mixed numbers.","3. Comparing decimal fractions, ordering them in ascending and descending order, and converting between decimal fractions, Proper fractions, and mixed numbers.","What is the proper fraction equivalent of the decimal fraction 0.75?","3/4","1/4","2/3","4/3","3/4","The correct fraction is 3/4.","/image-source/math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-proper-fraction-fe3aba41.png","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-of-numbers-is-2ae86e84","Math G6: Understanding and representing the place value of numbers - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-1-1 Understanding and representing the place value of numbers up to 12 digits, reading, writing, comparing, Ordering, rounding them.","3. Comparing and Ordering numbers up to 12 digits by using symbols (>, <, =) in ascending and descending order.","Which of the following numbers is greater than 987,654,321,098?","987,654,321,097","987,654,321,099","987,654,321,100","987,654,321,101","987,654,321,099","987,654,321,099 is greater than 987,654,321,098.","/image-source/math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-of-numbers-is-2ae86e84.png","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-value-of-e7ea9afd","Math G6: Understanding and representing the place value of numbers - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-1-1 Understanding and representing the place value of numbers up to 12 digits, reading, writing, comparing, Ordering, rounding them.","3. Comparing and Ordering numbers up to 12 digits by using symbols (>, <, =) in ascending and descending order.","What is the value of the digit 9 in the number 9,876,543,210,987?","Nine billion","Nine million","Nine hundred billion","Nine hundred million","Nine billion","The digit 9 is in the billions place.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-of-numbers-is-de690c9f","Math G6: Understanding and representing the place value of numbers - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-1-1 Understanding and representing the place value of numbers up to 12 digits, reading, writing, comparing, Ordering, rounding them.","3. Comparing and Ordering numbers up to 12 digits by using symbols (>, <, =) in ascending and descending order.","Which of the following numbers is less than 123,456,789,012?","123,456,789,013","123,456,789,011","123,456,789,012","123,456,789,014","123,456,789,011","123,456,789,011 is less than 123,456,789,012.","/image-source/math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-of-numbers-is-de690c9f.png","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-round-number-9-80e1aa4c","Math G6: Understanding and representing the place value of numbers - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-1-2 Distinguishing Proper fractions, mixed numbers, and improper fractions, representing, reading, writing, comparing, ordering, and rounding them.","2. Finding equivalent fractions, converting them to their simplest form, and rounding them to the nearest whole, half, or one.","Round the number 9,876,543,210 to the nearest million.","10,000,000,000","9,000,000,000","10,000,000","9,877,000,000","10,000,000,000","Rounding 9,876,543,210 to the nearest million gives 10,000,000,000.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-round-number-987-3697c781","Math G6: Understanding and representing the place value of numbers - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-1-2 Distinguishing Proper fractions, mixed numbers, and improper fractions, representing, reading, writing, comparing, ordering, and rounding them.","2. Finding equivalent fractions, converting them to their simplest form, and rounding them to the nearest whole, half, or one.","Round the number 987,654,321,098 to the nearest billion.","1,000,000,000,000","987,000,000,000","988,000,000,000","986,000,000,000","1,000,000,000,000","Rounding 987,654,321,098 to the nearest billion gives 1,000,000,000,000.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-of-numbers-is-b42b85e2","Math G6: Understanding and representing the place value of numbers - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-1-2 Distinguishing Proper fractions, mixed numbers, and improper fractions, representing, reading, writing, comparing, ordering, and rounding them.","2. Finding equivalent fractions, converting them to their simplest form, and rounding them to the nearest whole, half, or one.","Which of the following numbers is equal to 5,678,901,234,567 rounded to the nearest million?","5,679,000,000","5,678,000,000","5,677,000,000","5,680,000,000","5,679,000,000","Rounding 5,678,901,234,567 to the nearest million gives 5,679,000,000.","/image-source/math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-of-numbers-is-b42b85e2.png","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-place-value-e7743e02","Math G6: Understanding and representing the place value of numbers - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-1-1 Understanding and representing the place value of numbers up to 12 digits, reading, writing, comparing, Ordering, rounding them.","3. Comparing and Ordering numbers up to 12 digits by using symbols (>, <, =) in ascending and descending order.","What is the place value of the digit 3 in the number 3,456,789,012,345?","Three hundred billion","Three billion","Three million","Three trillion","Three trillion","The digit 3 is in the trillions place.","/image-source/math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-place-value-e7743e02.png","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-place-value-3ce124fe","Math G6: Understanding and representing the place value of numbers - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-1-1 Understanding and representing the place value of numbers up to 12 digits, reading, writing, comparing, Ordering, rounding them.","3. Comparing and Ordering numbers up to 12 digits by using symbols (>, <, =) in ascending and descending order.","What is the place value of the digit 5 in the number 456,789,012,345?","Five hundred billion","Five billion","Five million","Five hundred thousand","Five billion","The digit 5 is in the billions place.","/image-source/math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-place-value-3ce124fe.png","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-place-value-064cd4ff","Math G6: Understanding and representing the place value of numbers - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-1-1 Understanding and representing the place value of numbers up to 12 digits, reading, writing, comparing, Ordering, rounding them.","3. Comparing and Ordering numbers up to 12 digits by using symbols (>, <, =) in ascending and descending order.","What is the place value of the digit 1 in the number 1,234,567,890,123?","One hundred billion","One billion","One hundred million","One trillion","One trillion","In 1,234,567,890,123, the first digit 1 is in the trillions place.","/image-source/math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-place-value-064cd4ff.png","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-round-number-123-c7118f72","Math G6: Understanding and representing the place value of numbers - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-1-2 Distinguishing Proper fractions, mixed numbers, and improper fractions, representing, reading, writing, comparing, ordering, and rounding them.","2. Finding equivalent fractions, converting them to their simplest form, and rounding them to the nearest whole, half, or one.","Round the number 123,456,789,012 to the nearest ten million.","120,000,000,000","110,000,000,000","130,000,000,000","100,000,000,000","120,000,000,000","Rounding 123,456,789,012 to the nearest ten million gives 120,000,000,000.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-round-number-456-2201e287","Math G6: Understanding and representing the place value of numbers - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-1-1 Understanding and representing the place value of numbers up to 12 digits, reading, writing, comparing, Ordering, rounding them.","3. Comparing and Ordering numbers up to 12 digits by using symbols (>, <, =) in ascending and descending order.","Round the number 456,789 to the nearest thousand.","456,000","457,000","456,700","456,800","456,000","Rounding 456,789 to the nearest thousand gives 456,000 because the digit in the ones place is less than 5.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-of-numbers-is-e44e928a","Math G6: Understanding and representing the place value of numbers - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-1-1 Understanding and representing the place value of numbers up to 12 digits, reading, writing, comparing, Ordering, rounding them.","3. Comparing and Ordering numbers up to 12 digits by using symbols (>, <, =) in ascending and descending order.","Which of the following numbers is equal to 456,789?","456,780","456,790","456,700","456,800","456,780","The number 456,780 is not equal to 456,789 because it has a smaller digit in the ones place.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-round-number-456-55409189","Math G6: Understanding and representing the place value of numbers - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-1-1 Understanding and representing the place value of numbers up to 12 digits, reading, writing, comparing, Ordering, rounding them.","3. Comparing and Ordering numbers up to 12 digits by using symbols (>, <, =) in ascending and descending order.","Round the number 456,789 to the nearest hundred.","456,700","456,800","456,780","456,790","456,700","Rounding 456,789 to the nearest hundred gives 456,700 because the digit in the ones place is less than 5.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-value-of-c0fd047c","Math G6: Understanding and representing the place value of numbers - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-1-1 Understanding and representing the place value of numbers up to 12 digits, reading, writing, comparing, Ordering, rounding them.","3. Comparing and Ordering numbers up to 12 digits by using symbols (>, <, =) in ascending and descending order.","What is the value of the digit 7 in the number 456,789?","7 hundreds","7 thousands","7 tens","7 ones","7 ones","The digit 7 is in the ones place because it is the rightmost digit.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-write-number-456-e3995434","Math G6: Understanding and representing the place value of numbers - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-1-3 Understanding and representing decimal fractions, recognizing the place value of a digit in it, reading, writing, comparing, ordering, and rounding them, and converting between decimal fractions, Proper fractions and mixed numbers.","2. Reading decimal fractions and writing them in standard, verbal, and expanded forms.","Write the number 456,789 in expanded form.","400,000 + 50,000 + 6,000 + 700 + 80 + 9.","400,000 + 50,000 + 6,000 + 700 + 80 + 9,000.","400,000 + 50,000 + 6,000 + 700 + 80 + 90.","400,000 + 50,000 + 6,000 + 700 + 800 + 90.","400,000 + 50,000 + 6,000 + 700 + 80 + 9.","The correct expanded form of the number 456,789 is 400,000 + 50,000 + 6,000 + 700 + 80 + 9.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-write-number-456-e4a9018f","Math G6: Understanding and representing the place value of numbers - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-1-1 Understanding and representing the place value of numbers up to 12 digits, reading, writing, comparing, Ordering, rounding them.","2. Reading and writing numbers up to 12 digits in standard, verbal, and analytical forms.","Write the number 456,789 in standard form.","456,789","456,780","456,790","456,700","456,789","The correct standard form of the number 456,789 is 456,789.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-of-numbers-is-73e686ef","Math G6: Understanding and representing the place value of numbers - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-1-1 Understanding and representing the place value of numbers up to 12 digits, reading, writing, comparing, Ordering, rounding them.","3. Comparing and Ordering numbers up to 12 digits by using symbols (>, <, =) in ascending and descending order.","Which of the following numbers is less than 456,789?","456,780","456,790","456,700","456,800","456,700","The number 456,700 is less than 456,789 because it has a smaller digit in the ones place.","/image-source/math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-of-numbers-is-73e686ef.png","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-of-numbers-is-4446fb16","Math G6: Understanding and representing the place value of numbers - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-1-1 Understanding and representing the place value of numbers up to 12 digits, reading, writing, comparing, Ordering, rounding them.","3. Comparing and Ordering numbers up to 12 digits by using symbols (>, <, =) in ascending and descending order.","Which of the following numbers is greater than 456,789?","456,780","456,790","456,700","456,800","456,790","The number 456,790 is greater than 456,789 because it has a larger digit in the ones place.","/image-source/math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-of-numbers-is-4446fb16.png","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-write-number-456-982d83ba","Math G6: Understanding and representing the place value of numbers - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-1-1 Understanding and representing the place value of numbers up to 12 digits, reading, writing, comparing, Ordering, rounding them.","3. Comparing and Ordering numbers up to 12 digits by using symbols (>, <, =) in ascending and descending order.","Write the number 456,789 in words.","Four hundred fifty-six thousand seven hundred eighty-nine.","Four hundred fifty-six thousand seven hundred eighty-nine thousand.","Four hundred fifty-six thousand seven hundred eight thousand nine hundred.","Four hundred fifty-six thousand seven hundred eighty-nine thousand nine hundred.","Four hundred fifty-six thousand seven hundred eighty-nine.","The correct spelling of the number 456,789 in words is Four hundred fifty-six thousand seven hundred eighty-nine.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-place-value-f31f0b0c","Math G6: Understanding and representing the place value of numbers - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-1-1 Understanding and representing the place value of numbers up to 12 digits, reading, writing, comparing, Ordering, rounding them.","3. Comparing and Ordering numbers up to 12 digits by using symbols (>, <, =) in ascending and descending order.","What is the place value of the digit 4 in the number 456,789?","The digit 4 is in the thousands place.","The digit 4 is in the hundreds place.","The digit 4 is in the tens place.","The digit 4 is in the ones place.","The digit 4 is in the tens place.","The digit 4 is in the tens place because it is between the 5 and the 9.","/image-source/math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-place-value-f31f0b0c.png","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-of-numbers-is-6ad26981","Math G6: Understanding and representing the place value of numbers - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-1-1 Understanding and representing the place value of numbers up to 12 digits, reading, writing, comparing, Ordering, rounding them.","3. Comparing and Ordering numbers up to 12 digits by using symbols (>, <, =) in ascending and descending order.","Which of the following numbers is the smallest: 123,456,789, 123,456,788, or 123,456,790?","123,456,790 is the smallest number","123,456,789 is the smallest number","123,456,788 is the smallest number","We cannot compare these numbers","123,456,788 is the smallest number","Since 123,456,788 has the smallest value, it is the smallest number.","/image-source/math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-of-numbers-is-6ad26981.png","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-compare-numbers-1-5274a266","Math G6: Understanding and representing the place value of numbers - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-1-1 Understanding and representing the place value of numbers up to 12 digits, reading, writing, comparing, Ordering, rounding them.","3. Comparing and Ordering numbers up to 12 digits by using symbols (>, <, =) in ascending and descending order.","Compare the numbers 1,234,567,890 and 1,234,567,891.","1,234,567,890 is greater than 1,234,567,891","1,234,567,891 is greater than 1,234,567,890","Both numbers are equal","We cannot compare these numbers","1,234,567,891 is greater than 1,234,567,890","Since 1,234,567,891 has a greater value than 1,234,567,890, it is the greater number.","/image-source/math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-compare-numbers-1-5274a266.png","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-arrange-numbers-9-5c0aacd4","Math G6: Understanding and representing the place value of numbers - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-1-1 Understanding and representing the place value of numbers up to 12 digits, reading, writing, comparing, Ordering, rounding them.","3. Comparing and Ordering numbers up to 12 digits by using symbols (>, <, =) in ascending and descending order.","Arrange the numbers 9,876,543,210, 9,876,543,211, and 9,876,543,209 in ascending order.","9,876,543,211, 9,876,543,210, 9,876,543,209","9,876,543,209, 9,876,543,210, 9,876,543,211","9,876,543,210, 9,876,543,211, 9,876,543,209","9,876,543,211, 9,876,543,209, 9,876,543,210","9,876,543,209, 9,876,543,210, 9,876,543,211","In ascending order, the smallest number comes first, so the correct order is 9,876,543,209, 9,876,543,210, 9,876,543,211.","/image-source/math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-arrange-numbers-9-5c0aacd4.png","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-of-is-true-6a4bc568","Math G6: Understanding and representing the place value of numbers - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-1-1 Understanding and representing the place value of numbers up to 12 digits, reading, writing, comparing, Ordering, rounding them.","3. Comparing and Ordering numbers up to 12 digits by using symbols (>, <, =) in ascending and descending order.","Which of the following is true: 5,432,109,876 is greater than, less than, or equal to 5,432,109,875?","5,432,109,876 is less than 5,432,109,875","5,432,109,876 is equal to 5,432,109,875","5,432,109,876 is greater than 5,432,109,875","We cannot compare these numbers","5,432,109,876 is greater than 5,432,109,875","Since 5,432,109,876 has a greater value than 5,432,109,875, it is the greater number.","/image-source/math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-of-is-true-6a4bc568.png","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-of-numbers-is-8ded21b4","Math G6: Understanding and representing the place value of numbers - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-1-1 Understanding and representing the place value of numbers up to 12 digits, reading, writing, comparing, Ordering, rounding them.","3. Comparing and Ordering numbers up to 12 digits by using symbols (>, <, =) in ascending and descending order.","Which of the following numbers is greater, 456,789,012 or 456,789,011?","456,789,012 is greater than 456,789,011","456,789,011 is greater than 456,789,012","Both numbers are equal","We cannot compare these numbers","456,789,012 is greater than 456,789,011","Since 456,789,012 has a greater value than 456,789,011, it is the greater number.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-arrange-numbers-123-1b3cf9e3","Math G6: Understanding and representing the place value of numbers - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-1-1 Understanding and representing the place value of numbers up to 12 digits, reading, writing, comparing, Ordering, rounding them.","3. Comparing and Ordering numbers up to 12 digits by using symbols (>, <, =) in ascending and descending order.","Arrange the numbers 123,456,789, 123,456,788, and 123,456,790 in ascending order.","123,456,790, 123,456,789, 123,456,788","123,456,788, 123,456,789, 123,456,790","123,456,789, 123,456,788, 123,456,790","123,456,790, 123,456,788, 123,456,789","123,456,788, 123,456,789, 123,456,790","In ascending order, the smallest number comes first, so the correct order is 123,456,788, 123,456,789, 123,456,790.","/image-source/math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-arrange-numbers-123-1b3cf9e3.png","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-of-is-true-9299a53d","Math G6: Understanding and representing the place value of numbers - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-1-1 Understanding and representing the place value of numbers up to 12 digits, reading, writing, comparing, Ordering, rounding them.","3. Comparing and Ordering numbers up to 12 digits by using symbols (>, <, =) in ascending and descending order.","Which of the following is true: 987,654,321 is greater than, less than, or equal to 987,654,320?","987,654,321 is less than 987,654,320","987,654,321 is equal to 987,654,320","987,654,321 is greater than 987,654,320","We cannot compare these numbers","987,654,321 is greater than 987,654,320","Since 987,654,321 has a greater value than 987,654,320, it is the greater number.","/image-source/math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-of-is-true-9299a53d.png","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-compare-numbers-999-82ebce7a","Math G6: Understanding and representing the place value of numbers - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-1-1 Understanding and representing the place value of numbers up to 12 digits, reading, writing, comparing, Ordering, rounding them.","3. Comparing and Ordering numbers up to 12 digits by using symbols (>, <, =) in ascending and descending order.","Compare the numbers 999,999,999 and 1,000,000,000.","999,999,999 is greater than 1,000,000,000","1,000,000,000 is greater than 999,999,999","Both numbers are equal","We cannot compare these numbers","1,000,000,000 is greater than 999,999,999","Since 1,000,000,000 has a greater value than 999,999,999, it is the greater number.","/image-source/math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-compare-numbers-999-82ebce7a.png","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-compare-numbers-7-538e7c07","Math G6: Understanding and representing the place value of numbers - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-1-1 Understanding and representing the place value of numbers up to 12 digits, reading, writing, comparing, Ordering, rounding them.","3. Comparing and Ordering numbers up to 12 digits by using symbols (>, <, =) in ascending and descending order.","Compare the numbers 7,654,321,098 and 7,654,321,099.","7,654,321,098 is greater than 7,654,321,099","7,654,321,099 is greater than 7,654,321,098","Both numbers are equal","We cannot compare these numbers","7,654,321,099 is greater than 7,654,321,098","Since 7,654,321,099 has a greater value than 7,654,321,098, it is the greater number.","/image-source/math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-compare-numbers-7-538e7c07.png","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-arrange-numbers-12-051b4725","Math G6: Understanding and representing the place value of numbers - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-1-1 Understanding and representing the place value of numbers up to 12 digits, reading, writing, comparing, Ordering, rounding them.","3. Comparing and Ordering numbers up to 12 digits by using symbols (>, <, =) in ascending and descending order.","Arrange the numbers 12,345,678,901, 12,345,678,902, and 12,345,678,900 in descending order.","12,345,678,902, 12,345,678,901, 12,345,678,900","12,345,678,900, 12,345,678,901, 12,345,678,902","12,345,678,901, 12,345,678,900, 12,345,678,902","12,345,678,902, 12,345,678,900, 12,345,678,901","12,345,678,902, 12,345,678,901, 12,345,678,900","In descending order, the largest number comes first, so the correct order is 12,345,678,902, 12,345,678,901, 12,345,678,900.","/image-source/math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-arrange-numbers-12-051b4725.png","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-round-number-456-5ce099cd","Math G6: Understanding and representing the place value - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-1-2 Distinguishing Proper fractions, mixed numbers, and improper fractions, representing, reading, writing, comparing, ordering, and rounding them.","2. Finding equivalent fractions, converting them to their simplest form, and rounding them to the nearest whole, half, or one.","Round the number 456,789 to the nearest ten thousand.","450,000","460,000","470,000","480,000","460,000","Rounding 456,789 to the nearest ten thousand gives 460,000 because 456,789 is closer to 460,000 than 450,000.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-of-numbers-is-aac7eaad","Math G6: Understanding and representing the place value - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-1-1 Understanding and representing the place value of numbers up to 12 digits, reading, writing, comparing, Ordering, rounding them.","3. Comparing and Ordering numbers up to 12 digits by using symbols (>, <, =) in ascending and descending order.","Which of the following numbers is equal to 456,789?","456,780","456,789","456,790","456,781","456,789","The number 456,789 is equal to itself.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-write-number-456-89d13fd3","Math G6: Understanding and representing the place value - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-1-1 Understanding and representing the place value of numbers up to 12 digits, reading, writing, comparing, Ordering, rounding them.","2. Reading and writing numbers up to 12 digits in standard, verbal, and analytical forms.","Write the number 456,789 in standard form.","456,789","456,780","456,790","456,781","456,789","The correct standard form of the number 456,789 is 456,789.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-place-value-c413a1a2","Math G6: Understanding and representing the place value - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-1-1 Understanding and representing the place value of numbers up to 12 digits, reading, writing, comparing, Ordering, rounding them.","3. Comparing and Ordering numbers up to 12 digits by using symbols (>, <, =) in ascending and descending order.","What is the place value of the digit 4 in the number 456,789?","The place value of 4 is in the thousands place.","The place value of 4 is in the hundreds place.","The place value of 4 is in the tens place.","The place value of 4 is in the ones place.","The place value of 4 is in the tens place.","The place value of 4 is in the tens place because it is in the 10s column.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-read-number-456-4386feae","Math G6: Understanding and representing the place value - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-6 Performing the four arithmetic operations on decimal fractions, numbers and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Adding and subtracting decimal fractions up to a thousand place.","Read the number 456,789.","Four hundred fifty-six thousand seven hundred eighty-nine.","Four hundred fifty-six thousand seven hundred eighty-nine thousand.","Four hundred fifty-six thousand seven hundred eighty-nine dollars.","Four hundred fifty-six thousand seven hundred eighty-nine cents.","Four hundred fifty-six thousand seven hundred eighty-nine.","The correct reading of the number 456,789 is Four hundred fifty-six thousand seven hundred eighty-nine.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-write-number-456-ffa24862","Math G6: Understanding and representing the place value - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-1-3 Understanding and representing decimal fractions, recognizing the place value of a digit in it, reading, writing, comparing, ordering, and rounding them, and converting between decimal fractions, Proper fractions and mixed numbers.","2. Reading decimal fractions and writing them in standard, verbal, and expanded forms.","Write the number 456,789 in expanded form.","400,000 + 50,000 + 6,000 + 700 + 80 + 9.","360,000","440,000","4,000,000","400,000 + 50,000 + 6,000 + 700 + 80 + 9.","The correct expanded form of the number 456,789 is 400,000 + 50,000 + 6,000 + 700 + 80 + 9.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-round-number-456-37852a68","Math G6: Understanding and representing the place value - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-1-2 Distinguishing Proper fractions, mixed numbers, and improper fractions, representing, reading, writing, comparing, ordering, and rounding them.","2. Finding equivalent fractions, converting them to their simplest form, and rounding them to the nearest whole, half, or one.","Round the number 456,789 to the nearest hundred thousand.","400,000","450,000","500,000","600,000","450,000","Rounding 456,789 to the nearest hundred thousand gives 450,000 because 456,789 is closer to 450,000 than 400,000.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-of-numbers-is-27ccc70d","Math G6: Understanding and representing the place value - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-1-1 Understanding and representing the place value of numbers up to 12 digits, reading, writing, comparing, Ordering, rounding them.","3. Comparing and Ordering numbers up to 12 digits by using symbols (>, <, =) in ascending and descending order.","Which of the following numbers is greater than 456,789?","456,780","456,790","456,789","456,781","456,790","The number 456,790 is greater than 456,789 because it has a higher value in the tens place.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-write-number-456-4f513197","Math G6: Understanding and representing the place value - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-1-1 Understanding and representing the place value of numbers up to 12 digits, reading, writing, comparing, Ordering, rounding them.","2. Reading and writing numbers up to 12 digits in standard, verbal, and analytical forms.","Write the number 456,789 in words.","Four hundred fifty-six thousand seven hundred eighty-nine.","Four hundred fifty-six thousand seven hundred ninety-eight.","Four hundred fifty-six thousand seven hundred eighty-nine thousand.","Four hundred fifty-six thousand seven hundred eighty-nine dollars.","Four hundred fifty-six thousand seven hundred eighty-nine.","The correct word form of the number 456,789 is Four hundred fifty-six thousand seven hundred eighty-nine.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-compare-numbers-456-07347b76","Math G6: Understanding and representing the place value - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-1-1 Understanding and representing the place value of numbers up to 12 digits, reading, writing, comparing, Ordering, rounding them.","3. Comparing and Ordering numbers up to 12 digits by using symbols (>, <, =) in ascending and descending order.","Compare the numbers 456,789 and 456,780.","456,789 is greater than 456,780.","456,789 is less than 456,780.","456,789 is equal to 456,780.","The numbers are not comparable.","456,789 is greater than 456,780.","The number 456,789 is greater than 456,780 because it has a higher value in the ones place.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-box-contains-f2e57daf","Math G6: Understanding a random experiment - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-4-3-1 Understanding a random experiment, determining its outcomes, expressing the probabilities of their occurrence, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Finding all possible outcomes of a random experiment and their number by using tables, organized lists, tree diagrams, and the counting principle.","A box contains 5 yellow balls and 3 blue balls. What is the total number of possible outcomes if you randomly select one ball?","The total number of possible outcomes is 6","The total number of possible outcomes is 8","The total number of possible outcomes is 10","The total number of possible outcomes is 12","The total number of possible outcomes is 8","Adding the frequencies gives a total of 8 observations.","/image-source/math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-box-contains-f2e57daf.png","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-deck-of-f60b7ff0","Math G6: Understanding a random experiment - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-4-3-1 Understanding a random experiment, determining its outcomes, expressing the probabilities of their occurrence, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Finding all possible outcomes of a random experiment and their number by using tables, organized lists, tree diagrams, and the counting principle.","A deck of cards has 52 cards. What is the total number of possible outcomes if you randomly draw one card?","The total number of possible outcomes is 25","The total number of possible outcomes is 50","The total number of possible outcomes is 52","The total number of possible outcomes is 100","The total number of possible outcomes is 52","Since the deck has 52 cards, there are 52 possible outcomes when you randomly draw one card.","/image-source/math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-deck-of-f60b7ff0.png","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-spinner-has-70781656","Math G6: Understanding a random experiment - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-4-3-1 Understanding a random experiment, determining its outcomes, expressing the probabilities of their occurrence, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Finding all possible outcomes of a random experiment and their number by using tables, organized lists, tree diagrams, and the counting principle.","A spinner has 6 equal sections, numbered 1 to 6. What is the total number of possible outcomes if you randomly spin the spinner?","The total number of possible outcomes is 4","The total number of possible outcomes is 6","The total number of possible outcomes is 8","The total number of possible outcomes is 10","The total number of possible outcomes is 6","Since the spinner has 6 equal sections, there are 6 possible outcomes when you randomly spin it.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-spinner-has-bb1c38b1","Math G6: Understanding a random experiment - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-4-3-1 Understanding a random experiment, determining its outcomes, expressing the probabilities of their occurrence, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Finding all possible outcomes of a random experiment and their number by using tables, organized lists, tree diagrams, and the counting principle.","A spinner has 8 equal sections, numbered 1 to 8. What is the total number of possible outcomes if you randomly spin the spinner?","The total number of possible outcomes is 4","The total number of possible outcomes is 6","The total number of possible outcomes is 8","The total number of possible outcomes is 10","The total number of possible outcomes is 8","Since the spinner has 8 equal sections, there are 8 possible outcomes when you randomly spin it.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-bag-contains-9338fad2","Math G6: Understanding a random experiment - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-4-3-1 Understanding a random experiment, determining its outcomes, expressing the probabilities of their occurrence, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Finding all possible outcomes of a random experiment and their number by using tables, organized lists, tree diagrams, and the counting principle.","A bag contains 2 red apples and 4 green apples. What is the total number of possible outcomes if you randomly select one apple?","The total number of possible outcomes is 4","The total number of possible outcomes is 6","The total number of possible outcomes is 8","The total number of possible outcomes is 10","The total number of possible outcomes is 6","To find the total number of possible outcomes, we add the number of red and green apples: 2 + 4 = 6.","/image-source/math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-bag-contains-9338fad2.png","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-bag-contains-fa800d0c","Math G6: Understanding a random experiment - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-4-3-1 Understanding a random experiment, determining its outcomes, expressing the probabilities of their occurrence, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Finding all possible outcomes of a random experiment and their number by using tables, organized lists, tree diagrams, and the counting principle.","A bag contains 3 red marbles, 2 blue marbles, and 5 green marbles. What is the total number of possible outcomes if you randomly select one marble?","The total number of possible outcomes is 10","The total number of possible outcomes is 15","The total number of possible outcomes is 20","The total number of possible outcomes is 25","The total number of possible outcomes is 10","Adding the frequencies gives a total of 10 observations.","/image-source/math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-bag-contains-fa800d0c.png","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-box-contains-397ad5fe","Math G6: Understanding a random experiment - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-4-3-1 Understanding a random experiment, determining its outcomes, expressing the probabilities of their occurrence, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Finding all possible outcomes of a random experiment and their number by using tables, organized lists, tree diagrams, and the counting principle.","A box contains 4 yellow balls and 2 blue balls. What is the total number of possible outcomes if you randomly select one ball?","The total number of possible outcomes is 4","The total number of possible outcomes is 6","The total number of possible outcomes is 8","The total number of possible outcomes is 10","The total number of possible outcomes is 6","Adding the frequencies gives a total of 6 observations.","/image-source/math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-box-contains-397ad5fe.png","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-bag-contains-dd6fd61a","Math G6: Understanding a random experiment - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-4-3-1 Understanding a random experiment, determining its outcomes, expressing the probabilities of their occurrence, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Finding all possible outcomes of a random experiment and their number by using tables, organized lists, tree diagrams, and the counting principle.","A bag contains 1 red apple, 2 green apples, and 3 yellow apples. What is the total number of possible outcomes if you randomly select one apple?","The total number of possible outcomes is 4","The total number of possible outcomes is 5","The total number of possible outcomes is 6","The total number of possible outcomes is 8","The total number of possible outcomes is 6","To find the total number of possible outcomes, we add the number of red, green, and yellow apples: 1 + 2 + 3 = 6.","/image-source/math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-bag-contains-dd6fd61a.png","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-tom-has-5-4b96f79b","Math G6: Understanding a random experiment - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-4-3-1 Understanding a random experiment, determining its outcomes, expressing the probabilities of their occurrence, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Finding all possible outcomes of a random experiment and their number by using tables, organized lists, tree diagrams, and the counting principle.","Tom has 5 pencils in a jar. He randomly selects one pencil. What are all the possible outcomes?","There are 5 possible outcomes","There are 10 possible outcomes","There are 20 possible outcomes","There is only 1 possible outcome","There are 5 possible outcomes","Since Tom has 5 pencils, there are 5 possible outcomes when he randomly selects one pencil.","/image-source/math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-tom-has-5-4b96f79b.png","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-jar-contains-0ecedf66","Math G6: Understanding a random experiment - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-4-3-1 Understanding a random experiment, determining its outcomes, expressing the probabilities of their occurrence, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Finding all possible outcomes of a random experiment and their number by using tables, organized lists, tree diagrams, and the counting principle.","A jar contains 3 red marbles, 2 blue marbles, and 1 green marble. What is the total number of possible outcomes if you randomly select one marble?","The total number of possible outcomes is 5","The total number of possible outcomes is 6","The total number of possible outcomes is 8","The total number of possible outcomes is 10","The total number of possible outcomes is 6","Adding the frequencies gives a total of 6 observations.","/image-source/math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-jar-contains-0ecedf66.png","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-die-is-807763fc","Math G6: Understanding a random experiment - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-4-3-1 Understanding a random experiment, determining its outcomes, expressing the probabilities of their occurrence, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Finding all possible outcomes of a random experiment and their number by using tables, organized lists, tree diagrams, and the counting principle.","A die is rolled. What is the probability of rolling a number less than 4?","The probability of rolling a number less than 4 is 1/6.","The probability of rolling a number less than 4 is 2/6.","The probability of rolling a number less than 4 is 3/6.","The probability of rolling a number less than 4 is 4/6.","The probability of rolling a number less than 4 is 3/6.","There are 3 numbers less than 4 (1, 2, 3) out of 6 total numbers, so the probability is 3/6.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-spinner-has-c344c4e5","Math G6: Understanding a random experiment - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-4-3-1 Understanding a random experiment, determining its outcomes, expressing the probabilities of their occurrence, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Finding all possible outcomes of a random experiment and their number by using tables, organized lists, tree diagrams, and the counting principle.","A spinner has 8 equal sections, numbered 1 to 8. What is the probability of spinning a number greater than 5?","The probability of spinning a number greater than 5 is 1/8.","The probability of spinning a number greater than 5 is 2/8.","The probability of spinning a number greater than 5 is 3/8.","The probability of spinning a number greater than 5 is 4/8.","The probability of spinning a number greater than 5 is 4/8.","There are 3 numbers greater than 5 (6, 7, 8) out of 8 total numbers, so the probability is 3/8.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-coin-is-778c43e5","Math G6: Understanding a random experiment - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-4-3-1 Understanding a random experiment, determining its outcomes, expressing the probabilities of their occurrence, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Finding all possible outcomes of a random experiment and their number by using tables, organized lists, tree diagrams, and the counting principle.","A coin is flipped 5 times. What is the probability of getting heads on all 5 flips?","The probability of getting heads on all 5 flips is 1/32.","The probability of getting heads on all 5 flips is 1/16.","The probability of getting heads on all 5 flips is 1/8.","The probability of getting heads on all 5 flips is 1/4.","The probability of getting heads on all 5 flips is 1/32.","Since each flip has a 1/2 chance of getting heads, the probability of getting heads on all 5 flips is (1/2)^5 = 1/32.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-bag-contains-5fabae2c","Math G6: Understanding a random experiment - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-4-3-1 Understanding a random experiment, determining its outcomes, expressing the probabilities of their occurrence, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. Solving mathematical problems including real-life applications of probability, explaining their solutions, and using probability to make predictions.","A bag contains 8 red marbles, 6 blue marbles, and 4 green marbles. What is the probability of drawing a blue marble?","The probability of drawing a blue marble is 1/18.","The probability of drawing a blue marble is 2/18.","The probability of drawing a blue marble is 3/18.","The probability of drawing a blue marble is 4/18.","The probability of drawing a blue marble is 3/18.","The probability of drawing a blue marble is 6 out of 18, or 3/18.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-coin-is-1a75ecc0","Math G6: Understanding a random experiment - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-4-3-1 Understanding a random experiment, determining its outcomes, expressing the probabilities of their occurrence, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Finding all possible outcomes of a random experiment and their number by using tables, organized lists, tree diagrams, and the counting principle.","A coin is flipped 3 times. What is the probability of getting tails on all 3 flips?","The probability of getting tails on all 3 flips is 1/8.","The probability of getting tails on all 3 flips is 1/4.","The probability of getting tails on all 3 flips is 1/2.","The probability of getting tails on all 3 flips is 3/4.","The probability of getting tails on all 3 flips is 1/8.","Each flip has probability 1/2 of tails, so the probability of three tails is (1/2)³ = 1/8.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-jar-contains-729d8c97","Math G6: Understanding a random experiment - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-4-3-1 Understanding a random experiment, determining its outcomes, expressing the probabilities of their occurrence, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. Solving mathematical problems including real-life applications of probability, explaining their solutions, and using probability to make predictions.","A jar contains 12 red jelly beans and 8 blue jelly beans. What is the probability of drawing a blue jelly bean?","The probability of drawing a blue jelly bean is 1/20.","The probability of drawing a blue jelly bean is 2/20.","The probability of drawing a blue jelly bean is 3/20.","The probability of drawing a blue jelly bean is 4/20.","The probability of drawing a blue jelly bean is 2/20.","The probability of drawing a blue jelly bean is 8 out of 20, or 2/20.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-spinner-has-2b33f6f1","Math G6: Understanding a random experiment - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-4-3-1 Understanding a random experiment, determining its outcomes, expressing the probabilities of their occurrence, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Finding all possible outcomes of a random experiment and their number by using tables, organized lists, tree diagrams, and the counting principle.","A spinner has 12 equal sections, numbered 1 to 12. What is the probability of spinning a number less than 10?","The probability of spinning a number less than 10 is 1/12.","The probability of spinning a number less than 10 is 2/12.","The probability of spinning a number less than 10 is 3/12.","The probability of spinning a number less than 10 is 4/12.","The probability of spinning a number less than 10 is 3/12.","There are 9 numbers less than 10 (1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9) out of 12 total numbers, so the probability is 9/12.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-bag-contains-d2385359","Math G6: Understanding a random experiment - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-4-3-1 Understanding a random experiment, determining its outcomes, expressing the probabilities of their occurrence, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. Solving mathematical problems including real-life applications of probability, explaining their solutions, and using probability to make predictions.","A bag contains 15 yellow balls and 5 green balls. What is the probability of drawing a green ball?","The probability of drawing a green ball is 1/20.","The probability of drawing a green ball is 2/20.","The probability of drawing a green ball is 3/20.","The probability of drawing a green ball is 1/4.","The probability of drawing a green ball is 1/4.","The probability of drawing a green ball is 5 out of 20, or 1/4.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-die-is-06df0db9","Math G6: Understanding a random experiment - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-4-3-1 Understanding a random experiment, determining its outcomes, expressing the probabilities of their occurrence, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Finding all possible outcomes of a random experiment and their number by using tables, organized lists, tree diagrams, and the counting principle.","A die is rolled. What is the probability of rolling a number greater than 6?","The probability of rolling a number greater than 6 is 1/6.","The probability of rolling a number greater than 6 is 2/6.","The probability of rolling a number greater than 6 is 3/6.","The probability of rolling a number greater than 6 is 4/6.","The probability of rolling a number greater than 6 is 1/6.","There is 1 number greater than 6 (7) out of 6 total numbers, so the probability is 1/6.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-bag-contains-59c7f579","Math G6: Understanding a random experiment - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-4-3-1 Understanding a random experiment, determining its outcomes, expressing the probabilities of their occurrence, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. Solving mathematical problems including real-life applications of probability, explaining their solutions, and using probability to make predictions.","A bag contains 5 red marbles, 3 blue marbles, and 2 green marbles. What is the probability of drawing a blue marble?","The probability of drawing a blue marble is 1/10.","The probability of drawing a blue marble is 3/10.","The probability of drawing a blue marble is 2/10.","The probability of drawing a blue marble is 5/10.","The probability of drawing a blue marble is 3/10.","The probability of drawing a blue marble is 3 out of 10, or 3/10.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-six-sided-6698e93c","Math G6: Understanding a random experiment - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-4-3-1 Understanding a random experiment, determining its outcomes, expressing the probabilities of their occurrence, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Finding all possible outcomes of a random experiment and their number by using tables, organized lists, tree diagrams, and the counting principle.","A six-sided die is rolled. What is the probability of rolling an even number?","1/2","1/5","2/5","3/4","1/2","The favorable outcomes divided by the total outcomes gives 1/2.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-spinner-has-2adbac12","Math G6: Understanding a random experiment - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-4-3-1 Understanding a random experiment, determining its outcomes, expressing the probabilities of their occurrence, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Finding all possible outcomes of a random experiment and their number by using tables, organized lists, tree diagrams, and the counting principle.","A spinner has 10 equal sections, numbered 1 to 10. What is the probability of spinning a number greater than 7?","3/10","1/5","2/5","3/4","3/10","The favorable outcomes divided by the total outcomes gives 3/10.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-coin-is-1a5ab2b9","Math G6: Understanding a random experiment - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-4-3-1 Understanding a random experiment, determining its outcomes, expressing the probabilities of their occurrence, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. Solving mathematical problems including real-life applications of probability, explaining their solutions, and using probability to make predictions.","A coin is flipped twice. What is the probability of getting heads on both flips?","1/4","1/5","2/5","3/4","1/4","The favorable outcomes divided by the total outcomes gives 1/4.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-bag-contains-910ef213","Math G6: Understanding a random experiment - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-4-3-1 Understanding a random experiment, determining its outcomes, expressing the probabilities of their occurrence, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. Solving mathematical problems including real-life applications of probability, explaining their solutions, and using probability to make predictions.","A bag contains 5 red marbles, 3 blue marbles, and 2 green marbles. What is the probability of drawing a blue marble?","3/10","1/5","2/5","3/4","3/10","The favorable outcomes divided by the total outcomes gives 3/10.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-bag-contains-b4569d47","Math G6: Understanding a random experiment - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-4-3-1 Understanding a random experiment, determining its outcomes, expressing the probabilities of their occurrence, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. Solving mathematical problems including real-life applications of probability, explaining their solutions, and using probability to make predictions.","A bag contains 15 marbles, 5 of which are red. What is the probability of drawing a red marble?","1/3","1/5","2/5","3/4","1/3","The favorable outcomes divided by the total outcomes gives 1/3.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-coin-is-0512b6d3","Math G6: Understanding a random experiment - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-4-3-1 Understanding a random experiment, determining its outcomes, expressing the probabilities of their occurrence, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. Solving mathematical problems including real-life applications of probability, explaining their solutions, and using probability to make predictions.","A coin is flipped. What is the probability of getting heads?","1/2","1/5","2/5","3/4","1/2","The favorable outcomes divided by the total outcomes gives 1/2.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-box-contains-b1c7a026","Math G6: Understanding a random experiment - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-4-3-1 Understanding a random experiment, determining its outcomes, expressing the probabilities of their occurrence, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. Solving mathematical problems including real-life applications of probability, explaining their solutions, and using probability to make predictions.","A box contains 12 pencils, 8 of which are yellow. What is the probability of picking a yellow pencil?","2/3","1/5","2/5","3/4","2/3","The favorable outcomes divided by the total outcomes gives 2/3.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-spinner-has-cb161ffb","Math G6: Understanding a random experiment - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-4-3-1 Understanding a random experiment, determining its outcomes, expressing the probabilities of their occurrence, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Finding all possible outcomes of a random experiment and their number by using tables, organized lists, tree diagrams, and the counting principle.","A spinner has 8 equal sections, numbered 1 to 8. What is the probability of spinning a number less than 5?","1/2","1/5","2/5","3/4","1/2","The favorable outcomes divided by the total outcomes gives 1/2.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-fair-six-e505623f","Math G6: Understanding a random experiment - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-4-3-1 Understanding a random experiment, determining its outcomes, expressing the probabilities of their occurrence, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Finding all possible outcomes of a random experiment and their number by using tables, organized lists, tree diagrams, and the counting principle.","A fair six-sided die is rolled. What is the probability of rolling a number greater than 4?","1/3","1/5","2/5","3/4","1/3","The favorable outcomes divided by the total outcomes gives 1/3.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-box-contains-6a7f1b43","Math G6: Understanding a random experiment - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-4-3-1 Understanding a random experiment, determining its outcomes, expressing the probabilities of their occurrence, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. Solving mathematical problems including real-life applications of probability, explaining their solutions, and using probability to make predictions.","A box contains 18 crayons, 6 of which are blue. What is the probability of picking a blue crayon?","1/3","1/5","2/5","3/4","1/3","The favorable outcomes divided by the total outcomes gives 1/3.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-median-of-123f8999","Math G6: Understanding measures of central tendency and range - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-4-2-1 Understanding measures of central tendency and range, distinguishing between them, and finding and interpreting them.","3. Identifying outliers in a data set and describing their effect on the mean.","What is the median of the set: 1, 3, 5, 7, 9?","The middle value of the numbers","The value that appears most often","The sum of all numbers divided by the total count","The difference between the highest and lowest numbers","The middle value of the numbers","The median is the middle value of a set when it is arranged in order, which in this case is 5.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-number-is-mean-9e4049b2","Math G6: Understanding measures of central tendency and range - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-4-2-1 Understanding measures of central tendency and range, distinguishing between them, and finding and interpreting them.","3. Identifying outliers in a data set and describing their effect on the mean.","Which number is the mean of the set: 12, 15, 18, 21?","The sum of all numbers divided by the total count","The middle value of the numbers","The value that appears most often","The difference between the highest and lowest numbers","The value that appears most often","To find the mean, we add up all the numbers (12 + 15 + 18 + 21 = 66) and divide by the total count (4), which gives us 66 / 4 = 16.5.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-number-is-mean-224ec661","Math G6: Understanding measures of central tendency and range - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-4-2-1 Understanding measures of central tendency and range, distinguishing between them, and finding and interpreting them.","3. Identifying outliers in a data set and describing their effect on the mean.","Which number is the mean of the set: 24, 27, 30, 33?","The sum of all numbers divided by the total count","The middle value of the numbers","The value that appears most often","The difference between the highest and lowest numbers","The value that appears most often","To find the mean, we add up all the numbers (24 + 27 + 30 + 33 = 114) and divide by the total count (4), which gives us 114 / 4 = 28.5.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-mode-of-e1f9534b","Math G6: Understanding measures of central tendency and range - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-4-2-1 Understanding measures of central tendency and range, distinguishing between them, and finding and interpreting them.","1. Distinguishing the mean, median, mode, and range, finding them for a set of individual data values, and interpreting them in context.","Which statement correctly identifies the mode or modes of the data set shown?","4 and 8 are both modes.","Only 4 is the mode.","Only 8 is the mode.","The data set has no mode.","4 and 8 are both modes.","The values 4 and 8 occur with the same greatest frequency, so the data set is bimodal.","/image-source/math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-mode-of-e1f9534b.png","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-number-is-median-8cee335c","Math G6: Understanding measures of central tendency and range - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-4-2-1 Understanding measures of central tendency and range, distinguishing between them, and finding and interpreting them.","3. Identifying outliers in a data set and describing their effect on the mean.","Which number is the median of the set: 1, 3, 5, 7, 9, 11?","The middle value of the numbers","The value that appears most often","The sum of all numbers divided by the total count","The difference between the highest and lowest numbers","The middle value of the numbers","The median is the middle value of a set when it is arranged in order, which in this case is 5.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-median-of-bd7cf66f","Math G6: Understanding measures of central tendency and range - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-4-2-1 Understanding measures of central tendency and range, distinguishing between them, and finding and interpreting them.","3. Identifying outliers in a data set and describing their effect on the mean.","What is the median of the set: 2, 4, 6, 8, 10, 12?","The middle value of the numbers","The value that appears most often","The sum of all numbers divided by the total count","The difference between the highest and lowest numbers","The middle value of the numbers","The median is the middle value of a set when it is arranged in order, which in this case is 6.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-range-of-e58c3d5b","Math G6: Understanding measures of central tendency and range - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-4-2-1 Understanding measures of central tendency and range, distinguishing between them, and finding and interpreting them.","3. Identifying outliers in a data set and describing their effect on the mean.","What is the range of the set: 5, 10, 15, 20, 25?","The difference between the highest and lowest numbers","The sum of all numbers divided by the total count","The middle value of the numbers","The value that appears most often","The difference between the highest and lowest numbers","The range is the difference between the highest and lowest numbers in a set, which in this case is 20.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-mean-of-77c22fdc","Math G6: Understanding measures of central tendency and range - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-1 Adding and subtracting whole numbers up to seven digits, multiplying numbers up to three digits, dividing numbers of up to four digits by numbers of up to two digits, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","4. Solving mathematical problems up to three steps, including real-life applications on all four operations, and explaining their solutions.","What is the mean of a set of numbers?","The middle value of the numbers","The value that appears most often","The sum of all numbers divided by the total count","The difference between the highest and lowest numbers","The sum of all numbers divided by the total count","The mean is the average of all numbers in a set, found by adding up all the numbers and dividing by the total count.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-number-is-mode-0498fc42","Math G6: Understanding measures of central tendency and range - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-4-2-1 Understanding measures of central tendency and range, distinguishing between them, and finding and interpreting them.","1. Distinguishing the mean, median, mode, and range, finding them for a set of individual data values, and interpreting them in context.","What is the mode of the data set shown?","4","2","6","8","4","The mode is the value that occurs most often; in this data set, 4 occurs most often.","/image-source/math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-number-is-mode-0498fc42.png","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-range-of-554436fb","Math G6: Understanding measures of central tendency and range - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-4-2-1 Understanding measures of central tendency and range, distinguishing between them, and finding and interpreting them.","3. Identifying outliers in a data set and describing their effect on the mean.","What is the range of the set: 2, 4, 6, 8, 10?","The difference between the highest and lowest numbers","The sum of all numbers divided by the total count","The middle value of the numbers","The value that appears most often","The difference between the highest and lowest numbers","The range is the difference between the highest and lowest numbers in a set, which in this case is 8.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-median-of-a93ce1f3","Math G6: Understanding measures of central tendency and range - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-4-2-1 Understanding measures of central tendency and range, distinguishing between them, and finding and interpreting them.","1. Distinguishing the mean, median, mode, and range, finding them for a set of individual data values, and interpreting them in context.","What is the median of the numbers 2, 5, 8, 11, 14?","The sum of the numbers","The average of the numbers","The middle value when the numbers are arranged in order","The difference between the largest and smallest numbers","The middle value when the numbers are arranged in order","Since there are an odd number of values, the median is the middle number. When arranged in order, the numbers are 2, 5, 8, 11, 14. The middle number is 8.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-of-numbers-8648c127","Math G6: Understanding measures of central tendency and range - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-4-2-1 Understanding measures of central tendency and range, distinguishing between them, and finding and interpreting them.","3. Identifying outliers in a data set and describing their effect on the mean.","A set of numbers has a mode of 5. What does this mean?","The number 5 occurs most frequently in the set","The number 5 is the largest number in the set","The number 5 is the smallest number in the set","The number 5 is the average of the set","The number 5 occurs most frequently in the set","The mode is the number that appears most often in a set of numbers. If a set has a mode of 5, it means that 5 occurs more frequently than any other number in the set.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-of-numbers-2279b6d6","Math G6: Understanding measures of central tendency and range - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-4-2-1 Understanding measures of central tendency and range, distinguishing between them, and finding and interpreting them.","3. Identifying outliers in a data set and describing their effect on the mean.","A set of numbers has a median of 6. What does this mean?","The number 6 occurs most frequently in the set","The number 6 is the largest number in the set","The number 6 is the average of the set","The number 6 is the middle value when the numbers are arranged in order","The number 6 is the middle value when the numbers are arranged in order","The median is the middle value in a set of numbers when they are arranged in order. If a set has a median of 6, it means that 6 is the middle value when the numbers are arranged from smallest to largest.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-mode-of-6ac0866c","Math G6: Understanding measures of central tendency and range - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-4-2-1 Understanding measures of central tendency and range, distinguishing between them, and finding and interpreting them.","3. Identifying outliers in a data set and describing their effect on the mean.","What is the mode of the numbers 2, 4, 6, 6, 8?","The number 2 occurs most frequently","The number 6 occurs most frequently","The number 8 occurs most frequently","The number 4 occurs most frequently","The number 6 occurs most frequently","The mode is the number that appears most often in a set of numbers. In this case, the number 6 appears twice, which is more than any other number. Therefore, the mode is 6.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-line-graph-6747eaf6","Math G6: Understanding measures of central tendency and range - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-4-2-1 Understanding measures of central tendency and range, distinguishing between them, and finding and interpreting them.","2. Finding the mean, median, mode, and range for represented data by dot plots and , line graphs, and interpreting them.","A line graph shows the temperature for each day of the week. If the range of temperatures is 20 degrees, what does this mean?","The average temperature was 20 degrees","The highest temperature was 20 degrees","The difference between the highest and lowest temperatures was 20 degrees","The lowest temperature was 20 degrees","The difference between the highest and lowest temperatures was 20 degrees","The range is the difference between the largest and smallest values in a set of data. If the range of temperatures is 20 degrees, it means that the difference between the highest and lowest temperatures is 20 degrees.","/image-source/math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-line-graph-6747eaf6.png","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-range-of-c24f949c","Math G6: Understanding measures of central tendency and range - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-4-2-1 Understanding measures of central tendency and range, distinguishing between them, and finding and interpreting them.","3. Identifying outliers in a data set and describing their effect on the mean.","What is the range of the numbers 2, 4, 6, 8, 10?","The average of the numbers","The difference between the largest and smallest numbers","The sum of all the numbers","The middle value when the numbers are arranged in order","The difference between the largest and smallest numbers","To find the range, we subtract the smallest number from the largest number. In this case, the largest number is 10 and the smallest number is 2. So, the range is 10 - 2 = 8.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-mean-of-85ffa4b1","Math G6: Understanding measures of central tendency and range - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-4-2-1 Understanding measures of central tendency and range, distinguishing between them, and finding and interpreting them.","3. Identifying outliers in a data set and describing their effect on the mean.","What is the mean of the numbers 10, 12, 14, 16, 18?","The sum of the numbers","The average of the numbers","The middle value when the numbers are arranged in order","The difference between the largest and smallest numbers","The average of the numbers","To find the mean, we add up all the numbers and divide by the total count of numbers. In this case, the sum is 10 + 12 + 14 + 16 + 18 = 70, and there are 5 numbers. So, the mean is 70 / 5 = 14.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-dot-plot-533ebab7","Math G6: Understanding measures of central tendency and range - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-4-2-1 Understanding measures of central tendency and range, distinguishing between them, and finding and interpreting them.","2. Finding the mean, median, mode, and range for represented data by dot plots and , line graphs, and interpreting them.","A dot plot shows the scores of a class on a math test. If the mode of the scores is 80, what does this tell us?","Most students scored above 80","Most students scored below 80","The average score was 80","The score 80 occurred most frequently","The score 80 occurred most frequently","The mode is the value that appears most often in a set of data. If the mode of the scores is 80, it means that more students scored 80 than any other score.","/image-source/math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-dot-plot-533ebab7.png","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-median-of-390c421b","Math G6: Understanding measures of central tendency and range - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-4-2-1 Understanding measures of central tendency and range, distinguishing between them, and finding and interpreting them.","1. Distinguishing the mean, median, mode, and range, finding them for a set of individual data values, and interpreting them in context.","What is the median of the numbers 1, 3, 5, 7, 9?","The largest number in the list","The smallest number in the list","The middle value when the numbers are arranged in order","The sum of all the numbers","The middle value when the numbers are arranged in order","Since there are an odd number of values, the median is the middle number. When arranged in order, the numbers are 1, 3, 5, 7, 9. The middle number is 5.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-mean-of-b7e4fc0c","Math G6: Understanding measures of central tendency and range - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-4-2-1 Understanding measures of central tendency and range, distinguishing between them, and finding and interpreting them.","3. Identifying outliers in a data set and describing their effect on the mean.","What is the mean of the numbers 2, 4, 6, 8, 10?","The sum of all the numbers","The middle value when the numbers are arranged in order","The average of all the numbers","The difference between the largest and smallest numbers","The average of all the numbers","To find the mean, we add up all the numbers and divide by the total count of numbers. In this case, the sum is 2 + 4 + 6 + 8 + 10 = 30, and there are 5 numbers. So, the mean is 30 / 5 = 6.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-effect-of-64fb7def","Math G6: Understanding measures of central tendency and range - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-4-2-1 Understanding measures of central tendency and range, distinguishing between them, and finding and interpreting them.","3. Identifying outliers in a data set and describing their effect on the mean.","What is the effect of an outlier on the mode of a data set?","The mode increases","The mode decreases","The mode stays the same","The mode becomes zero","The mode stays the same","The mode is the most common value in a data set. An outlier does not affect the mode because it is a single value that is not part of the most common group.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-should-you-do-2722349c","Math G6: Understanding measures of central tendency and range - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-4-2-1 Understanding measures of central tendency and range, distinguishing between them, and finding and interpreting them.","3. Identifying outliers in a data set and describing their effect on the mean.","What should you do when you find an outlier in a data set?","Remove it","Add more data","Leave it as is","Check the data again","Leave it as is","When you find an outlier, you should check the data again to ensure that it's accurate. If the outlier is correct, you should leave it as is. If it's incorrect, you should remove it or add more data to verify the result.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-why-is-it-cf22893a","Math G6: Understanding measures of central tendency and range - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-4-2-1 Understanding measures of central tendency and range, distinguishing between them, and finding and interpreting them.","3. Identifying outliers in a data set and describing their effect on the mean.","Why is it important to identify outliers in a data set?","Because it helps to find the mean","Because it helps to find the median","Because it helps to understand the data","Because it helps to find the mode","Because it helps to understand the data","Identifying outliers is important because it helps to understand the data and make informed decisions. Outliers can affect the mean, median, and mode, so it's essential to consider them when analyzing data.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-happens-to-mean-a8b5a5c4","Math G6: Understanding measures of central tendency and range - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-4-2-1 Understanding measures of central tendency and range, distinguishing between them, and finding and interpreting them.","3. Identifying outliers in a data set and describing their effect on the mean.","What happens to the mean when an outlier is removed from a data set?","The mean stays the same","The mean increases","The mean decreases","The mean becomes zero","The mean decreases","When an outlier is removed, the mean moves closer to the other values in the data set, making it decrease.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-how-can-you-fef1e52a","Math G6: Understanding measures of central tendency and range - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-4-2-1 Understanding measures of central tendency and range, distinguishing between them, and finding and interpreting them.","3. Identifying outliers in a data set and describing their effect on the mean.","How can you identify an outlier in a data set?","By looking at the mean","By looking at the median","By looking at the value that is farthest from the mean","By looking at the value that is exactly in the middle","By looking at the value that is farthest from the mean","An outlier is a value that is much higher or lower than the other values in the data set. You can identify an outlier by looking at the value that is farthest from the mean.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-how-an-outlier-cf53b4d0","Math G6: Understanding measures of central tendency and range - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-4-2-1 Understanding measures of central tendency and range, distinguishing between them, and finding and interpreting them.","3. Identifying outliers in a data set and describing their effect on the mean.","How does an outlier affect the range of a data set?","The range increases","The range decreases","The range stays the same","The range becomes zero","The range increases","An outlier is a value that is much higher or lower than the other values in the data set. When an outlier is added, it increases the range because it adds more distance between the highest and lowest values.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-how-can-outliers-e609ada6","Math G6: Understanding measures of central tendency and range - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-4-2-1 Understanding measures of central tendency and range, distinguishing between them, and finding and interpreting them.","3. Identifying outliers in a data set and describing their effect on the mean.","How can outliers affect the interpretation of a data set?","They make the data more accurate","They make the data less accurate","They have no effect on the data","They make the data more interesting","They make the data less accurate","Outliers can make the data less accurate because they can affect the mean, median, and mode. It's essential to consider outliers when interpreting data to ensure that the results are accurate and reliable.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-effect-of-c173aa2d","Math G6: Understanding measures of central tendency and range - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-4-2-1 Understanding measures of central tendency and range, distinguishing between them, and finding and interpreting them.","3. Identifying outliers in a data set and describing their effect on the mean.","What is the effect of an outlier on the median of a data set?","The median increases","The median decreases","The median stays the same","The median becomes zero","The median stays the same","The median is the middle value in a data set when it is arranged in order. An outlier does not affect the median because it is a single value that is not part of the middle group.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-happens-to-mean-8be31e0c","Math G6: Understanding measures of central tendency and range - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-4-2-1 Understanding measures of central tendency and range, distinguishing between them, and finding and interpreting them.","3. Identifying outliers in a data set and describing their effect on the mean.","What happens to the mean when an outlier is added to a data set?","The mean stays the same","The mean increases","The mean decreases","The mean becomes zero","The mean decreases","An outlier is a value that is much higher or lower than the other values in the data set. When an outlier is added, it pulls the mean in its direction, making it decrease.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-an-outlier-7e02a8d0","Math G6: Understanding measures of central tendency and range - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-4-2-1 Understanding measures of central tendency and range, distinguishing between them, and finding and interpreting them.","1. Distinguishing the mean, median, mode, and range, finding them for a set of individual data values, and interpreting them in context.","What is an outlier in a data set?","A value that is close to the mean","A value that is higher than the median","A value that is much higher or lower than the other values","A value that is exactly in the middle","A value that is much higher or lower than the other values","An outlier is a value that is much higher or lower than the other values in the data set.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-average-of-655de686","Math G6: Understanding measures of central tendency and - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-4-2-1 Understanding measures of central tendency and range, distinguishing between them, and finding and interpreting them.","1. Distinguishing the mean, median, mode, and range, finding them for a set of individual data values, and interpreting them in context.","What is the average of a set of numbers called?","The mode","The median","The mean","The range","The mean","The mean is the average of a set of numbers.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-number-in-a-1530efdd","Math G6: Understanding measures of central tendency and - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-4-2-1 Understanding measures of central tendency and range, distinguishing between them, and finding and interpreting them.","1. Distinguishing the mean, median, mode, and range, finding them for a set of individual data values, and interpreting them in context.","Which number in a set of numbers is the middle value when the numbers are arranged in order?","The mode","The median","The mean","The range","The median","The median is the middle value in a set of numbers when arranged in order.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-find-range-of-f7e07e68","Math G6: Understanding measures of central tendency and - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-4-2-1 Understanding measures of central tendency and range, distinguishing between them, and finding and interpreting them.","3. Identifying outliers in a data set and describing their effect on the mean.","Find the range of the numbers 2, 4, 6, 8, 10.","2","4","6","8","8","The range is the difference between the largest and smallest numbers in the set. The largest number is 10 and the smallest number is 2, so the range is 10 - 2 = 8.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-number-appears-6306560e","Math G6: Understanding measures of central tendency and - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-4-2-1 Understanding measures of central tendency and range, distinguishing between them, and finding and interpreting them.","1. Distinguishing the mean, median, mode, and range, finding them for a set of individual data values, and interpreting them in context.","What is the number that appears most often in a set of numbers called?","The mode","The median","The mean","The range","The mode","The mode is the number that appears most often in a set of numbers.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-difference-largest-4adfc048","Math G6: Understanding measures of central tendency and - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-4-2-1 Understanding measures of central tendency and range, distinguishing between them, and finding and interpreting them.","1. Distinguishing the mean, median, mode, and range, finding them for a set of individual data values, and interpreting them in context.","What is the difference between the largest and smallest numbers in a set called?","The mode","The median","The mean","The range","The range","The range is the difference between the largest and smallest numbers in a set.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-find-mean-of-f914680a","Math G6: Understanding measures of central tendency and - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-4-2-1 Understanding measures of central tendency and range, distinguishing between them, and finding and interpreting them.","3. Identifying outliers in a data set and describing their effect on the mean.","Find the mean of the numbers 2, 4, 6, 8, 10.","6","5","7","30","6","Add the numbers to get 30, then divide by 5 numbers: 30 ÷ 5 = 6.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-find-mode-of-7af0c383","Math G6: Understanding measures of central tendency and - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-2 Distinguishing the factors and multiples of a number, representing and finding them, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","4. Factoring a number into its prime factors, and using prime factorization to find the greatest common divisor and the least common multiple of two  numbers or more.","Find the mode of the numbers 2, 4, 6, 8, 10, 4.","2","4","6","8","4","The mode is the number that appears most often in the set. The number 4 appears twice, which is more than any other number.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-find-median-of-af2b4817","Math G6: Understanding measures of central tendency and - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-1-1 Understanding and representing the place value of numbers up to 12 digits, reading, writing, comparing, Ordering, rounding them.","3. Comparing and Ordering numbers up to 12 digits by using symbols (>, <, =) in ascending and descending order.","Find the median of the numbers 1, 3, 5, 7, 9.","3","5","7","9","5","To find the median, arrange the numbers in order and find the middle value. The middle value is 5.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-rectangular-prism-2134f318","Math G6: Understanding powers of whole numbers, representing - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-3-3 Understanding volume and surface area and their units, distinguishing the formulas for a right rectangular prism, and applying them to find volume and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","2. Distinguishing the formula for the volume of a right rectangular prism and using it to calculate its volume.","A rectangular prism has a length of 2^2 meters, a width of 3 meters, and a height of 2 meters. What is the volume of the prism?","The volume of the prism is 24 cubic meters.","The volume of the prism is 12 cubic meters.","The volume of the prism is 48 cubic meters.","The volume of the prism is 36 cubic meters.","The volume of the prism is 24 cubic meters.","The length is 2^2 = 4 meters. Volume = 4 x 3 x 2 = 24 cubic meters.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-water-tank-339fcfad","Math G6: Understanding powers of whole numbers, representing - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-3 Understanding powers of whole numbers, representing and finding them, finding values of numerical expressions included it, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. Solving mathematical problems including real-life applications of numerical expressions with powers of whole numbers, and explaining their solution.","A water tank can hold 16 liters of water. If 2^3 liters of water are already in the tank, how many more liters can be added?","The tank can hold 8 more liters of water.","The tank can hold 12 more liters of water.","The tank can hold 16 more liters of water.","The tank can hold 24 more liters of water.","The tank can hold 8 more liters of water.","Since 2^3 = 8, we need to subtract 8 from the total capacity of the tank to find out how many more liters can be added. 16 - 8 = 8.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-bicycle-has-e070f486","Math G6: Understanding powers of whole numbers, representing - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-3 Understanding powers of whole numbers, representing and finding them, finding values of numerical expressions included it, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. Solving mathematical problems including real-life applications of numerical expressions with powers of whole numbers, and explaining their solution.","A bicycle has 2 wheels, and each wheel has 2^2 spokes. How many spokes are there in total?","There are 8 spokes in total.","There are 4 spokes in total.","There are 16 spokes in total.","There are 32 spokes in total.","There are 8 spokes in total.","Each wheel has 2^2 = 4 spokes. With 2 wheels, 2 x 4 = 8 spokes.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-rectangular-garden-9a99842c","Math G6: Understanding powers of whole numbers, representing - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-3-3 Understanding volume and surface area and their units, distinguishing the formulas for a right rectangular prism, and applying them to find volume and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Estimating volume and measuring it by using the appropriate metric unit (ml3, cm3, and m3).","A rectangular garden measures 4 meters by 2^2 meters. What is the area of the garden?","The area of the garden is 8 square meters.","The area of the garden is 16 square meters.","The area of the garden is 32 square meters.","The area of the garden is 64 square meters.","The area of the garden is 16 square meters.","To find the area of the garden, we need to multiply the length and the width. The width is 2^2 = 4 meters. So, the area is 4 x 4 = 16 square meters.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-rectangular-garden-e7882de9","Math G6: Understanding powers of whole numbers, representing - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-3-3 Understanding volume and surface area and their units, distinguishing the formulas for a right rectangular prism, and applying them to find volume and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Estimating volume and measuring it by using the appropriate metric unit (ml3, cm3, and m3).","A rectangular garden measures 2^2 meters by 3 meters. What is the area of the garden?","The area of the garden is 12 square meters.","The area of the garden is 8 square meters.","The area of the garden is 24 square meters.","The area of the garden is 16 square meters.","The area of the garden is 12 square meters.","2^2 = 4, so the area is 4 x 3 = 12 square meters.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-bookshelf-has-2b270b4f","Math G6: Understanding powers of whole numbers, representing - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-3 Understanding powers of whole numbers, representing and finding them, finding values of numerical expressions included it, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. Solving mathematical problems including real-life applications of numerical expressions with powers of whole numbers, and explaining their solution.","A bookshelf has 3 shelves, and each shelf can hold 2 boxes of books. If each box contains 8 books, how many books can the bookshelf hold in total?","The bookshelf can hold 48 books.","The bookshelf can hold 44 books.","The bookshelf can hold 52 books.","The bookshelf can hold 480 books.","The bookshelf can hold 48 books.","Multiply shelves, boxes per shelf, and books per box: 3 x 2 x 8 = 48 books.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-cube-has-a122ea28","Math G6: Understanding powers of whole numbers, representing - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-3 Understanding powers of whole numbers, representing and finding them, finding values of numerical expressions included it, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. Solving mathematical problems including real-life applications of numerical expressions with powers of whole numbers, and explaining their solution.","A cube has a volume of 2^3 cubic meters. What is the length of one side of the cube?","The length of one side of the cube is 2 meters.","The length of one side of the cube is 4 meters.","The length of one side of the cube is 8 meters.","The length of one side of the cube is 16 meters.","The length of one side of the cube is 2 meters.","The volume is 2^3 = 8 cubic meters. The cube root of 8 is 2, so each side is 2 meters.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-group-of-fd1310c9","Math G6: Understanding powers of whole numbers, representing - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-3 Understanding powers of whole numbers, representing and finding them, finding values of numerical expressions included it, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. Solving mathematical problems including real-life applications of numerical expressions with powers of whole numbers, and explaining their solution.","A group of students want to share some pencils equally. If they have 2^3 pencils and there are 4 students, how many pencils will each student get?","Each student will get 2 pencils.","Each student will get 4 pencils.","Each student will get 8 pencils.","Each student will get 12 pencils.","Each student will get 2 pencils.","2^3 = 8 pencils. Sharing equally among 4 students gives 8 ÷ 4 = 2 pencils each.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-cube-has-2aaa7322","Math G6: Understanding powers of whole numbers, representing - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-1-2 Understanding the properties of two-dimensional and three-dimensional geometric shapes and using them to identify and classify the shapes.","2. Distinguishing triangles and circles, identifying and naming their elements, and classifying triangles according to the lengths of their sides and the measures of their angles.","A cube has a side length of 2^2 meters. What is the volume of the cube?","The volume of the cube is 64 cubic meters.","The volume of the cube is 16 cubic meters.","The volume of the cube is 32 cubic meters.","The volume of the cube is 48 cubic meters.","The volume of the cube is 64 cubic meters.","The side length is 2^2 = 4 meters. Volume = 4 x 4 x 4 = 64 cubic meters.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-group-of-a37b8700","Math G6: Understanding powers of whole numbers, representing - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-3 Understanding powers of whole numbers, representing and finding them, finding values of numerical expressions included it, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. Solving mathematical problems including real-life applications of numerical expressions with powers of whole numbers, and explaining their solution.","A group of friends want to share some candy equally. If they have 2^3 pieces of candy and there are 4 friends, how many pieces of candy will each friend get?","Each friend will get 2 pieces of candy.","Each friend will get 4 pieces of candy.","Each friend will get 8 pieces of candy.","Each friend will get 12 pieces of candy.","Each friend will get 2 pieces of candy.","2^3 = 8 pieces. Sharing equally among 4 friends gives 8 ÷ 4 = 2 pieces each.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-value-of-0aacb6b7","Math G6: Understanding powers of whole numbers - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-3 Understanding powers of whole numbers, representing and finding them, finding values of numerical expressions included it, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Distinguishing a power of a whole number (with a whole-number exponent) as repeated multiplication and finding its value.","What is the value of 5^1?","5 to the power of 1 is 5","5 to the power of 1 is 10","5 to the power of 1 is 15","5 to the power of 1 is 20","5 to the power of 1 is 5","Any number to the power of 1 is itself.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-simplify-expression-2-1fb1ec45","Math G6: Understanding powers of whole numbers - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-3 Understanding powers of whole numbers, representing and finding them, finding values of numerical expressions included it, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. Solving mathematical problems including real-life applications of numerical expressions with powers of whole numbers, and explaining their solution.","Simplify the expression 2^3 - 1?","2 cubed is 8, minus 1 is 7","2 cubed is 8, minus 1 is 6","2 cubed is 8, minus 1 is 9","2 cubed is 8, minus 1 is 10","2 cubed is 8, minus 1 is 7","First, calculate 2 cubed, which is 8. Then, subtract 1 to get 7.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-value-of-b77d7920","Math G6: Understanding powers of whole numbers - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-3 Understanding powers of whole numbers, representing and finding them, finding values of numerical expressions included it, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Distinguishing a power of a whole number (with a whole-number exponent) as repeated multiplication and finding its value.","What is the value of 4^0?","4 to the power of 0 is 0","4 to the power of 0 is 1","4 to the power of 0 is 4","4 to the power of 0 is 16","4 to the power of 0 is 1","Any number to the power of 0 is 1.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-value-of-59aec2bf","Math G6: Understanding powers of whole numbers - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-3 Understanding powers of whole numbers, representing and finding them, finding values of numerical expressions included it, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Distinguishing a power of a whole number (with a whole-number exponent) as repeated multiplication and finding its value.","What is the value of 6^0?","6 to the power of 0 is 0","6 to the power of 0 is 1","6 to the power of 0 is 6","6 to the power of 0 is 36","6 to the power of 0 is 1","Any number to the power of 0 is 1.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-simplify-expression-4-23e8c249","Math G6: Understanding powers of whole numbers - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-3 Understanding powers of whole numbers, representing and finding them, finding values of numerical expressions included it, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. Solving mathematical problems including real-life applications of numerical expressions with powers of whole numbers, and explaining their solution.","Simplify the expression 4^2 - 3?","4 squared is 16, minus 3 is 13","4 squared is 16, minus 3 is 14","4 squared is 16, minus 3 is 15","4 squared is 16, minus 3 is 17","4 squared is 16, minus 3 is 13","First, calculate 4 squared, which is 16. Then, subtract 3 to get 13.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-value-of-19d6b493","Math G6: Understanding powers of whole numbers - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-3 Understanding powers of whole numbers, representing and finding them, finding values of numerical expressions included it, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Distinguishing a power of a whole number (with a whole-number exponent) as repeated multiplication and finding its value.","What is the value of 2^0 + 1?","2 to the power of 0 is 1, plus 1 is 2","2 to the power of 0 is 1, plus 1 is 3","2 to the power of 0 is 1, plus 1 is 4","2 to the power of 0 is 1, plus 1 is 5","2 to the power of 0 is 1, plus 1 is 2","First, calculate 2 to the power of 0, which is 1. Then, add 1 to get 2.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-simplify-expression-3-c1a2e1bd","Math G6: Understanding powers of whole numbers - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-3 Understanding powers of whole numbers, representing and finding them, finding values of numerical expressions included it, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. Solving mathematical problems including real-life applications of numerical expressions with powers of whole numbers, and explaining their solution.","Simplify the expression 3^2 + 5?","3 squared is 9, plus 5 is 14","3 squared is 9, plus 5 is 13","3 squared is 9, plus 5 is 12","3 squared is 9, plus 5 is 11","3 squared is 9, plus 5 is 14","First, calculate 3 squared, which is 9. Then, add 5 to get 14.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-2-to-d442cc1e","Math G6: Understanding powers of whole numbers - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-3 Understanding powers of whole numbers, representing and finding them, finding values of numerical expressions included it, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. Solving mathematical problems including real-life applications of numerical expressions with powers of whole numbers, and explaining their solution.","What is 2 to the power of 3?","2 cubed is 8","2 to the power of 3 is 6","2 to the power of 3 is 9","2 to the power of 3 is 10","2 cubed is 8","2 to the power of 3 means 2 multiplied by itself 3 times, which is 2 x 2 x 2 = 8.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-simplify-expression-2-b0ff0b4d","Math G6: Understanding powers of whole numbers - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-3 Understanding powers of whole numbers, representing and finding them, finding values of numerical expressions included it, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. Solving mathematical problems including real-life applications of numerical expressions with powers of whole numbers, and explaining their solution.","Simplify the expression 2^2 + 4?","2 squared is 4, plus 4 is 8","2 squared is 4, plus 4 is 6","2 squared is 4, plus 4 is 5","2 squared is 4, plus 4 is 7","2 squared is 4, plus 4 is 8","First, calculate 2 squared, which is 4. Then, add 4 to get 8.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-simplify-expression-3-9929cebb","Math G6: Understanding powers of whole numbers - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-1-2-3 Understanding powers of whole numbers, representing and finding them, finding values of numerical expressions included it, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. Solving mathematical problems including real-life applications of numerical expressions with powers of whole numbers, and explaining their solution.","Simplify the expression 3^2 + 2?","3 squared is 9, plus 2 is 11","3 squared is 9, plus 2 is 10","3 squared is 9, plus 2 is 12","3 squared is 9, plus 2 is 13","3 squared is 9, plus 2 is 11","First, calculate 3 squared, which is 9. Then, add 2 to get 11.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-of-polygons-is-73863dd0","Math G6: Understanding the properties of two-dimensional and three-di - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-1-2 Understanding the properties of two-dimensional and three-dimensional geometric shapes and using them to identify and classify the shapes.","3. Distinguishing quadrilaterals (parallelogram, rectangle, rhombus, square, and trapezoid), identifying and naming their elements, and classifying them according to the properties of their sides and angles.","Which of the following polygons is regular?","A triangle with two sides equal","A rectangle with sides of different lengths","A square with all sides equal","A circle","A square with all sides equal","A square is a type of regular polygon because all its sides and angles are equal.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-of-polygons-is-aadeacae","Math G6: Understanding the properties of two-dimensional and three-di - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-1-2 Understanding the properties of two-dimensional and three-dimensional geometric shapes and using them to identify and classify the shapes.","3. Distinguishing quadrilaterals (parallelogram, rectangle, rhombus, square, and trapezoid), identifying and naming their elements, and classifying them according to the properties of their sides and angles.","Which of the following polygons is irregular?","A square","A rectangle with equal sides","A triangle with two sides equal","A rectangle with sides of different lengths","A rectangle with sides of different lengths","An irregular polygon has sides and angles that are not all equal, like a rectangle with sides of different lengths.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-of-is-a-8e674ab6","Math G6: Understanding the properties of two-dimensional and three-di - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-1-2 Understanding the properties of two-dimensional and three-dimensional geometric shapes and using them to identify and classify the shapes.","1. Distinguishing polygons, classifying them into regular and irregular polygons, and naming them.","Which of the following is a characteristic of a regular polygon?","All sides are unequal","All angles are unequal","All sides and angles are equal","Some sides are equal","All sides and angles are equal","A regular polygon has all sides and angles equal, which is a key characteristic that distinguishes it from an irregular polygon.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-of-is-an-21f30c5b","Math G6: Understanding the properties of two-dimensional and three-di - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-1-2 Understanding the properties of two-dimensional and three-dimensional geometric shapes and using them to identify and classify the shapes.","3. Distinguishing quadrilaterals (parallelogram, rectangle, rhombus, square, and trapezoid), identifying and naming their elements, and classifying them according to the properties of their sides and angles.","Which of the following is an example of a regular polygon?","A rectangle with sides of different lengths","A square with all sides equal","A triangle with two sides equal","A circle","A square with all sides equal","A square is a type of regular polygon because all its sides and angles are equal.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-name-of-53e2f980","Math G6: Understanding the properties of two-dimensional and three-di - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-1-2 Understanding the properties of two-dimensional and three-dimensional geometric shapes and using them to identify and classify the shapes.","1. Distinguishing polygons, classifying them into regular and irregular polygons, and naming them.","What is the name of a polygon with five sides?","Hexagon","Pentagon","Heptagon","Octagon","Pentagon","A pentagon is a polygon with five sides, and it can be either regular or irregular.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-name-of-8a8113d4","Math G6: Understanding the properties of two-dimensional and three-di - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-1-2 Understanding the properties of two-dimensional and three-dimensional geometric shapes and using them to identify and classify the shapes.","1. Distinguishing polygons, classifying them into regular and irregular polygons, and naming them.","What is the name of a polygon with seven sides?","Hexagon","Heptagon","Octagon","Nonagon","Heptagon","A heptagon is a polygon with seven sides, and it can be either regular or irregular.","","",""
