"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-quadrilateral-has-all-445d0062","Math G6: Understanding the properties of two-dimensional and three-di - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-1-2 Understanding the properties of two-dimensional and three-dimensional geometric shapes and using them to identify and classify the shapes.","3. Distinguishing quadrilaterals (parallelogram, rectangle, rhombus, square, and trapezoid), identifying and naming their elements, and classifying them according to the properties of their sides and angles.","Which quadrilateral has all sides of equal length and all angles are right angles?","Rhombus","Rectangle","Square","Parallelogram","Square","A square is a special type of quadrilateral where all sides are of equal length and all angles are right angles.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-name-of-1faa3e10","Math G6: Understanding the properties of two-dimensional and three-di - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-1-2 Understanding the properties of two-dimensional and three-dimensional geometric shapes and using them to identify and classify the shapes.","3. Distinguishing quadrilaterals (parallelogram, rectangle, rhombus, square, and trapezoid), identifying and naming their elements, and classifying them according to the properties of their sides and angles.","What is the name of a quadrilateral with four sides of equal length, but not necessarily four right angles?","Square","Rhombus","Rectangle","Trapezoid","Rhombus","A rhombus is a quadrilateral with all sides of equal length, but its angles are not necessarily right angles.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-quadrilateral-has-all-8421cdfa","Math G6: Understanding the properties of two-dimensional and three-di - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-1-2 Understanding the properties of two-dimensional and three-dimensional geometric shapes and using them to identify and classify the shapes.","3. Distinguishing quadrilaterals (parallelogram, rectangle, rhombus, square, and trapezoid), identifying and naming their elements, and classifying them according to the properties of their sides and angles.","Which quadrilateral has all angles that are right angles and opposite sides that are equal in length?","Trapezoid","Rhombus","Rectangle","Parallelogram","Rectangle","A rectangle is a quadrilateral with all angles that are right angles and opposite sides that are equal in length.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-type-of-quadrilateral-63db0c5a","Math G6: Understanding the properties of two-dimensional and three-di - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-1-2 Understanding the properties of two-dimensional and three-dimensional geometric shapes and using them to identify and classify the shapes.","3. Distinguishing quadrilaterals (parallelogram, rectangle, rhombus, square, and trapezoid), identifying and naming their elements, and classifying them according to the properties of their sides and angles.","Which type of quadrilateral has two pairs of parallel sides?","Trapezoid","Rhombus","Rectangle","Parallelogram","Parallelogram","A parallelogram is a quadrilateral with two pairs of parallel sides, which means opposite sides are equal in length and parallel to each other.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-name-of-b39e5ec9","Math G6: Understanding the properties of two-dimensional and three-di - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-1-2 Understanding the properties of two-dimensional and three-dimensional geometric shapes and using them to identify and classify the shapes.","3. Distinguishing quadrilaterals (parallelogram, rectangle, rhombus, square, and trapezoid), identifying and naming their elements, and classifying them according to the properties of their sides and angles.","What is the name of a quadrilateral with four right angles and four sides of equal length?","Rectangle","Rhombus","Square","Trapezoid","Square","A square is a special type of rectangle where all four sides are of equal length, and all four angles are right angles.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-how-many-edges-502c65f7","Math G6: Understanding the properties of two-dimensional and three-di - Set 4","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-1-2 Understanding the properties of two-dimensional and three-dimensional geometric shapes and using them to identify and classify the shapes.","4. Distinguishing prisms (triangular, quadrilateral) and cubes, identifying their vertices, edges, faces, and bases, and drawing their nets.","How many edges does a quadrilateral prism have?","8 edges","10 edges","12 edges","16 edges","12 edges","A quadrilateral prism has 12 edges, which are the lines where the faces meet.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-name-of-90ed9b09","Math G6: Understanding the properties of two-dimensional and three-di - Set 4","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-1-2 Understanding the properties of two-dimensional and three-dimensional geometric shapes and using them to identify and classify the shapes.","4. Distinguishing prisms (triangular, quadrilateral) and cubes, identifying their vertices, edges, faces, and bases, and drawing their nets.","What is the name of a three-dimensional shape with six square faces?","Triangular prism","Quadrilateral prism","Cuboid","Cube","Cube","A cube is a three-dimensional shape with six square faces, 12 edges, and 8 vertices.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-net-of-3d2ab291","Math G6: Understanding the properties of two-dimensional and three-di - Set 4","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-1-2 Understanding the properties of two-dimensional and three-dimensional geometric shapes and using them to identify and classify the shapes.","1. Distinguishing polygons, classifying them into regular and irregular polygons, and naming them.","What is the net of a cube?","A two-dimensional shape with 6 squares","A two-dimensional shape with 8 triangles","A two-dimensional shape with 12 rectangles","A two-dimensional shape with 4 hexagons","A two-dimensional shape with 6 squares","The net of a cube is a two-dimensional shape with 6 squares, which can be folded to form a cube.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-how-many-rectangular-89a3f4ea","Math G6: Understanding the properties of two-dimensional and three-di - Set 4","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-1-2 Understanding the properties of two-dimensional and three-dimensional geometric shapes and using them to identify and classify the shapes.","4. Distinguishing prisms (triangular, quadrilateral) and cubes, identifying their vertices, edges, faces, and bases, and drawing their nets.","How many rectangular faces does a triangular prism have?","2 rectangular faces","3 rectangular faces","4 rectangular faces","5 rectangular faces","3 rectangular faces","A triangular prism has 3 rectangular faces, which are the sides of the prism, and 2 triangular faces, which are the bases.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-how-many-bases-58f59e11","Math G6: Understanding the properties of two-dimensional and three-di - Set 4","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-1-2 Understanding the properties of two-dimensional and three-dimensional geometric shapes and using them to identify and classify the shapes.","4. Distinguishing prisms (triangular, quadrilateral) and cubes, identifying their vertices, edges, faces, and bases, and drawing their nets.","How many bases does a triangular prism have?","1 base","2 bases","3 bases","4 bases","2 bases","A triangular prism has two bases, which are the triangular faces at the top and bottom.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-name-of-85a84c6e","Math G6: Understanding the properties of two-dimensional and three-di - Set 4","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-1-2 Understanding the properties of two-dimensional and three-dimensional geometric shapes and using them to identify and classify the shapes.","4. Distinguishing prisms (triangular, quadrilateral) and cubes, identifying their vertices, edges, faces, and bases, and drawing their nets.","What is the name of the point where two or more edges meet?","Face","Edge","Vertex","Base","Vertex","A vertex is the point where two or more edges meet, such as the corner of a cube or a prism.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-how-many-faces-51f7eb68","Math G6: Understanding the properties of two-dimensional and three-di - Set 4","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-1-2 Understanding the properties of two-dimensional and three-dimensional geometric shapes and using them to identify and classify the shapes.","4. Distinguishing prisms (triangular, quadrilateral) and cubes, identifying their vertices, edges, faces, and bases, and drawing their nets.","How many faces does a quadrilateral prism have?","4 faces","5 faces","6 faces","8 faces","6 faces","A quadrilateral prism has 6 faces, which are the two quadrilateral bases and the four rectangular sides.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-how-many-vertices-f5596fff","Math G6: Understanding the properties of two-dimensional and three-di - Set 4","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-1-2 Understanding the properties of two-dimensional and three-dimensional geometric shapes and using them to identify and classify the shapes.","4. Distinguishing prisms (triangular, quadrilateral) and cubes, identifying their vertices, edges, faces, and bases, and drawing their nets.","How many vertices does a cube have?","6 vertices","8 vertices","10 vertices","12 vertices","8 vertices","A cube has 8 vertices, which are the points where the edges meet.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-name-of-79e67966","Math G6: Understanding the properties of two-dimensional and three-di - Set 4","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-1-2 Understanding the properties of two-dimensional and three-dimensional geometric shapes and using them to identify and classify the shapes.","4. Distinguishing prisms (triangular, quadrilateral) and cubes, identifying their vertices, edges, faces, and bases, and drawing their nets.","What is the name of a three-dimensional shape with two identical faces that are triangles?","Triangular prism","Quadrilateral prism","Cuboid","Pyramid","Triangular prism","A triangular prism has two identical triangular faces and three rectangular faces.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-name-of-4c3ce6b7","Math G6: Understanding the properties of two-dimensional and three-di - Set 4","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-1-2 Understanding the properties of two-dimensional and three-dimensional geometric shapes and using them to identify and classify the shapes.","4. Distinguishing prisms (triangular, quadrilateral) and cubes, identifying their vertices, edges, faces, and bases, and drawing their nets.","What is the name of the flat surface of a three-dimensional shape?","Edge","Vertex","Face","Base","Face","A face is a flat surface of a three-dimensional shape, such as a cube or a prism.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-in-a-quadrilateral-59402a3b","Math G6: Understanding the properties of two-dimensional and three-di - Set 5","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-1-2 Understanding the properties of two-dimensional and three-dimensional geometric shapes and using them to identify and classify the shapes.","5. Identifying the sum of the interior angles of a triangle and a quadrilateral, and using them to find the measures of unknown angles.","In a quadrilateral, if three angles are 90 degrees, 60 degrees, and 30 degrees, what is the measure of the fourth angle?","180 degrees","162","198","1,800","180 degrees","Since the sum of the interior angles of a quadrilateral is 360 degrees, we can find the fourth angle by subtracting the sum of the given angles from 360 degrees: 360 - 90 - 60 - 30 = 180 degrees.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-sum-of-0893fa36","Math G6: Understanding the properties of two-dimensional and three-di - Set 5","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-1-2 Understanding the properties of two-dimensional and three-dimensional geometric shapes and using them to identify and classify the shapes.","5. Identifying the sum of the interior angles of a triangle and a quadrilateral, and using them to find the measures of unknown angles.","What is the sum of the interior angles of a triangle with angles 30 degrees, 60 degrees, and 90 degrees?","180 degrees","270 degrees","360 degrees","90 degrees","180 degrees","The sum of the interior angles of a triangle is always 180 degrees, regardless of the measures of the individual angles.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-in-a-quadrilateral-0a65e6e5","Math G6: Understanding the properties of two-dimensional and three-di - Set 5","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-1-2 Understanding the properties of two-dimensional and three-dimensional geometric shapes and using them to identify and classify the shapes.","5. Identifying the sum of the interior angles of a triangle and a quadrilateral, and using them to find the measures of unknown angles.","In a quadrilateral, if two angles are 90 degrees and 45 degrees, what is the sum of the other two angles?","225 degrees","135 degrees","180 degrees","270 degrees","225 degrees","Since the sum of the interior angles of a quadrilateral is 360 degrees, we can find the sum of the other two angles by subtracting the sum of the given angles from 360 degrees: 360 - 90 - 45 = 225 degrees.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-in-a-triangle-1054e98a","Math G6: Understanding the properties of two-dimensional and three-di - Set 5","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-1-2 Understanding the properties of two-dimensional and three-dimensional geometric shapes and using them to identify and classify the shapes.","5. Identifying the sum of the interior angles of a triangle and a quadrilateral, and using them to find the measures of unknown angles.","In a triangle, if one angle is 120 degrees, what is the sum of the other two angles?","60 degrees","54","66","600","60 degrees","Since the sum of the interior angles of a triangle is 180 degrees, we can find the sum of the other two angles by subtracting the given angle from 180 degrees: 180 - 120 = 60 degrees.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-sum-of-63ecf7dc","Math G6: Understanding the properties of two-dimensional and three-di - Set 5","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-1-2 Understanding the properties of two-dimensional and three-dimensional geometric shapes and using them to identify and classify the shapes.","5. Identifying the sum of the interior angles of a triangle and a quadrilateral, and using them to find the measures of unknown angles.","What is the sum of the interior angles of a hexagon?","The sum of the interior angles of a hexagon is 720 degrees.","540 degrees","900 degrees","1080 degrees","The sum of the interior angles of a hexagon is 720 degrees.","The sum of interior angles is (n - 2) x 180. For a hexagon, (6 - 2) x 180 = 720 degrees.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-in-a-quadrilateral-815f9e16","Math G6: Understanding the properties of two-dimensional and three-di - Set 5","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-1-2 Understanding the properties of two-dimensional and three-dimensional geometric shapes and using them to identify and classify the shapes.","5. Identifying the sum of the interior angles of a triangle and a quadrilateral, and using them to find the measures of unknown angles.","In a quadrilateral, if three angles are 90 degrees, 60 degrees, and 45 degrees, what is the measure of the fourth angle?","105 degrees","165 degrees","135 degrees","75 degrees","165 degrees","The angles of a quadrilateral sum to 360°, so the missing angle is 360° minus the other three angles, which is 165°.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-sum-of-69728b6f","Math G6: Understanding the properties of two-dimensional and three-di - Set 5","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-1-2 Understanding the properties of two-dimensional and three-dimensional geometric shapes and using them to identify and classify the shapes.","5. Identifying the sum of the interior angles of a triangle and a quadrilateral, and using them to find the measures of unknown angles.","What is the sum of the interior angles of a pentagon?","540 degrees","450 degrees","630 degrees","720 degrees","540 degrees","The sum of the interior angles of a polygon can be found using the formula (n-2)*180, where n is the number of sides. For a pentagon, n = 5, so the sum of the interior angles is (5-2)*180 = 540 degrees.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-in-a-triangle-837cd9be","Math G6: Understanding the properties of two-dimensional and three-di - Set 5","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-1-2 Understanding the properties of two-dimensional and three-dimensional geometric shapes and using them to identify and classify the shapes.","5. Identifying the sum of the interior angles of a triangle and a quadrilateral, and using them to find the measures of unknown angles.","In a triangle, if two angles are 60 degrees and 80 degrees, what is the measure of the third angle?","40 degrees","60 degrees","80 degrees","120 degrees","40 degrees","Since the sum of the interior angles of a triangle is 180 degrees, we can find the third angle by subtracting the sum of the given angles from 180 degrees: 180 - 60 - 80 = 40 degrees.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-sum-of-e93407dc","Math G6: Understanding the properties of two-dimensional and three-di - Set 5","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-1-2 Understanding the properties of two-dimensional and three-dimensional geometric shapes and using them to identify and classify the shapes.","5. Identifying the sum of the interior angles of a triangle and a quadrilateral, and using them to find the measures of unknown angles.","What is the sum of the interior angles of a quadrilateral?","360 degrees","270 degrees","180 degrees","90 degrees","360 degrees","The sum of the interior angles of a quadrilateral is always 360 degrees.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-sum-of-0b43ee3b","Math G6: Understanding the properties of two-dimensional and three-di - Set 5","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-1-2 Understanding the properties of two-dimensional and three-dimensional geometric shapes and using them to identify and classify the shapes.","5. Identifying the sum of the interior angles of a triangle and a quadrilateral, and using them to find the measures of unknown angles.","What is the sum of the interior angles of a triangle?","180 degrees","270 degrees","360 degrees","90 degrees","180 degrees","The sum of the interior angles of a triangle is always 180 degrees.","","",""
