"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-storage-container-cd73e89f","Math G6: Understanding volume and surface area and their units - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-3-3 Understanding volume and surface area and their units, distinguishing the formulas for a right rectangular prism, and applying them to find volume and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","2. Distinguishing the formula for the volume of a right rectangular prism and using it to calculate its volume.","A storage container has a length of 8 meters, a width of 4 meters, and a height of 2 meters. What is its volume?","32 cubic meters","40 cubic meters","48 cubic meters","64 cubic meters","64 cubic meters","To find the volume, multiply the length, width, and height: 8 meters * 4 meters * 2 meters = 64 cubic meters.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-water-tank-a7030535","Math G6: Understanding volume and surface area and their units - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-3-3 Understanding volume and surface area and their units, distinguishing the formulas for a right rectangular prism, and applying them to find volume and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","2. Distinguishing the formula for the volume of a right rectangular prism and using it to calculate its volume.","A water tank has a length of 10 meters, a width of 5 meters, and a height of 4 meters. What is its volume?","100 cubic meters","150 cubic meters","200 cubic meters","250 cubic meters","200 cubic meters","To find the volume, multiply the length, width, and height: 10 meters * 5 meters * 4 meters = 200 cubic meters.","","",""
