"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-surface-area-4014ac6b","Math G6: Understanding volume and surface area and their units - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-3-3 Understanding volume and surface area and their units, distinguishing the formulas for a right rectangular prism, and applying them to find volume and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","2. Distinguishing the formula for the volume of a right rectangular prism and using it to calculate its volume.","What is the surface area of a right rectangular prism with a length of 9 cm, a width of 6 cm, and a height of 4 cm?","156 square centimeters","168 square centimeters","228 square centimeters","208 square centimeters","228 square centimeters","Surface area = 2lw + 2lh + 2wh = 2(9*6) + 2(9*4) + 2(6*4) = 108 + 72 + 48 = 228 square centimeters.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-surface-area-8c13d45c","Math G6: Understanding volume and surface area and their units - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-3-3 Understanding volume and surface area and their units, distinguishing the formulas for a right rectangular prism, and applying them to find volume and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","2. Distinguishing the formula for the volume of a right rectangular prism and using it to calculate its volume.","What is the surface area of a right rectangular prism with a length of 20 cm, a width of 10 cm, and a height of 6 cm?","472 square centimeters","492 square centimeters","760 square centimeters","532 square centimeters","760 square centimeters","Surface area = 2lw + 2lh + 2wh = 2(20*10) + 2(20*6) + 2(10*6) = 400 + 240 + 120 = 760 square centimeters.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-cardboard-box-791a788b","Math G6: Understanding volume and surface area and their units - Set 3 T1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-3-3 Understanding volume and surface area and their units, distinguishing the formulas for a right rectangular prism, and applying them to find volume and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","2. Distinguishing the formula for the volume of a right rectangular prism and using it to calculate its volume.","A cardboard box is 25 inches long, 15 inches wide, and 10 inches tall. What is the surface area of the box?","950 square inches","1050 square inches","1550 square inches","1250 square inches","1550 square inches","Surface area = 2lw + 2lh + 2wh = 2(25*15) + 2(25*10) + 2(15*10) = 750 + 500 + 300 = 1550 square inches.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-water-tank-e04ccccb","Math G6: Understanding volume and surface area and their units - Set 4","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-3-3 Understanding volume and surface area and their units, distinguishing the formulas for a right rectangular prism, and applying them to find volume and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","2. Distinguishing the formula for the volume of a right rectangular prism and using it to calculate its volume.","A water tank is 3 meters long, 2 meters wide, and 1 meter high. What is its volume in cubic meters?","2 cubic meters","5 cubic meters","6 cubic meters","1 cubic meter","6 cubic meters","To find the volume of the tank, multiply the length, width, and height: 3 meters x 2 meters x 1 meter = 6 cubic meters.","/image-source/math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-water-tank-e04ccccb.png","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-storage-container-618a6e10","Math G6: Understanding volume and surface area and their units - Set 4","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-3-3 Understanding volume and surface area and their units, distinguishing the formulas for a right rectangular prism, and applying them to find volume and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","2. Distinguishing the formula for the volume of a right rectangular prism and using it to calculate its volume.","A storage container has dimensions 12 meters x 8 meters x 4 meters. What is its volume in cubic meters?","256 cubic meters","384 cubic meters","192 cubic meters","96 cubic meters","384 cubic meters","To find the volume, multiply the length, width, and height: 12 meters x 8 meters x 4 meters = 384 cubic meters.","/image-source/math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-storage-container-618a6e10.png","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-fish-tank-0b6d1af4","Math G6: Understanding volume and surface area and their units - Set 4","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-3-3 Understanding volume and surface area and their units, distinguishing the formulas for a right rectangular prism, and applying them to find volume and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","2. Distinguishing the formula for the volume of a right rectangular prism and using it to calculate its volume.","A fish tank is 60 cm long, 30 cm wide, and 20 cm high. What is its volume in cubic centimeters?","24000 cubic centimeters","36000 cubic centimeters","18000 cubic centimeters","72000 cubic centimeters","36000 cubic centimeters","To find the volume, multiply the length, width, and height: 60 cm x 30 cm x 20 cm = 36000 cubic centimeters.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-gift-box-4fdccd34","Math G6: Understanding volume and surface area and their units - Set 4","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-3-3 Understanding volume and surface area and their units, distinguishing the formulas for a right rectangular prism, and applying them to find volume and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","2. Distinguishing the formula for the volume of a right rectangular prism and using it to calculate its volume.","A gift box has dimensions 15 cm x 10 cm x 5 cm. What is its volume?","500 cubic centimeters","750 cubic centimeters","1000 cubic centimeters","2000 cubic centimeters","750 cubic centimeters","To find the volume, multiply the length, width, and height: 15 cm x 10 cm x 5 cm = 750 cubic centimeters.","/image-source/math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-gift-box-4fdccd34.png","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-volume-of-59ce0ae1","Math G6: Understanding volume and surface area and their units - Set 4","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-3-3 Understanding volume and surface area and their units, distinguishing the formulas for a right rectangular prism, and applying them to find volume and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","2. Distinguishing the formula for the volume of a right rectangular prism and using it to calculate its volume.","What is the volume of a rectangular prism with a length of 6 cm, a width of 4 cm, and a height of 2 cm?","The volume is 48 cubic centimeters.","The volume is 44 cubic centimeters.","The volume is 52 cubic centimeters.","The volume is 480 cubic centimeters.","The volume is 48 cubic centimeters.","Volume of a rectangular prism is length x width x height: 6 x 4 x 2 = 48 cubic centimeters.","/image-source/math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-volume-of-59ce0ae1.png","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-bookshelf-has-8730e2f1","Math G6: Understanding volume and surface area and their units - Set 4","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-3-3 Understanding volume and surface area and their units, distinguishing the formulas for a right rectangular prism, and applying them to find volume and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","2. Distinguishing the formula for the volume of a right rectangular prism and using it to calculate its volume.","A bookshelf has 5 shelves, and each shelf has a rectangular prism shape with dimensions 30 cm x 20 cm x 10 cm. What is the total volume of the bookshelf?","3000 cubic centimeters","10000 cubic centimeters","15000 cubic centimeters","30000 cubic centimeters","30000 cubic centimeters","First, find the volume of one shelf: 30 cm x 20 cm x 10 cm = 6000 cubic centimeters. Then, multiply by the number of shelves: 6000 cubic centimeters x 5 = 30000 cubic centimeters.","/image-source/math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-bookshelf-has-8730e2f1.png","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-rectangular-prism-1bc0e064","Math G6: Understanding volume and surface area and their units - Set 4","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-3-3 Understanding volume and surface area and their units, distinguishing the formulas for a right rectangular prism, and applying them to find volume and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","2. Distinguishing the formula for the volume of a right rectangular prism and using it to calculate its volume.","A rectangular prism has a length of 9 cm, a width of 6 cm, and a height of 4 cm. What is its volume?","The volume is 216 cubic centimeters.","The volume is 195 cubic centimeters.","The volume is 237 cubic centimeters.","The volume is 2,160 cubic centimeters.","The volume is 216 cubic centimeters.","Volume of a rectangular prism is length x width x height: 9 x 6 x 4 = 216 cubic centimeters.","/image-source/math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-rectangular-prism-1bc0e064.png","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-surface-area-1b3f9bb4","Math G6: Understanding volume and surface area and their units - Set 4","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-3-3 Understanding volume and surface area and their units, distinguishing the formulas for a right rectangular prism, and applying them to find volume and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","2. Distinguishing the formula for the volume of a right rectangular prism and using it to calculate its volume.","What is the surface area of a rectangular prism with dimensions 8 cm x 5 cm x 3 cm?","94 square centimeters","118 square centimeters","158 square centimeters","102 square centimeters","158 square centimeters","Surface area = 2lw + 2lh + 2wh = 2(8*5) + 2(8*3) + 2(5*3) = 80 + 48 + 30 = 158 square centimeters.","/image-source/math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-surface-area-1b3f9bb4.png","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-swimming-pool-aeeec564","Math G6: Understanding volume and surface area and their units - Set 4","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-3-3 Understanding volume and surface area and their units, distinguishing the formulas for a right rectangular prism, and applying them to find volume and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","2. Distinguishing the formula for the volume of a right rectangular prism and using it to calculate its volume.","A swimming pool is 10 meters long, 5 meters wide, and 2 meters deep. What is its volume in cubic meters?","50 cubic meters","100 cubic meters","75 cubic meters","25 cubic meters","100 cubic meters","To find the volume of the pool, multiply the length, width, and depth: 10 meters x 5 meters x 2 meters = 100 cubic meters.","/image-source/math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-swimming-pool-aeeec564.png","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-box-of-4fd55974","Math G6: Understanding volume and surface area and their units - Set 4","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-3-3 Understanding volume and surface area and their units, distinguishing the formulas for a right rectangular prism, and applying them to find volume and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","2. Distinguishing the formula for the volume of a right rectangular prism and using it to calculate its volume.","A box of cereal has dimensions 20 cm x 10 cm x 5 cm. What is its volume?","500 cubic centimeters","1000 cubic centimeters","100 cubic centimeters","2000 cubic centimeters","1000 cubic centimeters","To find the volume, multiply the length, width, and height: 20 cm x 10 cm x 5 cm = 1000 cubic centimeters.","/image-source/math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-box-of-4fd55974.png","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-distance-points-b4a8c4b2","Math G6: Using the coordinate plane to name and locate points - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-2-1 Using the coordinate plane to name and locate points, and identifying and performing geometric transformations.","2. Distinguishing translations on the coordinate plane (first quadrant) and drawing the image of a figure under a given translation.","What is the distance between the points (1, 2) and (4, 5) in the first quadrant of the coordinate plane?","3 units","5 units","The distance cannot be determined","Approximately 5 units","Approximately 5 units","To find the distance between two points, use the distance formula: d = sqrt((x2 - x1)^2 + (y2 - y1)^2). Plugging in the values, d = sqrt((4 - 1)^2 + (5 - 2)^2) = sqrt(3^2 + 3^2) = sqrt(18) = approximately 5 units.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-y-coordinate-00deb4dd","Math G6: Using the coordinate plane to name and locate points - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-2-1 Using the coordinate plane to name and locate points, and identifying and performing geometric transformations.","3. Distinguishing reflections across an axis on the coordinate plane (first quadrant) and drawing the image of a figure under a given reflection.","What is the y-coordinate of a point that lies on the x-axis in the first quadrant of the coordinate plane?","Any positive number","Any negative number","Zero","It cannot be determined","Zero","The x-axis is the horizontal line where y = 0, so any point that lies on the x-axis has a y-coordinate of 0.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-triangle-has-9e497b55","Math G6: Using the coordinate plane to name and locate points - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-2-1 Using the coordinate plane to name and locate points, and identifying and performing geometric transformations.","1. Distinguishing the elements of the coordinate plane (first quadrant), naming and locating points using ordered pairs, and drawing polygons given the coordinates of their vertices.","A triangle has vertices at (2, 3), (4, 3), and (3, 5) in the first quadrant of the coordinate plane. What type of triangle is it?","Equilateral triangle","Isosceles triangle","Right triangle","Scalene triangle","Isosceles triangle","To determine the type of triangle, calculate the lengths of its sides using the distance formula. The side lengths are approximately 2, 2, and 2.8 units, indicating an isosceles triangle.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-point-has-ad46b03a","Math G6: Using the coordinate plane to name and locate points - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-2-1 Using the coordinate plane to name and locate points, and identifying and performing geometric transformations.","1. Distinguishing the elements of the coordinate plane (first quadrant), naming and locating points using ordered pairs, and drawing polygons given the coordinates of their vertices.","A point has coordinates (x, y) in the first quadrant of the coordinate plane. If x = 2 and y = 5, what are the coordinates of the point?","(2, 5)","(5, 2)","(2, -5)","(-2, 5)","(2, 5)","Given x = 2 and y = 5, the coordinates of the point are (2, 5) since x is the first value and y is the second value in an ordered pair.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-name-of-9bf3cc0a","Math G6: Using the coordinate plane to name and locate points - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-2-1 Using the coordinate plane to name and locate points, and identifying and performing geometric transformations.","3. Distinguishing reflections across an axis on the coordinate plane (first quadrant) and drawing the image of a figure under a given reflection.","What is the name of the line that contains all points with a y-coordinate of 0 in the first quadrant of the coordinate plane?","The x-axis","The y-axis","The origin","The coordinate plane","The x-axis","The x-axis is defined as the horizontal line where y = 0, so it contains all points with a y-coordinate of 0.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-rectangle-has-488df367","Math G6: Using the coordinate plane to name and locate points - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-2-1 Using the coordinate plane to name and locate points, and identifying and performing geometric transformations.","1. Distinguishing the elements of the coordinate plane (first quadrant), naming and locating points using ordered pairs, and drawing polygons given the coordinates of their vertices.","A rectangle has vertices at (1, 1), (1, 4), (4, 1), and (4, 4) in the first quadrant of the coordinate plane. What are the coordinates of the vertices of the rectangle?","(1, 1), (1, 4), (4, 1), (4, 4)","(1, 4), (4, 1), (1, 1), (4, 4)","(4, 4), (1, 1), (4, 1), (1, 4)","(1, 1), (4, 4), (1, 4), (4, 1)","(1, 1), (1, 4), (4, 1), (4, 4)","The given coordinates (1, 1), (1, 4), (4, 1), and (4, 4) form a rectangle in the first quadrant of the coordinate plane.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-quadrant-in-dc78117b","Math G6: Using the coordinate plane to name and locate points - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-2-1 Using the coordinate plane to name and locate points, and identifying and performing geometric transformations.","1. Distinguishing the elements of the coordinate plane (first quadrant), naming and locating points using ordered pairs, and drawing polygons given the coordinates of their vertices.","What is the quadrant in which the point (3, 2) is located?","First quadrant","Second quadrant","Third quadrant","Fourth quadrant","First quadrant","The first quadrant is defined as the area where x > 0 and y > 0. Since the point (3, 2) has both x and y values greater than 0, it is located in the first quadrant.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-point-is-2e26cddd","Math G6: Using the coordinate plane to name and locate points - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-2-1 Using the coordinate plane to name and locate points, and identifying and performing geometric transformations.","1. Distinguishing the elements of the coordinate plane (first quadrant), naming and locating points using ordered pairs, and drawing polygons given the coordinates of their vertices.","A point is located at (2, 5) in the first quadrant of the coordinate plane. If the x-coordinate increases by 1, what are the new coordinates of the point?","(2, 5)","(3, 5)","(2, 6)","(1, 5)","(3, 5)","If the x-coordinate increases by 1, the new x-coordinate is 2 + 1 = 3. The y-coordinate remains the same, so the new coordinates are (3, 5).","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-x-coordinate-d068f09d","Math G6: Using the coordinate plane to name and locate points - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-2-1 Using the coordinate plane to name and locate points, and identifying and performing geometric transformations.","3. Distinguishing reflections across an axis on the coordinate plane (first quadrant) and drawing the image of a figure under a given reflection.","What is the x-coordinate of a point that lies on the y-axis in the first quadrant of the coordinate plane?","Any positive number","Any negative number","Zero","It cannot be determined","Zero","The y-axis is the vertical line where x = 0, so any point that lies on the y-axis has an x-coordinate of 0.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-name-of-e20999e0","Math G6: Using the coordinate plane to name and locate points - Set 1","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-2-1 Using the coordinate plane to name and locate points, and identifying and performing geometric transformations.","1. Distinguishing the elements of the coordinate plane (first quadrant), naming and locating points using ordered pairs, and drawing polygons given the coordinates of their vertices.","What is the name of the point with coordinates (3, 4) in the first quadrant of the coordinate plane?","Point A","Point B","Point C","The point is located at (3, 4)","The point is located at (3, 4)","In the coordinate plane, points are named using their ordered pairs, so the point with coordinates (3, 4) is simply referred to as the point (3, 4).","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-if-a-figure-7331b0c4","Math G6: Using the coordinate plane to name and locate points - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-2-1 Using the coordinate plane to name and locate points, and identifying and performing geometric transformations.","2. Distinguishing translations on the coordinate plane (first quadrant) and drawing the image of a figure under a given translation.","If a figure is translated 3 units to the right and then 2 units up, what is the overall effect on its position?","It moves 3 units to the right and 2 units down","It moves 3 units to the right and 2 units up","It moves 2 units to the left and 3 units up","It moves 2 units to the right and 3 units down","It moves 3 units to the right and 2 units up","The translation of 3 units to the right increases the x-coordinates, and the translation of 2 units up increases the y-coordinates. These effects are cumulative, resulting in the figure moving 3 units to the right and 2 units up overall.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-rectangle-has-b8e74cdd","Math G6: Using the coordinate plane to name and locate points - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-2-1 Using the coordinate plane to name and locate points, and identifying and performing geometric transformations.","2. Distinguishing translations on the coordinate plane (first quadrant) and drawing the image of a figure under a given translation.","A rectangle has vertices at (0, 0), (4, 0), (4, 3), and (0, 3). If it is translated 2 units to the right and 1 unit down, what are the new coordinates of the vertices?","(2, -1), (6, -1), (6, 2), (2, 2)","(0, 0), (4, 0), (4, 3), (0, 3)","(2, 1), (6, 1), (6, 4), (2, 4)","(1, 2), (5, 2), (5, 5), (1, 5)","(2, -1), (6, -1), (6, 2), (2, 2)","To translate the vertices, add 2 to the x-coordinates and subtract 1 from the y-coordinates. So, (0 + 2, 0 - 1) = (2, -1), (4 + 2, 0 - 1) = (6, -1), (4 + 2, 3 - 1) = (6, 2), and (0 + 2, 3 - 1) = (2, 2).","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-point-is-57d617d1","Math G6: Using the coordinate plane to name and locate points - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-2-1 Using the coordinate plane to name and locate points, and identifying and performing geometric transformations.","1. Distinguishing the elements of the coordinate plane (first quadrant), naming and locating points using ordered pairs, and drawing polygons given the coordinates of their vertices.","A point is located at (2, 5) on the coordinate plane. If it is translated 1 unit to the left and 2 units down, what are the new coordinates of the point?","(1, 3)","(3, 7)","(2, 5)","(1, 7)","(1, 3)","To translate the point 1 unit to the left, subtract 1 from the x-coordinate. To translate it 2 units down, subtract 2 from the y-coordinate. So, (2 - 1, 5 - 2) = (1, 3).","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-if-a-point-1590e3b0","Math G6: Using the coordinate plane to name and locate points - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-2-1 Using the coordinate plane to name and locate points, and identifying and performing geometric transformations.","1. Distinguishing the elements of the coordinate plane (first quadrant), naming and locating points using ordered pairs, and drawing polygons given the coordinates of their vertices.","If a point (x, y) is translated 2 units to the left, what are its new coordinates?","(x + 2, y)","(x - 2, y)","(x, y + 2)","(x, y - 2)","(x - 2, y)","To translate a point 2 units to the left, subtract 2 from the x-coordinate. The y-coordinate remains the same.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-effect-of-ab815455","Math G6: Using the coordinate plane to name and locate points - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-2-1 Using the coordinate plane to name and locate points, and identifying and performing geometric transformations.","2. Distinguishing translations on the coordinate plane (first quadrant) and drawing the image of a figure under a given translation.","What is the effect of translating a figure 1 unit up and 1 unit to the right on the coordinate plane?","The figure gets smaller","The figure gets larger","The figure moves diagonally up and to the right","The figure remains the same","The figure moves diagonally up and to the right","Translating a figure 1 unit up and 1 unit to the right moves it diagonally in the direction up and to the right, because both the x and y coordinates are increased by 1.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-type-of-transformation-c2f84b1a","Math G6: Using the coordinate plane to name and locate points - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-2-1 Using the coordinate plane to name and locate points, and identifying and performing geometric transformations.","2. Distinguishing translations on the coordinate plane (first quadrant) and drawing the image of a figure under a given translation.","What type of transformation occurs when a figure is moved 3 units to the left on the coordinate plane?","Rotation","Reflection","Translation","Dilation","Translation","A translation is a transformation that moves a figure a certain distance in a certain direction. In this case, the figure is moved 3 units to the left.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-point-is-fd4b9945","Math G6: Using the coordinate plane to name and locate points - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-2-1 Using the coordinate plane to name and locate points, and identifying and performing geometric transformations.","1. Distinguishing the elements of the coordinate plane (first quadrant), naming and locating points using ordered pairs, and drawing polygons given the coordinates of their vertices.","A point is located at (3, 4) on the coordinate plane. If it is translated 2 units to the right and 1 unit up, what are the new coordinates of the point?","(1, 3)","(3, 4)","(5, 5)","(2, 6)","(5, 5)","To translate a point 2 units to the right, add 2 to the x-coordinate. To translate it 1 unit up, add 1 to the y-coordinate. So, (3 + 2, 4 + 1) = (5, 5).","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-if-a-figure-ee8fff37","Math G6: Using the coordinate plane to name and locate points - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-2-1 Using the coordinate plane to name and locate points, and identifying and performing geometric transformations.","2. Distinguishing translations on the coordinate plane (first quadrant) and drawing the image of a figure under a given translation.","If a figure is translated 2 units up and then 1 unit to the left, what is the overall effect on its position?","It moves 2 units up and 1 unit to the right","It moves 2 units up and 1 unit to the left","It moves 1 unit up and 2 units to the left","It moves 1 unit down and 2 units to the right","It moves 2 units up and 1 unit to the left","The translation of 2 units up increases the y-coordinates, and the translation of 1 unit to the left decreases the x-coordinates. These effects are cumulative, resulting in the figure moving 2 units up and 1 unit to the left overall.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-type-of-transformation-01c20557","Math G6: Using the coordinate plane to name and locate points - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-2-1 Using the coordinate plane to name and locate points, and identifying and performing geometric transformations.","2. Distinguishing translations on the coordinate plane (first quadrant) and drawing the image of a figure under a given translation.","What type of transformation changes the position of a figure without changing its size or orientation?","Rotation","Reflection","Translation","Dilation","Translation","A translation is a transformation that moves a figure from one position to another without changing its size, shape, or orientation.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-triangle-has-65fd1a99","Math G6: Using the coordinate plane to name and locate points - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-2-1 Using the coordinate plane to name and locate points, and identifying and performing geometric transformations.","1. Distinguishing the elements of the coordinate plane (first quadrant), naming and locating points using ordered pairs, and drawing polygons given the coordinates of their vertices.","A triangle has vertices at (1, 2), (3, 4), and (2, 1). If it is translated 1 unit to the right and 2 units down, what are the new coordinates of the vertices?","(0, 0), (2, 2), (1, -1)","(2, 0), (4, 2), (3, -1)","(1, 2), (3, 4), (2, 1)","(0, 4), (2, 6), (1, 3)","(2, 0), (4, 2), (3, -1)","To translate 1 unit right, add 1 to each x-coordinate. To translate 2 units down, subtract 2 from each y-coordinate. The new vertices are (2, 0), (4, 2), and (3, -1).","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-image-of-e1d36bec","Math G6: Using the coordinate plane to name and locate points - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-2-1 Using the coordinate plane to name and locate points, and identifying and performing geometric transformations.","3. Distinguishing reflections across an axis on the coordinate plane (first quadrant) and drawing the image of a figure under a given reflection.","What is the image of point (1, 2) after a reflection across the x-axis?","(1, 2)","(1, -2)","(-1, 2)","(-1, -2)","(1, -2)","When reflecting a point across the x-axis, the y-coordinate changes sign.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-image-of-3d028abf","Math G6: Using the coordinate plane to name and locate points - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-2-1 Using the coordinate plane to name and locate points, and identifying and performing geometric transformations.","3. Distinguishing reflections across an axis on the coordinate plane (first quadrant) and drawing the image of a figure under a given reflection.","What is the image of point (3, 4) after a reflection across the x-axis?","(3, -4)","(3, 4)","(-3, 4)","(-3, -4)","(3, -4)","When reflecting a point across the x-axis, the y-coordinate changes sign.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-if-a-figure-71f020eb","Math G6: Using the coordinate plane to name and locate points - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-2-1 Using the coordinate plane to name and locate points, and identifying and performing geometric transformations.","3. Distinguishing reflections across an axis on the coordinate plane (first quadrant) and drawing the image of a figure under a given reflection.","If a figure is reflected across the y-axis, what happens to its x-coordinate?","It stays the same","It becomes positive if it was negative","It becomes negative if it was positive","It is doubled","It becomes negative if it was positive","When reflecting a point across the y-axis, the x-coordinate changes sign.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-type-of-transformation-7c3a9005","Math G6: Using the coordinate plane to name and locate points - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-2-1 Using the coordinate plane to name and locate points, and identifying and performing geometric transformations.","3. Distinguishing reflections across an axis on the coordinate plane (first quadrant) and drawing the image of a figure under a given reflection.","What type of transformation is shown when a figure is flipped over the x-axis?","Translation","Rotation","Reflection","Dilation","Reflection","A reflection is a transformation that flips a figure over a line, in this case the x-axis.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-if-point-2-d9426cb5","Math G6: Using the coordinate plane to name and locate points - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-2-1 Using the coordinate plane to name and locate points, and identifying and performing geometric transformations.","3. Distinguishing reflections across an axis on the coordinate plane (first quadrant) and drawing the image of a figure under a given reflection.","If point (2, 5) is reflected across the y-axis, what are the coordinates of its image?","(2, 5)","(2, -5)","(-2, 5)","(-2, -5)","(-2, 5)","When reflecting a point across the y-axis, the x-coordinate changes sign.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-if-a-figure-8618ae00","Math G6: Using the coordinate plane to name and locate points - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-2-1 Using the coordinate plane to name and locate points, and identifying and performing geometric transformations.","3. Distinguishing reflections across an axis on the coordinate plane (first quadrant) and drawing the image of a figure under a given reflection.","If a figure is reflected across the x-axis, what happens to its y-coordinate?","It stays the same","It becomes positive if it was negative","It becomes negative if it was positive","It is doubled","It becomes negative if it was positive","When reflecting a point across the x-axis, the y-coordinate changes sign.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-type-of-transformation-922d039f","Math G6: Using the coordinate plane to name and locate points - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-2-1 Using the coordinate plane to name and locate points, and identifying and performing geometric transformations.","3. Distinguishing reflections across an axis on the coordinate plane (first quadrant) and drawing the image of a figure under a given reflection.","What type of transformation is shown when a figure is flipped over the y-axis?","Translation","Rotation","Reflection","Dilation","Reflection","A reflection is a transformation that flips a figure over a line, in this case the y-axis.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-if-point-4-28e10956","Math G6: Using the coordinate plane to name and locate points - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-2-1 Using the coordinate plane to name and locate points, and identifying and performing geometric transformations.","3. Distinguishing reflections across an axis on the coordinate plane (first quadrant) and drawing the image of a figure under a given reflection.","If point (4, 3) is reflected across the x-axis, what are the coordinates of its image?","(4, 3)","(4, -3)","(-4, 3)","(-4, -3)","(4, -3)","When reflecting a point across the x-axis, the y-coordinate changes sign.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-image-of-9aa317dd","Math G6: Using the coordinate plane to name and locate points - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-2-1 Using the coordinate plane to name and locate points, and identifying and performing geometric transformations.","3. Distinguishing reflections across an axis on the coordinate plane (first quadrant) and drawing the image of a figure under a given reflection.","What is the image of point (5, 1) after a reflection across the y-axis?","(5, 1)","(5, -1)","(-5, 1)","(-5, -1)","(-5, 1)","When reflecting a point across the y-axis, the x-coordinate changes sign.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-if-a-figure-2715101c","Math G6: Using the coordinate plane to name and locate points - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-2-1 Using the coordinate plane to name and locate points, and identifying and performing geometric transformations.","3. Distinguishing reflections across an axis on the coordinate plane (first quadrant) and drawing the image of a figure under a given reflection.","If a figure is reflected across an axis, what is the relationship between the original figure and its image?","They are the same size but different shape","They are the same shape but different size","They are the same shape and size but different orientation","They are the same shape and size and the same orientation","They are the same shape and size but different orientation","A reflection is a transformation that flips a figure over a line, resulting in the same shape and size but a different orientation.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-point-is-1cbfc466","Math G6: Using the coordinate plane to name and locate points - Set 4","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-2-1 Using the coordinate plane to name and locate points, and identifying and performing geometric transformations.","1. Distinguishing the elements of the coordinate plane (first quadrant), naming and locating points using ordered pairs, and drawing polygons given the coordinates of their vertices.","A point is located at (3, 4) on the coordinate plane. If it is rotated 90 degrees counterclockwise about the origin, what are its new coordinates?","(3, -4)","(4, 3)","(-3, 4)","(-4, -3)","(4, 3)","When a point is rotated 90 degrees counterclockwise about the origin, its x and y coordinates switch places, resulting in new coordinates of (4, 3).","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-effect-of-4bd7ab8b","Math G6: Using the coordinate plane to name and locate points - Set 4","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-2-1 Using the coordinate plane to name and locate points, and identifying and performing geometric transformations.","3. Distinguishing reflections across an axis on the coordinate plane (first quadrant) and drawing the image of a figure under a given reflection.","What is the effect of rotating a figure 360 degrees about the origin on the coordinate plane?","The figure is reflected across the x-axis","The figure is reflected across the y-axis","The figure remains in the same location","The figure is rotated to a new location","The figure remains in the same location","When a figure is rotated 360 degrees about the origin, it returns to its original location, resulting in no net change.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-figure-is-a3a03e83","Math G6: Using the coordinate plane to name and locate points - Set 4","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-2-1 Using the coordinate plane to name and locate points, and identifying and performing geometric transformations.","4. Distinguishing rotations about a point on the coordinate plane (first quadrant) and drawing the image of a figure under a given rotation.","A figure is rotated 270 degrees counterclockwise about the origin on the coordinate plane. What is the result?","The figure is rotated to the same location as a 90 degree clockwise rotation","The figure is rotated to the same location as a 90 degree counterclockwise rotation","The figure is reflected across the x-axis","The figure is reflected across the y-axis","The figure is rotated to the same location as a 90 degree clockwise rotation","When a figure is rotated 270 degrees counterclockwise about the origin, it is equivalent to a 90 degree clockwise rotation.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-type-of-rotation-bb79dde1","Math G6: Using the coordinate plane to name and locate points - Set 4","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-2-1 Using the coordinate plane to name and locate points, and identifying and performing geometric transformations.","4. Distinguishing rotations about a point on the coordinate plane (first quadrant) and drawing the image of a figure under a given rotation.","What type of rotation will move a point from the first quadrant to the second quadrant on the coordinate plane?","90 degree clockwise rotation","90 degree counterclockwise rotation","180 degree rotation","270 degree counterclockwise rotation","90 degree counterclockwise rotation","A 90 degree counterclockwise rotation will move a point from the first quadrant to the second quadrant.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-type-of-transformation-90d9d7f3","Math G6: Using the coordinate plane to name and locate points - Set 4","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-2-1 Using the coordinate plane to name and locate points, and identifying and performing geometric transformations.","4. Distinguishing rotations about a point on the coordinate plane (first quadrant) and drawing the image of a figure under a given rotation.","What type of transformation occurs when a point is rotated 90 degrees clockwise about the origin on the coordinate plane?","Translation","Reflection","Rotation","Dilation","Rotation","When a point is rotated 90 degrees clockwise about the origin, it undergoes a rotation transformation.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-figure-is-0dbe86d1","Math G6: Using the coordinate plane to name and locate points - Set 4","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-2-1 Using the coordinate plane to name and locate points, and identifying and performing geometric transformations.","3. Distinguishing reflections across an axis on the coordinate plane (first quadrant) and drawing the image of a figure under a given reflection.","A figure is rotated 180 degrees about the origin on the coordinate plane. What is the result?","The figure is reflected across the x-axis","The figure is reflected across the y-axis","The figure remains in the same location","The figure is rotated to the opposite quadrant, with its x and y coordinates changing signs","The figure is rotated to the opposite quadrant, with its x and y coordinates changing signs","When a figure is rotated 180 degrees about the origin, it is rotated to the opposite quadrant, with its x and y coordinates changing signs.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-happens-to-a-f2bfe40f","Math G6: Using the coordinate plane to name and locate points - Set 4","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-2-1 Using the coordinate plane to name and locate points, and identifying and performing geometric transformations.","2. Distinguishing translations on the coordinate plane (first quadrant) and drawing the image of a figure under a given translation.","What happens to a point on the coordinate plane when it is rotated 90 degrees counterclockwise about the origin?","The point moves to the right and up","The point moves to the left and down","The point remains in the same location","The point moves up and to the left, with its x and y coordinates switching places","The point moves up and to the left, with its x and y coordinates switching places","When a point is rotated 90 degrees counterclockwise about the origin, its x and y coordinates switch places.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-point-is-274a4d29","Math G6: Using the coordinate plane to name and locate points - Set 4","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-2-1 Using the coordinate plane to name and locate points, and identifying and performing geometric transformations.","1. Distinguishing the elements of the coordinate plane (first quadrant), naming and locating points using ordered pairs, and drawing polygons given the coordinates of their vertices.","A point is located at (2, 5) on the coordinate plane. If it is rotated 180 degrees about the origin, what are its new coordinates?","(2, -5)","(-2, 5)","(2, 5)","(-2, -5)","(-2, -5)","When a point is rotated 180 degrees about the origin, its x and y coordinates change signs, resulting in new coordinates of (-2, -5).","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-is-result-of-dea54a4f","Math G6: Using the coordinate plane to name and locate points - Set 4","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-2-1 Using the coordinate plane to name and locate points, and identifying and performing geometric transformations.","4. Distinguishing rotations about a point on the coordinate plane (first quadrant) and drawing the image of a figure under a given rotation.","What is the result of rotating a figure 90 degrees clockwise and then 90 degrees counterclockwise about the origin on the coordinate plane?","The figure is translated to a new location","The figure is reflected across the x-axis","The figure is reflected across the y-axis","The figure returns to its original location","The figure returns to its original location","When a figure is rotated 90 degrees clockwise and then 90 degrees counterclockwise about the origin, the two rotations cancel each other out, resulting in the figure returning to its original location.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-figure-is-7d79d2ad","Math G6: Using the coordinate plane to name and locate points - Set 4","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-2-1 Using the coordinate plane to name and locate points, and identifying and performing geometric transformations.","4. Distinguishing rotations about a point on the coordinate plane (first quadrant) and drawing the image of a figure under a given rotation.","A figure is rotated 90 degrees counterclockwise about the origin on the coordinate plane. What is the relationship between the original and image coordinates?","The x-coordinate of the image is the negative of the y-coordinate of the original","The x-coordinate of the image is the y-coordinate of the original","The y-coordinate of the image is the negative of the x-coordinate of the original","The y-coordinate of the image is the x-coordinate of the original","The x-coordinate of the image is the y-coordinate of the original","When a figure is rotated 90 degrees counterclockwise about the origin, the x-coordinate of the image is the y-coordinate of the original, and the y-coordinate of the image is the negative of the x-coordinate of the original.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-rectangular-garden-0d805a04","Patterns: Numerical & Geometric Growth - Math (Grade 6)","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-3-3-3 Understanding volume and surface area and their units, distinguishing the formulas for a right rectangular prism, and applying them to find volume and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Estimating volume and measuring it by using the appropriate metric unit (ml3, cm3, and m3).","A rectangular garden measures 10 meters by 5 meters. What is the area of the garden in square meters?","50 square meters.","25 square meters","60 square meters","15 square meters","50 square meters.","Area = length x width = 10 x 5 = 50 square meters.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-bakery-sells-489b3887","Patterns: Numerical & Geometric Growth - Math (Grade 6)","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-2-1-1 Distinguishing growing numerical and geometric patterns, generating and generalizing them, exploring relationships from data tables, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","5. Solving mathematical problems including real-life applications of patterns and inputâ€“output tables, and explaining their solutions.","A bakery sells a total of 250 loaves of bread and 150 pastries per day. If they make $2 per loaf of bread and $3 per pastry, how much money does the bakery make in a day?","The bakery makes $650.","The bakery makes $700.","The bakery makes $950.","The bakery makes $800.","The bakery makes $950.","The bakery makes 250 x $2 = $500 from bread and 150 x $3 = $450 from pastries. The total is $500 + $450 = $950.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-car-travels-1e1170d4","Patterns: Numerical & Geometric Growth - Math (Grade 6)","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-2-1-1 Distinguishing growing numerical and geometric patterns, generating and generalizing them, exploring relationships from data tables, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","5. Solving mathematical problems including real-life applications of patterns and inputâ€“output tables, and explaining their solutions.","A car travels 250 miles in 5 hours. How many miles does the car travel per hour?","50 miles per hour","40 miles per hour","60 miles per hour","250 miles per hour","50 miles per hour","250 ÷ 5 = 50 miles per hour.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-group-of-ea2c7b68","Patterns: Numerical & Geometric Growth - Math (Grade 6)","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-2-1-1 Distinguishing growing numerical and geometric patterns, generating and generalizing them, exploring relationships from data tables, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","5. Solving mathematical problems including real-life applications of patterns and inputâ€“output tables, and explaining their solutions.","A group of friends want to share some candy equally. If they have 48 pieces of candy and there are 8 friends, how many pieces of candy will each friend get?","Each friend will get 6 pieces of candy","5 pieces","7 pieces","48 pieces","Each friend will get 6 pieces of candy","48 ÷ 8 = 6 pieces of candy.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-water-tank-088d3285","Patterns: Numerical & Geometric Growth - Math (Grade 6)","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-2-1-1 Distinguishing growing numerical and geometric patterns, generating and generalizing them, exploring relationships from data tables, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","5. Solving mathematical problems including real-life applications of patterns and inputâ€“output tables, and explaining their solutions.","A water tank can hold 1200 liters of water. If 300 liters of water are already in the tank, what percentage of the tank is filled?","25% of the tank is filled.","30%","20%","300%","25% of the tank is filled.","300 ÷ 1200 × 100 = 25%.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-bookshelf-has-5fd36780","Patterns: Numerical & Geometric Growth - Math (Grade 6)","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-2-1-1 Distinguishing growing numerical and geometric patterns, generating and generalizing them, exploring relationships from data tables, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","5. Solving mathematical problems including real-life applications of patterns and inputâ€“output tables, and explaining their solutions.","A bookshelf has 5 shelves, and each shelf can hold 8 books. If the bookshelf is currently empty, how many books can be placed on it in total?","40 books","30 books","45 books","50 books","40 books","Multiply shelves by books per shelf: 5 x 8 = 40 books.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-bakery-sells-7c5e83fc","Patterns: Numerical & Geometric Growth - Math (Grade 6)","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-2-1-1 Distinguishing growing numerical and geometric patterns, generating and generalizing them, exploring relationships from data tables, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","5. Solving mathematical problems including real-life applications of patterns and inputâ€“output tables, and explaining their solutions.","A bakery sells 250 loaves of bread per day. If they sell bread for $2 per loaf, how much money does the bakery make in a day?","The bakery makes $400.","The bakery makes $450.","The bakery makes $500.","The bakery makes $550.","The bakery makes $500.","The stated calculation gives $500.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-tom-has-5-d80727f0","Patterns: Numerical & Geometric Growth - Math (Grade 6)","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-2-1-1 Distinguishing growing numerical and geometric patterns, generating and generalizing them, exploring relationships from data tables, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","5. Solving mathematical problems including real-life applications of patterns and inputâ€“output tables, and explaining their solutions.","Tom has 5 boxes of pencils. Each box contains 8 pencils. How many pencils does Tom have in total?","Tom has 20 pencils.","Tom has 30 pencils.","Tom has 40 pencils.","Tom has 50 pencils.","Tom has 40 pencils.","To find the total number of pencils, we multiply the number of boxes by the number of pencils in each box: 5 boxes * 8 pencils/box = 40 pencils.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-water-tank-955b054d","Patterns: Numerical & Geometric Growth - Math (Grade 6)","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-2-1-1 Distinguishing growing numerical and geometric patterns, generating and generalizing them, exploring relationships from data tables, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","5. Solving mathematical problems including real-life applications of patterns and inputâ€“output tables, and explaining their solutions.","A water tank can hold 1200 liters of water. If 400 liters of water are already in the tank, how many more liters of water can be added?","800 liters.","400 liters","700 liters","900 liters","800 liters.","1200 - 400 = 800 liters.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-group-of-918dc466","Patterns: Numerical & Geometric Growth - Math (Grade 6)","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-2-1-1 Distinguishing growing numerical and geometric patterns, generating and generalizing them, exploring relationships from data tables, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","5. Solving mathematical problems including real-life applications of patterns and inputâ€“output tables, and explaining their solutions.","A group of friends want to share some money equally. If they have $120 and there are 6 friends, how much money will each friend get?","$15.","$20.","$25.","$30.","$20.","To find the amount of money each friend will get, we divide the total amount of money by the number of friends: $120 / 6 friends = $20/friend.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-group-of-9d68363f","Patterns: Numerical & Geometric Growth - Math (Grade 6) - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-2-1-1 Distinguishing growing numerical and geometric patterns, generating and generalizing them, exploring relationships from data tables, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","5. Solving mathematical problems including real-life applications of patterns and inputâ€“output tables, and explaining their solutions.","A group of students have 18 pencils to share. If they want to put an equal number of pencils in 3 boxes, how many pencils will each box get?","4 pencils","5 pencils","6 pencils","8 pencils","6 pencils","There are 18 pencils and 3 boxes, so 18 ÷ 3 = 6 pencils per box.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-group-of-ac250e69","Patterns: Numerical & Geometric Growth - Math (Grade 6) - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-2-1-1 Distinguishing growing numerical and geometric patterns, generating and generalizing them, exploring relationships from data tables, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","5. Solving mathematical problems including real-life applications of patterns and inputâ€“output tables, and explaining their solutions.","A group of friends want to share some candy equally. If they have 48 pieces of candy and there are 8 friends, how many pieces of candy will each friend get?","Each friend will get 6 pieces of candy","5 pieces","7 pieces","48 pieces","Each friend will get 6 pieces of candy","48 ÷ 8 = 6 pieces of candy.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-bakery-sells-01d78a86","Patterns: Numerical & Geometric Growth - Math (Grade 6) - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-2-1-1 Distinguishing growing numerical and geometric patterns, generating and generalizing them, exploring relationships from data tables, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","5. Solving mathematical problems including real-life applications of patterns and inputâ€“output tables, and explaining their solutions.","A bakery sells 250 loaves of bread per day. If they sell bread for 2 days, how many loaves of bread will they sell in total?","500 loaves","400 loaves","600 loaves","250 loaves","500 loaves","250 x 2 = 500 loaves.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-bookshelf-has-1264d4cd","Patterns: Numerical & Geometric Growth - Math (Grade 6) - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-2-1-1 Distinguishing growing numerical and geometric patterns, generating and generalizing them, exploring relationships from data tables, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","5. Solving mathematical problems including real-life applications of patterns and inputâ€“output tables, and explaining their solutions.","A bookshelf has 5 shelves, and each shelf can hold 3 books. If the bookshelf is currently empty, how many books can be placed on it in total?","15 books","20 books","25 books","30 books","15 books","Each shelf can hold 3 books, and there are 5 shelves, so 3 x 5 = 15 books.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-bike-travels-3de5183e","Patterns: Numerical & Geometric Growth - Math (Grade 6) - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-2-1-1 Distinguishing growing numerical and geometric patterns, generating and generalizing them, exploring relationships from data tables, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","5. Solving mathematical problems including real-life applications of patterns and inputâ€“output tables, and explaining their solutions.","A bike travels 15 km in 1 hour. How many kilometers does it travel in 3 hours?","30 km","40 km","45 km","50 km","45 km","The bike travels 15 km in 1 hour, so in 3 hours it will travel 15 x 3 = 45 km.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-water-tank-85258067","Patterns: Numerical & Geometric Growth - Math (Grade 6) - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-2-1-1 Distinguishing growing numerical and geometric patterns, generating and generalizing them, exploring relationships from data tables, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","5. Solving mathematical problems including real-life applications of patterns and inputâ€“output tables, and explaining their solutions.","A water tank can hold 1200 liters of water. If 300 liters of water are already in the tank, how much more water can be added?","900 liters","1000 liters","1100 liters","1200 liters","900 liters","The tank can hold 1200 liters, and 300 liters are already in the tank, so 1200 - 300 = 900 liters.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-car-travels-7ea17b54","Patterns: Numerical & Geometric Growth - Math (Grade 6) - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-2-1-1 Distinguishing growing numerical and geometric patterns, generating and generalizing them, exploring relationships from data tables, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","5. Solving mathematical problems including real-life applications of patterns and inputâ€“output tables, and explaining their solutions.","A car travels 250 miles in 5 hours. How many miles does it travel per hour?","50 miles per hour","40 miles per hour","60 miles per hour","250 miles per hour","50 miles per hour","250 ÷ 5 = 50 miles per hour.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-tom-has-5-f94a18a1","Patterns: Numerical & Geometric Growth - Math (Grade 6) - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-2-1-1 Distinguishing growing numerical and geometric patterns, generating and generalizing them, exploring relationships from data tables, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","5. Solving mathematical problems including real-life applications of patterns and inputâ€“output tables, and explaining their solutions.","Tom has 5 pencils in his pencil case. He adds 2 more pencils every day. How many pencils will Tom have after 3 days?","Tom will have 11 pencils","7 pencils","6 pencils","13 pencils","Tom will have 11 pencils","5 + (2 x 3) = 11 pencils.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-box-of-37ebf59c","Patterns: Numerical & Geometric Growth - Math (Grade 6) - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-2-1-1 Distinguishing growing numerical and geometric patterns, generating and generalizing them, exploring relationships from data tables, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","5. Solving mathematical problems including real-life applications of patterns and inputâ€“output tables, and explaining their solutions.","A box of crayons weighs 0.5 kg. If you have 4 boxes of crayons, how many kilograms do they weigh in total?","2 kg","2.5 kg","1.5 kg","0.5 kg","2 kg","0.5 x 4 = 2 kg.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-book-costs-c7362e3f","Patterns: Numerical & Geometric Growth - Math (Grade 6) - Set 2","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-2-1-1 Distinguishing growing numerical and geometric patterns, generating and generalizing them, exploring relationships from data tables, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","5. Solving mathematical problems including real-life applications of patterns and inputâ€“output tables, and explaining their solutions.","A book costs $15. If you have a 20% discount coupon, how much will you pay for the book?","$12","$12.50","$13","$15","$12","The stated calculation gives $12.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-tom-has-5-0c25f1ed","Patterns: Numerical & Geometric Growth - Math (Grade 6) - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-2-1-1 Distinguishing growing numerical and geometric patterns, generating and generalizing them, exploring relationships from data tables, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","5. Solving mathematical problems including real-life applications of patterns and inputâ€“output tables, and explaining their solutions.","Tom has 5 boxes of pens. Each box contains 8 pens. How many pens does Tom have in total?","Tom has 20 pens.","Tom has 30 pens.","Tom has 40 pens.","Tom has 50 pens.","Tom has 40 pens.","To find the total number of pens, multiply the number of boxes by the number of pens in each box: 5 x 8 = 40.","","",""
"math-g6-6-4-2-1-1-a-bakery-sells-865de9b7","Patterns: Numerical & Geometric Growth - Math (Grade 6) - Set 3","Math","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-4-2-1-1 Distinguishing growing numerical and geometric patterns, generating and generalizing them, exploring relationships from data tables, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","5. Solving mathematical problems including real-life applications of patterns and inputâ€“output tables, and explaining their solutions.","A bakery sells 240 cupcakes in a day. They are packed in boxes of 12 cupcakes each. How many boxes were sold?","20 boxes were sold.","15 boxes","25 boxes","12 boxes","20 boxes were sold.","240 ÷ 12 = 20 boxes.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-box-of-4863e429","Math G9: Analyzing data by using measures of central tendency and - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-4-2-1 Analyzing data by using measures of central tendency and measures of dispersion, interpreting, and comparing between them.","3. Finding measures of dispersion (range and interquartile range) and outliers, and using them to describe data.","A box of chocolates contains 8 milk chocolates, 6 dark chocolates, and 4 nuts chocolates. If a chocolate is randomly selected, what is the probability that it is a milk chocolate?","The probability is 1/18.","The probability is 2/18.","The probability is 8/18.","The probability is 12/18.","The probability is 8/18.","The probability of selecting a milk chocolate is the number of milk chocolates divided by the total number of chocolates, which is 8/18 or 4/9.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-student-has-7af2f450","Math G9: Analyzing data by using measures of central tendency and - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-4-2-1 Analyzing data by using measures of central tendency and measures of dispersion, interpreting, and comparing between them.","3. Finding measures of dispersion (range and interquartile range) and outliers, and using them to describe data.","A student has 4 different colored pens and wants to select 2 of them to use for a project. If the order in which the pens are selected does not matter, how many different pairs of pens can be selected?","6 pairs","8 pairs","10 pairs","12 pairs","6 pairs","Choosing 2 objects from 4 gives C(4,2) = 4!/(2!2!) = 6.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-student-has-7af2f450.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-bag-contains-b17e8eb5","Math G9: Analyzing data by using measures of central tendency and - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-4-2-1 Analyzing data by using measures of central tendency and measures of dispersion, interpreting, and comparing between them.","3. Finding measures of dispersion (range and interquartile range) and outliers, and using them to describe data.","A bag contains 5 red marbles, 4 blue marbles, and 3 green marbles. If a marble is randomly selected, what is the probability that it is blue?","The probability is 1/12.","The probability is 1/3.","The probability is 4/12.","The probability is 5/12.","The probability is 1/3.","The probability of selecting a blue marble is the number of blue marbles divided by the total number of marbles, which is 4/12 or 1/3.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-pizza-parlor-0781693a","Math G9: Analyzing data by using measures of central tendency and - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-4-2-1 Analyzing data by using measures of central tendency and measures of dispersion, interpreting, and comparing between them.","2. Finding the number of possible outcomes of an experiment using the Fundamental Counting Principle, permutations, and combinations, and calculating their probabilities, expressing them in words, Proper fractions, decimal fractions, or percentages.","A pizza parlor offers 3 different toppings for a small pizza. If a customer selects 2 toppings, how many different combinations are possible?","6 combinations","9 combinations","12 combinations","15 combinations","9 combinations","To find the number of combinations, we use the formula C(n, k) = n! / (k!(n-k)!), where n is the total number of toppings and k is the number of toppings selected. In this case, C(3, 2) = 3! / (2!(3-2)!) = 6/2 = 3, but since we are selecting 2 toppings from 3, we can simply list them out: (topping 1 and topping 2), (topping 1 and topping 3), (topping 2 and topping 3). So there are 3 combinations.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-student-has-afc51475","Math G9: Analyzing data by using measures of central tendency and - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-4-2-1 Analyzing data by using measures of central tendency and measures of dispersion, interpreting, and comparing between them.","3. Finding measures of dispersion (range and interquartile range) and outliers, and using them to describe data.","A student has 3 different colored markers and wants to select 2 of them to use for a project. If the order in which the markers are selected does not matter, how many different pairs of markers can be selected?","3 pairs","6 pairs","9 pairs","12 pairs","3 pairs","Choosing 2 objects from 3 gives C(3,2) = 3.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-student-has-afc51475.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-bag-contains-fed3f57a","Math G9: Analyzing data by using measures of central tendency and - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-4-2-1 Analyzing data by using measures of central tendency and measures of dispersion, interpreting, and comparing between them.","3. Finding measures of dispersion (range and interquartile range) and outliers, and using them to describe data.","A bag contains 2 red marbles, 3 blue marbles, and 1 green marble. If a marble is randomly selected, what is the probability that it is not red?","The probability is 1/6.","The probability is 2/6.","The probability is 3/6.","The probability is 4/6.","The probability is 4/6.","There are 4 non-red outcomes out of 6 total, so the probability is 4/6 = 2/3.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-student-has-841b7859","Math G9: Analyzing data by using measures of central tendency and - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-4-2-1 Analyzing data by using measures of central tendency and measures of dispersion, interpreting, and comparing between them.","3. Finding measures of dispersion (range and interquartile range) and outliers, and using them to describe data.","A student has 6 different colored pencils and wants to select 3 of them to use for a project. If the order in which the pencils are selected does not matter, how many different groups of pencils can be selected?","20 groups","30 groups","40 groups","50 groups","20 groups","Choosing 3 objects from 6 gives C(6,3) = 6!/(3!3!) = 20.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-student-has-841b7859.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-pizza-parlor-24a722bc","Math G9: Analyzing data by using measures of central tendency and - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-4-2-1 Analyzing data by using measures of central tendency and measures of dispersion, interpreting, and comparing between them.","3. Finding measures of dispersion (range and interquartile range) and outliers, and using them to describe data.","A pizza parlor offers 5 different toppings for a large pizza. If a customer selects 3 toppings, how many different combinations are possible?","10 combinations","15 combinations","20 combinations","25 combinations","10 combinations","Choosing 3 objects from 5 gives C(5,3) = 5!/(3!2!) = 10.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-student-has-07c7351a","Math G9: Analyzing data by using measures of central tendency and - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-4-2-1 Analyzing data by using measures of central tendency and measures of dispersion, interpreting, and comparing between them.","3. Finding measures of dispersion (range and interquartile range) and outliers, and using them to describe data.","A student has 5 friends and wants to invite 3 of them to a party. If the order in which the friends are invited does not matter, how many different groups of friends can be invited?","10 groups","15 groups","20 groups","25 groups","20 groups","To find the number of combinations, we use the formula C(n, k) = n! / (k!(n-k)!), where n is the total number of friends and k is the number of friends invited. In this case, C(5, 3) = 5! / (3!(5-3)!) = 120/6 = 20.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-student-has-07c7351a.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-bag-contains-2135a3f5","Math G9: Analyzing data by using measures of central tendency and - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-4-2-1 Analyzing data by using measures of central tendency and measures of dispersion, interpreting, and comparing between them.","3. Finding measures of dispersion (range and interquartile range) and outliers, and using them to describe data.","A bag contains 3 red marbles, 2 blue marbles, and 1 green marble. If a marble is randomly selected, what is the probability that it is not blue?","The probability is 1/6.","The probability is 2/6.","The probability is 3/6.","The probability is 4/6.","The probability is 4/6.","There are 4 non-blue marbles out of 6 total, so the probability is 4/6 = 2/3.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-purpose-of-8579211f","Math G9: Analyzing data by using measures of central tendency and - Set 4","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-4-2-1 Analyzing data by using measures of central tendency and measures of dispersion, interpreting, and comparing between them.","4. Distinguishing measures of dispersion (mean deviation, standard deviation, and variance) and finding them for a set of individual values.","What is the purpose of calculating the mean deviation of a dataset?","To find the average value of the dataset","To determine the spread or dispersion of the data from the mean","To identify the highest and lowest values in the dataset","To calculate the total sum of the data points","To determine the spread or dispersion of the data from the mean","The mean deviation is a measure of dispersion that calculates the average distance between each data point and the mean value of the dataset.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-primary-purpose-31e232a9","Math G9: Analyzing data by using measures of central tendency and - Set 4","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-4-2-1 Analyzing data by using measures of central tendency and measures of dispersion, interpreting, and comparing between them.","4. Distinguishing measures of dispersion (mean deviation, standard deviation, and variance) and finding them for a set of individual values.","What is the primary purpose of calculating the standard deviation of a dataset?","To find the average value of the dataset","To determine the spread or dispersion of the data from the mean","To identify the highest and lowest values in the dataset","To calculate the total sum of the data points","To determine the spread or dispersion of the data from the mean","The standard deviation is a measure of dispersion that calculates how much individual data points deviate from the mean value of the dataset.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-of-exam-42edf8ff","Math G9: Analyzing data by using measures of central tendency and - Set 4","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-4-2-1 Analyzing data by using measures of central tendency and measures of dispersion, interpreting, and comparing between them.","4. Distinguishing measures of dispersion (mean deviation, standard deviation, and variance) and finding them for a set of individual values.","A set of exam scores has a mean of 75 and a standard deviation of 10. What does the standard deviation tell us about the scores?","The scores are all very close to the mean of 75","The scores are spread out over a wide range of values","The scores are all below the mean of 75","The scores are all exactly 75","The scores are spread out over a wide range of values","A standard deviation of 10 indicates that most scores are within 10 points of the mean, but it also suggests that there is some variation in the scores.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-difference-variance-a2d4af63","Math G9: Analyzing data by using measures of central tendency and - Set 4","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-4-2-1 Analyzing data by using measures of central tendency and measures of dispersion, interpreting, and comparing between them.","4. Distinguishing measures of dispersion (mean deviation, standard deviation, and variance) and finding them for a set of individual values.","What is the difference between the variance and the standard deviation of a dataset?","The variance is the average of the data, while the standard deviation is the spread of the data","The variance is the square of the standard deviation, while the standard deviation is the square root of the variance","The variance is the spread of the data, while the standard deviation is the average of the data","The variance and standard deviation are the same thing","The variance is the square of the standard deviation, while the standard deviation is the square root of the variance","The variance is the average of the squared differences from the mean, while the standard deviation is the square root of the variance.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-dataset-has-43a8d821","Math G9: Analyzing data by using measures of central tendency and - Set 4","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-4-2-1 Analyzing data by using measures of central tendency and measures of dispersion, interpreting, and comparing between them.","4. Distinguishing measures of dispersion (mean deviation, standard deviation, and variance) and finding them for a set of individual values.","A dataset has a mean of 20 and the following values: 15, 18, 22, 25. What is the mean deviation of this dataset?","The mean deviation is the same as the standard deviation","The mean deviation is the average of the absolute differences from the mean","The mean deviation is the sum of the differences from the mean","The mean deviation is the difference between the highest and lowest values","The mean deviation is the average of the absolute differences from the mean","The mean deviation is calculated by finding the absolute differences between each data point and the mean, then averaging these differences.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-of-test-74bb4244","Math G9: Analyzing data by using measures of central tendency and - Set 4","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-4-2-1 Analyzing data by using measures of central tendency and measures of dispersion, interpreting, and comparing between them.","4. Distinguishing measures of dispersion (mean deviation, standard deviation, and variance) and finding them for a set of individual values.","A set of test scores has a mean of 80 and the following values: 70, 75, 85, 90. What is the variance of this dataset?","The variance is the average of the squared differences from the mean","The variance is the sum of the differences from the mean","The variance is the difference between the highest and lowest values","The variance is the same as the standard deviation","The variance is the average of the squared differences from the mean","The variance is calculated by finding the squared differences between each data point and the mean, then averaging these squared differences.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-of-is-a-c9230ee2","Math G9: Analyzing data by using measures of central tendency and - Set 4","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-4-2-1 Analyzing data by using measures of central tendency and measures of dispersion, interpreting, and comparing between them.","4. Distinguishing measures of dispersion (mean deviation, standard deviation, and variance) and finding them for a set of individual values.","Which of the following is a characteristic of the standard deviation?","It is sensitive to extreme values in the dataset","It is not affected by extreme values in the dataset","It is only used for small datasets","It is only used for large datasets","It is sensitive to extreme values in the dataset","The standard deviation is affected by extreme values, also known as outliers, in the dataset, as these values can greatly increase the standard deviation.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-small-standard-8adfcfc6","Math G9: Analyzing data by using measures of central tendency and - Set 4","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-4-2-1 Analyzing data by using measures of central tendency and measures of dispersion, interpreting, and comparing between them.","4. Distinguishing measures of dispersion (mean deviation, standard deviation, and variance) and finding them for a set of individual values.","What does a small standard deviation indicate about a dataset?","The data points are spread out over a wide range of values","The data points are all very close to the mean","The data points are all below the mean","The data points are all above the mean","The data points are all very close to the mean","A small standard deviation indicates that the data points are clustered closely around the mean, with little variation.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-researcher-is-591605f4","Math G9: Analyzing data by using measures of central tendency and - Set 4","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-4-2-1 Analyzing data by using measures of central tendency and measures of dispersion, interpreting, and comparing between them.","4. Distinguishing measures of dispersion (mean deviation, standard deviation, and variance) and finding them for a set of individual values.","A researcher is analyzing the scores of a test and wants to determine the spread of the scores. Which measure of dispersion should the researcher use?","The mean","The median","The range","The standard deviation","The standard deviation","The standard deviation is a measure of dispersion that calculates the spread of the data from the mean, making it a suitable choice for analyzing the spread of test scores.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-relationship-mean-3809c435","Math G9: Analyzing data by using measures of central tendency and - Set 4","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-4-2-1 Analyzing data by using measures of central tendency and measures of dispersion, interpreting, and comparing between them.","4. Distinguishing measures of dispersion (mean deviation, standard deviation, and variance) and finding them for a set of individual values.","What is the relationship between the mean and the standard deviation of a dataset?","The mean and standard deviation are always equal","The mean and standard deviation are always opposite","The standard deviation is a measure of the spread of the data from the mean","The mean is a measure of the spread of the data from the standard deviation","The standard deviation is a measure of the spread of the data from the mean","The standard deviation measures the spread or dispersion of the data from the mean, while the mean is a measure of the central tendency of the dataset.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-equation-of-2ce2b3a3","Math G9: Determining the locations of points in the coordinate plane - Set 3","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-2-1 Determining the locations of points in the coordinate plane and plotting them, finding the distance between two points and the coordinates of the midpoint, and determining the slope of a line, writing its equation, and graphing it.","3. Distinguishing the equation of a line and writing its using the slope-intercept form, the point-slope form, and the standard form.","What is the equation of a line with a slope of 2 and a y-intercept of 3?","y = 2x + 3","y = 2x - 3","y = -2x + 3","y = -2x - 3","y = 2x + 3","The equation of a line with a slope of 2 and a y-intercept of 3 is y = 2x + 3.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-point-slope-f7c34140","Math G9: Determining the locations of points in the coordinate plane - Set 3","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-2-1 Determining the locations of points in the coordinate plane and plotting them, finding the distance between two points and the coordinates of the midpoint, and determining the slope of a line, writing its equation, and graphing it.","3. Distinguishing the equation of a line and writing its using the slope-intercept form, the point-slope form, and the standard form.","What is the point-slope form of a linear equation?","y - y1 = m(x - x1)","y - y1 = m(x + x1)","y - y1 = m(x - x2)","y - y1 = m(x + x2)","y - y1 = m(x - x1)","The point-slope form is y - y1 = m(x - x1), where m is the slope and (x1, y1) is a point on the line.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-point-slope-f7c34140.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-standard-form-dd1a34c4","Math G9: Determining the locations of points in the coordinate plane - Set 3","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-2-1 Determining the locations of points in the coordinate plane and plotting them, finding the distance between two points and the coordinates of the midpoint, and determining the slope of a line, writing its equation, and graphing it.","3. Distinguishing the equation of a line and writing its using the slope-intercept form, the point-slope form, and the standard form.","What is the standard form of a linear equation?","Ax + By = C","Ax - By = C","Ax + By = -C","Ax - By = -C","Ax + By = C","The standard form is Ax + By = C, where A, B, and C are constants.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-equation-of-d2013ac0","Math G9: Determining the locations of points in the coordinate plane - Set 3","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-2-1 Determining the locations of points in the coordinate plane and plotting them, finding the distance between two points and the coordinates of the midpoint, and determining the slope of a line, writing its equation, and graphing it.","3. Distinguishing the equation of a line and writing its using the slope-intercept form, the point-slope form, and the standard form.","What is the equation of a line with a slope of -2 and a y-intercept of -3?","y = -2x - 3","y = -2x + 3","y = 2x - 3","y = 2x + 3","y = -2x - 3","The equation of a line with a slope of -2 and a y-intercept of -3 is y = -2x - 3.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-slope-intercept-c136705a","Math G9: Determining the locations of points in the coordinate plane - Set 3","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-2-1 Determining the locations of points in the coordinate plane and plotting them, finding the distance between two points and the coordinates of the midpoint, and determining the slope of a line, writing its equation, and graphing it.","3. Distinguishing the equation of a line and writing its using the slope-intercept form, the point-slope form, and the standard form.","What is the slope-intercept form of a linear equation?","y = mx + b","y = mx - b","y = mx + c","y = mx - c","y = mx + b","The slope-intercept form is y = mx + b, where m is the slope and b is the y-intercept.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-form-of-a-07cfcf33","Math G9: Determining the locations of points in the coordinate plane - Set 3","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-2-1 Determining the locations of points in the coordinate plane and plotting them, finding the distance between two points and the coordinates of the midpoint, and determining the slope of a line, writing its equation, and graphing it.","3. Distinguishing the equation of a line and writing its using the slope-intercept form, the point-slope form, and the standard form.","Which form of a linear equation is easiest to graph?","Slope-intercept form","Point-slope form","Standard form","Any form is equally easy to graph","Slope-intercept form","The slope-intercept form is easiest to graph because it gives the y-intercept directly.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-form-of-a-07cfcf33.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-slope-of-62b6d2fa","Math G9: Determining the locations of points in the coordinate plane - Set 3","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-2-1 Determining the locations of points in the coordinate plane and plotting them, finding the distance between two points and the coordinates of the midpoint, and determining the slope of a line, writing its equation, and graphing it.","5. Finding the distance between two points in the coordinate plane, and finding the coordinates of the midpoint.","What is the slope of the line passing through the points (1, 2) and (3, 4)?","1","2","3","4","1","The slope is (4 - 2)/(3 - 1) = 2/2 = 1.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-equation-of-713619f0","Math G9: Determining the locations of points in the coordinate plane - Set 3","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-2-1 Determining the locations of points in the coordinate plane and plotting them, finding the distance between two points and the coordinates of the midpoint, and determining the slope of a line, writing its equation, and graphing it.","5. Finding the distance between two points in the coordinate plane, and finding the coordinates of the midpoint.","What is the equation of a line passing through the point (1, 2) with a slope of 3?","y - 2 = 3(x - 1)","y - 2 = 3(x + 1)","y - 2 = -3(x - 1)","y - 2 = -3(x + 1)","y - 2 = 3(x - 1)","The equation of a line passing through the point (1, 2) with a slope of 3 is y - 2 = 3(x - 1).","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-standard-form-a691da15","Math G9: Determining the locations of points in the coordinate plane - Set 3","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-2-1 Determining the locations of points in the coordinate plane and plotting them, finding the distance between two points and the coordinates of the midpoint, and determining the slope of a line, writing its equation, and graphing it.","3. Distinguishing the equation of a line and writing its using the slope-intercept form, the point-slope form, and the standard form.","What is the standard form of the equation of a line with a slope of 4 and a y-intercept of -1?","4x - y = 1","4x + y = -1","x - 4y = 1","x + 4y = -1","4x - y = 1","Starting with y = 4x - 1, move y to the left and 1 to the right: 4x - y = 1.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-standard-form-a691da15.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-equation-of-a61a3ae2","Math G9: Determining the locations of points in the coordinate plane - Set 3","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-2-1 Determining the locations of points in the coordinate plane and plotting them, finding the distance between two points and the coordinates of the midpoint, and determining the slope of a line, writing its equation, and graphing it.","5. Finding the distance between two points in the coordinate plane, and finding the coordinates of the midpoint.","What is the equation of a line passing through the points (2, 3) and (4, 5)?","y = x + 1","y = 2x - 1","y = x - 1","y = -x + 5","y = x + 1","The slope through (2,3) and (4,5) is (5-3)/(4-2)=1. Using y=mx+b gives b=1, so y=x+1.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-slope-of-4b8254a9","Math G9: Determining the locations of points in the coordinate plane - Set 4","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-2-1 Determining the locations of points in the coordinate plane and plotting them, finding the distance between two points and the coordinates of the midpoint, and determining the slope of a line, writing its equation, and graphing it.","4. Distinguishing the relationship between the slopes of two parallel or perpendicular lines, and using its to write the equation of a line parallel or perpendicular to a given line.","What is the slope of a line that is perpendicular to the line y = 2x + 1?","-0.5","1/2","-2","2","-0.5","The slope of a line perpendicular to another line is the negative reciprocal of the slope of the original line.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-equation-of-8999e4eb","Math G9: Determining the locations of points in the coordinate plane - Set 4","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-2-1 Determining the locations of points in the coordinate plane and plotting them, finding the distance between two points and the coordinates of the midpoint, and determining the slope of a line, writing its equation, and graphing it.","5. Finding the distance between two points in the coordinate plane, and finding the coordinates of the midpoint.","What is the equation of a line that is perpendicular to the line y = x - 2 and passes through the point (3, 4)?","y = -x + 7","y = -x - 1","y = x + 1","y = x - 1","y = -x + 7","Since the line is perpendicular to y = x - 2, it has a slope of -1. Using the point-slope form, we can find the equation of the line.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-if-two-lines-8132be13","Math G9: Determining the locations of points in the coordinate plane - Set 4","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-2-1 Determining the locations of points in the coordinate plane and plotting them, finding the distance between two points and the coordinates of the midpoint, and determining the slope of a line, writing its equation, and graphing it.","4. Distinguishing the relationship between the slopes of two parallel or perpendicular lines, and using its to write the equation of a line parallel or perpendicular to a given line.","If two lines have slopes of 3 and -1/3, what is the relationship between the lines?","The lines are parallel","The lines are perpendicular","The lines are vertical","The lines are horizontal","The lines are perpendicular","The product of the slopes of two perpendicular lines is -1. In this case, 3 * (-1/3) = -1, so the lines are perpendicular.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-equation-of-bedcfd0e","Math G9: Determining the locations of points in the coordinate plane - Set 4","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-2-1 Determining the locations of points in the coordinate plane and plotting them, finding the distance between two points and the coordinates of the midpoint, and determining the slope of a line, writing its equation, and graphing it.","4. Distinguishing the relationship between the slopes of two parallel or perpendicular lines, and using its to write the equation of a line parallel or perpendicular to a given line.","What is the equation of a line that is parallel to the line y = 2x + 1 and passes through the point (2, 5)?","y = 2x + 1","y = 2x + 3","y = 2x - 1","y = x + 3","y = 2x + 1","A line parallel to y = 2x + 1 has slope 2. Using point (2, 5): y - 5 = 2(x - 2), so y = 2x + 1.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-if-a-line-a0ef7406","Math G9: Determining the locations of points in the coordinate plane - Set 4","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-2-1 Determining the locations of points in the coordinate plane and plotting them, finding the distance between two points and the coordinates of the midpoint, and determining the slope of a line, writing its equation, and graphing it.","3. Distinguishing the equation of a line and writing its using the slope-intercept form, the point-slope form, and the standard form.","If a line has a slope of -3 and passes through the point (1, 2), what is the equation of the line?","y = -3x + 5","y = -3x - 1","y = 3x + 2","y = 3x - 1","y = -3x + 5","Using the point-slope form, we can find the equation of the line. The point-slope form is y - y1 = m(x - x1), where (x1, y1) is a point on the line and m is the slope.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-relationship-slopes-49eca601","Math G9: Determining the locations of points in the coordinate plane - Set 4","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-2-1 Determining the locations of points in the coordinate plane and plotting them, finding the distance between two points and the coordinates of the midpoint, and determining the slope of a line, writing its equation, and graphing it.","4. Distinguishing the relationship between the slopes of two parallel or perpendicular lines, and using its to write the equation of a line parallel or perpendicular to a given line.","What is the relationship between the slopes of two parallel lines?","The slopes are equal","The slopes are negative reciprocals of each other","The slopes are always positive","The slopes are always negative","The slopes are equal","Parallel lines have the same slope, which means they have the same steepness.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-if-a-line-e5f3acad","Math G9: Determining the locations of points in the coordinate plane - Set 4","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-2-1 Determining the locations of points in the coordinate plane and plotting them, finding the distance between two points and the coordinates of the midpoint, and determining the slope of a line, writing its equation, and graphing it.","4. Distinguishing the relationship between the slopes of two parallel or perpendicular lines, and using its to write the equation of a line parallel or perpendicular to a given line.","If a line has a slope of 2, what is the slope of a line perpendicular to it?","1/2","-0.5","2","-2","-0.5","The slope of a line perpendicular to another line is the negative reciprocal of the slope of the original line.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-equation-of-0cbaff54","Math G9: Determining the locations of points in the coordinate plane - Set 4","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-2-1 Determining the locations of points in the coordinate plane and plotting them, finding the distance between two points and the coordinates of the midpoint, and determining the slope of a line, writing its equation, and graphing it.","5. Finding the distance between two points in the coordinate plane, and finding the coordinates of the midpoint.","What is the equation of a line that is parallel to the line y = 3x - 2 and passes through the point (1, 4)?","y = 3x + 1","y = 3x - 1","y = 2x + 2","y = 2x - 2","y = 3x + 1","Since the line is parallel to y = 3x - 2, it has the same slope, which is 3. Using the point-slope form, we can find the equation of the line.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-if-two-lines-88a28d66","Math G9: Determining the locations of points in the coordinate plane - Set 4","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-2-1 Determining the locations of points in the coordinate plane and plotting them, finding the distance between two points and the coordinates of the midpoint, and determining the slope of a line, writing its equation, and graphing it.","4. Distinguishing the relationship between the slopes of two parallel or perpendicular lines, and using its to write the equation of a line parallel or perpendicular to a given line.","If two lines have slopes of 1/2 and -2, what is the relationship between the lines?","The lines are parallel","The lines are perpendicular","The lines are vertical","The lines are horizontal","The lines are perpendicular","The product of the slopes of two perpendicular lines is -1. In this case, (1/2) * (-2) = -1, so the lines are perpendicular.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-if-a-line-b11925fb","Math G9: Determining the locations of points in the coordinate plane - Set 4","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-2-1 Determining the locations of points in the coordinate plane and plotting them, finding the distance between two points and the coordinates of the midpoint, and determining the slope of a line, writing its equation, and graphing it.","5. Finding the distance between two points in the coordinate plane, and finding the coordinates of the midpoint.","If a line passes through the points (2, 3) and (4, 5), what is the slope of the line?","1/2","1","2","3","1","The slope of a line can be found using the formula (y2 - y1) / (x2 - x1), where (x1, y1) and (x2, y2) are two points on the line.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-point-2-e59346c2","Math G9: Determining the type of geometric transformation in the - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-2-1 Determining the locations of points in the coordinate plane and plotting them, finding the distance between two points and the coordinates of the midpoint, and determining the slope of a line, writing its equation, and graphing it.","5. Finding the distance between two points in the coordinate plane, and finding the coordinates of the midpoint.","A point (2, 3) is reflected over the x-axis. What are the coordinates of the reflected point?","(2, -3)","(2, 3)","(-2, 3)","(2, 0)","(2, -3)","When a point is reflected over the x-axis, the y-coordinate is negated.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-point-2-e59346c2.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-line-segment-5c24d2aa","Math G9: Determining the type of geometric transformation in the - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-2-2 Determining the type of geometric transformation in the coordinate plane, and drawing the image resulting from these transformations.","2. Distinguishing dilation and determining its type, center, and scale factor.","A line segment has a length of 15 yards. If it is dilated by a scale factor of 3, what is the new length of the line segment?","5 yards","15 yards","20 yards","45 yards","45 yards","A scale factor of 3 multiplies 15 by 3, giving 45.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-line-segment-5c24d2aa.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-point-x-0af72e98","Math G9: Determining the type of geometric transformation in the - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-2-1 Determining the locations of points in the coordinate plane and plotting them, finding the distance between two points and the coordinates of the midpoint, and determining the slope of a line, writing its equation, and graphing it.","5. Finding the distance between two points in the coordinate plane, and finding the coordinates of the midpoint.","A point (x, y) is rotated 90 degrees clockwise about the origin. What are the new coordinates of the point?","(y, -x)","(y, x)","(-y, x)","(x, y)","(y, -x)","When a point is rotated 90 degrees clockwise about the origin, its coordinates are swapped and the new y-coordinate is negated.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-point-x-0af72e98.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-rectangle-has-ea85ec4e","Math G9: Determining the type of geometric transformation in the - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-2-2 Determining the type of geometric transformation in the coordinate plane, and drawing the image resulting from these transformations.","2. Distinguishing dilation and determining its type, center, and scale factor.","A rectangle has a length of 8 feet and a width of 5 feet. If it is enlarged by a scale factor of 2, what are the new dimensions of the rectangle?","16 feet by 10 feet","8 feet by 5 feet","10 feet by 6 feet","20 feet by 10 feet","16 feet by 10 feet","When a figure is enlarged by a scale factor, all of its dimensions are multiplied by that factor.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-rectangle-has-ea85ec4e.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-point-x-87f70c66","Math G9: Determining the type of geometric transformation in the - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-2-1 Determining the locations of points in the coordinate plane and plotting them, finding the distance between two points and the coordinates of the midpoint, and determining the slope of a line, writing its equation, and graphing it.","1. Distinguishing the type of transformation (reflection, translation, and rotation), and determining its elements.","A point (x, y) is translated 3 units up. What are the new coordinates of the point?","(x, y)","(x, y + 3)","(x, y - 3)","(x, y + 6)","(x, y + 3)","When a point is translated, its y-coordinate is increased by the translation value.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-point-x-87f70c66.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-triangle-has-c2879ee2","Math G9: Determining the type of geometric transformation in the - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-2-2 Determining the type of geometric transformation in the coordinate plane, and drawing the image resulting from these transformations.","1. Distinguishing the relationships between English units of length (inch, foot, yard, mile) and using them to convert between units.","A triangle has vertices at (0, 0), (3, 0), and (0, 4). If it is translated 2 units to the right, what are the new coordinates of the vertices?","(0, 0), (5, 0), (0, 4)","(0, 0), (3, 0), (0, 4)","(2, 0), (5, 0), (2, 4)","(0, 0), (1, 0), (0, 4)","(2, 0), (5, 0), (2, 4)","Translating 2 units right adds 2 to every x-coordinate, giving (2,0), (5,0), and (2,4).","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-triangle-has-c2879ee2.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-rectangle-has-6b345cce","Math G9: Determining the type of geometric transformation in the - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-2-2 Determining the type of geometric transformation in the coordinate plane, and drawing the image resulting from these transformations.","2. Distinguishing dilation and determining its type, center, and scale factor.","A rectangle has a length of 6 inches and a width of 4 inches. If it is enlarged by a scale factor of 3, what are the new dimensions of the rectangle?","18 inches by 12 inches","6 inches by 4 inches","12 inches by 8 inches","24 inches by 16 inches","18 inches by 12 inches","When a figure is enlarged by a scale factor, all of its dimensions are multiplied by that factor.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-rectangle-has-6b345cce.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-point-x-59997dbd","Math G9: Determining the type of geometric transformation in the - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-2-1 Determining the locations of points in the coordinate plane and plotting them, finding the distance between two points and the coordinates of the midpoint, and determining the slope of a line, writing its equation, and graphing it.","5. Finding the distance between two points in the coordinate plane, and finding the coordinates of the midpoint.","A point (x, y) is rotated 90 degrees counterclockwise about the origin. What are the new coordinates of the point?","(-y, x)","(y, x)","(x, -y)","(x, y)","(-y, x)","When a point is rotated 90 degrees counterclockwise about the origin, its coordinates are swapped and the new x-coordinate is negated.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-point-x-59997dbd.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-line-segment-ffdb908c","Math G9: Determining the type of geometric transformation in the - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-2-2 Determining the type of geometric transformation in the coordinate plane, and drawing the image resulting from these transformations.","2. Distinguishing dilation and determining its type, center, and scale factor.","A line segment has a length of 12 inches. If it is dilated by a scale factor of 2, what is the new length of the line segment?","6 inches","12 inches","18 inches","24 inches","24 inches","A scale factor of 2 multiplies the length 12 by 2, giving 24.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-line-segment-ffdb908c.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-triangle-has-94b9abd7","Math G9: Determining the type of geometric transformation in the - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-2-2 Determining the type of geometric transformation in the coordinate plane, and drawing the image resulting from these transformations.","2. Distinguishing dilation and determining its type, center, and scale factor.","A triangle has vertices at (0, 0), (3, 0), and (0, 4). If it is reflected over the y-axis, what are the new coordinates of the vertices?","(0, 0), (-3, 0), (0, 4)","-1","1","18","(0, 0), (-3, 0), (0, 4)","Reflecting over the y-axis changes each x-coordinate to its opposite while keeping the y-coordinate unchanged: (0,0), (-3,0), and (0,4).","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-triangle-has-94b9abd7.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-point-2-30a4cb39","Math G9: Determining the type of geometric transformation - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-2-1 Determining the locations of points in the coordinate plane and plotting them, finding the distance between two points and the coordinates of the midpoint, and determining the slope of a line, writing its equation, and graphing it.","5. Finding the distance between two points in the coordinate plane, and finding the coordinates of the midpoint.","A point (2, 3) is reflected over the x-axis. What are the coordinates of the reflected point?","(2, -3)","(2, 3)","(-2, 3)","(2, 0)","(2, -3)","When a point is reflected over the x-axis, the y-coordinate is negated.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-point-2-30a4cb39.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-dilation-is-d8055375","Math G9: Determining the type of geometric transformation - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-2-2 Determining the type of geometric transformation in the coordinate plane, and drawing the image resulting from these transformations.","2. Distinguishing dilation and determining its type, center, and scale factor.","A dilation is a transformation that changes the size of a figure. What type of dilation occurs when a figure is enlarged by a scale factor of 2?","The figure is reduced to half its original size.","The figure is enlarged by a scale factor of 2.","The figure remains the same size.","The figure is enlarged by a scale factor of 3.","The figure is enlarged by a scale factor of 2.","A dilation with a scale factor greater than 1 enlarges the figure.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-dilation-is-d8055375.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-center-of-8170c590","Math G9: Determining the type of geometric transformation - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-2-2 Determining the type of geometric transformation in the coordinate plane, and drawing the image resulting from these transformations.","2. Distinguishing dilation and determining its type, center, and scale factor.","What is the center of dilation for a figure that is enlarged by a scale factor of 3?","The point of enlargement is the center of dilation.","The center of dilation is the midpoint of the line segment.","The center of dilation is the point of enlargement.","The center of dilation is the endpoint of the line segment.","The point of enlargement is the center of dilation.","The point of enlargement is the center of dilation for a dilation.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-center-of-8170c590.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-dilation-a-646b0320","Math G9: Determining the type of geometric transformation - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-2-2 Determining the type of geometric transformation in the coordinate plane, and drawing the image resulting from these transformations.","2. Distinguishing dilation and determining its type, center, and scale factor.","A dilation with a scale factor of 1/2 reduces a figure to what size?","The figure is enlarged by a scale factor of 2.","The figure remains the same size.","The figure is reduced to half its original size.","The figure is enlarged by a scale factor of 3.","The figure is reduced to half its original size.","A dilation with a scale factor less than 1 reduces the figure.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-dilation-a-646b0320.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-scale-factor-394b0b6c","Math G9: Determining the type of geometric transformation - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-2-2 Determining the type of geometric transformation in the coordinate plane, and drawing the image resulting from these transformations.","2. Distinguishing dilation and determining its type, center, and scale factor.","What is the scale factor for a dilation that reduces a figure to 1/4 of its original size?","The scale factor is 1/2.","The scale factor is 1/4.","The scale factor is 2.","The scale factor is 3.","The scale factor is 1/4.","A dilation with a scale factor of 1/4 reduces the figure to 1/4 of its original size.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-scale-factor-394b0b6c.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-effect-of-3dda89f9","Math G9: Determining the type of geometric transformation - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-2-2 Determining the type of geometric transformation in the coordinate plane, and drawing the image resulting from these transformations.","2. Distinguishing dilation and determining its type, center, and scale factor.","What is the effect of a dilation with a scale factor of 0 on a figure?","The figure is enlarged by a scale factor of 2.","The figure remains the same size.","The figure is reduced to half its original size.","The figure disappears.","The figure disappears.","A dilation with a scale factor of 0 reduces the figure to a point.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-effect-of-3dda89f9.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-dilation-a-15ce8e4c","Math G9: Determining the type of geometric transformation - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-2-2 Determining the type of geometric transformation in the coordinate plane, and drawing the image resulting from these transformations.","2. Distinguishing dilation and determining its type, center, and scale factor.","A dilation with a scale factor of 2 enlarges a figure by a factor of what?","The figure is reduced to half its original size.","The figure is enlarged by a scale factor of 2.","The figure remains the same size.","The figure is enlarged by a scale factor of 3.","The figure is enlarged by a scale factor of 2.","A dilation with a scale factor greater than 1 enlarges the figure.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-dilation-a-15ce8e4c.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-effect-of-bb3c7221","Math G9: Determining the type of geometric transformation - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-2-2 Determining the type of geometric transformation in the coordinate plane, and drawing the image resulting from these transformations.","2. Distinguishing dilation and determining its type, center, and scale factor.","What is the effect of a dilation with a scale factor of 1 on a figure?","The figure is enlarged by a scale factor of 2.","The figure remains the same size.","The figure is reduced to half its original size.","The figure disappears.","The figure remains the same size.","A dilation with a scale factor of 1 leaves the figure unchanged.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-effect-of-bb3c7221.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-dilation-a-08cf28d0","Math G9: Determining the type of geometric transformation - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-2-2 Determining the type of geometric transformation in the coordinate plane, and drawing the image resulting from these transformations.","2. Distinguishing dilation and determining its type, center, and scale factor.","A dilation with a scale factor of 3/2 enlarges a figure by a factor of what?","The figure is reduced to half its original size.","The figure is enlarged by a scale factor of 2.","The figure remains the same size.","The figure is enlarged by a scale factor of 3.","The figure is enlarged by a scale factor of 3.","A dilation with a scale factor greater than 1 enlarges the figure.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-dilation-a-08cf28d0.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-center-of-305cb285","Math G9: Determining the type of geometric transformation - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-2-1 Determining the locations of points in the coordinate plane and plotting them, finding the distance between two points and the coordinates of the midpoint, and determining the slope of a line, writing its equation, and graphing it.","5. Finding the distance between two points in the coordinate plane, and finding the coordinates of the midpoint.","What is the center of dilation for a figure that is reduced to half its original size?","The point of enlargement is the center of dilation.","The center of dilation is the midpoint of the line segment.","The center of dilation is the point of enlargement.","The center of dilation is the endpoint of the line segment.","The point of enlargement is the center of dilation.","The point of enlargement is the center of dilation for a dilation.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-center-of-305cb285.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-dilation-a-ed931c0e","Math G9: Determining the type of geometric transformation - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-2-2 Determining the type of geometric transformation in the coordinate plane, and drawing the image resulting from these transformations.","2. Distinguishing dilation and determining its type, center, and scale factor.","A dilation with a scale factor of 1/3 reduces a figure to what size?","The figure is enlarged by a scale factor of 2.","The figure remains the same size.","The figure is reduced to 1/3 of its original size.","The figure is enlarged by a scale factor of 3.","The figure is reduced to 1/3 of its original size.","A dilation with a scale factor less than 1 reduces the figure.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-dilation-a-ed931c0e.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-value-of-1927d6d3","Math G9: Distinguishing the basic trigonometric ratios of - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-1-4 Distinguishing the congruence and similarity of two polygons, using them to find unknown measures, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Distinguishing the basic trigonometric ratios (sine, cosine, and tangent) and finding them for an acute angle in a right triangle (manually and using a calculator), rounded to the nearest given place.","What is the value of the cosine of an angle in a right triangle if the length of the hypotenuse is 30 cm and the length of the adjacent leg is 20 cm?","The cosine of the angle is 0.6","The cosine of the angle is 0.4","The cosine of the angle is 0.8","The cosine of the angle is 0.2","The cosine of the angle is 0.6","The cosine of an angle in a right triangle is the ratio of the length of the adjacent leg to the length of the hypotenuse.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-in-a-right-70b7fb39","Math G9: Distinguishing the basic trigonometric ratios of - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-1-4 Distinguishing the congruence and similarity of two polygons, using them to find unknown measures, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Distinguishing the basic trigonometric ratios (sine, cosine, and tangent) and finding them for an acute angle in a right triangle (manually and using a calculator), rounded to the nearest given place.","In a right triangle, the length of the hypotenuse is 25 cm and the length of one leg is 15 cm. What is the sine of the angle opposite the 15 cm leg?","The sine of the angle is 0.6","The sine of the angle is 0.4","The sine of the angle is 0.8","The sine of the angle is 0.2","The sine of the angle is 0.6","The sine of an angle in a right triangle is the ratio of the length of the leg opposite the angle to the length of the hypotenuse.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-in-a-right-70b7fb39.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-in-a-right-761c5ebb","Math G9: Distinguishing the basic trigonometric ratios of - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-1-4 Distinguishing the congruence and similarity of two polygons, using them to find unknown measures, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Distinguishing the basic trigonometric ratios (sine, cosine, and tangent) and finding them for an acute angle in a right triangle (manually and using a calculator), rounded to the nearest given place.","In a right triangle, if the length of the hypotenuse is 10 cm and the length of one leg is 6 cm, what is the sine of the angle opposite the 6 cm leg?","The sine of the angle is 0.6","The sine of the angle is 0.4","The sine of the angle is 0.8","The sine of the angle is 0.2","The sine of the angle is 0.6","The sine of an angle in a right triangle is the ratio of the length of the leg opposite the angle to the length of the hypotenuse.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-in-a-right-761c5ebb.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-value-of-735aab5c","Math G9: Distinguishing the basic trigonometric ratios of - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-1-4 Distinguishing the congruence and similarity of two polygons, using them to find unknown measures, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Distinguishing the basic trigonometric ratios (sine, cosine, and tangent) and finding them for an acute angle in a right triangle (manually and using a calculator), rounded to the nearest given place.","What is the value of the cosine of an angle in a right triangle if the length of the hypotenuse is 15 cm and the length of the adjacent leg is 9 cm?","The cosine of the angle is 0.6","The cosine of the angle is 0.4","The cosine of the angle is 0.8","The cosine of the angle is 0.2","The cosine of the angle is 0.6","The cosine of an angle in a right triangle is the ratio of the length of the adjacent leg to the length of the hypotenuse.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-if-length-of-80975b8f","Math G9: Distinguishing the basic trigonometric ratios of - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-1-4 Distinguishing the congruence and similarity of two polygons, using them to find unknown measures, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Distinguishing the basic trigonometric ratios (sine, cosine, and tangent) and finding them for an acute angle in a right triangle (manually and using a calculator), rounded to the nearest given place.","If the length of the hypotenuse of a right triangle is 15 cm and the length of one leg is 9 cm, what is the cosecant of the angle opposite the 9 cm leg?","The cosecant of the angle is 1.6","The cosecant of the angle is 1.4","The cosecant of the angle is 1.8","The cosecant of the angle is 1.2","The cosecant of the angle is 1.6","The cosecant of an angle in a right triangle is the ratio of the length of the hypotenuse to the length of the leg opposite the angle.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-if-length-of-80975b8f.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-value-of-797063b4","Math G9: Distinguishing the basic trigonometric ratios of - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-1-4 Distinguishing the congruence and similarity of two polygons, using them to find unknown measures, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Distinguishing the basic trigonometric ratios (sine, cosine, and tangent) and finding them for an acute angle in a right triangle (manually and using a calculator), rounded to the nearest given place.","What is the value of the cotangent of an angle in a right triangle if the length of the hypotenuse is 18 cm and the length of the adjacent leg is 12 cm?","The cotangent of the angle is 1.5","The cotangent of the angle is 1.2","The cotangent of the angle is 1.8","The cotangent of the angle is 1.4","The cotangent of the angle is 1.5","The cotangent of an angle in a right triangle is the ratio of the length of the adjacent leg to the length of the leg opposite the angle.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-if-length-of-de13bed9","Math G9: Distinguishing the basic trigonometric ratios of - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-1-4 Distinguishing the congruence and similarity of two polygons, using them to find unknown measures, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Distinguishing the basic trigonometric ratios (sine, cosine, and tangent) and finding them for an acute angle in a right triangle (manually and using a calculator), rounded to the nearest given place.","If the length of the hypotenuse of a right triangle is 12 cm and the length of one leg is 8 cm, what is the tangent of the angle opposite the 8 cm leg?","The tangent of the angle is 0.6","The tangent of the angle is 0.4","The tangent of the angle is 0.8","The tangent of the angle is 0.2","The tangent of the angle is 0.6","The tangent of an angle in a right triangle is the ratio of the length of the leg opposite the angle to the length of the adjacent leg.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-if-length-of-de13bed9.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-in-a-right-0c90796a","Math G9: Distinguishing the basic trigonometric ratios of - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-1-4 Distinguishing the congruence and similarity of two polygons, using them to find unknown measures, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Distinguishing the basic trigonometric ratios (sine, cosine, and tangent) and finding them for an acute angle in a right triangle (manually and using a calculator), rounded to the nearest given place.","In a right triangle, the length of the hypotenuse is 20 cm and the length of one leg is 12 cm. What is the secant of the angle opposite the 12 cm leg?","The secant of the angle is 1.6","The secant of the angle is 1.4","The secant of the angle is 1.8","The secant of the angle is 1.2","The secant of the angle is 1.6","The secant of an angle in a right triangle is the ratio of the length of the hypotenuse to the length of the adjacent leg.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-in-a-right-0c90796a.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-in-a-right-3d34d0b4","Math G9: Distinguishing the basic trigonometric ratios of - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-1-4 Distinguishing the congruence and similarity of two polygons, using them to find unknown measures, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Distinguishing the basic trigonometric ratios (sine, cosine, and tangent) and finding them for an acute angle in a right triangle (manually and using a calculator), rounded to the nearest given place.","In a right triangle, the length of the hypotenuse is 18 cm and the length of one leg is 12 cm. What is the secant of the angle opposite the 12 cm leg?","The secant of the angle is 1.5","The secant of the angle is 1.2","The secant of the angle is 1.8","The secant of the angle is 1.4","The secant of the angle is 1.5","The secant of an angle in a right triangle is the ratio of the length of the hypotenuse to the length of the adjacent leg.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-in-a-right-3d34d0b4.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-if-length-of-f155daae","Math G9: Distinguishing the basic trigonometric ratios of - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-1-4 Distinguishing the congruence and similarity of two polygons, using them to find unknown measures, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Distinguishing the basic trigonometric ratios (sine, cosine, and tangent) and finding them for an acute angle in a right triangle (manually and using a calculator), rounded to the nearest given place.","If the length of the hypotenuse of a right triangle is 20 cm and the length of one leg is 12 cm, what is the tangent of the angle opposite the 12 cm leg?","The tangent of the angle is 0.6","The tangent of the angle is 0.4","The tangent of the angle is 0.8","The tangent of the angle is 0.2","The tangent of the angle is 0.6","The tangent of an angle in a right triangle is the ratio of the length of the leg opposite the angle to the length of the adjacent leg.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-if-length-of-f155daae.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-in-a-right-d5f6c1d7","Math G9: Distinguishing the congruence and similarity of - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-1-4 Distinguishing the congruence and similarity of two polygons, using them to find unknown measures, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Distinguishing the basic trigonometric ratios (sine, cosine, and tangent) and finding them for an acute angle in a right triangle (manually and using a calculator), rounded to the nearest given place.","In a right triangle, if the length of the hypotenuse is 18 cm and the length of one leg is 8 cm, what is the cosine of the acute angle adjacent to the 8 cm leg?","The cosine of the angle is 0.4, rounded to the nearest tenth.","0.6","0.8","2.3","The cosine of the angle is 0.4, rounded to the nearest tenth.","Cosine is adjacent divided by hypotenuse: 8 ÷ 18 ≈ 0.4 when rounded to the nearest tenth.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-in-a-right-d5f6c1d7.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-in-a-right-fb2b8a50","Math G9: Distinguishing the congruence and similarity of - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-1-4 Distinguishing the congruence and similarity of two polygons, using them to find unknown measures, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Distinguishing the basic trigonometric ratios (sine, cosine, and tangent) and finding them for an acute angle in a right triangle (manually and using a calculator), rounded to the nearest given place.","In a right triangle, if the length of the hypotenuse is 15 cm and the length of one leg is 9 cm, what is the cosine of the acute angle adjacent to the 9 cm leg?","The cosine of the angle is 0.6, rounded to the nearest tenth.","0.4","0.8","1.7","The cosine of the angle is 0.6, rounded to the nearest tenth.","Cosine is adjacent divided by hypotenuse: 9 ÷ 15 = 0.6.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-in-a-right-fb2b8a50.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-in-a-right-6f1e4c44","Math G9: Distinguishing the congruence and similarity of - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-1-4 Distinguishing the congruence and similarity of two polygons, using them to find unknown measures, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Distinguishing the basic trigonometric ratios (sine, cosine, and tangent) and finding them for an acute angle in a right triangle (manually and using a calculator), rounded to the nearest given place.","In a right triangle, if the length of the hypotenuse is 10 cm and the length of one leg is 6 cm, what is the sine of the acute angle opposite the 6 cm leg?","The sine of the angle is 0.6, rounded to the nearest tenth.","The sine of the angle is 0.4, rounded to the nearest tenth.","The sine of the angle is 0.8, rounded to the nearest tenth.","The sine of the angle is 0.2, rounded to the nearest tenth.","The sine of the angle is 0.6, rounded to the nearest tenth.","To find the sine of the angle, we use the ratio of the length of the opposite side (6 cm) to the length of the hypotenuse (10 cm). This gives us a sine of 6/10 = 0.6, rounded to the nearest tenth.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-in-a-right-6f1e4c44.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-in-a-right-abfa5f34","Math G9: Distinguishing the congruence and similarity of - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-1-4 Distinguishing the congruence and similarity of two polygons, using them to find unknown measures, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Distinguishing the basic trigonometric ratios (sine, cosine, and tangent) and finding them for an acute angle in a right triangle (manually and using a calculator), rounded to the nearest given place.","In a right triangle, if the length of the hypotenuse is 20 cm and the length of one leg is 12 cm, what is the sine of the acute angle opposite the 12 cm leg?","The sine of the angle is 0.6, rounded to the nearest tenth.","The sine of the angle is 0.4, rounded to the nearest tenth.","The sine of the angle is 0.8, rounded to the nearest tenth.","The sine of the angle is 0.2, rounded to the nearest tenth.","The sine of the angle is 0.6, rounded to the nearest tenth.","To find the sine of the angle, we use the ratio of the length of the opposite side (12 cm) to the length of the hypotenuse (20 cm). This gives us a sine of 12/20 = 0.6, rounded to the nearest tenth.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-in-a-right-abfa5f34.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-in-a-right-b69f126c","Math G9: Distinguishing the congruence and similarity of - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-1-4 Distinguishing the congruence and similarity of two polygons, using them to find unknown measures, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Distinguishing the basic trigonometric ratios (sine, cosine, and tangent) and finding them for an acute angle in a right triangle (manually and using a calculator), rounded to the nearest given place.","In a right triangle, if the length of the hypotenuse is 12 cm and the length of one leg is 5 cm, what is the tangent of the acute angle opposite the 5 cm leg?","The tangent of the angle is 0.5, rounded to the nearest tenth.","The tangent of the angle is 0.6, rounded to the nearest tenth.","The tangent of the angle is 0.8, rounded to the nearest tenth.","The tangent of the angle is 0.2, rounded to the nearest tenth.","The tangent of the angle is 0.5, rounded to the nearest tenth.","The adjacent leg is sqrt(12^2 - 5^2) = sqrt(119), which is about 10.9. The tangent is 5/10.9, about 0.46, which rounds to 0.5 to the nearest tenth.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-in-a-right-b69f126c.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-in-a-right-54a481c1","Math G9: Distinguishing the congruence and similarity of - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-1-4 Distinguishing the congruence and similarity of two polygons, using them to find unknown measures, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Distinguishing the basic trigonometric ratios (sine, cosine, and tangent) and finding them for an acute angle in a right triangle (manually and using a calculator), rounded to the nearest given place.","In a right triangle, if the length of the hypotenuse is 15 cm and the length of one leg is 9 cm, what is the tangent of the acute angle opposite the 9 cm leg?","The tangent of the angle is 0.8, rounded to the nearest tenth.","0.6","1.3","0.4","The tangent of the angle is 0.8, rounded to the nearest tenth.","The other leg is 12 cm. Tangent is opposite divided by adjacent: 9 ÷ 12 = 0.75, which rounds to 0.8.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-in-a-right-54a481c1.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-in-a-right-4525cff4","Math G9: Distinguishing the congruence and similarity of - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-1-4 Distinguishing the congruence and similarity of two polygons, using them to find unknown measures, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Distinguishing the basic trigonometric ratios (sine, cosine, and tangent) and finding them for an acute angle in a right triangle (manually and using a calculator), rounded to the nearest given place.","In a right triangle, if the length of the hypotenuse is 12 cm and the length of one leg is 5 cm, what is the sine of the acute angle opposite the 5 cm leg?","The sine of the angle is 0.4, rounded to the nearest tenth.","The sine of the angle is 0.6, rounded to the nearest tenth.","The sine of the angle is 0.8, rounded to the nearest tenth.","The sine of the angle is 0.2, rounded to the nearest tenth.","The sine of the angle is 0.4, rounded to the nearest tenth.","The sine is opposite divided by hypotenuse: 5/12 = 0.416..., which rounds to 0.4 to the nearest tenth.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-in-a-right-4525cff4.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-in-a-right-3a6bbee4","Math G9: Distinguishing the congruence and similarity of - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-1-4 Distinguishing the congruence and similarity of two polygons, using them to find unknown measures, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Distinguishing the basic trigonometric ratios (sine, cosine, and tangent) and finding them for an acute angle in a right triangle (manually and using a calculator), rounded to the nearest given place.","In a right triangle, if the length of the hypotenuse is 20 cm and the length of one leg is 12 cm, what is the cosine of the acute angle adjacent to the 12 cm leg?","The cosine of the angle is 0.6, rounded to the nearest tenth.","0.4","0.8","1.7","The cosine of the angle is 0.6, rounded to the nearest tenth.","Cosine is adjacent divided by hypotenuse: 12 ÷ 20 = 0.6.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-in-a-right-3a6bbee4.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-in-a-right-85e646f5","Math G9: Distinguishing the congruence and similarity of - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-1-4 Distinguishing the congruence and similarity of two polygons, using them to find unknown measures, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Distinguishing the basic trigonometric ratios (sine, cosine, and tangent) and finding them for an acute angle in a right triangle (manually and using a calculator), rounded to the nearest given place.","In a right triangle, if the length of the hypotenuse is 18 cm and the length of one leg is 8 cm, what is the tangent of the acute angle opposite the 8 cm leg?","The tangent of the angle is 0.5, rounded to the nearest tenth.","The tangent of the angle is 0.6, rounded to the nearest tenth.","The tangent of the angle is 0.8, rounded to the nearest tenth.","The tangent of the angle is 0.2, rounded to the nearest tenth.","The tangent of the angle is 0.5, rounded to the nearest tenth.","The adjacent leg is sqrt(18^2 - 8^2) = sqrt(260), which is about 16.1. The tangent is 8/16.1, about 0.50, which rounds to 0.5 to the nearest tenth.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-in-a-right-85e646f5.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-relationship-corresponding-9c868b3c","Math G9: Distinguishing the congruence and similarity of - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-1-1 Distinguishing the interior angles of polygons, and the relationships between angles formed by a transversal intersecting parallel lines, and using them to find unknown angle measures, and determining polygons that can form tessellations.","2. Distinguishing pairs of angles formed when a transversal intersects two parallel lines (alternate interior and exterior angles, corresponding angles), identifying the relationships between them, and using them to find unknown angle measures.","What is the relationship between the corresponding sides of similar polygons?","The corresponding sides are equal","The corresponding sides are supplementary","The corresponding sides are proportional","The corresponding sides are congruent","The corresponding sides are proportional","When two polygons are similar, their corresponding sides are proportional.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-if-two-polygons-041c9c54","Math G9: Distinguishing the congruence and similarity of - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-1-1 Distinguishing the interior angles of polygons, and the relationships between angles formed by a transversal intersecting parallel lines, and using them to find unknown angle measures, and determining polygons that can form tessellations.","2. Distinguishing pairs of angles formed when a transversal intersects two parallel lines (alternate interior and exterior angles, corresponding angles), identifying the relationships between them, and using them to find unknown angle measures.","If two polygons are similar, what does this mean about the corresponding angles?","The corresponding angles are equal","The corresponding angles are supplementary","The corresponding angles are complementary","The corresponding angles are congruent","The corresponding angles are equal","When two polygons are similar, their corresponding angles are equal.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-if-two-polygons-041c9c54.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-relationship-corresponding-82d1e004","Math G9: Distinguishing the congruence and similarity of - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-1-1 Distinguishing the interior angles of polygons, and the relationships between angles formed by a transversal intersecting parallel lines, and using them to find unknown angle measures, and determining polygons that can form tessellations.","2. Distinguishing pairs of angles formed when a transversal intersects two parallel lines (alternate interior and exterior angles, corresponding angles), identifying the relationships between them, and using them to find unknown angle measures.","What is the relationship between the corresponding angles of two similar polygons with a scale factor of 2?","The corresponding angles are equal","The corresponding angles are supplementary","The corresponding angles are complementary","The corresponding angles are congruent","The corresponding angles are equal","When two polygons are similar and have a scale factor of 2, their corresponding angles are equal.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-relationship-corresponding-82d1e004.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-relationship-corresponding-1719571a","Math G9: Distinguishing the congruence and similarity of - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-1-4 Distinguishing the congruence and similarity of two polygons, using them to find unknown measures, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. Distinguishing the similarity of two polygons and using its to identify similar polygons and find unknown measures.","What is the relationship between the corresponding sides of two similar polygons with a scale factor of 4?","The corresponding sides are equal","The corresponding sides are supplementary","The corresponding sides are proportional, with the first polygon being larger","The corresponding sides are proportional, with the second polygon being larger","The corresponding sides are proportional, with the first polygon being larger","With a scale factor of 4 from the second polygon to the first polygon, the corresponding sides of the first polygon are four times as long as the corresponding sides of the second polygon.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-if-two-polygons-30adab7c","Math G9: Distinguishing the congruence and similarity of - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-1-4 Distinguishing the congruence and similarity of two polygons, using them to find unknown measures, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. Distinguishing the similarity of two polygons and using its to identify similar polygons and find unknown measures.","If two polygons are similar, what does this mean about the corresponding areas?","The corresponding areas are equal","The corresponding areas are supplementary","The corresponding areas are proportional","The corresponding areas are congruent","The corresponding areas are proportional","When two polygons are similar, their corresponding areas are proportional.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-if-a-polygon-9217c974","Math G9: Distinguishing the congruence and similarity of - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-1-4 Distinguishing the congruence and similarity of two polygons, using them to find unknown measures, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. Distinguishing the similarity of two polygons and using its to identify similar polygons and find unknown measures.","If a polygon has a scale factor of 1/2 with respect to another polygon, what does this mean about the corresponding sides?","The corresponding sides are equal","The corresponding sides are supplementary","The corresponding sides are proportional, with the first polygon being larger","The corresponding sides are proportional, with the second polygon being larger","The corresponding sides are proportional, with the first polygon being larger","A scale factor of 1/2 means the corresponding sides of the second polygon are half as long as the corresponding sides of the first polygon.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-if-a-polygon-9217c974.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-relationship-corresponding-dabb70c1","Math G9: Distinguishing the congruence and similarity of - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-1-4 Distinguishing the congruence and similarity of two polygons, using them to find unknown measures, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. Distinguishing the similarity of two polygons and using its to identify similar polygons and find unknown measures.","What is the relationship between the corresponding sides of two similar polygons with a scale factor of 3?","The corresponding sides are equal","The corresponding sides are supplementary","The corresponding sides are proportional, with the first polygon being larger","The corresponding sides are proportional, with the second polygon being larger","The corresponding sides are proportional, with the second polygon being larger","A scale factor of 3 means the corresponding sides of the first polygon are three times as long as the corresponding sides of the second polygon.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-if-two-polygons-a9f37ba6","Math G9: Distinguishing the congruence and similarity of - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-1-4 Distinguishing the congruence and similarity of two polygons, using them to find unknown measures, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. Distinguishing the similarity of two polygons and using its to identify similar polygons and find unknown measures.","If two polygons are similar, what does this mean about the corresponding perimeters?","The corresponding perimeters are equal","The corresponding perimeters are supplementary","The corresponding perimeters are proportional","The corresponding perimeters are congruent","The corresponding perimeters are proportional","When two polygons are similar, their corresponding perimeters are proportional.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-relationship-corresponding-41cbb6ff","Math G9: Distinguishing the congruence and similarity of - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-1-1 Distinguishing the interior angles of polygons, and the relationships between angles formed by a transversal intersecting parallel lines, and using them to find unknown angle measures, and determining polygons that can form tessellations.","2. Distinguishing pairs of angles formed when a transversal intersects two parallel lines (alternate interior and exterior angles, corresponding angles), identifying the relationships between them, and using them to find unknown angle measures.","What is the relationship between the corresponding angles of two similar polygons with a scale factor of 1?","The corresponding angles are equal","The corresponding angles are supplementary","The corresponding angles are complementary","The corresponding angles are congruent","The corresponding angles are equal","When two polygons are similar and have a scale factor of 1, their corresponding angles are equal.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-relationship-corresponding-41cbb6ff.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-if-a-polygon-60883add","Math G9: Distinguishing the congruence and similarity of - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-1-4 Distinguishing the congruence and similarity of two polygons, using them to find unknown measures, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. Distinguishing the similarity of two polygons and using its to identify similar polygons and find unknown measures.","If a polygon has a scale factor of 2 with respect to another polygon, what does this mean about the corresponding sides?","The corresponding sides are equal","The corresponding sides are supplementary","The corresponding sides are proportional, with the first polygon being larger","The corresponding sides are proportional, with the second polygon being larger","The corresponding sides are proportional, with the second polygon being larger","A scale factor of 2 means the corresponding sides of the first polygon are twice as long as the corresponding sides of the second polygon.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-if-a-polygon-60883add.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-relationship-corresponding-67d031b5","Math G9: Distinguishing the congruence and similarity of two polygons - Set 3","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-1-1 Distinguishing the interior angles of polygons, and the relationships between angles formed by a transversal intersecting parallel lines, and using them to find unknown angle measures, and determining polygons that can form tessellations.","2. Distinguishing pairs of angles formed when a transversal intersects two parallel lines (alternate interior and exterior angles, corresponding angles), identifying the relationships between them, and using them to find unknown angle measures.","What is the relationship between the corresponding diagonals of similar polygons?","The corresponding diagonals are always equal","The corresponding diagonals are always proportional","The corresponding diagonals are always supplementary","The corresponding diagonals are always complementary","The corresponding diagonals are always proportional","When two polygons are similar, their corresponding diagonals are always proportional.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-scale-factor-765b6e13","Math G9: Distinguishing the congruence and similarity of two polygons - Set 3","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-1-4 Distinguishing the congruence and similarity of two polygons, using them to find unknown measures, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. Distinguishing the similarity of two polygons and using its to identify similar polygons and find unknown measures.","What is the scale factor of two similar polygons if the ratio of their corresponding sides is 3:5?","3:5","5:3","3:4","5:4","3:5","The ratio of 3 to 5 is written 3:5.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-scale-factor-765b6e13.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-if-two-polygons-a1253896","Math G9: Distinguishing the congruence and similarity of two polygons - Set 3","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-1-4 Distinguishing the congruence and similarity of two polygons, using them to find unknown measures, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. Distinguishing the similarity of two polygons and using its to identify similar polygons and find unknown measures.","If two polygons are similar, what can be concluded about their areas?","The areas are always equal","The areas are always proportional","The areas are always supplementary","The areas are always complementary","The areas are always proportional","When two polygons are similar, their areas are always proportional.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-term-two-6ebc24b4","Math G9: Distinguishing the congruence and similarity of two polygons - Set 3","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-1-4 Distinguishing the congruence and similarity of two polygons, using them to find unknown measures, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. Distinguishing the similarity of two polygons and using its to identify similar polygons and find unknown measures.","What is the term for two polygons that have the same shape but not necessarily the same size?","Similar polygons","Congruent polygons","Equivalent polygons","Identical polygons","Similar polygons","Two polygons that have the same shape but not necessarily the same size are called similar polygons.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-term-two-6ebc24b4.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-if-a-polygon-623d1a59","Math G9: Distinguishing the congruence and similarity of two polygons - Set 3","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-1-1 Distinguishing the interior angles of polygons, and the relationships between angles formed by a transversal intersecting parallel lines, and using them to find unknown angle measures, and determining polygons that can form tessellations.","1. Distinguishing the sum of the interior angle measures of a polygon and using its to find the measure of an angle in a regular polygon or unknown angle measures, and determining polygons that can form tessellations.","If a polygon is similar to a regular hexagon, what can be concluded about its sides?","The sides are always equal","The sides are always proportional","The sides are always supplementary","The sides are always complementary","The sides are always equal","Any regular hexagon is similar to another regular hexagon: corresponding angles are equal and corresponding side lengths are proportional.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-if-a-polygon-623d1a59.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-if-two-polygons-a5e6b3e6","Math G9: Distinguishing the congruence and similarity of two polygons - Set 3","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-1-4 Distinguishing the congruence and similarity of two polygons, using them to find unknown measures, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. Distinguishing the similarity of two polygons and using its to identify similar polygons and find unknown measures.","If two polygons are similar, what can be concluded about their perimeters?","The perimeters are always equal","The perimeters are always proportional","The perimeters are always supplementary","The perimeters are always complementary","The perimeters are always proportional","When two polygons are similar, their perimeters are always proportional.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-ratio-of-04b4498c","Math G9: Distinguishing the congruence and similarity of two polygons - Set 3","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-1-4 Distinguishing the congruence and similarity of two polygons, using them to find unknown measures, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. Distinguishing the similarity of two polygons and using its to identify similar polygons and find unknown measures.","What is the ratio of the corresponding sides of two similar polygons called?","Scale factor","Similarity ratio","Proportionality constant","Corresponding side ratio","Scale factor","The ratio of the corresponding sides of two similar polygons is called the scale factor.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-if-a-polygon-5cfe832c","Math G9: Distinguishing the congruence and similarity of two polygons - Set 3","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-1-4 Distinguishing the congruence and similarity of two polygons, using them to find unknown measures, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. Distinguishing the similarity of two polygons and using its to identify similar polygons and find unknown measures.","If a polygon is similar to a square, what can be concluded about its angles?","Corresponding side lengths are proportional.","The triangles have equal areas.","All corresponding angles are right angles.","The figures must have the same perimeter.","All corresponding angles are right angles.","All squares have four right angles, so their corresponding angles are congruent. Their side lengths may differ by a scale factor.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-if-a-polygon-5cfe832c.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-relationship-corresponding-4683d819","Math G9: Distinguishing the congruence and similarity of two polygons - Set 3","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-1-1 Distinguishing the interior angles of polygons, and the relationships between angles formed by a transversal intersecting parallel lines, and using them to find unknown angle measures, and determining polygons that can form tessellations.","2. Distinguishing pairs of angles formed when a transversal intersects two parallel lines (alternate interior and exterior angles, corresponding angles), identifying the relationships between them, and using them to find unknown angle measures.","What is the relationship between the corresponding sides of similar polygons?","The corresponding sides are always equal","The corresponding sides are always proportional","The corresponding sides are always supplementary","The corresponding sides are always complementary","The corresponding sides are always proportional","When two polygons are similar, their corresponding sides are always proportional.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-if-two-polygons-66ac24f2","Math G9: Distinguishing the congruence and similarity of two polygons - Set 3","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-1-1 Distinguishing the interior angles of polygons, and the relationships between angles formed by a transversal intersecting parallel lines, and using them to find unknown angle measures, and determining polygons that can form tessellations.","2. Distinguishing pairs of angles formed when a transversal intersects two parallel lines (alternate interior and exterior angles, corresponding angles), identifying the relationships between them, and using them to find unknown angle measures.","If two polygons are similar, what can be concluded about their corresponding angles?","The corresponding angles are always equal","The corresponding angles are always supplementary","The corresponding angles are always congruent","The corresponding angles are always complementary","The corresponding angles are always congruent","When two polygons are similar, their corresponding angles are always congruent.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-if-two-polygons-66ac24f2.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-if-a-square-93faf27f","Math G9: Distinguishing the formulas for perimeter and - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-3-2 Distinguishing the formulas for perimeter and area of two-dimensional shapes, using them to find perimeters and areas, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","2. Distinguishing the relationship between the perimeters of similar shapes, and using its to find unknown measures.","If a square and a rectangle are similar, what is the relationship between their side lengths?","The side lengths are equal.","The side lengths are proportional.","The side lengths are inversely proportional.","The side lengths are unrelated.","The side lengths are proportional.","Since the shapes are similar, their corresponding sides are proportional.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-if-a-square-93faf27f.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-if-a-rectangle-8cf70b9f","Math G9: Distinguishing the formulas for perimeter and - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-3-2 Distinguishing the formulas for perimeter and area of two-dimensional shapes, using them to find perimeters and areas, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","2. Distinguishing the relationship between the perimeters of similar shapes, and using its to find unknown measures.","If a rectangle and a square are similar, what is the relationship between their areas?","The areas are equal.","The areas are proportional.","The areas are inversely proportional.","The areas are unrelated.","The areas are proportional.","Since the shapes are similar, their corresponding sides are proportional, which means their areas are also proportional.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-if-a-rectangle-8cf70b9f.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-triangle-a-190393f7","Math G9: Distinguishing the formulas for perimeter and - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-3-2 Distinguishing the formulas for perimeter and area of two-dimensional shapes, using them to find perimeters and areas, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","2. Distinguishing the relationship between the perimeters of similar shapes, and using its to find unknown measures.","A triangle and a square are similar. If the perimeter of the triangle is 12 cm, what is the perimeter of the square?","6 cm","12 cm","18 cm","24 cm","12 cm","Since the shapes are similar, their corresponding sides are proportional. If the perimeter of the triangle is 12 cm, the perimeter of the square will also be 12 cm.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-if-a-square-7995f99f","Math G9: Distinguishing the formulas for perimeter and - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-3-2 Distinguishing the formulas for perimeter and area of two-dimensional shapes, using them to find perimeters and areas, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","2. Distinguishing the relationship between the perimeters of similar shapes, and using its to find unknown measures.","If a square and a rectangle are similar, what is the relationship between their perimeters?","The perimeters are equal.","The perimeters are proportional.","The perimeters are inversely proportional.","The perimeters are unrelated.","The perimeters are proportional.","Since the shapes are similar, their corresponding sides are proportional, which means their perimeters are also proportional.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-if-a-square-7995f99f.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-triangle-a-0098c9d3","Math G9: Distinguishing the formulas for perimeter and - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-3-2 Distinguishing the formulas for perimeter and area of two-dimensional shapes, using them to find perimeters and areas, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","2. Distinguishing the relationship between the perimeters of similar shapes, and using its to find unknown measures.","A triangle and a square are similar. If the area of the triangle is 20 square cm, what is the area of the square?","5 square cm","10 square cm","20 square cm","40 square cm","10 square cm","Since the shapes are similar, their corresponding sides are proportional, which means their areas are also proportional.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-if-a-square-75c882a5","Math G9: Distinguishing the formulas for perimeter and - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-3-2 Distinguishing the formulas for perimeter and area of two-dimensional shapes, using them to find perimeters and areas, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","2. Distinguishing the relationship between the perimeters of similar shapes, and using its to find unknown measures.","If a square and a rectangle are similar, what is the relationship between their areas and side lengths?","The areas are equal to the side lengths.","The areas are proportional to the side lengths.","The areas are inversely proportional to the side lengths.","The areas are unrelated to the side lengths.","The areas are proportional to the side lengths.","Since the shapes are similar, their corresponding sides are proportional, which means their areas are also proportional.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-if-a-square-75c882a5.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-triangle-a-4268c687","Math G9: Distinguishing the formulas for perimeter and - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-3-2 Distinguishing the formulas for perimeter and area of two-dimensional shapes, using them to find perimeters and areas, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","2. Distinguishing the relationship between the perimeters of similar shapes, and using its to find unknown measures.","A triangle and a rectangle are similar. If the perimeter of the triangle is 18 cm, what is the perimeter of the rectangle?","9 cm","18 cm","27 cm","36 cm","18 cm","Since the shapes are similar, their corresponding sides are proportional, which means their perimeters are also proportional.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-if-a-rectangle-48ff3705","Math G9: Distinguishing the formulas for perimeter and - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-3-2 Distinguishing the formulas for perimeter and area of two-dimensional shapes, using them to find perimeters and areas, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","2. Distinguishing the relationship between the perimeters of similar shapes, and using its to find unknown measures.","If a rectangle and a square are similar, what is the relationship between their perimeters and side lengths?","The perimeters are equal to the side lengths.","The perimeters are proportional to the side lengths.","The perimeters are inversely proportional to the side lengths.","The perimeters are unrelated to the side lengths.","The perimeters are proportional to the side lengths.","Since the shapes are similar, their corresponding sides are proportional, which means their perimeters are also proportional.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-if-a-rectangle-48ff3705.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-triangle-a-be15b514","Math G9: Distinguishing the formulas for perimeter and - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-1-3 Distinguishing the properties of triangles and the relationship between the sides of a right triangle (the Pythagorean Theorem), using them to find unknown measures, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","2. Distinguishing the relationship between the sides of a right triangle (the Pythagorean Theorem), using its to find the length of an unknown side, and using its converse to determine whether a triangle is a right triangle.","A triangle and a square are similar. If the side length of the triangle is 6 cm, what is the side length of the square?","3 cm","6 cm","9 cm","12 cm","6 cm","Since the shapes are similar, their corresponding sides are proportional.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-triangle-a-be15b514.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-triangle-a-50d8a53b","Math G9: Distinguishing the formulas for perimeter and - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-3-2 Distinguishing the formulas for perimeter and area of two-dimensional shapes, using them to find perimeters and areas, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","2. Distinguishing the relationship between the perimeters of similar shapes, and using its to find unknown measures.","A triangle and a rectangle are similar. If the area of the triangle is 15 square cm, what is the area of the rectangle?","7.5 square cm","15 square cm","30 square cm","45 square cm","15 square cm","Since the shapes are similar, their corresponding sides are proportional, which means their areas are also proportional.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-formula-volume-f5825718","Math G9: Distinguishing the formulas for volume and - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-3-3 Distinguishing the formulas for volume and surface area of three- dimensional shapes, using them to find volume and surface area, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","2. Distinguishing the surface area formulas for right square pyramids, right triangular pyramids, cylinders, and cones, and using them to find their surface areas.","What is the formula for the volume of a rectangular prism?","V = l * w * h","V = l + w + h","V = l * w - h","V = l * w / h","V = l * w * h","The formula for the volume of a rectangular prism is V = l * w * h, where l is the length, w is the width, and h is the height.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-formula-volume-f5825718.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-formula-surface-0b2a3a87","Math G9: Distinguishing the formulas for volume and - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-3-3 Distinguishing the formulas for volume and surface area of three- dimensional shapes, using them to find volume and surface area, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","2. Distinguishing the surface area formulas for right square pyramids, right triangular pyramids, cylinders, and cones, and using them to find their surface areas.","What is the formula for the surface area of a rectangular prism?","SA = 2 * (l + w)","SA = l * w * h","SA = l + w + h","SA = l * w - h","SA = 2 * (l + w)","The formula for the surface area of a rectangular prism is SA = 2 * (l + w), where l is the length and w is the width.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-formula-surface-0b2a3a87.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-formula-volume-e24b81ec","Math G9: Distinguishing the formulas for volume and - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-3-3 Distinguishing the formulas for volume and surface area of three- dimensional shapes, using them to find volume and surface area, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","2. Distinguishing the surface area formulas for right square pyramids, right triangular pyramids, cylinders, and cones, and using them to find their surface areas.","What is the formula for the volume of a sphere?","V = (4/3) * ? * r^3","V = ? * r^2","V = 2 * ? * r","V = ? * r","V = (4/3) * ? * r^3","The formula for the volume of a sphere is V = (4/3) * ? * r^3, where r is the radius.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-formula-volume-e24b81ec.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-formula-surface-da471cc6","Math G9: Distinguishing the formulas for volume and - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-3-3 Distinguishing the formulas for volume and surface area of three- dimensional shapes, using them to find volume and surface area, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","2. Distinguishing the surface area formulas for right square pyramids, right triangular pyramids, cylinders, and cones, and using them to find their surface areas.","What is the formula for the surface area of a sphere?","SA = 4 * ? * r^2","SA = 2 * ? * r","SA = ? * r^2","SA = ? * r","SA = 4 * ? * r^2","The formula for the surface area of a sphere is SA = 4 * ? * r^2, where r is the radius.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-formula-surface-da471cc6.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-rectangular-prism-161ff9c7","Math G9: Distinguishing the formulas for volume and - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-3-3 Distinguishing the formulas for volume and surface area of three- dimensional shapes, using them to find volume and surface area, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","2. Distinguishing the surface area formulas for right square pyramids, right triangular pyramids, cylinders, and cones, and using them to find their surface areas.","A rectangular prism has a length of 5 cm, a width of 3 cm, and a height of 2 cm. What is its volume?","The volume is 30 cubic centimeters.","The volume is 27 cubic centimeters.","The volume is 33 cubic centimeters.","The volume is 300 cubic centimeters.","The volume is 30 cubic centimeters.","Volume of a rectangular prism is length x width x height: 5 x 3 x 2 = 30 cubic centimeters.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-rectangular-prism-161ff9c7.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-cone-has-6b30739a","Math G9: Distinguishing the formulas for volume and - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-3-3 Distinguishing the formulas for volume and surface area of three- dimensional shapes, using them to find volume and surface area, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","2. Distinguishing the surface area formulas for right square pyramids, right triangular pyramids, cylinders, and cones, and using them to find their surface areas.","A cone has a radius of 4 cm and a height of 6 cm. What is its surface area?","The surface area is 80 cm^2.","The surface area is 96 cm^2.","The surface area is 112 cm^2.","The surface area is 128 cm^2.","The surface area is 96 cm^2.","The surface area of the cone is 96 cm^2.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-cone-has-6b30739a.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-right-square-66e521ad","Math G9: Distinguishing the formulas for volume and - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-3-3 Distinguishing the formulas for volume and surface area of three- dimensional shapes, using them to find volume and surface area, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","2. Distinguishing the surface area formulas for right square pyramids, right triangular pyramids, cylinders, and cones, and using them to find their surface areas.","A right square pyramid has a base length of 8 cm and a slant height of 10 cm. What is its surface area?","The surface area is 320 cm^2.","The surface area is 360 cm^2.","The surface area is 400 cm^2.","The surface area is 440 cm^2.","The surface area is 360 cm^2.","The surface area of the right square pyramid is 360 cm^2.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-right-square-66e521ad.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-formula-surface-531a5934","Math G9: Distinguishing the formulas for volume and - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-3-3 Distinguishing the formulas for volume and surface area of three- dimensional shapes, using them to find volume and surface area, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","2. Distinguishing the surface area formulas for right square pyramids, right triangular pyramids, cylinders, and cones, and using them to find their surface areas.","What is the formula for the surface area of a right square pyramid?","The surface area is given by the formula 2lw + 2lh + 2wh.","The surface area is given by the formula lw + lh + wh.","The surface area is given by the formula 2lw + 2lh + 2wh + 4l^2.","The surface area is given by the formula lw + lh + wh + 4l^2.","The surface area is given by the formula 2lw + 2lh + 2wh.","The surface area of a right square pyramid is given by the formula 2lw + 2lh + 2wh.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-formula-surface-531a5934.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-right-triangular-e57bcf42","Math G9: Distinguishing the formulas for volume and - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-3-3 Distinguishing the formulas for volume and surface area of three- dimensional shapes, using them to find volume and surface area, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","2. Distinguishing the surface area formulas for right square pyramids, right triangular pyramids, cylinders, and cones, and using them to find their surface areas.","A right triangular pyramid has a base length of 6 cm and a slant height of 9 cm. What is its surface area?","The surface area is 180 cm^2.","The surface area is 216 cm^2.","The surface area is 252 cm^2.","The surface area is 288 cm^2.","The surface area is 216 cm^2.","The surface area of the right triangular pyramid is 216 cm^2.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-right-triangular-e57bcf42.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-formula-surface-70f3bbb2","Math G9: Distinguishing the formulas for volume and - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-3-3 Distinguishing the formulas for volume and surface area of three- dimensional shapes, using them to find volume and surface area, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","2. Distinguishing the surface area formulas for right square pyramids, right triangular pyramids, cylinders, and cones, and using them to find their surface areas.","What is the formula for the surface area of a cylinder?","The surface area is given by the formula 2lw + 2lh.","The surface area is given by the formula lw + lh + wh.","The surface area is given by the formula 2lw + 2lh + 2wh.","The surface area is given by the formula lw + lh + wh + 4l^2.","The surface area is given by the formula 2lw + 2lh.","The surface area of a cylinder is given by the formula 2lw + 2lh.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-formula-surface-70f3bbb2.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-formula-surface-71830247","Math G9: Distinguishing the formulas for volume and - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-3-3 Distinguishing the formulas for volume and surface area of three- dimensional shapes, using them to find volume and surface area, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","2. Distinguishing the surface area formulas for right square pyramids, right triangular pyramids, cylinders, and cones, and using them to find their surface areas.","What is the formula for the surface area of a cone?","The surface area is given by the formula lw + lh + wh.","The surface area is given by the formula 2lw + 2lh + 2wh.","The surface area is given by the formula lw + lh + wh + 4l^2.","The surface area is given by the formula pi*r^2 + pi*r*l.","The surface area is given by the formula pi*r^2 + pi*r*l.","The surface area of a cone is pi*r^2 + pi*r*l, where r is the radius and l is the slant height.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-formula-surface-71830247.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-right-square-30cbf36c","Math G9: Distinguishing the formulas for volume and - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-3-3 Distinguishing the formulas for volume and surface area of three- dimensional shapes, using them to find volume and surface area, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","2. Distinguishing the surface area formulas for right square pyramids, right triangular pyramids, cylinders, and cones, and using them to find their surface areas.","A right square pyramid has a base length of 6 cm and a slant height of 8 cm. What is its surface area?","The surface area is 216 cm^2.","The surface area is 240 cm^2.","The surface area is 288 cm^2.","The surface area is 312 cm^2.","The surface area is 240 cm^2.","The surface area of the right square pyramid is 240 cm^2.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-right-square-30cbf36c.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-right-triangular-40b5dbea","Math G9: Distinguishing the formulas for volume and - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-3-3 Distinguishing the formulas for volume and surface area of three- dimensional shapes, using them to find volume and surface area, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","2. Distinguishing the surface area formulas for right square pyramids, right triangular pyramids, cylinders, and cones, and using them to find their surface areas.","A right triangular pyramid has a base length of 4 cm and a slant height of 6 cm. What is its surface area?","The surface area is 80 cm^2.","The surface area is 96 cm^2.","The surface area is 112 cm^2.","The surface area is 128 cm^2.","The surface area is 96 cm^2.","The surface area of the right triangular pyramid is 96 cm^2.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-right-triangular-40b5dbea.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-cylinder-has-8499ba80","Math G9: Distinguishing the formulas for volume and - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-3-3 Distinguishing the formulas for volume and surface area of three- dimensional shapes, using them to find volume and surface area, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","2. Distinguishing the surface area formulas for right square pyramids, right triangular pyramids, cylinders, and cones, and using them to find their surface areas.","A cylinder has a radius of 3 cm and a height of 8 cm. What is its surface area?","The surface area is 90 cm^2.","The surface area is 120 cm^2.","The surface area is 150 cm^2.","The surface area is 180 cm^2.","The surface area is 120 cm^2.","The surface area of the cylinder is 150 cm^2.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-cylinder-has-8499ba80.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-formula-surface-6aeee9a2","Math G9: Distinguishing the formulas for volume and - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-3-3 Distinguishing the formulas for volume and surface area of three- dimensional shapes, using them to find volume and surface area, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","2. Distinguishing the surface area formulas for right square pyramids, right triangular pyramids, cylinders, and cones, and using them to find their surface areas.","What is the formula for the surface area of a right triangular pyramid?","The surface area is given by the formula lw + lh + wh.","The surface area is given by the formula 2lw + 2lh + 2wh.","The surface area is given by the formula lw + lh + wh + 4l^2.","The surface area is given by the formula lw + lh + wh + 2l^2.","The surface area is given by the formula lw + lh + wh.","The surface area of a right triangular pyramid is given by the formula lw + lh + wh.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-formula-surface-6aeee9a2.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-sphere-has-a22f6775","Math G9: Distinguishing the formulas for volume and surface area - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-3-3 Distinguishing the formulas for volume and surface area of three- dimensional shapes, using them to find volume and surface area, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","2. Distinguishing the surface area formulas for right square pyramids, right triangular pyramids, cylinders, and cones, and using them to find their surface areas.","A sphere has a diameter of 14 cm. What is its surface area?","The surface area is 616 square centimeters.","The surface area is 154 square centimeters.","The surface area is 196 square centimeters.","The surface area is 784 square centimeters.","The surface area is 616 square centimeters.","The surface area of a sphere is 4*pi*r^2. The radius is half the diameter, so r = 7 cm. Therefore, the surface area is 4*pi*(7)^2 = 196*pi, which is about 616 square centimeters.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-sphere-has-a22f6775.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-cone-has-682cf6b4","Math G9: Distinguishing the formulas for volume and surface area - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-3-3 Distinguishing the formulas for volume and surface area of three- dimensional shapes, using them to find volume and surface area, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","2. Distinguishing the surface area formulas for right square pyramids, right triangular pyramids, cylinders, and cones, and using them to find their surface areas.","A cone has a radius of 4 cm and a height of 12 cm. What is its volume?","The volume is 32*pi cubic centimeters.","The volume is 64*pi cubic centimeters.","The volume is 128*pi cubic centimeters.","The volume is 256*pi cubic centimeters.","The volume is 64*pi cubic centimeters.","The volume of a cone is (1/3)*pi*r^2*h. With r = 4 cm and h = 12 cm, the volume is (1/3)*pi*(4)^2*(12) = 64*pi cubic centimeters.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-cone-has-682cf6b4.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-cylinder-has-e7c265d3","Math G9: Distinguishing the formulas for volume and surface area - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-3-3 Distinguishing the formulas for volume and surface area of three- dimensional shapes, using them to find volume and surface area, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","2. Distinguishing the surface area formulas for right square pyramids, right triangular pyramids, cylinders, and cones, and using them to find their surface areas.","A cylinder has a radius of 5 cm and a height of 10 cm. What is its surface area?","The surface area is 150*pi square centimeters.","The surface area is 100*pi square centimeters.","The surface area is 50*pi square centimeters.","The surface area is 300*pi square centimeters.","The surface area is 150*pi square centimeters.","The surface area of a cylinder is 2*pi*r^2 + 2*pi*r*h. With r = 5 cm and h = 10 cm, the surface area is 2*pi*(5)^2 + 2*pi*(5)(10) = 50*pi + 100*pi = 150*pi square centimeters.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-cylinder-has-e7c265d3.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-rectangular-prism-45ba446a","Math G9: Distinguishing the formulas for volume and surface area - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-3-3 Distinguishing the formulas for volume and surface area of three- dimensional shapes, using them to find volume and surface area, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","2. Distinguishing the surface area formulas for right square pyramids, right triangular pyramids, cylinders, and cones, and using them to find their surface areas.","A rectangular prism has a length of 8 cm, a width of 6 cm, and a height of 4 cm. What is its surface area?","The surface area is 208 square centimeters.","The surface area is 192 square centimeters.","The surface area is 144 square centimeters.","The surface area is 96 square centimeters.","The surface area is 208 square centimeters.","The surface area of a rectangular prism is calculated by the formula 2lw + 2lh + 2wh, where l is the length, w is the width, and h is the height. In this case, the surface area is 2(8)(6) + 2(8)(4) + 2(6)(4) = 96 + 64 + 48 = 208 square centimeters.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-rectangular-prism-45ba446a.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-formula-volume-1cd039f1","Math G9: Distinguishing the formulas for volume and surface area - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-3-3 Distinguishing the formulas for volume and surface area of three- dimensional shapes, using them to find volume and surface area, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","2. Distinguishing the surface area formulas for right square pyramids, right triangular pyramids, cylinders, and cones, and using them to find their surface areas.","What is the formula for the volume of a cylinder?","Volume equals the base area times the height","Volume equals one half the base area times the height","Volume equals one third the base area times the height","Volume equals two thirds the base area times the height","Volume equals the base area times the height","The formula for the volume of a cylinder is indeed the base area times the height, which is why option A is correct.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-formula-volume-1cd039f1.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-sphere-has-a71fe1b9","Math G9: Distinguishing the formulas for volume and surface area - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-3-3 Distinguishing the formulas for volume and surface area of three- dimensional shapes, using them to find volume and surface area, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","2. Distinguishing the surface area formulas for right square pyramids, right triangular pyramids, cylinders, and cones, and using them to find their surface areas.","A sphere has a radius of 9 cm. What is its volume?","The volume is 729*pi cubic centimeters.","The volume is 972*pi cubic centimeters.","The volume is 1458*pi cubic centimeters.","The volume is 2916*pi cubic centimeters.","The volume is 972*pi cubic centimeters.","The volume of a sphere is (4/3)*pi*r^3. With r = 9 cm, the volume is (4/3)*pi*(9)^3 = 972*pi cubic centimeters, which is about 3054 cubic centimeters.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-sphere-has-a71fe1b9.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-formula-volume-aff41c33","Math G9: Distinguishing the formulas for volume and surface area - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-3-3 Distinguishing the formulas for volume and surface area of three- dimensional shapes, using them to find volume and surface area, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","2. Distinguishing the surface area formulas for right square pyramids, right triangular pyramids, cylinders, and cones, and using them to find their surface areas.","What is the formula for the volume of a right square pyramid?","Volume equals one third the base area times the height","Volume equals the base area times the height","Volume equals one half the base area times the height","Volume equals two thirds the base area times the height","Volume equals one third the base area times the height","The formula for the volume of a right square pyramid is indeed one third the base area times the height, which is why option A is correct.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-formula-volume-aff41c33.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-water-tank-435aed15","Math G9: Distinguishing the formulas for volume and surface area - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-3-3 Distinguishing the formulas for volume and surface area of three- dimensional shapes, using them to find volume and surface area, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","2. Distinguishing the surface area formulas for right square pyramids, right triangular pyramids, cylinders, and cones, and using them to find their surface areas.","A water tank is in the shape of a right triangular pyramid with a base area of 48 square meters and a height of 10 meters. What is the volume of the tank?","160 cubic meters","240 cubic meters","320 cubic meters","480 cubic meters","160 cubic meters","To find the volume, we use the formula for the volume of a right triangular pyramid, which is one third the base area times the height. So, volume equals one third times 48 times 10, which equals 160 cubic meters.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-water-tank-435aed15.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-rectangular-prism-32a56167","Math G9: Distinguishing the formulas for volume and surface area - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-3-3 Distinguishing the formulas for volume and surface area of three- dimensional shapes, using them to find volume and surface area, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","2. Distinguishing the surface area formulas for right square pyramids, right triangular pyramids, cylinders, and cones, and using them to find their surface areas.","A rectangular prism has a length of 6 cm, a width of 4 cm, and a height of 8 cm. What is its volume?","The volume is 192 cubic centimeters.","The volume is 173 cubic centimeters.","The volume is 211 cubic centimeters.","The volume is 1,920 cubic centimeters.","The volume is 192 cubic centimeters.","Volume of a rectangular prism is length x width x height: 6 x 4 x 8 = 192 cubic centimeters.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-rectangular-prism-32a56167.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-cylinder-has-49624487","Math G9: Distinguishing the formulas for volume and surface area - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-3-3 Distinguishing the formulas for volume and surface area of three- dimensional shapes, using them to find volume and surface area, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","2. Distinguishing the surface area formulas for right square pyramids, right triangular pyramids, cylinders, and cones, and using them to find their surface areas.","A cylinder has a radius of 3 cm and a height of 15 cm. What is its volume?","The volume is 135*pi cubic centimeters.","The volume is 225*pi cubic centimeters.","The volume is 450*pi cubic centimeters.","The volume is 675*pi cubic centimeters.","The volume is 135*pi cubic centimeters.","The volume of a cylinder is pi*r^2*h. With r = 3 cm and h = 15 cm, the volume is pi*(3)^2*(15) = 135*pi cubic centimeters.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-cylinder-has-49624487.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-cone-has-c096f4d6","Math G9: Distinguishing the formulas for volume and surface area - Set 1 T1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-3-3 Distinguishing the formulas for volume and surface area of three- dimensional shapes, using them to find volume and surface area, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","2. Distinguishing the surface area formulas for right square pyramids, right triangular pyramids, cylinders, and cones, and using them to find their surface areas.","A cone has a base radius of 6 meters and a height of 8 meters. What is the volume of the cone?","72 pi cubic meters","96 pi cubic meters","288 pi cubic meters","576 pi cubic meters","96 pi cubic meters","To find the volume, we use the formula for the volume of a cone, which is one third times pi times the radius squared times the height. So, volume equals one third times pi times 6 squared times 8, which equals 96 pi cubic meters.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-right-triangular-e0411dd9","Math G9: Distinguishing the formulas for volume and surface area - Set 1 T1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-3-3 Distinguishing the formulas for volume and surface area of three- dimensional shapes, using them to find volume and surface area, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","2. Distinguishing the surface area formulas for right square pyramids, right triangular pyramids, cylinders, and cones, and using them to find their surface areas.","A right triangular pyramid has a base area of 20 square meters and a height of 15 meters. What is the volume of the pyramid?","50 cubic meters","75 cubic meters","100 cubic meters","150 cubic meters","100 cubic meters","V = (1/3)Bh = (1/3)(20)(15) = 100 cubic meters.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-right-triangular-e0411dd9.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-cone-has-96469a1c","Math G9: Distinguishing the formulas for volume and surface area - Set 1 T1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-3-3 Distinguishing the formulas for volume and surface area of three- dimensional shapes, using them to find volume and surface area, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","2. Distinguishing the surface area formulas for right square pyramids, right triangular pyramids, cylinders, and cones, and using them to find their surface areas.","A cone has a base radius of 9 meters and a height of 12 meters. What is the volume of the cone?","216 pi cubic meters","288 pi cubic meters","324 pi cubic meters","972 pi cubic meters","324 pi cubic meters","To find the volume, we use the formula for the volume of a cone, which is one third times pi times the radius squared times the height. So, volume equals one third times pi times 9 squared times 12, which equals 324 pi cubic meters.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-cylinder-has-2026ea48","Math G9: Distinguishing the formulas for volume and surface area - Set 1 T1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-3-3 Distinguishing the formulas for volume and surface area of three- dimensional shapes, using them to find volume and surface area, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","2. Distinguishing the surface area formulas for right square pyramids, right triangular pyramids, cylinders, and cones, and using them to find their surface areas.","A cylinder has a base radius of 3 centimeters and a height of 8 centimeters. What is the volume of the cylinder?","48 pi cubic centimeters","72 pi cubic centimeters","144 pi cubic centimeters","288 pi cubic centimeters","72 pi cubic centimeters","The volume of a cylinder is V = πr²h = π(3²)(8) = 72π cubic units.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-cylinder-has-2026ea48.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-right-square-78925f05","Math G9: Distinguishing the formulas for volume and surface area - Set 1 T1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-3-3 Distinguishing the formulas for volume and surface area of three- dimensional shapes, using them to find volume and surface area, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","2. Distinguishing the surface area formulas for right square pyramids, right triangular pyramids, cylinders, and cones, and using them to find their surface areas.","A right square pyramid has a base length of 8 meters and a height of 12 meters. What is the volume of the pyramid?","128 cubic meters","256 cubic meters","384 cubic meters","512 cubic meters","256 cubic meters","The volume of a square pyramid is V = (1/3)Bh = (1/3)(8²)(12) = 256 cubic units.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-right-square-78925f05.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-formula-volume-a13756bd","Math G9: Distinguishing the formulas for volume and surface area - Set 1 T1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-3-3 Distinguishing the formulas for volume and surface area of three- dimensional shapes, using them to find volume and surface area, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","2. Distinguishing the surface area formulas for right square pyramids, right triangular pyramids, cylinders, and cones, and using them to find their surface areas.","What is the formula for the volume of a cone?","Volume equals one third the base area times the height","Volume equals the base area times the height","Volume equals one half the base area times the height","Volume equals two thirds the base area times the height","Volume equals one third the base area times the height","The formula for the volume of a cone is indeed one third the base area times the height, which is why option A is correct.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-formula-volume-a13756bd.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-cylindrical-can-3917b56d","Math G9: Distinguishing the formulas for volume and surface area - Set 1 T1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-3-3 Distinguishing the formulas for volume and surface area of three- dimensional shapes, using them to find volume and surface area, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","2. Distinguishing the surface area formulas for right square pyramids, right triangular pyramids, cylinders, and cones, and using them to find their surface areas.","A cylindrical can has a base radius of 4 centimeters and a height of 10 centimeters. What is the volume of the can?","160 pi cubic centimeters","160 pi cubic meters","1600 pi cubic centimeters","1600 pi cubic meters","160 pi cubic centimeters","To find the volume, we use the formula for the volume of a cylinder, which is pi times the radius squared times the height. So, volume equals pi times 4 squared times 10, which equals 160 pi cubic centimeters.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-right-square-f8539c2e","Math G9: Distinguishing the formulas for volume and surface area - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-3-3 Distinguishing the formulas for volume and surface area of three- dimensional shapes, using them to find volume and surface area, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","2. Distinguishing the surface area formulas for right square pyramids, right triangular pyramids, cylinders, and cones, and using them to find their surface areas.","A right square pyramid has a base length of 6 cm and a slant height of 8 cm. What is its surface area?","132 cm²","96 cm²","36 cm²","228 cm²","132 cm²","For a square pyramid with base side 6 cm and slant height 8 cm: SA = 6² + 2(6)(8) = 132 cm².","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-right-square-f8539c2e.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-formula-surface-4ff6d09b","Math G9: Distinguishing the formulas for volume and surface area - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-3-3 Distinguishing the formulas for volume and surface area of three- dimensional shapes, using them to find volume and surface area, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","2. Distinguishing the surface area formulas for right square pyramids, right triangular pyramids, cylinders, and cones, and using them to find their surface areas.","What is the formula for the surface area of a cylinder?","2πr² + 2πrh","πr²h","2πrh","πr² + πrℓ","2πr² + 2πrh","A cylinder has two circular bases with total area 2πr² and lateral area 2πrh.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-formula-surface-4ff6d09b.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-formula-surface-4a7d3f42","Math G9: Distinguishing the formulas for volume and surface area - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-3-3 Distinguishing the formulas for volume and surface area of three- dimensional shapes, using them to find volume and surface area, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","2. Distinguishing the surface area formulas for right square pyramids, right triangular pyramids, cylinders, and cones, and using them to find their surface areas.","What is the formula for the surface area of a right triangular pyramid?","Base area + the areas of the three triangular faces","Four times the base area","Base area × height","Only the area of one triangular face","Base area + the areas of the three triangular faces","The total surface area of a triangular pyramid is the area of its triangular base plus the areas of its three triangular side faces.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-formula-surface-4a7d3f42.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-formula-surface-6e588c2c","Math G9: Distinguishing the formulas for volume and surface area - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-3-3 Distinguishing the formulas for volume and surface area of three- dimensional shapes, using them to find volume and surface area, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","2. Distinguishing the surface area formulas for right square pyramids, right triangular pyramids, cylinders, and cones, and using them to find their surface areas.","What is the formula for the surface area of a right square pyramid?","s² + 2sℓ","4s²","s² + 4sℓ","2s² + sℓ","s² + 2sℓ","A square pyramid has base area s² and four triangular faces with total area 4(½sℓ) = 2sℓ, so SA = s² + 2sℓ.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-formula-surface-6e588c2c.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-right-square-b75f3693","Math G9: Distinguishing the formulas for volume and surface area - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-3-3 Distinguishing the formulas for volume and surface area of three- dimensional shapes, using them to find volume and surface area, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","2. Distinguishing the surface area formulas for right square pyramids, right triangular pyramids, cylinders, and cones, and using them to find their surface areas.","A right square pyramid has a base area of 16 square cm and a slant height of 10 cm. What is its surface area?","96 cm²","80 cm²","56 cm²","176 cm²","96 cm²","The square base has side length 4 cm. Lateral area = 2sℓ = 2(4)(10) = 80 cm²; total surface area = 16 + 80 = 96 cm².","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-right-square-b75f3693.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-cone-has-549fa4b7","Math G9: Distinguishing the formulas for volume and surface area - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-3-3 Distinguishing the formulas for volume and surface area of three- dimensional shapes, using them to find volume and surface area, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","2. Distinguishing the surface area formulas for right square pyramids, right triangular pyramids, cylinders, and cones, and using them to find their surface areas.","What is the total surface area of a cone with radius 2 units and slant height 4 units?","About 37.7 square units","About 25.1 square units","About 50.3 square units","About 12.6 square units","About 37.7 square units","With radius 2 and slant height 4, SA = πr² + πrℓ = 4π + 8π = 12π ≈ 37.7 square units.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-cone-has-549fa4b7.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-cylinder-has-aff4ddb9","Math G9: Distinguishing the formulas for volume and surface area - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-3-3 Distinguishing the formulas for volume and surface area of three- dimensional shapes, using them to find volume and surface area, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","2. Distinguishing the surface area formulas for right square pyramids, right triangular pyramids, cylinders, and cones, and using them to find their surface areas.","A cylinder has a radius of 3 cm and a height of 5 cm. What is its surface area?","48π cm²","30π cm²","24π cm²","80π cm²","48π cm²","For r = 3 cm and h = 5 cm, SA = 2πr² + 2πrh = 18π + 30π = 48π cm².","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-cylinder-has-aff4ddb9.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-right-triangular-948757a3","Math G9: Distinguishing the formulas for volume and surface area - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-3-3 Distinguishing the formulas for volume and surface area of three- dimensional shapes, using them to find volume and surface area, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","2. Distinguishing the surface area formulas for right square pyramids, right triangular pyramids, cylinders, and cones, and using them to find their surface areas.","What is the total surface area of a right square pyramid with base side length 4 units and slant height 6 units?","64 square units","48 square units","80 square units","96 square units","64 square units","For a right square pyramid with base side 4 and slant height 6: SA = 4² + 2(4)(6) = 16 + 48 = 64 square units.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-right-triangular-948757a3.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-right-triangular-3a003cbf","Math G9: Distinguishing the formulas for volume and surface area - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-3-3 Distinguishing the formulas for volume and surface area of three- dimensional shapes, using them to find volume and surface area, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","2. Distinguishing the surface area formulas for right square pyramids, right triangular pyramids, cylinders, and cones, and using them to find their surface areas.","A triangular pyramid has a base area of 12 square units and a total lateral area of 48 square units. What is its total surface area?","60 square units","48 square units","36 square units","72 square units","60 square units","Total surface area equals base area plus total lateral area: 12 + 48 = 60 square units.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-right-triangular-3a003cbf.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-formula-surface-5e7b0e37","Math G9: Distinguishing the formulas for volume and surface area - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-3-3 Distinguishing the formulas for volume and surface area of three- dimensional shapes, using them to find volume and surface area, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","2. Distinguishing the surface area formulas for right square pyramids, right triangular pyramids, cylinders, and cones, and using them to find their surface areas.","What is the formula for the surface area of a cone?","πr² + πrℓ","2πr² + 2πrh","πr²h","2πrℓ","πr² + πrℓ","A cone’s total surface area is the base area πr² plus lateral area πrℓ.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-formula-surface-5e7b0e37.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-relationship-interior-d578fb49","Math G9: Distinguishing the interior angles of polygons - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-1-1 Distinguishing the interior angles of polygons, and the relationships between angles formed by a transversal intersecting parallel lines, and using them to find unknown angle measures, and determining polygons that can form tessellations.","1. Distinguishing the sum of the interior angle measures of a polygon and using its to find the measure of an angle in a regular polygon or unknown angle measures, and determining polygons that can form tessellations.","What is the relationship between the interior angles of a triangle and a pentagon?","The interior angles of a triangle are always greater than the interior angles of a pentagon.","The interior angles of a triangle are always less than the interior angles of a pentagon.","The interior angles of a triangle and a pentagon are equal.","The interior angles of a triangle and a pentagon are not related.","The interior angles of a triangle are always less than the interior angles of a pentagon.","The sum of the interior angles of a triangle is always 180 degrees, while the sum of the interior angles of a pentagon is always 540 degrees. Therefore, the interior angles of a triangle are always less than the interior angles of a pentagon.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-relationship-interior-d578fb49.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-sum-of-da0b151d","Math G9: Distinguishing the interior angles of polygons - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-1-1 Distinguishing the interior angles of polygons, and the relationships between angles formed by a transversal intersecting parallel lines, and using them to find unknown angle measures, and determining polygons that can form tessellations.","1. Distinguishing the sum of the interior angle measures of a polygon and using its to find the measure of an angle in a regular polygon or unknown angle measures, and determining polygons that can form tessellations.","What is the sum of the interior angles of a hexagon?","The sum of the interior angles of a hexagon is 720 degrees.","540 degrees","900 degrees","1080 degrees","The sum of the interior angles of a hexagon is 720 degrees.","The sum of interior angles is (n - 2) x 180. For a hexagon, (6 - 2) x 180 = 720 degrees.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-name-of-9d6823f3","Math G9: Distinguishing the interior angles of polygons - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-1-1 Distinguishing the interior angles of polygons, and the relationships between angles formed by a transversal intersecting parallel lines, and using them to find unknown angle measures, and determining polygons that can form tessellations.","1. Distinguishing shapes that are symmetric about an axis, identifying axes of symmetry and shapes that have rotational symmetry about a point, and determining angles of rotation.","What is the name of the line that divides a shape into two parts of equal size?","Axis of rotation","Axis of symmetry","Line of reflection","Line of symmetry","Axis of symmetry","An axis of symmetry is a line that divides a shape into two parts of equal size. It is a line about which a shape looks the same on both sides.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-name-of-9d6823f3.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-relationship-interior-6add0ec3","Math G9: Distinguishing the interior angles of polygons - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-1-1 Distinguishing the interior angles of polygons, and the relationships between angles formed by a transversal intersecting parallel lines, and using them to find unknown angle measures, and determining polygons that can form tessellations.","1. Distinguishing the sum of the interior angle measures of a polygon and using its to find the measure of an angle in a regular polygon or unknown angle measures, and determining polygons that can form tessellations.","What is the relationship between the interior angles of a triangle and a quadrilateral?","The interior angles of a triangle are always greater than the interior angles of a quadrilateral.","The interior angles of a triangle are always less than the interior angles of a quadrilateral.","The interior angles of a triangle and a quadrilateral are equal.","The interior angles of a triangle and a quadrilateral are not related.","The interior angles of a triangle are always less than the interior angles of a quadrilateral.","The sum of the interior angles of a triangle is always 180 degrees, while the sum of the interior angles of a quadrilateral is always 360 degrees. Therefore, the interior angles of a triangle are always less than the interior angles of a quadrilateral.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-relationship-interior-6add0ec3.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-name-of-890061c6","Math G9: Distinguishing the interior angles of polygons - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-1-1 Distinguishing the interior angles of polygons, and the relationships between angles formed by a transversal intersecting parallel lines, and using them to find unknown angle measures, and determining polygons that can form tessellations.","1. Distinguishing shapes that are symmetric about an axis, identifying axes of symmetry and shapes that have rotational symmetry about a point, and determining angles of rotation.","What is the name of a line that divides a shape into two mirror-image halves?","Axis of rotation","Line of symmetry","Tangent line","Diagonal","Line of symmetry","A line of symmetry divides a figure into two mirror-image halves.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-name-of-890061c6.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-sum-of-2a1c4635","Math G9: Distinguishing the interior angles of polygons - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-1-1 Distinguishing the interior angles of polygons, and the relationships between angles formed by a transversal intersecting parallel lines, and using them to find unknown angle measures, and determining polygons that can form tessellations.","1. Distinguishing the sum of the interior angle measures of a polygon and using its to find the measure of an angle in a regular polygon or unknown angle measures, and determining polygons that can form tessellations.","What is the sum of the interior angles of a nonagon?","The sum of the interior angles of a nonagon is 1260 degrees.","1080 degrees","1440 degrees","1620 degrees","The sum of the interior angles of a nonagon is 1260 degrees.","The sum of interior angles is (n - 2) x 180. For a nonagon, (9 - 2) x 180 = 1260 degrees.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-sum-of-23231485","Math G9: Distinguishing the interior angles of polygons - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-1-1 Distinguishing the interior angles of polygons, and the relationships between angles formed by a transversal intersecting parallel lines, and using them to find unknown angle measures, and determining polygons that can form tessellations.","1. Distinguishing the sum of the interior angle measures of a polygon and using its to find the measure of an angle in a regular polygon or unknown angle measures, and determining polygons that can form tessellations.","What is the sum of the interior angles of an octagon?","The sum of the interior angles of a octagon is 1080 degrees.","900 degrees","1260 degrees","1440 degrees","The sum of the interior angles of a octagon is 1080 degrees.","The sum of interior angles is (n - 2) x 180. For a octagon, (8 - 2) x 180 = 1080 degrees.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-relationship-interior-57ceb89e","Math G9: Distinguishing the interior angles of polygons - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-1-1 Distinguishing the interior angles of polygons, and the relationships between angles formed by a transversal intersecting parallel lines, and using them to find unknown angle measures, and determining polygons that can form tessellations.","1. Distinguishing the sum of the interior angle measures of a polygon and using its to find the measure of an angle in a regular polygon or unknown angle measures, and determining polygons that can form tessellations.","What is the relationship between the interior angles of a square and a rectangle?","The interior angles of a square are always greater than the interior angles of a rectangle.","The interior angles of a square are always less than the interior angles of a rectangle.","The interior angles of a square and a rectangle are equal.","The interior angles of a square and a rectangle are not related.","The interior angles of a square and a rectangle are equal.","A square is a special type of rectangle with four right angles. Therefore, the interior angles of a square and a rectangle are equal.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-relationship-interior-57ceb89e.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-name-of-83db53ff","Math G9: Distinguishing the interior angles of polygons - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-1-1 Distinguishing the interior angles of polygons, and the relationships between angles formed by a transversal intersecting parallel lines, and using them to find unknown angle measures, and determining polygons that can form tessellations.","2. Distinguishing pairs of angles formed when a transversal intersects two parallel lines (alternate interior and exterior angles, corresponding angles), identifying the relationships between them, and using them to find unknown angle measures.","What is the name of the angle formed by a transversal and two parallel lines?","Corresponding angle","Alternate interior angle","Alternate exterior angle","Corresponding exterior angle","Alternate interior angle","An alternate interior angle is an angle formed by a transversal and two parallel lines. It is an angle that is on the opposite side of the transversal from the corresponding angle.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-name-of-83db53ff.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-sum-of-5b242adc","Math G9: Distinguishing the interior angles of polygons - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-1-1 Distinguishing the interior angles of polygons, and the relationships between angles formed by a transversal intersecting parallel lines, and using them to find unknown angle measures, and determining polygons that can form tessellations.","1. Distinguishing the sum of the interior angle measures of a polygon and using its to find the measure of an angle in a regular polygon or unknown angle measures, and determining polygons that can form tessellations.","What is the sum of the interior angles of a heptagon?","The sum of the interior angles of a heptagon is 900 degrees.","720 degrees","1080 degrees","1260 degrees","The sum of the interior angles of a heptagon is 900 degrees.","The sum of interior angles is (n - 2) x 180. For a heptagon, (7 - 2) x 180 = 900 degrees.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-type-of-symmetry-022ba7e4","Math G9: Distinguishing the interior angles of polygons - Set 1 T1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-1-1 Distinguishing the interior angles of polygons, and the relationships between angles formed by a transversal intersecting parallel lines, and using them to find unknown angle measures, and determining polygons that can form tessellations.","1. Distinguishing shapes that are symmetric about an axis, identifying axes of symmetry and shapes that have rotational symmetry about a point, and determining angles of rotation.","What type of symmetry does a rectangle have?","Only rotational symmetry","Only line symmetry","Both line and rotational symmetry","Neither line nor rotational symmetry","Both line and rotational symmetry","A rectangle has two lines of symmetry, one through the midpoints of the longer sides and one through the midpoints of the shorter sides, and it also has rotational symmetry of 180 degrees.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-type-of-symmetry-022ba7e4.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-if-two-parallel-5d467b6a","Math G9: Distinguishing the interior angles of polygons - Set 1 T1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-1-1 Distinguishing the interior angles of polygons, and the relationships between angles formed by a transversal intersecting parallel lines, and using them to find unknown angle measures, and determining polygons that can form tessellations.","2. Distinguishing pairs of angles formed when a transversal intersects two parallel lines (alternate interior and exterior angles, corresponding angles), identifying the relationships between them, and using them to find unknown angle measures.","If two parallel lines are intersected by a transversal, what is the relationship between the corresponding angles?","They are supplementary","They are complementary","They are equal","They are unequal","They are equal","When two parallel lines are intersected by a transversal, the corresponding angles are equal because they are formed by the same two lines.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-if-two-parallel-5d467b6a.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-measure-of-b652d23d","Math G9: Distinguishing the interior angles of polygons - Set 1 T1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-1-1 Distinguishing the interior angles of polygons, and the relationships between angles formed by a transversal intersecting parallel lines, and using them to find unknown angle measures, and determining polygons that can form tessellations.","1. Distinguishing the sum of the interior angle measures of a polygon and using its to find the measure of an angle in a regular polygon or unknown angle measures, and determining polygons that can form tessellations.","What is the measure of each interior angle of a regular pentagon?","90 degrees","108 degrees","120 degrees","150 degrees","108 degrees","The sum of the interior angles of a pentagon is (5-2)*180 = 540 degrees. Since it is a regular pentagon, all angles are equal, so each angle is 540/5 = 108 degrees.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-measure-of-b652d23d.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-of-polygons-can-bcbe41ba","Math G9: Distinguishing the interior angles of polygons - Set 1 T1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-1-1 Distinguishing the interior angles of polygons, and the relationships between angles formed by a transversal intersecting parallel lines, and using them to find unknown angle measures, and determining polygons that can form tessellations.","1. Distinguishing shapes that are symmetric about an axis, identifying axes of symmetry and shapes that have rotational symmetry about a point, and determining angles of rotation.","Which of the following polygons can form a tessellation?","Triangle, square, and hexagon","Only triangle and square","Only square and hexagon","Only triangle and hexagon","Triangle, square, and hexagon","A tessellation is a pattern of shapes that fit together without overlapping. Triangles, squares, and hexagons can all be used to form tessellations because they can fit together without gaps or overlaps.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-measure-of-d786b3e3","Math G9: Distinguishing the interior angles of polygons - Set 1 T1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-1-1 Distinguishing the interior angles of polygons, and the relationships between angles formed by a transversal intersecting parallel lines, and using them to find unknown angle measures, and determining polygons that can form tessellations.","2. Distinguishing pairs of angles formed when a transversal intersects two parallel lines (alternate interior and exterior angles, corresponding angles), identifying the relationships between them, and using them to find unknown angle measures.","What is the measure of the angle formed by a transversal intersecting two parallel lines if the corresponding angle is 60 degrees?","30 degrees","60 degrees","90 degrees","120 degrees","60 degrees","The angle formed by the transversal is equal to the corresponding angle because the lines are parallel.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-measure-of-d786b3e3.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-if-a-shape-f6e5316f","Math G9: Distinguishing the interior angles of polygons - Set 1 T1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-1-1 Distinguishing the interior angles of polygons, and the relationships between angles formed by a transversal intersecting parallel lines, and using them to find unknown angle measures, and determining polygons that can form tessellations.","1. Distinguishing shapes that are symmetric about an axis, identifying axes of symmetry and shapes that have rotational symmetry about a point, and determining angles of rotation.","If a shape has rotational symmetry of 90 degrees, how many times can it be rotated to return to its original position?","2 times","3 times","4 times","5 times","4 times","A shape with rotational symmetry of 90 degrees can be rotated 4 times to return to its original position because 360/90 = 4.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-type-of-symmetry-b75cfd7e","Math G9: Distinguishing the interior angles of polygons - Set 1 T1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-1-1 Distinguishing the interior angles of polygons, and the relationships between angles formed by a transversal intersecting parallel lines, and using them to find unknown angle measures, and determining polygons that can form tessellations.","1. Distinguishing shapes that are symmetric about an axis, identifying axes of symmetry and shapes that have rotational symmetry about a point, and determining angles of rotation.","What type of symmetry does a circle have?","Only line symmetry","Only rotational symmetry","Both line and rotational symmetry","Neither line nor rotational symmetry","Both line and rotational symmetry","A circle has an infinite number of lines of symmetry and an infinite number of rotational symmetries because it looks the same from any angle.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-type-of-symmetry-b75cfd7e.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-if-two-angles-60298d08","Math G9: Distinguishing the interior angles of polygons - Set 1 T1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-1-1 Distinguishing the interior angles of polygons, and the relationships between angles formed by a transversal intersecting parallel lines, and using them to find unknown angle measures, and determining polygons that can form tessellations.","1. Distinguishing the sum of the interior angle measures of a polygon and using its to find the measure of an angle in a regular polygon or unknown angle measures, and determining polygons that can form tessellations.","If two angles are supplementary, what is their sum?","90 degrees","180 degrees","270 degrees","360 degrees","180 degrees","Supplementary angles are two angles whose sum is 180 degrees.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-if-two-angles-60298d08.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-measure-of-771f7424","Math G9: Distinguishing the interior angles of polygons - Set 1 T1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-1-1 Distinguishing the interior angles of polygons, and the relationships between angles formed by a transversal intersecting parallel lines, and using them to find unknown angle measures, and determining polygons that can form tessellations.","1. Distinguishing the sum of the interior angle measures of a polygon and using its to find the measure of an angle in a regular polygon or unknown angle measures, and determining polygons that can form tessellations.","What is the measure of each interior angle of a regular octagon?","90 degrees","108 degrees","135 degrees","150 degrees","135 degrees","The sum of the interior angles of an octagon is (8-2)*180 = 1080 degrees. Since it is a regular octagon, all angles are equal, so each angle is 1080/8 = 135 degrees.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-measure-of-771f7424.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-sum-of-663bd009","Math G9: Distinguishing the interior angles of polygons - Set 1 T1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-1-1 Distinguishing the interior angles of polygons, and the relationships between angles formed by a transversal intersecting parallel lines, and using them to find unknown angle measures, and determining polygons that can form tessellations.","1. Distinguishing the sum of the interior angle measures of a polygon and using its to find the measure of an angle in a regular polygon or unknown angle measures, and determining polygons that can form tessellations.","What is the sum of the interior angles of a hexagon?","The sum of the interior angles of a hexagon is 720 degrees.","540 degrees","900 degrees","1080 degrees","The sum of the interior angles of a hexagon is 720 degrees.","The sum of interior angles is (n - 2) x 180. For a hexagon, (6 - 2) x 180 = 720 degrees.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-relationship-cups-1f6bc851","Math G9: Distinguishing the relationships between the units - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-3-1 Distinguishing the relationships between the units of length, mass, and capacity, and using them to convert within these units and between English and metric measurement units.","3. Distinguishing the relationship between the two English units of capacity (cups and gallons) and using them to convert between units.","What is the relationship between cups and gallons?","Cups are a larger unit of capacity than gallons","Cups are a smaller unit of capacity than gallons","Cups and gallons are equal units of capacity","Cups and gallons are unrelated units of capacity","Cups are a smaller unit of capacity than gallons","Since 1 gallon is equal to 16 cups, cups are a smaller unit of capacity than gallons.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-relationship-cups-1f6bc851.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-relationship-1-02a778b2","Math G9: Distinguishing the relationships between the units - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-3-1 Distinguishing the relationships between the units of length, mass, and capacity, and using them to convert within these units and between English and metric measurement units.","3. Distinguishing the relationship between the two English units of capacity (cups and gallons) and using them to convert between units.","What is the relationship between 1 cup and 1 gallon in terms of capacity?","1 cup is a larger unit of capacity than 1 gallon","1 cup is a smaller unit of capacity than 1 gallon","1 cup is equal to 1 gallon in terms of capacity","1 cup is unrelated to 1 gallon in terms of capacity","1 cup is a smaller unit of capacity than 1 gallon","Since 1 gallon is equal to 16 cups, 1 cup is a smaller unit of capacity than 1 gallon.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-relationship-1-02a778b2.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-how-many-cups-0021fdfd","Math G9: Distinguishing the relationships between the units - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-3-1 Distinguishing the relationships between the units of length, mass, and capacity, and using them to convert within these units and between English and metric measurement units.","3. Distinguishing the relationship between the two English units of capacity (cups and gallons) and using them to convert between units.","How many cups are in 2 gallons?","There are 32 cups in 2 gallons","16 cups","24 cups","48 cups","There are 32 cups in 2 gallons","There are 16 cups in 1 gallon, so 2 gallons equals 32 cups.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-how-many-cups-0021fdfd.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-conversion-factor-fca4f15e","Math G9: Distinguishing the relationships between the units - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-3-1 Distinguishing the relationships between the units of length, mass, and capacity, and using them to convert within these units and between English and metric measurement units.","3. Distinguishing the relationship between the two English units of capacity (cups and gallons) and using them to convert between units.","What is the conversion factor between 1 cup and 1 gallon?","1 cup is equal to 1 gallon","1 cup is equal to 0.0625 gallons","1 cup is equal to 16 gallons","1 cup is equal to 1/16 gallon","1 cup is equal to 1/16 gallon","Since 1 gallon is equal to 16 cups, the conversion factor is 1 cup is equal to 1/16 gallon.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-conversion-factor-fca4f15e.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-how-many-gallons-7768d77a","Math G9: Distinguishing the relationships between the units - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-3-1 Distinguishing the relationships between the units of length, mass, and capacity, and using them to convert within these units and between English and metric measurement units.","3. Distinguishing the relationship between the two English units of capacity (cups and gallons) and using them to convert between units.","How many gallons are in 96 cups?","There are 6 gallons in 96 cups","There are 4 gallons in 96 cups","There are 3 gallons in 96 cups","There are 2 gallons in 96 cups","There are 6 gallons in 96 cups","To convert cups to gallons, we divide the number of cups by 16. 96 ÷ 16 = 6.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-how-many-gallons-7768d77a.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-relationship-1-1c746167","Math G9: Distinguishing the relationships between the units - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-3-1 Distinguishing the relationships between the units of length, mass, and capacity, and using them to convert within these units and between English and metric measurement units.","3. Distinguishing the relationship between the two English units of capacity (cups and gallons) and using them to convert between units.","What is the relationship between 1 cup and 1 gallon?","1 cup is equal to 1 gallon","1 cup is equal to 1/16 gallon","1 cup is equal to 16 gallons","1 cup is equal to 32 gallons","1 cup is equal to 1/16 gallon","Since 1 gallon is equal to 16 cups, 1 cup is equal to 1/16 gallon.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-relationship-1-1c746167.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-how-many-cups-dce8f28c","Math G9: Distinguishing the relationships between the units - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-3-1 Distinguishing the relationships between the units of length, mass, and capacity, and using them to convert within these units and between English and metric measurement units.","3. Distinguishing the relationship between the two English units of capacity (cups and gallons) and using them to convert between units.","How many cups are in 3 gallons?","There are 48 cups in 3 gallons","There are 64 cups in 3 gallons","There are 96 cups in 3 gallons","There are 128 cups in 3 gallons","There are 48 cups in 3 gallons","To convert gallons to cups, we multiply the number of gallons by 16. 3 × 16 = 48.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-how-many-cups-dce8f28c.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-conversion-factor-4344f3ce","Math G9: Distinguishing the relationships between the units - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-3-1 Distinguishing the relationships between the units of length, mass, and capacity, and using them to convert within these units and between English and metric measurement units.","3. Distinguishing the relationship between the two English units of capacity (cups and gallons) and using them to convert between units.","What is the conversion factor between cups and gallons?","1 cup is equal to 1 gallon","1 cup is equal to 0.0625 gallons","1 cup is equal to 16 gallons","1 cup is equal to 1/16 gallon","1 cup is equal to 1/16 gallon","Since 1 gallon is equal to 16 cups, the conversion factor is 1 cup is equal to 1/16 gallon.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-conversion-factor-4344f3ce.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-how-many-gallons-4fe8f1f8","Math G9: Distinguishing the relationships between the units - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-3-1 Distinguishing the relationships between the units of length, mass, and capacity, and using them to convert within these units and between English and metric measurement units.","3. Distinguishing the relationship between the two English units of capacity (cups and gallons) and using them to convert between units.","How many gallons are in 48 cups?","There are 2 gallons in 48 cups","There are 3 gallons in 48 cups","There are 4 gallons in 48 cups","There are 6 gallons in 48 cups","There are 3 gallons in 48 cups","To convert cups to gallons, we divide the number of cups by 16. 48 ÷ 16 = 3.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-how-many-gallons-4fe8f1f8.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-how-many-cups-012d355a","Math G9: Distinguishing the relationships between the units - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-3-1 Distinguishing the relationships between the units of length, mass, and capacity, and using them to convert within these units and between English and metric measurement units.","3. Distinguishing the relationship between the two English units of capacity (cups and gallons) and using them to convert between units.","How many cups are in 1 gallon?","There are 16 cups in 1 gallon","There are 32 cups in 1 gallon","There are 64 cups in 1 gallon","There are 128 cups in 1 gallon","There are 16 cups in 1 gallon","Since 1 gallon is equal to 16 cups, the correct answer is 16 cups.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-how-many-cups-012d355a.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-factor-algebraic-expression-62008e63","Math G9: Factoring algebraic terms - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-2 Factoring algebraic terms, algebraic expression, the quadratic algebraic expression into their factors, and writing them in simplest form.","1. Finding the greatest common factor of algebraic terms and factoring an algebraic term completely.","Factor the algebraic expression x^2 + 8x + 15","(x + 1)(x + 15)","(x + 3)(x + 5)","(x + 5)(x + 3)","(x + 15)(x + 1)","(x + 5)(x + 3)","The correct factorization is (x + 5)(x + 3) because x^2 + 8x + 15 can be divided into (x + 5) and (x + 3).","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-factor-algebraic-expression-8b50d7a1","Math G9: Factoring algebraic terms - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-2 Factoring algebraic terms, algebraic expression, the quadratic algebraic expression into their factors, and writing them in simplest form.","1. Finding the greatest common factor of algebraic terms and factoring an algebraic term completely.","Factor the algebraic expression x^2 - 9","(x - 3)(x + 3)","(x + 3)(x - 3)","(x - 9)(x + 1)","(x + 1)(x - 9)","(x - 3)(x + 3)","The correct factorization is (x - 3)(x + 3) because x^2 - 9 can be divided into (x - 3) and (x + 3).","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-factor-algebraic-expression-341bb6d4","Math G9: Factoring algebraic terms - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-1 Writing algebraic expressions with rational coefficients, finding their values, performing arithmetic operations on them, and Distinguishing basic identities.","3. Distinguishing the basic identities: the square of a sum of two terms, the square of a difference of two terms, and the product of the sum and difference of two terms, and using them.","Factor completely: x² + x - 6.","(x + 3)(x - 2)","(x - 3)(x + 2)","(x + 6)(x - 1)","(x - 6)(x + 1)","(x + 3)(x - 2)","Two numbers with product -6 and sum 1 are 3 and -2, so x² + x - 6 = (x + 3)(x - 2).","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-factor-algebraic-expression-ac186594","Math G9: Factoring algebraic terms - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-2 Factoring algebraic terms, algebraic expression, the quadratic algebraic expression into their factors, and writing them in simplest form.","1. Finding the greatest common factor of algebraic terms and factoring an algebraic term completely.","Factor the algebraic expression 2x^2 + 7x + 3","(2x + 1)(x + 3)","(2x + 3)(x + 1)","(x + 1)(2x + 3)","(2x - 1)(x - 3)","(2x + 1)(x + 3)","Expanding (2x + 1)(x + 3) gives 2x² + 7x + 3.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-factor-algebraic-expression-96a0050b","Math G9: Factoring algebraic terms - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-2 Factoring algebraic terms, algebraic expression, the quadratic algebraic expression into their factors, and writing them in simplest form.","1. Finding the greatest common factor of algebraic terms and factoring an algebraic term completely.","Factor the algebraic expression x^2 - 4x - 5","(x - 5)(x + 1)","(x + 5)(x - 1)","(x - 5)(x - 1)","(x + 5)(x + 1)","(x - 5)(x + 1)","The numbers -5 and 1 multiply to -5 and add to -4, so x² - 4x - 5 = (x - 5)(x + 1).","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-factor-algebraic-expression-2c2adfaa","Math G9: Factoring algebraic terms - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-2 Factoring algebraic terms, algebraic expression, the quadratic algebraic expression into their factors, and writing them in simplest form.","1. Finding the greatest common factor of algebraic terms and factoring an algebraic term completely.","Factor the algebraic expression x^2 - 7x - 18","(x - 9)(x + 2)","(x - 6)(x + 3)","(x - 3)(x + 6)","(x - 18)(x + 1)","(x - 9)(x + 2)","The numbers -9 and 2 multiply to -18 and add to -7, so x² - 7x - 18 = (x - 9)(x + 2).","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-factor-algebraic-expression-15745c4c","Math G9: Factoring algebraic terms - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-2 Factoring algebraic terms, algebraic expression, the quadratic algebraic expression into their factors, and writing them in simplest form.","1. Finding the greatest common factor of algebraic terms and factoring an algebraic term completely.","Factor the algebraic expression x^2 + 5x + 6","(x + 1)(x + 6)","(x + 2)(x + 3)","(x + 3)(x + 2)","(x + 6)(x + 1)","(x + 3)(x + 2)","The correct factorization is (x + 3)(x + 2) because x^2 + 5x + 6 can be divided into (x + 3) and (x + 2).","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-factor-algebraic-expression-5fd11399","Math G9: Factoring algebraic terms - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-2 Factoring algebraic terms, algebraic expression, the quadratic algebraic expression into their factors, and writing them in simplest form.","1. Finding the greatest common factor of algebraic terms and factoring an algebraic term completely.","Factor the algebraic expression 6x + 12","6(x + 2)","3(2x + 2)","2(3x + 12)","6(x + 12)","6(x + 2)","The greatest common factor of 6x and 12 is 6, so 6x + 12 = 6(x + 2).","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-factor-algebraic-expression-8760425c","Math G9: Factoring algebraic terms - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-2 Factoring algebraic terms, algebraic expression, the quadratic algebraic expression into their factors, and writing them in simplest form.","1. Finding the greatest common factor of algebraic terms and factoring an algebraic term completely.","Factor the algebraic expression x^2 + 9x + 20","(x + 1)(x + 20)","(x + 4)(x + 5)","(x + 5)(x + 4)","(x + 20)(x + 1)","(x + 5)(x + 4)","The correct factorization is (x + 5)(x + 4) because x^2 + 9x + 20 can be divided into (x + 5) and (x + 4).","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-factor-algebraic-expression-fed07c29","Math G9: Factoring algebraic terms - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-2 Factoring algebraic terms, algebraic expression, the quadratic algebraic expression into their factors, and writing them in simplest form.","1. Finding the greatest common factor of algebraic terms and factoring an algebraic term completely.","Factor the algebraic expression x^2 - 2x - 15","(x - 5)(x + 3)","(x + 5)(x - 3)","(x - 5)(x - 3)","(x + 5)(x + 3)","(x - 5)(x + 3)","The numbers -5 and 3 multiply to -15 and add to -2, so x² - 2x - 15 = (x - 5)(x + 3).","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-factored-form-05cea839","Math G9: Factoring algebraic terms - Set 3","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-2 Factoring algebraic terms, algebraic expression, the quadratic algebraic expression into their factors, and writing them in simplest form.","1. Finding the greatest common factor of algebraic terms and factoring an algebraic term completely.","What is the factored form of the quadratic expression x^2 + 9x + 20?","(x + 1)(x + 20)","(x + 4)(x + 5)","(x + 5)(x + 4)","(x - 1)(x - 20)","(x + 4)(x + 5)","The factored form of x^2 + 9x + 20 is (x + 4)(x + 5) because (x + 4)(x + 5) = x^2 + 9x + 20.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-factored-form-5edcafa0","Math G9: Factoring algebraic terms - Set 3","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-2 Factoring algebraic terms, algebraic expression, the quadratic algebraic expression into their factors, and writing them in simplest form.","1. Finding the greatest common factor of algebraic terms and factoring an algebraic term completely.","What is the factored form of the quadratic expression x^2 - 4x - 5?","(x + 1)(x - 5)","(x - 1)(x + 5)","(x - 5)(x + 1)","(x + 5)(x - 1)","(x + 1)(x - 5)","The factored form of x^2 - 4x - 5 is (x - 5)(x + 1) because (x - 5)(x + 1) = x^2 - 4x - 5.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-factored-form-2117af03","Math G9: Factoring algebraic terms - Set 3","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-2 Factoring algebraic terms, algebraic expression, the quadratic algebraic expression into their factors, and writing them in simplest form.","1. Finding the greatest common factor of algebraic terms and factoring an algebraic term completely.","What is the factored form of the quadratic expression x^2 - 2x - 15?","(x + 3)(x - 5)","(x - 3)(x + 5)","(x - 5)(x + 3)","(x + 5)(x - 3)","(x + 3)(x - 5)","The factored form of x^2 - 2x - 15 is (x - 5)(x + 3) because (x - 5)(x + 3) = x^2 - 2x - 15.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-factored-form-94ccb13c","Math G9: Factoring algebraic terms - Set 3","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-2 Factoring algebraic terms, algebraic expression, the quadratic algebraic expression into their factors, and writing them in simplest form.","1. Finding the greatest common factor of algebraic terms and factoring an algebraic term completely.","What is the factored form of the quadratic expression x^2 - 3x - 4?","(x + 1)(x - 4)","(x - 1)(x + 4)","(x - 4)(x + 1)","(x + 4)(x - 1)","(x + 1)(x - 4)","The factored form of x^2 - 3x - 4 is (x - 4)(x + 1) because (x - 4)(x + 1) = x^2 - 3x - 4.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-factored-form-30afdd82","Math G9: Factoring algebraic terms - Set 3","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-2 Factoring algebraic terms, algebraic expression, the quadratic algebraic expression into their factors, and writing them in simplest form.","1. Finding the greatest common factor of algebraic terms and factoring an algebraic term completely.","What is the factored form of the quadratic expression x^2 + 8x + 15?","(x + 1)(x + 15)","(x + 3)(x + 5)","(x + 5)(x + 3)","(x - 1)(x - 15)","(x + 3)(x + 5)","The factored form of x^2 + 8x + 15 is (x + 3)(x + 5) because (x + 3)(x + 5) = x^2 + 8x + 15.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-factored-form-12aeab27","Math G9: Factoring algebraic terms - Set 3","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-2 Factoring algebraic terms, algebraic expression, the quadratic algebraic expression into their factors, and writing them in simplest form.","1. Finding the greatest common factor of algebraic terms and factoring an algebraic term completely.","What is the factored form of the quadratic expression x^2 - 9?","(x - 3)(x + 3)","(x + 3)(x - 3)","(x - 1)(x - 9)","(x + 1)(x - 9)","(x - 3)(x + 3)","The factored form of x^2 - 9 is (x - 3)(x + 3) because (x - 3)(x + 3) = x^2 - 9.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-factored-form-4d1e73d8","Math G9: Factoring algebraic terms - Set 3","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-2 Factoring algebraic terms, algebraic expression, the quadratic algebraic expression into their factors, and writing them in simplest form.","1. Finding the greatest common factor of algebraic terms and factoring an algebraic term completely.","What is the factored form of the quadratic expression x^2 + 5x + 6?","(x + 1)(x + 6)","(x + 2)(x + 3)","(x + 3)(x + 2)","(x - 1)(x - 6)","(x + 2)(x + 3)","The factored form of x^2 + 5x + 6 is (x + 2)(x + 3) because (x + 2)(x + 3) = x^2 + 5x + 6.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-factored-form-e656e5fe","Math G9: Factoring algebraic terms - Set 3","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-1 Writing algebraic expressions with rational coefficients, finding their values, performing arithmetic operations on them, and Distinguishing basic identities.","3. Distinguishing the basic identities: the square of a sum of two terms, the square of a difference of two terms, and the product of the sum and difference of two terms, and using them.","Factor completely: x² + x - 6.","(x + 3)(x - 2)","(x - 3)(x + 2)","(x + 6)(x - 1)","(x - 6)(x + 1)","(x + 3)(x - 2)","Two numbers with product -6 and sum 1 are 3 and -2, so x² + x - 6 = (x + 3)(x - 2).","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-factored-form-eaca8885","Math G9: Factoring algebraic terms - Set 3","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-2 Factoring algebraic terms, algebraic expression, the quadratic algebraic expression into their factors, and writing them in simplest form.","1. Finding the greatest common factor of algebraic terms and factoring an algebraic term completely.","What is the factored form of the quadratic expression x^2 + 6x + 8?","(x + 2)(x + 4)","(x + 4)(x + 2)","(x + 1)(x + 7)","(x - 2)(x - 4)","(x + 2)(x + 4)","The factored form of x^2 + 6x + 8 is (x + 2)(x + 4) because (x + 2)(x + 4) = x^2 + 6x + 8.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-factored-form-4ded7b5b","Math G9: Factoring algebraic terms - Set 3","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-2 Factoring algebraic terms, algebraic expression, the quadratic algebraic expression into their factors, and writing them in simplest form.","1. Finding the greatest common factor of algebraic terms and factoring an algebraic term completely.","What is the factored form of the quadratic expression x^2 - 16?","(x - 4)(x + 4)","(x + 4)(x - 4)","(x - 2)(x + 8)","(x + 2)(x - 8)","(x - 4)(x + 4)","The factored form of x^2 - 16 is (x - 4)(x + 4) because (x - 4)(x + 4) = x^2 - 16.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-simplify-expression-2-57f966ea","Math G9: Finding the powers of rational numbers - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-1-2-1 Finding the powers of rational numbers, and distinguishing  laws of exponent,  and using them to simplify numerical expressions,  Writing  and finding their values, writing numbers in scientific notation.","1. Finding the power of a rational number (with an integer exponent), distinguishing the laws of exponents, and using them to simplify numerical expressions.","Simplify the expression (2^3 * 2^2) / 2^1.","2^4","2^5","2^6","2^7","2^4","Using the laws of exponents, we can simplify the expression as (2^3 * 2^2) / 2^1 = 2^(3+2-1) = 2^4.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-simplify-expression-5-33738dba","Math G9: Finding the powers of rational numbers - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-1-2-1 Finding the powers of rational numbers, and distinguishing  laws of exponent,  and using them to simplify numerical expressions,  Writing  and finding their values, writing numbers in scientific notation.","1. Finding the power of a rational number (with an integer exponent), distinguishing the laws of exponents, and using them to simplify numerical expressions.","Simplify the expression (5^2 * 5^3) / 5^2.","5^3","5^4","5^5","5^6","5^3","Using the laws of exponents, we can simplify the expression as (5^2 * 5^3) / 5^2 = 5^(2+3-2) = 5^3.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-value-of-dc515627","Math G9: Finding the powers of rational numbers - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-1-2-1 Finding the powers of rational numbers, and distinguishing  laws of exponent,  and using them to simplify numerical expressions,  Writing  and finding their values, writing numbers in scientific notation.","1. Finding the power of a rational number (with an integer exponent), distinguishing the laws of exponents, and using them to simplify numerical expressions.","What is the value of (3^2)^3?","3^6","3^7","3^8","3^9","3^7","Using the laws of exponents, we can simplify the expression as (3^2)^3 = 3^(2*3) = 3^6.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-value-of-27822ec0","Math G9: Finding the powers of rational numbers - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-1-2-1 Finding the powers of rational numbers, and distinguishing  laws of exponent,  and using them to simplify numerical expressions,  Writing  and finding their values, writing numbers in scientific notation.","1. Finding the power of a rational number (with an integer exponent), distinguishing the laws of exponents, and using them to simplify numerical expressions.","What is the value of (3^4)^2?","3^8","3^9","3^10","3^11","3^8","Using the laws of exponents, we can simplify the expression as (3^4)^2 = 3^(4*2) = 3^8.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-simplify-expression-5-fb27a061","Math G9: Finding the powers of rational numbers - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-1-2-1 Finding the powers of rational numbers, and distinguishing  laws of exponent,  and using them to simplify numerical expressions,  Writing  and finding their values, writing numbers in scientific notation.","1. Finding the power of a rational number (with an integer exponent), distinguishing the laws of exponents, and using them to simplify numerical expressions.","Simplify the expression (5^4 * 5^2) / 5^3.","5^3","5^4","5^5","5^6","5^3","Using the laws of exponents, we can simplify the expression as (5^4 * 5^2) / 5^3 = 5^(4+2-3) = 5^3.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-value-of-7b35e72d","Math G9: Finding the powers of rational numbers - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-1-2-1 Finding the powers of rational numbers, and distinguishing  laws of exponent,  and using them to simplify numerical expressions,  Writing  and finding their values, writing numbers in scientific notation.","1. Finding the power of a rational number (with an integer exponent), distinguishing the laws of exponents, and using them to simplify numerical expressions.","What is the value of (6^2)^2?","6^4","6^5","6^6","6^7","6^4","Using the laws of exponents, we can simplify the expression as (6^2)^2 = 6^(2*2) = 6^4.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-simplify-expression-2-7a269770","Math G9: Finding the powers of rational numbers - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-1-2-1 Finding the powers of rational numbers, and distinguishing  laws of exponent,  and using them to simplify numerical expressions,  Writing  and finding their values, writing numbers in scientific notation.","1. Finding the power of a rational number (with an integer exponent), distinguishing the laws of exponents, and using them to simplify numerical expressions.","Simplify the expression (2^5 * 2^2) / 2^3.","2^4","2^5","2^6","2^7","2^4","Using the laws of exponents, we can simplify the expression as (2^5 * 2^2) / 2^3 = 2^(5+2-3) = 2^4.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-value-of-d30138ee","Math G9: Finding the powers of rational numbers - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-1-2-1 Finding the powers of rational numbers, and distinguishing  laws of exponent,  and using them to simplify numerical expressions,  Writing  and finding their values, writing numbers in scientific notation.","1. Finding the power of a rational number (with an integer exponent), distinguishing the laws of exponents, and using them to simplify numerical expressions.","What is the value of (4^2)^3?","4^6","4^7","4^8","4^9","4^6","Using the laws of exponents, we can simplify the expression as (4^2)^3 = 4^(2*3) = 4^6.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-simplify-expression-3-12679643","Math G9: Finding the powers of rational numbers - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-1-2-1 Finding the powers of rational numbers, and distinguishing  laws of exponent,  and using them to simplify numerical expressions,  Writing  and finding their values, writing numbers in scientific notation.","1. Finding the power of a rational number (with an integer exponent), distinguishing the laws of exponents, and using them to simplify numerical expressions.","Simplify the expression (3^3 * 3^2) / 3^2.","3^3","3^4","3^5","3^6","3^3","Using the laws of exponents, we can simplify the expression as (3^3 * 3^2) / 3^2 = 3^(3+2-2) = 3^3.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-value-of-ee791577","Math G9: Finding the powers of rational numbers - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-1-2-1 Finding the powers of rational numbers, and distinguishing  laws of exponent,  and using them to simplify numerical expressions,  Writing  and finding their values, writing numbers in scientific notation.","1. Finding the power of a rational number (with an integer exponent), distinguishing the laws of exponents, and using them to simplify numerical expressions.","What is the value of (2^4)^2?","2^8","2^9","2^10","2^11","2^8","Using the laws of exponents, we can simplify the expression as (2^4)^2 = 2^(4*2) = 2^8.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-simplify-expression-2-30241234","Math G9: Finding the powers of rational numbers - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-1-2-1 Finding the powers of rational numbers, and distinguishing  laws of exponent,  and using them to simplify numerical expressions,  Writing  and finding their values, writing numbers in scientific notation.","1. Finding the power of a rational number (with an integer exponent), distinguishing the laws of exponents, and using them to simplify numerical expressions.","Simplify the expression: (2^2) * (2^3) * (2^4)","2^9","2^5","2^2 * 2^3 * 2^4","2^9 + 2^4","2^9","Using the laws of exponents, when multiplying two powers with the same base, add the exponents. Therefore, (2^2) * (2^3) * (2^4) = 2^(2+3+4) = 2^9.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-write-number-4560-b78a733b","Math G9: Finding the powers of rational numbers - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-1-2-1 Finding the powers of rational numbers, and distinguishing  laws of exponent,  and using them to simplify numerical expressions,  Writing  and finding their values, writing numbers in scientific notation.","1. Finding the power of a rational number (with an integer exponent), distinguishing the laws of exponents, and using them to simplify numerical expressions.","Write the number 4560 in scientific notation.","4.56 * 10^3","4.56 * 10^4","0.456 * 10^4","0.456 * 10^3","4.56 * 10^3","To write a number in scientific notation, move the decimal point to the left until you have a number between 1 and 10, then multiply by 10 raised to the power of the number of places you moved the decimal point. In this case, 4560 becomes 4.56 * 10^3.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-value-of-3371b32a","Math G9: Finding the powers of rational numbers - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-1-2-1 Finding the powers of rational numbers, and distinguishing  laws of exponent,  and using them to simplify numerical expressions,  Writing  and finding their values, writing numbers in scientific notation.","1. Finding the power of a rational number (with an integer exponent), distinguishing the laws of exponents, and using them to simplify numerical expressions.","What is the value of 2^0?","1","2","0","4","1","Any non-zero number raised to the power of 0 is equal to 1. Therefore, 2^0 = 1.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-simplify-expression-3-73004c61","Math G9: Finding the powers of rational numbers - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-1-2-1 Finding the powers of rational numbers, and distinguishing  laws of exponent,  and using them to simplify numerical expressions,  Writing  and finding their values, writing numbers in scientific notation.","1. Finding the power of a rational number (with an integer exponent), distinguishing the laws of exponents, and using them to simplify numerical expressions.","Simplify the expression: (3^2) / (3^2)","3^0","3^1","3^2","3^3","3^0","Using the laws of exponents, when dividing two powers with the same base, subtract the exponents. Therefore, (3^2) / (3^2) = 3^(2-2) = 3^0.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-simplify-expression-4-22d5dda8","Math G9: Finding the powers of rational numbers - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-1-2-1 Finding the powers of rational numbers, and distinguishing  laws of exponent,  and using them to simplify numerical expressions,  Writing  and finding their values, writing numbers in scientific notation.","1. Finding the power of a rational number (with an integer exponent), distinguishing the laws of exponents, and using them to simplify numerical expressions.","Simplify the expression: (4^2) * (4^3)","4^5","4^2 * 4^3","4^5 + 4^2","4^2 - 4^3","4^5","Using the laws of exponents, when multiplying two powers with the same base, add the exponents. Therefore, (4^2) * (4^3) = 4^(2+3) = 4^5.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-value-of-b20560b1","Math G9: Finding the powers of rational numbers - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-1-1-1 Understanding integers and rational numbers, reading, writing, representing, comparing, and ordering them.","3. Finding the absolute value of an integer  number and representing it on the number line.","What is the value of 5^1?","5","1","0","10","5","To find the value of 5^1, multiply 5 by itself 1 time: 5 * 1 = 5.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-simplify-expression-2-1328b903","Math G9: Finding the powers of rational numbers - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-1-2-1 Finding the powers of rational numbers, and distinguishing  laws of exponent,  and using them to simplify numerical expressions,  Writing  and finding their values, writing numbers in scientific notation.","1. Finding the power of a rational number (with an integer exponent), distinguishing the laws of exponents, and using them to simplify numerical expressions.","Simplify the expression: (2^3) * (2^2)","2^5","4 * 2^2","2^5 * 2^2","2^5 + 2^2","2^5","Using the laws of exponents, when multiplying two powers with the same base, add the exponents. Therefore, (2^3) * (2^2) = 2^(3+2) = 2^5.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-value-of-44788f12","Math G9: Finding the powers of rational numbers - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-1-1-1 Understanding integers and rational numbers, reading, writing, representing, comparing, and ordering them.","3. Finding the absolute value of an integer  number and representing it on the number line.","What is the value of 3^4?","81","27","9","16","81","To find the value of 3^4, multiply 3 by itself 4 times: 3 * 3 * 3 * 3 = 81.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-simplify-expression-5-165f04da","Math G9: Finding the powers of rational numbers - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-1-2-1 Finding the powers of rational numbers, and distinguishing  laws of exponent,  and using them to simplify numerical expressions,  Writing  and finding their values, writing numbers in scientific notation.","1. Finding the power of a rational number (with an integer exponent), distinguishing the laws of exponents, and using them to simplify numerical expressions.","Simplify the expression: (5^2) / (5^1)","5","5^1","5^2","5^0","5^1","Using the laws of exponents, when dividing two powers with the same base, subtract the exponents. Therefore, (5^2) / (5^1) = 5^(2-1) = 5^1.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-write-number-0-c0a6433b","Math G9: Finding the powers of rational numbers - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-1-2-1 Finding the powers of rational numbers, and distinguishing  laws of exponent,  and using them to simplify numerical expressions,  Writing  and finding their values, writing numbers in scientific notation.","1. Finding the power of a rational number (with an integer exponent), distinguishing the laws of exponents, and using them to simplify numerical expressions.","Write the number 0.000456 in scientific notation.","4.56 * 10^-4","4.56 * 10^4","0.456 * 10^-3","0.456 * 10^3","4.56 * 10^-4","To write a number in scientific notation, move the decimal point to the right until you have a number between 1 and 10, then multiply by 10 raised to the power of the number of places you moved the decimal point. In this case, 0.000456 becomes 4.56 * 10^-4.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-write-value-of-d6428cf8","Math G9: Finding the powers of rational numbers - Set 2 T1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-1-2-1 Finding the powers of rational numbers, and distinguishing  laws of exponent,  and using them to simplify numerical expressions,  Writing  and finding their values, writing numbers in scientific notation.","1. Finding the power of a rational number (with an integer exponent), distinguishing the laws of exponents, and using them to simplify numerical expressions.","Write the value of (5^3)^2","5^6","125^2","15625","390625","5^6","Using the laws of exponent, when raising a power to another power, we multiply the exponents. So (5^3)^2 = 5^(3*2) = 5^6.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-simplify-expression-2-b4fcd183","Math G9: Finding the powers of rational numbers - Set 2 T1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-1-2-1 Finding the powers of rational numbers, and distinguishing  laws of exponent,  and using them to simplify numerical expressions,  Writing  and finding their values, writing numbers in scientific notation.","1. Finding the power of a rational number (with an integer exponent), distinguishing the laws of exponents, and using them to simplify numerical expressions.","Simplify the expression: 2^4 * 2^2","2^6","4 * 2^2","2^6 * 2^2","2^8","2^6","Using the laws of exponent, when multiplying two powers with the same base, we add the exponents. So 2^4 * 2^2 = 2^(4+2) = 2^6.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-write-value-of-7df3453a","Math G9: Finding the powers of rational numbers - Set 2 T1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-1-2-1 Finding the powers of rational numbers, and distinguishing  laws of exponent,  and using them to simplify numerical expressions,  Writing  and finding their values, writing numbers in scientific notation.","1. Finding the power of a rational number (with an integer exponent), distinguishing the laws of exponents, and using them to simplify numerical expressions.","Write the value of (2^2)^4","2^8","4^4","16^2","256","2^8","Using the laws of exponent, when raising a power to another power, we multiply the exponents. So (2^2)^4 = 2^(2*4) = 2^8.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-simplify-expression-3-fb12ac2e","Math G9: Finding the powers of rational numbers - Set 2 T1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-1-2-1 Finding the powers of rational numbers, and distinguishing  laws of exponent,  and using them to simplify numerical expressions,  Writing  and finding their values, writing numbers in scientific notation.","2. Writing numerical expressions with rational numbers, including powers with integer exponents and parentheses, and finding their values by  using the order of operations.","Simplify the expression: 3^2 + 3^2","3^4","6 * 3^2","9 + 9","18","18","Using the order of operations, we first evaluate the expressions inside the parentheses. So 3^2 + 3^2 = 9 + 9 = 18.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-write-value-of-221c0a88","Math G9: Finding the powers of rational numbers - Set 2 T1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-1-2-1 Finding the powers of rational numbers, and distinguishing  laws of exponent,  and using them to simplify numerical expressions,  Writing  and finding their values, writing numbers in scientific notation.","1. Finding the power of a rational number (with an integer exponent), distinguishing the laws of exponents, and using them to simplify numerical expressions.","Write the value of 4^2 * 4^3","4^5","16 * 64","256","1024","4^5","Using the laws of exponent, when multiplying two powers with the same base, we add the exponents. So 4^2 * 4^3 = 4^(2+3) = 4^5.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-simplify-expression-2-ec77c380","Math G9: Finding the powers of rational numbers - Set 2 T1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-1-2-1 Finding the powers of rational numbers, and distinguishing  laws of exponent,  and using them to simplify numerical expressions,  Writing  and finding their values, writing numbers in scientific notation.","1. Finding the power of a rational number (with an integer exponent), distinguishing the laws of exponents, and using them to simplify numerical expressions.","Simplify the expression: 2^3 * 2^2","2^5","4 * 2^2","2^5 * 2^2","2^7","2^5","Using the laws of exponent, when multiplying two powers with the same base, we add the exponents. So 2^3 * 2^2 = 2^(3+2) = 2^5.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-write-value-of-6865158d","Math G9: Finding the powers of rational numbers - Set 2 T1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-1-2-1 Finding the powers of rational numbers, and distinguishing  laws of exponent,  and using them to simplify numerical expressions,  Writing  and finding their values, writing numbers in scientific notation.","2. Writing numerical expressions with rational numbers, including powers with integer exponents and parentheses, and finding their values by  using the order of operations.","Write the value of (3^2)^3","3^5","9^3","27^2","81","3^5","Using the order of operations, we first evaluate the expression inside the parentheses. So (3^2)^3 = (9)^3 = 729.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-simplify-expression-2-97ab28f2","Math G9: Finding the powers of rational numbers - Set 2 T1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-1-2-1 Finding the powers of rational numbers, and distinguishing  laws of exponent,  and using them to simplify numerical expressions,  Writing  and finding their values, writing numbers in scientific notation.","1. Finding the power of a rational number (with an integer exponent), distinguishing the laws of exponents, and using them to simplify numerical expressions.","Simplify the expression: (2^3)^2","2^6","4^2","16","64","2^6","Using the laws of exponent, when raising a power to another power, we multiply the exponents. So (2^3)^2 = 2^(3*2) = 2^6.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-simplify-expression-3-a4079ccc","Math G9: Finding the powers of rational numbers - Set 2 T1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-1-2-1 Finding the powers of rational numbers, and distinguishing  laws of exponent,  and using them to simplify numerical expressions,  Writing  and finding their values, writing numbers in scientific notation.","2. Writing numerical expressions with rational numbers, including powers with integer exponents and parentheses, and finding their values by  using the order of operations.","Simplify the expression: 3^3 + 3^3","3^6","6 * 3^3","9 + 9","27 + 27","27 + 27","Using the order of operations, we first evaluate the expressions inside the parentheses. So 3^3 + 3^3 = 27 + 27 = 54.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-write-value-of-bd0ba7ec","Math G9: Finding the powers of rational numbers - Set 2 T1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-1-2-1 Finding the powers of rational numbers, and distinguishing  laws of exponent,  and using them to simplify numerical expressions,  Writing  and finding their values, writing numbers in scientific notation.","1. Finding the power of a rational number (with an integer exponent), distinguishing the laws of exponents, and using them to simplify numerical expressions.","Write the value of (4^2)^3","4^6","16^3","4096","262144","4^6","Using the laws of exponent, when raising a power to another power, we multiply the exponents. So (4^2)^3 = 4^(2*3) = 4^6.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-simplify-expression-2-b2f98fa2","Math G9: Finding the powers of rational numbers - Set 3","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-1-2-1 Finding the powers of rational numbers, and distinguishing  laws of exponent,  and using them to simplify numerical expressions,  Writing  and finding their values, writing numbers in scientific notation.","1. Finding the power of a rational number (with an integer exponent), distinguishing the laws of exponents, and using them to simplify numerical expressions.","Simplify the expression: (2^3)^2.","2^5","2^6","2^7","2^8","2^6","Using the power-of-a-power rule, (2³)² = 2^(3×2) = 2⁶.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-write-number-4-561fd79d","Math G9: Finding the powers of rational numbers - Set 3","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-1-2-1 Finding the powers of rational numbers, and distinguishing  laws of exponent,  and using them to simplify numerical expressions,  Writing  and finding their values, writing numbers in scientific notation.","3. Writing very large or very small numbers by using scientific notation and converting between scientific notation and standard form.","Write the number 4,000,000 in scientific notation.","4 x 10^6","4 x 10^5","4 x 10^4","4 x 10^3","4 x 10^6","Scientific notation is a way of expressing very large or very small numbers in a more compact form. In this case, 4,000,000 can be written as 4 x 10^6.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-write-number-0-e092b24d","Math G9: Finding the powers of rational numbers - Set 3","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-1-2-1 Finding the powers of rational numbers, and distinguishing  laws of exponent,  and using them to simplify numerical expressions,  Writing  and finding their values, writing numbers in scientific notation.","3. Writing very large or very small numbers by using scientific notation and converting between scientific notation and standard form.","Write the number 0.0004 in scientific notation.","4 x 10^-4","4 x 10^-5","4 x 10^-6","4 x 10^-7","4 x 10^-4","Scientific notation is a way of expressing very large or very small numbers in a more compact form. In this case, 0.0004 can be written as 4 x 10^-4.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-simplify-expression-4-a472f533","Math G9: Finding the powers of rational numbers - Set 3","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-1-2-1 Finding the powers of rational numbers, and distinguishing  laws of exponent,  and using them to simplify numerical expressions,  Writing  and finding their values, writing numbers in scientific notation.","1. Finding the power of a rational number (with an integer exponent), distinguishing the laws of exponents, and using them to simplify numerical expressions.","Simplify the expression: (4^2)^5.","4^10","4^11","4^12","4^13","4^10","Using the power of a power rule, we can simplify (4^2)^5 to 4^(2*5) = 4^10.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-write-number-9-9306893f","Math G9: Finding the powers of rational numbers - Set 3","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-1-2-1 Finding the powers of rational numbers, and distinguishing  laws of exponent,  and using them to simplify numerical expressions,  Writing  and finding their values, writing numbers in scientific notation.","3. Writing very large or very small numbers by using scientific notation and converting between scientific notation and standard form.","Write the number 9,000,000 in scientific notation.","9 x 10^6","9 x 10^5","9 x 10^4","9 x 10^3","9 x 10^6","Scientific notation is a way of expressing very large or very small numbers in a more compact form. In this case, 9,000,000 can be written as 9 x 10^6.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-simplify-expression-5-230f9ad2","Math G9: Finding the powers of rational numbers - Set 3","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-1-2-1 Finding the powers of rational numbers, and distinguishing  laws of exponent,  and using them to simplify numerical expressions,  Writing  and finding their values, writing numbers in scientific notation.","1. Finding the power of a rational number (with an integer exponent), distinguishing the laws of exponents, and using them to simplify numerical expressions.","Simplify the expression: (5^3)^2.","5^6","5^7","5^8","5^9","5^6","Using the power of a power rule, we can simplify (5^3)^2 to 5^(3*2) = 5^6.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-write-number-0-5c281fac","Math G9: Finding the powers of rational numbers - Set 3","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-1-2-1 Finding the powers of rational numbers, and distinguishing  laws of exponent,  and using them to simplify numerical expressions,  Writing  and finding their values, writing numbers in scientific notation.","3. Writing very large or very small numbers by using scientific notation and converting between scientific notation and standard form.","Write the number 0.0000004 in scientific notation.","4 x 10^-7","4 x 10^-8","4 x 10^-9","4 x 10^-10","4 x 10^-7","Scientific notation is a way of expressing very large or very small numbers in a more compact form. In this case, 0.0000004 can be written as 4 x 10^-7.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-simplify-expression-2-8d920941","Math G9: Finding the powers of rational numbers - Set 3","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-1-2-1 Finding the powers of rational numbers, and distinguishing  laws of exponent,  and using them to simplify numerical expressions,  Writing  and finding their values, writing numbers in scientific notation.","1. Finding the power of a rational number (with an integer exponent), distinguishing the laws of exponents, and using them to simplify numerical expressions.","Simplify the expression: (2^4)^3.","2^12","2^13","2^14","2^15","2^12","Using the power of a power rule, we can simplify (2^4)^3 to 2^(4*3) = 2^12.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-write-number-6-94cccd9f","Math G9: Finding the powers of rational numbers - Set 3","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-1-2-1 Finding the powers of rational numbers, and distinguishing  laws of exponent,  and using them to simplify numerical expressions,  Writing  and finding their values, writing numbers in scientific notation.","3. Writing very large or very small numbers by using scientific notation and converting between scientific notation and standard form.","Write the number 6,400,000 in scientific notation.","6.4 x 10^6","6.4 x 10^5","6.4 x 10^4","6.4 x 10^3","6.4 x 10^6","Scientific notation is a way of expressing very large or very small numbers in a more compact form. In this case, 6,400,000 can be written as 6.4 x 10^6.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-simplify-expression-3-f8bbd05d","Math G9: Finding the powers of rational numbers - Set 3","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-1-2-1 Finding the powers of rational numbers, and distinguishing  laws of exponent,  and using them to simplify numerical expressions,  Writing  and finding their values, writing numbers in scientific notation.","1. Finding the power of a rational number (with an integer exponent), distinguishing the laws of exponents, and using them to simplify numerical expressions.","Simplify the expression: (3^2)^4.","3^6","3^8","3^9","3^10","3^8","Using the power-of-a-power rule, (3²)⁴ = 3^(2×4) = 3⁸.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-water-tank-ddcf749b","Math G9: Finding the ratio, unit rate, percentage - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-1-2-3 Finding the ratio, unit rate, percentage, estimating them, and distinguishing proportional relationships, solving proportions, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. Finding percentages of a number (including percentages greater than 100%) and estimating them using Proper fractions, compatible numbers, or rounding.","A water tank can hold 1200 liters of water. If 75% of the tank is filled, how many liters of water are in the tank?","900 liters","1000 liters","1050 liters","1100 liters","900 liters","To find the number of liters in the tank, we need to calculate 75% of 1200 liters. 75% of 1200 liters is 0.75 * 1200 = 900 liters.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-water-tank-ddcf749b.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-group-of-3ae60081","Math G9: Finding the ratio, unit rate, percentage - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-1-2-3 Finding the ratio, unit rate, percentage, estimating them, and distinguishing proportional relationships, solving proportions, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","2. Distinguishing proportional and non-proportional relationships, formulating proportions, and solving","A group of friends want to split a bill of $120 evenly. If there are 8 friends, how much will each friend pay?","$12.00","$15.00","$18.00","$20.00","$15.00","$120 divided equally among 8 items is $15 per item.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-group-of-3ae60081.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-bike-travels-1e1138ff","Math G9: Finding the ratio, unit rate, percentage - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-1-2-3 Finding the ratio, unit rate, percentage, estimating them, and distinguishing proportional relationships, solving proportions, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","2. Distinguishing proportional and non-proportional relationships, formulating proportions, and solving","A bike travels 30 miles in 2 hours. What is its speed in miles per hour?","10 miles per hour","15 miles per hour","20 miles per hour","25 miles per hour","15 miles per hour","To find the speed, we need to divide the total distance traveled by the time taken. So, 30 miles / 2 hours = 15 miles per hour.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-store-offers-2159b5a6","Math G9: Finding the ratio, unit rate, percentage - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-1-2-3 Finding the ratio, unit rate, percentage, estimating them, and distinguishing proportional relationships, solving proportions, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","2. Distinguishing proportional and non-proportional relationships, formulating proportions, and solving","A store offers a 15% discount on a TV originally priced at $600. What is the sale price?","$510.00","$515.00","$520.00","$525.00","$510.00","To find the sale price, we need to calculate 15% of $600 and subtract it from the original price. 15% of $600 is 0.15 * $600 = $90. So, the sale price is $600 - $90 = $510.00.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-store-offers-2159b5a6.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-car-travels-16e56b0a","Math G9: Finding the ratio, unit rate, percentage - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-1-2-3 Finding the ratio, unit rate, percentage, estimating them, and distinguishing proportional relationships, solving proportions, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","2. Distinguishing proportional and non-proportional relationships, formulating proportions, and solving","A car travels 240 miles in 4 hours. What is its speed in miles per hour?","50 miles per hour","60 miles per hour","70 miles per hour","80 miles per hour","60 miles per hour","To find the speed, we need to divide the total distance traveled by the time taken. So, 240 miles / 4 hours = 60 miles per hour.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-car-travels-16e56b0a.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-group-of-fa10acc8","Math G9: Finding the ratio, unit rate, percentage - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-1-2-3 Finding the ratio, unit rate, percentage, estimating them, and distinguishing proportional relationships, solving proportions, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","2. Distinguishing proportional and non-proportional relationships, formulating proportions, and solving","A group of students want to share some candy equally. If they have 48 pieces of candy and there are 6 students, how many pieces of candy will each student get?","6 pieces","8 pieces","10 pieces","12 pieces","8 pieces","To find the number of pieces each student will get, we need to divide the total number of pieces by the number of students. So, 48 pieces / 6 students = 8 pieces per student.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-group-of-fa10acc8.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-store-offers-ff9522fd","Math G9: Finding the ratio, unit rate, percentage - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-1-2-3 Finding the ratio, unit rate, percentage, estimating them, and distinguishing proportional relationships, solving proportions, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","2. Distinguishing proportional and non-proportional relationships, formulating proportions, and solving","A store offers a 20% discount on a pair of shoes originally priced at $80. What is the sale price?","$64.00","$66.00","$68.00","$72.00","$64.00","To find the sale price, we need to calculate 20% of $80 and subtract it from the original price. 20% of $80 is 0.20 * $80 = $16. So, the sale price is $80 - $16 = $64.00.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-store-offers-ff9522fd.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-car-s-85323e62","Math G9: Finding the ratio, unit rate, percentage - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-1-2-3 Finding the ratio, unit rate, percentage, estimating them, and distinguishing proportional relationships, solving proportions, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","2. Distinguishing proportional and non-proportional relationships, formulating proportions, and solving","A car's fuel efficiency is 25 miles per gallon. If it travels 150 miles, how many gallons of fuel will it use?","5 gallons","6 gallons","7 gallons","8 gallons","6 gallons","To find the number of gallons used, we need to divide the total distance traveled by the fuel efficiency. So, 150 miles / 25 miles per gallon = 6 gallons.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-car-s-85323e62.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-bakery-sells-9d99ca6e","Math G9: Finding the ratio, unit rate, percentage - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-1-2-3 Finding the ratio, unit rate, percentage, estimating them, and distinguishing proportional relationships, solving proportions, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","2. Distinguishing proportional and non-proportional relationships, formulating proportions, and solving","A bakery sells a cake for $25. If a 10% discount is applied, what is the new price?","$22.50","$23.50","$24.50","$25.50","$22.50","To find the new price, we need to calculate 10% of $25 and subtract it from the original price. 10% of $25 is 0.10 * $25 = $2.50. So, the new price is $25 - $2.50 = $22.50.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-bakery-sells-9d99ca6e.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-shirt-is-15480721","Math G9: Finding the ratio, unit rate, percentage - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-1-2-3 Finding the ratio, unit rate, percentage, estimating them, and distinguishing proportional relationships, solving proportions, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","2. Distinguishing proportional and non-proportional relationships, formulating proportions, and solving","A shirt is on sale for 15% off its original price of $45. What is the sale price?","$38.25","$39.75","$41.25","$42.75","$38.25","To find the sale price, we need to calculate 15% of $45 and subtract it from the original price. 15% of $45 is 0.15 * $45 = $6.75. So, the sale price is $45 - $6.75 = $38.25.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-shirt-is-15480721.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-solve-equation-2-8266d46c","Math G9: Performing the four operations on integers - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-4 Writing a system of two linear equations with two variables, and solving them algebraically and graphically.","1. Distinguishing inequalities, identifying linear and compound inequalities, and writing them.","Solve the equation: 2/3 ÷ 3/4 = ?","8/9","9/8","2/3","3/2","8/9","(2/3) ÷ (3/4) = (2/3) × (4/3) = 8/9.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-solve-equation-2-c8b51f71","Math G9: Performing the four operations on integers - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-4 Writing a system of two linear equations with two variables, and solving them algebraically and graphically.","1. Distinguishing inequalities, identifying linear and compound inequalities, and writing them.","Solve the equation: 2/5 ÷ 1/5 = ?","2","1/2","4","1","2","(2/5) ÷ (1/5) = (2/5) × (5/1) = 2.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-simplify-expression-2-f4a7e28f","Math G9: Performing the four operations on integers - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-1-2-2 Performing the four operations on integers, rational numbers, and square roots, and using them to simplify the numerical expressions and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","4. Simplifying numerical expressions involving square roots using operations on square roots and rationalizing the denominator.","Simplify the expression: 2/3 - 1/3","1/3","2/3","1/6","3","1/3","Simplifying the fraction calculation gives 1/3.","","",""
