"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-simplify-expression-sqrt-099044b9","Math G9: Performing the four operations on integers - Set 3","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-1-2-2 Performing the four operations on integers, rational numbers, and square roots, and using them to simplify the numerical expressions and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","4. Simplifying numerical expressions involving square roots using operations on square roots and rationalizing the denominator.","Simplify the expression: sqrt(36) - sqrt(9)","3","5","7","1","3","√36 - √9 = 6 - 3 = 3.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-simplify-expression-sqrt-7f3e10c9","Math G9: Performing the four operations on integers - Set 3","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-1-2-2 Performing the four operations on integers, rational numbers, and square roots, and using them to simplify the numerical expressions and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","4. Simplifying numerical expressions involving square roots using operations on square roots and rationalizing the denominator.","Simplify the expression: sqrt(49) × sqrt(16)","28","11","21","196","28","√49 × √16 = 7 × 4 = 28.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-simplify-expression-sqrt-56f10468","Math G9: Performing the four operations on integers - Set 3","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-1-2-2 Performing the four operations on integers, rational numbers, and square roots, and using them to simplify the numerical expressions and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","4. Simplifying numerical expressions involving square roots using operations on square roots and rationalizing the denominator.","Simplify the expression: sqrt(25) ÷ sqrt(9)","5/3","3/5","15","1/15","5/3","√25 ÷ √9 = 5 ÷ 3 = 5/3.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-simplify-expression-sqrt-a2534638","Math G9: Performing the four operations on integers - Set 3","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-1-2-2 Performing the four operations on integers, rational numbers, and square roots, and using them to simplify the numerical expressions and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","4. Simplifying numerical expressions involving square roots using operations on square roots and rationalizing the denominator.","Simplify the expression: sqrt(36) ÷ sqrt(4)","3","1/3","9","6","3","√36 ÷ √4 = 6 ÷ 2 = 3.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-simplify-expression-sqrt-d9301974","Math G9: Performing the four operations on integers - Set 3","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-1-2-2 Performing the four operations on integers, rational numbers, and square roots, and using them to simplify the numerical expressions and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","4. Simplifying numerical expressions involving square roots using operations on square roots and rationalizing the denominator.","Simplify the expression: sqrt(64) + sqrt(16)","12","6","18","3","12","√64 + √16 = 8 + 4 = 12.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-simplify-expression-sqrt-66c3ec3e","Math G9: Performing the four operations on integers - Set 3","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-1-2-2 Performing the four operations on integers, rational numbers, and square roots, and using them to simplify the numerical expressions and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","4. Simplifying numerical expressions involving square roots using operations on square roots and rationalizing the denominator.","Simplify the expression: sqrt(9) - sqrt(1)","2","8","4","1/2","2","√9 - √1 = 3 - 1 = 2.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-simplify-expression-sqrt-d17df808","Math G9: Performing the four operations on integers - Set 3","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-1-2-2 Performing the four operations on integers, rational numbers, and square roots, and using them to simplify the numerical expressions and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","4. Simplifying numerical expressions involving square roots using operations on square roots and rationalizing the denominator.","Simplify the expression: sqrt(25) × sqrt(25)","25","10","5","100","25","√25 × √25 = 5 × 5 = 25.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-simplified-form-ef78c1cd","Math G9: Performing the four operations on integers - Set 4","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-2 Factoring algebraic terms, algebraic expression, the quadratic algebraic expression into their factors, and writing them in simplest form.","1. Finding the greatest common factor of algebraic terms and factoring an algebraic term completely.","What is the simplified form of the expression 4 * sqrt(9) - 2 * sqrt(16)?","10/8","4","6/2","8/4","4","√9 = 3 and √16 = 4, so 4(3) - 2(4) = 12 - 8 = 4.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-rule-function-0ac823fd","Math G9: Understanding arithmetic sequences and relationships, representing - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-1-1 Understanding arithmetic sequences and relationships, representing them graphically, distinguishing linear relationships, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Describing a function, determining its domain and range, writing its rule using two variables, and finding its values for given inputs from its domain.","What is the rule for the function f(x) = 3x - 2?","f(x) = 3x + 2","f(x) = 3x - 2","f(x) = x + 2","f(x) = x - 2","f(x) = 3x - 2","The rule for the function f(x) = 3x - 2 is f(x) = 3x - 2, which is the correct option.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-value-of-9f6ea2c9","Math G9: Understanding arithmetic sequences and relationships, representing - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-1-1 Understanding arithmetic sequences and relationships, representing them graphically, distinguishing linear relationships, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Describing a function, determining its domain and range, writing its rule using two variables, and finding its values for given inputs from its domain.","What is the value of f(-2) for the function f(x) = x^2?","4","6","8","10","4","To find the value of f(-2) for the function f(x) = x^2, we substitute x = -2 into the function and get f(-2) = (-2)^2 = 4.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-rule-function-be24af45","Math G9: Understanding arithmetic sequences and relationships, representing - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-1-1 Understanding arithmetic sequences and relationships, representing them graphically, distinguishing linear relationships, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Describing a function, determining its domain and range, writing its rule using two variables, and finding its values for given inputs from its domain.","What is the rule for the function f(x) = x^2?","f(x) = x^2","f(x) = x + 1","f(x) = x - 1","f(x) = 1 - x","f(x) = x^2","The rule for the function f(x) = x^2 is f(x) = x^2, which is the correct option.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-value-of-a4af82ac","Math G9: Understanding arithmetic sequences and relationships, representing - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-1-1 Understanding arithmetic sequences and relationships, representing them graphically, distinguishing linear relationships, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Describing a function, determining its domain and range, writing its rule using two variables, and finding its values for given inputs from its domain.","What is the value of f(3) for the function f(x) = 2x + 1?","5","6","7","8","7","To find the value of f(3) for the function f(x) = 2x + 1, we substitute x = 3 into the function and get f(3) = 2(3) + 1 = 7.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-domain-of-8d260064","Math G9: Understanding arithmetic sequences and relationships, representing - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-1-1-2 Understanding real numbers, classifying, comparing, and  Ordering them.","1. Finding the square root of a number and approximating it to the nearest whole number or to the nearest decimal place, by using a calculator and without using one.","What is the domain of f(x) = √x?","[0, ∞)","(-∞, ∞)","(0, ∞)","(-∞, 0]","[0, ∞)","The square-root function f(x)=√x is defined for x ≥ 0.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-range-of-94073f17","Math G9: Understanding arithmetic sequences and relationships, representing - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-1-1 Understanding arithmetic sequences and relationships, representing them graphically, distinguishing linear relationships, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Describing a function, determining its domain and range, writing its rule using two variables, and finding its values for given inputs from its domain.","What is the range of f(x) = √x?","[0, ∞)","(-∞, ∞)","(0, ∞)","(-∞, 0]","[0, ∞)","The square-root function produces only nonnegative outputs, including zero.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-range-of-a68b1e4c","Math G9: Understanding arithmetic sequences and relationships, representing - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-1-1 Understanding arithmetic sequences and relationships, representing them graphically, distinguishing linear relationships, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Describing a function, determining its domain and range, writing its rule using two variables, and finding its values for given inputs from its domain.","What is the range of the function f(x) = 2x + 1?","(-∞, ∞)","[0, ∞)","(-∞, 0]","{0}","(-∞, ∞)","A nonconstant linear function has all real numbers as its range.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-domain-of-8288b45b","Math G9: Understanding arithmetic sequences and relationships, representing - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-1-2 Understanding functions, distinguishing linear and quadratic functions, identifying their properties, and representing them graphically.","3. Distinguishing quadratic functions (parabolas), representing its, finding their maximum and minimum values, domain and range, and their zeros algebraically and graphically.","What is the domain of the function f(x) = 1 / (x - 2)?","(-∞, 2) ∪ (2, ∞)","(-∞, ∞)","[2, ∞)","(-∞, 2]","(-∞, 2) ∪ (2, ∞)","The denominator cannot equal zero, so x ≠ 2.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-rule-function-eaad0bfe","Math G9: Understanding arithmetic sequences and relationships, representing - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-1-1 Understanding arithmetic sequences and relationships, representing them graphically, distinguishing linear relationships, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Describing a function, determining its domain and range, writing its rule using two variables, and finding its values for given inputs from its domain.","What is the rule for the function f(x) = 2x + 1?","f(x) = 2x - 1","f(x) = 2x + 1","f(x) = x + 1","f(x) = x - 1","f(x) = 2x + 1","The rule for the function f(x) = 2x + 1 is f(x) = 2x + 1, which is the correct option.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-domain-of-c1ad4e04","Math G9: Understanding arithmetic sequences and relationships - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-1-1 Understanding arithmetic sequences and relationships, representing them graphically, distinguishing linear relationships, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Describing a function, determining its domain and range, writing its rule using two variables, and finding its values for given inputs from its domain.","What is the domain of the function f(x) = 2x - 1?","(-∞, ∞)","[0, ∞)","x ≠ 0","{0}","(-∞, ∞)","A linear function is defined for every real input, so its domain is (-∞, ∞).","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-domain-of-e97e98ce","Math G9: Understanding arithmetic sequences and relationships - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-1-1 Understanding arithmetic sequences and relationships, representing them graphically, distinguishing linear relationships, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Describing a function, determining its domain and range, writing its rule using two variables, and finding its values for given inputs from its domain.","What is the domain of the function f(x) = x^2?","(-∞, ∞)","[0, ∞)","(-∞, 0]","x ≠ 0","(-∞, ∞)","The function f(x)=x² is defined for every real number, so its domain is (-∞, ∞).","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-range-of-060ef1a9","Math G9: Understanding arithmetic sequences and relationships - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-1-1 Understanding arithmetic sequences and relationships, representing them graphically, distinguishing linear relationships, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Describing a function, determining its domain and range, writing its rule using two variables, and finding its values for given inputs from its domain.","What is the range of the function f(x) = x^2?","[0, ∞)","(-∞, ∞)","(-∞, 0]","(0, ∞)","[0, ∞)","For f(x)=x², outputs are never negative and zero is included, so the range is [0, ∞).","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-value-of-c2872829","Math G9: Understanding arithmetic sequences and relationships - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-1-1 Understanding arithmetic sequences and relationships, representing them graphically, distinguishing linear relationships, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Describing a function, determining its domain and range, writing its rule using two variables, and finding its values for given inputs from its domain.","What is the value of f(-2) for the function f(x) = x^2?","4","2","0","1","4","To find the value of f(-2) for the function f(x) = x^2, substitute x = -2 into the function: f(-2) = (-2)^2 = 4.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-rule-function-4dce752e","Math G9: Understanding arithmetic sequences and relationships - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-1-1 Understanding arithmetic sequences and relationships, representing them graphically, distinguishing linear relationships, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Describing a function, determining its domain and range, writing its rule using two variables, and finding its values for given inputs from its domain.","What is the rule for the function f(x) = 2x + 3?","f(x) = 2x - 3","f(x) = 2x + 3","f(x) = x - 2","f(x) = x + 1","f(x) = 2x + 3","The rule for the function f(x) = 2x + 3 is f(x) = 2x + 3.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-domain-of-91aa409f","Math G9: Understanding arithmetic sequences and relationships - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-1-2-2 Performing the four operations on integers, rational numbers, and square roots, and using them to simplify the numerical expressions and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","4. Simplifying numerical expressions involving square roots using operations on square roots and rationalizing the denominator.","What is the domain of the function f(x) = 1 / (x - 2)?","(-∞, 2) ∪ (2, ∞)","(-∞, ∞)","[2, ∞)","(-∞, 2]","(-∞, 2) ∪ (2, ∞)","A rational function with denominator x - 2 is undefined at x = 2, so its domain is all real numbers except 2.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-value-of-fc0d8761","Math G9: Understanding arithmetic sequences and relationships - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-1-1 Understanding arithmetic sequences and relationships, representing them graphically, distinguishing linear relationships, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","2. Expressing an arithmetic sequence as a linear function and representing it graphically.","What is the value of f(4) for the function f(x) = 2x + 3?","11","9","7","13","11","Substitute x = 4 into the function: the resulting value is 11.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-range-of-dfb72b60","Math G9: Understanding arithmetic sequences and relationships - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-1-1 Understanding arithmetic sequences and relationships, representing them graphically, distinguishing linear relationships, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Describing a function, determining its domain and range, writing its rule using two variables, and finding its values for given inputs from its domain.","What is the range of the function f(x) = 2x + 3?","(-∞, ∞)","[0, ∞)","(-∞, 0]","{0}","(-∞, ∞)","A nonconstant linear function can produce every real output, so its range is (-∞, ∞).","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-rule-function-8504fc9f","Math G9: Understanding arithmetic sequences and relationships - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-1-1 Understanding arithmetic sequences and relationships, representing them graphically, distinguishing linear relationships, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Describing a function, determining its domain and range, writing its rule using two variables, and finding its values for given inputs from its domain.","What is the rule for the function f(x) = x^2?","f(x) = x^2","f(x) = x + 2","f(x) = x - 2","f(x) = 2x","f(x) = x^2","The rule for the function f(x) = x^2 is f(x) = x^2.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-rule-function-d12577c3","Math G9: Understanding arithmetic sequences and relationships - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-1-1 Understanding arithmetic sequences and relationships, representing them graphically, distinguishing linear relationships, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","1. Describing a function, determining its domain and range, writing its rule using two variables, and finding its values for given inputs from its domain.","What is the rule for the function f(x) = 2x - 1?","f(x) = 2x + 1","f(x) = 2x - 1","f(x) = x - 2","f(x) = x + 1","f(x) = 2x - 1","The rule for the function f(x) = 2x - 1 is f(x) = 2x - 1.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-school-has-7a2c3034","Math G9: Understanding arithmetic sequences and relationships - Set 4","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-1-1 Understanding arithmetic sequences and relationships, representing them graphically, distinguishing linear relationships, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","2. Expressing an arithmetic sequence as a linear function and representing it graphically.","A school has 240 students in a class. If they want to divide the students into groups of 12, how many groups can be formed?","15 groups","20 groups","18 groups","25 groups","20 groups","To find the number of groups that can be formed, we divide the total number of students by the number of students per group. Number of groups = Total students / Students per group = 240 students / 12 students per group = 20 groups.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-school-has-7a2c3034.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-box-of-7ab8196a","Math G9: Understanding arithmetic sequences and relationships - Set 4","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-1-1 Understanding arithmetic sequences and relationships, representing them graphically, distinguishing linear relationships, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","2. Expressing an arithmetic sequence as a linear function and representing it graphically.","A box of pencils weighs 1.2 kg. If a person buys 5 boxes of pencils, what is the total weight of the pencils?","4.8 kg","5.5 kg","6.0 kg","7.2 kg","6.0 kg","To find the total weight of the pencils, we multiply the weight of one box by the number of boxes. Total weight = Weight of one box * Number of boxes = 1.2 kg * 5 boxes = 6.0 kg.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-box-of-7ab8196a.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-group-of-d8c122f1","Math G9: Understanding arithmetic sequences and relationships - Set 4","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-1-1 Understanding arithmetic sequences and relationships, representing them graphically, distinguishing linear relationships, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","2. Expressing an arithmetic sequence as a linear function and representing it graphically.","A group of friends want to share some money equally. If they have $120 and there are 6 friends, how much money will each friend get?","10 dollars","15 dollars","20 dollars","30 dollars","20 dollars","To find the amount each friend will get, we divide the total amount of money by the number of friends. Amount per friend = Total money / Number of friends = $120 / 6 friends = $20 per friend.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-group-of-d8c122f1.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-car-travels-27e5a4ba","Math G9: Understanding arithmetic sequences and relationships - Set 4","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-1-1 Understanding arithmetic sequences and relationships, representing them graphically, distinguishing linear relationships, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","2. Expressing an arithmetic sequence as a linear function and representing it graphically.","A car travels 320 km in 8 hours. What is its average speed?","20 km/h","25 km/h","30 km/h","40 km/h","40 km/h","To find the average speed, we divide the total distance traveled by the total time taken. Average speed = Total distance / Total time = 320 km / 8 hours = 40 km/h.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-car-travels-27e5a4ba.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-water-tank-6db37d16","Math G9: Understanding arithmetic sequences and relationships - Set 4","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-1-1 Understanding arithmetic sequences and relationships, representing them graphically, distinguishing linear relationships, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","2. Expressing an arithmetic sequence as a linear function and representing it graphically.","A water tank can hold 1200 liters of water. If 300 liters of water are already in the tank, how many more liters of water can be added?","900 liters.","300 liters","800 liters","1000 liters","900 liters.","1200 - 300 = 900 liters.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-water-tank-6db37d16.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-bakery-sells-13c6ba75","Math G9: Understanding arithmetic sequences and relationships - Set 4","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-1-1 Understanding arithmetic sequences and relationships, representing them graphically, distinguishing linear relationships, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","2. Expressing an arithmetic sequence as a linear function and representing it graphically.","A bakery sells 480 muffins in 8 days. If they sell the same number of muffins each day, how many muffins does the bakery sell per day?","60 muffins","55 muffins","50 muffins","45 muffins","60 muffins","To find the number of muffins sold per day, we divide the total number of muffins sold by the total number of days. Number of muffins per day = Total muffins / Total days = 480 muffins / 8 days = 60 muffins per day.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-bakery-sells-13c6ba75.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-car-travels-ced1da21","Math G9: Understanding arithmetic sequences and relationships - Set 4","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-1-1 Understanding arithmetic sequences and relationships, representing them graphically, distinguishing linear relationships, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","2. Expressing an arithmetic sequence as a linear function and representing it graphically.","A car travels 250 km in 5 hours. What is its average speed?","50 km/h","100 km/h","125 km/h","250 km/h","50 km/h","To find the average speed, we divide the total distance traveled by the total time taken. Average speed = Total distance / Total time = 250 km / 5 hours = 50 km/h.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-car-travels-ced1da21.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-bakery-sells-0c5e5515","Math G9: Understanding arithmetic sequences and relationships - Set 4","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-1-1 Understanding arithmetic sequences and relationships, representing them graphically, distinguishing linear relationships, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","2. Expressing an arithmetic sequence as a linear function and representing it graphically.","A bakery sells 720 muffins in 10 days. If they sell the same number of muffins each day, how many muffins does the bakery sell per day?","60 muffins","65 muffins","72 muffins","80 muffins","72 muffins","To find the number of muffins sold per day, we divide the total number of muffins sold by the total number of days. Number of muffins per day = Total muffins / Total days = 720 muffins / 10 days = 72 muffins per day.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-bakery-sells-0c5e5515.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-bike-travels-b616efc0","Math G9: Understanding arithmetic sequences and relationships - Set 4","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-1-1 Understanding arithmetic sequences and relationships, representing them graphically, distinguishing linear relationships, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","2. Expressing an arithmetic sequence as a linear function and representing it graphically.","A bike travels 180 km in 6 hours. What is its average speed?","20 km/h","30 km/h","25 km/h","50 km/h","30 km/h","To find the average speed, we divide the total distance traveled by the total time taken. Average speed = Total distance / Total time = 180 km / 6 hours = 30 km/h.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-group-of-132c6ede","Math G9: Understanding arithmetic sequences and relationships - Set 4","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-1-1 Understanding arithmetic sequences and relationships, representing them graphically, distinguishing linear relationships, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","2. Expressing an arithmetic sequence as a linear function and representing it graphically.","A group of friends want to share some candy equally. If they have 48 pieces of candy and there are 8 friends, how many pieces of candy will each friend get?","Each friend will get 6 pieces of candy","5 pieces","7 pieces","48 pieces","Each friend will get 6 pieces of candy","48 ÷ 8 = 6 pieces of candy.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-group-of-132c6ede.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-of-graphs-represents-d8ca4a8e","Math G9: Understanding functions, distinguishing linear and quadratic - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-1-2 Understanding functions, distinguishing linear and quadratic functions, identifying their properties, and representing them graphically.","2. Distinguishing linear functions and representing them graphically.","Which of the following graphs represents a linear function?","A graph with a parabola","A graph with a straight line","A graph with a circle","A graph with a triangle","A graph with a straight line","A linear function has a straight line graph, whereas a quadratic function has a parabola graph.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-of-graphs-represents-d8ca4a8e.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-slope-of-20dc26e6","Math G9: Understanding functions, distinguishing linear and quadratic - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-2-1 Determining the locations of points in the coordinate plane and plotting them, finding the distance between two points and the coordinates of the midpoint, and determining the slope of a line, writing its equation, and graphing it.","5. Finding the distance between two points in the coordinate plane, and finding the coordinates of the midpoint.","What is the slope of a horizontal line?","0","1","2","undefined","0","The slope of a horizontal line is always 0.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-equation-of-1f11d37d","Math G9: Understanding functions, distinguishing linear and quadratic - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-2-1 Determining the locations of points in the coordinate plane and plotting them, finding the distance between two points and the coordinates of the midpoint, and determining the slope of a line, writing its equation, and graphing it.","3. Distinguishing the equation of a line and writing its using the slope-intercept form, the point-slope form, and the standard form.","What is the equation of a line in slope-intercept form?","y = mx + b","y = mx - b","y = mx^2 + b","y = mx^2 - b","y = mx + b","The slope-intercept form of a line is y = mx + b, where m is the slope and b is the y-intercept.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-of-equations-represents-c8f33270","Math G9: Understanding functions, distinguishing linear and quadratic - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-1-2 Understanding functions, distinguishing linear and quadratic functions, identifying their properties, and representing them graphically.","2. Distinguishing linear functions and representing them graphically.","Which of the following equations represents a linear function?","y = 2x^2 + 3","y = 2x - 1","y = 3x^2 + 2x - 1","y = 3x + 2","y = 2x - 1","A linear function is in the form y = mx + b, where m is the slope and b is the y-intercept.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-equation-of-737ae1eb","Math G9: Understanding functions, distinguishing linear and quadratic - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-2-1 Determining the locations of points in the coordinate plane and plotting them, finding the distance between two points and the coordinates of the midpoint, and determining the slope of a line, writing its equation, and graphing it.","3. Distinguishing the equation of a line and writing its using the slope-intercept form, the point-slope form, and the standard form.","What is the equation of a horizontal line?","y = mx + b","y = mx - b","y = mx^2 + b","y = b","y = b","The equation of a horizontal line is always in the form y = b.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-of-equations-represents-039a7a2f","Math G9: Understanding functions, distinguishing linear and quadratic - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-1-2 Understanding functions, distinguishing linear and quadratic functions, identifying their properties, and representing them graphically.","2. Distinguishing linear functions and representing them graphically.","Which of the following equations represents a linear function with a slope of 0?","y = 2x^2 + 1","y = 2x - 1","y = 3x^2 + 2x - 1","y = 3x + 2","y = 3x + 2","A linear function with a slope of 0 is in the form y = b.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-of-equations-represents-039a7a2f.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-equation-of-bf7fbb20","Math G9: Understanding functions, distinguishing linear and quadratic - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-2-1 Determining the locations of points in the coordinate plane and plotting them, finding the distance between two points and the coordinates of the midpoint, and determining the slope of a line, writing its equation, and graphing it.","3. Distinguishing the equation of a line and writing its using the slope-intercept form, the point-slope form, and the standard form.","What is the equation of a line with a slope of 1 and a y-intercept of 2?","y = x + 2","y = x - 2","y = x^2 + 2","y = x^2 - 2","y = x + 2","The equation of a line with a slope of 1 and a y-intercept of 2 is y = x + 2.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-of-graphs-represents-17f02829","Math G9: Understanding functions, distinguishing linear and quadratic - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-1-2 Understanding functions, distinguishing linear and quadratic functions, identifying their properties, and representing them graphically.","3. Distinguishing quadratic functions (parabolas), representing its, finding their maximum and minimum values, domain and range, and their zeros algebraically and graphically.","Which of the following graphs represents a linear function with a slope of -3?","A graph with a parabola","A graph with a straight line","A graph with a circle","A graph with a triangle","A graph with a straight line","A linear function with a slope of -3 has a straight line graph with a negative slope.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-of-graphs-represents-17f02829.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-of-equations-represents-1e7d5bff","Math G9: Understanding functions, distinguishing linear and quadratic - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-1-2 Understanding functions, distinguishing linear and quadratic functions, identifying their properties, and representing them graphically.","2. Distinguishing linear functions and representing them graphically.","Which of the following equations represents a linear function with a slope of 2?","y = 2x^2 + 1","y = 2x - 1","y = 3x^2 + 2x - 1","y = 3x + 2","y = 2x - 1","A linear function with a slope of 2 is in the form y = 2x + b.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-of-equations-represents-1e7d5bff.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-y-intercept-39d88434","Math G9: Understanding functions, distinguishing linear and quadratic - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-2-1 Determining the locations of points in the coordinate plane and plotting them, finding the distance between two points and the coordinates of the midpoint, and determining the slope of a line, writing its equation, and graphing it.","3. Distinguishing the equation of a line and writing its using the slope-intercept form, the point-slope form, and the standard form.","What is the y-intercept of a line in slope-intercept form?","m","b","x","y","b","The y-intercept of a line in slope-intercept form is the value of b.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-of-graphs-represents-5f72ecae","Math G9: Understanding functions - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-1-2 Understanding functions, distinguishing linear and quadratic functions, identifying their properties, and representing them graphically.","2. Distinguishing linear functions and representing them graphically.","Which of the following graphs represents a linear function?","A parabola that opens upwards","A straight line with a positive slope","A curve that gets closer to the x-axis as x increases","A circle centered at the origin","A straight line with a positive slope","A linear function has a graph that is a straight line, and the slope of the line can be positive or negative. In this case, the graph is a straight line with a positive slope.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-of-graphs-represents-5f72ecae.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-of-equations-represents-fd2d6010","Math G9: Understanding functions - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-1-2 Understanding functions, distinguishing linear and quadratic functions, identifying their properties, and representing them graphically.","2. Distinguishing linear functions and representing them graphically.","Which of the following equations represents a linear function with a y-intercept of 2?","y = 2x + 1","y = x + 2","y = x^2 + 2","y = -x + 1","y = x + 2","The equation y = x + 2 represents a linear function with a y-intercept of 2, because the constant term is 2.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-slope-of-1eb792ce","Math G9: Understanding functions - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-1-2 Understanding functions, distinguishing linear and quadratic functions, identifying their properties, and representing them graphically.","2. Distinguishing linear functions and representing them graphically.","What is the slope of the linear function represented by the graph that passes through the points (1, 2) and (3, 4)?","A slope of 1","A slope of -1","A slope of 2","A slope of -2","A slope of 1","The slope of the linear function can be found by using the formula m = (y2 - y1)/(x2 - x1), where (x1, y1) and (x2, y2) are two points on the graph. In this case, m = (4-2)/(3-1) = 1.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-slope-of-1eb792ce.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-equation-of-98b29169","Math G9: Understanding functions - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-2-1 Determining the locations of points in the coordinate plane and plotting them, finding the distance between two points and the coordinates of the midpoint, and determining the slope of a line, writing its equation, and graphing it.","3. Distinguishing the equation of a line and writing its using the slope-intercept form, the point-slope form, and the standard form.","What is the equation of the linear function that passes through the points (2, 3) and (4, 5)?","y = x + 1","y = x - 1","y = 2x - 1","y = x/2 + 1","y = x + 1","To find the equation of the linear function, we can use the slope-intercept form y = mx + b, where m is the slope and b is the y-intercept. The slope is (5-3)/(4-2) = 1, and the y-intercept can be found by substituting one of the points into the equation. Using the point (2, 3), we get 3 = 2(1) + b, so b = 1. Therefore, the equation of the linear function is y = x + 1.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-of-is-an-4b3b78c0","Math G9: Understanding functions - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-1-2 Understanding functions, distinguishing linear and quadratic functions, identifying their properties, and representing them graphically.","2. Distinguishing linear functions and representing them graphically.","Which of the following is an example of a linear function?","The area of a circle as a function of its radius","The volume of a cube as a function of its side length","The distance traveled by a car as a function of time, assuming a constant speed","The height of a projectile as a function of time, assuming a constant acceleration","The distance traveled by a car as a function of time, assuming a constant speed","The distance traveled by a car as a function of time, assuming a constant speed, is an example of a linear function, because the distance traveled is directly proportional to the time traveled.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-y-intercept-5ba8e1da","Math G9: Understanding functions - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-1-2 Understanding functions, distinguishing linear and quadratic functions, identifying their properties, and representing them graphically.","2. Distinguishing linear functions and representing them graphically.","What is the y-intercept of the linear function represented by the equation y = 2x - 3?","A y-intercept of 2","A y-intercept of -3","A y-intercept of 3","A y-intercept of -2","A y-intercept of -3","The equation y = 2x - 3 represents a linear function with a y-intercept of -3, because the constant term is -3.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-of-equations-represents-99b8c316","Math G9: Understanding functions - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-1-2 Understanding functions, distinguishing linear and quadratic functions, identifying their properties, and representing them graphically.","2. Distinguishing linear functions and representing them graphically.","Which of the following equations represents a linear function with a slope of 1/2?","y = 2x + 1","y = x/2 + 1","y = x^2 + 1","y = -x + 1","y = x/2 + 1","The equation y = x/2 + 1 represents a linear function with a slope of 1/2, because the coefficient of x is 1/2.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-of-equations-represents-99b8c316.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-slope-of-407efd0f","Math G9: Understanding functions - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-1-2 Understanding functions, distinguishing linear and quadratic functions, identifying their properties, and representing them graphically.","2. Distinguishing linear functions and representing them graphically.","What is the slope of the linear function represented by the equation y = -3x - 2?","A slope of 3","A slope of -3","A slope of 2","A slope of -2","A slope of -3","The equation y = -3x - 2 represents a linear function with a slope of -3, because the coefficient of x is -3.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-of-is-a-d7223398","Math G9: Understanding functions - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-1-2 Understanding functions, distinguishing linear and quadratic functions, identifying their properties, and representing them graphically.","2. Distinguishing linear functions and representing them graphically.","Which of the following is a characteristic of a linear function?","The graph is a parabola that opens upwards","The graph is a straight line with a constant slope","The graph is a curve that gets closer to the x-axis as x increases","The graph is a circle centered at the origin","The graph is a straight line with a constant slope","A linear function has a graph that is a straight line with a constant slope, which means that the rate of change of the output variable with respect to the input variable is constant.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-type-of-function-fffb29a9","Math G9: Understanding functions - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-1-2 Understanding functions, distinguishing linear and quadratic functions, identifying their properties, and representing them graphically.","2. Distinguishing linear functions and representing them graphically.","What type of function is represented by the equation y = 2x + 1?","A quadratic function with a positive leading coefficient","A linear function with a positive slope","A quadratic function with a negative leading coefficient","A linear function with a negative slope","A linear function with a positive slope","The equation y = 2x + 1 represents a linear function because it can be written in the form y = mx + b, where m is the slope and b is the y-intercept. In this case, the slope is positive.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-how-do-you-aaf1d63c","Math G9: Understanding functions - Set 3","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-3 Writing linear and quadratic equations, solving them algebraically and graphically, and estimating their solutions from graph representations.","4. Estimating the solution of a quadratic equation from its graphical representations, and solving quadratic equations graphically and algebraically using factoring, the quadratic formula, or completing the square.","How do you find the axis of symmetry of a quadratic function?","By using the formula x = -b/2a","By graphing the function and finding the line that passes through the vertex","By setting y = 0 and solving for x","By using the quadratic formula","By using the formula x = -b/2a","To find the axis of symmetry of a quadratic function, use the formula x = -b/2a.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-how-do-you-aaf1d63c.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-axis-of-f12daed9","Math G9: Understanding functions - Set 3","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-1-1 Distinguishing the interior angles of polygons, and the relationships between angles formed by a transversal intersecting parallel lines, and using them to find unknown angle measures, and determining polygons that can form tessellations.","1. Distinguishing shapes that are symmetric about an axis, identifying axes of symmetry and shapes that have rotational symmetry about a point, and determining angles of rotation.","What is the axis of symmetry of a parabola?","A line that passes through the vertex of the parabola","A line that is perpendicular to the x-axis","A line that is perpendicular to the y-axis","A line that passes through the x-intercepts of the parabola","A line that passes through the vertex of the parabola","The axis of symmetry of a parabola is a line that passes through the vertex of the parabola.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-axis-of-f12daed9.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-how-do-you-ba7c22a3","Math G9: Understanding functions - Set 3","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-1-2 Understanding functions, distinguishing linear and quadratic functions, identifying their properties, and representing them graphically.","3. Distinguishing quadratic functions (parabolas), representing its, finding their maximum and minimum values, domain and range, and their zeros algebraically and graphically.","How do you determine the maximum or minimum value of a quadratic function?","By finding the vertex of the parabola","By graphing the function and finding the highest or lowest point","By using the quadratic formula","By setting y = 0 and solving for x","By finding the vertex of the parabola","To determine the maximum or minimum value of a quadratic function, find the vertex of the parabola.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-range-of-1daf2d22","Math G9: Understanding functions - Set 3","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-1-2 Understanding functions, distinguishing linear and quadratic functions, identifying their properties, and representing them graphically.","3. Distinguishing quadratic functions (parabolas), representing its, finding their maximum and minimum values, domain and range, and their zeros algebraically and graphically.","What is the range of a quadratic function?","All real numbers","Only positive numbers","Only negative numbers","The set of all y values for which the function is defined","The set of all y values for which the function is defined","The range of a quadratic function is the set of all y values for which the function is defined.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-domain-of-116a9743","Math G9: Understanding functions - Set 3","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-1-2 Understanding functions, distinguishing linear and quadratic functions, identifying their properties, and representing them graphically.","3. Distinguishing quadratic functions (parabolas), representing its, finding their maximum and minimum values, domain and range, and their zeros algebraically and graphically.","What is the domain of a quadratic function?","All real numbers","Only positive numbers","Only negative numbers","The set of all x values for which the function is defined","The set of all x values for which the function is defined","The domain of a quadratic function is the set of all x values for which the function is defined.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-how-do-you-c4be6398","Math G9: Understanding functions - Set 3","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-3 Writing linear and quadratic equations, solving them algebraically and graphically, and estimating their solutions from graph representations.","4. Estimating the solution of a quadratic equation from its graphical representations, and solving quadratic equations graphically and algebraically using factoring, the quadratic formula, or completing the square.","How do you find the x-intercepts of a quadratic function?","By setting y = 0 and solving for x","By setting x = 0 and solving for y","By graphing the function and finding the points where it crosses the x-axis","By using the quadratic formula","By setting y = 0 and solving for x","To find the x-intercepts of a quadratic function, set y = 0 and solve for x.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-vertex-of-78eb6bd2","Math G9: Understanding functions - Set 3","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-2-1 Determining the locations of points in the coordinate plane and plotting them, finding the distance between two points and the coordinates of the midpoint, and determining the slope of a line, writing its equation, and graphing it.","3. Distinguishing the equation of a line and writing its using the slope-intercept form, the point-slope form, and the standard form.","What is the vertex of a parabola?","The highest or lowest point on the graph","The x-intercept of the graph","The y-intercept of the graph","The point where the graph touches the x-axis","The highest or lowest point on the graph","The vertex of a parabola is the highest or lowest point on the graph.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-vertex-of-78eb6bd2.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-general-form-7ea15d2f","Math G9: Understanding functions - Set 3","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-2 Factoring algebraic terms, algebraic expression, the quadratic algebraic expression into their factors, and writing them in simplest form.","3. Distinguishing quadratic algebraic including perfect square trinomials, expressions of the form x + c, ax + bx + c, and the difference of two squares, and Factoring them into two factors.","What is the general form of a quadratic function?","y = mx + b","y = mx^2 + bx + c","y = x^2 - 4","y = 2x","y = x^2 - 4","A quadratic function is in the form y = ax^2 + bx + c, where a, b, and c are constants.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-how-do-you-a8951c64","Math G9: Understanding functions - Set 3","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-3 Writing linear and quadratic equations, solving them algebraically and graphically, and estimating their solutions from graph representations.","4. Estimating the solution of a quadratic equation from its graphical representations, and solving quadratic equations graphically and algebraically using factoring, the quadratic formula, or completing the square.","How do you determine if a function is quadratic or not?","By graphing the function and looking for a parabola","By checking if the function has a squared variable","By using the quadratic formula","By setting y = 0 and solving for x","By checking if the function has a squared variable","To determine if a function is quadratic or not, check if the function has a squared variable.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-difference-a-34f1ee73","Math G9: Understanding functions - Set 3","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-1-2 Understanding functions, distinguishing linear and quadratic functions, identifying their properties, and representing them graphically.","2. Distinguishing linear functions and representing them graphically.","What is the difference between a linear function and a quadratic function?","A linear function has highest exponent 1, while a quadratic function has highest exponent 2.","A linear function has highest exponent 2, while a quadratic function has highest exponent 1.","Both functions must have highest exponent 3.","The two terms mean exactly the same thing.","A linear function has highest exponent 1, while a quadratic function has highest exponent 2.","The degree of a linear function is 1, while the degree of a quadratic function is 2.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-solve-inequality-x-579e9ac0","Math G9: Understanding inequalities - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-5 Understanding inequalities, distinguishing linear inequalities and compound inequalities, writing and solving them, and representing their solutions on the number line.","2. Solving simple linear inequalities and inequalities that include parentheses, and representing their solutions on the number line.","Solve the inequality x - 3 > 7.","x > 10","x < 10","x ≥ 10","x ≤ 10","x > 10","Add 3 to both sides of x - 3 > 7 to get x > 10.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-solve-inequality-x-feea7596","Math G9: Understanding inequalities - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-5 Understanding inequalities, distinguishing linear inequalities and compound inequalities, writing and solving them, and representing their solutions on the number line.","2. Solving simple linear inequalities and inequalities that include parentheses, and representing their solutions on the number line.","Solve the inequality: x - 1 > 6.","x > 7","x < 7","x ≥ 7","x ≤ 7","x > 7","Add 1 to both sides of x - 1 > 6 to get x > 7.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-solve-inequality-x-c11f5f06","Math G9: Understanding inequalities - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-5 Understanding inequalities, distinguishing linear inequalities and compound inequalities, writing and solving them, and representing their solutions on the number line.","2. Solving simple linear inequalities and inequalities that include parentheses, and representing their solutions on the number line.","Solve the inequality x + 4 < 9.","x < 5","x > 5","x ≤ 5","x ≥ 5","x < 5","Subtract 4 from both sides of x + 4 < 9 to get x < 5.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-solve-inequality-2x-763dac20","Math G9: Understanding inequalities - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-5 Understanding inequalities, distinguishing linear inequalities and compound inequalities, writing and solving them, and representing their solutions on the number line.","2. Solving simple linear inequalities and inequalities that include parentheses, and representing their solutions on the number line.","Solve the inequality: 2x - 4 > 12.","x > 8","x < 8","x ≥ 8","x ≤ 8","x > 8","Solve 2x - 4 > 12: add 4 to get 2x > 16, then divide by 2 to get x > 8.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-solve-inequality-4x-ae215813","Math G9: Understanding inequalities - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-5 Understanding inequalities, distinguishing linear inequalities and compound inequalities, writing and solving them, and representing their solutions on the number line.","2. Solving simple linear inequalities and inequalities that include parentheses, and representing their solutions on the number line.","Solve the inequality 4x - 2 < 18.","x < 5","x > 5","x = 5","x ? 4","x < 5","Add 2 to get 4x < 20, then divide by 4: x < 5.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-solve-inequality-x-1da56bcc","Math G9: Understanding inequalities - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-5 Understanding inequalities, distinguishing linear inequalities and compound inequalities, writing and solving them, and representing their solutions on the number line.","2. Solving simple linear inequalities and inequalities that include parentheses, and representing their solutions on the number line.","Solve the inequality: x + 2 > 9.","x > 7","x < 7","x ≥ 7","x ≤ 7","x > 7","Subtract 2 from both sides of x + 2 > 9 to get x > 7.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-solve-inequality-2x-c3efc5cb","Math G9: Understanding inequalities - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-5 Understanding inequalities, distinguishing linear inequalities and compound inequalities, writing and solving them, and representing their solutions on the number line.","2. Solving simple linear inequalities and inequalities that include parentheses, and representing their solutions on the number line.","Solve the inequality 2x + 5 > 11.","x > 3","x < 3","x ≥ 3","x ≤ 3","x > 3","Subtract 5 to get 2x > 6, then divide by 2: x > 3.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-solve-inequality-5x-0b1bef99","Math G9: Understanding inequalities - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-5 Understanding inequalities, distinguishing linear inequalities and compound inequalities, writing and solving them, and representing their solutions on the number line.","2. Solving simple linear inequalities and inequalities that include parentheses, and representing their solutions on the number line.","Solve the inequality 5x - 3 > 22.","x > 5","x < 5","x = 5","x ? 5","x > 5","To solve the inequality, we need to isolate the variable x. Add 3 to both sides to get 5x > 25, then divide both sides by 5 to get x > 5.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-solve-inequality-3x-d9f47913","Math G9: Understanding inequalities - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-5 Understanding inequalities, distinguishing linear inequalities and compound inequalities, writing and solving them, and representing their solutions on the number line.","2. Solving simple linear inequalities and inequalities that include parentheses, and representing their solutions on the number line.","Solve the inequality 3x + 1 > 16.","x > 5","x < 5","x = 5","x ? 5","x > 5","To solve the inequality, we need to isolate the variable x. Subtract 1 from both sides to get 3x > 15, then divide both sides by 3 to get x > 5.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-solve-inequality-x-c8411788","Math G9: Understanding inequalities - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-5 Understanding inequalities, distinguishing linear inequalities and compound inequalities, writing and solving them, and representing their solutions on the number line.","2. Solving simple linear inequalities and inequalities that include parentheses, and representing their solutions on the number line.","Solve the inequality: x - 2 < 5.","x < 7","x > 7","x ≤ 7","x ≥ 7","x < 7","Add 2 to both sides of x - 2 < 5 to get x < 7.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-solve-compound-inequality-26cca3b0","Math G9: Understanding inequalities - Set 3","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-5 Understanding inequalities, distinguishing linear inequalities and compound inequalities, writing and solving them, and representing their solutions on the number line.","2. Solving simple linear inequalities and inequalities that include parentheses, and representing their solutions on the number line.","Solve the compound inequality: x + 3 > 7 and x - 2 < 5.","x > 4 and x < 7","x > 4 or x < 7","x < 4 and x > 7","x > 4 or x > 7","x > 4 and x < 7","To solve the compound inequality, we need to find the intersection of the two inequalities. First, we solve x + 3 > 7, which gives x > 4. Then, we solve x - 2 < 5, which gives x < 7. Since x must be greater than 4 and less than 7, the solution is x > 4 and x < 7.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-solve-absolute-value-41068ab8","Math G9: Understanding inequalities - Set 3","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-5 Understanding inequalities, distinguishing linear inequalities and compound inequalities, writing and solving them, and representing their solutions on the number line.","3. Solving compound inequalities and inequalities that include absolute value, and representing their solutions on the number line.","Solve the absolute value inequality: |x| > 1.","x > 1 or x < -1","x > 1 or x ? -1","x < 1 or x > -1","x < 1 or x ? -1","x > 1 or x < -1","To solve the absolute value inequality, we need to find the values of x that make the absolute value greater than 1. This means that x must be greater than 1 or less than -1. Therefore, the solution is x > 1 or x < -1.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-solve-compound-inequality-031e76ab","Math G9: Understanding inequalities - Set 3","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-5 Understanding inequalities, distinguishing linear inequalities and compound inequalities, writing and solving them, and representing their solutions on the number line.","2. Solving simple linear inequalities and inequalities that include parentheses, and representing their solutions on the number line.","Solve the compound inequality: x - 4 > 2 and x + 2 < 9.","x > 6 and x < 7","x > 6 or x < 7","x < 6 and x > 7","x > 6 or x > 7","x > 6 and x < 7","To solve the compound inequality, we need to find the intersection of the two inequalities. First, we solve x - 4 > 2, which gives x > 6. Then, we solve x + 2 < 9, which gives x < 7. Since x must be greater than 6 and less than 7, the solution is x > 6 and x < 7.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-solve-absolute-value-7bbf8227","Math G9: Understanding inequalities - Set 3","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-5 Understanding inequalities, distinguishing linear inequalities and compound inequalities, writing and solving them, and representing their solutions on the number line.","3. Solving compound inequalities and inequalities that include absolute value, and representing their solutions on the number line.","Solve the absolute value inequality: |x - 2| > 1.","x - 2 < -1 or x - 2 > 1","x - 2 ≤ -1 or x - 2 ≥ 1","x - 2 > -1 or x - 2 < 1","x - 2 > -1 or x - 2 > 1","x - 2 < -1 or x - 2 > 1","To solve the absolute value inequality, we need to find the values of x that make the absolute value greater than 1. This means that x - 2 must be less than -1 or greater than 1. Therefore, the solution is x - 2 < -1 or x - 2 > 1.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-solve-compound-inequality-1b4c14b0","Math G9: Understanding inequalities - Set 3","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-5 Understanding inequalities, distinguishing linear inequalities and compound inequalities, writing and solving them, and representing their solutions on the number line.","2. Solving simple linear inequalities and inequalities that include parentheses, and representing their solutions on the number line.","Solve the compound inequality: x - 2 > 1 and x + 1 < 9.","x > 3 and x < 8","x > 3 or x < 8","x < 3 and x > 8","x > 3 or x > 8","x > 3 and x < 8","To solve the compound inequality, we need to find the intersection of the two inequalities. First, we solve x - 2 > 1, which gives x > 3. Then, we solve x + 1 < 9, which gives x < 8. Since x must be greater than 3 and less than 8, the solution is x > 3 and x < 8.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-solve-absolute-value-87faf0ff","Math G9: Understanding inequalities - Set 3","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-5 Understanding inequalities, distinguishing linear inequalities and compound inequalities, writing and solving them, and representing their solutions on the number line.","3. Solving compound inequalities and inequalities that include absolute value, and representing their solutions on the number line.","Solve the absolute value inequality: |x| < 3.","-3 < x < 3","x < -3 or x > 3","-3 ≤ x ≤ 3","x ≤ -3 or x ≥ 3","-3 < x < 3","The inequality |x| < 3 means x is less than 3 units from zero, so -3 < x < 3.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-solve-absolute-value-42cfd05c","Math G9: Understanding inequalities - Set 3","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-5 Understanding inequalities, distinguishing linear inequalities and compound inequalities, writing and solving them, and representing their solutions on the number line.","3. Solving compound inequalities and inequalities that include absolute value, and representing their solutions on the number line.","Solve the absolute value inequality: |x - 4| ≥ 2.","x - 4 ≤ -2 or x - 4 ≥ 2","-2 ≤ x - 4 ≤ 2","x - 4 < -2 or x - 4 > 2","-2 < x - 4 < 2","x - 4 ≤ -2 or x - 4 ≥ 2","For |x - 4| ≥ 2, the expression x - 4 is at most -2 or at least 2: x - 4 ≤ -2 or x - 4 ≥ 2.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-solve-compound-inequality-33e49873","Math G9: Understanding inequalities - Set 3","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-5 Understanding inequalities, distinguishing linear inequalities and compound inequalities, writing and solving them, and representing their solutions on the number line.","2. Solving simple linear inequalities and inequalities that include parentheses, and representing their solutions on the number line.","Solve the compound inequality: 2x - 5 > 3 and x + 2 < 7.","4 < x < 5","x < 4","x > 5","x ≤ 4 or x ≥ 5","4 < x < 5","Combining x > 4 and x < 5 gives 4 < x < 5.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-solve-compound-inequality-b6b158c3","Math G9: Understanding inequalities - Set 3","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-4 Writing a system of two linear equations with two variables, and solving them algebraically and graphically.","1. Distinguishing inequalities, identifying linear and compound inequalities, and writing them.","Solve the compound inequality: 2x + 1 > 5 and x - 3 < 2.","2 < x < 5","x < 2 or x > 5","2 ≤ x ≤ 5","x ≤ 2 or x ≥ 5","2 < x < 5","Combining the two strict inequalities gives 2 < x < 5.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-solve-absolute-value-ff5a6339","Math G9: Understanding inequalities - Set 3","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-5 Understanding inequalities, distinguishing linear inequalities and compound inequalities, writing and solving them, and representing their solutions on the number line.","3. Solving compound inequalities and inequalities that include absolute value, and representing their solutions on the number line.","Solve the absolute value inequality: |x + 1| ≤ 2.","-3 ≤ x ≤ 1","x ≤ -3 or x ≥ 1","-1 ≤ x ≤ 3","x < -3 or x > 1","-3 ≤ x ≤ 1","For |x + 1| ≤ 2, write -2 ≤ x + 1 ≤ 2, then subtract 1 to get -3 ≤ x ≤ 1.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-arrange-integers-in-d58595ed","Math G9: Understanding integers and rational numbers - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-1-1-1 Understanding integers and rational numbers, reading, writing, representing, comparing, and ordering them.","2. Comparing integers numbers and ordering them in ascending and descending order.","Arrange the following integers in ascending order: 2, -3, 0, 1","-3, 0, 1, 2","2, 0, 1, -3","0, -3, 1, 2","1, 0, -3, 2","-3, 0, 1, 2","The correct order is -3, 0, 1, 2.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-arrange-integers-in-d58595ed.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-of-integers-is-7c7b4217","Math G9: Understanding integers and rational numbers - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-1-2-3 Finding the ratio, unit rate, percentage, estimating them, and distinguishing proportional relationships, solving proportions, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. Finding percentages of a number (including percentages greater than 100%) and estimating them using Proper fractions, compatible numbers, or rounding.","Which of the following integers is greater than -2?","-1 is greater than -2","-3 is greater than -2","-2 is greater than -1","0 is greater than -2","-1 is greater than -2","Since -1 is greater than -2, the correct answer is A.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-of-integers-is-7c7b4217.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-arrange-integers-in-8c63d2ae","Math G9: Understanding integers and rational numbers - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-1-1-1 Understanding integers and rational numbers, reading, writing, representing, comparing, and ordering them.","2. Comparing integers numbers and ordering them in ascending and descending order.","Arrange the following integers in descending order: 4, -2, 0, 1","4, 1, 0, -2","1, 0, 4, -2","0, -2, 1, 4","-2, 0, 1, 4","4, 1, 0, -2","The correct order is 4, 1, 0, -2.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-arrange-integers-in-8c63d2ae.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-of-integers-is-2caa0371","Math G9: Understanding integers and rational numbers - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-1-1-1 Understanding integers and rational numbers, reading, writing, representing, comparing, and ordering them.","2. Comparing integers numbers and ordering them in ascending and descending order.","Which number is greater than -3?","-4","-5","-2","-6","-2","Among these negative integers, -2 is greatest because it is closest to zero.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-of-integers-is-2caa0371.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-of-integers-is-8b199594","Math G9: Understanding integers and rational numbers - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-1-1-1 Understanding integers and rational numbers, reading, writing, representing, comparing, and ordering them.","2. Comparing integers numbers and ordering them in ascending and descending order.","Which of the following integers is less than 0?","-1 is less than 0","1 is less than 0","0 is less than -1","2 is less than 0","-1 is less than 0","Since -1 is less than 0, the correct answer is A.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-of-integers-is-8b199594.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-arrange-integers-in-f7d6216c","Math G9: Understanding integers and rational numbers - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-1-1-1 Understanding integers and rational numbers, reading, writing, representing, comparing, and ordering them.","2. Comparing integers numbers and ordering them in ascending and descending order.","Arrange the following integers in ascending order: -3, 2, 0, -1","-3, -1, 0, 2","2, 0, -1, -3","0, -1, -3, 2","-1, 0, -3, 2","-3, -1, 0, 2","The correct order is -3, -1, 0, 2.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-arrange-integers-in-f7d6216c.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-of-integers-is-c8a85dca","Math G9: Understanding integers and rational numbers - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-1-2-3 Finding the ratio, unit rate, percentage, estimating them, and distinguishing proportional relationships, solving proportions, and solving mathematical problems including real-life applications.","3. Finding percentages of a number (including percentages greater than 100%) and estimating them using Proper fractions, compatible numbers, or rounding.","Which of the following integers is greater than 2?","1 is greater than 2","3 is greater than 2","2 is greater than 1","0 is greater than 2","3 is greater than 2","Since 3 is greater than 2, the correct answer is B.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-of-integers-is-c8a85dca.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-arrange-integers-in-1a35c5b8","Math G9: Understanding integers and rational numbers - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-1-1-1 Understanding integers and rational numbers, reading, writing, representing, comparing, and ordering them.","2. Comparing integers numbers and ordering them in ascending and descending order.","Arrange the following integers in descending order: -1, 3, 0, -4","-4, -1, 0, 3","3, 0, -1, -4","0, -1, -4, 3","-4, 0, -1, 3","3, 0, -1, -4","In descending order, the numbers are 3, 0, -1, -4.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-arrange-integers-in-1a35c5b8.png","",""
