"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-absolute-value-b912d09f","Math G9: Understanding integers and rational numbers - Set 3","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-1-1-1 Understanding integers and rational numbers, reading, writing, representing, comparing, and ordering them.","3. Finding the absolute value of an integer  number and representing it on the number line.","What is the absolute value of -3?","The value is negative 3","The value is 3","The value is -3","The value is zero","The value is 3","The absolute value of a number is its distance from zero on the number line, which is always positive.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-of-is-absolute-a224de26","Math G9: Understanding integers and rational numbers - Set 3","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-1-1-1 Understanding integers and rational numbers, reading, writing, representing, comparing, and ordering them.","3. Finding the absolute value of an integer  number and representing it on the number line.","Which of the following is the absolute value of 1?","The value is 0","The value is 1","The value is -1","The value is 2","The value is 1","The absolute value of a number is its distance from zero on the number line, which is always positive.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-find-square-root-13ea5629","Math G9: Understanding real numbers, classifying, comparing, and - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-1-1-2 Understanding real numbers, classifying, comparing, and  Ordering them.","1. Finding the square root of a number and approximating it to the nearest whole number or to the nearest decimal place, by using a calculator and without using one.","Find the square root of 81 and approximate it to the nearest whole number.","The square root of 81 is 9.000 and approximates to 9.","The square root of 81 is 8.000 and approximates to 8.","The square root of 81 is 9.000 and approximates to 9.0.","The square root of 81 is 10.000 and approximates to 10.","The square root of 81 is 9.000 and approximates to 9.","The square root of 81 is 9, and when approximated to the nearest whole number, it remains 9.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-approximate-square-root-3b1f38e6","Math G9: Understanding real numbers, classifying, comparing, and - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-1-1-2 Understanding real numbers, classifying, comparing, and  Ordering them.","1. Finding the square root of a number and approximating it to the nearest whole number or to the nearest decimal place, by using a calculator and without using one.","Approximate the square root of 64 to the nearest decimal place.","The square root of 64 is 8.000 and approximates to 8.","The square root of 64 is 7.937 and approximates to 7.9.","The square root of 64 is 8.000 and approximates to 8.0.","The square root of 64 is 7.000 and approximates to 7.","The square root of 64 is 8.000 and approximates to 8.0.","The square root of 64 is 8, and when approximated to the nearest decimal place, it remains 8.0.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-approximate-square-root-59f546f0","Math G9: Understanding real numbers, classifying, comparing, and - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-1-1-2 Understanding real numbers, classifying, comparing, and  Ordering them.","1. Finding the square root of a number and approximating it to the nearest whole number or to the nearest decimal place, by using a calculator and without using one.","Approximate the square root of 16 to the nearest decimal place.","The square root of 16 is 4.000 and approximates to 4.","The square root of 16 is 3.000 and approximates to 3.","The square root of 16 is 4.000 and approximates to 4.0.","The square root of 16 is 5.000 and approximates to 5.","The square root of 16 is 4.000 and approximates to 4.0.","The square root of 16 is 4, and when approximated to the nearest decimal place, it remains 4.0.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-find-square-root-6581f2e8","Math G9: Understanding real numbers, classifying, comparing, and - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-1-1-2 Understanding real numbers, classifying, comparing, and  Ordering them.","1. Finding the square root of a number and approximating it to the nearest whole number or to the nearest decimal place, by using a calculator and without using one.","Find the square root of 25 and approximate it to the nearest whole number.","The square root of 25 is 4.999 and approximates to 5.","The square root of 25 is 5.000 and approximates to 5.","The square root of 25 is 4.000 and approximates to 4.","The square root of 25 is 6.000 and approximates to 6.","The square root of 25 is 5.000 and approximates to 5.","The square root of 25 is 5, and when approximated to the nearest whole number, it remains 5.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-approximate-square-root-5688fd81","Math G9: Understanding real numbers, classifying, comparing, and - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-1-1-2 Understanding real numbers, classifying, comparing, and  Ordering them.","1. Finding the square root of a number and approximating it to the nearest whole number or to the nearest decimal place, by using a calculator and without using one.","Approximate the square root of 0.25 to the nearest decimal place.","The square root of 0.25 is 0.500 and approximates to 0.5.","The square root of 0.25 is 0.000 and approximates to 0.","The square root of 0.25 is 0.500 and approximates to 0.50.","The square root of 0.25 is 1.000 and approximates to 1.","The square root of 0.25 is 0.500 and approximates to 0.5.","The square root of 0.25 is 0.5, and when approximated to the nearest decimal place, it remains 0.5.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-find-square-root-efe85219","Math G9: Understanding real numbers, classifying, comparing, and - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-1-1-2 Understanding real numbers, classifying, comparing, and  Ordering them.","1. Finding the square root of a number and approximating it to the nearest whole number or to the nearest decimal place, by using a calculator and without using one.","Find the square root of 1 and approximate it to the nearest whole number.","The square root of 1 is 1.000 and approximates to 1.","The square root of 1 is 0.000 and approximates to 0.","The square root of 1 is 1.000 and approximates to 1.0.","The square root of 1 is 2.000 and approximates to 2.","The square root of 1 is 1.000 and approximates to 1.","The square root of 1 is 1, and when approximated to the nearest whole number, it remains 1.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-find-square-root-b2ea591c","Math G9: Understanding real numbers, classifying, comparing, and - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-1-1-2 Understanding real numbers, classifying, comparing, and  Ordering them.","1. Finding the square root of a number and approximating it to the nearest whole number or to the nearest decimal place, by using a calculator and without using one.","Find the square root of 36 and approximate it to the nearest whole number.","The square root of 36 is 6.000 and approximates to 6.","The square root of 36 is 5.000 and approximates to 5.","The square root of 36 is 6.000 and approximates to 6.0.","The square root of 36 is 7.000 and approximates to 7.","The square root of 36 is 6.000 and approximates to 6.","The square root of 36 is 6, and when approximated to the nearest whole number, it remains 6.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-approximate-square-root-c2f05d2c","Math G9: Understanding real numbers, classifying, comparing, and - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-1-1-2 Understanding real numbers, classifying, comparing, and  Ordering them.","1. Finding the square root of a number and approximating it to the nearest whole number or to the nearest decimal place, by using a calculator and without using one.","Approximate the square root of 49 to the nearest decimal place.","The square root of 49 is 7.000 and approximates to 7.","The square root of 49 is 6.000 and approximates to 6.","The square root of 49 is 7.000 and approximates to 7.0.","The square root of 49 is 8.000 and approximates to 8.","The square root of 49 is 7.000 and approximates to 7.0.","The square root of 49 is 7, and when approximated to the nearest decimal place, it remains 7.0.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-approximate-square-root-b8c2afb6","Math G9: Understanding real numbers, classifying, comparing, and - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-1-1-2 Understanding real numbers, classifying, comparing, and  Ordering them.","1. Finding the square root of a number and approximating it to the nearest whole number or to the nearest decimal place, by using a calculator and without using one.","Approximate the square root of 4 to the nearest decimal place.","The square root of 4 is 2.000 and approximates to 2.","The square root of 4 is 1.000 and approximates to 1.","The square root of 4 is 2.000 and approximates to 2.0.","The square root of 4 is 3.000 and approximates to 3.","The square root of 4 is 2.000 and approximates to 2.0.","The square root of 4 is 2, and when approximated to the nearest decimal place, it remains 2.0.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-find-square-root-cb2552ca","Math G9: Understanding real numbers, classifying, comparing, and - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-1-1-2 Understanding real numbers, classifying, comparing, and  Ordering them.","1. Finding the square root of a number and approximating it to the nearest whole number or to the nearest decimal place, by using a calculator and without using one.","Find the square root of 9 and approximate it to the nearest whole number.","The square root of 9 is 3.000 and approximates to 3.","The square root of 9 is 2.000 and approximates to 2.","The square root of 9 is 3.000 and approximates to 3.0.","The square root of 9 is 4.000 and approximates to 4.","The square root of 9 is 3.000 and approximates to 3.","The square root of 9 is 3, and when approximated to the nearest whole number, it remains 3.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-result-of-5f92b0dc","Math G9: Writing algebraic expressions with rational coefficients - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-1 Writing algebraic expressions with rational coefficients, finding their values, performing arithmetic operations on them, and Distinguishing basic identities.","3. Distinguishing the basic identities: the square of a sum of two terms, the square of a difference of two terms, and the product of the sum and difference of two terms, and using them.","What is the result of (2x + 1)(2x - 1)?","4x^2 + 4x + 1","4x^2 - 4x + 1","4x^2 + 4x - 1","4x^2 - 4x - 1","4x^2 - 4x - 1","The product of the sum and difference of two terms is (a + b)(a - b) = a^2 - b^2.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-result-of-393b2e3b","Math G9: Writing algebraic expressions with rational coefficients - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-1 Writing algebraic expressions with rational coefficients, finding their values, performing arithmetic operations on them, and Distinguishing basic identities.","3. Distinguishing the basic identities: the square of a sum of two terms, the square of a difference of two terms, and the product of the sum and difference of two terms, and using them.","What is the result of (2x + 1)^2?","4x^2 + 4x + 1","4x^2 - 4x + 1","4x^2 + 4x - 1","4x^2 - 4x - 1","4x^2 + 4x + 1","The square of a sum of two terms is (a + b)^2 = a^2 + 2ab + b^2.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-result-of-8955e4ff","Math G9: Writing algebraic expressions with rational coefficients - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-1 Writing algebraic expressions with rational coefficients, finding their values, performing arithmetic operations on them, and Distinguishing basic identities.","3. Distinguishing the basic identities: the square of a sum of two terms, the square of a difference of two terms, and the product of the sum and difference of two terms, and using them.","What is the result of (x + 3)(x - 3)?","x^2 + 6x + 9","x^2 - 6x + 9","x^2 + 6x - 9","x^2 - 6x - 9","x^2 - 6x - 9","The product of the sum and difference of two terms is (a + b)(a - b) = a^2 - b^2.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-result-of-019c6b63","Math G9: Writing algebraic expressions with rational coefficients - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-1 Writing algebraic expressions with rational coefficients, finding their values, performing arithmetic operations on them, and Distinguishing basic identities.","3. Distinguishing the basic identities: the square of a sum of two terms, the square of a difference of two terms, and the product of the sum and difference of two terms, and using them.","What is the result of (x - 3)^2?","x^2 + 6x + 9","x^2 - 6x + 9","x^2 + 6x - 9","x^2 - 6x - 9","x^2 + 6x - 9","The square of a difference of two terms is (a - b)^2 = a^2 - 2ab + b^2.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-result-of-8987bb9c","Math G9: Writing algebraic expressions with rational coefficients - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-1 Writing algebraic expressions with rational coefficients, finding their values, performing arithmetic operations on them, and Distinguishing basic identities.","3. Distinguishing the basic identities: the square of a sum of two terms, the square of a difference of two terms, and the product of the sum and difference of two terms, and using them.","What is the result of (x + 3)^2?","x^2 + 6x + 9","x^2 - 6x + 9","x^2 + 6x - 9","x^2 - 6x - 9","x^2 + 6x + 9","The square of a sum of two terms is (a + b)^2 = a^2 + 2ab + b^2.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-result-of-10ca806b","Math G9: Writing algebraic expressions with rational coefficients - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-1 Writing algebraic expressions with rational coefficients, finding their values, performing arithmetic operations on them, and Distinguishing basic identities.","3. Distinguishing the basic identities: the square of a sum of two terms, the square of a difference of two terms, and the product of the sum and difference of two terms, and using them.","What is the result of (x + 2)(x - 2)?","x^2 + 4x + 4","x^2 - 4x + 4","x^2 + 4x - 4","x^2 - 4x - 4","x^2 - 4x - 4","The product of the sum and difference of two terms is (a + b)(a - b) = a^2 - b^2.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-result-of-3f6e3ac7","Math G9: Writing algebraic expressions with rational coefficients - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-1 Writing algebraic expressions with rational coefficients, finding their values, performing arithmetic operations on them, and Distinguishing basic identities.","3. Distinguishing the basic identities: the square of a sum of two terms, the square of a difference of two terms, and the product of the sum and difference of two terms, and using them.","What is the result of (x - 2)^2?","x^2 + 4x + 4","x^2 - 4x + 4","x^2 + 4x - 4","x^2 - 4x - 4","x^2 + 4x - 4","The square of a difference of two terms is (a - b)^2 = a^2 - 2ab + b^2.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-result-of-d33462be","Math G9: Writing algebraic expressions with rational coefficients - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-1 Writing algebraic expressions with rational coefficients, finding their values, performing arithmetic operations on them, and Distinguishing basic identities.","3. Distinguishing the basic identities: the square of a sum of two terms, the square of a difference of two terms, and the product of the sum and difference of two terms, and using them.","What is the result of (x + 2)^2?","x^2 + 4x + 4","x^2 - 4x + 4","x^2 + 4x - 4","x^2 - 4x - 4","x^2 + 4x + 4","The square of a sum of two terms is (a + b)^2 = a^2 + 2ab + b^2.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-result-of-a4e0d3ec","Math G9: Writing algebraic expressions with rational coefficients - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-1 Writing algebraic expressions with rational coefficients, finding their values, performing arithmetic operations on them, and Distinguishing basic identities.","3. Distinguishing the basic identities: the square of a sum of two terms, the square of a difference of two terms, and the product of the sum and difference of two terms, and using them.","What is the result of (2x - 1)^2?","4x^2 + 4x + 1","4x^2 - 4x + 1","4x^2 + 4x - 1","4x^2 - 4x - 1","4x^2 + 4x - 1","The square of a difference of two terms is (a - b)^2 = a^2 - 2ab + b^2.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-result-of-b6eeec4b","Math G9: Writing algebraic expressions with rational coefficients - Set 3","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-1 Writing algebraic expressions with rational coefficients, finding their values, performing arithmetic operations on them, and Distinguishing basic identities.","3. Distinguishing the basic identities: the square of a sum of two terms, the square of a difference of two terms, and the product of the sum and difference of two terms, and using them.","What is the result of (x + 3)(x + 3)?","x^2 + 6x + 9","x^2 + 6x - 9","x^2 - 6x + 9","x^2 - 6x - 9","x^2 + 6x + 9","The result of (x + 3)(x + 3) is the square of the sum of x and 3, which is (x + 3)^2 = x^2 + 6x + 9.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-result-of-ed896d09","Math G9: Writing algebraic expressions with rational coefficients - Set 3","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-1 Writing algebraic expressions with rational coefficients, finding their values, performing arithmetic operations on them, and Distinguishing basic identities.","2. Performing the four operations on algebraic expressions and simplifying them.","What is the result of (x - 3)(x - 3)?","x^2 + 6x + 9","x^2 + 6x - 9","x^2 - 6x + 9","x^2 - 6x - 9","x^2 - 6x + 9","Using (a-b)², (x - 3)² = x² - 6x + 9.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-result-of-4194b92b","Math G9: Writing algebraic expressions with rational coefficients - Set 3","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-1 Writing algebraic expressions with rational coefficients, finding their values, performing arithmetic operations on them, and Distinguishing basic identities.","3. Distinguishing the basic identities: the square of a sum of two terms, the square of a difference of two terms, and the product of the sum and difference of two terms, and using them.","What is the result of (x + 1)(x - 1)?","x² - 1","x² + 1","x² - 2x + 1","x² + 2x + 1","x² - 1","The product of conjugates is (x + 1)(x - 1) = x² - 1.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-result-of-1f03db3b","Math G9: Writing algebraic expressions with rational coefficients - Set 3","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-1 Writing algebraic expressions with rational coefficients, finding their values, performing arithmetic operations on them, and Distinguishing basic identities.","2. Performing the four operations on algebraic expressions and simplifying them.","What is the result of (x - 2)(x - 2)?","x^2 + 4x + 4","x^2 + 4x - 4","x^2 - 4x + 4","x^2 - 4x - 4","x^2 - 4x + 4","(x - 2)² = x² - 4x + 4.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-result-of-9af8dcde","Math G9: Writing algebraic expressions with rational coefficients - Set 3","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-1 Writing algebraic expressions with rational coefficients, finding their values, performing arithmetic operations on them, and Distinguishing basic identities.","3. Distinguishing the basic identities: the square of a sum of two terms, the square of a difference of two terms, and the product of the sum and difference of two terms, and using them.","What is the result of (x + 4)(x + 4)?","x^2 + 8x + 16","x^2 + 8x - 16","x^2 - 8x + 16","x^2 - 8x - 16","x^2 + 8x + 16","The result of (x + 4)(x + 4) is the square of the sum of x and 4, which is (x + 4)^2 = x^2 + 8x + 16.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-result-of-17237372","Math G9: Writing algebraic expressions with rational coefficients - Set 3","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-1 Writing algebraic expressions with rational coefficients, finding their values, performing arithmetic operations on them, and Distinguishing basic identities.","2. Performing the four operations on algebraic expressions and simplifying them.","What is the result of (x - 1)(x - 1)?","x^2 + 2x + 1","x^2 + 2x - 1","x^2 - 2x + 1","x^2 - 2x - 1","x^2 - 2x + 1","(x - 1)² = x² - 2x + 1.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-result-of-c33c35b1","Math G9: Writing algebraic expressions with rational coefficients - Set 3","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-1 Writing algebraic expressions with rational coefficients, finding their values, performing arithmetic operations on them, and Distinguishing basic identities.","3. Distinguishing the basic identities: the square of a sum of two terms, the square of a difference of two terms, and the product of the sum and difference of two terms, and using them.","What is the result of (x + 6)(x + 6)?","x^2 + 12x + 36","x^2 + 12x - 36","x^2 - 12x + 36","x^2 - 12x - 36","x^2 + 12x + 36","The result of (x + 6)(x + 6) is the square of the sum of x and 6, which is (x + 6)^2 = x^2 + 12x + 36.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-result-of-b556048d","Math G9: Writing algebraic expressions with rational coefficients - Set 3","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-1 Writing algebraic expressions with rational coefficients, finding their values, performing arithmetic operations on them, and Distinguishing basic identities.","3. Distinguishing the basic identities: the square of a sum of two terms, the square of a difference of two terms, and the product of the sum and difference of two terms, and using them.","What is the result of (x + 5)(x + 5)?","x^2 + 10x + 25","x^2 + 10x - 25","x^2 - 10x + 25","x^2 - 10x - 25","x^2 + 10x + 25","The result of (x + 5)(x + 5) is the square of the sum of x and 5, which is (x + 5)^2 = x^2 + 10x + 25.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-result-of-dbf48e3b","Math G9: Writing algebraic expressions with rational coefficients - Set 3","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-1 Writing algebraic expressions with rational coefficients, finding their values, performing arithmetic operations on them, and Distinguishing basic identities.","3. Distinguishing the basic identities: the square of a sum of two terms, the square of a difference of two terms, and the product of the sum and difference of two terms, and using them.","What is the result of (x + 2)(x - 2)?","x² - 4","x² + 4","x² - 4x + 4","x² + 4x + 4","x² - 4","The product of conjugates is (x + 2)(x - 2) = x² - 4.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-result-of-d6bc9b35","Math G9: Writing algebraic expressions with rational coefficients - Set 3","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-1 Writing algebraic expressions with rational coefficients, finding their values, performing arithmetic operations on them, and Distinguishing basic identities.","2. Performing the four operations on algebraic expressions and simplifying them.","What is the result of (x - 4)(x - 4)?","x^2 + 8x + 16","x^2 + 8x - 16","x^2 - 8x + 16","x^2 - 8x - 16","x^2 - 8x + 16","Using (a-b)² = a² - 2ab + b², (x - 4)² = x² - 8x + 16.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-write-inequality-x-b381e296","Math G9: Writing a system of two linear equations with two variables - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-4 Writing a system of two linear equations with two variables, and solving them algebraically and graphically.","1. Distinguishing inequalities, identifying linear and compound inequalities, and writing them.","Write the inequality x - 3 > 5 in slope-intercept form.","y = x + 8","y = x - 8","y = -x + 8","y = x - 2","y = x - 8","To write the inequality in slope-intercept form, we need to isolate y. We can do this by adding 3 to both sides of the inequality, which gives us x > 8. Then, we can rewrite the inequality as y = x - 8.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-write-inequality-x-b381e296.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-solve-system-of-326e9799","Math G9: Writing a system of two linear equations with two variables - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-4 Writing a system of two linear equations with two variables, and solving them algebraically and graphically.","1. Distinguishing inequalities, identifying linear and compound inequalities, and writing them.","Solve the system of linear equations: 2x + 3y = 6 and x - 2y = -3.","x = 3/7, y = 1 5/7","x = 1 3/7, y = 1 5/7","x = 3/7, y = 2 5/7","x = 1 5/7, y = 3/7","x = 3/7, y = 1 5/7","Solving the two equations gives x = 3/7, y = 1 5/7.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-solve-system-of-121628f6","Math G9: Writing a system of two linear equations with two variables - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-4 Writing a system of two linear equations with two variables, and solving them algebraically and graphically.","1. Distinguishing inequalities, identifying linear and compound inequalities, and writing them.","Solve the system of linear equations: x + y = 3 and x - y = 1.","x = 2, y = 1","x = 3, y = 1","x = 2, y = 2","x = 1, y = 2","x = 2, y = 1","Solving the two equations gives x = 2, y = 1.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-write-linear-inequality-3ba301db","Math G9: Writing a system of two linear equations with two variables - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-4 Writing a system of two linear equations with two variables, and solving them algebraically and graphically.","1. Distinguishing inequalities, identifying linear and compound inequalities, and writing them.","Write the linear inequality 3x + 2y > 6 in slope-intercept form.","y > -3/2x + 3","y < -3/2x + 3","y > 3/2x - 3","y < 3/2x - 3","y > -3/2x + 3","To write the inequality in slope-intercept form, we need to isolate y. We can do this by subtracting 3x from both sides of the inequality, which gives us 2y > -3x + 6. Then, we can divide both sides of the inequality by 2, which gives us y > -3/2x + 3.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-write-linear-inequality-3ba301db.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-graph-linear-inequality-8c5b5b6c","Math G9: Writing a system of two linear equations with two variables - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-4 Writing a system of two linear equations with two variables, and solving them algebraically and graphically.","1. Distinguishing inequalities, identifying linear and compound inequalities, and writing them.","Graph the linear inequality x - 2y > 3.","The region above the line x - 2y = 3","The region below the line x - 2y = 3","The region above the line x - 2y = -3","The region below the line x - 2y = -3","The region above the line x - 2y = 3","To graph the inequality, we need to first graph the line x - 2y = 3. Then, we need to test a point in each region to see which region satisfies the inequality. We can choose the point (0, 0) and substitute it into the inequality. This gives us 0 - 2(0) > 3, which is false. Therefore, the region above the line x - 2y = 3 satisfies the inequality.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-graph-linear-inequality-8c5b5b6c.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-solve-system-of-a7406e04","Math G9: Writing a system of two linear equations with two variables - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-4 Writing a system of two linear equations with two variables, and solving them algebraically and graphically.","1. Distinguishing inequalities, identifying linear and compound inequalities, and writing them.","Solve the system of linear equations: x + y = 4 and x - y = 2.","x = 3, y = 1","x = 4, y = 1","x = 3, y = 2","x = 1, y = 3","x = 3, y = 1","Solving the two equations gives x = 3, y = 1.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-write-compound-inequality-f90001ed","Math G9: Writing a system of two linear equations with two variables - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-4 Writing a system of two linear equations with two variables, and solving them algebraically and graphically.","1. Distinguishing inequalities, identifying linear and compound inequalities, and writing them.","Write the compound inequality 2x - 5 > 3 and 2x - 5 < 7 as a double inequality.","2x - 5 > 3 and 2x - 5 < 7","2x - 5 < 3 and 2x - 5 > 7","8 < 2x - 5 < 12","2x - 5 < 3 or 2x - 5 > 7","8 < 2x - 5 < 12","To write the compound inequality as a double inequality, we need to combine the two inequalities into a single inequality. We can do this by finding the intersection of the two inequalities. The first inequality is 2x - 5 > 3, which simplifies to 2x > 8. The second inequality is 2x - 5 < 7, which simplifies to 2x < 12. Therefore, the double inequality is 8 < 2x - 5 < 12.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-graph-linear-inequality-b4ac8e6d","Math G9: Writing a system of two linear equations with two variables - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-4 Writing a system of two linear equations with two variables, and solving them algebraically and graphically.","1. Distinguishing inequalities, identifying linear and compound inequalities, and writing them.","Graph the linear inequality 2x + 3y < 6.","The region below the line 2x + 3y = 6","The region above the line 2x + 3y = 6","The region below the line 2x + 3y = -6","The region above the line 2x + 3y = -6","The region above the line 2x + 3y = 6","To graph the inequality, we need to first graph the line 2x + 3y = 6. Then, we need to test a point in each region to see which region satisfies the inequality. We can choose the point (0, 0) and substitute it into the inequality. This gives us 2(0) + 3(0) < 6, which is true. Therefore, the region above the line 2x + 3y = 6 satisfies the inequality.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-graph-linear-inequality-b4ac8e6d.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-graph-linear-inequality-e7120f72","Math G9: Writing a system of two linear equations with two variables - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-4 Writing a system of two linear equations with two variables, and solving them algebraically and graphically.","1. Distinguishing inequalities, identifying linear and compound inequalities, and writing them.","Graph the linear inequality 2x - 3y < 6.","The region below the line 2x - 3y = 6","The region above the line 2x - 3y = 6","The region below the line 2x - 3y = -6","The region above the line 2x - 3y = -6","The region below the line 2x - 3y = 6","To graph the inequality, we need to first graph the line 2x - 3y = 6. Then, we need to test a point in each region to see which region satisfies the inequality. We can choose the point (0, 0) and substitute it into the inequality. This gives us 2(0) - 3(0) < 6, which is true. Therefore, the region below the line 2x - 3y = 6 satisfies the inequality.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-graph-linear-inequality-e7120f72.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-solve-system-of-fecd3676","Math G9: Writing a system of two linear equations with two variables - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-4 Writing a system of two linear equations with two variables, and solving them algebraically and graphically.","1. Distinguishing inequalities, identifying linear and compound inequalities, and writing them.","Solve the system of linear equations: x + 2y = 4 and 3x - 2y = 2.","x = 1 1/2, y = 1 1/4","x = 2 1/2, y = 1 1/4","x = 1 1/2, y = 2 1/4","x = 1 1/4, y = 1 1/2","x = 1 1/2, y = 1 1/4","Solving the two equations gives x = 1 1/2, y = 1 1/4.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-term-a-2f3e60e4","Math G9: Writing a system of two linear equations with two variables - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-4 Writing a system of two linear equations with two variables, and solving them algebraically and graphically.","2. Solving a system of two linear equations with two variables graphically, and using graphical representations to distinguish between consistent and inconsistent systems, and between independent and dependent systems.","What is the term for a system of two linear equations with two variables where the two lines intersect at exactly one point?","Consistent and independent","Consistent and dependent","Inconsistent","Nonlinear","Consistent and independent","A system with exactly one solution is consistent and independent.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-term-a-7db143d6","Math G9: Writing a system of two linear equations with two variables - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-4 Writing a system of two linear equations with two variables, and solving them algebraically and graphically.","2. Solving a system of two linear equations with two variables graphically, and using graphical representations to distinguish between consistent and inconsistent systems, and between independent and dependent systems.","What is the term for a system of two linear equations with two variables where the two lines are the same line?","Consistent system","Inconsistent system","Independent system","Dependent system","Dependent system","A dependent system is one where the two equations have infinitely many common solutions, which is the case when the lines are the same line.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-system-of-593e9a66","Math G9: Writing a system of two linear equations with two variables - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-4 Writing a system of two linear equations with two variables, and solving them algebraically and graphically.","1. Distinguishing inequalities, identifying linear and compound inequalities, and writing them.","A system of two linear equations with two variables is graphically represented by two lines. What is the result if the two lines are the same line?","The system has exactly one solution.","The system has infinitely many solutions.","The system has no solution.","The lines are parallel and never intersect.","The system has infinitely many solutions.","When two lines are the same line, it means that the system of equations has infinitely many solutions, as every point on the line satisfies both equations.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-system-of-593e9a66.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-term-a-3f2fe4a7","Math G9: Writing a system of two linear equations with two variables - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-4 Writing a system of two linear equations with two variables, and solving them algebraically and graphically.","2. Solving a system of two linear equations with two variables graphically, and using graphical representations to distinguish between consistent and inconsistent systems, and between independent and dependent systems.","What is the term for a system of two linear equations with two variables where the two lines are parallel and never intersect?","Consistent system","Inconsistent system","Independent system","Dependent system","Inconsistent system","An inconsistent system is one where the two equations have no common solution, which is the case when the lines are parallel and never intersect.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-term-a-5e771ffc","Math G9: Writing a system of two linear equations with two variables - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-4 Writing a system of two linear equations with two variables, and solving them algebraically and graphically.","2. Solving a system of two linear equations with two variables graphically, and using graphical representations to distinguish between consistent and inconsistent systems, and between independent and dependent systems.","What is the term for a system of two linear equations with two variables where the two lines are perpendicular to each other?","Consistent system","Inconsistent system","Independent system","Dependent system","Dependent system","A dependent system is one where the two equations have infinitely many common solutions, which is the case when the lines are perpendicular to each other.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-system-of-e4b2749a","Math G9: Writing a system of two linear equations with two variables - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-4 Writing a system of two linear equations with two variables, and solving them algebraically and graphically.","1. Distinguishing inequalities, identifying linear and compound inequalities, and writing them.","A system of two linear equations with two variables is graphically represented by two lines. What is the result if the two lines are perpendicular to each other?","The system has exactly one solution.","The system has infinitely many solutions.","The system has no solution.","The lines are parallel and never intersect.","The system has infinitely many solutions.","When two lines are perpendicular to each other, it means that the system of equations has infinitely many solutions, as every point on the lines satisfies both equations.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-system-of-e4b2749a.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-term-a-3961cb3c","Math G9: Writing a system of two linear equations with two variables - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-4 Writing a system of two linear equations with two variables, and solving them algebraically and graphically.","2. Solving a system of two linear equations with two variables graphically, and using graphical representations to distinguish between consistent and inconsistent systems, and between independent and dependent systems.","What is the term for a system of two linear equations with two variables where the two lines intersect at more than one point?","Consistent system","Inconsistent system","Independent system","Dependent system","Dependent system","A dependent system is one where the two equations have infinitely many common solutions, which is the case when the lines intersect at more than one point.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-system-of-e515ab1c","Math G9: Writing a system of two linear equations with two variables - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-4 Writing a system of two linear equations with two variables, and solving them algebraically and graphically.","1. Distinguishing inequalities, identifying linear and compound inequalities, and writing them.","A system of two linear equations with two variables is graphically represented by two lines. What is the result if the two lines intersect at more than one point?","The system has exactly one solution.","The system has infinitely many solutions.","The system has no solution.","The lines are parallel and never intersect.","The system has infinitely many solutions.","When two lines intersect at more than one point, it means that the system of equations has infinitely many solutions, as every point of intersection satisfies both equations.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-system-of-e515ab1c.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-system-of-c7eded6d","Math G9: Writing a system of two linear equations with two variables - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-4 Writing a system of two linear equations with two variables, and solving them algebraically and graphically.","1. Distinguishing inequalities, identifying linear and compound inequalities, and writing them.","A system of two linear equations with two variables is graphically represented by two lines. What is the result if the two lines are parallel and never intersect?","The system has exactly one solution.","The system has infinitely many solutions.","The system has no solution.","The lines intersect at exactly one point.","The lines intersect at exactly one point.","When two lines are parallel and never intersect, it means that the system of equations has no solution, as there is no point that satisfies both equations.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-system-of-c7eded6d.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-system-of-8dbf6bb5","Math G9: Writing a system of two linear equations with two variables - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-4 Writing a system of two linear equations with two variables, and solving them algebraically and graphically.","1. Distinguishing inequalities, identifying linear and compound inequalities, and writing them.","A system of two linear equations with two variables is graphically represented by two lines. What is the result if the two lines intersect at exactly one point?","The system has no solution.","The system has infinitely many solutions.","The system has exactly one solution.","The lines are parallel and never intersect.","The system has exactly one solution.","When two lines intersect at exactly one point, it means that the system of equations has a unique solution, which is the point of intersection.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-system-of-8dbf6bb5.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-of-is-a-ed6a69a0","Math G9: Writing a system of two linear - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-4 Writing a system of two linear equations with two variables, and solving them algebraically and graphically.","1. Distinguishing inequalities, identifying linear and compound inequalities, and writing them.","Which of the following is a linear inequality?","x^2 + 2x - 3 > 0","2x - 5 < 3","x^3 - 2x^2 + x + 1 = 0","x - 2 > 0","x - 2 > 0","A linear inequality is an inequality that can be written in the form ax + b > c, ax + b < c, ax + b ? c, or ax + b ? c, where a, b, and c are constants.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-solve-system-of-df502807","Math G9: Writing a system of two linear - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-4 Writing a system of two linear equations with two variables, and solving them algebraically and graphically.","1. Distinguishing inequalities, identifying linear and compound inequalities, and writing them.","Solve the system of linear equations: x - 2y = -3 and 2x + 3y = 7.","x = 5/7, y = 1 6/7","x = 1 5/7, y = 1 6/7","x = 5/7, y = 2 6/7","x = 1 6/7, y = 5/7","x = 5/7, y = 1 6/7","Solving the two equations gives x = 5/7, y = 1 6/7.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-solve-system-of-7f51a6f1","Math G9: Writing a system of two linear - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-4 Writing a system of two linear equations with two variables, and solving them algebraically and graphically.","1. Distinguishing inequalities, identifying linear and compound inequalities, and writing them.","Solve the system of linear equations: x + y = 4 and x - y = 2.","x = 3, y = 1","x = 4, y = 1","x = 3, y = 2","x = 1, y = 3","x = 3, y = 1","Solving the two equations gives x = 3, y = 1.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-of-is-a-df016de1","Math G9: Writing a system of two linear - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-4 Writing a system of two linear equations with two variables, and solving them algebraically and graphically.","1. Distinguishing inequalities, identifying linear and compound inequalities, and writing them.","Which of the following is a linear inequality?","x^2 + 2x - 3 > 0","2x - 5 < 3","x^3 - 2x^2 + x + 1 = 0","x - 2 > 0","x - 2 > 0","A linear inequality is an inequality that can be written in the form ax + b > c, ax + b < c, ax + b ? c, or ax + b ? c, where a, b, and c are constants.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-solve-system-of-52ac89dd","Math G9: Writing a system of two linear - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-4 Writing a system of two linear equations with two variables, and solving them algebraically and graphically.","1. Distinguishing inequalities, identifying linear and compound inequalities, and writing them.","Solve the system of linear equations: x + y = 4 and x - y = 2.","x = 3, y = 1","x = 4, y = 1","x = 3, y = 2","x = 1, y = 3","x = 3, y = 1","Solving the two equations gives x = 3, y = 1.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-of-is-a-45dcf6dc","Math G9: Writing a system of two linear - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-4 Writing a system of two linear equations with two variables, and solving them algebraically and graphically.","1. Distinguishing inequalities, identifying linear and compound inequalities, and writing them.","Which of the following is a compound inequality?","x > 2","2x - 5 < 3","x^2 + 2x - 3 > 0","2 < x ? 4","2 < x ? 4","A compound inequality is an inequality that contains two or more inequalities joined by the words 'and' or 'or'.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-solve-system-of-8faa80d1","Math G9: Writing a system of two linear - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-4 Writing a system of two linear equations with two variables, and solving them algebraically and graphically.","1. Distinguishing inequalities, identifying linear and compound inequalities, and writing them.","Solve the system of linear equations: 2x + 3y = 7 and x - 2y = -3.","x = 5/7, y = 1 6/7","x = 1 5/7, y = 1 6/7","x = 5/7, y = 2 6/7","x = 1 6/7, y = 5/7","x = 5/7, y = 1 6/7","Solving the two equations gives x = 5/7, y = 1 6/7.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-of-is-a-29b22196","Math G9: Writing a system of two linear - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-4 Writing a system of two linear equations with two variables, and solving them algebraically and graphically.","1. Distinguishing inequalities, identifying linear and compound inequalities, and writing them.","Which of the following is a linear equation?","x^2 + 2x - 3 = 0","2x - 5 < 3","x^3 - 2x^2 + x + 1 = 0","x - 2 = 0","x - 2 = 0","A linear equation is an equation that can be written in the form ax + b = c, where a, b, and c are constants.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-solve-system-of-ccbdcfb5","Math G9: Writing a system of two linear - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-4 Writing a system of two linear equations with two variables, and solving them algebraically and graphically.","1. Distinguishing inequalities, identifying linear and compound inequalities, and writing them.","Solve the system of linear equations: x + 2y = 6 and 2x - 3y = 5.","x = 4, y = 1","x = 5, y = 1","x = 4, y = 2","x = 1, y = 4","x = 4, y = 1","Solving the two equations gives x = 4, y = 1.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-of-is-a-06cbb98b","Math G9: Writing a system of two linear - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-4 Writing a system of two linear equations with two variables, and solving them algebraically and graphically.","1. Distinguishing inequalities, identifying linear and compound inequalities, and writing them.","Which of the following is a compound inequality?","x > 2","2x - 5 < 3","x^2 + 2x - 3 > 0","2 < x ? 4","2 < x ? 4","A compound inequality is an inequality that contains two or more inequalities joined by the words 'and' or 'or'.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-solution-to-0d5b72d3","Math G9: Writing linear and quadratic equations - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-3 Writing linear and quadratic equations, solving them algebraically and graphically, and estimating their solutions from graph representations.","1. Writing and solving a system of two linear equations with two variables, both algebraically (by substitution or elimination) and graphically.","What is the solution to the system of equations 3x - 2y = 5 and x + 2y = 9?","x = 7 and y = 1","x = 7/2 and y = 11/4","x = 7/3 and y = 11/3","x = 11/3 and y = 7/3","x = 7/2 and y = 11/4","Add the two equations: (3x - 2y) + (x + 2y) = 5 + 9, giving 4x = 14, so x = 3.5. Substitute into x + 2y = 9: 3.5 + 2y = 9, so y = 2.75. Verification: 3(3.5) - 2(2.75) = 10.5 - 5.5 = 5 ✓ and 3.5 + 2(2.75) = 3.5 + 5.5 = 9 ✓","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-solution-to-6497b7b7","Math G9: Writing linear and quadratic equations - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-3 Writing linear and quadratic equations, solving them algebraically and graphically, and estimating their solutions from graph representations.","3. Distinguishing linear equations with two variables, and finding ordered pairs that satisfy them using substitution.","What is the solution to the system of equations x + y = 5 and 2x - 2y = 2?","x = 3 and y = 2","x = 2 and y = 3","x = 1 and y = 4","x = 4 and y = 1","x = 3 and y = 2","Substitute x = 3 and y = 2 into both equations. The ordered pair satisfies each equation, so it is the solution.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-solution-to-ca64344c","Math G9: Writing linear and quadratic equations - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-3 Writing linear and quadratic equations, solving them algebraically and graphically, and estimating their solutions from graph representations.","1. Writing and solving a system of two linear equations with two variables, both algebraically (by substitution or elimination) and graphically.","What is the solution to the system of equations x + 2y = 4 and 3x - 2y = 2?","x = 2 and y = 1","x = 3/2 and y = 5/4","x = 3 and y = 1/2","x = 1 and y = 3/2","x = 3/2 and y = 5/4","Add the two equations: (x + 2y) + (3x - 2y) = 4 + 2, giving 4x = 6, so x = 1.5. Substitute into x + 2y = 4: 1.5 + 2y = 4, so y = 1.25. Verification: 1.5 + 2(1.25) = 1.5 + 2.5 = 4 ✓ and 3(1.5) - 2(1.25) = 4.5 - 2.5 = 2 ✓","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-solution-to-24c2b85f","Math G9: Writing linear and quadratic equations - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-3 Writing linear and quadratic equations, solving them algebraically and graphically, and estimating their solutions from graph representations.","1. Writing and solving a system of two linear equations with two variables, both algebraically (by substitution or elimination) and graphically.","What is the solution to the system of equations y = 2x - 1 and y = -x + 4?","x = 1 and y = 1","x = 5/3 and y = 7/3","x = 3 and y = 5","x = 5 and y = 3","x = 5/3 and y = 7/3","Set the two equations equal: 2x - 1 = -x + 4, giving 3x = 5, so x = 5/3. Substitute into y = 2x - 1: y = 2(5/3) - 1 = 10/3 - 3/3 = 7/3. Verification: 2(5/3) - 1 = 10/3 - 3/3 = 7/3 ✓ and -(5/3) + 4 = -5/3 + 12/3 = 7/3 ✓","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-how-can-you-fb506082","Math G9: Writing linear and quadratic equations - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-3 Writing linear and quadratic equations, solving them algebraically and graphically, and estimating their solutions from graph representations.","1. Writing and solving a system of two linear equations with two variables, both algebraically (by substitution or elimination) and graphically.","How can you solve the system of equations 2x + 3y = 7 and x - 2y = -3?","By graphing the two equations and finding the point of intersection","By using only the substitution method","By using only the elimination method","By adding the two equations together and solving for x","By graphing the two equations and finding the point of intersection","Graphing the two equations and finding the point of intersection is one way to solve the system of equations. This method involves plotting the two lines on a coordinate plane and finding the point where they intersect, which represents the solution to the system.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-solution-to-c71338b0","Math G9: Writing linear and quadratic equations - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-3 Writing linear and quadratic equations, solving them algebraically and graphically, and estimating their solutions from graph representations.","1. Writing and solving a system of two linear equations with two variables, both algebraically (by substitution or elimination) and graphically.","What is the solution to the system of equations x + y = 4 and x - y = 2?","x = 3 and y = 1","x = 2 and y = 2","x = 1 and y = 3","x = 0 and y = 4","x = 3 and y = 1","To solve the system of equations, we can add the two equations together to eliminate the y variable, resulting in 2x = 6, so x = 3. Then, we can substitute x = 3 into one of the original equations to find y = 1.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-solution-to-b520e57d","Math G9: Writing linear and quadratic equations - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-3 Writing linear and quadratic equations, solving them algebraically and graphically, and estimating their solutions from graph representations.","1. Writing and solving a system of two linear equations with two variables, both algebraically (by substitution or elimination) and graphically.","What is the solution to the system of equations y = x + 2 and y = -2x - 1?","x = -1 and y = 1","x = 1 and y = 3","x = 3 and y = 5","x = 1 and y = -3","x = -1 and y = 1","To solve the system of equations, we can set the two equations equal to each other, resulting in x + 2 = -2x - 1. Solving for x, we get 3x = -3, so x = -1. Then, we can substitute x = -1 into one of the original equations to find y = 1.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-how-can-you-b3fb262f","Math G9: Writing linear and quadratic equations - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-3 Writing linear and quadratic equations, solving them algebraically and graphically, and estimating their solutions from graph representations.","1. Writing and solving a system of two linear equations with two variables, both algebraically (by substitution or elimination) and graphically.","How can you solve the system of equations 2x + 5y = 17 and x - 3y = -7?","By using only the substitution method","By using only the elimination method","By graphing the two equations and finding the point of intersection","By adding the two equations together and solving for x","By graphing the two equations and finding the point of intersection","Graphing the two equations and finding the point of intersection is one way to solve the system of equations. This method involves plotting the two lines on a coordinate plane and finding the point where they intersect, which represents the solution to the system.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-solution-to-ce437244","Math G9: Writing linear and quadratic equations - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-3 Writing linear and quadratic equations, solving them algebraically and graphically, and estimating their solutions from graph representations.","1. Writing and solving a system of two linear equations with two variables, both algebraically (by substitution or elimination) and graphically.","What is the solution to the system of equations 2x + 3y = 12 and x - 2y = -3?","x = 0 and y = 4","x = 15/7 and y = 18/7","x = 3 and y = 2","x = 6 and y = 2","x = 15/7 and y = 18/7","From x - 2y = -3, we get x = 2y - 3. Substitute into 2x + 3y = 12: 2(2y - 3) + 3y = 12, giving 4y - 6 + 3y = 12, so 7y = 18, y = 18/7. Then x = 2(18/7) - 3 = 36/7 - 21/7 = 15/7. Verification: 2(15/7) + 3(18/7) = 30/7 + 54/7 = 84/7 = 12 ✓ and 15/7 - 2(18/7) = 15/7 - 36/7 = -21/7 = -3 ✓","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-solution-to-4d3ae8e1","Math G9: Writing linear and quadratic equations - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-3 Writing linear and quadratic equations, solving them algebraically and graphically, and estimating their solutions from graph representations.","1. Writing and solving a system of two linear equations with two variables, both algebraically (by substitution or elimination) and graphically.","What is the solution to the system of equations x + 4y = 20 and 3x - 4y = 4?","x = 4 and y = 4","x = 6 and y = 3.5","x = 8 and y = 3","x = 6 and y = 4","x = 6 and y = 3.5","Add the two equations: (x + 4y) + (3x - 4y) = 20 + 4, giving 4x = 24, so x = 6. Substitute into x + 4y = 20: 6 + 4y = 20, so y = 3.5. Verification: 6 + 4(3.5) = 6 + 14 = 20 ✓ and 3(6) - 4(3.5) = 18 - 14 = 4 ✓","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-solution-to-be934ed5","Math G9: Writing linear and quadratic equations - Set 1 T1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-3 Writing linear and quadratic equations, solving them algebraically and graphically, and estimating their solutions from graph representations.","1. Writing and solving a system of two linear equations with two variables, both algebraically (by substitution or elimination) and graphically.","What is the solution to the system of equations 2x + 2y = 8 and x - 3y = -5?","x = 1 and y = 3","x = 1.75 and y = 2.25","x = 4 and y = 0","x = 2 and y = 4","x = 1.75 and y = 2.25","To solve: from 2x + 2y = 8, we get x + y = 4, so x = 4 - y. Substitute into x - 3y = -5: (4 - y) - 3y = -5, giving 4 - 4y = -5, so y = 2.25 and x = 1.75. Verification: 2(1.75) + 2(2.25) = 3.5 + 4.5 = 8 ✓ and 1.75 - 3(2.25) = 1.75 - 6.75 = -5 ✓","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-solve-equation-2x-55b9ee32","Math G9: Writing linear and quadratic equations - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-3 Writing linear and quadratic equations, solving them algebraically and graphically, and estimating their solutions from graph representations.","2. Solving equations that include an absolute value on one side and representing their solutions graphically.","Solve the equation |2x - 5| = 1.","x = 3 or x = 2","x = 2 or x = 3","x = 1 or x = 4","x = 0 or x = 5","x = 3 or x = 2","To solve the equation |2x - 5| = 1, we need to isolate the absolute value expression. This can be done by setting up two separate equations: 2x - 5 = 1 and 2x - 5 = -1. Solving these equations gives us x = 3 and x = 2, respectively.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-solve-equation-x-20dce8b7","Math G9: Writing linear and quadratic equations - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-3 Writing linear and quadratic equations, solving them algebraically and graphically, and estimating their solutions from graph representations.","2. Solving equations that include an absolute value on one side and representing their solutions graphically.","Solve the equation |x - 1| = 6.","x = 7 or x = -5","x = -5 or x = 7","x = 0 or x = 8","x = 1 or x = -7","x = 7 or x = -5","To solve the equation |x - 1| = 6, we need to isolate the absolute value expression. This can be done by setting up two separate equations: x - 1 = 6 and x - 1 = -6. Solving these equations gives us x = 7 and x = -5, respectively.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-solve-equation-x-d963d16c","Math G9: Writing linear and quadratic equations - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-3 Writing linear and quadratic equations, solving them algebraically and graphically, and estimating their solutions from graph representations.","2. Solving equations that include an absolute value on one side and representing their solutions graphically.","Solve the equation |x - 3| = 5.","x = -8 or x = 13","x = 2 or x = 8","x = -10 or x = 18","x = 0 or x = 10","x = -8 or x = 13","To solve the equation |x - 3| = 5, we need to isolate the absolute value expression. This can be done by setting up two separate equations: x - 3 = 5 and x - 3 = -5. Solving these equations gives us x = 8 and x = -8, respectively.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-solve-equation-x-4d309937","Math G9: Writing linear and quadratic equations - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-3 Writing linear and quadratic equations, solving them algebraically and graphically, and estimating their solutions from graph representations.","2. Solving equations that include an absolute value on one side and representing their solutions graphically.","Solve the equation |x - 5| = 2.","x = 7 or x = 3","x = 3 or x = 7","x = 0 or x = 7","x = 1 or x = 6","x = 7 or x = 3","To solve the equation |x - 5| = 2, we need to isolate the absolute value expression. This can be done by setting up two separate equations: x - 5 = 2 and x - 5 = -2. Solving these equations gives us x = 7 and x = 3, respectively.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-solve-equation-2x-c893a1a7","Math G9: Writing linear and quadratic equations - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-3 Writing linear and quadratic equations, solving them algebraically and graphically, and estimating their solutions from graph representations.","4. Estimating the solution of a quadratic equation from its graphical representations, and solving quadratic equations graphically and algebraically using factoring, the quadratic formula, or completing the square.","Solve the equation |2x + 5| = 9.","x = 2 or x = -7","x = 7 or x = -2","x = 2 only","x = -7 only","x = 2 or x = -7","For |2x + 5| = 9, 2x + 5 = 9 or -9, giving x = 2 or x = -7.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-solve-equation-x-3af9a02e","Math G9: Writing linear and quadratic equations - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-3 Writing linear and quadratic equations, solving them algebraically and graphically, and estimating their solutions from graph representations.","2. Solving equations that include an absolute value on one side and representing their solutions graphically.","Solve the equation |x - 2| = 9.","x = -7 or x = 11","x = -11 or x = 7","x = -1 or x = 13","x = 0 or x = 10","x = -7 or x = 11","To solve the equation |x - 2| = 9, we need to isolate the absolute value expression. This can be done by setting up two separate equations: x - 2 = 9 and x - 2 = -9. Solving these equations gives us x = 11 and x = -7, respectively.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-solve-equation-2x-726543a7","Math G9: Writing linear and quadratic equations - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-3 Writing linear and quadratic equations, solving them algebraically and graphically, and estimating their solutions from graph representations.","2. Solving equations that include an absolute value on one side and representing their solutions graphically.","Solve the equation |2x + 1| = 7.","x = -4 or x = 3","x = -2 or x = 4","x = -3 or x = 2","x = 0 or x = 7","x = -4 or x = 3","To solve the equation |2x + 1| = 7, we need to isolate the absolute value expression. This can be done by setting up two separate equations: 2x + 1 = 7 and 2x + 1 = -7. Solving these equations gives us x = 3 and x = -4, respectively.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-solve-equation-3x-787afe3b","Math G9: Writing linear and quadratic equations - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-3 Writing linear and quadratic equations, solving them algebraically and graphically, and estimating their solutions from graph representations.","2. Solving equations that include an absolute value on one side and representing their solutions graphically.","Solve the equation |3x - 2| = 11.","x = 3.73 or x = -3.73","x = 4.73 or x = -4.73","x = 3.67 or x = -3.67","x = 4.67 or x = -4.67","x = 3.73 or x = -3.73","To solve the equation |3x - 2| = 11, we need to isolate the absolute value expression. This can be done by setting up two separate equations: 3x - 2 = 11 and 3x - 2 = -11. Solving these equations gives us x = 3.73 and x = -3.73, respectively.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-solve-equation-x-191308f2","Math G9: Writing linear and quadratic equations - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-3 Writing linear and quadratic equations, solving them algebraically and graphically, and estimating their solutions from graph representations.","2. Solving equations that include an absolute value on one side and representing their solutions graphically.","Solve the equation |x + 4| = 3.","x = -7 or x = 1","x = -1 or x = 7","x = -3 or x = 5","x = 0 or x = 4","x = -7 or x = 1","To solve the equation |x + 4| = 3, we need to isolate the absolute value expression. This can be done by setting up two separate equations: x + 4 = 3 and x + 4 = -3. Solving these equations gives us x = -1 and x = -7, respectively.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-solve-equation-x-f95c945a","Math G9: Writing linear and quadratic equations - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-3 Writing linear and quadratic equations, solving them algebraically and graphically, and estimating their solutions from graph representations.","4. Estimating the solution of a quadratic equation from its graphical representations, and solving quadratic equations graphically and algebraically using factoring, the quadratic formula, or completing the square.","Solve the equation |x + 2| = 8.","x = 6 or x = -10","x = 10 or x = -6","x = 6 only","x = -10 only","x = 6 or x = -10","For |x + 2| = 8, x + 2 = 8 or x + 2 = -8, so x = 6 or x = -10.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-quadratic-equation-eb618c10","Math G9: Writing linear and quadratic equations - Set 4","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-3 Writing linear and quadratic equations, solving them algebraically and graphically, and estimating their solutions from graph representations.","4. Estimating the solution of a quadratic equation from its graphical representations, and solving quadratic equations graphically and algebraically using factoring, the quadratic formula, or completing the square.","A quadratic equation in the form of y = ax^2 + bx + c is graphed. If the parabola has a y-intercept of (0, -2) and an x-intercept of (-4, 0), what is the estimated solution of the equation?","The solution is y = -2","The solution is x = -4","The solutions are x = -4 and x = 4","The solutions are x = -3 and x = 1","The solution is x = -4","Since the parabola has a y-intercept of (0, -2) and an x-intercept of (-4, 0), the solution of the equation is x = -4.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-quadratic-equation-eb618c10.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-quadratic-equation-3f9a1fe6","Math G9: Writing linear and quadratic equations - Set 4","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-3 Writing linear and quadratic equations, solving them algebraically and graphically, and estimating their solutions from graph representations.","4. Estimating the solution of a quadratic equation from its graphical representations, and solving quadratic equations graphically and algebraically using factoring, the quadratic formula, or completing the square.","A quadratic equation in the form of y = ax^2 + bx + c is graphed. If the parabola has a vertex at (1, -1) and an axis of symmetry at x = 1, what is the estimated solution of the equation?","The solution is x = 1","The solution is y = -1","The solutions are x = 1 and x = 3","The solutions are x = -3 and x = 1","The solutions are x = 1 and x = 3","Since the vertex of the parabola is at (1, -1) and the axis of symmetry is at x = 1, the solutions of the equation are x = 1 and x = 3.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-quadratic-equation-3f9a1fe6.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-quadratic-equation-2fd5c5d5","Math G9: Writing linear and quadratic equations - Set 4","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-3 Writing linear and quadratic equations, solving them algebraically and graphically, and estimating their solutions from graph representations.","4. Estimating the solution of a quadratic equation from its graphical representations, and solving quadratic equations graphically and algebraically using factoring, the quadratic formula, or completing the square.","A quadratic equation in the form of y = ax^2 + bx + c is graphed. If the parabola has a vertex at (-1, 2) and an axis of symmetry at x = -1, what is the estimated solution of the equation?","The solution is x = -1","The solution is y = 2","The solutions are x = -1 and x = 3","The solutions are x = -3 and x = 1","The solutions are x = -1 and x = 3","Since the vertex of the parabola is at (-1, 2) and the axis of symmetry is at x = -1, the solutions of the equation are x = -1 and x = 3.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-quadratic-equation-2fd5c5d5.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-quadratic-equation-15258152","Math G9: Writing linear and quadratic equations - Set 4","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-3 Writing linear and quadratic equations, solving them algebraically and graphically, and estimating their solutions from graph representations.","4. Estimating the solution of a quadratic equation from its graphical representations, and solving quadratic equations graphically and algebraically using factoring, the quadratic formula, or completing the square.","A quadratic equation in the form of y = ax^2 + bx + c is graphed. If the vertex of the parabola is at (-2, 3), what is the estimated solution of the equation?","The solution is x = -2","The solution is y = 3","The solutions are x = -2 and x = 4","The solutions are x = -4 and x = 0","The solutions are x = -2 and x = 4","Since the vertex of the parabola is at (-2, 3), the solutions of the equation are x = -2 and x = 4.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-quadratic-equation-15258152.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-quadratic-equation-63b0697b","Math G9: Writing linear and quadratic equations - Set 4","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-3 Writing linear and quadratic equations, solving them algebraically and graphically, and estimating their solutions from graph representations.","4. Estimating the solution of a quadratic equation from its graphical representations, and solving quadratic equations graphically and algebraically using factoring, the quadratic formula, or completing the square.","A quadratic equation in the form of y = ax^2 + bx + c is graphed. If the axis of symmetry is x = -1, what is the estimated solution of the equation?","The solution is x = -1","The solution is y = -1","The solutions are x = -1 and x = 3","The solutions are x = -3 and x = 1","The solutions are x = -1 and x = 3","Since the axis of symmetry is x = -1, the solutions of the equation are x = -1 and x = 3.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-quadratic-equation-63b0697b.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-quadratic-equation-b065a37a","Math G9: Writing linear and quadratic equations - Set 4","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-3 Writing linear and quadratic equations, solving them algebraically and graphically, and estimating their solutions from graph representations.","4. Estimating the solution of a quadratic equation from its graphical representations, and solving quadratic equations graphically and algebraically using factoring, the quadratic formula, or completing the square.","A quadratic equation in the form of y = ax^2 + bx + c is graphed. If the parabola opens upwards and has a y-intercept of (0, 2), what is the estimated solution of the equation?","The solution is y = 2","The solution is x = 0","The solutions are x = 0 and x = 4","The solutions are x = -4 and x = 0","The solution is x = 0","Since the parabola opens upwards and has a y-intercept of (0, 2), the solution of the equation is x = 0.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-quadratic-equation-b065a37a.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-quadratic-equation-21bcbd59","Math G9: Writing linear and quadratic equations - Set 4","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-3 Writing linear and quadratic equations, solving them algebraically and graphically, and estimating their solutions from graph representations.","4. Estimating the solution of a quadratic equation from its graphical representations, and solving quadratic equations graphically and algebraically using factoring, the quadratic formula, or completing the square.","A quadratic equation in the form of y = ax^2 + bx + c is graphed. If the parabola opens downwards and has a y-intercept of (0, -3), what is the estimated solution of the equation?","The solution is y = -3","The solution is x = 0","The solutions are x = 0 and x = 4","The solutions are x = -4 and x = 0","The solution is x = 0","Since the parabola opens downwards and has a y-intercept of (0, -3), the solution of the equation is x = 0.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-quadratic-equation-21bcbd59.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-quadratic-equation-661038ba","Math G9: Writing linear and quadratic equations - Set 4","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-3 Writing linear and quadratic equations, solving them algebraically and graphically, and estimating their solutions from graph representations.","4. Estimating the solution of a quadratic equation from its graphical representations, and solving quadratic equations graphically and algebraically using factoring, the quadratic formula, or completing the square.","A quadratic equation in the form of y = ax^2 + bx + c is graphed. If the parabola has a vertex at (2, -3) and an axis of symmetry at x = 2, what is the estimated solution of the equation?","The solution is x = 2","The solution is y = -3","The solutions are x = 2 and x = 4","The solutions are x = -4 and x = 2","The solutions are x = 2 and x = 4","Since the vertex of the parabola is at (2, -3) and the axis of symmetry is at x = 2, the solutions of the equation are x = 2 and x = 4.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-quadratic-equation-661038ba.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-quadratic-equation-2b580ab3","Math G9: Writing linear and quadratic equations - Set 4","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-3 Writing linear and quadratic equations, solving them algebraically and graphically, and estimating their solutions from graph representations.","4. Estimating the solution of a quadratic equation from its graphical representations, and solving quadratic equations graphically and algebraically using factoring, the quadratic formula, or completing the square.","A quadratic equation in the form of y = ax^2 + bx + c is graphed. If the parabola has a y-intercept of (0, 1) and an x-intercept of (3, 0), what is the estimated solution of the equation?","The solution is y = 1","The solution is x = 3","The solutions are x = 3 and x = -4","The solutions are x = -3 and x = 4","The solution is x = 3","Since the parabola has a y-intercept of (0, 1) and an x-intercept of (3, 0), the solution of the equation is x = 3.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-quadratic-equation-2b580ab3.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-graph-system-of-d566ba2f","Math G9: Writing linear and quadratic equations, solving - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-2-1 Determining the locations of points in the coordinate plane and plotting them, finding the distance between two points and the coordinates of the midpoint, and determining the slope of a line, writing its equation, and graphing it.","3. Distinguishing the equation of a line and writing its using the slope-intercept form, the point-slope form, and the standard form.","Graph the system of linear equations: x + 2y = 4 and x - 2y = 2.","The lines are parallel and do not intersect.","The lines intersect at (1, 1).","The lines intersect at (0, 2).","The lines intersect at (2, 0).","The lines intersect at (2, 0).","To graph the system, we can plot the two lines on the same coordinate plane. The first line has a slope of -1/2 and a y-intercept of 2, while the second line has a slope of -1/2 and a y-intercept of 2. The lines intersect at the point (2, 0).","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-graph-system-of-d566ba2f.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-solve-system-of-e1c85997","Math G9: Writing linear and quadratic equations, solving - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-3 Writing linear and quadratic equations, solving them algebraically and graphically, and estimating their solutions from graph representations.","1. Writing and solving a system of two linear equations with two variables, both algebraically (by substitution or elimination) and graphically.","Solve the system of linear equations: x + y = 4 and 2x - y = 2.","x = 2, y = 2","x = 3, y = 2","x = 2, y = 3","x = 1, y = 3","x = 2, y = 2","Solving the two equations gives x = 2, y = 2.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-graph-system-of-77916f2c","Math G9: Writing linear and quadratic equations, solving - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-2-1 Determining the locations of points in the coordinate plane and plotting them, finding the distance between two points and the coordinates of the midpoint, and determining the slope of a line, writing its equation, and graphing it.","3. Distinguishing the equation of a line and writing its using the slope-intercept form, the point-slope form, and the standard form.","Graph the system of linear equations: x + 2y = 4 and 2x - y = 1.","The lines are parallel and do not intersect.","The lines intersect at (1, 1).","The lines intersect at (0, 2).","The lines intersect at (2, 0).","The lines intersect at (2, 0).","To graph the system, we can plot the two lines on the same coordinate plane. The first line has a slope of -1/2 and a y-intercept of 2, while the second line has a slope of 2 and a y-intercept of -1. The lines intersect at the point (2, 0).","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-graph-system-of-77916f2c.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-graph-system-of-44d82b43","Math G9: Writing linear and quadratic equations, solving - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-2-1 Determining the locations of points in the coordinate plane and plotting them, finding the distance between two points and the coordinates of the midpoint, and determining the slope of a line, writing its equation, and graphing it.","3. Distinguishing the equation of a line and writing its using the slope-intercept form, the point-slope form, and the standard form.","Graph the system of linear equations: x + y = 2 and x - y = 1.","The lines are parallel and do not intersect.","The lines intersect at (1, 1).","The lines intersect at (0, 1).","The lines intersect at (1, 0).","The lines intersect at (0, 1).","To graph the system, we can plot the two lines on the same coordinate plane. The first line has a slope of -1 and a y-intercept of 2, while the second line has a slope of 1 and a y-intercept of -1. The lines intersect at the point (0, 1).","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-graph-system-of-44d82b43.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-solve-system-of-3d56fe09","Math G9: Writing linear and quadratic equations, solving - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-3 Writing linear and quadratic equations, solving them algebraically and graphically, and estimating their solutions from graph representations.","1. Writing and solving a system of two linear equations with two variables, both algebraically (by substitution or elimination) and graphically.","Solve the system of linear equations: 2x + 3y = 6 and x - 2y = -3.","x = 3/7, y = 1 5/7","x = 1 3/7, y = 1 5/7","x = 3/7, y = 2 5/7","x = 1 5/7, y = 3/7","x = 3/7, y = 1 5/7","Solving the two equations gives x = 3/7, y = 1 5/7.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-solve-system-of-847ac0e1","Math G9: Writing linear and quadratic equations, solving - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-3 Writing linear and quadratic equations, solving them algebraically and graphically, and estimating their solutions from graph representations.","1. Writing and solving a system of two linear equations with two variables, both algebraically (by substitution or elimination) and graphically.","Solve the system of linear equations: x + 2y = 6 and x - 3y = -2.","x = 2 4/5, y = 1 3/5","x = 3 4/5, y = 1 3/5","x = 2 4/5, y = 2 3/5","x = 1 3/5, y = 2 4/5","x = 2 4/5, y = 1 3/5","Solving the two equations gives x = 2 4/5, y = 1 3/5.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-graph-system-of-423df56e","Math G9: Writing linear and quadratic equations, solving - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-2-1 Determining the locations of points in the coordinate plane and plotting them, finding the distance between two points and the coordinates of the midpoint, and determining the slope of a line, writing its equation, and graphing it.","3. Distinguishing the equation of a line and writing its using the slope-intercept form, the point-slope form, and the standard form.","Graph the system of linear equations: 2x + y = 3 and x + 2y = 4.","The lines are parallel and do not intersect.","The lines intersect at (1, 1).","The lines intersect at (0, 2).","The lines intersect at (2, 0).","The lines intersect at (0, 2).","To graph the system, we can plot the two lines on the same coordinate plane. The first line has a slope of -2 and a y-intercept of 3, while the second line has a slope of -1/2 and a y-intercept of 2. The lines intersect at the point (0, 2).","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-graph-system-of-423df56e.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-solve-system-of-ce94543a","Math G9: Writing linear and quadratic equations, solving - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-3 Writing linear and quadratic equations, solving them algebraically and graphically, and estimating their solutions from graph representations.","1. Writing and solving a system of two linear equations with two variables, both algebraically (by substitution or elimination) and graphically.","Solve the system of linear equations: x + 3y = 5 and 2x - 2y = 2.","x = 2, y = 1","x = 3, y = 1","x = 2, y = 2","x = 1, y = 2","x = 2, y = 1","Solving the two equations gives x = 2, y = 1.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-graph-system-of-0daf33a6","Math G9: Writing linear and quadratic equations, solving - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-2-1 Determining the locations of points in the coordinate plane and plotting them, finding the distance between two points and the coordinates of the midpoint, and determining the slope of a line, writing its equation, and graphing it.","3. Distinguishing the equation of a line and writing its using the slope-intercept form, the point-slope form, and the standard form.","Graph the system of linear equations: x + y = 2 and 2x + 2y = 4.","The lines are parallel and do not intersect.","The lines intersect at (1, 1).","The lines intersect at (0, 2).","The lines intersect at (2, 0).","The lines intersect at (2, 0).","To graph the system, we can plot the two lines on the same coordinate plane. The first line has a slope of -1 and a y-intercept of 2, while the second line has a slope of -1 and a y-intercept of 2. The lines intersect at the point (2, 0).","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-graph-system-of-0daf33a6.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-solve-system-of-23c3df24","Math G9: Writing linear and quadratic equations, solving - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-3 Writing linear and quadratic equations, solving them algebraically and graphically, and estimating their solutions from graph representations.","1. Writing and solving a system of two linear equations with two variables, both algebraically (by substitution or elimination) and graphically.","Solve the system of linear equations: x + 2y = 6 and x - 2y = -2.","x = 2, y = 2","x = 3, y = 2","x = 2, y = 3","x = 4, y = 1","x = 2, y = 2","Solving the two equations gives x = 2, y = 2.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-quadratic-equation-25dad116","Math G9: Writing linear and quadratic equations, solving - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-3 Writing linear and quadratic equations, solving them algebraically and graphically, and estimating their solutions from graph representations.","4. Estimating the solution of a quadratic equation from its graphical representations, and solving quadratic equations graphically and algebraically using factoring, the quadratic formula, or completing the square.","A quadratic equation in the form of y = ax^2 + bx + c can be graphed as a parabola. What is the y-coordinate of the vertex of the parabola if the equation is y = x^2 - 4x - 3?","The y-coordinate of the vertex is -7.","The y-coordinate of the vertex is -6.","The y-coordinate of the vertex is -3.","The y-coordinate of the vertex is 7.","The y-coordinate of the vertex is -7.","The vertex occurs at x = 2. Substituting gives y = -7.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-quadratic-equation-25dad116.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-quadratic-equation-e846ae55","Math G9: Writing linear and quadratic equations, solving - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-3 Writing linear and quadratic equations, solving them algebraically and graphically, and estimating their solutions from graph representations.","4. Estimating the solution of a quadratic equation from its graphical representations, and solving quadratic equations graphically and algebraically using factoring, the quadratic formula, or completing the square.","A quadratic equation in the form of y = ax^2 + bx + c can be graphed as a parabola. What is the x-coordinate of the vertex of the parabola if the equation is y = 2x^2 + 12x + 7?","The x-coordinate is -3.","The x-coordinate is -2.","The x-coordinate is 3.","The x-coordinate is -4.","The x-coordinate is -3.","For y = ax^2 + bx + c, the x-coordinate is -b/(2a). Here it is -3.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-quadratic-equation-e846ae55.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-quadratic-equation-2dfaa749","Math G9: Writing linear and quadratic equations, solving - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-3 Writing linear and quadratic equations, solving them algebraically and graphically, and estimating their solutions from graph representations.","4. Estimating the solution of a quadratic equation from its graphical representations, and solving quadratic equations graphically and algebraically using factoring, the quadratic formula, or completing the square.","A quadratic equation in the form of y = ax^2 + bx + c can be graphed as a parabola. What is the y-intercept of the parabola if the equation is y = x^2 - 3x - 2?","The y-intercept is -2.","The y-intercept is -1.","The y-intercept is 2.","The y-intercept is 0.","The y-intercept is -2.","The y-intercept occurs when x = 0, so y = -2.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-quadratic-equation-2dfaa749.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-quadratic-equation-507fa01d","Math G9: Writing linear and quadratic equations, solving - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-3 Writing linear and quadratic equations, solving them algebraically and graphically, and estimating their solutions from graph representations.","4. Estimating the solution of a quadratic equation from its graphical representations, and solving quadratic equations graphically and algebraically using factoring, the quadratic formula, or completing the square.","A quadratic equation in the form of y = ax^2 + bx + c can be graphed as a parabola. What is the x-intercept of the parabola if the equation is y = x^2 + 2x - 3?","The x-intercept is -3.","The x-intercept is -1.","The x-intercept is 1.","The x-intercept is 3.","The x-intercept is 3.","To find the x-intercept, we can set y = 0 and solve for x. So, we have 0 = x^2 + 2x - 3. We can factor the equation to get 0 = (x + 3)(x - 1). Then, we can set each factor equal to 0 and solve for x to get x = -3 or x = 1.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-quadratic-equation-507fa01d.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-quadratic-equation-cf631795","Math G9: Writing linear and quadratic equations, solving - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-3 Writing linear and quadratic equations, solving them algebraically and graphically, and estimating their solutions from graph representations.","4. Estimating the solution of a quadratic equation from its graphical representations, and solving quadratic equations graphically and algebraically using factoring, the quadratic formula, or completing the square.","A quadratic equation in the form of y = ax^2 + bx + c can be graphed as a parabola. What is the y-intercept of the parabola if the equation is y = x^2 + 5x + 6?","The y-intercept is 6.","The y-intercept is 7.","The y-intercept is -6.","The y-intercept is 0.","The y-intercept is 6.","The y-intercept occurs when x = 0, so y = 6.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-quadratic-equation-cf631795.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-quadratic-equation-e4028352","Math G9: Writing linear and quadratic equations, solving - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-3 Writing linear and quadratic equations, solving them algebraically and graphically, and estimating their solutions from graph representations.","4. Estimating the solution of a quadratic equation from its graphical representations, and solving quadratic equations graphically and algebraically using factoring, the quadratic formula, or completing the square.","A quadratic equation in the form of y = ax^2 + bx + c can be graphed as a parabola. What is the x-coordinate of the axis of symmetry of the parabola if the equation is y = x^2 + 4x - 5?","The x-coordinate is -2.","The x-coordinate is -1.","The x-coordinate is 2.","The x-coordinate is -3.","The x-coordinate is -2.","For y = ax^2 + bx + c, the x-coordinate is -b/(2a). Here it is -2.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-quadratic-equation-e4028352.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-quadratic-equation-b03ed7a4","Math G9: Writing linear and quadratic equations, solving - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-3-2-1 Determining the locations of points in the coordinate plane and plotting them, finding the distance between two points and the coordinates of the midpoint, and determining the slope of a line, writing its equation, and graphing it.","3. Distinguishing the equation of a line and writing its using the slope-intercept form, the point-slope form, and the standard form.","A quadratic equation in the form of y = ax^2 + bx + c can be graphed as a parabola. What is the x-coordinate of the axis of symmetry of the parabola if the equation is y = x^2 - 2x - 3?","The x-coordinate of the axis of symmetry is 1.","The x-coordinate of the axis of symmetry is 2.","The x-coordinate of the axis of symmetry is -1.","The x-coordinate of the axis of symmetry is 0.","The x-coordinate of the axis of symmetry is 1.","The axis of symmetry is a vertical line, x = -b/(2a), so x = 1.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-quadratic-equation-b03ed7a4.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-quadratic-equation-d6f45d6d","Math G9: Writing linear and quadratic equations, solving - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-3 Writing linear and quadratic equations, solving them algebraically and graphically, and estimating their solutions from graph representations.","4. Estimating the solution of a quadratic equation from its graphical representations, and solving quadratic equations graphically and algebraically using factoring, the quadratic formula, or completing the square.","A quadratic equation in the form of y = ax^2 + bx + c can be graphed as a parabola. What is the x-intercept of the parabola if the equation is y = x^2 - 6x + 8?","The x-intercept is -1.","The x-intercept is 2.","The x-intercept is 4.","The x-intercept is 8.","The x-intercept is 2.","To find the x-intercept, we can set y = 0 and solve for x. So, we have 0 = x^2 - 6x + 8. We can factor the equation to get 0 = (x - 2)(x - 4). Then, we can set each factor equal to 0 and solve for x to get x = 2 or x = 4.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-quadratic-equation-d6f45d6d.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-quadratic-equation-c74b0e1d","Math G9: Writing linear and quadratic equations, solving - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-3 Writing linear and quadratic equations, solving them algebraically and graphically, and estimating their solutions from graph representations.","4. Estimating the solution of a quadratic equation from its graphical representations, and solving quadratic equations graphically and algebraically using factoring, the quadratic formula, or completing the square.","A quadratic equation in the form of y = ax^2 + bx + c can be graphed as a parabola. What is the x-coordinate of the vertex of the parabola if the equation is y = x^2 + x - 2?","The x-coordinate is -1/2.","The x-coordinate is 1/2.","The x-coordinate is -2.","The x-coordinate is 2.","The x-coordinate is -1/2.","For y = ax² + bx + c, the vertex x-coordinate is -b/(2a). Here a = 1 and b = 1, so x = -1/2.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-quadratic-equation-c74b0e1d.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-quadratic-equation-5fc5183f","Math G9: Writing linear and quadratic equations, solving - Set 2","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-2-2-3 Writing linear and quadratic equations, solving them algebraically and graphically, and estimating their solutions from graph representations.","4. Estimating the solution of a quadratic equation from its graphical representations, and solving quadratic equations graphically and algebraically using factoring, the quadratic formula, or completing the square.","A quadratic equation in the form of y = ax^2 + bx + c can be graphed as a parabola. What is the y-coordinate of the vertex of the parabola if the equation is y = x^2 - 5x + 6?","The y-coordinate of the vertex is -1 1/4.","The y-coordinate of the vertex is 3/4.","The y-coordinate of the vertex is 6.","The y-coordinate of the vertex is 1/4.","The y-coordinate of the vertex is -1 1/4.","The vertex occurs at x = 2 1/2. Substituting gives y = -1 1/4.","/image-source/math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-quadratic-equation-5fc5183f.png","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-bag-contains-4ee0f707","Math G9: Writing the sample space of a random experiment - Set 4","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-4-3-1 Writing the sample space of a random experiment, finding the number of possible outcomes, distinguishing types of events, and calculating their probabilities.","4. Calculating theoretical and experimental probabilities of an event, comparing them, and using them for prediction.","A bag contains 5 red marbles, 4 blue marbles, and 3 green marbles. What is the probability of drawing a blue marble?","The probability of drawing a blue marble is 1/12.","The probability of drawing a blue marble is 1/3.","The probability of drawing a blue marble is 4/12.","The probability of drawing a blue marble is 5/12.","The probability of drawing a blue marble is 1/3.","To find the probability, we divide the number of blue marbles (4) by the total number of marbles (12).","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-coin-is-f80e90a1","Math G9: Writing the sample space of a random experiment - Set 4","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-4-3-1 Writing the sample space of a random experiment, finding the number of possible outcomes, distinguishing types of events, and calculating their probabilities.","4. Calculating theoretical and experimental probabilities of an event, comparing them, and using them for prediction.","A coin is flipped 10 times. What is the probability of getting exactly 5 heads?","The probability of getting exactly 5 heads is 1/2.","The probability of getting exactly 5 heads is 1/10.","The probability of getting exactly 5 heads is 10/1024.","The probability of getting exactly 5 heads is 1/1024.","The probability of getting exactly 5 heads is 10/1024.","We can use the binomial probability formula to find the probability. The probability of getting exactly 5 heads is 10/1024.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-spinner-has-ab9e17d6","Math G9: Writing the sample space of a random experiment - Set 4","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-4-3-1 Writing the sample space of a random experiment, finding the number of possible outcomes, distinguishing types of events, and calculating their probabilities.","4. Calculating theoretical and experimental probabilities of an event, comparing them, and using them for prediction.","A spinner has 8 equal sections, numbered 1 to 8. What is the probability of spinning a number greater than 5?","The probability of spinning a number greater than 5 is 1/8.","The probability of spinning a number greater than 5 is 2/8.","The probability of spinning a number greater than 5 is 3/8.","The probability of spinning a number greater than 5 is 4/8.","The probability of spinning a number greater than 5 is 4/8.","There are 3 numbers greater than 5 (6, 7, 8). To find the probability, we divide the number of favorable outcomes (3) by the total number of outcomes (8).","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-deck-of-7896e636","Math G9: Writing the sample space of a random experiment - Set 4","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-4-3-1 Writing the sample space of a random experiment, finding the number of possible outcomes, distinguishing types of events, and calculating their probabilities.","4. Calculating theoretical and experimental probabilities of an event, comparing them, and using them for prediction.","A deck of cards has 52 cards. What is the probability of drawing a heart?","The probability of drawing a heart is 1/4.","The probability of drawing a heart is 1/2.","The probability of drawing a heart is 13/52.","The probability of drawing a heart is 39/52.","The probability of drawing a heart is 13/52.","There are 13 hearts in a deck of cards. To find the probability, we divide the number of hearts (13) by the total number of cards (52).","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-bag-contains-11d7af89","Math G9: Writing the sample space of a random experiment - Set 4","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-4-3-1 Writing the sample space of a random experiment, finding the number of possible outcomes, distinguishing types of events, and calculating their probabilities.","4. Calculating theoretical and experimental probabilities of an event, comparing them, and using them for prediction.","A bag contains 15 red balls and 10 blue balls. What is the probability of drawing a red ball?","The probability of drawing a red ball is 1/5.","The probability of drawing a red ball is 1/3.","The probability of drawing a red ball is 15/25.","The probability of drawing a red ball is 10/25.","The probability of drawing a red ball is 15/25.","To find the probability, we divide the number of red balls (15) by the total number of balls (25).","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-coin-is-5447e4de","Math G9: Writing the sample space of a random experiment - Set 4","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-4-3-1 Writing the sample space of a random experiment, finding the number of possible outcomes, distinguishing types of events, and calculating their probabilities.","4. Calculating theoretical and experimental probabilities of an event, comparing them, and using them for prediction.","A coin is flipped 5 times. What is the probability of getting exactly 2 tails?","The probability of getting exactly 2 tails is 1/2.","The probability of getting exactly 2 tails is 1/10.","The probability of getting exactly 2 tails is 5/32.","The probability of getting exactly 2 tails is 10/32.","The probability of getting exactly 2 tails is 5/32.","We can use the binomial probability formula to find the probability. The probability of getting exactly 2 tails is 5/32.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-spinner-has-36959e14","Math G9: Writing the sample space of a random experiment - Set 4","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-4-3-1 Writing the sample space of a random experiment, finding the number of possible outcomes, distinguishing types of events, and calculating their probabilities.","4. Calculating theoretical and experimental probabilities of an event, comparing them, and using them for prediction.","A spinner has 12 equal sections, numbered 1 to 12. What is the probability of spinning a number less than 9?","The probability of spinning a number less than 9 is 1/12.","The probability of spinning a number less than 9 is 2/12.","The probability of spinning a number less than 9 is 8/12.","The probability of spinning a number less than 9 is 9/12.","The probability of spinning a number less than 9 is 8/12.","There are 8 numbers less than 9 (1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8). To find the probability, we divide the number of favorable outcomes (8) by the total number of outcomes (12).","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-deck-of-19c69655","Math G9: Writing the sample space of a random experiment - Set 4","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-4-3-1 Writing the sample space of a random experiment, finding the number of possible outcomes, distinguishing types of events, and calculating their probabilities.","3. Distinguishing types of events (simple and compound, mutually exclusive and non-mutually exclusive, complementary, independent and dependent) and calculating their probabilities.","A deck of cards has 52 cards. What is the probability of drawing a card that is not a heart?","The probability of drawing a card that is not a heart is 1/4.","The probability of drawing a card that is not a heart is 1/2.","The probability of drawing a card that is not a heart is 39/52.","The probability of drawing a card that is not a heart is 13/52.","The probability of drawing a card that is not a heart is 39/52.","There are 39 cards that are not hearts. To find the probability, we divide the number of non-hearts (39) by the total number of cards (52).","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-bag-contains-16486b97","Math G9: Writing the sample space of a random experiment - Set 4","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-4-3-1 Writing the sample space of a random experiment, finding the number of possible outcomes, distinguishing types of events, and calculating their probabilities.","4. Calculating theoretical and experimental probabilities of an event, comparing them, and using them for prediction.","A bag contains 8 red balls and 6 blue balls. What is the probability of drawing a blue ball?","The probability of drawing a blue ball is 1/7.","The probability of drawing a blue ball is 1/2.","The probability of drawing a blue ball is 6/14.","The probability of drawing a blue ball is 8/14.","The probability of drawing a blue ball is 1/2.","To find the probability, we divide the number of blue balls (6) by the total number of balls (14).","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-a-coin-is-936c9742","Math G9: Writing the sample space of a random experiment - Set 4","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-4-3-1 Writing the sample space of a random experiment, finding the number of possible outcomes, distinguishing types of events, and calculating their probabilities.","4. Calculating theoretical and experimental probabilities of an event, comparing them, and using them for prediction.","A coin is flipped 8 times. What is the probability of getting exactly 4 heads?","The probability of getting exactly 4 heads is 1/2.","The probability of getting exactly 4 heads is 1/8.","The probability of getting exactly 4 heads is 70/256.","The probability of getting exactly 4 heads is 56/256.","The probability of getting exactly 4 heads is 56/256.","We can use the binomial probability formula to find the probability. The probability of getting exactly 4 heads is 56/256.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-term-events-9e8e9cd1","Math G9: Writing the sample space of a - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-4-3-1 Writing the sample space of a random experiment, finding the number of possible outcomes, distinguishing types of events, and calculating their probabilities.","3. Distinguishing types of events (simple and compound, mutually exclusive and non-mutually exclusive, complementary, independent and dependent) and calculating their probabilities.","What is the term for events that do not affect each other?","Independent events","Dependent events","Mutually exclusive events","Complementary events","Independent events","Independent events are events that do not affect each other.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-of-events-is-aaaee73b","Math G9: Writing the sample space of a - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-4-3-1 Writing the sample space of a random experiment, finding the number of possible outcomes, distinguishing types of events, and calculating their probabilities.","4. Calculating theoretical and experimental probabilities of an event, comparing them, and using them for prediction.","Which of the following events is an example of a complementary event?","Rolling a 3 on a single die","Flipping a coin and getting heads","Drawing a red card from a deck","Not rolling a 3 on a single die","Not rolling a 3 on a single die","Not rolling a 3 on a single die is the complementary event of rolling a 3 on a single die.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-term-an-858abfa2","Math G9: Writing the sample space of a - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-4-3-1 Writing the sample space of a random experiment, finding the number of possible outcomes, distinguishing types of events, and calculating their probabilities.","3. Distinguishing types of events (simple and compound, mutually exclusive and non-mutually exclusive, complementary, independent and dependent) and calculating their probabilities.","What is the term for an event that cannot occur at the same time?","Mutually exclusive event","Independent event","Dependent event","Complementary event","Mutually exclusive event","Mutually exclusive events are events that cannot occur at the same time.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-of-events-is-45bf472d","Math G9: Writing the sample space of a - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-4-3-1 Writing the sample space of a random experiment, finding the number of possible outcomes, distinguishing types of events, and calculating their probabilities.","4. Calculating theoretical and experimental probabilities of an event, comparing them, and using them for prediction.","Which of the following events is an example of a dependent event?","Rolling a 3 on a single die and then rolling a 6 on the same die","Flipping a coin and then flipping it again","Drawing a card from a deck and then drawing another card from the same deck","Rolling a 4 on a single die and then rolling a 5 on the same die","Rolling a 3 on a single die and then rolling a 6 on the same die","Rolling a 3 on a single die and then rolling a 6 on the same die are dependent events because the outcome of the second roll is affected by the outcome of the first roll.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-term-an-29d03ecc","Math G9: Writing the sample space of a - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-4-3-1 Writing the sample space of a random experiment, finding the number of possible outcomes, distinguishing types of events, and calculating their probabilities.","3. Distinguishing types of events (simple and compound, mutually exclusive and non-mutually exclusive, complementary, independent and dependent) and calculating their probabilities.","What is the term for an event that can occur in multiple ways?","A simple event","A compound event","An event that can occur in no ways","An event that can occur in any number of ways","A compound event","A compound event is an event that can occur in multiple ways.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-of-events-is-3c25294a","Math G9: Writing the sample space of a - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-4-3-1 Writing the sample space of a random experiment, finding the number of possible outcomes, distinguishing types of events, and calculating their probabilities.","4. Calculating theoretical and experimental probabilities of an event, comparing them, and using them for prediction.","Which of the following events is an example of a compound event?","Rolling a 3 on a single die","Flipping a coin and getting heads","Drawing a red card from a deck","Rolling a 3 and a 6 on a single die","Rolling a 3 and a 6 on a single die","Rolling a 3 and a 6 on a single die is a compound event because it can occur in multiple ways.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-of-events-is-100bafb8","Math G9: Writing the sample space of a - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-4-3-1 Writing the sample space of a random experiment, finding the number of possible outcomes, distinguishing types of events, and calculating their probabilities.","3. Distinguishing types of events (simple and compound, mutually exclusive and non-mutually exclusive, complementary, independent and dependent) and calculating their probabilities.","Which of the following events is an example of a mutually exclusive event?","Rolling a 3 and a 6 on a single die","Flipping a coin and getting heads","Drawing a red card and a black card from a deck","Rolling a 4 on a single die","Drawing a red card and a black card from a deck","Drawing a red card and a black card from a deck are mutually exclusive events because they cannot occur at the same time.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-term-an-49955b49","Math G9: Writing the sample space of a - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-4-3-1 Writing the sample space of a random experiment, finding the number of possible outcomes, distinguishing types of events, and calculating their probabilities.","3. Distinguishing types of events (simple and compound, mutually exclusive and non-mutually exclusive, complementary, independent and dependent) and calculating their probabilities.","What is the term for an event that is the opposite of another event?","Complementary event","Independent event","Dependent event","Mutually exclusive event","Complementary event","A complementary event is an event that is the opposite of another event.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-is-term-an-42ce316b","Math G9: Writing the sample space of a - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-4-3-1 Writing the sample space of a random experiment, finding the number of possible outcomes, distinguishing types of events, and calculating their probabilities.","3. Distinguishing types of events (simple and compound, mutually exclusive and non-mutually exclusive, complementary, independent and dependent) and calculating their probabilities.","What is the term for an event that can occur only one way?","An event that can occur in multiple ways","A simple event","An event that can occur in no ways","An event that can occur in any number of ways","A simple event","A simple event is an event that can occur only one way.","","",""
"math-g9-9-4-2-1-1-of-events-is-b03aa3c2","Math G9: Writing the sample space of a - Set 1","Math","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Math","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-4-4-3-1 Writing the sample space of a random experiment, finding the number of possible outcomes, distinguishing types of events, and calculating their probabilities.","4. Calculating theoretical and experimental probabilities of an event, comparing them, and using them for prediction.","Which of the following events is an example of an independent event?","Rolling a 3 on a single die and then rolling a 6 on the same die","Flipping a coin and then flipping it again","Drawing a card from a deck and then drawing another card from the same deck","Rolling a 4 on a single die and then rolling a 5 on the same die","Flipping a coin and then flipping it again","Flipping a coin and then flipping it again are independent events because the outcome of the second flip is not affected by the outcome of the first flip.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-main-reason-2258f5d1","Science G6: Classifying living organisms into groups based on common - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-2-1 Classifying living organisms into groups based on common phenotypic traits.","3.Classifying animals and microorganisms from local environment into groups based on common phenotypic characteristics.","What is the main reason scientists group animals into different categories?","To make it easier to learn about each animal species","To understand their habitats and behaviors","To identify their common characteristics and traits","To learn about their favorite foods","To identify their common characteristics and traits","The main reason scientists group animals into different categories is to identify their common characteristics and traits, which helps us understand their habitats and behaviors.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-benefit-of-cb5589a2","Science G6: Classifying living organisms into groups based on common - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-2-1 Classifying living organisms into groups based on common phenotypic traits.","3.Classifying animals and microorganisms from local environment into groups based on common phenotypic characteristics.","What is the benefit of understanding the characteristics of different animal groups?","It makes it harder to learn about each animal species","It makes it easier to remember their names","It helps us understand their habitats and behaviors","It makes it harder to identify their characteristics","It helps us understand their habitats and behaviors","The benefit of understanding the characteristics of different animal groups is that it helps us understand their habitats and behaviors, which is essential for learning about each animal species.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-why-do-scientists-adaa6d59","Science G6: Classifying living organisms into groups based on common - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-2-1 Classifying living organisms into groups based on common phenotypic traits.","3.Classifying animals and microorganisms from local environment into groups based on common phenotypic characteristics.","Why do scientists classify animals into different groups based on their characteristics?","To make it easier to learn about each animal species","To understand their habitats and behaviors","To identify their common characteristics and traits","To learn about their favorite foods","To identify their common characteristics and traits","Scientists classify animals into different groups based on their characteristics to identify their common characteristics and traits, which helps us understand their habitats and behaviors.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-main-reason-b0c1a861","Science G6: Classifying living organisms into groups based on common - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-2-1 Classifying living organisms into groups based on common phenotypic traits.","3.Classifying animals and microorganisms from local environment into groups based on common phenotypic characteristics.","What is the main reason scientists group animals together?","They have the same favorite food","They have similar body shapes and features","They live in the same type of habitat","They are the same size","They have similar body shapes and features","Scientists group animals together based on their common characteristics and traits, such as body shape and features.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-why-do-scientists-792733d8","Science G6: Classifying living organisms into groups based on common - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-2-1 Classifying living organisms into groups based on common phenotypic traits.","3.Classifying animals and microorganisms from local environment into groups based on common phenotypic characteristics.","Why do scientists classify animals into different groups?","To make it easier to remember their names","To understand their habitats and behaviors","To identify their common characteristics and traits","To learn about their favorite foods","To identify their common characteristics and traits","Scientists classify animals into different groups to identify their common characteristics and traits, which helps us understand their habitats and behaviors.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-main-purpose-bb7175f4","Science G6: Classifying living organisms into groups based on common - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-2-1 Classifying living organisms into groups based on common phenotypic traits.","3.Classifying animals and microorganisms from local environment into groups based on common phenotypic characteristics.","What is the main purpose of classifying animals into groups?","To create a list of all the different animal species","To understand the relationships between different animal species","To identify the characteristics that make each group unique","To learn about the different types of habitats","To understand the relationships between different animal species","The main purpose of classifying animals into groups is to understand the relationships between different animal species and identify the characteristics that make each group unique.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-why-do-scientists-7fd36457","Science G6: Classifying living organisms into groups based on common - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-2-1 Classifying living organisms into groups based on common phenotypic traits.","2.Determining the common characteristics and traits of various animals in order to justify their classification within specific groups.","Why do scientists use characteristics like body shape and size to classify animals?","Because they are easy to observe","Because they are important for survival","Because they are unique to each group","Because they are not important for classification","Because they are unique to each group","Scientists use characteristics like body shape and size to classify animals because they are unique to each group and help us understand their relationships.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-benefit-of-ba3df3fd","Science G6: Classifying living organisms into groups based on common - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-2-1 Classifying living organisms into groups based on common phenotypic traits.","3.Classifying animals and microorganisms from local environment into groups based on common phenotypic characteristics.","What is the benefit of classifying animals into groups?","It makes it harder to learn about each animal species","It makes it easier to remember their names","It helps us understand their habitats and behaviors","It makes it harder to identify their characteristics","It helps us understand their habitats and behaviors","The benefit of classifying animals into groups is that it helps us understand their habitats and behaviors, which is essential for learning about each animal species.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-why-do-scientists-f42e0509","Science G6: Classifying living organisms into groups based on common - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-2-1 Classifying living organisms into groups based on common phenotypic traits.","3.Classifying animals and microorganisms from local environment into groups based on common phenotypic characteristics.","Why do scientists group animals into categories like mammals and birds?","Because they are easy to remember","Because they are similar in size","Because they have similar body shapes and features","Because they live in the same type of habitat","Because they have similar body shapes and features","Scientists group animals into categories like mammals and birds because they have similar body shapes and features, which helps us understand their relationships.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-why-do-scientists-093e173b","Science G6: Classifying living organisms into groups based on common - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-2-1 Classifying living organisms into groups based on common phenotypic traits.","2.Determining the common characteristics and traits of various animals in order to justify their classification within specific groups.","Why do scientists use characteristics like diet and habitat to classify animals?","Because they are easy to observe","Because they are important for survival","Because they are unique to each group","Because they are not important for classification","Because they are unique to each group","Scientists use characteristics like diet and habitat to classify animals because they are unique to each group and help us understand their relationships.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-why-is-it-19081f4c","Science G6: Classifying living organisms into groups based on common - Set 3","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-2-1 Classifying living organisms into groups based on common phenotypic traits.","3.Classifying animals and microorganisms from local environment into groups based on common phenotypic characteristics.","Why is it important to classify living organisms into groups?","To understand the diversity of life on Earth and to identify patterns and relationships","To name and describe each individual organism","To determine the age of the organisms","To identify the most common organisms in an ecosystem","To understand the diversity of life on Earth and to identify patterns and relationships","Classifying living organisms into groups is important because it helps us to understand the diversity of life on Earth and to identify patterns and relationships between different organisms, which is essential for understanding the natural world.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-a-benefit-4b76cd94","Science G6: Classifying living organisms into groups based on common - Set 3","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-2-1 Classifying living organisms into groups based on common phenotypic traits.","3.Classifying animals and microorganisms from local environment into groups based on common phenotypic characteristics.","What is a benefit of classifying animals and microorganisms from the local environment?","It helps us to understand the importance of each individual organism","It allows us to identify the most common organisms in the environment","It enables us to appreciate the diversity of life and to identify patterns and relationships","It helps us to determine the age of the organisms","It enables us to appreciate the diversity of life and to identify patterns and relationships","Classifying animals and microorganisms from the local environment enables us to appreciate the diversity of life and to identify patterns and relationships, which is essential for understanding the natural world and our place within it.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-main-reason-50ecf5cd","Science G6: Classifying living organisms into groups based on common - Set 3","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-2-1 Classifying living organisms into groups based on common phenotypic traits.","3.Classifying animals and microorganisms from local environment into groups based on common phenotypic characteristics.","What is the main reason we group living organisms into categories?","To understand their habitats and behaviors","To identify their unique characteristics and features","To learn about their life cycles and reproduction methods","To study their interactions with the environment","To identify their unique characteristics and features","We group living organisms into categories to identify their unique characteristics and features, making it easier to understand and study them.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-a-characteristic-d01489d8","Science G6: Classifying living organisms into groups based on common - Set 3","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-2-1 Classifying living organisms into groups based on common phenotypic traits.","3.Classifying animals and microorganisms from local environment into groups based on common phenotypic characteristics.","What is a characteristic that can be used to classify microorganisms?","The shape of their cells","The type of habitat they live in","The color of their colonies","The speed at which they move","The shape of their cells","The shape of their cells is a characteristic that can be used to classify microorganisms, as different microorganisms have distinct cell shapes.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-of-is-a-8050af57","Science G6: Classifying living organisms into groups based on common - Set 3","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-2-1 Classifying living organisms into groups based on common phenotypic traits.","3.Classifying animals and microorganisms from local environment into groups based on common phenotypic characteristics.","Which of the following is a common way to classify animals?","By the color of their fur or feathers","By the type of food they eat","By their body structure and physical features","By the sounds they make","By their body structure and physical features","Classifying animals by their body structure and physical features is a common way to group them, as it helps us understand their relationships and characteristics.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-why-do-scientists-5e45d8f3","Science G6: Classifying living organisms into groups based on common - Set 3","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-2-1 Classifying living organisms into groups based on common phenotypic traits.","3.Classifying animals and microorganisms from local environment into groups based on common phenotypic characteristics.","Why do scientists classify living organisms into groups?","To name and describe each individual organism","To understand the diversity of life on Earth","To identify the most common organisms in an ecosystem","To determine the age of the organisms","To understand the diversity of life on Earth","Scientists classify living organisms into groups to understand the diversity of life on Earth and to identify patterns and relationships between different organisms.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-of-is-an-7bd54f12","Science G6: Classifying living organisms into groups based on common - Set 3","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-2-1 Classifying living organisms into groups based on common phenotypic traits.","3.Classifying animals and microorganisms from local environment into groups based on common phenotypic characteristics.","Which of the following is an example of a phenotypic characteristic?","The ability to fly","The color of an organism's eyes","The shape of an organism's DNA","The type of food an organism eats","The color of an organism's eyes","The color of an organism's eyes is an example of a phenotypic characteristic, as it is a physical trait that can be observed and measured.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-how-do-scientists-a59efa32","Science G6: Classifying living organisms into groups based on common - Set 3","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-2-1 Classifying living organisms into groups based on common phenotypic traits.","3.Classifying animals and microorganisms from local environment into groups based on common phenotypic characteristics.","How do scientists typically classify animals from the local environment?","By their ability to adapt to different environments","By their body structure and physical features","By the type of food they eat and their diet","By the sounds they make and their communication methods","By their body structure and physical features","Scientists typically classify animals from the local environment by their body structure and physical features, as this helps to identify their relationships and characteristics.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-purpose-of-1f4f9f6e","Science G6: Classifying living organisms into groups based on common - Set 3","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-2-1 Classifying living organisms into groups based on common phenotypic traits.","3.Classifying animals and microorganisms from local environment into groups based on common phenotypic characteristics.","What is the purpose of classifying microorganisms from the local environment?","To understand their role in the ecosystem and their interactions with other organisms","To identify the most common microorganisms in the environment","To determine the age of the microorganisms","To name and describe each individual microorganism","To understand their role in the ecosystem and their interactions with other organisms","The purpose of classifying microorganisms from the local environment is to understand their role in the ecosystem and their interactions with other organisms, which helps us to appreciate their importance and impact.","","",""
