"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-impact-of-b0ab12d1","Science G6: Describing the components of the ecosystem and explaining - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-2 Describing the components of the ecosystem and explaining the impact of the availability of different resources in ecosystems on the survival and sustainability of living organisms, and proposing solutions to problems that affect the stability of the ecosystem.","1. Describing the biotic and abiotic components of ecosystems and their interaction to provide the needs of living organisms, and its impact on the survival and stability of the ecosystem.","What is the impact of overhunting on an ecosystem?","It helps to control the population of animals","It makes the ecosystem more fragile","It disrupts the balance of the ecosystem and can lead to the extinction of species","It makes the air cleaner","It disrupts the balance of the ecosystem and can lead to the extinction of species","Overhunting can disrupt the balance of the ecosystem and can lead to the extinction of species, making it more fragile.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-role-of-5a5adf24","Science G6: Describing the components of the ecosystem and explaining - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-3 Representing the relationships between living organisms in which matter is circulated in the ecosystem, and identifying the relationship between plants and energy obtained from the sun to produce food.","3. Explaining the process of photosynthesis and its role in determining the relationship between plants and the energy obtained from the sun to produce food.","What is the role of symbiotic relationships in an ecosystem?","They make the ecosystem more fragile","They help plants grow by providing nutrients","They provide a variety of food sources for animals","They help living organisms survive and thrive","They help living organisms survive and thrive","Symbiotic relationships help living organisms survive and thrive by providing mutual benefits.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-impact-of-43b4a1b6","Science G6: Describing the components of the ecosystem and explaining - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-2 Describing the components of the ecosystem and explaining the impact of the availability of different resources in ecosystems on the survival and sustainability of living organisms, and proposing solutions to problems that affect the stability of the ecosystem.","1. Describing the biotic and abiotic components of ecosystems and their interaction to provide the needs of living organisms, and its impact on the survival and stability of the ecosystem.","What is the impact of climate change on an ecosystem?","It makes the ecosystem more stable","It helps plants grow faster","It disrupts the balance of the ecosystem and can lead to the extinction of species","It makes the air cooler","It disrupts the balance of the ecosystem and can lead to the extinction of species","Climate change can disrupt the balance of the ecosystem and can lead to the extinction of species, making it more fragile.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-importance-of-51e86f30","Science G6: Describing the components of the ecosystem and explaining - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-2 Describing the components of the ecosystem and explaining the impact of the availability of different resources in ecosystems on the survival and sustainability of living organisms, and proposing solutions to problems that affect the stability of the ecosystem.","2. Determining the causes of changes in habitats and their impact on plants and animals that lives there.","What is the importance of conservation in an ecosystem?","It makes the ecosystem more fragile","It helps plants grow by providing nutrients","It provides a variety of food sources for animals","It helps living organisms survive and thrive","It helps living organisms survive and thrive","Conservation helps living organisms survive and thrive by protecting their habitats and preventing harm to the ecosystem.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-are-two-main-9fe18ac8","Science G6: Describing the components of the ecosystem and explaining - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-2 Describing the components of the ecosystem and explaining the impact of the availability of different resources in ecosystems on the survival and sustainability of living organisms, and proposing solutions to problems that affect the stability of the ecosystem.","1. Describing the biotic and abiotic components of ecosystems and their interaction to provide the needs of living organisms, and its impact on the survival and stability of the ecosystem.","What are the two main types of components found in an ecosystem?","Living things and non-living things","Animals and plants","Water and land","Plants and animals","Living things and non-living things","An ecosystem consists of living things (biotic) and non-living things (abiotic).","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-role-of-cb168aea","Science G6: Describing the components of the ecosystem and explaining - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-3 Representing the relationships between living organisms in which matter is circulated in the ecosystem, and identifying the relationship between plants and energy obtained from the sun to produce food.","1. Clarifying the cycle of matter between living organisms, and the transfer of energy in the ecosystem through food chain, and classify their different roles (producer, consumer, predator, decomposer)","What is the role of sunlight in an ecosystem?","It provides food for animals","It gives plants the energy to grow","It makes the air cooler","It makes the water warmer","It gives plants the energy to grow","Sunlight provides the energy that plants need to grow, which in turn supports the food chain.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-importance-of-6c09bb1d","Science G6: Describing the components of the ecosystem and explaining - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-3 Representing the relationships between living organisms in which matter is circulated in the ecosystem, and identifying the relationship between plants and energy obtained from the sun to produce food.","1. Clarifying the cycle of matter between living organisms, and the transfer of energy in the ecosystem through food chain, and classify their different roles (producer, consumer, predator, decomposer)","What is the importance of water in an ecosystem?","It provides shelter for animals","It gives plants the energy to grow","It helps plants make food through photosynthesis","It helps animals regulate their body temperature","It helps plants make food through photosynthesis","Water is essential for plants to make food through photosynthesis, which supports the food chain.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-impact-of-fe088441","Science G6: Describing the components of the ecosystem and explaining - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-4 Describing the effect of environmental changes on plants and animals that live in specific environments. Inferring how behavioral and structural adaptations can help plants and animals survive in their habitats","4. Describing the climatic conditions in different environments and their impact on living organisms.","What is the impact of pollution on an ecosystem?","It makes the air cleaner","It helps plants grow faster","It harms living organisms and disrupts the balance of the ecosystem","It makes the water colder","It harms living organisms and disrupts the balance of the ecosystem","Pollution can harm living organisms and disrupt the balance of the ecosystem, making it difficult for them to survive.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-role-of-8118d409","Science G6: Describing the components of the ecosystem and explaining - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-3 Representing the relationships between living organisms in which matter is circulated in the ecosystem, and identifying the relationship between plants and energy obtained from the sun to produce food.","1. Clarifying the cycle of matter between living organisms, and the transfer of energy in the ecosystem through food chain, and classify their different roles (producer, consumer, predator, decomposer)","What is the role of decomposers in an ecosystem?","They make food for animals","They help plants grow by providing nutrients","They break down dead plants and animals into nutrients","They make the air cooler","They break down dead plants and animals into nutrients","Decomposers break down dead plants and animals into nutrients, which are then used by other living organisms.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-importance-of-66286535","Science G6: Describing the components of the ecosystem and explaining - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-2 Describing the components of the ecosystem and explaining the impact of the availability of different resources in ecosystems on the survival and sustainability of living organisms, and proposing solutions to problems that affect the stability of the ecosystem.","1. Describing the biotic and abiotic components of ecosystems and their interaction to provide the needs of living organisms, and its impact on the survival and stability of the ecosystem.","What is the importance of biodiversity in an ecosystem?","It makes the ecosystem more fragile","It makes the ecosystem more stable","It provides a variety of food sources for animals","It makes the ecosystem less interesting","It provides a variety of food sources for animals","Biodiversity provides a variety of food sources for animals, making the ecosystem more stable and resilient.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-are-non-living-5f66df3e","Science G6: Describing the components of the ecosystem - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-2 Describing the components of the ecosystem and explaining the impact of the availability of different resources in ecosystems on the survival and sustainability of living organisms, and proposing solutions to problems that affect the stability of the ecosystem.","1. Describing the biotic and abiotic components of ecosystems and their interaction to provide the needs of living organisms, and its impact on the survival and stability of the ecosystem.","What are the non-living parts of an ecosystem called?","Abiotic components","Biotic components","Consumers","Decomposers","Abiotic components","Abiotic components are the non-living physical and chemical parts of an ecosystem, such as water, light, air, and soil.","/image-source/sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-are-non-living-5f66df3e.png","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-can-happen-to-0aa491bc","Science G6: Describing the components of the ecosystem - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-2 Describing the components of the ecosystem and explaining the impact of the availability of different resources in ecosystems on the survival and sustainability of living organisms, and proposing solutions to problems that affect the stability of the ecosystem.","2. Determining the causes of changes in habitats and their impact on plants and animals that lives there.","What can happen to an ecosystem when there is a lack of nutrients?","It becomes more diverse","It becomes less diverse and may even disappear","It becomes more fertile","It becomes more polluted","It becomes less diverse and may even disappear","A lack of nutrients can lead to a decrease in the diversity of plants and animals in an ecosystem, and may even cause it to disappear.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-importance-of-656a8623","Science G6: Describing the components of the ecosystem - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-3 Representing the relationships between living organisms in which matter is circulated in the ecosystem, and identifying the relationship between plants and energy obtained from the sun to produce food.","1. Clarifying the cycle of matter between living organisms, and the transfer of energy in the ecosystem through food chain, and classify their different roles (producer, consumer, predator, decomposer)","What is the importance of water in an ecosystem?","It provides food for animals","It gives plants the energy to grow","It helps plants absorb nutrients and oxygen","It makes the air clean","It helps plants absorb nutrients and oxygen","Water is essential for plants to absorb nutrients and oxygen, which in turn supports the food chain in an ecosystem.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-can-happen-to-af21671b","Science G6: Describing the components of the ecosystem - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-2 Describing the components of the ecosystem and explaining the impact of the availability of different resources in ecosystems on the survival and sustainability of living organisms, and proposing solutions to problems that affect the stability of the ecosystem.","2. Determining the causes of changes in habitats and their impact on plants and animals that lives there.","What can happen to an ecosystem when there is a change in temperature?","It becomes more diverse","It becomes less diverse and may even disappear","It becomes more fertile","It becomes more polluted","It becomes less diverse and may even disappear","A change in temperature can lead to a decrease in the diversity of plants and animals in an ecosystem, and may even cause it to disappear.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-role-of-56e99024","Science G6: Describing the components of the ecosystem - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-3 Representing the relationships between living organisms in which matter is circulated in the ecosystem, and identifying the relationship between plants and energy obtained from the sun to produce food.","1. Clarifying the cycle of matter between living organisms, and the transfer of energy in the ecosystem through food chain, and classify their different roles (producer, consumer, predator, decomposer)","What is the role of decomposers in an ecosystem?","They produce oxygen","They help plants grow","They break down dead plants and animals into nutrients","They make the air clean","They break down dead plants and animals into nutrients","Decomposers, such as bacteria and fungi, break down dead plants and animals into nutrients that can be used by other living organisms.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-can-happen-to-edd8532e","Science G6: Describing the components of the ecosystem - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-2 Describing the components of the ecosystem and explaining the impact of the availability of different resources in ecosystems on the survival and sustainability of living organisms, and proposing solutions to problems that affect the stability of the ecosystem.","1. Describing the biotic and abiotic components of ecosystems and their interaction to provide the needs of living organisms, and its impact on the survival and stability of the ecosystem.","What can happen to an ecosystem when there is an overabundance of a particular species?","It becomes more diverse","It becomes less diverse and may even disappear","It becomes more fertile","It becomes more polluted","It becomes less diverse and may even disappear","An overabundance of a particular species can lead to a decrease in the diversity of other species in an ecosystem, and may even cause it to disappear.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-importance-of-75a88c6b","Science G6: Describing the components of the ecosystem - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-3 Representing the relationships between living organisms in which matter is circulated in the ecosystem, and identifying the relationship between plants and energy obtained from the sun to produce food.","1. Clarifying the cycle of matter between living organisms, and the transfer of energy in the ecosystem through food chain, and classify their different roles (producer, consumer, predator, decomposer)","What is the importance of soil in an ecosystem?","It provides food for animals","It gives plants the energy to grow","It helps plants absorb water and nutrients","It makes the air clean","It helps plants absorb water and nutrients","Soil provides essential nutrients and water to plants, which in turn supports the food chain in an ecosystem.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-happens-to-an-7c2d72ef","Science G6: Describing the components of the ecosystem - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-2 Describing the components of the ecosystem and explaining the impact of the availability of different resources in ecosystems on the survival and sustainability of living organisms, and proposing solutions to problems that affect the stability of the ecosystem.","2. Determining the causes of changes in habitats and their impact on plants and animals that lives there.","What happens to an ecosystem when there is a lack of water?","It becomes more fertile","It becomes more diverse","It becomes less diverse and may even disappear","It becomes more polluted","It becomes less diverse and may even disappear","A lack of water can lead to a decrease in the diversity of plants and animals in an ecosystem, and may even cause it to disappear.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-role-of-04e59683","Science G6: Describing the components of the ecosystem - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-3 Representing the relationships between living organisms in which matter is circulated in the ecosystem, and identifying the relationship between plants and energy obtained from the sun to produce food.","1. Clarifying the cycle of matter between living organisms, and the transfer of energy in the ecosystem through food chain, and classify their different roles (producer, consumer, predator, decomposer)","What is the role of sunlight in an ecosystem?","It provides food for animals","It gives plants the energy to grow","It helps animals move faster","It makes the soil fertile","It gives plants the energy to grow","Sunlight provides the energy that plants need to grow, which in turn supports the food chain in an ecosystem.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-are-living-parts-a187af59","Science G6: Describing the components of the ecosystem - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-2 Describing the components of the ecosystem and explaining the impact of the availability of different resources in ecosystems on the survival and sustainability of living organisms, and proposing solutions to problems that affect the stability of the ecosystem.","2. Determining the causes of changes in habitats and their impact on plants and animals that lives there.","What are the living parts of an ecosystem called?","Plants and animals","Rocks and water","Soil and sunlight","Air and clouds","Plants and animals","The living parts of an ecosystem are called biotic components, which include plants and animals.","/image-source/sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-are-living-parts-a187af59.png","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-main-reason-b20663cf","Science G6: Describing the components of the ecosystem - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-2 Describing the components of the ecosystem and explaining the impact of the availability of different resources in ecosystems on the survival and sustainability of living organisms, and proposing solutions to problems that affect the stability of the ecosystem.","2. Determining the causes of changes in habitats and their impact on plants and animals that lives there.","What is the main reason why some animals migrate to different habitats?","They are looking for food to eat.","They are trying to escape from predators.","They are trying to find a place to live.","They are trying to find a mate.","They are trying to find a place to live.","Animals migrate to different habitats to find a place to live, which provides them with the necessary resources such as food, water, and shelter.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-main-reason-23b97f59","Science G6: Describing the components of the ecosystem - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-2 Describing the components of the ecosystem and explaining the impact of the availability of different resources in ecosystems on the survival and sustainability of living organisms, and proposing solutions to problems that affect the stability of the ecosystem.","1. Describing the biotic and abiotic components of ecosystems and their interaction to provide the needs of living organisms, and its impact on the survival and stability of the ecosystem.","What is the main reason why some plants are able to grow in areas with low levels of light?","They are able to absorb nutrients from the air.","They have adapted to the low levels of light.","They have deep roots to access water and nutrients.","They are able to grow in areas with low levels of light because they are able to photosynthesize quickly.","They have adapted to the low levels of light.","Some plants are able to grow in areas with low levels of light because they have adapted to the conditions, such as having a special type of leaf structure that is able to absorb what little light is available.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-main-reason-5ad52479","Science G6: Describing the components of the ecosystem - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-2 Describing the components of the ecosystem and explaining the impact of the availability of different resources in ecosystems on the survival and sustainability of living organisms, and proposing solutions to problems that affect the stability of the ecosystem.","1. Describing the biotic and abiotic components of ecosystems and their interaction to provide the needs of living organisms, and its impact on the survival and stability of the ecosystem.","What is the main reason why some animals are able to live in areas with high levels of noise?","They have adapted to the high levels of noise.","They are able to migrate to areas with lower levels of noise.","They are able to hibernate during the winter.","They have thick fur or feathers to keep warm.","They have adapted to the high levels of noise.","Some animals are able to live in areas with high levels of noise because they have adapted to the conditions, such as having a special type of auditory system that is able to filter out the noise.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-main-reason-750e8ebe","Science G6: Describing the components of the ecosystem - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-2 Describing the components of the ecosystem and explaining the impact of the availability of different resources in ecosystems on the survival and sustainability of living organisms, and proposing solutions to problems that affect the stability of the ecosystem.","1. Describing the biotic and abiotic components of ecosystems and their interaction to provide the needs of living organisms, and its impact on the survival and stability of the ecosystem.","What is the main reason why some plants are able to grow in areas with high levels of pollution?","They are able to absorb nutrients from the air.","They have adapted to the high levels of pollution.","They have deep roots to access water and nutrients.","They are able to grow in areas with high levels of pollution because they are able to photosynthesize quickly.","They have adapted to the high levels of pollution.","Some plants are able to grow in areas with high levels of pollution because they have adapted to the conditions, such as having a special type of root system that is able to absorb pollutants.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-main-reason-686a3a56","Science G6: Describing the components of the ecosystem - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-2 Describing the components of the ecosystem and explaining the impact of the availability of different resources in ecosystems on the survival and sustainability of living organisms, and proposing solutions to problems that affect the stability of the ecosystem.","1. Describing the biotic and abiotic components of ecosystems and their interaction to provide the needs of living organisms, and its impact on the survival and stability of the ecosystem.","What is the main reason why some animals are able to live in areas with low levels of oxygen?","They have adapted to the low levels of oxygen.","They are able to migrate to areas with higher levels of oxygen.","They are able to hibernate during the winter.","They have thick fur or feathers to keep warm.","They have adapted to the low levels of oxygen.","Some animals are able to live in areas with low levels of oxygen because they have adapted to the conditions, such as having a special type of respiratory system that is able to extract oxygen from the air.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-main-reason-9fe15549","Science G6: Describing the components of the ecosystem - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-2 Describing the components of the ecosystem and explaining the impact of the availability of different resources in ecosystems on the survival and sustainability of living organisms, and proposing solutions to problems that affect the stability of the ecosystem.","1. Describing the biotic and abiotic components of ecosystems and their interaction to provide the needs of living organisms, and its impact on the survival and stability of the ecosystem.","What is the main reason why some plants are able to grow in areas with high levels of salt?","They have adapted to the high levels of salt.","They are able to absorb nutrients from the air.","They have deep roots to access water and nutrients.","They are able to grow in areas with high levels of salt because they are able to photosynthesize quickly.","They have adapted to the high levels of salt.","Some plants are able to grow in areas with high levels of salt because they have adapted to the conditions, such as having a special type of root system that is able to absorb salt.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-main-reason-bc40da4a","Science G6: Describing the components of the ecosystem - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-2 Describing the components of the ecosystem and explaining the impact of the availability of different resources in ecosystems on the survival and sustainability of living organisms, and proposing solutions to problems that affect the stability of the ecosystem.","1. Describing the biotic and abiotic components of ecosystems and their interaction to provide the needs of living organisms, and its impact on the survival and stability of the ecosystem.","What is the main reason why some animals are able to live in areas with extreme temperatures?","They have thick fur or feathers to keep warm.","They are able to hibernate during the winter.","They are able to migrate to warmer areas.","They have adapted to the extreme temperatures.","They have adapted to the extreme temperatures.","Some animals are able to live in areas with extreme temperatures because they have adapted to the conditions, such as having a thick layer of fat or being able to slow down their metabolism.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-main-reason-5ff70c36","Science G6: Describing the components of the ecosystem - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-2 Describing the components of the ecosystem and explaining the impact of the availability of different resources in ecosystems on the survival and sustainability of living organisms, and proposing solutions to problems that affect the stability of the ecosystem.","1. Describing the biotic and abiotic components of ecosystems and their interaction to provide the needs of living organisms, and its impact on the survival and stability of the ecosystem.","What is the main reason why some plants are able to grow in areas with poor soil?","They have deep roots to access water and nutrients.","They have adapted to the poor soil conditions.","They are able to absorb nutrients from the air.","They are able to grow in areas with poor soil because they are able to photosynthesize quickly.","They have adapted to the poor soil conditions.","Some plants are able to grow in areas with poor soil because they have adapted to the poor soil conditions, such as having a shallow root system or being able to absorb nutrients from the air.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-why-do-some-c8b8c4eb","Science G6: Describing the components of the ecosystem - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-2 Describing the components of the ecosystem and explaining the impact of the availability of different resources in ecosystems on the survival and sustainability of living organisms, and proposing solutions to problems that affect the stability of the ecosystem.","1. Describing the biotic and abiotic components of ecosystems and their interaction to provide the needs of living organisms, and its impact on the survival and stability of the ecosystem.","Why do some animals hibernate during the winter?","They are trying to escape from the cold weather.","They are trying to find a place to live.","They are trying to conserve energy and survive the winter.","They are trying to find food to eat.","They are trying to conserve energy and survive the winter.","Animals hibernate during the winter to conserve energy and survive the winter by slowing down their metabolism and reducing their body temperature.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-happens-to-a-8e91ae93","Science G6: Describing the components of the ecosystem - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-2 Describing the components of the ecosystem and explaining the impact of the availability of different resources in ecosystems on the survival and sustainability of living organisms, and proposing solutions to problems that affect the stability of the ecosystem.","1. Describing the biotic and abiotic components of ecosystems and their interaction to provide the needs of living organisms, and its impact on the survival and stability of the ecosystem.","What happens to a plant if it does not receive enough water?","It will grow faster and stronger.","It will stay the same size and shape.","It will start to wilt and turn yellow.","It will die quickly.","It will start to wilt and turn yellow.","If a plant does not receive enough water, it will start to wilt and turn yellow because it is unable to carry out photosynthesis and transport nutrients.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-term-a-ac832cf0","Science G6: Describing the concept of force and distinguishing between - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-2-1 Describing the concept of force and distinguishing between different types","1. Determining the relationship of distance to motion and explaining how the position of an object can be determined using a reference point.","What is the term for a push or a pull that can cause an object to change its motion?","Energy","Force","Motion","Friction","Force","Force is a push or a pull that can cause an object to change its motion, shape, or size.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-term-amount-b94fc6fa","Science G6: Describing the concept of force and distinguishing between - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-1-1 Exploring the physical properties of matter, distinguishing the molecular structure of its different states, and explaining the change of matter states due to heat.","2. Distinguishing the different materials in terms of the physical properties of the substance that can be calculated or measured, such as mass, volume, density, buoyancy, color and boiling point","What is the term for the amount of matter in an object?","Weight","Mass","Volume","Density","Mass","Mass is a measure of the amount of matter in an object, and it is an important concept in understanding forces and motion.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-type-of-force-4169a1c5","Science G6: Describing the concept of force and distinguishing between - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-2-1 Describing the concept of force and distinguishing between different types","3. Distinguishing between types of forces based on their causes (gravity, friction, and magnetism).","What type of force is responsible for keeping a chair on the floor?","Gravity","Friction","Normal force","Magnetic force","Normal force","The normal force is the force exerted by the floor on the chair, keeping it in place and preventing it from falling through the floor.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-term-point-381da486","Science G6: Describing the concept of force and distinguishing between - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-2-1 Describing the concept of force and distinguishing between different types","1. Determining the relationship of distance to motion and explaining how the position of an object can be determined using a reference point.","What is the term for the point from which the position of an object is measured?","Reference frame","Coordinate system","Reference point","Origin","Reference point","A reference point is the point from which the position of an object is measured, allowing us to determine its location and distance from other objects.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-happens-to-distance-71eb7da7","Science G6: Describing the concept of force and distinguishing between - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-2-1 Describing the concept of force and distinguishing between different types","1. Determining the relationship of distance to motion and explaining how the position of an object can be determined using a reference point.","What happens to the distance an object travels when the force applied to it increases?","It decreases","It stays the same","It increases","It becomes zero","It increases","When the force applied to an object increases, the distance it travels also increases, as the object accelerates and gains more speed.","/image-source/sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-happens-to-distance-71eb7da7.png","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-relationship-distance-4ede525e","Science G6: Describing the concept of force and distinguishing between - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-2-1 Describing the concept of force and distinguishing between different types","1. Determining the relationship of distance to motion and explaining how the position of an object can be determined using a reference point.","What is the relationship between distance and motion?","Distance decreases as motion increases","Distance increases as motion decreases","Distance is not related to motion","Distance increases as motion increases","Distance increases as motion increases","As an object moves, it covers a greater distance, so distance and motion are directly related.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-happens-to-an-923919cd","Science G6: Describing the concept of force and distinguishing between - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-2-1 Describing the concept of force and distinguishing between different types","1. Determining the relationship of distance to motion and explaining how the position of an object can be determined using a reference point.","What happens to an object when a force is applied to it?","It remains at rest","It changes its motion","It disappears","It becomes heavier","It changes its motion","When a force is applied to an object, it can cause the object to change its motion, either by speeding up, slowing down, or changing direction.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-how-can-you-60489348","Science G6: Describing the concept of force and distinguishing between - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-2-1 Describing the concept of force and distinguishing between different types","1. Determining the relationship of distance to motion and explaining how the position of an object can be determined using a reference point.","How can you determine the position of an object using a reference point?","By measuring its speed","By measuring its distance from the reference point","By measuring its mass","By measuring its volume","By measuring its distance from the reference point","By measuring the distance between the object and a reference point, you can determine the object's position relative to that point.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-type-of-force-bb55a8ec","Science G6: Describing the concept of force and distinguishing between - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-2-1 Describing the concept of force and distinguishing between different types","3. Distinguishing between types of forces based on their causes (gravity, friction, and magnetism).","What type of force is responsible for keeping a ball on the ground when you throw it upwards?","Gravity","Friction","Normal force","Magnetic force","Gravity","Gravity is the force that pulls objects towards each other, and it is what keeps the ball on the ground when you throw it upwards.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-type-of-force-de538c2f","Science G6: Describing the concept of force and distinguishing between - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-2-1 Describing the concept of force and distinguishing between different types","1. Determining the relationship of distance to motion and explaining how the position of an object can be determined using a reference point.","What type of force opposes motion between two surfaces that are in contact?","Normal force","Friction","Gravity","Magnetic force","Friction","Friction is the force that opposes motion between two surfaces that are in contact, and it can cause objects to slow down or come to a stop.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-term-process-a5afdf36","Science G6: Describing the different patterns in the - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-3 Representing the relationships between living organisms in which matter is circulated in the ecosystem, and identifying the relationship between plants and energy obtained from the sun to produce food.","3. Explaining the process of photosynthesis and its role in determining the relationship between plants and the energy obtained from the sun to produce food.","What is the term for the process by which plants make their own food?","Photosynthesis is the process by which plants make their own food.","Respiration is the process by which plants make their own food.","Transpiration is the process by which plants make their own food.","Decomposition is the process by which plants make their own food.","Photosynthesis is the process by which plants make their own food.","Photosynthesis is the process by which plants make their own food using sunlight, water, and carbon dioxide.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-main-difference-472d474f","Science G6: Describing the different patterns in the - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-1-4 Describing the different patterns in the life cycles of animals and plants and the changes accom-panying them, and comparing them.","2.Describing the changes that occur to animals and plants during life cycles and predict them based on the pattern of reproduction and the life cycle.","What is the main difference between the life cycle of a butterfly and a frog?","A butterfly goes through four stages of life, while a frog goes through three stages.","A butterfly undergoes metamorphosis, but a frog does not.","A butterfly lays eggs, while a frog lays tadpoles.","A butterfly has a complete life cycle, while a frog has an incomplete life cycle.","A butterfly lays eggs, while a frog lays tadpoles.","A butterfly lays eggs, while a frog lays tadpoles. This is a key difference between their life cycles.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-main-difference-d3821629","Science G6: Describing the different patterns in the - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-1-4 Describing the different patterns in the life cycles of animals and plants and the changes accom-panying them, and comparing them.","2.Describing the changes that occur to animals and plants during life cycles and predict them based on the pattern of reproduction and the life cycle.","What is the main difference between the life cycle of a plant and an animal?","A plant has a complete life cycle, while an animal has an incomplete life cycle.","A plant undergoes photosynthesis, but an animal does not.","A plant grows from a seed, while an animal grows from an egg.","A plant has a fixed number of cells, while an animal has a variable number of cells.","A plant has a complete life cycle, while an animal has an incomplete life cycle.","A plant has a complete life cycle, which includes growth, reproduction, and death, while an animal has an incomplete life cycle, which includes growth and reproduction, but not death.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-term-process-5c36edc3","Science G6: Describing the different patterns in the - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-1-4 Describing the different patterns in the life cycles of animals and plants and the changes accom-panying them, and comparing them.","1.Describing the different patterns in life cycles of different animals (insects, am-phibians, and mammals) and different plants, and comparing them.","What is the term for the process by which an animal grows and develops from a fertilized egg?","Growth is the process by which an animal grows and develops from a fertilized egg.","Development is the process by which an animal grows and develops from a fertilized egg.","Metamorphosis is the process by which an animal grows and develops from a fertilized egg.","Differentiation is the process by which an animal grows and develops from a fertilized egg.","Development is the process by which an animal grows and develops from a fertilized egg.","Development is the process by which an animal grows and develops from a fertilized egg, involving cell division, growth, and differentiation.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-main-difference-978a773f","Science G6: Describing the different patterns in the - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-1-4 Describing the different patterns in the life cycles of animals and plants and the changes accom-panying them, and comparing them.","1.Describing the different patterns in life cycles of different animals (insects, am-phibians, and mammals) and different plants, and comparing them.","What is the main difference between the life cycle of a mammal and a bird?","A mammal gives birth to live young, while a bird lays eggs.","A mammal has a fixed number of cells, while a bird has a variable number of cells.","A mammal undergoes metamorphosis, while a bird does not.","A mammal has a complete life cycle, while a bird has an incomplete life cycle.","A mammal gives birth to live young, while a bird lays eggs.","A mammal gives birth to live young, while a bird lays eggs, which is a key difference between their life cycles.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-term-process-35c4aa37","Science G6: Describing the different patterns in the - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-1-1 Exploring the physical properties of matter, distinguishing the molecular structure of its different states, and explaining the change of matter states due to heat.","1. Explaining the changes in the composition and properties of matter as a result of a chemical reaction. Concluding that the mass of the substance remains preserved during the chemical reaction and when forming mixtures.","What is the term for the process by which an animal changes its form or structure?","Metamorphosis is the process by which an animal changes its form or structure.","Growth is the process by which an animal changes its form or structure.","Development is the process by which an animal changes its form or structure.","Differentiation is the process by which an animal changes its form or structure.","Metamorphosis is the process by which an animal changes its form or structure.","Metamorphosis is the process by which an animal changes its form or structure, often involving a dramatic change in appearance or function.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-main-difference-a750422d","Science G6: Describing the different patterns in the - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-1-4 Describing the different patterns in the life cycles of animals and plants and the changes accom-panying them, and comparing them.","2.Describing the changes that occur to animals and plants during life cycles and predict them based on the pattern of reproduction and the life cycle.","What is the main difference between the life cycle of a plant and a fungus?","A plant grows from a seed, while a fungus grows from a spore.","A plant undergoes photosynthesis, but a fungus does not.","A plant has a fixed number of cells, while a fungus has a variable number of cells.","A plant has a complete life cycle, while a fungus has an incomplete life cycle.","A plant grows from a seed, while a fungus grows from a spore.","A plant grows from a seed, while a fungus grows from a spore, which is a key difference between their life cycles.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-term-process-b33c9ab6","Science G6: Describing the different patterns in the - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-1-4 Describing the different patterns in the life cycles of animals and plants and the changes accom-panying them, and comparing them.","1.Describing the different patterns in life cycles of different animals (insects, am-phibians, and mammals) and different plants, and comparing them.","What is the term for the process by which an organism produces offspring?","Reproduction is the process by which an organism produces offspring.","Growth is the process by which an organism produces offspring.","Development is the process by which an organism produces offspring.","Differentiation is the process by which an organism produces offspring.","Reproduction is the process by which an organism produces offspring.","Reproduction is the process by which an organism produces offspring, involving the creation of new individuals through various mechanisms such as sexual or asexual reproduction.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-main-difference-889f10c8","Science G6: Describing the different patterns in the - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-1-4 Describing the different patterns in the life cycles of animals and plants and the changes accom-panying them, and comparing them.","1.Describing the different patterns in life cycles of different animals (insects, am-phibians, and mammals) and different plants, and comparing them.","What is the main difference between the life cycle of an insect and a mammal?","An insect undergoes metamorphosis, while a mammal does not.","An insect has a fixed number of cells, while a mammal has a variable number of cells.","An insect grows from an egg, while a mammal grows from a fertilized egg.","An insect has a complete life cycle, while a mammal has an incomplete life cycle.","An insect undergoes metamorphosis, while a mammal does not.","An insect undergoes metamorphosis, which involves a dramatic change in form or structure, while a mammal does not undergo such a transformation.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-term-process-7f8cf70a","Science G6: Describing the different patterns in the - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-1-4 Describing the different patterns in the life cycles of animals and plants and the changes accom-panying them, and comparing them.","1.Describing the different patterns in life cycles of different animals (insects, am-phibians, and mammals) and different plants, and comparing them.","What is the term for the process by which an organism grows and develops?","Growth is the process by which an organism grows and develops.","Development is the process by which an organism grows and develops.","Metamorphosis is the process by which an organism grows and develops.","Differentiation is the process by which an organism grows and develops.","Growth is the process by which an organism grows and develops.","Growth is the process by which an organism grows and develops, involving an increase in size or complexity over time.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-main-difference-b1e9cf96","Science G6: Describing the different patterns in the - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-1-4 Describing the different patterns in the life cycles of animals and plants and the changes accom-panying them, and comparing them.","1.Describing the different patterns in life cycles of different animals (insects, am-phibians, and mammals) and different plants, and comparing them.","What is the main difference between the life cycle of a mammal and a fish?","Mammals give birth to live young, while fish lay eggs.","Mammals live on land, while fish live in water.","Mammals have fur, while fish have scales.","Mammals eat plants, while fish eat meat.","Mammals give birth to live young, while fish lay eggs.","Mammals give birth to live young, while fish lay eggs, which is a key difference in their life cycles.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-purpose-of-8dae6947","Science G6: Describing the different patterns in the - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-1-4 Describing the different patterns in the life cycles of animals and plants and the changes accom-panying them, and comparing them.","2.Describing the changes that occur to animals and plants during life cycles and predict them based on the pattern of reproduction and the life cycle.","What is the purpose of a seed in the life cycle of a plant?","To provide food for animals.","To protect the plant from harm.","To grow into a new plant.","To store water for the plant.","To grow into a new plant.","A seed is a small plant that grows into a new plant, allowing the species to reproduce and continue its life cycle.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-main-difference-6918d959","Science G6: Describing the different patterns in the - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-1-4 Describing the different patterns in the life cycles of animals and plants and the changes accom-panying them, and comparing them.","2.Describing the changes that occur to animals and plants during life cycles and predict them based on the pattern of reproduction and the life cycle.","What is the main difference between the life cycle of a plant and an animal?","Plants make their own food, while animals eat food.","Plants live on land, while animals live in water.","Plants have roots, while animals have legs.","Plants reproduce by seeds, while animals reproduce by eggs.","Plants make their own food, while animals eat food.","Plants make their own food through photosynthesis, while animals eat food to survive, which is a key difference in their life cycles.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-main-change-e6ec1d20","Science G6: Describing the different patterns in the - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-1-4 Describing the different patterns in the life cycles of animals and plants and the changes accom-panying them, and comparing them.","2.Describing the changes that occur to animals and plants during life cycles and predict them based on the pattern of reproduction and the life cycle.","What is the main change that occurs in the life cycle of a butterfly?","The butterfly grows bigger and stronger.","The butterfly lays eggs and starts a new generation.","The caterpillar transforms into a beautiful butterfly.","The butterfly migrates to a new location for the winter.","The caterpillar transforms into a beautiful butterfly.","In the life cycle of a butterfly, the caterpillar transforms into a beautiful butterfly through a process called metamorphosis.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-purpose-of-b4dd1cd9","Science G6: Describing the different patterns in the - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-1-4 Describing the different patterns in the life cycles of animals and plants and the changes accom-panying them, and comparing them.","2.Describing the changes that occur to animals and plants during life cycles and predict them based on the pattern of reproduction and the life cycle.","What is the purpose of a flower in the life cycle of a plant?","To provide food for animals.","To protect the plant from harm.","To make seeds and reproduce.","To store water for the plant.","To make seeds and reproduce.","A flower is a part of the plant that makes seeds and reproduces, allowing the species to continue its life cycle.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-purpose-of-64b8ab6e","Science G6: Describing the different patterns in the - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-3 Representing the relationships between living organisms in which matter is circulated in the ecosystem, and identifying the relationship between plants and energy obtained from the sun to produce food.","1. Clarifying the cycle of matter between living organisms, and the transfer of energy in the ecosystem through food chain, and classify their different roles (producer, consumer, predator, decomposer)","What is the purpose of a pupa in the life cycle of a butterfly?","To protect the butterfly from predators.","To provide food for the butterfly.","To transform the caterpillar into a butterfly.","To store energy for the butterfly.","To transform the caterpillar into a butterfly.","A pupa is a stage in the life cycle of a butterfly where the caterpillar transforms into a butterfly through a process called metamorphosis.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-main-change-c0f0c95d","Science G6: Describing the different patterns in the - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-1-4 Describing the different patterns in the life cycles of animals and plants and the changes accom-panying them, and comparing them.","2.Describing the changes that occur to animals and plants during life cycles and predict them based on the pattern of reproduction and the life cycle.","What is the main change that occurs in the life cycle of a frog?","The frog grows bigger and stronger.","The frog lays eggs and starts a new generation.","The tadpole transforms into a frog.","The frog migrates to a new location for the winter.","The tadpole transforms into a frog.","In the life cycle of a frog, the tadpole transforms into a frog through a process called metamorphosis.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-purpose-of-4fdc3954","Science G6: Describing the different patterns in the - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-1-4 Describing the different patterns in the life cycles of animals and plants and the changes accom-panying them, and comparing them.","2.Describing the changes that occur to animals and plants during life cycles and predict them based on the pattern of reproduction and the life cycle.","What is the purpose of a seedling in the life cycle of a plant?","To provide food for animals.","To protect the plant from harm.","To grow into a mature plant.","To store water for the plant.","To grow into a mature plant.","A seedling is a young plant that grows into a mature plant, allowing the species to continue its life cycle.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-main-change-b3a6ebf0","Science G6: Describing the different patterns in the - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-1-4 Describing the different patterns in the life cycles of animals and plants and the changes accom-panying them, and comparing them.","2.Describing the changes that occur to animals and plants during life cycles and predict them based on the pattern of reproduction and the life cycle.","What is the main change that occurs in the life cycle of a tree?","The tree grows bigger and stronger.","The tree lays seeds and starts a new generation.","The seedling transforms into a mature tree.","The tree migrates to a new location for the winter.","The seedling transforms into a mature tree.","In the life cycle of a tree, the seedling transforms into a mature tree through a process of growth and development.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-purpose-of-20836f54","Science G6: Describing the different patterns in the - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-3 Representing the relationships between living organisms in which matter is circulated in the ecosystem, and identifying the relationship between plants and energy obtained from the sun to produce food.","1. Clarifying the cycle of matter between living organisms, and the transfer of energy in the ecosystem through food chain, and classify their different roles (producer, consumer, predator, decomposer)","What is the purpose of a cocoon in the life cycle of a butterfly?","To protect the butterfly from predators.","To provide food for the butterfly.","To transform the caterpillar into a butterfly.","To store energy for the butterfly.","To transform the caterpillar into a butterfly.","A cocoon is a stage in the life cycle of a butterfly where the caterpillar transforms into a butterfly through a process called metamorphosis.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-main-reason-5cd13d8a","Science G6: Describing the effect of environmental changes - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-4 Describing the effect of environmental changes on plants and animals that live in specific environments. Inferring how behavioral and structural adaptations can help plants and animals survive in their habitats","3. Describing how structural and behavioral adaptations can help plants and animals live and survive in specific habitats.","What is the main reason why some animals migrate to higher elevations during the summer?","To find more food and shelter.","To escape from predators and find safety.","To find cooler temperatures and avoid heat stress.","To change their color and blend in with their surroundings.","To find cooler temperatures and avoid heat stress.","Some animals migrate to higher elevations during the summer to find cooler temperatures and avoid heat stress.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-why-do-some-3d69d6f5","Science G6: Describing the effect of environmental changes - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-4 Describing the effect of environmental changes on plants and animals that live in specific environments. Inferring how behavioral and structural adaptations can help plants and animals survive in their habitats","3. Describing how structural and behavioral adaptations can help plants and animals live and survive in specific habitats.","Why do some plants have sharp thorns?","To attract pollinators like bees and butterflies.","To repel predators and insects.","To conserve water and prevent water loss.","To protect themselves from herbivores and predators.","To protect themselves from herbivores and predators.","Plants have sharp thorns to protect themselves from herbivores and predators.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-how-do-some-d79bd818","Science G6: Describing the effect of environmental changes - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-4 Describing the effect of environmental changes on plants and animals that live in specific environments. Inferring how behavioral and structural adaptations can help plants and animals survive in their habitats","3. Describing how structural and behavioral adaptations can help plants and animals live and survive in specific habitats.","How do some plants adapt to living in salty environments?","By growing in areas with low salt levels.","By producing chemicals that repel salt.","By storing water and salt in their leaves.","By developing roots that can absorb salt and water.","By developing roots that can absorb salt and water.","Some plants adapt to living in salty environments by developing roots that can absorb salt and water.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-main-reason-16c88099","Science G6: Describing the effect of environmental changes - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-4 Describing the effect of environmental changes on plants and animals that live in specific environments. Inferring how behavioral and structural adaptations can help plants and animals survive in their habitats","3. Describing how structural and behavioral adaptations can help plants and animals live and survive in specific habitats.","What is the main reason why some animals hibernate during the winter?","To find more food and shelter.","To escape from predators and find safety.","To conserve energy and survive the cold temperatures.","To change their color and blend in with their surroundings.","To conserve energy and survive the cold temperatures.","Some animals hibernate during the winter to conserve energy and survive the cold temperatures.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-why-do-some-e2d17a18","Science G6: Describing the effect of environmental changes - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-4 Describing the effect of environmental changes on plants and animals that live in specific environments. Inferring how behavioral and structural adaptations can help plants and animals survive in their habitats","3. Describing how structural and behavioral adaptations can help plants and animals live and survive in specific habitats.","Why do some plants have waxy leaves?","To attract pollinators like bees and butterflies.","To repel predators and insects.","To conserve water and prevent water loss.","To grow faster and stronger.","To conserve water and prevent water loss.","Plants have waxy leaves to conserve water and prevent water loss, which helps them survive in dry environments.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-main-reason-4cc402e9","Science G6: Describing the effect of environmental changes - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-4 Describing the effect of environmental changes on plants and animals that live in specific environments. Inferring how behavioral and structural adaptations can help plants and animals survive in their habitats","3. Describing how structural and behavioral adaptations can help plants and animals live and survive in specific habitats.","What is the main reason why some animals have large ears?","To hear predators coming from far away.","To regulate their body temperature.","To communicate with other animals.","To see better in the dark.","To hear predators coming from far away.","Some animals have large ears to hear predators coming from far away, which helps them stay safe.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-how-do-some-b5e6cb0e","Science G6: Describing the effect of environmental changes - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-4 Describing the effect of environmental changes on plants and animals that live in specific environments. Inferring how behavioral and structural adaptations can help plants and animals survive in their habitats","3. Describing how structural and behavioral adaptations can help plants and animals live and survive in specific habitats.","How do some animals adapt to living in cold environments?","By growing thicker fur or feathers.","By changing the color of their skin to blend in with their surroundings.","By moving to warmer locations.","By eating more food to stay warm.","By growing thicker fur or feathers.","Some animals adapt to living in cold environments by growing thicker fur or feathers to keep warm.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-main-reason-eb1b4deb","Science G6: Describing the effect of environmental changes - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-4 Describing the effect of environmental changes on plants and animals that live in specific environments. Inferring how behavioral and structural adaptations can help plants and animals survive in their habitats","3. Describing how structural and behavioral adaptations can help plants and animals live and survive in specific habitats.","What is the main reason why some plants have deep roots?","To absorb more sunlight and water.","To grow taller and reach more sunlight.","To anchor themselves in the soil and prevent being uprooted.","To store food and water for later use.","To anchor themselves in the soil and prevent being uprooted.","Plants have deep roots to anchor themselves in the soil and prevent being uprooted by strong winds or water.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-why-do-some-7fc54afa","Science G6: Describing the effect of environmental changes - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-4 Describing the effect of environmental changes on plants and animals that live in specific environments. Inferring how behavioral and structural adaptations can help plants and animals survive in their habitats","3. Describing how structural and behavioral adaptations can help plants and animals live and survive in specific habitats.","Why do some animals migrate to different locations during certain times of the year?","To find more food and shelter.","To escape from predators and find safety.","To find a mate and reproduce.","To change their color and blend in with their surroundings.","To find more food and shelter.","Animals migrate to find more food and shelter, which is essential for their survival.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-happens-to-a-3dec9daa","Science G6: Describing the effect of environmental changes - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-4 Describing the effect of environmental changes on plants and animals that live in specific environments. Inferring how behavioral and structural adaptations can help plants and animals survive in their habitats","3. Describing how structural and behavioral adaptations can help plants and animals live and survive in specific habitats.","What happens to a plant's growth when it is placed in a very cold environment?","The plant grows faster and stronger.","The plant's growth slows down and it may die.","The plant stays the same size and doesn't change.","The plant grows in a different direction.","The plant's growth slows down and it may die.","Plants grow slower or may die when placed in a very cold environment because their cells are damaged by the cold temperature.","/image-source/sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-happens-to-a-3dec9daa.png","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-why-do-some-b390d0ae","Science G6: Describing the effect of environmental changes - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-4 Describing the effect of environmental changes on plants and animals that live in specific environments. Inferring how behavioral and structural adaptations can help plants and animals survive in their habitats","3. Describing how structural and behavioral adaptations can help plants and animals live and survive in specific habitats.","Why do some animals in the Arctic have antifreeze proteins in their blood?","To keep them warm in the cold weather.","To help them blend in with their surroundings.","To prevent their blood from freezing in the cold temperatures.","To attract prey.","To prevent their blood from freezing in the cold temperatures.","The antifreeze proteins in the blood of Arctic animals help to prevent their blood from freezing in the cold temperatures, allowing them to survive in the harsh Arctic environment.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-impact-of-8fee5131","Science G6: Describing the effect of environmental changes - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-4 Describing the effect of environmental changes on plants and animals that live in specific environments. Inferring how behavioral and structural adaptations can help plants and animals survive in their habitats","3. Describing how structural and behavioral adaptations can help plants and animals live and survive in specific habitats.","What is the impact of a sudden change in humidity on a rainforest ecosystem?","It causes an increase in plant growth.","It leads to a decrease in animal populations.","It results in the death of many plants and animals.","It has no impact on the ecosystem.","It results in the death of many plants and animals.","A sudden change in humidity can lead to the death of many plants and animals in a rainforest ecosystem due to the disruption of their delicate water balance.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-how-do-plants-fe7123a9","Science G6: Describing the effect of environmental changes - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-4 Describing the effect of environmental changes on plants and animals that live in specific environments. Inferring how behavioral and structural adaptations can help plants and animals survive in their habitats","4. Describing the climatic conditions in different environments and their impact on living organisms.","How do plants in a mountainous environment adapt to the low oxygen levels?","They grow taller to reach more sunlight.","They develop deep roots to absorb oxygen.","They produce specialized roots to breathe air.","They don't need to adapt to the low oxygen levels.","They produce specialized roots to breathe air.","Plants in a mountainous environment produce specialized roots to breathe air, allowing them to survive in low-oxygen conditions.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-effect-of-4f96d7e8","Science G6: Describing the effect of environmental changes - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-4 Describing the effect of environmental changes on plants and animals that live in specific environments. Inferring how behavioral and structural adaptations can help plants and animals survive in their habitats","3. Describing how structural and behavioral adaptations can help plants and animals live and survive in specific habitats.","What is the effect of a strong wind on a grassland ecosystem?","It leads to an increase in plant growth.","It causes a decrease in animal populations.","It results in the death of many plants and animals.","It has no impact on the ecosystem.","It results in the death of many plants and animals.","A strong wind can lead to the death of many plants and animals in a grassland ecosystem due to the physical damage caused by the wind.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-why-do-some-f4db6f4c","Science G6: Describing the effect of environmental changes - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-4 Describing the effect of environmental changes on plants and animals that live in specific environments. Inferring how behavioral and structural adaptations can help plants and animals survive in their habitats","3. Describing how structural and behavioral adaptations can help plants and animals live and survive in specific habitats.","Why do some animals in the desert have large ears?","To keep them warm in the cold weather.","To help them blend in with their surroundings.","To radiate excess heat and stay cool.","To attract prey.","To radiate excess heat and stay cool.","The large ears of desert animals help them to radiate excess heat and stay cool in the hot desert environment.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-impact-of-2a85d3d5","Science G6: Describing the effect of environmental changes - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-4 Describing the effect of environmental changes on plants and animals that live in specific environments. Inferring how behavioral and structural adaptations can help plants and animals survive in their habitats","3. Describing how structural and behavioral adaptations can help plants and animals live and survive in specific habitats.","What is the impact of a sudden change in temperature on a coral reef ecosystem?","It causes an increase in fish populations.","It leads to a decrease in coral growth.","It results in the death of many coral polyps.","It has no impact on the ecosystem.","It results in the death of many coral polyps.","A sudden change in temperature can lead to the death of many coral polyps in a coral reef ecosystem, causing a decline in the overall health of the reef.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-how-do-plants-67a17a33","Science G6: Describing the effect of environmental changes - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-4 Describing the effect of environmental changes on plants and animals that live in specific environments. Inferring how behavioral and structural adaptations can help plants and animals survive in their habitats","4. Describing the climatic conditions in different environments and their impact on living organisms.","How do plants in a swamp environment adapt to the presence of water?","They grow taller to reach more sunlight.","They develop deep roots to absorb oxygen.","They produce specialized roots to breathe air.","They don't need to adapt to the water.","They produce specialized roots to breathe air.","Plants in a swamp environment develop specialized roots to breathe air, allowing them to survive in low-oxygen conditions.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-effect-of-6ee575e9","Science G6: Describing the effect of environmental changes - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-4 Describing the effect of environmental changes on plants and animals that live in specific environments. Inferring how behavioral and structural adaptations can help plants and animals survive in their habitats","3. Describing how structural and behavioral adaptations can help plants and animals live and survive in specific habitats.","What is the effect of a prolonged drought on a forest ecosystem?","It leads to an increase in plant growth.","It causes a decrease in animal populations.","It results in the death of many plants and animals.","It has no impact on the ecosystem.","It results in the death of many plants and animals.","A prolonged drought can lead to the death of many plants and animals in a forest ecosystem due to the lack of water.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-why-do-some-680ea507","Science G6: Describing the effect of environmental changes - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-4 Describing the effect of environmental changes on plants and animals that live in specific environments. Inferring how behavioral and structural adaptations can help plants and animals survive in their habitats","3. Describing how structural and behavioral adaptations can help plants and animals live and survive in specific habitats.","Why do some animals in the Arctic have white fur?","To keep them warm in the cold weather.","To help them blend in with their snowy surroundings.","To scare away predators.","To attract prey.","To help them blend in with their snowy surroundings.","The white fur of Arctic animals helps them blend in with their snowy surroundings, making it easier for them to hunt and avoid predators.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-happens-to-plants-0d9b3928","Science G6: Describing the effect of environmental changes - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-4 Describing the effect of environmental changes on plants and animals that live in specific environments. Inferring how behavioral and structural adaptations can help plants and animals survive in their habitats","2. Predicting the changes that will happen to living organisms as a result of  of changes in their environments.","What happens to plants in a desert environment due to the lack of water?","They grow faster and stronger.","They adapt to store water in their stems.","They die quickly due to dehydration.","They don't need water to survive.","They die quickly due to dehydration.","Plants in a desert environment have adaptations to conserve water, but if they don't have enough water, they will eventually die.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-main-difference-dc4e3abb","Science G6: Describing the factors and processes that affected the Earth's - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-3-2-3 Describing the factors and processes that affected the Earth›s surface which changed some of its","1. Distinguishing mineral and its properties and describing its relationship to rocks, and how they are formed.","What is the main difference between an igneous rock and a sedimentary rock?","Igneous rocks are formed from the cooling of magma, while sedimentary rocks are formed from the compression of sediments.","Igneous rocks are formed from the cooling of lava, while sedimentary rocks are formed from the cooling of magma.","Igneous rocks are formed from the cooling of lava, while sedimentary rocks are formed from the compression of sediments.","Igneous rocks are formed from the compression of sediments, while sedimentary rocks are formed from the cooling of magma.","Igneous rocks are formed from the cooling of magma, while sedimentary rocks are formed from the compression of sediments.","Igneous rocks are formed from the cooling of magma, while sedimentary rocks are formed from the compression of sediments.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-term-process-a757b7a9","Science G6: Describing the factors and processes that affected the Earth's - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-3-2-3 Describing the factors and processes that affected the Earth›s surface which changed some of its","3. Explaining the factors of erosion, weathering, and sedimentation and their causes, and predicting the patterns of their action and their effects on earth.","What is the term for the process of breaking down rocks into smaller pieces?","Weathering","Erosion","Deposition","Plate Tectonics","Weathering","Weathering is the term for the process of breaking down rocks into smaller pieces.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-main-difference-3b70e430","Science G6: Describing the factors and processes that affected the Earth's - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-3-2-3 Describing the factors and processes that affected the Earth›s surface which changed some of its","1. Distinguishing mineral and its properties and describing its relationship to rocks, and how they are formed.","What is the main difference between a mineral and a fossil?","A mineral is a naturally occurring inorganic solid, while a fossil is a naturally occurring organic solid.","A mineral is a naturally occurring organic solid, while a fossil is a naturally occurring inorganic solid.","A mineral is a naturally occurring solid, while a fossil is a naturally occurring rock.","A mineral is a naturally occurring rock, while a fossil is a naturally occurring solid.","A mineral is a naturally occurring inorganic solid, while a fossil is a naturally occurring organic solid.","A mineral is a naturally occurring inorganic solid, while a fossil is a naturally occurring organic solid.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-term-study-183b5edd","Science G6: Describing the factors and processes that affected the Earth's - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-3-2-3 Describing the factors and processes that affected the Earth›s surface which changed some of its","2. Describing the impact of earthquakes and volcanoes in shaping the Earth's surface and changing its features.","What is the term for the study of the Earth's physical structure, composition, and processes?","Geology","Mineralogy","Meteorology","Ecology","Geology","Geology is the term for the study of the Earth's physical structure, composition, and processes.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-process-called-ba152aca","Science G6: Describing the factors and processes that affected the Earth's - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-3-2-3 Describing the factors and processes that affected the Earth›s surface which changed some of its","2. Describing the impact of earthquakes and volcanoes in shaping the Earth's surface and changing its features.","What is the process called when rocks are formed from the cooling and solidification of magma?","Weathering","Erosion","Deposition","Igneous Formation","Igneous Formation","Igneous Formation is the process called when rocks are formed from the cooling and solidification of magma.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-term-movement-bc00cc79","Science G6: Describing the factors and processes that affected the Earth's - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-3-2 Understanding the principle of energy conservation during its transformations and apply it in daily life.","1. Describing how energy is transferred from one place to another in its surroundings and between objects and systems.","What is the term for the movement of rocks and minerals from one place to another?","Weathering","Erosion","Deposition","Plate Tectonics","Erosion","Erosion is the term for the movement of rocks and minerals from one place to another.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-main-difference-f85a2981","Science G6: Describing the factors and processes that affected the Earth's - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-3-2-3 Describing the factors and processes that affected the Earth›s surface which changed some of its","1. Distinguishing mineral and its properties and describing its relationship to rocks, and how they are formed.","What is the main difference between a metamorphic rock and an igneous rock?","Metamorphic rocks form when magma cools, while igneous rocks form from compacted sediments.","Metamorphic rocks form when existing rocks change from heat and pressure, while igneous rocks form when magma or lava cools.","Metamorphic rocks form only in rivers, while igneous rocks form only in deserts.","Metamorphic rocks form from living things, while igneous rocks form from soil.","Metamorphic rocks form when existing rocks change from heat and pressure, while igneous rocks form when magma or lava cools.","Metamorphic rocks form when existing rocks are changed by heat and pressure. Igneous rocks form when melted rock cools and hardens.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-term-study-fedf5ebc","Science G6: Describing the factors and processes that affected the Earth's - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-3-2-3 Describing the factors and processes that affected the Earth›s surface which changed some of its","1. Distinguishing mineral and its properties and describing its relationship to rocks, and how they are formed.","What is the term for the study of the composition and properties of minerals?","Mineralogy","Geology","Meteorology","Ecology","Mineralogy","Mineralogy is the term for the study of the composition and properties of minerals.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-process-called-98056975","Science G6: Describing the factors and processes that affected the Earth's - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-3-2-3 Describing the factors and processes that affected the Earth›s surface which changed some of its","1. Distinguishing mineral and its properties and describing its relationship to rocks, and how they are formed.","What is the process called when minerals are formed inside the Earth?","Metamorphism","Weathering","Erosion","Crystallization","Crystallization","Crystallization is the process called when minerals are formed inside the Earth.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-main-difference-ff412dde","Science G6: Describing the factors and processes that affected the Earth's - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-3-2-3 Describing the factors and processes that affected the Earth›s surface which changed some of its","1. Distinguishing mineral and its properties and describing its relationship to rocks, and how they are formed.","What is the main difference between a mineral and a rock?","A mineral is a type of rock, while a rock is a mixture of minerals.","A rock is a naturally occurring solid, while a mineral is a naturally occurring inorganic solid.","A rock is made up of many minerals, while a mineral is a single type of rock.","A rock is formed from the cooling of magma, while a mineral is formed from the cooling of lava.","A rock is a naturally occurring solid, while a mineral is a naturally occurring inorganic solid.","A rock is a naturally occurring solid, while a mineral is a naturally occurring inorganic solid.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-term-movement-1e953d31","Science G6: Describing the factors and processes that affected the Earth's - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-3-2-3 Describing the factors and processes that affected the Earth›s surface which changed some of its","3. Explaining the factors of erosion, weathering, and sedimentation and their causes, and predicting the patterns of their action and their effects on earth.","What is the term for the movement of the Earth's crust that causes earthquakes?","Tectonic plate movement","Weathering","Erosion","Geological changes","Tectonic plate movement","Tectonic plate movement refers to the movement of the Earth's crust, which can cause earthquakes and volcanic eruptions.","","",""
