"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-main-cause-73f0a5af","Science G6: Describing the factors and processes that - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-3-2-3 Describing the factors and processes that affected the Earth›s surface which changed some of its","3. Explaining the factors of erosion, weathering, and sedimentation and their causes, and predicting the patterns of their action and their effects on earth.","What is the main cause of erosion?","Weathering","Water","Wind","Gravity","Water","Water is the main cause of erosion, as it can wear away rocks and soil over time.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-result-of-d9a0e4d1","Science G6: Describing the factors and processes that - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-3-2-3 Describing the factors and processes that affected the Earth›s surface which changed some of its","1. Distinguishing mineral and its properties and describing its relationship to rocks, and how they are formed.","What is the result of sedimentation?","New rocks are formed","Rocks are broken down into smaller pieces","Rocks are carried away by wind or water","New landforms are created","New landforms are created","Sedimentation results in the creation of new landforms.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-main-difference-1744f9c1","Science G6: Describing the types of rocks and - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-3-2-4 Describing the types of rocks and their relationship to minerals and distinguishing their characteristics and","2. Describing the types of rocks in his area, explaining their characteristics, and their use.","What is the main difference between an igneous rock and a sedimentary rock?","Igneous rocks are formed from the cooling of magma, while sedimentary rocks are formed from the compression of sand and minerals.","Sedimentary rocks are formed from the cooling of magma, while igneous rocks are formed from the compression of sand and minerals.","Igneous rocks are formed from the compression of sand and minerals, while sedimentary rocks are formed from the cooling of magma.","Sedimentary rocks are formed from the erosion of existing rocks, while igneous rocks are formed from the cooling of magma.","Igneous rocks are formed from the cooling of magma, while sedimentary rocks are formed from the compression of sand and minerals.","Igneous rocks are formed from the cooling of magma, while sedimentary rocks are formed from the compression of sand and minerals.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-primary-characteristic-f2a0c0de","Science G6: Describing the types of rocks and - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-3-2-4 Describing the types of rocks and their relationship to minerals and distinguishing their characteristics and","2. Describing the types of rocks in his area, explaining their characteristics, and their use.","What is the primary characteristic of metamorphic rocks?","They are formed from the cooling of magma.","They are formed from the compression of sand and minerals.","They are formed from the alteration of existing rocks under high pressure and temperature.","They are formed from the erosion of existing rocks.","They are formed from the alteration of existing rocks under high pressure and temperature.","Metamorphic rocks are formed from the alteration of existing rocks under high pressure and temperature.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-type-of-rock-63fbc4d3","Science G6: Describing the types of rocks and - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-3-2-4 Describing the types of rocks and their relationship to minerals and distinguishing their characteristics and","2. Describing the types of rocks in his area, explaining their characteristics, and their use.","Which type of rock is formed from the cooling and solidification of magma?","Sedimentary rock","Igneous rock","Metamorphic rock","Foliated rock","Igneous rock","Igneous rock is formed from the cooling and solidification of magma.","/image-source/sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-type-of-rock-63fbc4d3.png","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-process-called-1923abee","Science G6: Describing the types of rocks and - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-3-2-4 Describing the types of rocks and their relationship to minerals and distinguishing their characteristics and","2. Describing the types of rocks in his area, explaining their characteristics, and their use.","What is the process called when existing rocks are broken down into smaller particles?","Weathering","Erosion","Deposition","Metamorphism","Weathering","Weathering is the process called when existing rocks are broken down into smaller particles.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-type-of-rock-74717423","Science G6: Describing the types of rocks and - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-3-2-3 Describing the factors and processes that affected the Earth›s surface which changed some of its","1. Distinguishing mineral and its properties and describing its relationship to rocks, and how they are formed.","Which type of rock is formed from the cooling and solidification of magma beneath the Earth's surface?","Igneous rock","Sedimentary rock","Metamorphic rock","Foliated rock","Igneous rock","Igneous rock is formed from the cooling and solidification of magma beneath the Earth's surface.","/image-source/sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-type-of-rock-74717423.png","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-process-called-c5664ab8","Science G6: Describing the types of rocks and - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-3-2-4 Describing the types of rocks and their relationship to minerals and distinguishing their characteristics and","2. Describing the types of rocks in his area, explaining their characteristics, and their use.","What is the process called when rocks are broken down into smaller particles and carried away by natural forces?","Weathering","Erosion","Deposition","Metamorphism","Erosion","Erosion is the process called when rocks are broken down into smaller particles and carried away by natural forces.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-type-of-rock-405f8393","Science G6: Describing the types of rocks and - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-3-2-4 Describing the types of rocks and their relationship to minerals and distinguishing their characteristics and","2. Describing the types of rocks in his area, explaining their characteristics, and their use.","Which type of rock is formed from the compression of sand and minerals over time?","Igneous rock","Sedimentary rock","Metamorphic rock","Foliated rock","Sedimentary rock","Sedimentary rock is formed from the compression of sand and minerals over time.","/image-source/sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-type-of-rock-405f8393.png","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-term-rocks-e42cfa46","Science G6: Describing the types of rocks and - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-3-2-4 Describing the types of rocks and their relationship to minerals and distinguishing their characteristics and","2. Describing the types of rocks in his area, explaining their characteristics, and their use.","What is the term for rocks that are formed from the cooling and solidification of magma, but have a layered or banded structure?","Igneous rocks","Sedimentary rocks","Metamorphic rocks","Foliated rocks","Foliated rocks","Foliated rocks are formed from the cooling and solidification of magma, but have a layered or banded structure.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-type-of-rock-3e4675ef","Science G6: Describing the types of rocks and - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-3-2-4 Describing the types of rocks and their relationship to minerals and distinguishing their characteristics and","2. Describing the types of rocks in his area, explaining their characteristics, and their use.","Which type of rock is formed from the compression of sand and minerals?","Igneous rock","Sedimentary rock","Metamorphic rock","Foliated rock","Sedimentary rock","Sedimentary rock is formed from the compression of sand and minerals.","/image-source/sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-type-of-rock-3e4675ef.png","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-term-rocks-b743a236","Science G6: Describing the types of rocks and - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-3-2-4 Describing the types of rocks and their relationship to minerals and distinguishing their characteristics and","2. Describing the types of rocks in his area, explaining their characteristics, and their use.","What is the term for rocks that are formed from the alteration of existing rocks under high pressure and temperature?","Metamorphic rocks","Igneous rocks","Sedimentary rocks","Foliated rocks","Metamorphic rocks","Metamorphic rocks are formed from the alteration of existing rocks under high pressure and temperature.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-are-three-main-53c4a1a2","Science G6: Describing the types of rocks and their relationship to - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-3-2-4 Describing the types of rocks and their relationship to minerals and distinguishing their characteristics and","2. Describing the types of rocks in his area, explaining their characteristics, and their use.","What are the three main types of rocks?","Igneous, Sedimentary, and Fossil","Igneous, Sedimentary, and Metamorphic","Sedimentary, Metamorphic, and Foliated","Igneous, Foliated, and Nonfoliated","Igneous, Sedimentary, and Metamorphic","The three main types of rocks are Igneous, Sedimentary, and Metamorphic, each with distinct characteristics and formation processes.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-type-of-rock-d6b8579b","Science G6: Describing the types of rocks and their relationship to - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-3-2-3 Describing the factors and processes that affected the Earth›s surface which changed some of its","1. Distinguishing mineral and its properties and describing its relationship to rocks, and how they are formed.","Which type of rock is formed from the cooling and solidification of magma or lava?","Sedimentary rock","Metamorphic rock","Igneous rock","Foliated rock","Igneous rock","Igneous rocks are formed when magma or lava cools and solidifies, either beneath the Earth's surface or on the surface as a result of volcanic activity.","/image-source/sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-type-of-rock-d6b8579b.png","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-process-called-22ca8c22","Science G6: Describing the types of rocks and their relationship to - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-1-1 Exploring the physical properties of matter, distinguishing the molecular structure of its different states, and explaining the change of matter states due to heat.","1. Explaining the changes in the composition and properties of matter as a result of a chemical reaction. Concluding that the mass of the substance remains preserved during the chemical reaction and when forming mixtures.","What is the process called when rocks are changed by heat and pressure, resulting in a new type of rock?","Weathering","Erosion","Metamorphism","Deposition","Metamorphism","Metamorphism is the process by which rocks are altered by heat, pressure, and chemical processes, resulting in changes to their mineral composition and structure.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-type-of-rock-ac5a1b1b","Science G6: Describing the types of rocks and their relationship to - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-3-2-4 Describing the types of rocks and their relationship to minerals and distinguishing their characteristics and","2. Describing the types of rocks in his area, explaining their characteristics, and their use.","Which type of rock is composed of the remains of plants and animals, such as fossils?","Igneous rock","Metamorphic rock","Sedimentary rock","Mineral rock","Sedimentary rock","Sedimentary rocks are formed from the accumulation and compression of sediments, such as mineral particles, rock fragments, and the remains of plants and animals, including fossils.","/image-source/sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-type-of-rock-ac5a1b1b.png","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-term-rocks-cc7f9527","Science G6: Describing the types of rocks and their relationship to - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-1-1 Exploring the physical properties of matter, distinguishing the molecular structure of its different states, and explaining the change of matter states due to heat.","1. Explaining the changes in the composition and properties of matter as a result of a chemical reaction. Concluding that the mass of the substance remains preserved during the chemical reaction and when forming mixtures.","What is the term for rocks that are formed from the alteration of existing rocks under high pressure and temperature conditions?","Sedimentary rocks","Igneous rocks","Metamorphic rocks","Foliated rocks","Metamorphic rocks","Metamorphic rocks are formed when existing rocks are subjected to high pressure and temperature conditions, causing changes to their mineral composition and structure.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-type-of-rock-ab017280","Science G6: Describing the types of rocks and their relationship to - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-3-2-4 Describing the types of rocks and their relationship to minerals and distinguishing their characteristics and","2. Describing the types of rocks in his area, explaining their characteristics, and their use.","Which type of rock can be either foliated or nonfoliated?","Igneous rock","Sedimentary rock","Metamorphic rock","Mineral rock","Metamorphic rock","Metamorphic rocks can exhibit foliation, which is the arrangement of minerals in a layered or banded pattern, or they can be nonfoliated, lacking any visible layering or banding.","/image-source/sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-type-of-rock-ab017280.png","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-an-example-f1f63939","Science G6: Describing the types of rocks and their relationship to - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-3-2-4 Describing the types of rocks and their relationship to minerals and distinguishing their characteristics and","2. Describing the types of rocks in his area, explaining their characteristics, and their use.","What is an example of a sedimentary rock that is formed from the cementation of sand-sized particles?","Shale","Limestone","Sandstone","Granite","Sandstone","Sandstone is a type of sedimentary rock that is formed from the cementation of sand-sized particles, such as quartz or feldspar, which are bound together by minerals like silica or calcium carbonate.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-type-of-rock-738a81bf","Science G6: Describing the types of rocks and their relationship to - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-1-4 Exploring the chemical properties of materials, and distinguishing between the interactions of acids and bases, their chemical properties, and their uses.","2. Distinguishing between acids and bases and give examples of each type.","Which type of rock is often used in construction and is a common building material?","Igneous rock","Sedimentary rock","Metamorphic rock","All of the above","All of the above","All three types of rocks - igneous, sedimentary, and metamorphic - can be used in construction, depending on their properties and availability, with examples including granite, limestone, and marble.","/image-source/sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-type-of-rock-738a81bf.png","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-process-called-485f083d","Science G6: Describing the types of rocks and their relationship to - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-3-2-4 Describing the types of rocks and their relationship to minerals and distinguishing their characteristics and","2. Describing the types of rocks in his area, explaining their characteristics, and their use.","What is the process called when rocks are broken down into smaller particles through exposure to wind, water, and ice?","Weathering","Erosion","Deposition","Metamorphism","Weathering","Weathering is the process by which rocks are broken down into smaller particles through exposure to environmental factors such as wind, water, and ice, which can cause mechanical or chemical changes to the rock.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-type-of-rock-032d2c71","Science G6: Describing the types of rocks and their relationship to - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-3-2-4 Describing the types of rocks and their relationship to minerals and distinguishing their characteristics and","2. Describing the types of rocks in his area, explaining their characteristics, and their use.","Which type of rock is formed from the alteration of shale under high pressure and temperature conditions?","Slate","Schist","Gneiss","Marble","Slate","Slate is a type of metamorphic rock that is formed from the alteration of shale under low-grade metamorphic conditions, resulting in a fine-grained, foliated rock with a smooth, dull appearance.","/image-source/sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-type-of-rock-032d2c71.png","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-type-of-wave-9f65dfab","Science G6: Describing waves, distinguishing between their properties - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-4-1 Describing waves, distinguishing between their properties theoretically and graphically, and predicting their movement.","4. Describing the transmission of sound and light as waves through material media and space and distinguishing between them.","What type of wave can travel through a vacuum?","Light waves can travel through a vacuum.","Sound waves cannot travel through a vacuum.","Water waves can travel through a vacuum.","Ocean waves can travel through a vacuum.","Light waves can travel through a vacuum.","Light waves are a type of electromagnetic wave that can travel through a vacuum.","/image-source/sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-type-of-wave-9f65dfab.png","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-main-difference-ad297a81","Science G6: Describing waves, distinguishing between their properties - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-4-1 Describing waves, distinguishing between their properties theoretically and graphically, and predicting their movement.","4. Describing the transmission of sound and light as waves through material media and space and distinguishing between them.","What is the main difference between sound waves and light waves?","Sound waves can travel through solid objects.","Light waves can travel through solid objects.","Sound waves are faster than light waves.","Light waves are faster than sound waves.","Light waves are faster than sound waves.","Light waves are faster than sound waves, and they can travel through a vacuum.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-can-sound-waves-8ba5153c","Science G6: Describing waves, distinguishing between their properties - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-4-1 Describing waves, distinguishing between their properties theoretically and graphically, and predicting their movement.","4. Describing the transmission of sound and light as waves through material media and space and distinguishing between them.","Can sound waves travel through a vacuum?","Yes, sound waves can travel through a vacuum.","No, sound waves cannot travel through a vacuum.","Maybe, it depends on the situation.","It's not possible to determine.","No, sound waves cannot travel through a vacuum.","Sound waves need a medium to travel, and a vacuum does not have any particles to transmit the sound.","/image-source/sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-can-sound-waves-8ba5153c.png","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-type-of-medium-6d7ceaf0","Science G6: Describing waves, distinguishing between their properties - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-4-1 Describing waves, distinguishing between their properties theoretically and graphically, and predicting their movement.","4. Describing the transmission of sound and light as waves through material media and space and distinguishing between them.","What type of medium do sound waves need to travel?","Sound waves need a solid medium to travel.","Sound waves need a liquid medium to travel.","Sound waves need a gas medium to travel.","Sound waves can travel through any medium.","Sound waves need a gas medium to travel.","Sound waves need a gas medium, such as air, to travel.","/image-source/sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-type-of-medium-6d7ceaf0.png","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-main-property-7ae43fdb","Science G6: Describing waves, distinguishing between their properties - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-4-1 Describing waves, distinguishing between their properties theoretically and graphically, and predicting their movement.","2. Distinguishing between the properties of sound and light waves theoretically and graphically.","Which listed property clearly differs among water waves, sound waves, and electromagnetic waves?","Their speed in a given medium","All require a material medium","All are longitudinal","All travel at the speed of light","Their speed in a given medium","These waves can travel at different speeds. Electromagnetic waves travel at the speed of light in a vacuum, whereas sound and water waves are much slower.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-can-light-waves-f8eb4711","Science G6: Describing waves, distinguishing between their properties - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-4-2 Understanding the concepts of reflection and refraction of light, transmission of sound, and explaining their role in interaction and communication in the surrounding environment.","3. Describing the transmission of sound by absorbing or reflecting it through different media and objects.","Can light waves travel through solid objects?","Yes, light waves can travel through solid objects.","No, light waves cannot travel through solid objects.","Maybe, it depends on the situation.","It's not possible to determine.","Yes, light waves can travel through solid objects.","Light waves can travel through solid objects, but they may be absorbed or reflected.","/image-source/sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-can-light-waves-f8eb4711.png","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-can-light-waves-cef09d86","Science G6: Describing waves, distinguishing between their properties - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-4-1 Describing waves, distinguishing between their properties theoretically and graphically, and predicting their movement.","4. Describing the transmission of sound and light as waves through material media and space and distinguishing between them.","Can light waves travel through a gas medium?","Yes, light waves can travel through a gas medium.","No, light waves cannot travel through a gas medium.","Maybe, it depends on the situation.","It's not possible to determine.","Yes, light waves can travel through a gas medium.","Light waves can travel through any medium, including gas, but they may be absorbed or reflected.","/image-source/sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-can-light-waves-cef09d86.png","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-speed-of-e272bc35","Science G6: Describing waves, distinguishing between their properties - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-4-1 Describing waves, distinguishing between their properties theoretically and graphically, and predicting their movement.","2. Distinguishing between the properties of sound and light waves theoretically and graphically.","What is the speed of light in a vacuum?","The speed of light is slower in a vacuum.","The speed of light is faster in a vacuum.","The speed of light is the same in any medium.","The speed of light is not measurable in a vacuum.","The speed of light is faster in a vacuum.","The speed of light in a vacuum is approximately 299,792 kilometers per second.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-type-of-wave-539c2591","Science G6: Describing waves, distinguishing between their properties - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-4-1 Describing waves, distinguishing between their properties theoretically and graphically, and predicting their movement.","4. Describing the transmission of sound and light as waves through material media and space and distinguishing between them.","What type of wave is light?","Mechanical wave","Electromagnetic wave","Water wave","Sound wave","Electromagnetic wave","Light is a type of electromagnetic wave that can travel through a vacuum.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-can-sound-waves-7f393ff7","Science G6: Describing waves, distinguishing between their properties - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-4-1 Describing waves, distinguishing between their properties theoretically and graphically, and predicting their movement.","4. Describing the transmission of sound and light as waves through material media and space and distinguishing between them.","Can sound waves travel through space?","Yes, sound waves can travel through space.","No, sound waves cannot travel through space.","Maybe, it depends on the situation.","It's not possible to determine.","No, sound waves cannot travel through space.","Sound waves need a medium to travel, and space is a vacuum that does not have any particles to transmit the sound.","/image-source/sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-can-sound-waves-7f393ff7.png","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-can-sound-waves-d4d9236b","Science G6: Describing waves, distinguishing between their properties - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-4-1 Describing waves, distinguishing between their properties theoretically and graphically, and predicting their movement.","4. Describing the transmission of sound and light as waves through material media and space and distinguishing between them.","Can sound waves travel through a solid, liquid, or gas?","Only through a solid.","Only through a liquid.","Only through a gas.","Through a solid, liquid, or gas.","Through a solid, liquid, or gas.","Sound can travel through solids, liquids, and gases, but it cannot travel through a vacuum.","/image-source/sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-can-sound-waves-d4d9236b.png","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-type-of-wave-cfd199e7","Science G6: Describing waves, distinguishing between their properties - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-4-1 Describing waves, distinguishing between their properties theoretically and graphically, and predicting their movement.","2. Distinguishing between the properties of sound and light waves theoretically and graphically.","Which type of wave has a shorter wavelength?","Sound waves have a shorter wavelength.","Light waves have a shorter wavelength.","Both sound and light waves have the same wavelength.","Neither sound nor light waves have a shorter wavelength.","Sound waves have a shorter wavelength.","Sound waves have a shorter wavelength.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-main-difference-e93beaef","Science G6: Describing waves, distinguishing between their properties - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-4-1 Describing waves, distinguishing between their properties theoretically and graphically, and predicting their movement.","4. Describing the transmission of sound and light as waves through material media and space and distinguishing between them.","What is the main difference between the speed of sound and light waves?","Sound waves travel faster than light waves in air.","Light waves travel much faster than sound waves in air.","Sound waves and light waves travel at the same speed in air.","Light waves cannot travel through air.","Light waves travel much faster than sound waves in air.","Light waves travel much faster than sound waves in air. Sound also needs a medium to travel, while light can travel through a vacuum.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-type-of-wave-a5835893","Science G6: Describing waves, distinguishing between their properties - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-4-1 Describing waves, distinguishing between their properties theoretically and graphically, and predicting their movement.","4. Describing the transmission of sound and light as waves through material media and space and distinguishing between them.","Which type of wave can travel through a vacuum?","Only sound waves can travel through a vacuum.","Only light waves can travel through a vacuum.","Both sound and light waves can travel through a vacuum.","Neither sound nor light waves can travel through a vacuum.","Only light waves can travel through a vacuum.","Light waves can travel through a vacuum.","/image-source/sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-type-of-wave-a5835893.png","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-main-property-cadd8cfc","Science G6: Describing waves, distinguishing between their properties - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-4-1 Describing waves, distinguishing between their properties theoretically and graphically, and predicting their movement.","2. Distinguishing between the properties of sound and light waves theoretically and graphically.","What is the main property that distinguishes sound waves from light waves?","Frequency and speed.","Speed and wavelength.","Frequency and wavelength.","Speed and frequency.","Frequency and speed.","Frequency and speed are the main properties that distinguish sound waves from light waves.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-type-of-wave-738c49cd","Science G6: Describing waves, distinguishing between their properties - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-4-1 Describing waves, distinguishing between their properties theoretically and graphically, and predicting their movement.","2. Distinguishing between the properties of sound and light waves theoretically and graphically.","Which type of wave has a longer wavelength?","Sound waves have a longer wavelength.","Light waves have a longer wavelength.","Both sound and light waves have the same wavelength.","Neither sound nor light waves have a longer wavelength.","Light waves have a longer wavelength.","Light waves have a longer wavelength.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-main-property-43d2aa12","Science G6: Describing waves, distinguishing between their properties - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-4-1 Describing waves, distinguishing between their properties theoretically and graphically, and predicting their movement.","2. Distinguishing between the properties of sound and light waves theoretically and graphically.","What is the main property that determines the pitch of a sound wave?","Frequency.","Speed.","Wavelength.","Amplitude.","Frequency.","Frequency determines the pitch of a sound wave.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-can-light-waves-39600952","Science G6: Describing waves, distinguishing between their properties - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-4-1 Describing waves, distinguishing between their properties theoretically and graphically, and predicting their movement.","4. Describing the transmission of sound and light as waves through material media and space and distinguishing between them.","Can light waves travel through a solid object?","Yes, but only if the object is transparent.","Yes, but only if the object is opaque.","No, light waves cannot travel through a solid object.","It depends on the type of solid object.","No, light waves cannot travel through a solid object.","Light waves cannot travel through a solid object.","/image-source/sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-can-light-waves-39600952.png","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-main-property-84b2495f","Science G6: Describing waves, distinguishing between their properties - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-4-1 Describing waves, distinguishing between their properties theoretically and graphically, and predicting their movement.","2. Distinguishing between the properties of sound and light waves theoretically and graphically.","What is the main property that determines the color of a light wave?","Frequency.","Speed.","Wavelength.","Amplitude.","Wavelength.","Wavelength determines the color of a light wave.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-can-sound-waves-2ae82f46","Science G6: Describing waves, distinguishing between their properties - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-4-1 Describing waves, distinguishing between their properties theoretically and graphically, and predicting their movement.","4. Describing the transmission of sound and light as waves through material media and space and distinguishing between them.","Can sound waves travel through a vacuum?","Yes, but only if the vacuum is at a high temperature.","Yes, but only if the vacuum is at a low temperature.","No, sound waves cannot travel through a vacuum.","It depends on the type of vacuum.","No, sound waves cannot travel through a vacuum.","Sound waves cannot travel through a vacuum.","/image-source/sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-can-sound-waves-2ae82f46.png","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-happens-to-a-b64c2bff","Science G6: Describing waves, distinguishing between their properties - Set 3","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-4-1 Describing waves, distinguishing between their properties theoretically and graphically, and predicting their movement.","2. Distinguishing between the properties of sound and light waves theoretically and graphically.","What happens to a wave when it travels from deep water to shallow water?","The wave will move faster and get taller","The wave will move slower and get shorter","The wave will keep the same speed and height","The wave will change direction completely","The wave will move slower and get shorter","As a wave travels from deep water to shallow water, it will slow down and get shorter due to the decrease in water depth.","/image-source/sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-happens-to-a-b64c2bff.png","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-how-movement-of-a3990ad4","Science G6: Describing waves, distinguishing between their properties - Set 3","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-4-1 Describing waves, distinguishing between their properties theoretically and graphically, and predicting their movement.","3. Predicting the movement of the wave when exposed to some natural influences.","How does the movement of a wave change when it is affected by the gravitational pull of the moon?","The wave will move faster and get taller","The wave will move slower and get shorter","The wave will keep the same speed and height","The wave will change direction completely","The wave will move faster and get taller","The gravitational pull of the moon causes the ocean water to bulge, resulting in higher high tides and lower low tides, which affects the movement of waves.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-happens-to-a-116a15e7","Science G6: Describing waves, distinguishing between their properties - Set 3","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-4-1 Describing waves, distinguishing between their properties theoretically and graphically, and predicting their movement.","2. Distinguishing between the properties of sound and light waves theoretically and graphically.","What happens to a wave when it hits a sloping beach?","The wave will keep moving forward without any change","The wave will bounce back in the opposite direction","The wave will break and lose energy","The wave will change direction completely","The wave will break and lose energy","When a wave hits a sloping beach, it will break and lose energy due to the decrease in water depth and the friction with the beach.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-how-movement-of-9c7f7376","Science G6: Describing waves, distinguishing between their properties - Set 3","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-4-1 Describing waves, distinguishing between their properties theoretically and graphically, and predicting their movement.","3. Predicting the movement of the wave when exposed to some natural influences.","How does the movement of a wave change when it is affected by the shape of the coastline?","The wave will keep the same speed and direction","The wave will change speed but not direction","The wave will change direction but not speed","The wave will change both speed and direction","The wave will change both speed and direction","The shape of the coastline can affect the movement of a wave by changing its speed and direction, due to the interaction with the coastline features such as bays and headlands.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-happens-to-a-33f8eef4","Science G6: Describing waves, distinguishing between their properties - Set 3","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-1-1 Exploring the physical properties of matter, distinguishing the molecular structure of its different states, and explaining the change of matter states due to heat.","1. Explaining the changes in the composition and properties of matter as a result of a chemical reaction. Concluding that the mass of the substance remains preserved during the chemical reaction and when forming mixtures.","What happens to a wave when it passes through a region with a different water temperature?","The wave will keep the same speed and direction","The wave will change speed but not direction","The wave will change direction but not speed","The wave will change both speed and direction","The wave will change speed but not direction","When a wave passes through a region with a different water temperature, it will change speed but not direction, due to the change in water properties.","/image-source/sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-happens-to-a-33f8eef4.png","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-happens-to-a-d9ce79b0","Science G6: Describing waves, distinguishing between their properties - Set 3","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-4-1 Describing waves, distinguishing between their properties theoretically and graphically, and predicting their movement.","3. Predicting the movement of the wave when exposed to some natural influences.","What happens to a wave when it travels from one medium to another with a different density?","The wave will keep the same speed and direction","The wave will change speed but not direction","The wave will change direction but not speed","The wave will change both speed and direction","The wave will change speed but not direction","When a wave travels from one medium to another with a different density, it will change speed but not direction, due to the change in medium properties.","/image-source/sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-happens-to-a-d9ce79b0.png","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-how-movement-of-ddc4955a","Science G6: Describing waves, distinguishing between their properties - Set 3","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-4-1 Describing waves, distinguishing between their properties theoretically and graphically, and predicting their movement.","3. Predicting the movement of the wave when exposed to some natural influences.","How does the movement of a wave change when it is exposed to wind?","The wave will move slower and get shorter","The wave will move faster and get taller","The wave will keep the same speed and height","The wave will change direction completely","The wave will move faster and get taller","When a wave is exposed to wind, it will move faster and get taller due to the energy transferred from the wind.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-happens-to-a-09470d1a","Science G6: Describing waves, distinguishing between their properties - Set 3","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-4-1 Describing waves, distinguishing between their properties theoretically and graphically, and predicting their movement.","2. Distinguishing between the properties of sound and light waves theoretically and graphically.","What happens to a wave when it passes through a narrow opening?","The wave will get wider and slower","The wave will get narrower and faster","The wave will keep the same speed and width","The wave will change direction completely","The wave will get narrower and faster","When a wave passes through a narrow opening, it will get narrower and faster due to the decrease in width.","/image-source/sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-happens-to-a-09470d1a.png","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-how-movement-of-74d425dc","Science G6: Describing waves, distinguishing between their properties - Set 3","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-4-1 Describing waves, distinguishing between their properties theoretically and graphically, and predicting their movement.","3. Predicting the movement of the wave when exposed to some natural influences.","How does the movement of a wave change when it hits a rock or a wall?","The wave will keep moving forward without any change","The wave will bounce back in the opposite direction","The wave will move faster and get taller","The wave will stop moving completely","The wave will bounce back in the opposite direction","When a wave hits a rock or a wall, it will bounce back in the opposite direction, which is known as reflection.","/image-source/sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-how-movement-of-74d425dc.png","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-how-movement-of-eae338b3","Science G6: Describing waves, distinguishing between their properties - Set 3","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-4-1 Describing waves, distinguishing between their properties theoretically and graphically, and predicting their movement.","3. Predicting the movement of the wave when exposed to some natural influences.","How does the movement of a wave change when it is affected by the presence of ocean currents?","The wave will keep the same speed and direction","The wave will change speed but not direction","The wave will change direction but not speed","The wave will change both speed and direction","The wave will change both speed and direction","Ocean currents can affect the movement of a wave by changing its speed and direction, due to the interaction with the moving water.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-main-effect-057b7098","Science G6: Determining the causes and effects of earthquakes and volcanoes - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-3-2-5 Determining the causes and effects of earthquakes and volcanoes and identifying the sites most vulnerable to earthquakes and volcanoes.","1. Relating the change of the Earth's shape with the external and internal geological processes.","What is the main effect of earthquakes?","Changes in the weather","Damage to buildings","Volcanic eruptions","Changes in the Earth's rotation","Damage to buildings","Earthquakes can cause significant damage to buildings and infrastructure, leading to loss of life and property.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-process-called-fd89de8f","Science G6: Determining the causes and effects of earthquakes and volcanoes - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-3-2-2 Deducing the relationship of the Earth›s spheres to each other and predicting the interactions, and changes that occur between them and the geological effects resulting from them.","1. Describing the Earth's spheres and Plate tectonics, identifying the layers of the Earth that make up the lithosphere and the biosphere, and distinguishing between them.","What is the process called when the Earth's crust is pushed up to form mountains?","Weathering","Erosion","Plate Tectonics","Folding","Plate Tectonics","Folding occurs when rock layers bend under compressional stress without breaking.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-site-most-e12b6b84","Science G6: Determining the causes and effects of earthquakes and volcanoes - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-3-2-5 Determining the causes and effects of earthquakes and volcanoes and identifying the sites most vulnerable to earthquakes and volcanoes.","1. Relating the change of the Earth's shape with the external and internal geological processes.","What is the site most vulnerable to earthquakes?","Mountains","Oceans","Coastal areas","Forests","Coastal areas","Coastal areas are most vulnerable to earthquakes because they are located near the boundary between tectonic plates.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-main-cause-d6976244","Science G6: Determining the causes and effects of earthquakes and volcanoes - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-3-2-3 Describing the factors and processes that affected the Earth›s surface which changed some of its","3. Explaining the factors of erosion, weathering, and sedimentation and their causes, and predicting the patterns of their action and their effects on earth.","What is the main cause of earthquakes?","Movement of the Earth's crust","Volcanic eruptions","Weather changes","Changes in the Earth's rotation","Movement of the Earth's crust","Earthquakes are caused by the movement of the Earth's crust, which can be sudden and violent.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-process-called-61099dfa","Science G6: Determining the causes and effects of earthquakes and volcanoes - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-3-2-2 Deducing the relationship of the Earth›s spheres to each other and predicting the interactions, and changes that occur between them and the geological effects resulting from them.","1. Describing the Earth's spheres and Plate tectonics, identifying the layers of the Earth that make up the lithosphere and the biosphere, and distinguishing between them.","What is the process called when the Earth's crust is broken and moved?","Weathering","Erosion","Plate Tectonics","Folding","Plate Tectonics","Plate Tectonics is the process by which the Earth's crust is broken and moved, resulting in earthquakes and volcanic activity.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-term-movement-416854fd","Science G6: Determining the causes and effects of earthquakes and volcanoes - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-3-2-2 Deducing the relationship of the Earth›s spheres to each other and predicting the interactions, and changes that occur between them and the geological effects resulting from them.","2. Describing how magma moves Earth's plates, using that for explaining the formation of oceans and mountains.","What is the term for the movement of the Earth's crust that can cause earthquakes?","Convection currents","Plate movement","Weather patterns","Tectonic plates","Tectonic plates","Tectonic plates are large, rigid slabs of the Earth's crust that move relative to each other, causing earthquakes.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-term-movement-473ea106","Science G6: Determining the causes and effects of earthquakes and volcanoes - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-3-2-2 Deducing the relationship of the Earth›s spheres to each other and predicting the interactions, and changes that occur between them and the geological effects resulting from them.","2. Describing how magma moves Earth's plates, using that for explaining the formation of oceans and mountains.","What is the term for the movement of the Earth's crust that can cause volcanic eruptions?","Convection currents","Plate movement","Weather patterns","Tectonic plates","Tectonic plates","Tectonic plates are large, rigid slabs of the Earth's crust that move relative to each other, causing volcanic eruptions.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-site-most-a1700ef8","Science G6: Determining the causes and effects of earthquakes and volcanoes - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-3-2-5 Determining the causes and effects of earthquakes and volcanoes and identifying the sites most vulnerable to earthquakes and volcanoes.","1. Relating the change of the Earth's shape with the external and internal geological processes.","What is the site most vulnerable to volcanic eruptions?","Mountains","Oceans","Coastal areas","Volcanic regions","Volcanic regions","Volcanic regions are most vulnerable to volcanic eruptions because they are located near active volcanoes.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-main-cause-b5cbc468","Science G6: Determining the causes and effects of earthquakes and volcanoes - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-3-2-3 Describing the factors and processes that affected the Earth›s surface which changed some of its","3. Explaining the factors of erosion, weathering, and sedimentation and their causes, and predicting the patterns of their action and their effects on earth.","What is the main cause of volcanic eruptions?","Movement of the Earth's crust","Weather changes","Changes in the Earth's rotation","Magma buildup","Magma buildup","Volcanic eruptions are caused by the buildup of magma beneath the Earth's surface, which can eventually be released through a volcanic eruption.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-effect-of-a561f66d","Science G6: Determining the causes and effects of earthquakes and volcanoes - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-3-2-3 Describing the factors and processes that affected the Earth›s surface which changed some of its","3. Explaining the factors of erosion, weathering, and sedimentation and their causes, and predicting the patterns of their action and their effects on earth.","What is the effect of earthquakes on the Earth's surface?","Changes in the weather","Damage to buildings","Creation of new land","Changes in the Earth's rotation","Damage to buildings","Earthquakes can cause significant damage to buildings and infrastructure, leading to loss of life and property.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-term-hot-5ab21d3f","Science G6: Determining the causes and effects of earthquakes and volcanoes - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-3-2-5 Determining the causes and effects of earthquakes and volcanoes and identifying the sites most vulnerable to earthquakes and volcanoes.","1. Relating the change of the Earth's shape with the external and internal geological processes.","What is the term for the hot, molten rock that flows from a volcano during an eruption?","Lava","Magma","Ash","Gas","Lava","Lava is the hot, molten rock that flows from a volcano during an eruption, which can cause damage to surrounding areas.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-main-reason-8a081426","Science G6: Determining the causes and effects of earthquakes and volcanoes - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-3-2-1Classifying the layers of the atmosphere and identifying their components, characteristics, changes, its effects on the environment and their benefits to humans.","2. Explaining the movement of air masses and air currents and describing their effects on Earth’s weather.","What is the main reason for the formation of volcanoes?","Movement of the Earth's crust","Changes in the weather","Human activities","Magma buildup","Magma buildup","Volcanoes form when magma from the Earth's interior builds up and is released through a volcanic vent or fissure.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-main-effect-9859d97a","Science G6: Determining the causes and effects of earthquakes and volcanoes - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-4 Describing the effect of environmental changes on plants and animals that live in specific environments. Inferring how behavioral and structural adaptations can help plants and animals survive in their habitats","2. Predicting the changes that will happen to living organisms as a result of  of changes in their environments.","What is the main effect of a volcanic eruption on the surrounding environment?","It becomes cleaner","It becomes more fertile","It becomes more polluted","It remains the same","It becomes more polluted","A volcanic eruption can cause significant damage to the surrounding environment, making it more polluted and potentially hazardous to living organisms.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-main-reason-8f586a37","Science G6: Determining the causes and effects of earthquakes and volcanoes - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-3-2-1Classifying the layers of the atmosphere and identifying their components, characteristics, changes, its effects on the environment and their benefits to humans.","2. Explaining the movement of air masses and air currents and describing their effects on Earth’s weather.","What is the main reason for earthquakes?","Movement of the Earth's crust","Volcanic eruptions","Changes in the weather","Human activities","Movement of the Earth's crust","Earthquakes occur due to the movement of the Earth's crust, which can cause the ground to shake violently.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-process-called-22a057f0","Science G6: Determining the causes and effects of earthquakes and volcanoes - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-3-2-3 Describing the factors and processes that affected the Earth›s surface which changed some of its","3. Explaining the factors of erosion, weathering, and sedimentation and their causes, and predicting the patterns of their action and their effects on earth.","What is the process called when magma from the Earth's interior rises to the surface?","Weathering","Erosion","Plate tectonics","Volcanic eruption","Volcanic eruption","A volcanic eruption occurs when magma from the Earth's interior rises to the surface, releasing hot ash, gas, and rock.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-term-ground-7a029f50","Science G6: Determining the causes and effects of earthquakes and volcanoes - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-3-2-5 Determining the causes and effects of earthquakes and volcanoes and identifying the sites most vulnerable to earthquakes and volcanoes.","1. Relating the change of the Earth's shape with the external and internal geological processes.","What is the term for the ground shaking caused by the movement of the Earth's crust?","Tsunami","Earthquake","Volcanic eruption","Landslide","Earthquake","An earthquake is the ground shaking caused by the movement of the Earth's crust, which can cause damage to buildings and infrastructure.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-main-cause-f2149d8b","Science G6: Determining the causes and effects of earthquakes and volcanoes - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-3-2-1Classifying the layers of the atmosphere and identifying their components, characteristics, changes, its effects on the environment and their benefits to humans.","2. Explaining the movement of air masses and air currents and describing their effects on Earth’s weather.","What is the main cause of volcanic eruptions?","Movement of the Earth's crust","Changes in the weather","Human activities","Magma buildup","Magma buildup","Volcanic eruptions occur when magma from the Earth's interior builds up and is released through a volcanic vent or fissure.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-term-shaking-e5cd325d","Science G6: Determining the causes and effects of earthquakes and volcanoes - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-3-2-5 Determining the causes and effects of earthquakes and volcanoes and identifying the sites most vulnerable to earthquakes and volcanoes.","1. Relating the change of the Earth's shape with the external and internal geological processes.","What is the term for the shaking of the ground caused by the movement of the Earth's crust during an earthquake?","Tremor","Seismic activity","Volcanic eruption","Landslide","Tremor","A tremor is the shaking of the ground caused by the movement of the Earth's crust during an earthquake.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-term-movement-ef82f5a5","Science G6: Determining the causes and effects of earthquakes and volcanoes - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-3-2-5 Determining the causes and effects of earthquakes and volcanoes and identifying the sites most vulnerable to earthquakes and volcanoes.","1. Relating the change of the Earth's shape with the external and internal geological processes.","What is the term for the movement of the Earth's crust that can cause earthquakes and volcanic eruptions?","Weathering","Erosion","Plate tectonics","Landslide","Plate tectonics","Plate tectonics is the movement of the Earth's crust that can cause earthquakes and volcanic eruptions as the plates interact and move.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-main-effect-e13466f6","Science G6: Determining the causes and effects of earthquakes and volcanoes - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-3-2-5 Determining the causes and effects of earthquakes and volcanoes and identifying the sites most vulnerable to earthquakes and volcanoes.","1. Relating the change of the Earth's shape with the external and internal geological processes.","What is the main effect of an earthquake on buildings and infrastructure?","They become stronger","They become weaker","They remain the same","They disappear","They become weaker","An earthquake can cause significant damage to buildings and infrastructure, making them weaker and more prone to collapse.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-process-called-64d46a1e","Science G6: Determining the causes and effects of - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-2-1 Describing the concept of force and distinguishing between different types","2. Distinguishing between balanced and unbalanced forces and describing their effects on motion.","What is the process called when the Earth's crust is broken and new crust is formed?","Weathering","Erosion","Plate Tectonics","Volcanic Eruption","Plate Tectonics","Plate Tectonics is the process by which the Earth's crust is broken and new crust is formed.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-term-study-1ce537f7","Science G6: Determining the causes and effects of - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-1-1 Exploring the physical properties of matter, distinguishing the molecular structure of its different states, and explaining the change of matter states due to heat.","3. Comparing between conductors and insulators in terms of their physical properties, supported by examples","What is the term for the study of the Earth's physical structure, composition, and processes?","Geography","Meteorology","Oceanography","Geology","Geology","Geology is the study of the Earth's physical structure, composition, and processes.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-process-by-f9186bb4","Science G6: Determining the causes and effects of - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-3-2-3 Describing the factors and processes that affected the Earth›s surface which changed some of its","3. Explaining the factors of erosion, weathering, and sedimentation and their causes, and predicting the patterns of their action and their effects on earth.","What is the process by which the Earth's surface is worn away by natural forces?","Weathering","Erosion","Plate Tectonics","Volcanic Eruption","Erosion","Erosion is the process by which the Earth's surface is worn away by natural forces.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-main-cause-71174b36","Science G6: Determining the causes and effects of - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-3-2-1Classifying the layers of the atmosphere and identifying their components, characteristics, changes, its effects on the environment and their benefits to humans.","2. Explaining the movement of air masses and air currents and describing their effects on Earth’s weather.","What is the main cause of volcanic eruptions?","Movement of the Earth's crust","Weather changes","Tectonic plate movement","Human activities","Tectonic plate movement","Tectonic plate movement is the main cause of volcanic eruptions.","","",""
