"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-affects-magnitude-of-e4bb0eab","Science G6: Explaining the factors affecting types of - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-2-3 Explaining the factors affecting types of forces, such as gravitational force, friction, and magnetism.","2. Explaining how frictional force is generated and the factors affecting its magnitude, and explaining the effect of air resistance on the motion of objects.","What affects the magnitude of frictional force?","Only the weight of the object.","Only the surface area of the object.","Both the weight and surface area of the object.","Neither the weight nor surface area of the object.","Both the weight and surface area of the object.","The magnitude of frictional force is affected by both the weight and surface area of the object.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-generates-frictional-force-4d5f2453","Science G6: Explaining the factors affecting types of - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-2-3 Explaining the factors affecting types of forces, such as gravitational force, friction, and magnetism.","2. Explaining how frictional force is generated and the factors affecting its magnitude, and explaining the effect of air resistance on the motion of objects.","What generates frictional force?","Only smooth surfaces.","Only rough surfaces.","Both smooth and rough surfaces.","Neither smooth nor rough surfaces.","Both smooth and rough surfaces.","Friction can occur between both smooth and rough surfaces, although rougher surfaces often produce greater friction.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-frictional-force-2be2eee8","Science G6: Explaining the factors affecting types of - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-2-3 Explaining the factors affecting types of forces, such as gravitational force, friction, and magnetism.","2. Explaining how frictional force is generated and the factors affecting its magnitude, and explaining the effect of air resistance on the motion of objects.","What is frictional force?","The force that pulls objects towards each other.","The force that opposes motion between two surfaces that are in contact.","The force that pushes objects away from each other.","The force that makes objects move faster.","The force that opposes motion between two surfaces that are in contact.","Frictional force is the force that opposes motion between two surfaces that are in contact.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-can-reduce-air-aa62b4e1","Science G6: Explaining the factors affecting types of - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-2-3 Explaining the factors affecting types of forces, such as gravitational force, friction, and magnetism.","2. Explaining how frictional force is generated and the factors affecting its magnitude, and explaining the effect of air resistance on the motion of objects.","What can reduce air resistance?","Increasing the weight of the object.","Increasing the surface area of the object.","Using a smooth surface.","Using a streamlined shape.","Using a streamlined shape.","Using a streamlined shape can reduce air resistance.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-how-air-resistance-654d96ad","Science G6: Explaining the factors affecting types of - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-2-3 Explaining the factors affecting types of forces, such as gravitational force, friction, and magnetism.","2. Explaining how frictional force is generated and the factors affecting its magnitude, and explaining the effect of air resistance on the motion of objects.","How does air resistance affect the motion of objects?","It makes objects move faster.","It makes objects move slower.","It has no effect on the motion of objects.","It makes objects move in a straight line.","It makes objects move slower.","Air resistance makes objects move slower by opposing their motion.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-effect-of-de116df1","Science G6: Explaining the factors affecting types of - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-2-3 Explaining the factors affecting types of forces, such as gravitational force, friction, and magnetism.","2. Explaining how frictional force is generated and the factors affecting its magnitude, and explaining the effect of air resistance on the motion of objects.","What is the effect of decreasing the weight of an object on frictional force?","It increases frictional force.","It decreases frictional force.","It has no effect on frictional force.","It makes frictional force zero.","It decreases frictional force.","Decreasing the weight of an object decreases frictional force.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-effect-of-0c9a6e95","Science G6: Explaining the factors affecting types of - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-2-3 Explaining the factors affecting types of forces, such as gravitational force, friction, and magnetism.","2. Explaining how frictional force is generated and the factors affecting its magnitude, and explaining the effect of air resistance on the motion of objects.","What is the effect of increasing the surface roughness of an object on frictional force?","It decreases frictional force.","It increases frictional force.","It has no effect on frictional force.","It makes frictional force zero.","It increases frictional force.","Increasing the surface roughness of an object increases frictional force.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-effect-of-8a9a52e0","Science G6: Explaining the factors affecting types of - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-2-3 Explaining the factors affecting types of forces, such as gravitational force, friction, and magnetism.","2. Explaining how frictional force is generated and the factors affecting its magnitude, and explaining the effect of air resistance on the motion of objects.","What is the effect of increasing the surface area of an object on air resistance?","It decreases air resistance.","It increases air resistance.","It has no effect on air resistance.","It makes air resistance zero.","It increases air resistance.","Increasing the surface area of an object increases air resistance.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-effect-of-593b00c0","Science G6: Explaining the factors affecting types of - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-2-3 Explaining the factors affecting types of forces, such as gravitational force, friction, and magnetism.","2. Explaining how frictional force is generated and the factors affecting its magnitude, and explaining the effect of air resistance on the motion of objects.","What is the effect of increasing the speed of an object on air resistance?","It decreases air resistance.","It increases air resistance.","It has no effect on air resistance.","It makes air resistance zero.","It increases air resistance.","Increasing the speed of an object increases air resistance.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-air-resistance-1d278b06","Science G6: Explaining the factors affecting types of - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-2-3 Explaining the factors affecting types of forces, such as gravitational force, friction, and magnetism.","2. Explaining how frictional force is generated and the factors affecting its magnitude, and explaining the effect of air resistance on the motion of objects.","What is air resistance?","The force that pulls objects towards the ground.","The force that opposes the motion of objects through the air.","The force that pushes objects away from the ground.","The force that makes objects move slower.","The force that opposes the motion of objects through the air.","Air resistance is the force that opposes the motion of objects through the air.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-why-do-we-c9d20c76","Science G6: Explaining the phenomena related to the - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-3-1-2 Explaining the phenomena related to the movement of the earth, the moon and the sun and the resulting","3. Explaining the occurrence of the lunar eclipse and the solar eclipse.","Why do we experience longer days during summer?","Because the Earth rotates faster.","Because the Earth's axis is tilted towards the Sun.","Because the Sun is closer to the Earth.","Because the Earth is moving faster.","Because the Earth's axis is tilted towards the Sun.","The Earth's tilt on its axis causes the longer days during summer.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-why-do-we-9069177d","Science G6: Explaining the phenomena related to the - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-3-1-2 Explaining the phenomena related to the movement of the earth, the moon and the sun and the resulting","3. Explaining the occurrence of the lunar eclipse and the solar eclipse.","Why do we experience different times of day in different parts of the world?","Because the Earth is flat.","Because the Earth rotates on its side.","Because the Earth rotates on its axis.","Because the Earth is moving faster.","Because the Earth rotates on its axis.","The Earth's rotation on its axis causes different times of day in different parts of the world.","/image-source/sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-why-do-we-9069177d.png","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-main-reason-1b3f1eab","Science G6: Explaining the phenomena related to the - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-3-1-2 Explaining the phenomena related to the movement of the earth, the moon and the sun and the resulting","2. Explaining the occurrence of the phenomena of night and day, and the four seasons.","What is the main reason why we have day and night?","The Earth rotates on its axis.","The Earth rotates around the Sun.","The Sun moves around the Earth.","The Moon blocks the Sun's light.","The Earth rotates on its axis.","Day and night occur because the Earth rotates on its axis. As the Earth spins, different parts face towards or away from the Sun, creating the cycle of day and night.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-why-do-we-5c69fcce","Science G6: Explaining the phenomena related to the - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-3-1-2 Explaining the phenomena related to the movement of the earth, the moon and the sun and the resulting","2. Explaining the occurrence of the phenomena of night and day, and the four seasons.","Why do we experience different seasons throughout the year?","The Earth's axis is tilted towards the Sun.","The Earth's axis is tilted away from the Sun.","The Earth rotates faster in summer.","The Earth rotates slower in winter.","The Earth's axis is tilted towards the Sun.","The Earth's tilt on its axis causes the changing seasons.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-primary-source-df7f6185","Science G6: Explaining the phenomena related to the - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-3-1-2 Explaining the phenomena related to the movement of the earth, the moon and the sun and the resulting","3. Explaining the occurrence of the lunar eclipse and the solar eclipse.","What is the primary source of light and heat for our planet?","The Moon","The Sun","The Earth","The Stars","The Sun","The Sun is the primary source of light and heat for our planet.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-effect-of-df2f2ff1","Science G6: Explaining the phenomena related to the - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-3-1-2 Explaining the phenomena related to the movement of the earth, the moon and the sun and the resulting","2. Explaining the occurrence of the phenomena of night and day, and the four seasons.","What is the effect of the Sun's movement on the Earth's temperature?","The Earth's temperature remains constant.","The Earth's temperature increases during the day.","The Earth's temperature decreases at night.","The Earth's temperature changes throughout the year.","The Earth's temperature changes throughout the year.","The Sun's movement causes the Earth's temperature to change throughout the year.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-why-do-we-922299dd","Science G6: Explaining the phenomena related to the - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-3-1-2 Explaining the phenomena related to the movement of the earth, the moon and the sun and the resulting","2. Explaining the occurrence of the phenomena of night and day, and the four seasons.","Why do we see the Sun in the sky during the day?","Because the Sun is close to the Earth.","Because the Earth rotates on its axis.","Because the Sun is very bright.","Because the Earth is tilted on its axis.","Because the Earth rotates on its axis.","The Earth's rotation on its axis causes the Sun to appear in the sky during the day.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-main-reason-e7a1fb85","Science G6: Explaining the phenomena related to the - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-3-1-2 Explaining the phenomena related to the movement of the earth, the moon and the sun and the resulting","2. Explaining the occurrence of the phenomena of night and day, and the four seasons.","What is the main reason why we have a 24-hour clock?","Because the Earth rotates on its side.","Because the Earth rotates around the Sun.","Because the Earth rotates on its axis.","Because the Moon orbits the Earth.","Because the Earth rotates on its axis.","The Earth's rotation on its axis causes day and night, which is why we have a 24-hour clock.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-why-do-we-0a656c68","Science G6: Explaining the phenomena related to the - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-3-1-2 Explaining the phenomena related to the movement of the earth, the moon and the sun and the resulting","3. Explaining the occurrence of the lunar eclipse and the solar eclipse.","Why do we experience colder temperatures during winter?","Because the Earth is closer to the Sun.","Because the Earth's axis is tilted away from the Sun.","Because the Earth rotates faster.","Because the Sun is weaker.","Because the Earth's axis is tilted away from the Sun.","The Earth's tilt on its axis causes the colder temperatures during winter.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-effect-of-7057d0d5","Science G6: Explaining the phenomena related to the - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-3-1-2 Explaining the phenomena related to the movement of the earth, the moon and the sun and the resulting","2. Explaining the occurrence of the phenomena of night and day, and the four seasons.","What is the effect of the Sun's movement on plant growth?","Plants grow faster during the night.","Plants grow slower during the day.","Plants grow faster during the day.","Plants grow at the same rate throughout the year.","Plants grow faster during the day.","The Sun's movement provides plants with the necessary light and heat for growth.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-effect-of-030fb288","Science G6: Explaining the phenomena related to the - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-3-1-2 Explaining the phenomena related to the movement of the earth, the moon and the sun and the resulting","1. Explaining the phenomenon of tides, and explaining the influence of the moon in its occurrence and its geological effects.","What is the effect of the moon's gravitational pull on the Earth's oceans during a full moon when the moon is at its closest point to the Earth?","The oceans are pulled away from the Earth.","The oceans are pulled towards the Earth.","The oceans do not change.","The oceans are pulled towards the moon.","The oceans are pulled towards the moon.","The Moon's gravitational pull creates a tidal bulge by attracting the ocean water on the side of the Earth facing the Moon. This causes the oceans to bulge out towards the Moon, creating high tides.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-term-regular-759259d7","Science G6: Explaining the phenomena related to the - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-3-1-2 Explaining the phenomena related to the movement of the earth, the moon and the sun and the resulting","1. Explaining the phenomenon of tides, and explaining the influence of the moon in its occurrence and its geological effects.","What is the term for the regular rise and fall of the sea level caused by the gravitational pull of the moon?","Tides","Currents","Waves","Surf","Tides","Tides are the regular rise and fall of the sea level caused by the gravitational pull of the moon.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-effect-of-c70d0da0","Science G6: Explaining the phenomena related to the - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-3-1-2 Explaining the phenomena related to the movement of the earth, the moon and the sun and the resulting","1. Explaining the phenomenon of tides, and explaining the influence of the moon in its occurrence and its geological effects.","What is the effect of the moon's gravitational pull on the Earth's oceans during a full moon?","The oceans are pulled away from the Earth.","The oceans are pulled towards the Earth.","The oceans do not change.","The oceans are pulled towards the moon.","The oceans are pulled towards the moon.","The Moon's gravitational pull creates a tidal bulge by attracting the ocean water on the side of the Earth facing the Moon. This causes the oceans to bulge out towards the Moon, creating high tides.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-term-point-d5ddb0c8","Science G6: Explaining the phenomena related to the - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-3-1-2 Explaining the phenomena related to the movement of the earth, the moon and the sun and the resulting","1. Explaining the phenomenon of tides, and explaining the influence of the moon in its occurrence and its geological effects.","What is the term for the point on Earth where the moon's gravitational pull is strongest?","The tide line","The high tide mark","The low tide mark","The point of maximum gravitational pull","The point of maximum gravitational pull","The point on Earth where the moon's gravitational pull is strongest is called the point of maximum gravitational pull.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-happens-to-ocean-3c8943d9","Science G6: Explaining the phenomena related to the - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-3-1-2 Explaining the phenomena related to the movement of the earth, the moon and the sun and the resulting","3. Explaining the occurrence of the lunar eclipse and the solar eclipse.","What happens to the ocean water when the moon is directly overhead?","The water is pulled away from the Earth.","The water is pulled towards the Earth.","The water does not change.","The water is pulled towards the moon.","The water is pulled towards the Earth.","The moon's gravitational pull causes the ocean water to be pulled towards the Earth when the moon is directly overhead.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-effect-of-507c209b","Science G6: Explaining the phenomena related to the - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-3-1-2 Explaining the phenomena related to the movement of the earth, the moon and the sun and the resulting","1. Explaining the phenomenon of tides, and explaining the influence of the moon in its occurrence and its geological effects.","What is the effect of the moon's gravitational pull on the Earth's oceans during a new moon?","The oceans are pulled away from the Earth.","The oceans are pulled towards the Earth.","The oceans do not change.","The oceans are pulled towards the moon.","The oceans are pulled towards the moon.","The Moon's gravitational pull creates a tidal bulge by attracting the ocean water on the side of the Earth facing the Moon. This causes the oceans to bulge out towards the Moon, creating high tides.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-term-difference-5c337ad6","Science G6: Explaining the phenomena related to the - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-3-1-2 Explaining the phenomena related to the movement of the earth, the moon and the sun and the resulting","3. Explaining the occurrence of the lunar eclipse and the solar eclipse.","What is the term for the difference in height between high tide and low tide?","Tidal range","Tidal cycle","Tidal period","Tidal amplitude","Tidal range","The term for the difference in height between high tide and low tide is called tidal range.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-effect-of-bf3d3471","Science G6: Explaining the phenomena related to the - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-3-1-2 Explaining the phenomena related to the movement of the earth, the moon and the sun and the resulting","1. Explaining the phenomenon of tides, and explaining the influence of the moon in its occurrence and its geological effects.","What is the effect of the moon's gravitational pull on the Earth's oceans during a quarter moon?","The oceans are pulled away from the Earth.","The oceans are pulled towards the Earth.","The oceans do not change.","The oceans are pulled towards the moon.","The oceans are pulled towards the moon.","The Moon's gravitational pull creates a tidal bulge by attracting the ocean water on the side of the Earth facing the Moon. This causes the oceans to bulge out towards the Moon, creating high tides.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-term-regular-3ed5419e","Science G6: Explaining the phenomena related to the - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-3-1-2 Explaining the phenomena related to the movement of the earth, the moon and the sun and the resulting","3. Explaining the occurrence of the lunar eclipse and the solar eclipse.","What is the term for the regular movement of the Earth's oceans caused by the gravitational pull of the moon?","Tidal movement","Ocean currents","Wave motion","Tidal flow","Tidal movement","The term for the regular movement of the Earth's oceans caused by the gravitational pull of the moon is called tidal movement.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-causes-ocean-water-ac6a81fb","Science G6: Explaining the phenomena related to the - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-3-1-2 Explaining the phenomena related to the movement of the earth, the moon and the sun and the resulting","3. Explaining the occurrence of the lunar eclipse and the solar eclipse.","What causes the ocean water to bulge out in two areas on Earth?","The sun's gravity pulls on the Earth's oceans.","The moon's gravity pulls on the Earth's oceans.","The Earth's rotation causes the oceans to bulge.","The wind and waves cause the oceans to bulge.","The moon's gravity pulls on the Earth's oceans.","The moon's gravity pulls on the Earth's oceans, causing the water to bulge out in two areas.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-should-you-do-701b6f0f","Science G6: Explaining the phenomena related to the - Set 3","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-3-1-2 Explaining the phenomena related to the movement of the earth, the moon and the sun and the resulting","3. Explaining the occurrence of the lunar eclipse and the solar eclipse.","What should you do if you are not able to observe a solar eclipse?","Try to observe the eclipse through a camera lens.","Use a pair of binoculars to get a closer look.","Watch the eclipse on a computer or TV screen.","Look directly at the Sun without any protection.","Watch the eclipse on a computer or TV screen.","If you are not able to observe a solar eclipse, you can watch the eclipse on a computer or TV screen to stay safe.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-a-solar-fb3b2d66","Science G6: Explaining the phenomena related to the - Set 3","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-3-1-2 Explaining the phenomena related to the movement of the earth, the moon and the sun and the resulting","3. Explaining the occurrence of the lunar eclipse and the solar eclipse.","What is a solar eclipse?","A lunar eclipse that occurs at night.","A lunar eclipse that occurs during the day.","A phenomenon that occurs when the Moon passes between the Earth and the Sun.","A phenomenon that occurs when the Earth passes between the Sun and the Moon.","A phenomenon that occurs when the Moon passes between the Earth and the Sun.","A solar eclipse is a phenomenon that occurs when the Moon passes between the Earth and the Sun, blocking the sunlight that normally reaches the Earth.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-should-you-do-9f0a94d7","Science G6: Explaining the phenomena related to the - Set 3","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-3-1-2 Explaining the phenomena related to the movement of the earth, the moon and the sun and the resulting","3. Explaining the occurrence of the lunar eclipse and the solar eclipse.","What should you do if you experience any eye discomfort or vision problems after looking at a solar eclipse?","Try to ignore the discomfort and continue observing the eclipse.","Use eye drops to relieve the discomfort.","Seek medical attention immediately.","Wait for a few hours and see if the discomfort goes away.","Seek medical attention immediately.","If you experience any eye discomfort or vision problems after looking at a solar eclipse, you should seek medical attention immediately.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-why-is-it-dd389a93","Science G6: Explaining the phenomena related to the - Set 3","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-3-1-2 Explaining the phenomena related to the movement of the earth, the moon and the sun and the resulting","3. Explaining the occurrence of the lunar eclipse and the solar eclipse.","Why is it important to follow safety guidelines during a solar eclipse?","Because it is a rare and exciting event.","Because it is a good opportunity to take photos.","Because it is a chance to learn about the Sun and the Moon.","Because looking directly at the Sun can cause serious eye damage.","Because looking directly at the Sun can cause serious eye damage.","It is important to follow safety guidelines during a solar eclipse because looking directly at the Sun can cause serious eye damage.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-why-is-it-3cf172cc","Science G6: Explaining the phenomena related to the - Set 3","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-3-1-2 Explaining the phenomena related to the movement of the earth, the moon and the sun and the resulting","3. Explaining the occurrence of the lunar eclipse and the solar eclipse.","Why is it not safe to look directly at the Sun during a solar eclipse?","Because the Sun is too small to cause eye damage.","Because the Sun is too far away to cause eye damage.","Because the Sun's light is not strong enough to cause eye damage.","Because the Sun's ultraviolet and infrared radiation can cause serious eye damage.","Because the Sun's ultraviolet and infrared radiation can cause serious eye damage.","It is not safe to look directly at the Sun during a solar eclipse because the Sun's ultraviolet and infrared radiation can cause serious eye damage.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-best-way-fde6c775","Science G6: Explaining the phenomena related to the - Set 3","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-3-1-2 Explaining the phenomena related to the movement of the earth, the moon and the sun and the resulting","3. Explaining the occurrence of the lunar eclipse and the solar eclipse.","What is the best way to observe a solar eclipse?","By looking directly at the Sun without any protection.","By using a pair of binoculars to get a closer look.","By using special solar viewing glasses or a handheld solar viewer.","By using a telescope to get a detailed view.","By using special solar viewing glasses or a handheld solar viewer.","The best way to observe a solar eclipse is by using special solar viewing glasses or a handheld solar viewer to protect your eyes from the Sun's harmful rays.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-why-is-it-aba63df2","Science G6: Explaining the phenomena related to the - Set 3","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-3-1-2 Explaining the phenomena related to the movement of the earth, the moon and the sun and the resulting","3. Explaining the occurrence of the lunar eclipse and the solar eclipse.","Why is it safe to look directly at the Moon during a lunar eclipse?","Because the Moon is too small to cause eye damage.","Because the Moon is too far away to cause eye damage.","Because the Earth's atmosphere blocks the Sun's harmful rays during a lunar eclipse.","Because the Sun's light is not strong enough to cause eye damage during a lunar eclipse.","Because the Earth's atmosphere blocks the Sun's harmful rays during a lunar eclipse.","It is safe to look directly at the Moon during a lunar eclipse because the Earth's atmosphere blocks the Sun's harmful rays.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-difference-a-cf6f25bf","Science G6: Explaining the phenomena related to the - Set 3","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-3-1-2 Explaining the phenomena related to the movement of the earth, the moon and the sun and the resulting","3. Explaining the occurrence of the lunar eclipse and the solar eclipse.","What is the difference between a lunar eclipse and a solar eclipse?","A lunar eclipse occurs during the day and a solar eclipse occurs at night.","A lunar eclipse occurs when the Earth passes between the Sun and the Moon and a solar eclipse occurs when the Moon passes between the Earth and the Sun.","A lunar eclipse is caused by the Moon passing between the Earth and the Sun and a solar eclipse is caused by the Earth passing between the Sun and the Moon.","A lunar eclipse is caused by the Earth passing between the Sun and the Moon and a solar eclipse is caused by the Moon passing between the Earth and the Sun.","A lunar eclipse occurs when the Earth passes between the Sun and the Moon and a solar eclipse occurs when the Moon passes between the Earth and the Sun.","A lunar eclipse occurs when the Earth passes between the Sun and the Moon, while a solar eclipse occurs when the Moon passes between the Earth and the Sun.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-why-a-solar-abd2c600","Science G6: Explaining the phenomena related to the - Set 3","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-3-1-2 Explaining the phenomena related to the movement of the earth, the moon and the sun and the resulting","3. Explaining the occurrence of the lunar eclipse and the solar eclipse.","Why does a solar eclipse only occur during the day?","Because the Moon is too small to block the Sun's light.","Because the Earth is too far away from the Sun.","Because the Sun is too big to be blocked by the Moon.","Because the Moon passes between the Earth and the Sun during the day.","Because the Moon passes between the Earth and the Sun during the day.","A solar eclipse only occurs during the day because the Moon passes between the Earth and the Sun, blocking the sunlight that normally reaches the Earth.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-causes-a-lunar-6f4fefef","Science G6: Explaining the phenomena related to the - Set 3","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-3-1-2 Explaining the phenomena related to the movement of the earth, the moon and the sun and the resulting","3. Explaining the occurrence of the lunar eclipse and the solar eclipse.","What causes a lunar eclipse?","The Earth passes between the Sun and the Moon.","The Moon passes between the Earth and the Sun.","The Earth and Moon are aligned with the Sun.","The Sun passes between the Earth and the Moon.","The Earth passes between the Sun and the Moon.","A lunar eclipse occurs when the Earth passes between the Sun and the Moon, blocking the sunlight that normally reflects off the Moon's surface.","","",""
