"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-why-is-it-68b3bd19","Science G6: Inferring the impact of human activity - Set 3","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-5 Inferring the impact of human activity on environmental habitats and populations, and predicting its effect, proposing solutions for their protection","1. Explaining human interaction with environments, and inferring the positive and negative impact of human activities on the environmental habitats and populations.","Why is it essential to reduce air pollution?","So we can make our air and water cleaner","To make our landfills bigger","To increase air pollution and harm our health","To make our electricity bills cheaper","So we can make our air and water cleaner","Reducing air pollution helps make our air and water cleaner, protecting our health and the environment.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-why-is-it-cde0155e","Science G6: Inferring the impact of human activity - Set 3","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-5 Inferring the impact of human activity on environmental habitats and populations, and predicting its effect, proposing solutions for their protection","1. Explaining human interaction with environments, and inferring the positive and negative impact of human activities on the environmental habitats and populations.","Why is it crucial to conserve energy?","So we can waste more energy and resources","To make our air and water cleaner","To ensure there's enough energy for essential uses","To make our electricity bills cheaper","To ensure there's enough energy for essential uses","Conserving energy ensures there's enough energy for essential uses, such as powering homes and hospitals.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-why-is-it-17173161","Science G6: Inferring the impact of human activity - Set 3","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-5 Inferring the impact of human activity on environmental habitats and populations, and predicting its effect, proposing solutions for their protection","1. Explaining human interaction with environments, and inferring the positive and negative impact of human activities on the environmental habitats and populations.","Why is it essential to protect our forests?","So we can cut down more trees for paper","To make our air and water cleaner","To provide homes for animals and plants","To make our landfills bigger","To provide homes for animals and plants","Protecting our forests provides homes for animals and plants and helps maintain a healthy ecosystem.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-can-we-do-f52fd7d8","Science G6: Inferring the impact of human activity - Set 3","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-5 Inferring the impact of human activity on environmental habitats and populations, and predicting its effect, proposing solutions for their protection","1. Explaining human interaction with environments, and inferring the positive and negative impact of human activities on the environmental habitats and populations.","What can we do to prevent soil erosion?","Cut down more trees to make way for buildings","Use pesticides to kill plants","Plant more trees and cover crops to hold the soil in place","Build more roads to increase traffic","Plant more trees and cover crops to hold the soil in place","Planting more trees and cover crops helps hold the soil in place and prevent erosion.","/image-source/sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-can-we-do-f52fd7d8.png","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-why-is-it-8c720086","Science G6: Inferring the impact of human activity - Set 3","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-5 Inferring the impact of human activity on environmental habitats and populations, and predicting its effect, proposing solutions for their protection","1. Explaining human interaction with environments, and inferring the positive and negative impact of human activities on the environmental habitats and populations.","Why is it crucial to reduce, reuse, and recycle?","So we can waste more energy and resources","To make our landfills bigger","To conserve natural resources and reduce waste","To make our trash cans smell worse","To conserve natural resources and reduce waste","Reducing, reusing, and recycling helps conserve natural resources and reduce waste.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-can-we-do-29a17d80","Science G6: Inferring the impact of human activity - Set 3","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-5 Inferring the impact of human activity on environmental habitats and populations, and predicting its effect, proposing solutions for their protection","1. Explaining human interaction with environments, and inferring the positive and negative impact of human activities on the environmental habitats and populations.","What can we do to protect endangered species?","Hunt them for their fur or meat","Create more habitats for them to live in","Release non-native species into their habitats","Pollute their habitats with chemicals","Create more habitats for them to live in","Creating more habitats for endangered species helps protect them from extinction.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-why-is-it-aa2ef13a","Science G6: Inferring the impact of human activity - Set 3","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-5 Inferring the impact of human activity on environmental habitats and populations, and predicting its effect, proposing solutions for their protection","1. Explaining human interaction with environments, and inferring the positive and negative impact of human activities on the environmental habitats and populations.","Why is it essential to conserve water?","So we can have enough water for swimming pools","To keep our lakes and rivers clean","To ensure there's enough water for drinking and farming","To make our water bills cheaper","To ensure there's enough water for drinking and farming","Conserving water ensures there's enough water for drinking, farming, and other essential uses.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-can-we-do-b6881d8f","Science G6: Inferring the impact of human activity - Set 3","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-5 Inferring the impact of human activity on environmental habitats and populations, and predicting its effect, proposing solutions for their protection","1. Explaining human interaction with environments, and inferring the positive and negative impact of human activities on the environmental habitats and populations.","What can we do to reduce pollution in our oceans?","Recycle more plastic bottles and bags","Use public transportation to reduce carbon emissions","Plant more trees to absorb carbon dioxide","Use reusable bags and containers for shopping","Recycle more plastic bottles and bags","Recycling plastic bottles and bags helps reduce the amount of waste that ends up in our oceans, harming marine life.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-importance-of-024648a7","Science G6: Representing biological communities and identifying types - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-1 Representing biological communities and identifying types of population that lives in it, describ-ing the interrelationships among them, and their inter-action with the abiotic components, and the impact of biological communities changes on their survival and sustainability.","3.Describing the effect of different changes in biological communities on the surviv-al and sustainability of different species.","What is the importance of abiotic components in a biological community?","Abiotic components help living things grow and thrive","Abiotic components harm living things and make them weaker","Abiotic components have no effect on living things","Abiotic components are only important for plants","Abiotic components help living things grow and thrive","Abiotic components, such as water, air, and sunlight, help living things grow and thrive in a biological community.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-a-biological-e6c8d3d8","Science G6: Representing biological communities and identifying types - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-4 Describing the effect of environmental changes on plants and animals that live in specific environments. Inferring how behavioral and structural adaptations can help plants and animals survive in their habitats","2. Predicting the changes that will happen to living organisms as a result of  of changes in their environments.","What is a biological community?","A group of plants and animals living together in a specific area","A type of animal that lives in a group","A type of plant that grows in a specific climate","A type of rock that forms in the ocean","A group of plants and animals living together in a specific area","A biological community is a group of plants and animals living together in a specific area, interacting with each other and their environment.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-do-living-organisms-ffdc583c","Science G6: Representing biological communities and identifying types - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-1 Representing biological communities and identifying types of population that lives in it, describ-ing the interrelationships among them, and their inter-action with the abiotic components, and the impact of biological communities changes on their survival and sustainability.","2.Identifying the interrelationships among living organisms, and their interaction with the abiotic components of their habitats to obtain their needs.","What do living organisms need to reproduce?","Food, water, and shelter","Food, water, and air","Food, water, and friends","Food, water, and a safe place to live","Food, water, and air","Living organisms need food, water, and air to reproduce, as well as a safe place to live and friends to help them.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-do-living-organisms-5c7cef61","Science G6: Representing biological communities and identifying types - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-1 Representing biological communities and identifying types of population that lives in it, describ-ing the interrelationships among them, and their inter-action with the abiotic components, and the impact of biological communities changes on their survival and sustainability.","2.Identifying the interrelationships among living organisms, and their interaction with the abiotic components of their habitats to obtain their needs.","What do living organisms need to survive in their habitats?","Food, water, and shelter","Food, water, and air","Food, shelter, and friends","Water, shelter, and exercise","Food, water, and air","Living organisms need food, water, and air to survive in their habitats.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-happens-a-biological-e8e12404","Science G6: Representing biological communities and identifying types - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-1 Representing biological communities and identifying types of population that lives in it, describ-ing the interrelationships among them, and their inter-action with the abiotic components, and the impact of biological communities changes on their survival and sustainability.","3.Describing the effect of different changes in biological communities on the surviv-al and sustainability of different species.","What happens when a biological community becomes too crowded?","The living things in the community get stronger","The living things in the community get weaker","The living things in the community adapt to the change","The living things in the community disappear","The living things in the community get weaker","When a biological community becomes too crowded, the living things in the community may get weaker or disappear if they are not able to adapt to the change.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-role-of-712caaa9","Science G6: Representing biological communities and identifying types - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-1 Representing biological communities and identifying types of population that lives in it, describ-ing the interrelationships among them, and their inter-action with the abiotic components, and the impact of biological communities changes on their survival and sustainability.","3.Describing the effect of different changes in biological communities on the surviv-al and sustainability of different species.","What is the role of predators in a biological community?","Predators help living things grow and thrive","Predators harm living things and make them weaker","Predators have no effect on living things","Predators help keep the population of living things in balance","Predators help keep the population of living things in balance","Predators help keep the population of living things in balance by controlling the number of prey animals.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-importance-of-e2093113","Science G6: Representing biological communities and identifying types - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-1 Representing biological communities and identifying types of population that lives in it, describ-ing the interrelationships among them, and their inter-action with the abiotic components, and the impact of biological communities changes on their survival and sustainability.","3.Describing the effect of different changes in biological communities on the surviv-al and sustainability of different species.","What is the importance of symbiotic relationships in a biological community?","Symbiotic relationships harm living things and make them weaker","Symbiotic relationships have no effect on living things","Symbiotic relationships help living things grow and thrive","Symbiotic relationships are only important for plants","Symbiotic relationships help living things grow and thrive","Symbiotic relationships, such as mutualism and commensalism, help living things grow and thrive in a biological community.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-a-population-e56e1e42","Science G6: Representing biological communities and identifying types - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-4 Describing the effect of environmental changes on plants and animals that live in specific environments. Inferring how behavioral and structural adaptations can help plants and animals survive in their habitats","2. Predicting the changes that will happen to living organisms as a result of  of changes in their environments.","What is a population?","A group of living things that live in a specific area","A type of animal that lives in a group","A type of plant that grows in a specific climate","A type of rock that forms in the ocean","A group of living things that live in a specific area","A population is a group of living things that live in a specific area, such as a group of birds or a group of trees.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-happens-a-biological-6915868c","Science G6: Representing biological communities and identifying types - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-1 Representing biological communities and identifying types of population that lives in it, describ-ing the interrelationships among them, and their inter-action with the abiotic components, and the impact of biological communities changes on their survival and sustainability.","3.Describing the effect of different changes in biological communities on the surviv-al and sustainability of different species.","What happens when a biological community changes?","The living things in the community get stronger","The living things in the community get weaker","The living things in the community adapt to the change","The living things in the community disappear","The living things in the community adapt to the change","When a biological community changes, the living things in the community may adapt to the change, but they may also get weaker or disappear if they are not able to adapt.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-an-abiotic-086e1c83","Science G6: Representing biological communities and identifying types - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-4 Describing the effect of environmental changes on plants and animals that live in specific environments. Inferring how behavioral and structural adaptations can help plants and animals survive in their habitats","1. Identifying the physical factors that affect the survival of plants and animals in specific habitats","What is an abiotic component?","A living thing that helps other living things","A non-living thing that affects living things","A type of plant that grows in a specific climate","A type of animal that lives in a group","A non-living thing that affects living things","An abiotic component is a non-living thing that affects living things, such as water, air, and sunlight.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-can-happen-to-766491ed","Science G6: Representing biological communities and identifying types - Set 1 T1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-1 Representing biological communities and identifying types of population that lives in it, describ-ing the interrelationships among them, and their inter-action with the abiotic components, and the impact of biological communities changes on their survival and sustainability.","3.Describing the effect of different changes in biological communities on the surviv-al and sustainability of different species.","What can happen to a community if it is affected by climate change?","The community will become stronger","The community will stay the same","The community will become weaker","The community will disappear","The community will become weaker","Climate change can affect a community by changing the availability of resources, leading to a weaker community.","/image-source/sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-can-happen-to-766491ed.png","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-why-is-it-0c0b4b47","Science G6: Representing biological communities and identifying types - Set 1 T1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-4 Describing the effect of environmental changes on plants and animals that live in specific environments. Inferring how behavioral and structural adaptations can help plants and animals survive in their habitats","4. Describing the climatic conditions in different environments and their impact on living organisms.","Why is it important for living things to adapt to their habitats?","So they can survive in different environments","So they can compete with other living things","So they can live in isolation","So they can harm other living things","So they can survive in different environments","Living things need to adapt to their habitats in order to survive and thrive in different environments.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-can-happen-to-5f9884dc","Science G6: Representing biological communities and identifying types - Set 1 T1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-4 Describing the effect of environmental changes on plants and animals that live in specific environments. Inferring how behavioral and structural adaptations can help plants and animals survive in their habitats","2. Predicting the changes that will happen to living organisms as a result of  of changes in their environments.","What can happen to a community if it loses a key species?","The community will become stronger","The community will stay the same","The community will become weaker","The community will disappear","The community will become weaker","If a community loses a key species, it can become weaker and less able to survive.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-do-plants-animals-d4f6dacd","Science G6: Representing biological communities and identifying types - Set 1 T1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-4 Describing the effect of environmental changes on plants and animals that live in specific environments. Inferring how behavioral and structural adaptations can help plants and animals survive in their habitats","3. Describing how structural and behavioral adaptations can help plants and animals live and survive in specific habitats.","What do plants and animals need to survive in their habitats?","They need only water to survive","They need only sunlight to survive","They need air, water, and food to survive","They need only shelter to survive","They need air, water, and food to survive","Plants and animals need air, water, and food to survive in their habitats.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-a-population-bdfb17c4","Science G6: Representing biological communities and identifying types - Set 1 T1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-1 Representing biological communities and identifying types of population that lives in it, describ-ing the interrelationships among them, and their inter-action with the abiotic components, and the impact of biological communities changes on their survival and sustainability.","3.Describing the effect of different changes in biological communities on the surviv-al and sustainability of different species.","What is a population?","A group of living things that live in the same area","A group of living things that have the same characteristics","A group of living things that are the same species","A group of living things that live in different areas","A group of living things that are the same species","A population is a group of living things that are the same species and live in the same area.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-why-do-living-c6ccb21c","Science G6: Representing biological communities and identifying types - Set 1 T1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-1 Representing biological communities and identifying types of population that lives in it, describ-ing the interrelationships among them, and their inter-action with the abiotic components, and the impact of biological communities changes on their survival and sustainability.","2.Identifying the interrelationships among living organisms, and their interaction with the abiotic components of their habitats to obtain their needs.","Why do living things need shelter in their habitats?","So they can protect themselves from the sun","So they can protect themselves from the wind","So they can protect themselves from predators","So they can protect themselves from the cold","So they can protect themselves from predators","Living things need shelter in their habitats to protect themselves from predators and other dangers.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-do-we-call-b24072fe","Science G6: Representing biological communities and identifying types - Set 1 T1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-1 Representing biological communities and identifying types of population that lives in it, describ-ing the interrelationships among them, and their inter-action with the abiotic components, and the impact of biological communities changes on their survival and sustainability.","3.Describing the effect of different changes in biological communities on the surviv-al and sustainability of different species.","What do we call a group of living things that live in the same area and interact with each other?","A forest is a type of plant","A group of animals that live together","A type of rock formation","A type of weather condition","A group of animals that live together","A biological community is a group of living things that live in the same area and interact with each other.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-why-is-it-fa32bec7","Science G6: Representing biological communities and identifying types - Set 1 T1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-1 Representing biological communities and identifying types of population that lives in it, describ-ing the interrelationships among them, and their inter-action with the abiotic components, and the impact of biological communities changes on their survival and sustainability.","1.Describing the biological community and the types of population and living or-ganisms that live in it, and their ability to survive in their habitats through the avail-ability of the necessities of life.","Why is it important for living things to interact with each other in a community?","So they can compete for resources","So they can live in isolation","So they can help each other survive","So they can harm each other","So they can help each other survive","Living things interact with each other in a community to help each other survive and thrive.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-an-abiotic-0d99e07e","Science G6: Representing biological communities and identifying types - Set 1 T1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-4-1 Identifying the inheritance of traits, explaining the variation in them, tracing their transmission from one generation to the next, distinguishing their types (dominant and recessive), and clarifying the impact of the environment on them.","5. Identifying some of the environmental factors that affect the acquired traits of animals and plants (the amount of food, the amount of water, the amount of animal movement), and identifying the traits that are affected by environmental factors (height, weight, and color).","What is an abiotic factor?","A living thing that lives in a community","A non-living thing that affects a community","A type of food that animals eat","A type of shelter that animals live in","A non-living thing that affects a community","An abiotic factor is a non-living thing that affects a community, such as water, air, or sunlight.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-role-of-6f0d5a86","Science G6: Representing biological communities and identifying types - Set 1 T1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-3 Representing the relationships between living organisms in which matter is circulated in the ecosystem, and identifying the relationship between plants and energy obtained from the sun to produce food.","1. Clarifying the cycle of matter between living organisms, and the transfer of energy in the ecosystem through food chain, and classify their different roles (producer, consumer, predator, decomposer)","What is the role of decomposers in a community?","They help to clean up the community","They help to make food for other living things","They help to break down dead plants and animals","They help to make shelter for other living things","They help to break down dead plants and animals","Decomposers help to break down dead plants and animals, recycling nutrients and helping to keep the community healthy.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-do-living-organisms-95b23bd4","Science G6: Representing biological communities and identifying types - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-3 Representing the relationships between living organisms in which matter is circulated in the ecosystem, and identifying the relationship between plants and energy obtained from the sun to produce food.","3. Explaining the process of photosynthesis and its role in determining the relationship between plants and the energy obtained from the sun to produce food.","What do living organisms in a forest need to grow?","Food and shelter","Water and sunlight","Air and water","Food and air","Water and sunlight","Living organisms in a forest need water and sunlight to grow. Water provides them with the necessary nutrients, and sunlight helps them undergo photosynthesis.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-do-living-organisms-e8901eca","Science G6: Representing biological communities and identifying types - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-3 Representing the relationships between living organisms in which matter is circulated in the ecosystem, and identifying the relationship between plants and energy obtained from the sun to produce food.","2. Classifying living organisms into (autotrophic - heterotrophic).","What do living organisms in a forest need to reproduce?","Food and shelter","Water and sunlight","Air and water","Food and air","Water and sunlight","Living organisms in a forest need water and sunlight to reproduce. Water helps them produce offspring, and sunlight provides them with the necessary energy.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-do-plants-animals-cc6610a8","Science G6: Representing biological communities and identifying types - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-1-3 Identifying the main body systems and their specialized organs and linking them to their func-tions that supports growth and survival of living organ-isms (plants and animals).","1.Identifying the main systems in animal's body and its specialized organs, and link-ing them to their functions that helps them grow and survive (digestive, circulato-ry, excretory, respiratory, skeletal, muscular, and nervous).","What do plants and animals in a mountain need to survive?","Water and food","Food and shelter","Air and water","Water and shelter","Air and water","Plants and animals in a mountain need air and water to survive. Air provides them with oxygen, and water helps them regulate their body temperature.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-do-plants-animals-3e64cc12","Science G6: Representing biological communities and identifying types - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-1-3 Identifying the main body systems and their specialized organs and linking them to their func-tions that supports growth and survival of living organ-isms (plants and animals).","1.Identifying the main systems in animal's body and its specialized organs, and link-ing them to their functions that helps them grow and survive (digestive, circulato-ry, excretory, respiratory, skeletal, muscular, and nervous).","What do plants and animals in a desert need to survive?","Water and food","Food and shelter","Air and water","Water and shelter","Air and water","Plants and animals in a desert need air and water to survive. Air provides them with oxygen, and water helps them regulate their body temperature.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-do-plants-animals-01fdeadb","Science G6: Representing biological communities and identifying types - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-4 Describing the effect of environmental changes on plants and animals that live in specific environments. Inferring how behavioral and structural adaptations can help plants and animals survive in their habitats","4. Describing the climatic conditions in different environments and their impact on living organisms.","What do plants and animals in a forest need to survive?","Water and sunlight","Food and shelter","Air and water","Food and air","Food and shelter","Plants and animals in a forest need food and shelter to survive. Food provides energy, and shelter protects them from harsh weather conditions.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-do-living-organisms-5db363e8","Science G6: Representing biological communities and identifying types - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-1 Representing biological communities and identifying types of population that lives in it, describ-ing the interrelationships among them, and their inter-action with the abiotic components, and the impact of biological communities changes on their survival and sustainability.","2.Identifying the interrelationships among living organisms, and their interaction with the abiotic components of their habitats to obtain their needs.","What do living organisms in a pond need to live?","Water and sunlight","Food and shelter","Air and water","Food and air","Water and sunlight","Living organisms in a pond need water and sunlight to live. Water provides them with the necessary nutrients, and sunlight helps them undergo photosynthesis.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-do-plants-animals-84adf1c9","Science G6: Representing biological communities and identifying types - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-3 Representing the relationships between living organisms in which matter is circulated in the ecosystem, and identifying the relationship between plants and energy obtained from the sun to produce food.","2. Classifying living organisms into (autotrophic - heterotrophic).","What do plants and animals in a river need to survive?","Water and food","Food and shelter","Air and water","Water and shelter","Water and food","Plants and animals in a river need water and food to survive. Water provides them with the necessary nutrients, and food helps them obtain energy.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-do-living-organisms-7e66eb24","Science G6: Representing biological communities and identifying types - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-3 Representing the relationships between living organisms in which matter is circulated in the ecosystem, and identifying the relationship between plants and energy obtained from the sun to produce food.","2. Classifying living organisms into (autotrophic - heterotrophic).","What do living organisms in a forest need to adapt?","Food and shelter","Water and sunlight","Air and water","Food and air","Water and sunlight","Living organisms in a forest need water and sunlight to adapt. Water helps them adjust to their environment, and sunlight provides them with the necessary energy.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-do-plants-animals-07e1bdbf","Science G6: Representing biological communities and identifying types - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-3 Representing the relationships between living organisms in which matter is circulated in the ecosystem, and identifying the relationship between plants and energy obtained from the sun to produce food.","2. Classifying living organisms into (autotrophic - heterotrophic).","What do plants and animals in a lake need to survive?","Water and food","Food and shelter","Air and water","Water and shelter","Water and food","Plants and animals in a lake need water and food to survive. Water provides them with the necessary nutrients, and food helps them obtain energy.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-do-living-organisms-1c7a5cd4","Science G6: Representing biological communities and identifying types - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-3 Representing the relationships between living organisms in which matter is circulated in the ecosystem, and identifying the relationship between plants and energy obtained from the sun to produce food.","2. Classifying living organisms into (autotrophic - heterotrophic).","What do living organisms in a forest need to interact?","Food and shelter","Water and sunlight","Air and water","Food and air","Water and sunlight","Living organisms in a forest need water and sunlight to interact. Water helps them communicate, and sunlight provides them with the necessary energy.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-why-is-it-22db0267","Science G6: Representing biological communities and identifying types - Set 2 T1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-1 Representing biological communities and identifying types of population that lives in it, describ-ing the interrelationships among them, and their inter-action with the abiotic components, and the impact of biological communities changes on their survival and sustainability.","2.Identifying the interrelationships among living organisms, and their interaction with the abiotic components of their habitats to obtain their needs.","Why is it important for living organisms to understand their interrelationships with abiotic components?","To obtain their needs and survive","To compete for resources and harm each other","To adapt to changes and sustain themselves","To have no relationship","To obtain their needs and survive","It is important for living organisms to understand their interrelationships with abiotic components to obtain their needs and survive.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-how-do-living-3c92c571","Science G6: Representing biological communities and identifying types - Set 2 T1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-1 Representing biological communities and identifying types of population that lives in it, describ-ing the interrelationships among them, and their inter-action with the abiotic components, and the impact of biological communities changes on their survival and sustainability.","2.Identifying the interrelationships among living organisms, and their interaction with the abiotic components of their habitats to obtain their needs.","How do living organisms in a habitat interact with each other?","They compete for resources","They cooperate to obtain their needs","They have no relationship","They harm each other","They cooperate to obtain their needs","Living organisms in a habitat interact with each other by cooperating to obtain their needs.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-impact-of-96508b8e","Science G6: Representing biological communities and identifying types - Set 2 T1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-1 Representing biological communities and identifying types of population that lives in it, describ-ing the interrelationships among them, and their inter-action with the abiotic components, and the impact of biological communities changes on their survival and sustainability.","2.Identifying the interrelationships among living organisms, and their interaction with the abiotic components of their habitats to obtain their needs.","What is the impact of changes in a habitat on living organisms?","They adapt to the changes","They die off","They move to a new habitat","They do not change","They die off","Changes in a habitat can have a negative impact on living organisms, causing them to die off.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-do-plants-animals-d6fd476c","Science G6: Representing biological communities and identifying types - Set 2 T1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-4 Describing the effect of environmental changes on plants and animals that live in specific environments. Inferring how behavioral and structural adaptations can help plants and animals survive in their habitats","3. Describing how structural and behavioral adaptations can help plants and animals live and survive in specific habitats.","What do plants and animals need to survive in their habitats?","Water and sunlight","Food and shelter","Air and water","Food and air","Air and water","Plants and animals need air and water to breathe and drink, respectively, in order to survive.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-do-living-organisms-62cf77b9","Science G6: Representing biological communities and identifying types - Set 2 T1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-1 Representing biological communities and identifying types of population that lives in it, describ-ing the interrelationships among them, and their inter-action with the abiotic components, and the impact of biological communities changes on their survival and sustainability.","2.Identifying the interrelationships among living organisms, and their interaction with the abiotic components of their habitats to obtain their needs.","What do living organisms in a habitat need to obtain their needs?","Abiotic components like water and sunlight","Other living organisms like plants and animals","Both abiotic and biotic components","Neither abiotic nor biotic components","Other living organisms like plants and animals","Living organisms in a habitat need other living organisms like plants and animals to obtain their needs.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-relationship-plants-54a82122","Science G6: Representing biological communities and identifying types - Set 2 T1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-2 Describing the components of the ecosystem and explaining the impact of the availability of different resources in ecosystems on the survival and sustainability of living organisms, and proposing solutions to problems that affect the stability of the ecosystem.","2. Determining the causes of changes in habitats and their impact on plants and animals that lives there.","What is the relationship between plants and animals in a habitat?","Plants provide food for animals","Animals provide shelter for plants","Plants and animals compete for resources","Plants and animals have no relationship","Plants provide food for animals","Plants provide food for animals, such as fruits and leaves.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-why-do-living-5591e18b","Science G6: Representing biological communities and identifying types - Set 2 T1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-1 Representing biological communities and identifying types of population that lives in it, describ-ing the interrelationships among them, and their inter-action with the abiotic components, and the impact of biological communities changes on their survival and sustainability.","2.Identifying the interrelationships among living organisms, and their interaction with the abiotic components of their habitats to obtain their needs.","Why do living organisms in a habitat interact with abiotic components?","To obtain their needs","To compete for resources","To avoid all interactions with the environment","To harm each other","To obtain their needs","Organisms compete for limited resources such as food, water, space, and mates.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-happens-abiotic-components-bd14ee93","Science G6: Representing biological communities and identifying types - Set 2 T1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-1 Representing biological communities and identifying types of population that lives in it, describ-ing the interrelationships among them, and their inter-action with the abiotic components, and the impact of biological communities changes on their survival and sustainability.","2.Identifying the interrelationships among living organisms, and their interaction with the abiotic components of their habitats to obtain their needs.","What happens when the abiotic components of a habitat change?","The living organisms adapt to the changes","The living organisms die off","The living organisms move to a new habitat","The living organisms do not change","The living organisms die off","When the abiotic components of a habitat change, the living organisms may die off if they cannot adapt.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-why-is-it-9eaf85a1","Science G6: Representing biological communities and identifying types - Set 2 T1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-1 Representing biological communities and identifying types of population that lives in it, describ-ing the interrelationships among them, and their inter-action with the abiotic components, and the impact of biological communities changes on their survival and sustainability.","2.Identifying the interrelationships among living organisms, and their interaction with the abiotic components of their habitats to obtain their needs.","Why is it important for living organisms to interact with abiotic components?","To obtain their needs and survive","To compete for resources and harm each other","To adapt to changes and sustain themselves","To have no relationship","To obtain their needs and survive","It is important for living organisms to interact with abiotic components to obtain their needs and survive.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-do-living-organisms-96460047","Science G6: Representing biological communities and identifying types - Set 2 T1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-1 Representing biological communities and identifying types of population that lives in it, describ-ing the interrelationships among them, and their inter-action with the abiotic components, and the impact of biological communities changes on their survival and sustainability.","2.Identifying the interrelationships among living organisms, and their interaction with the abiotic components of their habitats to obtain their needs.","What do living organisms in a habitat need to sustain themselves?","Abiotic components like water and sunlight","Biotic components like plants and animals","Both abiotic and biotic components","Neither abiotic nor biotic components","Both abiotic and biotic components","An ecosystem contains both biotic components (living organisms) and abiotic components (non-living factors).","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-happens-to-a-ee03b089","Science G6: Representing biological communities and identifying types - Set 3","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-1 Representing biological communities and identifying types of population that lives in it, describ-ing the interrelationships among them, and their inter-action with the abiotic components, and the impact of biological communities changes on their survival and sustainability.","3.Describing the effect of different changes in biological communities on the surviv-al and sustainability of different species.","What happens to a meadow when many of the plants are removed?","The number of insects increases.","The number of animals increases.","The number of plants and animals decreases.","The meadow becomes a forest.","The number of plants and animals decreases.","When many of the plants are removed, it can lead to a decrease in the number of plants and animals that depend on them for food and shelter.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-happens-to-a-6cca14d9","Science G6: Representing biological communities and identifying types - Set 3","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-1 Representing biological communities and identifying types of population that lives in it, describ-ing the interrelationships among them, and their inter-action with the abiotic components, and the impact of biological communities changes on their survival and sustainability.","3.Describing the effect of different changes in biological communities on the surviv-al and sustainability of different species.","What happens to a biological community when a natural disaster occurs?","The community becomes healthier.","The community stays the same.","The community becomes less diverse.","The community disappears.","The community disappears.","Natural disasters can cause harm or death to plants and animals, leading to a decrease in the diversity of the community or even its disappearance.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-effect-of-119557c7","Science G6: Representing biological communities and identifying types - Set 3","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-1 Representing biological communities and identifying types of population that lives in it, describ-ing the interrelationships among them, and their inter-action with the abiotic components, and the impact of biological communities changes on their survival and sustainability.","3.Describing the effect of different changes in biological communities on the surviv-al and sustainability of different species.","What is the effect of climate change on a biological community?","The community becomes healthier.","The community stays the same.","The community becomes less diverse.","The community disappears.","The community disappears.","Climate change can cause changes in temperature and precipitation patterns, leading to a decrease in the diversity of the community or even its disappearance.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-happens-to-a-2f19aa84","Science G6: Representing biological communities and identifying types - Set 3","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-1 Representing biological communities and identifying types of population that lives in it, describ-ing the interrelationships among them, and their inter-action with the abiotic components, and the impact of biological communities changes on their survival and sustainability.","3.Describing the effect of different changes in biological communities on the surviv-al and sustainability of different species.","What happens to a biological community when a new species of invasive plant is introduced?","The community becomes healthier.","The community stays the same.","The community becomes less diverse.","The community disappears.","The community becomes less diverse.","Invasive plants can outcompete native plants for resources, leading to a decrease in the diversity of the community.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-effect-of-5e215e78","Science G6: Representing biological communities and identifying types - Set 3","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-4 Describing the effect of environmental changes on plants and animals that live in specific environments. Inferring how behavioral and structural adaptations can help plants and animals survive in their habitats","3. Describing how structural and behavioral adaptations can help plants and animals live and survive in specific habitats.","What is the effect of habitat destruction on a biological community?","The community becomes healthier.","The community stays the same.","The community becomes less diverse.","The community disappears.","The community disappears.","Habitat destruction can cause harm or death to plants and animals, leading to a decrease in the diversity of the community or even its disappearance.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-happens-to-a-3e3237f5","Science G6: Representing biological communities and identifying types - Set 3","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-1 Representing biological communities and identifying types of population that lives in it, describ-ing the interrelationships among them, and their inter-action with the abiotic components, and the impact of biological communities changes on their survival and sustainability.","3.Describing the effect of different changes in biological communities on the surviv-al and sustainability of different species.","What happens to a coral reef when the water temperature rises?","The coral grows faster.","The coral stays the same.","The coral bleaches and dies.","The coral becomes more colorful.","The coral bleaches and dies.","When the water temperature rises, the coral can become stressed and bleach, leading to its death.","/image-source/sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-happens-to-a-3e3237f5.png","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-effect-of-0e70cfb7","Science G6: Representing biological communities and identifying types - Set 3","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-4 Describing the effect of environmental changes on plants and animals that live in specific environments. Inferring how behavioral and structural adaptations can help plants and animals survive in their habitats","3. Describing how structural and behavioral adaptations can help plants and animals live and survive in specific habitats.","What is the effect of pollution on a biological community?","The community becomes healthier.","The community stays the same.","The community becomes less diverse.","The community disappears.","The community disappears.","Pollution can harm or kill plants and animals, leading to a decrease in the diversity of the community.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-happens-to-a-aef3e4ab","Science G6: Representing biological communities and identifying types - Set 3","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-1 Representing biological communities and identifying types of population that lives in it, describ-ing the interrelationships among them, and their inter-action with the abiotic components, and the impact of biological communities changes on their survival and sustainability.","3.Describing the effect of different changes in biological communities on the surviv-al and sustainability of different species.","What happens to a forest ecosystem when many trees die due to disease?","The number of birds and squirrels increases.","The number of plants and animals decreases.","The forest becomes a grassland.","The trees grow back stronger than before.","The number of plants and animals decreases.","When many trees die, it can lead to a decrease in the number of plants and animals that depend on them for food and shelter.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-effect-of-733a9e15","Science G6: Representing biological communities and identifying types - Set 3","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-1 Representing biological communities and identifying types of population that lives in it, describ-ing the interrelationships among them, and their inter-action with the abiotic components, and the impact of biological communities changes on their survival and sustainability.","3.Describing the effect of different changes in biological communities on the surviv-al and sustainability of different species.","What is the effect of introducing a new species of fish to a lake on the native fish population?","The native fish population increases.","The native fish population stays the same.","The native fish population decreases.","The native fish population disappears.","The native fish population decreases.","Introducing a new species of fish can lead to competition for food and habitat, which can cause the native fish population to decrease.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-effect-of-382a8c77","Science G6: Representing biological communities and identifying types - Set 3","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-1 Representing biological communities and identifying types of population that lives in it, describ-ing the interrelationships among them, and their inter-action with the abiotic components, and the impact of biological communities changes on their survival and sustainability.","3.Describing the effect of different changes in biological communities on the surviv-al and sustainability of different species.","What is the effect of overhunting on a biological community?","The community becomes healthier.","The community stays the same.","The community becomes less diverse.","The community disappears.","The community disappears.","Overhunting can lead to the extinction of certain species, causing the community to become less diverse or even disappear.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-main-source-467680c0","Science G6: Representing the relationships between living organisms - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-3 Representing the relationships between living organisms in which matter is circulated in the ecosystem, and identifying the relationship between plants and energy obtained from the sun to produce food.","2. Classifying living organisms into (autotrophic - heterotrophic).","What is the main source of energy for autotrophic organisms?","Water and sunlight","Food and shelter","Air and exercise","Other organisms to eat","Water and sunlight","Autotrophic organisms, like plants, use sunlight as their main source of energy to make their own food.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-living-organisms-break-436aa9a3","Science G6: Representing the relationships between living organisms - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-3 Representing the relationships between living organisms in which matter is circulated in the ecosystem, and identifying the relationship between plants and energy obtained from the sun to produce food.","2. Classifying living organisms into (autotrophic - heterotrophic).","Which living organisms break down dead organisms?","Plants that make their own food","Animals that eat other animals","Fungi that break down dead organisms","Bacteria that decompose organic matter","Fungi that break down dead organisms","Fungi and bacteria are examples of heterotrophic organisms that break down dead organisms to obtain nutrients.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-type-of-living-8a8c21ef","Science G6: Representing the relationships between living organisms - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-3 Representing the relationships between living organisms in which matter is circulated in the ecosystem, and identifying the relationship between plants and energy obtained from the sun to produce food.","2. Classifying living organisms into (autotrophic - heterotrophic).","What type of living organisms make their own food?","Plants and animals that eat other animals","Plants that make their own food","Animals that eat plants","Fungi that break down dead organisms","Plants that make their own food","Autotrophic organisms, like plants, make their own food using sunlight, water, and carbon dioxide.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-living-organisms-cannot-5c6ec939","Science G6: Representing the relationships between living organisms - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-3 Representing the relationships between living organisms in which matter is circulated in the ecosystem, and identifying the relationship between plants and energy obtained from the sun to produce food.","2. Classifying living organisms into (autotrophic - heterotrophic).","Which living organisms cannot make their own food?","Plants that make their own food","Animals that eat other animals","Fungi that break down dead organisms","Bacteria that decompose organic matter","Plants that make their own food","Heterotrophic organisms, like animals, cannot make their own food and need to eat other organisms to survive.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-do-plants-use-60712741","Science G6: Representing the relationships between living organisms - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-3 Representing the relationships between living organisms in which matter is circulated in the ecosystem, and identifying the relationship between plants and energy obtained from the sun to produce food.","3. Explaining the process of photosynthesis and its role in determining the relationship between plants and the energy obtained from the sun to produce food.","What do plants use to make their own food?","Water and air","Sunlight, water, and carbon dioxide","Soil and minerals","Fertilizers and pesticides","Sunlight, water, and carbon dioxide","Photosynthesis requires sunlight, water, and carbon dioxide.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-living-organisms-are-301e040b","Science G6: Representing the relationships between living organisms - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-3 Representing the relationships between living organisms in which matter is circulated in the ecosystem, and identifying the relationship between plants and energy obtained from the sun to produce food.","2. Classifying living organisms into (autotrophic - heterotrophic).","Which living organisms are autotrophic?","Fungi that break down dead organisms","Bacteria that decompose organic matter","Plants that make their own food","Animals that eat other animals","Plants that make their own food","Autotrophic organisms, like plants, make their own food using sunlight, water, and carbon dioxide.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-do-heterotrophic-organisms-7e071847","Science G6: Representing the relationships between living organisms - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-3 Representing the relationships between living organisms in which matter is circulated in the ecosystem, and identifying the relationship between plants and energy obtained from the sun to produce food.","2. Classifying living organisms into (autotrophic - heterotrophic).","What do heterotrophic organisms need to survive?","Water and sunlight","Food and shelter","Air and exercise","Other organisms to eat","Other organisms to eat","Heterotrophic organisms, like animals, need to eat other organisms to survive because they cannot make their own food.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-type-of-living-73c43318","Science G6: Representing the relationships between living organisms - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-3 Representing the relationships between living organisms in which matter is circulated in the ecosystem, and identifying the relationship between plants and energy obtained from the sun to produce food.","2. Classifying living organisms into (autotrophic - heterotrophic).","Which type of living organisms cannot make their own food and need to eat other organisms?","Autotrophic organisms","Heterotrophic organisms","Fungi that break down dead organisms","Bacteria that decompose organic matter","Heterotrophic organisms","Heterotrophic organisms, like animals, cannot make their own food and need to eat other organisms to survive.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-do-heterotrophic-organisms-22a86a57","Science G6: Representing the relationships between living organisms - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-3 Representing the relationships between living organisms in which matter is circulated in the ecosystem, and identifying the relationship between plants and energy obtained from the sun to produce food.","2. Classifying living organisms into (autotrophic - heterotrophic).","What do heterotrophic organisms use to obtain energy?","Sunlight and water","Food and shelter","Air and exercise","Other organisms to eat","Other organisms to eat","Heterotrophic organisms, like animals, use other organisms as their source of energy because they cannot make their own food.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-type-of-living-6c0b815a","Science G6: Representing the relationships between living organisms - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-3 Representing the relationships between living organisms in which matter is circulated in the ecosystem, and identifying the relationship between plants and energy obtained from the sun to produce food.","2. Classifying living organisms into (autotrophic - heterotrophic).","Which type of living organisms make their own food using sunlight?","Fungi that break down dead organisms","Bacteria that decompose organic matter","Plants that make their own food","Animals that eat other animals","Plants that make their own food","Autotrophic organisms, like plants, make their own food using sunlight, water, and carbon dioxide.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-result-of-76c26c87","Science G6: Representing the relationships between living organisms - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-3 Representing the relationships between living organisms in which matter is circulated in the ecosystem, and identifying the relationship between plants and energy obtained from the sun to produce food.","3. Explaining the process of photosynthesis and its role in determining the relationship between plants and the energy obtained from the sun to produce food.","What is the result of photosynthesis in terms of carbon dioxide?","The carbon dioxide is released into the air.","The carbon dioxide is stored in the form of glucose.","The carbon dioxide is used to power the movement of plants.","The carbon dioxide is not affected during photosynthesis.","The carbon dioxide is released into the air.","Photosynthesis releases carbon dioxide into the air as a byproduct, which is essential for the survival of most living organisms.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-process-by-e76a4b13","Science G6: Representing the relationships between living organisms - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-3 Representing the relationships between living organisms in which matter is circulated in the ecosystem, and identifying the relationship between plants and energy obtained from the sun to produce food.","3. Explaining the process of photosynthesis and its role in determining the relationship between plants and the energy obtained from the sun to produce food.","What is the process by which plants make their own food from sunlight?","Plants absorb water from the soil and release oxygen into the air.","Plants make their own food from sunlight, water, and air.","Plants absorb nutrients from the soil and release carbon dioxide into the air.","Plants make their own food from sunlight, but they don't need water or air.","Plants make their own food from sunlight, water, and air.","Photosynthesis is the process by which plants make their own food from sunlight, water, and air. This process is essential for plant growth and development.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-role-of-67fa6b29","Science G6: Representing the relationships between living organisms - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-3 Representing the relationships between living organisms in which matter is circulated in the ecosystem, and identifying the relationship between plants and energy obtained from the sun to produce food.","3. Explaining the process of photosynthesis and its role in determining the relationship between plants and the energy obtained from the sun to produce food.","What is the role of sunlight in the process of photosynthesis?","Sunlight provides the energy needed for plants to grow and develop.","Sunlight helps plants to absorb water from the soil.","Sunlight is not necessary for photosynthesis to occur.","Sunlight is used to make the food for plants.","Sunlight is used to make the food for plants.","Sunlight provides the energy needed for plants to make their own food through photosynthesis.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-byproduct-of-6ff36c2d","Science G6: Representing the relationships between living organisms - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-3 Representing the relationships between living organisms in which matter is circulated in the ecosystem, and identifying the relationship between plants and energy obtained from the sun to produce food.","3. Explaining the process of photosynthesis and its role in determining the relationship between plants and the energy obtained from the sun to produce food.","What is the byproduct of photosynthesis that is released into the air?","Carbon dioxide","Oxygen","Nitrogen","Water vapor","Oxygen","Photosynthesis releases oxygen into the air as a byproduct, which is essential for the survival of most living organisms.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-main-source-96d39af7","Science G6: Representing the relationships between living organisms - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-3 Representing the relationships between living organisms in which matter is circulated in the ecosystem, and identifying the relationship between plants and energy obtained from the sun to produce food.","3. Explaining the process of photosynthesis and its role in determining the relationship between plants and the energy obtained from the sun to produce food.","What is the main source of energy for photosynthesis?","Water from the soil","Carbon dioxide from the air","Sunlight from the sun","Nutrients from the soil","Sunlight from the sun","Sunlight is the main source of energy for photosynthesis, which is used to convert carbon dioxide and water into glucose and oxygen.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-role-of-18d8c097","Science G6: Representing the relationships between living organisms - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-3 Representing the relationships between living organisms in which matter is circulated in the ecosystem, and identifying the relationship between plants and energy obtained from the sun to produce food.","3. Explaining the process of photosynthesis and its role in determining the relationship between plants and the energy obtained from the sun to produce food.","What is the role of chlorophyll in photosynthesis?","Chlorophyll helps plants to absorb water from the soil.","Chlorophyll provides the energy needed for photosynthesis to occur.","Chlorophyll helps plants to make their own food from sunlight.","Chlorophyll is not necessary for photosynthesis to occur.","Chlorophyll helps plants to make their own food from sunlight.","Chlorophyll is a green pigment that helps plants to make their own food from sunlight through photosynthesis.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-result-of-6e55f6af","Science G6: Representing the relationships between living organisms - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-3 Representing the relationships between living organisms in which matter is circulated in the ecosystem, and identifying the relationship between plants and energy obtained from the sun to produce food.","3. Explaining the process of photosynthesis and its role in determining the relationship between plants and the energy obtained from the sun to produce food.","What is the result of photosynthesis in terms of energy?","The energy is released into the air as heat.","The energy is stored in the form of glucose.","The energy is used to power the movement of plants.","The energy is not stored or released during photosynthesis.","The energy is stored in the form of glucose.","Photosynthesis stores energy in the form of glucose, which is used by plants to grow and develop.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-relationship-photosynthesis-eb82b7e4","Science G6: Representing the relationships between living organisms - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-3 Representing the relationships between living organisms in which matter is circulated in the ecosystem, and identifying the relationship between plants and energy obtained from the sun to produce food.","3. Explaining the process of photosynthesis and its role in determining the relationship between plants and the energy obtained from the sun to produce food.","What is the relationship between photosynthesis and the energy obtained from the sun?","Photosynthesis uses energy from the sun to make food for plants.","Photosynthesis releases energy from the sun into the air.","Photosynthesis does not use energy from the sun.","Photosynthesis is not related to the energy obtained from the sun.","Photosynthesis uses energy from the sun to make food for plants.","Photosynthesis uses energy from the sun to make food for plants, which is essential for plant growth and development.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-byproduct-of-5897263c","Science G6: Representing the relationships between living organisms - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-3 Representing the relationships between living organisms in which matter is circulated in the ecosystem, and identifying the relationship between plants and energy obtained from the sun to produce food.","3. Explaining the process of photosynthesis and its role in determining the relationship between plants and the energy obtained from the sun to produce food.","What is the byproduct of photosynthesis that is used by plants to grow and develop?","Carbon dioxide","Oxygen","Glucose","Water vapor","Glucose","Photosynthesis produces glucose, which is used by plants to grow and develop.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-role-of-916d53a9","Science G6: Representing the relationships between living organisms - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-3 Representing the relationships between living organisms in which matter is circulated in the ecosystem, and identifying the relationship between plants and energy obtained from the sun to produce food.","3. Explaining the process of photosynthesis and its role in determining the relationship between plants and the energy obtained from the sun to produce food.","What is the role of water in the process of photosynthesis?","Water is not necessary for photosynthesis to occur.","Water provides the energy needed for photosynthesis.","Water is used to make the food for plants.","Water is absorbed by plants and released into the air as water vapor.","Water is used to make the food for plants.","Water is used by plants to make their own food through photosynthesis.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-of-organisms-is-bcab1b9d","Science G6: Representing the relationships between living organisms - Set 2 T1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-3 Representing the relationships between living organisms in which matter is circulated in the ecosystem, and identifying the relationship between plants and energy obtained from the sun to produce food.","2. Classifying living organisms into (autotrophic - heterotrophic).","Which of the following organisms is an autotroph?","A human being that eats food from a store","A dog that eats dog food","A tree that makes its own food from sunlight","A car that runs on gasoline","A tree that makes its own food from sunlight","A tree is an autotroph because it makes its own food from sunlight.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-type-of-organism-c7e6a708","Science G6: Representing the relationships between living organisms - Set 2 T1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-3 Representing the relationships between living organisms in which matter is circulated in the ecosystem, and identifying the relationship between plants and energy obtained from the sun to produce food.","2. Classifying living organisms into (autotrophic - heterotrophic).","What type of organism makes its own food from sunlight?","A plant that gets its food from other plants","An animal that eats other animals for food","An organism that makes its own food from sunlight","A fungus that gets its food from dead plants","An organism that makes its own food from sunlight","This organism is an example of an autotroph, which makes its own food from sunlight.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-main-difference-7db69ba3","Science G6: Representing the relationships between living organisms - Set 2 T1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-3 Representing the relationships between living organisms in which matter is circulated in the ecosystem, and identifying the relationship between plants and energy obtained from the sun to produce food.","2. Classifying living organisms into (autotrophic - heterotrophic).","What is the main difference between autotrophic and heterotrophic organisms?","Autotrophs are bigger than heterotrophs","Heterotrophs are smarter than autotrophs","Autotrophs make their own food, while heterotrophs eat other organisms for food","Autotrophs live on land, while heterotrophs live in water","Autotrophs make their own food, while heterotrophs eat other organisms for food","Autotrophs make their own food from sunlight, while heterotrophs eat other organisms for food.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-of-is-an-875d8d86","Science G6: Representing the relationships between living organisms - Set 2 T1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-3 Representing the relationships between living organisms in which matter is circulated in the ecosystem, and identifying the relationship between plants and energy obtained from the sun to produce food.","2. Classifying living organisms into (autotrophic - heterotrophic).","Which of the following is an example of a heterotrophic organism?","A grasshopper that eats plants for food","A tree that makes its own food from sunlight","A car that runs on gasoline","A rock that does not move or eat","A grasshopper that eats plants for food","A grasshopper is a heterotroph because it eats other organisms for food.","","",""
