"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-effect-of-6c3362d4","Science G6: Understanding Newton's three laws of motion - Set 3","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-2-2 Understanding Newton›s three laws of motion and using them to explain body motion","3. Defining acceleration and its unit, calculating acceleration by knowing the change in velocity and time, and showing the effect of changing the direction of movement on acceleration.","What is the effect of changing the direction of movement on an object's acceleration?","Acceleration remains the same.","Acceleration increases.","Acceleration decreases.","Acceleration becomes zero.","Acceleration decreases.","Changing the direction of movement changes an object's acceleration.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-unit-of-38b6781e","Science G6: Understanding Newton's three laws of motion - Set 3","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-2-2 Understanding Newton›s three laws of motion and using them to explain body motion","3. Defining acceleration and its unit, calculating acceleration by knowing the change in velocity and time, and showing the effect of changing the direction of movement on acceleration.","What is the unit of acceleration?","Meter per second","Meter per second squared","Second per meter","Second squared per meter","Meter per second squared","The unit of acceleration is meter per second squared.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-effect-of-c99764ca","Science G6: Understanding Newton's three laws of motion - Set 3","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-2-2 Understanding Newton›s three laws of motion and using them to explain body motion","3. Defining acceleration and its unit, calculating acceleration by knowing the change in velocity and time, and showing the effect of changing the direction of movement on acceleration.","What is the effect of increasing the mass of an object on its acceleration?","Acceleration decreases.","Acceleration remains the same.","Acceleration increases.","Acceleration becomes zero.","Acceleration decreases.","Increasing the mass of an object decreases its acceleration.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-effect-of-6520b401","Science G6: Understanding Newton's three laws of motion - Set 3","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-2-3 Explaining the factors affecting types of forces, such as gravitational force, friction, and magnetism.","2. Explaining how frictional force is generated and the factors affecting its magnitude, and explaining the effect of air resistance on the motion of objects.","What is the effect of increasing the force applied on an object on its acceleration?","Acceleration decreases.","Acceleration remains the same.","Acceleration increases.","Acceleration becomes zero.","Acceleration increases.","Increasing the force applied on an object increases its acceleration.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-happens-to-acceleration-93ecaa31","Science G6: Understanding Newton's three laws of motion - Set 3","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-2-2 Understanding Newton›s three laws of motion and using them to explain body motion","3. Defining acceleration and its unit, calculating acceleration by knowing the change in velocity and time, and showing the effect of changing the direction of movement on acceleration.","What happens to acceleration when the direction of movement changes?","Acceleration remains the same.","Acceleration increases.","Acceleration decreases.","Acceleration becomes zero.","Acceleration decreases.","Acceleration changes when the direction of movement changes.","/image-source/sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-happens-to-acceleration-93ecaa31.png","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-relationship-force-9293ab66","Science G6: Understanding Newton's three laws of motion - Set 3","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-2-2 Understanding Newton›s three laws of motion and using them to explain body motion","1. Explaining the relationship between gravitational force and the weights of objects, and identifying the factors that affect it.","What is the relationship between force, mass, and acceleration?","Force = Mass x Acceleration","Force = Mass / Acceleration","Force = Acceleration / Mass","Force = Mass + Acceleration","Force = Mass x Acceleration","Force is equal to mass times acceleration.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-happens-to-motion-06c0b9fa","Science G6: Understanding Newton's three laws of motion - Set 4","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-2-3 Explaining the factors affecting types of forces, such as gravitational force, friction, and magnetism.","2. Explaining how frictional force is generated and the factors affecting its magnitude, and explaining the effect of air resistance on the motion of objects.","What happens to the motion of an object when a force is applied to it?","The object's motion remains the same","The object's motion slows down","The object's motion speeds up","The object's motion changes direction","The object's motion speeds up","When a force is applied to an object, its motion changes, either speeding up, slowing down, or changing direction, depending on the direction and magnitude of the force.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-newton-s-77899fb4","Science G6: Understanding Newton's three laws of motion - Set 4","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-2-2 Understanding Newton›s three laws of motion and using them to explain body motion","1. Explaining the relationship between gravitational force and the weights of objects, and identifying the factors that affect it.","What is Newton's First Law of Motion also known as?","The Law of Action and Reaction","The Law of Inertia","The Law of Gravity","The Law of Friction","The Law of Inertia","Newton's First Law of Motion is also known as the Law of Inertia, which states that an object at rest will remain at rest, and an object in motion will continue to move with a constant velocity, unless acted upon by an external force.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-term-force-02d049b6","Science G6: Understanding Newton's three laws of motion - Set 4","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-2-1 Describing the concept of force and distinguishing between different types","3. Distinguishing between types of forces based on their causes (gravity, friction, and magnetism).","What is the term for the force that attracts objects towards each other?","Gravity","Friction","Normal force","Tension","Gravity","Gravity is the force that attracts objects towards each other, and it is one of the four fundamental forces of nature.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-relationship-force-cc907e4e","Science G6: Understanding Newton's three laws of motion - Set 4","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-2-2 Understanding Newton›s three laws of motion and using them to explain body motion","1. Explaining the relationship between gravitational force and the weights of objects, and identifying the factors that affect it.","What is the relationship between the force applied to an object and its acceleration?","The force is directly proportional to the acceleration","The force is inversely proportional to the acceleration","The force is unrelated to the acceleration","The force is equal to the acceleration","The force is directly proportional to the acceleration","According to Newton's Second Law of Motion, the force applied to an object is directly proportional to its acceleration, meaning that the greater the force, the greater the acceleration will be.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-an-example-6f606934","Science G6: Understanding Newton's three laws of motion - Set 4","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-2-2 Understanding Newton›s three laws of motion and using them to explain body motion","4. Explaining Newton's three laws of motion and their real life applications.","What is an example of Newton's First Law of Motion in everyday life?","A car accelerating on the road","A ball rolling on the ground","A person sitting on a chair","A book lying on a table","A book lying on a table","When a book lies on a table, it remains at rest due to its inertia, which is an example of Newton's First Law of Motion, where the book's motion remains unchanged unless acted upon by an external force.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-relationship-normal-465937fa","Science G6: Understanding Newton's three laws of motion - Set 4","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-2-2 Understanding Newton›s three laws of motion and using them to explain body motion","1. Explaining the relationship between gravitational force and the weights of objects, and identifying the factors that affect it.","What is the relationship between the normal force and the weight of an object?","The normal force is greater than the weight","The normal force is equal to the weight","The normal force is less than the weight","The normal force is opposite to the weight","The normal force is equal to the weight","The normal force is equal to the weight of an object when it is in equilibrium, meaning that the force exerted by the surface on the object is equal to the force exerted by the object on the surface.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-term-force-2349051d","Science G6: Understanding Newton's three laws of motion - Set 4","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-2-2 Understanding Newton›s three laws of motion and using them to explain body motion","4. Explaining Newton's three laws of motion and their real life applications.","What is the term for the force that opposes motion between two surfaces that are in contact?","Friction","Gravity","Normal force","Tension","Friction","Friction is the force that opposes motion between two surfaces that are in contact, and it can be either static or kinetic, depending on whether the surfaces are moving or stationary.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-an-example-ea8faefe","Science G6: Understanding Newton's three laws of motion - Set 4","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-2-2 Understanding Newton›s three laws of motion and using them to explain body motion","4. Explaining Newton's three laws of motion and their real life applications.","What is an example of Newton's Third Law of Motion in everyday life?","A car accelerating on the road","A ball bouncing on the ground","A person pushing against a wall","A rocket launching into space","A rocket launching into space","When a rocket launches into space, it produces a large amount of thrust, which is an example of Newton's Third Law of Motion, where the action of the rocket's engines produces an equal and opposite reaction force that propels the rocket forward.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-purpose-of-35a56a7f","Science G6: Understanding Newton's three laws of motion - Set 4","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-2-2 Understanding Newton›s three laws of motion and using them to explain body motion","1. Explaining the relationship between gravitational force and the weights of objects, and identifying the factors that affect it.","What is the purpose of Newton's Third Law of Motion?","To describe the motion of objects on Earth","To explain the behavior of gases and liquids","To describe the forces that act between objects","To predict the motion of objects in space","To describe the forces that act between objects","Newton's Third Law of Motion states that for every action, there is an equal and opposite reaction, which helps to explain the forces that act between objects and how they interact with each other.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-relationship-force-dd3e8ed0","Science G6: Understanding Newton's three laws of motion - Set 4","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-2-2 Understanding Newton›s three laws of motion and using them to explain body motion","1. Explaining the relationship between gravitational force and the weights of objects, and identifying the factors that affect it.","What is the relationship between force and acceleration?","Force and acceleration are directly proportional","Force and acceleration are inversely proportional","Force and acceleration are unrelated","Force and acceleration are equal","Force and acceleration are directly proportional","According to Newton's Second Law of Motion, force and acceleration are directly proportional, meaning that the greater the force applied to an object, the greater its acceleration will be.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-disadvantage-of-c63f6d4e","Science G6: Understanding the concept of electric charge - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-4-2 Understanding the concepts of reflection and refraction of light, transmission of sound, and explaining their role in interaction and communication in the surrounding environment.","1. Explaining the concept of electric charge and explaining the attraction and repulsion of charged bodies theoretically and graphically.","What is the disadvantage of using a series circuit?","It is more expensive to build","It is more difficult to repair","If one device breaks, the whole circuit breaks","It is more energy-efficient","If one device breaks, the whole circuit breaks","The disadvantage of using a series circuit is that if one device breaks, the whole circuit breaks.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-a-magnet-0ff0d6ec","Science G6: Understanding the concept of electric charge - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-1-3-4 Describing the effect of environmental changes on plants and animals that live in specific environments. Inferring how behavioral and structural adaptations can help plants and animals survive in their habitats","3. Describing how structural and behavioral adaptations can help plants and animals live and survive in specific habitats.","What is a magnet?","A small rock that sticks to a fridge","A piece of metal that can attract other metals","A type of plant that grows in the ground","A type of animal that lives in the ocean","A piece of metal that can attract other metals","Magnets are objects that can attract other metals.","/image-source/sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-a-magnet-0ff0d6ec.png","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-advantage-of-20fc8479","Science G6: Understanding the concept of electric charge - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-4-2 Understanding the concepts of reflection and refraction of light, transmission of sound, and explaining their role in interaction and communication in the surrounding environment.","1. Explaining the concept of electric charge and explaining the attraction and repulsion of charged bodies theoretically and graphically.","What is the advantage of using a parallel circuit?","It is more expensive to build","It is more difficult to repair","It allows us to use multiple devices at the same time","It is more energy-efficient","It allows us to use multiple devices at the same time","The advantage of using a parallel circuit is that it allows us to use multiple devices at the same time.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-why-do-we-d9f0cd9f","Science G6: Understanding the concept of electric charge - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-4-2 Understanding the concepts of reflection and refraction of light, transmission of sound, and explaining their role in interaction and communication in the surrounding environment.","1. Explaining the concept of electric charge and explaining the attraction and repulsion of charged bodies theoretically and graphically.","Why do we use parallel circuits in our homes?","Because they are more expensive to build","Because they are more difficult to repair","Because they allow us to use multiple devices at the same time","Because they are more energy-efficient","Because they allow us to use multiple devices at the same time","We use parallel circuits in our homes because they allow us to use multiple devices at the same time.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-happens-a-device-0e0816ff","Science G6: Understanding the concept of electric charge - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-5-1Understanding the concept of electric charge, explaining the attraction and repulsion of charged bodies, and comparing electrical circuits connected in series and in parallel.","2. Explaining how electric current flows in electrical circuits.","What happens when a device is connected in a parallel circuit?","The current flows through only one device","The voltage increases through all the devices","The resistance decreases through all the devices","The current flows through all the devices","The current flows through all the devices","When a device is connected in a parallel circuit, the current flows through all the devices.","/image-source/sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-happens-a-device-0e0816ff.png","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-how-are-magnets-d31621d5","Science G6: Understanding the concept of electric charge - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-4-2 Understanding the concepts of reflection and refraction of light, transmission of sound, and explaining their role in interaction and communication in the surrounding environment.","1. Explaining the concept of electric charge and explaining the attraction and repulsion of charged bodies theoretically and graphically.","How are magnets formed?","By heating metal in a fire","By cooling metal in a cold place","By rubbing two sticks together","By heating metal in a special machine","By heating metal in a special machine","Magnets are formed when certain metals are heated or cooled in a special way.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-happens-two-magnets-4873034a","Science G6: Understanding the concept of electric charge - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-4-2 Understanding the concepts of reflection and refraction of light, transmission of sound, and explaining their role in interaction and communication in the surrounding environment.","1. Explaining the concept of electric charge and explaining the attraction and repulsion of charged bodies theoretically and graphically.","What happens when two magnets are brought close together?","They stick together","They fall apart","They push each other away","They pull each other towards each other","They pull each other towards each other","When two magnets are brought close together, they either attract or repel each other.","/image-source/sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-happens-two-magnets-4873034a.png","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-difference-a-f907b7d6","Science G6: Understanding the concept of electric charge - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-5-1Understanding the concept of electric charge, explaining the attraction and repulsion of charged bodies, and comparing electrical circuits connected in series and in parallel.","2. Explaining how electric current flows in electrical circuits.","What is the difference between a series circuit and a parallel circuit?","A series circuit has more wires than a parallel circuit","A series circuit has fewer wires than a parallel circuit","A series circuit has all the devices connected one after the other","A parallel circuit has all the devices connected at the same time","A series circuit has all the devices connected one after the other","In a series circuit, devices are connected one after the other, while in a parallel circuit, devices are connected at the same time.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-happens-a-device-f452fa94","Science G6: Understanding the concept of electric charge - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-5-1Understanding the concept of electric charge, explaining the attraction and repulsion of charged bodies, and comparing electrical circuits connected in series and in parallel.","2. Explaining how electric current flows in electrical circuits.","What happens when a device is connected in a series circuit?","The current flows through all the devices","The current flows through only one device","The voltage increases through all the devices","The resistance decreases through all the devices","The current flows through all the devices","When a device is connected in a series circuit, the current flows through all the devices.","/image-source/sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-happens-a-device-f452fa94.png","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-are-two-parts-b7bc3f6e","Science G6: Understanding the concept of electric charge - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-4-2 Understanding the concepts of reflection and refraction of light, transmission of sound, and explaining their role in interaction and communication in the surrounding environment.","1. Explaining the concept of electric charge and explaining the attraction and repulsion of charged bodies theoretically and graphically.","What are the two parts of a magnet called?","The top and bottom","The front and back","The north and south poles","The left and right sides","The north and south poles","Magnets have two poles, a north pole and a south pole.","/image-source/sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-are-two-parts-b7bc3f6e.png","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-path-electric-667077b7","Science G6: Understanding the concept of electric charge - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-5-1Understanding the concept of electric charge, explaining the attraction and repulsion of charged bodies, and comparing electrical circuits connected in series and in parallel.","2. Explaining how electric current flows in electrical circuits.","What is the path that electric current flows through in a circuit?","The path of least resistance","The path of greatest resistance","The path of the circuit wire","The path of the circuit breaker","The path of least resistance","Electric current flows through the path of least resistance in a circuit, which allows it to flow easily and efficiently.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-difference-a-72e38bde","Science G6: Understanding the concept of electric charge - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-5-1Understanding the concept of electric charge, explaining the attraction and repulsion of charged bodies, and comparing electrical circuits connected in series and in parallel.","2. Explaining how electric current flows in electrical circuits.","What is the difference between a series circuit and a parallel circuit?","A series circuit has more components than a parallel circuit","A series circuit has less resistance than a parallel circuit","A series circuit has components connected one after the other, while a parallel circuit has components connected side by side","A series circuit has components connected in a loop","A series circuit has components connected one after the other, while a parallel circuit has components connected side by side","A series circuit has components connected one after the other, while a parallel circuit has components connected side by side.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-happens-two-objects-a4994c7f","Science G6: Understanding the concept of electric charge - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-4-2 Understanding the concepts of reflection and refraction of light, transmission of sound, and explaining their role in interaction and communication in the surrounding environment.","1. Explaining the concept of electric charge and explaining the attraction and repulsion of charged bodies theoretically and graphically.","What happens when two objects with the same electric charge are brought near each other?","They attract each other","They repel each other","They do not affect each other","They become neutral","They repel each other","When two objects with the same electric charge are brought near each other, they repel each other due to the like charges pushing away from each other.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-happens-a-circuit-be1d0fed","Science G6: Understanding the concept of electric charge - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-5-1Understanding the concept of electric charge, explaining the attraction and repulsion of charged bodies, and comparing electrical circuits connected in series and in parallel.","2. Explaining how electric current flows in electrical circuits.","What happens when a circuit is connected in series?","The voltage increases","The current increases","The resistance increases","The circuit becomes more complex","The resistance increases","When a circuit is connected in series, the resistance increases because each component in the circuit adds to the total resistance.","/image-source/sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-happens-a-circuit-be1d0fed.png","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-why-is-it-8640b7c3","Science G6: Understanding the concept of electric charge - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-4-2 Understanding the concepts of reflection and refraction of light, transmission of sound, and explaining their role in interaction and communication in the surrounding environment.","1. Explaining the concept of electric charge and explaining the attraction and repulsion of charged bodies theoretically and graphically.","Why is it important to follow the correct path of electric current in a circuit?","To increase the voltage","To increase the current","To decrease the resistance","To ensure the circuit works safely and efficiently","To ensure the circuit works safely and efficiently","It is important to follow the correct path of electric current in a circuit to ensure the circuit works safely and efficiently.","/image-source/sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-why-is-it-8640b7c3.png","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-happens-a-circuit-cb8b9a7a","Science G6: Understanding the concept of electric charge - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-5-1Understanding the concept of electric charge, explaining the attraction and repulsion of charged bodies, and comparing electrical circuits connected in series and in parallel.","2. Explaining how electric current flows in electrical circuits.","What happens when a circuit is broken?","The current increases","The current decreases","The circuit becomes more complex","The circuit stops working","The circuit stops working","When a circuit is broken, the circuit stops working because there is no path for the electric current to flow.","/image-source/sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-happens-a-circuit-cb8b9a7a.png","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-unit-of-423417cc","Science G6: Understanding the concept of electric charge - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-4-2 Understanding the concepts of reflection and refraction of light, transmission of sound, and explaining their role in interaction and communication in the surrounding environment.","1. Explaining the concept of electric charge and explaining the attraction and repulsion of charged bodies theoretically and graphically.","What is the unit of measurement for electric current?","Volts","Amps","Ohms","Watts","Amps","The unit of measurement for electric current is Amps.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-why-do-objects-302d92f1","Science G6: Understanding the concept of electric charge - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-5-1Understanding the concept of electric charge, explaining the attraction and repulsion of charged bodies, and comparing electrical circuits connected in series and in parallel.","2. Explaining how electric current flows in electrical circuits.","Why do objects with opposite electric charges attract each other?","Because they have the same electric charge","Because they have opposite electric charges","Because they are connected in a series circuit","Because they are connected in a parallel circuit","Because they have opposite electric charges","Objects with opposite electric charges attract each other because the like charges push away from each other, and the unlike charges pull towards each other.","/image-source/sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-why-do-objects-302d92f1.png","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-happens-a-circuit-df9ed522","Science G6: Understanding the concept of electric charge - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-5-1Understanding the concept of electric charge, explaining the attraction and repulsion of charged bodies, and comparing electrical circuits connected in series and in parallel.","2. Explaining how electric current flows in electrical circuits.","What happens when a circuit is connected in parallel?","The voltage increases","The current increases","The resistance decreases","The circuit becomes more complex","The circuit becomes more complex","When a circuit is connected in parallel, the circuit becomes more complex because each component in the circuit has its own separate path for the electric current to flow.","/image-source/sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-happens-a-circuit-df9ed522.png","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-flow-of-4e8663d8","Science G6: Understanding the concept of electric charge - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-5-1Understanding the concept of electric charge, explaining the attraction and repulsion of charged bodies, and comparing electrical circuits connected in series and in parallel.","2. Explaining how electric current flows in electrical circuits.","What is the flow of electric charge through a circuit called?","Electric current","Electric voltage","Electric resistance","Electric power","Electric current","The flow of electric charge through a circuit is called electric current.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-why-do-objects-5711d7d5","Science G6: Understanding the concept of electric charge - Set 3","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-4-2 Understanding the concepts of reflection and refraction of light, transmission of sound, and explaining their role in interaction and communication in the surrounding environment.","1. Explaining the concept of electric charge and explaining the attraction and repulsion of charged bodies theoretically and graphically.","Why do objects with the same electric charge repel each other?","Because they have opposite types of charge","Because they have the same type of charge","Because they are made of the same material","Because they are made of different materials","Because they have the same type of charge","Like electric charges repel each other, while unlike charges attract.","/image-source/sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-why-do-objects-5711d7d5.png","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-happens-two-objects-f5166de7","Science G6: Understanding the concept of electric charge - Set 3","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-4-2 Understanding the concepts of reflection and refraction of light, transmission of sound, and explaining their role in interaction and communication in the surrounding environment.","1. Explaining the concept of electric charge and explaining the attraction and repulsion of charged bodies theoretically and graphically.","What happens when two objects with the same electric charge are brought near each other?","They attract each other","They repel each other","They stay the same","They disappear","They repel each other","When two objects with the same electric charge are brought near each other, they repel each other due to the same type of charge.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-main-difference-d59861c2","Science G6: Understanding the concept of electric charge - Set 3","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-5-1Understanding the concept of electric charge, explaining the attraction and repulsion of charged bodies, and comparing electrical circuits connected in series and in parallel.","2. Explaining how electric current flows in electrical circuits.","What is the main difference between a series circuit and a parallel circuit?","Series circuits have more wires than parallel circuits","Parallel circuits have more lights than series circuits","Series circuits have all devices connected one after the other","Parallel circuits have all devices connected at the same time","Series circuits have all devices connected one after the other","In a series circuit, all devices are connected one after the other, whereas in a parallel circuit, all devices are connected at the same time.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-happens-a-charged-c141ad62","Science G6: Understanding the concept of electric charge - Set 3","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-4-2 Understanding the concepts of reflection and refraction of light, transmission of sound, and explaining their role in interaction and communication in the surrounding environment.","1. Explaining the concept of electric charge and explaining the attraction and repulsion of charged bodies theoretically and graphically.","What happens when a charged object is brought near an uncharged object?","The uncharged object becomes charged","The charged object becomes uncharged","The uncharged object stays the same","The charged object disappears","The uncharged object becomes charged","When a charged object is brought near an uncharged object, the charged object transfers its charge to the uncharged object, making it charged.","/image-source/sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-happens-a-charged-c141ad62.png","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-why-do-we-92fb670d","Science G6: Understanding the concept of electric charge - Set 3","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-4-2 Understanding the concepts of reflection and refraction of light, transmission of sound, and explaining their role in interaction and communication in the surrounding environment.","1. Explaining the concept of electric charge and explaining the attraction and repulsion of charged bodies theoretically and graphically.","Why do we use parallel circuits in homes?","Because they are more expensive","Because they are less safe","Because all devices can be turned on at the same time","Because they are more complicated","Because all devices can be turned on at the same time","We use parallel circuits in homes because all devices can be turned on at the same time, making it more convenient.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-effect-of-c149321f","Science G6: Understanding the concept of electric charge - Set 3","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-4-2 Understanding the concepts of reflection and refraction of light, transmission of sound, and explaining their role in interaction and communication in the surrounding environment.","1. Explaining the concept of electric charge and explaining the attraction and repulsion of charged bodies theoretically and graphically.","What is the effect of adding a new device to a series circuit?","The voltage increases","The current increases","The resistance increases","The entire circuit becomes unpowered","The entire circuit becomes unpowered","When a new device is added to a series circuit, the entire circuit becomes unpowered because the new device adds more resistance.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-why-do-objects-4fd7b0be","Science G6: Understanding the concept of electric charge - Set 3","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-4-2 Understanding the concepts of reflection and refraction of light, transmission of sound, and explaining their role in interaction and communication in the surrounding environment.","1. Explaining the concept of electric charge and explaining the attraction and repulsion of charged bodies theoretically and graphically.","Why do objects with opposite electric charges attract each other?","Because they have the same type of charge","Because they have different types of charge","Because they are made of the same material","Because they are made of different materials","Because they have different types of charge","Objects with opposite electric charges attract each other because they have different types of charge, which creates a force of attraction.","/image-source/sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-why-do-objects-4fd7b0be.png","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-happens-a-device-8a558c26","Science G6: Understanding the concept of electric charge - Set 3","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-4-2 Understanding the concepts of reflection and refraction of light, transmission of sound, and explaining their role in interaction and communication in the surrounding environment.","1. Explaining the concept of electric charge and explaining the attraction and repulsion of charged bodies theoretically and graphically.","What happens when a device in a parallel circuit breaks?","The entire circuit becomes unpowered","Only the broken device becomes unpowered","The other devices in the circuit become unpowered","The circuit becomes more complicated","Only the broken device becomes unpowered","When a device in a parallel circuit breaks, only the broken device becomes unpowered, and the other devices in the circuit continue to function.","/image-source/sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-happens-a-device-8a558c26.png","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-why-do-we-efc89c52","Science G6: Understanding the concept of electric charge - Set 3","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-4-2 Understanding the concepts of reflection and refraction of light, transmission of sound, and explaining their role in interaction and communication in the surrounding environment.","1. Explaining the concept of electric charge and explaining the attraction and repulsion of charged bodies theoretically and graphically.","Why do we use series circuits in some electronic devices?","Because they are more expensive","Because they are less safe","Because the devices need to be powered one after the other","Because they are more complicated","Because the devices need to be powered one after the other","We use series circuits in some electronic devices because the devices need to be powered one after the other, making it necessary to use a series circuit.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-effect-of-4ff09ca6","Science G6: Understanding the concept of electric charge - Set 3","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-5-1Understanding the concept of electric charge, explaining the attraction and repulsion of charged bodies, and comparing electrical circuits connected in series and in parallel.","2. Explaining how electric current flows in electrical circuits.","What is the effect of increasing the resistance in a series circuit?","The voltage increases","The current increases","The power increases","The entire circuit becomes unpowered","The entire circuit becomes unpowered","When the resistance in a series circuit is increased, the entire circuit becomes unpowered because the increased resistance makes it difficult for the current to flow.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-energy-of-e3c96396","Science G6: Understanding the concept of energy and - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-3-2 Understanding the principle of energy conservation during its transformations and apply it in daily life.","1. Describing how energy is transferred from one place to another in its surroundings and between objects and systems.","What is the energy of motion called?","Potential energy","Kinetic energy","Thermal energy","Electrical energy","Kinetic energy","Kinetic energy is the energy of motion. It is the energy an object has due to its motion, such as a rolling ball or a moving car.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-happens-to-potential-7198e099","Science G6: Understanding the concept of energy and - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-3-2 Understanding the principle of energy conservation during its transformations and apply it in daily life.","1. Describing how energy is transferred from one place to another in its surroundings and between objects and systems.","What happens to the potential energy of an object when it is lifted to a higher position?","It increases","It decreases","It stays the same","It is converted to kinetic energy","It increases","When an object is lifted to a higher position, its potential energy increases. This is because the object's position changes, and its potential energy is transferred to its new position.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-energy-stored-496439f1","Science G6: Understanding the concept of energy and - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-3-2 Understanding the principle of energy conservation during its transformations and apply it in daily life.","1. Explaining the concept of wave and representing it graphically.","What is the energy stored in an object due to its position or state?","The energy used to move an object","The energy of motion","The energy stored in an object","The energy released when an object falls","The energy stored in an object","Potential energy is the energy stored in an object due to its position or state. For example, a ball at the top of a hill has potential energy that is converted to kinetic energy as it rolls down the hill.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-type-of-energy-9a3567c4","Science G6: Understanding the concept of energy and - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-3-2 Understanding the principle of energy conservation during its transformations and apply it in daily life.","1. Explaining the concept of wave and representing it graphically.","What type of energy is associated with the motion of an object?","The energy stored in an object","The energy used to move an object","The energy released when an object falls","The energy of motion","The energy of motion","Kinetic energy is the energy associated with the motion of an object. For example, a rolling ball has kinetic energy due to its motion.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-happens-to-potential-5c66ef3f","Science G6: Understanding the concept of energy and - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-3-2 Understanding the principle of energy conservation during its transformations and apply it in daily life.","1. Describing how energy is transferred from one place to another in its surroundings and between objects and systems.","What happens to the potential energy of an object when it falls from a height?","It increases","It decreases","It stays the same","It is converted to kinetic energy","It is converted to kinetic energy","When an object falls from a height, its potential energy is converted to kinetic energy. This is because the object's position changes, and its potential energy is transferred to its motion.","/image-source/sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-happens-to-potential-5c66ef3f.png","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-an-example-ba24d8cb","Science G6: Understanding the concept of energy and - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-3-1 Understanding the concept of energy and work, distinguishing between them, and giving real life examples .","2. Explaining the concept of potential energy and kinetic energy and their relationship to body movement.","What is an example of potential energy in everyday life?","A running car","A ball in motion","A ball at the top of a hill","A book on a table","A ball at the top of a hill","A ball at the top of a hill is an example of potential energy in everyday life. The ball has stored energy due to its position, which is converted to kinetic energy as it rolls down the hill.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-an-example-5b14115c","Science G6: Understanding the concept of energy and - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-3-1 Understanding the concept of energy and work, distinguishing between them, and giving real life examples .","2. Explaining the concept of potential energy and kinetic energy and their relationship to body movement.","What is an example of kinetic energy in everyday life?","A ball at the top of a hill","A book on a table","A running car","A ball in motion","A ball in motion","A ball in motion is an example of kinetic energy in everyday life. The ball has energy due to its motion, which is a result of its potential energy being converted to kinetic energy.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-relationship-potential-54dada7b","Science G6: Understanding the concept of energy and - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-3-2 Understanding the principle of energy conservation during its transformations and apply it in daily life.","1. Describing how energy is transferred from one place to another in its surroundings and between objects and systems.","What is the relationship between potential energy and kinetic energy?","They are the same thing","One is always greater than the other","They are always equal","One is converted to the other","One is converted to the other","Potential energy and kinetic energy are related in that one is converted to the other. For example, the potential energy of an object at the top of a hill is converted to kinetic energy as it rolls down the hill.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-happens-to-kinetic-9404517d","Science G6: Understanding the concept of energy and - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-3-2 Understanding the principle of energy conservation during its transformations and apply it in daily life.","1. Describing how energy is transferred from one place to another in its surroundings and between objects and systems.","What happens to the kinetic energy of an object when it comes to a stop?","It increases","It decreases","It stays the same","It is converted to potential energy","It decreases","When an object comes to a stop, its kinetic energy decreases because it is transferred into other forms of energy, such as heat or sound.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-an-example-c2d8e437","Science G6: Understanding the concept of energy and - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-3-2 Understanding the principle of energy conservation during its transformations and apply it in daily life.","1. Explaining the concept of wave and representing it graphically.","What is an example of a situation where potential energy is converted to kinetic energy?","A book on a table","A ball at the top of a hill","A running car","A ball in motion","A ball at the top of a hill","A ball at the top of a hill is an example of a situation where potential energy is converted to kinetic energy. As the ball rolls down the hill, its potential energy is converted to kinetic energy.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-main-purpose-9350617f","Science G6: Understanding the concept of energy and - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-3-1 Understanding the concept of energy and work, distinguishing between them, and giving real life examples .","3. Selectsing the simple machine that facilitates completing a task from among different machines, and provides examples of the benefits of simple machines in everyday life","What is the main purpose of a simple machine?","To make work easier","To make work harder","To make work more complicated","To make work more time-consuming","To make work easier","Simple machines are designed to make tasks easier to perform.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-simple-machine-is-6253a622","Science G6: Understanding the concept of energy and - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-3-1 Understanding the concept of energy and work, distinguishing between them, and giving real life examples .","2. Explaining the concept of potential energy and kinetic energy and their relationship to body movement.","Which simple machine is used to change the direction of a force?","Wheel and Axle","Inclined Plane","Pulley","Lever","Lever","A lever is used to change the direction of a force, making it easier to lift heavy objects.","/image-source/sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-simple-machine-is-6253a622.png","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-main-advantage-5395909f","Science G6: Understanding the concept of energy and - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-3-1 Understanding the concept of energy and work, distinguishing between them, and giving real life examples .","3. Selectsing the simple machine that facilitates completing a task from among different machines, and provides examples of the benefits of simple machines in everyday life","What is the main advantage of using a simple machine?","It makes work more difficult","It saves time and energy","It makes work more complicated","It makes work more time-consuming","It saves time and energy","The main advantage of using a simple machine is that it saves time and energy by making tasks easier to perform.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-simple-machine-is-4f97454d","Science G6: Understanding the concept of energy and - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-3-1 Understanding the concept of energy and work, distinguishing between them, and giving real life examples .","2. Explaining the concept of potential energy and kinetic energy and their relationship to body movement.","Which simple machine is used to lift heavy loads?","Wheel and Axle","Inclined Plane","Pulley","Lever","Lever","A lever is used to lift heavy loads by changing the direction of the force applied.","/image-source/sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-simple-machine-is-4f97454d.png","",""
