"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-main-advantage-1963fe2b","Science G6: Understanding the concept of energy and work - Set 3","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-3-1 Understanding the concept of energy and work, distinguishing between them, and giving real life examples .","3. Selectsing the simple machine that facilitates completing a task from among different machines, and provides examples of the benefits of simple machines in everyday life","What is the main advantage of using a simple machine?","It makes tasks more difficult","It makes tasks more expensive","It makes tasks easier and faster","It makes tasks more complicated","It makes tasks easier and faster","The main advantage of using a simple machine is that it makes tasks easier and faster.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-simple-machine-is-b28522ec","Science G6: Understanding the concept of energy and work - Set 3","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-3-1 Understanding the concept of energy and work, distinguishing between them, and giving real life examples .","2. Explaining the concept of potential energy and kinetic energy and their relationship to body movement.","Which simple machine is used to lift heavy objects with a rope?","Wheel and Axle","Inclined Plane","Pulley","Lever","Pulley","A pulley is used to lift heavy objects with a rope, making it easier to lift heavy loads.","/image-source/sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-simple-machine-is-b28522ec.png","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-purpose-of-77422560","Science G6: Understanding the concept of energy and work - Set 3","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-3-1 Understanding the concept of energy and work, distinguishing between them, and giving real life examples .","2. Explaining the concept of potential energy and kinetic energy and their relationship to body movement.","What is the purpose of an inclined plane?","To make a task more difficult","To make a task easier and faster","To lift heavy objects","To change the direction of a force","To make a task easier and faster","An inclined plane is used to make a task easier and faster by reducing the amount of effort needed.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-term-movement-d42ed84e","Science G6: Understanding the concepts of reflection and - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-4-2 Understanding the concepts of reflection and refraction of light, transmission of sound, and explaining their role in interaction and communication in the surrounding environment.","2. Explaining the eye's vision of the objects and colors around it.","What is the term for the movement of electric charges from one object to another?","Electric current is the flow of electrons through a conductor.","Electric charges can only be transferred through a conductor.","Electric charges can be transferred through a non-conductor.","Electric charges can only be transferred through a wire.","Electric current is the flow of electrons through a conductor.","Electric current is the flow of electrons through a conductor, which can be a wire or any other material that allows the transfer of electric charges.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-happens-two-objects-1492a017","Science G6: Understanding the concepts of reflection and - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-4-2 Understanding the concepts of reflection and refraction of light, transmission of sound, and explaining their role in interaction and communication in the surrounding environment.","1. Explaining the concept of electric charge and explaining the attraction and repulsion of charged bodies theoretically and graphically.","What happens when two objects with the same electric charge are brought close together?","The objects attract each other.","The objects repel each other.","The objects become neutral.","The objects become charged.","The objects repel each other.","Like charges repel each other due to the force of electric repulsion.","/image-source/sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-happens-two-objects-1492a017.png","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-force-attracts-749d76d7","Science G6: Understanding the concepts of reflection and - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-4-2 Understanding the concepts of reflection and refraction of light, transmission of sound, and explaining their role in interaction and communication in the surrounding environment.","1. Explaining the concept of electric charge and explaining the attraction and repulsion of charged bodies theoretically and graphically.","What is the force that attracts two objects with opposite electric charges?","Static electricity is the buildup of electric charges on objects.","Electric charges can only be positive or negative.","Opposite charges attract each other.","Like charges repel each other.","Opposite charges attract each other.","Opposite charges attract each other due to the force of electric attraction.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-happens-an-object-4458b074","Science G6: Understanding the concepts of reflection and - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-4-2 Understanding the concepts of reflection and refraction of light, transmission of sound, and explaining their role in interaction and communication in the surrounding environment.","2. Explaining the eye's vision of the objects and colors around it.","What happens when an object is rubbed with a cloth?","The object becomes heavier.","The object becomes lighter.","The object gains electrons and becomes negatively charged.","The object loses electrons and becomes positively charged.","The object loses electrons and becomes positively charged.","The object loses electrons and becomes positively charged due to the transfer of electrons from the cloth.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-term-buildup-c8d832dc","Science G6: Understanding the concepts of reflection and - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-4-2 Understanding the concepts of reflection and refraction of light, transmission of sound, and explaining their role in interaction and communication in the surrounding environment.","2. Explaining the eye's vision of the objects and colors around it.","What is the term for the buildup of electric charges on objects?","Static electricity is the buildup of electric charges on objects.","Electric charges can only be positive or negative.","Opposite charges attract each other.","Like charges repel each other.","Static electricity is the buildup of electric charges on objects.","Static electricity is the buildup of electric charges on objects, which can occur due to the transfer of electrons from one object to another.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-term-force-3549d9df","Science G6: Understanding the concepts of reflection and - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-4-2 Understanding the concepts of reflection and refraction of light, transmission of sound, and explaining their role in interaction and communication in the surrounding environment.","1. Explaining the concept of electric charge and explaining the attraction and repulsion of charged bodies theoretically and graphically.","What is the term for the force that pushes two objects with the same electric charge apart?","Static electricity is the buildup of electric charges on objects.","Electric charges can only be positive or negative.","Opposite charges attract each other.","Like charges repel each other.","Like charges repel each other.","Like charges repel each other due to the force of electric repulsion.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-term-flow-3b3d6aba","Science G6: Understanding the concepts of reflection and - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-5-1Understanding the concept of electric charge, explaining the attraction and repulsion of charged bodies, and comparing electrical circuits connected in series and in parallel.","2. Explaining how electric current flows in electrical circuits.","What is the term for the flow of electric charges through a conductor?","Electric current is the flow of electrons through a conductor.","Electric charges can only be transferred through a conductor.","Electric charges can be transferred through a non-conductor.","Electric charges can only be transferred through a wire.","Electric current is the flow of electrons through a conductor.","Electric current is the flow of electrons through a conductor, which can be a wire or any other material that allows the transfer of electric charges.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-term-transfer-ae4e5b1c","Science G6: Understanding the concepts of reflection and - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-4-2 Understanding the concepts of reflection and refraction of light, transmission of sound, and explaining their role in interaction and communication in the surrounding environment.","2. Explaining the eye's vision of the objects and colors around it.","What is the term for the transfer of electric charges from one object to another?","Electric current is the flow of electrons through a conductor.","Electric charges can only be transferred through a conductor.","Electric charges can be transferred through a non-conductor.","Electric charges can only be transferred through a wire.","Electric charges can be transferred through a non-conductor.","Electric charges can be transferred through a non-conductor, such as a piece of cloth or a plastic bag.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-happens-two-objects-8ffbd4c5","Science G6: Understanding the concepts of reflection and - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-4-2 Understanding the concepts of reflection and refraction of light, transmission of sound, and explaining their role in interaction and communication in the surrounding environment.","1. Explaining the concept of electric charge and explaining the attraction and repulsion of charged bodies theoretically and graphically.","What happens when two objects with the same electric charge are brought close together?","Static electricity is the buildup of electric charges on objects.","Electric charges can only be positive or negative.","Opposite charges attract each other.","Like charges repel each other.","Like charges repel each other.","Objects with the same electric charge repel each other.","/image-source/sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-happens-two-objects-8ffbd4c5.png","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-term-movement-901e0e6a","Science G6: Understanding the concepts of reflection and - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-5-1Understanding the concept of electric charge, explaining the attraction and repulsion of charged bodies, and comparing electrical circuits connected in series and in parallel.","2. Explaining how electric current flows in electrical circuits.","What is the term for the movement of electric charges through a conductor?","Electric current is the flow of electrons through a conductor.","Electric charges can only be transferred through a conductor.","Electric charges can be transferred through a non-conductor.","Electric charges can only be transferred through a wire.","Electric current is the flow of electrons through a conductor.","Electric current is the flow of electrons through a conductor, which can be a wire or any other material that allows the transfer of electric charges.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-happens-to-sound-32144823","Science G6: Understanding the concepts of reflection and - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-4-2 Understanding the concepts of reflection and refraction of light, transmission of sound, and explaining their role in interaction and communication in the surrounding environment.","3. Describing the transmission of sound by absorbing or reflecting it through different media and objects.","What happens to sound when it passes from one medium to another?","The sound speed increases and becomes louder.","The sound speed decreases and becomes softer.","The sound is absorbed and disappears.","The sound is reflected back to the source.","The sound speed decreases and becomes softer.","When sound passes from one medium to another, its speed and intensity may change, but it is not absorbed or reflected.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-why-do-we-605c21f5","Science G6: Understanding the concepts of reflection and - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-4-2 Understanding the concepts of reflection and refraction of light, transmission of sound, and explaining their role in interaction and communication in the surrounding environment.","2. Explaining the eye's vision of the objects and colors around it.","Why do we hear a sound differently when we are underwater?","Because the sound waves are too weak to reach us.","Because the sound waves are too strong and overpower us.","Because the sound waves travel faster in water and reach us sooner.","Because the sound waves are absorbed by the water and disappear.","Because the sound waves travel faster in water and reach us sooner.","We hear a sound differently when we are underwater because the sound waves travel faster in water and reach us sooner.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-process-called-eec9acaf","Science G6: Understanding the concepts of reflection and - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-4-2 Understanding the concepts of reflection and refraction of light, transmission of sound, and explaining their role in interaction and communication in the surrounding environment.","2. Explaining the eye's vision of the objects and colors around it.","What is the process called when sound waves pass from one medium to another?","Reflection","Refraction","Diffraction","Absorption","Refraction","The process of sound waves passing from one medium to another is called refraction.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-happens-to-sound-bab449d2","Science G6: Understanding the concepts of reflection and - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-4-2 Understanding the concepts of reflection and refraction of light, transmission of sound, and explaining their role in interaction and communication in the surrounding environment.","3. Describing the transmission of sound by absorbing or reflecting it through different media and objects.","What happens to sound when it hits a liquid?","The sound is absorbed and disappears.","The sound is reflected back to the source.","The sound passes through the liquid without any change.","The sound is blocked and cannot pass through.","The sound passes through the liquid without any change.","When sound hits a liquid, it passes through the liquid without any significant change.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-why-do-we-2e958031","Science G6: Understanding the concepts of reflection and - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-4-2 Understanding the concepts of reflection and refraction of light, transmission of sound, and explaining their role in interaction and communication in the surrounding environment.","2. Explaining the eye's vision of the objects and colors around it.","Why do we hear a echo in a large empty room?","Because the sound waves are too weak to reach us.","Because the sound waves are too strong and overpower us.","Because the sound waves bounce back off the walls and return to us.","Because the sound waves are absorbed by the walls and disappear.","Because the sound waves bounce back off the walls and return to us.","We hear an echo in a large empty room because the sound waves bounce back off the walls and return to us.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-process-called-ca11b053","Science G6: Understanding the concepts of reflection and - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-4-2 Understanding the concepts of reflection and refraction of light, transmission of sound, and explaining their role in interaction and communication in the surrounding environment.","2. Explaining the eye's vision of the objects and colors around it.","What is the process called when sound waves bounce back off a surface?","Reflection","Refraction","Diffraction","Absorption","Reflection","The process of sound waves bouncing back off a surface is called reflection.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-happens-to-sound-a53afb28","Science G6: Understanding the concepts of reflection and - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-4-2 Understanding the concepts of reflection and refraction of light, transmission of sound, and explaining their role in interaction and communication in the surrounding environment.","3. Describing the transmission of sound by absorbing or reflecting it through different media and objects.","What happens to sound when it hits a solid object?","The sound is absorbed and disappears.","The sound is reflected back to the source.","The sound passes through the object without any change.","The sound is blocked and cannot pass through.","The sound is reflected back to the source.","When sound hits a solid object, it bounces back and is reflected back to the source.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-why-do-we-543f3912","Science G6: Understanding the concepts of reflection and - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-4-2 Understanding the concepts of reflection and refraction of light, transmission of sound, and explaining their role in interaction and communication in the surrounding environment.","2. Explaining the eye's vision of the objects and colors around it.","Why do we hear a sound more clearly when we are standing near a wall?","Because the sound waves are too weak to reach us.","Because the sound waves are too strong and overpower us.","Because the sound waves bounce back off the wall and return to us.","Because the sound waves are absorbed by the wall and disappear.","Because the sound waves bounce back off the wall and return to us.","We hear a sound more clearly when we are standing near a wall because the sound waves bounce back off the wall and return to us.","/image-source/sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-why-do-we-543f3912.png","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-why-do-we-157f676e","Science G6: Understanding the concepts of reflection and - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-4-2 Understanding the concepts of reflection and refraction of light, transmission of sound, and explaining their role in interaction and communication in the surrounding environment.","3. Describing the transmission of sound by absorbing or reflecting it through different media and objects.","Why do we hear a sound differently when we are standing near a metal object?","Because the sound waves are too weak to reach us.","Because the sound waves are too strong and overpower us.","Because the sound waves bounce back off the metal and return to us.","Because the sound waves are absorbed by the metal and disappear.","Because the sound waves bounce back off the metal and return to us.","We hear a sound differently when we are standing near a metal object because the sound waves bounce back off the metal and return to us.","/image-source/sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-why-do-we-157f676e.png","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-why-do-we-77870e3e","Science G6: Understanding the concepts of reflection and - Set 2","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-4-2 Understanding the concepts of reflection and refraction of light, transmission of sound, and explaining their role in interaction and communication in the surrounding environment.","2. Explaining the eye's vision of the objects and colors around it.","Why do we hear a sound more clearly when we are standing in an open field?","Because the sound waves are too weak to reach us.","Because the sound waves are too strong and overpower us.","Because the sound waves bounce back off the ground and return to us.","Because the sound waves are absorbed by the ground and disappear.","Because the sound waves bounce back off the ground and return to us.","We hear a sound more clearly when we are standing in an open field because the sound waves bounce back off the ground and return to us.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-happens-to-energy-16088341","Science G6: Understanding the principle of energy conservation during - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-3-2 Understanding the principle of energy conservation during its transformations and apply it in daily life.","1. Describing how energy is transferred from one place to another in its surroundings and between objects and systems.","What happens to the energy of a moving car when it brakes?","The energy is created","The energy is destroyed","The energy is transferred to the surroundings as heat","The energy remains the same","The energy is transferred to the surroundings as heat","When a moving car brakes, its kinetic energy is transferred to the surroundings as heat, which is a form of energy conservation.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-how-a-bicycle-c8a5f718","Science G6: Understanding the principle of energy conservation during - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-3-2 Understanding the principle of energy conservation during its transformations and apply it in daily life.","1. Explaining the concept of wave and representing it graphically.","How does a bicycle pedal convert muscle energy into kinetic energy?","It uses a magnet to attract muscle energy from the rider","It uses a fan to blow muscle energy into the air","It converts the muscle energy of the rider into kinetic energy using gears","It uses a battery to store muscle energy from the rider","It converts the muscle energy of the rider into kinetic energy using gears","A bicycle pedal converts the muscle energy of the rider into kinetic energy using gears, which is a form of energy conversion.","/image-source/sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-how-a-bicycle-c8a5f718.png","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-main-purpose-672108c2","Science G6: Understanding the principle of energy conservation during - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-3-2 Understanding the principle of energy conservation during its transformations and apply it in daily life.","1. Explaining the concept of wave and representing it graphically.","What is the main purpose of a waterwheel in a hydroelectric power plant?","To create a fire in the water","To destroy energy by heating the surroundings","To transfer the kinetic energy of water into electricity using blades","To create a vacuum in the water","To transfer the kinetic energy of water into electricity using blades","A waterwheel transfers the kinetic energy of water into electricity using blades, which is a form of energy conversion.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-how-a-car-9fed4f64","Science G6: Understanding the principle of energy conservation during - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-3-2 Understanding the principle of energy conservation during its transformations and apply it in daily life.","1. Explaining the concept of wave and representing it graphically.","How does a car engine convert chemical energy into kinetic energy?","It uses a magnet to attract chemical energy from the fuel","It uses a fan to blow chemical energy into the air","It converts the chemical energy of the fuel into kinetic energy using pistons","It uses a battery to store chemical energy from the fuel","It converts the chemical energy of the fuel into kinetic energy using pistons","A car engine converts the chemical energy of the fuel into kinetic energy using pistons, which is a form of energy conversion.","/image-source/sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-how-a-car-9fed4f64.png","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-how-a-generator-fc0d65ec","Science G6: Understanding the principle of energy conservation during - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-3-2 Understanding the principle of energy conservation during its transformations and apply it in daily life.","1. Explaining the concept of wave and representing it graphically.","How does a generator convert mechanical energy into electrical energy?","It uses a magnet to attract mechanical energy from the surroundings","It uses a fan to blow mechanical energy into the air","It converts the mechanical energy of the surroundings into electrical energy using coils","It uses a battery to store mechanical energy from the surroundings","It converts the mechanical energy of the surroundings into electrical energy using coils","A generator converts the mechanical energy of the surroundings into electrical energy using coils, which is a form of energy conversion.","/image-source/sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-how-a-generator-fc0d65ec.png","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-happens-to-energy-943f36f2","Science G6: Understanding the principle of energy conservation during - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-3-2 Understanding the principle of energy conservation during its transformations and apply it in daily life.","1. Describing how energy is transferred from one place to another in its surroundings and between objects and systems.","What happens to the energy of a person when they climb a staircase?","The energy is destroyed","The energy is transferred to the surroundings as heat","The energy is transferred to the surroundings as sound","The energy remains the same","The energy is transferred to the surroundings as heat","When a person climbs a staircase, their potential energy is transferred to the surroundings as heat, which is a form of energy conservation.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-happens-to-energy-5bdeeb99","Science G6: Understanding the principle of energy conservation during - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-3-2 Understanding the principle of energy conservation during its transformations and apply it in daily life.","1. Describing how energy is transferred from one place to another in its surroundings and between objects and systems.","What happens to the energy of a falling object when it hits the ground?","The energy is destroyed","The energy is transferred to the surroundings as heat","The energy is transferred to the surroundings as sound","The energy remains the same","The energy is transferred to the surroundings as heat","When a falling object hits the ground, its potential energy is transferred to the surroundings as heat, which is a form of energy conservation.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-how-a-wind-88d1b960","Science G6: Understanding the principle of energy conservation during - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-3-2 Understanding the principle of energy conservation during its transformations and apply it in daily life.","1. Explaining the concept of wave and representing it graphically.","How does a wind turbine generate electricity?","It uses a magnet to attract electricity from the air","It uses a fan to blow electricity into the air","It converts the kinetic energy of wind into electricity using blades","It uses a battery to store electricity from the wind","It converts the kinetic energy of wind into electricity using blades","A wind turbine converts the kinetic energy of wind into electricity using blades, which is a form of energy conversion.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-main-purpose-b202c477","Science G6: Understanding the principle of energy conservation during - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-3-2 Understanding the principle of energy conservation during its transformations and apply it in daily life.","1. Describing how energy is transferred from one place to another in its surroundings and between objects and systems.","What is the main purpose of a refrigerator in a household?","To create a vacuum in the room","To destroy energy by heating the surroundings","To transfer heat from the inside to the outside using a compressor","To create a fire in the kitchen","To transfer heat from the inside to the outside using a compressor","A refrigerator transfers heat from the inside to the outside using a compressor, which is a form of energy conservation.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-how-a-solar-867a93e9","Science G6: Understanding the principle of energy conservation during - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-3-2 Understanding the principle of energy conservation during its transformations and apply it in daily life.","1. Explaining the concept of wave and representing it graphically.","How does a solar panel convert sunlight into usable energy?","It converts sunlight into electricity using a battery","It converts sunlight into heat using a fan","It converts sunlight into electricity using photovoltaic cells","It converts sunlight into water using a pump","It converts sunlight into electricity using photovoltaic cells","A solar panel converts sunlight into electricity using photovoltaic cells, which is a form of energy conversion.","/image-source/sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-how-a-solar-867a93e9.png","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-happens-to-energy-02c3ec78","Science G6: Understanding the principle of energy conservation during - Set 1 T1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-3-2 Understanding the principle of energy conservation during its transformations and apply it in daily life.","1. Explaining the concept of wave and representing it graphically.","What happens to the energy of a wave as it travels through a medium with no energy loss?","It increases","It decreases","It stays the same","It disappears","It stays the same","In an ideal medium with no energy loss, the energy of a wave stays the same as it travels. In real materials, some energy can be absorbed or spread out.","/image-source/sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-happens-to-energy-02c3ec78.png","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-medium-a-23c71ffe","Science G6: Understanding the principle of energy conservation during - Set 1 T1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-2-1 Describing the concept of force and distinguishing between different types","1. Determining the relationship of distance to motion and explaining how the position of an object can be determined using a reference point.","What is the medium that a wave travels through?","Air","Water","Solid objects","All of the above","All of the above","A wave can travel through air, water, or solid objects, depending on the type of wave.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-a-wave-3aeb55ff","Science G6: Understanding the principle of energy conservation during - Set 1 T1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-3-2 Understanding the principle of energy conservation during its transformations and apply it in daily life.","1. Explaining the concept of wave and representing it graphically.","What is a wave?","A movement of water in the ocean","A disturbance that travels through a medium","A type of animal that lives in the sea","A type of rock that forms in the ground","A disturbance that travels through a medium","A wave is a disturbance that travels through a medium, such as water or air.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-how-can-you-7e48dea9","Science G6: Understanding the principle of energy conservation during - Set 1 T1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-3-2 Understanding the principle of energy conservation during its transformations and apply it in daily life.","1. Explaining the concept of wave and representing it graphically.","How can you represent a wave graphically?","By drawing a straight line","By drawing a curve that goes up and down","By drawing a circle","By drawing a square","By drawing a curve that goes up and down","A wave can be represented graphically by drawing a curve that goes up and down, showing the movement of the wave.","/image-source/sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-how-can-you-7e48dea9.png","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-can-a-wave-ef8cfc72","Science G6: Understanding the principle of energy conservation during - Set 1 T1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-4-1 Describing waves, distinguishing between their properties theoretically and graphically, and predicting their movement.","2. Distinguishing between the properties of sound and light waves theoretically and graphically.","Can a wave be heard?","Yes, if it is a sound wave","Yes, if it is a light wave","No, waves are only seen","No, waves are only felt","Yes, if it is a sound wave","A sound wave can be heard, as it creates sound vibrations in the air.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-can-a-wave-abb4bd28","Science G6: Understanding the principle of energy conservation during - Set 1 T1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-3-2 Understanding the principle of energy conservation during its transformations and apply it in daily life.","1. Explaining the concept of wave and representing it graphically.","Can a wave be created by a person?","Yes, by throwing a stone into a pond","Yes, by blowing a whistle","No, waves are only natural","No, waves are only man-made","Yes, by throwing a stone into a pond","A person can create a wave by throwing a stone into a pond, which creates ripples in the water.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-difference-a-e5d309ea","Science G6: Understanding the principle of energy conservation during - Set 1 T1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-3-2 Understanding the principle of energy conservation during its transformations and apply it in daily life.","1. Explaining the concept of wave and representing it graphically.","What is the difference between a wave and a ripple?","A wave is bigger than a ripple","A wave is smaller than a ripple","A wave is a type of ripple","A ripple is a type of wave","A ripple is a type of wave","A ripple is a small type of wave, usually seen on the surface of water.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-an-example-a9e6dac1","Science G6: Understanding the principle of energy conservation during - Set 1 T1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-3-2 Understanding the principle of energy conservation during its transformations and apply it in daily life.","1. Explaining the concept of wave and representing it graphically.","What is an example of a wave in everyday life?","A tree swaying in the wind","Ripples spreading across a pond","A bird flying through the air","A car driving down the road","Ripples spreading across a pond","Ripples spreading across a pond are water waves, which are a common example of waves in everyday life.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-purpose-of-35f321d5","Science G6: Understanding the principle of energy conservation during - Set 1 T1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-3-2 Understanding the principle of energy conservation during its transformations and apply it in daily life.","1. Explaining the concept of wave and representing it graphically.","What is the purpose of representing a wave graphically?","To show the size of the wave","To show the shape of the wave","To show the energy of the wave","To show the speed of the wave","To show the shape of the wave","Representing a wave graphically helps to show the shape of the wave, which can be useful for understanding its behavior.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-can-a-wave-21c1cd88","Science G6: Understanding the principle of energy conservation during - Set 1 T1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-3-2 Understanding the principle of energy conservation during its transformations and apply it in daily life.","1. Explaining the concept of wave and representing it graphically.","Can a wave be felt?","Yes, if it is a big wave","Yes, if it is a small wave","No, waves are only seen","No, waves are only heard","Yes, if it is a big wave","A big wave can be felt, as it can cause objects to move or vibrate.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-how-a-bicycle-704cf4d9","Science G6: Understanding the principle of energy conservation - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-3-2 Understanding the principle of energy conservation during its transformations and apply it in daily life.","1. Describing how energy is transferred from one place to another in its surroundings and between objects and systems.","How does a bicycle pedal transfer energy to the wheels?","The energy is transferred through a magic spell.","The energy is transferred from the ground to the wheels.","The energy is transferred from the pedals to the wheels through a system of gears.","The energy is transferred from the pedals to the wheels through a system of levers.","The energy is transferred from the pedals to the wheels through a system of gears.","When you pedal a bicycle, the energy is transferred from the pedals to the wheels through a system of gears, which turns the wheels and propels the bicycle forward.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-how-a-wind-b3029a88","Science G6: Understanding the principle of energy conservation - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-3-2 Understanding the principle of energy conservation during its transformations and apply it in daily life.","1. Explaining the concept of wave and representing it graphically.","How does a wind turbine transfer energy from the wind?","The energy is transferred through a series of mirrors.","The energy is transferred from the wind to the wind turbine through a wire.","The energy is transferred from the wind to the wind turbine through the flow of air.","The energy is transferred from the wind turbine to the surrounding air.","The energy is transferred from the wind to the wind turbine through the flow of air.","When wind flows over a wind turbine, the energy is transferred from the wind to the wind turbine through the flow of air, which turns the blades and generates electricity.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-an-example-5edc1a6b","Science G6: Understanding the principle of energy conservation - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-3-1 Understanding the concept of energy and work, distinguishing between them, and giving real life examples .","2. Explaining the concept of potential energy and kinetic energy and their relationship to body movement.","What is an example of energy transformation in a refrigerator?","The energy is transformed from electrical to thermal.","The energy is transformed from thermal to electrical.","The energy is transformed from kinetic to potential.","The energy is transformed from potential to kinetic.","The energy is transformed from electrical to thermal.","When a refrigerator is turned on, the electrical energy is transformed into thermal energy, which cools the interior of the refrigerator.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-how-a-solar-c78cc15b","Science G6: Understanding the principle of energy conservation - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-3-2 Understanding the principle of energy conservation during its transformations and apply it in daily life.","1. Explaining the concept of wave and representing it graphically.","How does a solar panel transfer energy from the sun?","The energy is transferred through a series of mirrors.","The energy is transferred from the sun to the solar panel through light.","The energy is transferred from the solar panel to the sun through a wire.","The energy is transferred from the solar panel to the surrounding air.","The energy is transferred from the sun to the solar panel through light.","When sunlight hits a solar panel, the energy is transferred from the sun to the solar panel through light, which is converted into electrical energy.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-happens-to-energy-fb1c7297","Science G6: Understanding the principle of energy conservation - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-3-2 Understanding the principle of energy conservation during its transformations and apply it in daily life.","1. Explaining the concept of wave and representing it graphically.","What happens to the energy when you throw a ball?","The energy disappears into thin air.","The energy is transferred from the ball to the ground.","The energy is transferred from the ball to the surrounding air.","The energy is stored in the ball.","The energy is transferred from the ball to the surrounding air.","When you throw a ball, the energy is transferred from the ball to the surrounding air as kinetic energy, which propels the ball forward.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-happens-to-energy-7634612d","Science G6: Understanding the principle of energy conservation - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-3-2 Understanding the principle of energy conservation during its transformations and apply it in daily life.","1. Explaining the concept of wave and representing it graphically.","What happens to the energy when you rub your hands together to warm them up?","The energy disappears into thin air.","The energy is transferred from your hands to the surrounding air.","The energy is created from nothing.","The energy is stored in your hands.","The energy is transferred from your hands to the surrounding air.","When you rub your hands together, the energy is transferred from your hands to the surrounding air as heat, which warms them up.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-is-an-example-1ccca52b","Science G6: Understanding the principle of energy conservation - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-3-1 Understanding the concept of energy and work, distinguishing between them, and giving real life examples .","2. Explaining the concept of potential energy and kinetic energy and their relationship to body movement.","What is an example of energy transformation in a light bulb?","The energy is transformed from electrical to thermal.","The energy is transformed from thermal to electrical.","The energy is transformed from kinetic to potential.","The energy is transformed from potential to kinetic.","The energy is transformed from electrical to thermal.","When a light bulb is turned on, the electrical energy is transformed into thermal energy, which produces light and heat.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-how-a-fan-46d550ae","Science G6: Understanding the principle of energy conservation - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-3-2 Understanding the principle of energy conservation during its transformations and apply it in daily life.","1. Describing how energy is transferred from one place to another in its surroundings and between objects and systems.","How does a fan transfer energy to the air?","The energy is transferred through a series of magnets.","The energy is transferred from the fan blades to the surrounding air.","The energy is transferred from the fan motor to the fan blades.","The energy is transferred from the air to the fan blades.","The energy is transferred from the fan blades to the surrounding air.","When a fan is turned on, the energy is transferred from the fan blades to the surrounding air, creating a flow of air.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-happens-to-energy-b0f5333c","Science G6: Understanding the principle of energy conservation - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-3-2 Understanding the principle of energy conservation during its transformations and apply it in daily life.","1. Explaining the concept of wave and representing it graphically.","What happens to the energy when you climb a staircase?","The energy disappears into thin air.","The energy is transferred from your body to the staircase.","The energy is transferred from the staircase to your body.","The energy is stored in your body.","The energy is transferred from your body to the staircase.","When you climb a staircase, the energy is transferred from your body to the staircase as work, which lifts you up the stairs.","","",""
"sci-g6-6-5-1-2-1-how-a-waterwheel-94ac39df","Science G6: Understanding the principle of energy conservation - Set 1","Science","6","Assessment for Grade 6 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","6-5-2-3-2 Understanding the principle of energy conservation during its transformations and apply it in daily life.","1. Explaining the concept of wave and representing it graphically.","How does a waterwheel transfer energy from the water?","The energy is transferred through a series of magnets.","The energy is transferred from the water to the waterwheel through a pipe.","The energy is transferred from the water to the waterwheel through the flow of water.","The energy is transferred from the waterwheel to the surrounding air.","The energy is transferred from the water to the waterwheel through the flow of water.","When water flows over a waterwheel, the energy is transferred from the water to the waterwheel through the flow of water, which turns the wheel.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-meant-by-bb4c554a","Science G9: Analyzing information and data related to - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-7 Analyzing information and data related to the theory of plate motion and continental drift and predicting its results and benefits.","2. Explaining what is meant by moving plate boundaries, listing their types (convergence boundaries, divergence boundaries, and lateral or transformational boundaries), and distinguishing each type.","What is meant by moving plate boundaries in the theory of plate motion?","Areas where the Earth's plates are stationary","Regions where the Earth's plates are moving apart","Boundaries where the Earth's plates are colliding","Locations where the Earth's plates are sliding past each other","Boundaries where the Earth's plates are colliding","Moving plate boundaries refer to areas where the Earth's plates are interacting with each other, such as colliding, moving apart, or sliding past each other.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-result-of-71ed1530","Science G9: Analyzing information and data related to - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-7 Analyzing information and data related to the theory of plate motion and continental drift and predicting its results and benefits.","2. Explaining what is meant by moving plate boundaries, listing their types (convergence boundaries, divergence boundaries, and lateral or transformational boundaries), and distinguishing each type.","What is the result of the Earth's plates moving apart at a divergence boundary?","The creation of new oceanic crust","The formation of mountains","The creation of fault lines","The destruction of the Earth's crust","The creation of new oceanic crust","At a divergence boundary, the Earth's plates moving apart results in the creation of new oceanic crust.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-result-of-cb2e8e5f","Science G9: Analyzing information and data related to - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-7 Analyzing information and data related to the theory of plate motion and continental drift and predicting its results and benefits.","2. Explaining what is meant by moving plate boundaries, listing their types (convergence boundaries, divergence boundaries, and lateral or transformational boundaries), and distinguishing each type.","What is the result of the Earth's plates sliding past each other at a lateral or transformational boundary?","The creation of new oceanic crust","The formation of mountains","The creation of fault lines","The destruction of the Earth's crust","The creation of fault lines","At a lateral or transformational boundary, the Earth's plates sliding past each other results in the creation of fault lines.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-result-of-85142946","Science G9: Analyzing information and data related to - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-7 Analyzing information and data related to the theory of plate motion and continental drift and predicting its results and benefits.","2. Explaining what is meant by moving plate boundaries, listing their types (convergence boundaries, divergence boundaries, and lateral or transformational boundaries), and distinguishing each type.","What is the result of the Earth's plates colliding at a convergence boundary?","The creation of new oceanic crust","The formation of mountains","The creation of fault lines","The destruction of the Earth's crust","The formation of mountains","At a convergence boundary, the Earth's plates colliding results in the formation of mountains.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-type-of-plate-9a9ca21d","Science G9: Analyzing information and data related to - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-7 Analyzing information and data related to the theory of plate motion and continental drift and predicting its results and benefits.","2. Explaining what is meant by moving plate boundaries, listing their types (convergence boundaries, divergence boundaries, and lateral or transformational boundaries), and distinguishing each type.","What type of plate boundary is characterized by the Earth's plates moving apart and creating new crust?","Convergence boundary","Divergence boundary","Lateral or transformational boundary","Continental boundary","Divergence boundary","Divergence boundaries are characterized by the Earth's plates moving apart and creating new crust.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-type-of-plate-428e608a","Science G9: Analyzing information and data related to - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-7 Analyzing information and data related to the theory of plate motion and continental drift and predicting its results and benefits.","2. Explaining what is meant by moving plate boundaries, listing their types (convergence boundaries, divergence boundaries, and lateral or transformational boundaries), and distinguishing each type.","What type of plate boundary is characterized by the Earth's plates sliding past each other and creating fault lines?","Convergence boundary","Divergence boundary","Lateral or transformational boundary","Oceanic boundary","Lateral or transformational boundary","Lateral or transformational boundaries are characterized by the Earth's plates sliding past each other and creating fault lines.","/image-source/sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-type-of-plate-428e608a.png","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-type-of-plate-b06b0316","Science G9: Analyzing information and data related to - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-7 Analyzing information and data related to the theory of plate motion and continental drift and predicting its results and benefits.","2. Explaining what is meant by moving plate boundaries, listing their types (convergence boundaries, divergence boundaries, and lateral or transformational boundaries), and distinguishing each type.","What type of plate boundary is characterized by the Earth's plates moving apart?","Convergence boundary","Divergence boundary","Lateral or transformational boundary","Continental boundary","Divergence boundary","Divergence boundaries are characterized by the Earth's plates moving apart, resulting in the creation of new crust.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-type-of-plate-ec773a4f","Science G9: Analyzing information and data related to - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-7 Analyzing information and data related to the theory of plate motion and continental drift and predicting its results and benefits.","2. Explaining what is meant by moving plate boundaries, listing their types (convergence boundaries, divergence boundaries, and lateral or transformational boundaries), and distinguishing each type.","What type of plate boundary is characterized by the Earth's plates sliding past each other?","Convergence boundary","Divergence boundary","Lateral or transformational boundary","Oceanic boundary","Lateral or transformational boundary","Lateral or transformational boundaries are characterized by the Earth's plates sliding past each other, resulting in the creation of fault lines.","/image-source/sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-type-of-plate-ec773a4f.png","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-type-of-plate-53e6dc0d","Science G9: Analyzing information and data related to - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-7 Analyzing information and data related to the theory of plate motion and continental drift and predicting its results and benefits.","2. Explaining what is meant by moving plate boundaries, listing their types (convergence boundaries, divergence boundaries, and lateral or transformational boundaries), and distinguishing each type.","What type of plate boundary is characterized by the Earth's plates colliding and forming mountains?","Convergence boundary","Divergence boundary","Lateral or transformational boundary","Continental boundary","Convergence boundary","Convergence boundaries are characterized by the Earth's plates colliding and forming mountains.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-type-of-plate-92426424","Science G9: Analyzing information and data related to - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-7 Analyzing information and data related to the theory of plate motion and continental drift and predicting its results and benefits.","2. Explaining what is meant by moving plate boundaries, listing their types (convergence boundaries, divergence boundaries, and lateral or transformational boundaries), and distinguishing each type.","What type of plate boundary is characterized by the Earth's plates colliding?","Convergence boundary","Divergence boundary","Lateral or transformational boundary","Continental boundary","Convergence boundary","Convergence boundaries are characterized by the Earth's plates colliding, resulting in the formation of mountains.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-term-ability-87caae5a","Science G9: Analyzing information related to the movement - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-4-3Understanding specific heat and the factors affecting it.","2. Determining the factors affecting the body's absorption or loss of thermal energy.","What is the term for the ability of a material to conduct heat energy?","Specific heat capacity is the ability of a material to absorb heat energy.","Thermal conductivity is the ability of a material to conduct heat energy.","Heat capacity is the ability of a material to retain heat energy.","Latent heat is the energy required to change the state of a substance.","Thermal conductivity is the ability of a material to conduct heat energy.","Thermal conductivity is the ability of a material to conduct heat energy, which depends on the material's properties and structure.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-term-energy-00d28d8b","Science G9: Analyzing information related to the movement - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-1-2 Analyzing information related to the movement of celestial bodies and their apparent and relative locations, and deducing the conditions prevailing in","2. Analyzing information related to the movement of celestial bodies across the sky to know their apparent and relative locations.","What is the term for the energy required to change the state of a substance from liquid to gas?","Latent heat of fusion is the energy required to change the state of a substance from solid to liquid.","Latent heat of vaporization is the energy required to change the state of a substance from liquid to gas.","Specific heat capacity is the energy required to change the temperature of a substance.","Heat capacity is the energy required to change the state of a substance.","Latent heat of vaporization is the energy required to change the state of a substance from liquid to gas.","Latent heat of vaporization is the energy required to change the state of a substance from liquid to gas, which is a constant value for a given substance.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-primary-method-abdf3456","Science G9: Analyzing information related to the movement - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-4-2 Understanding the mechanism of heat transmission and conduction between objects, and measuring temperature.","2. Comparing the methods of transmission and conduction of thermal energy between objects.","What is the primary method of heat transfer that occurs through a solid material?","Conduction is the transfer of heat through a gas.","Convection is the transfer of heat through a liquid.","Conduction is the transfer of heat through a solid.","Radiation is the transfer of heat through empty space.","Conduction is the transfer of heat through a solid.","Conduction is the transfer of heat through a solid material, where heat energy is transferred through direct contact between particles.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-term-energy-adddc11a","Science G9: Analyzing information related to the movement - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-4 Explaining the properties of liquids, comparing crystalline and amorphous solids, and describing the pattern of crystals in solids.","3. Describing the organization of molecules in crystalline solids by building models that describe their structure.","What is the term for the energy required to change the temperature of a substance?","Latent heat of fusion is the energy required to change the state of a substance from solid to liquid.","Latent heat of vaporization is the energy required to change the state of a substance from liquid to gas.","Specific heat capacity is the energy required to change the temperature of a substance.","Heat capacity is the energy required to change the state of a substance.","Specific heat capacity is the energy required to change the temperature of a substance.","Specific heat capacity is the energy required to change the temperature of a substance, which depends on the material's properties and structure.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-effect-of-5248398f","Science G9: Analyzing information related to the movement - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-1-2 Analyzing information related to the movement of celestial bodies and their apparent and relative locations, and deducing the conditions prevailing in","2. Analyzing information related to the movement of celestial bodies across the sky to know their apparent and relative locations.","What is the effect of increasing the mass of a substance on its heat capacity?","The heat capacity of a substance decreases as its mass increases.","The heat capacity of a substance remains constant as its mass increases.","The heat capacity of a substance increases as its mass increases.","The heat capacity of a substance has no relation to its mass.","The heat capacity of a substance increases as its mass increases.","As the mass of a substance increases, its heat capacity also increases, as more heat energy is required to change the temperature of the substance.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-effect-of-aacf413e","Science G9: Analyzing information related to the movement - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-4-2 Understanding the mechanism of heat transmission and conduction between objects, and measuring temperature.","2. Comparing the methods of transmission and conduction of thermal energy between objects.","What is the effect of decreasing the temperature of a substance on its thermal energy?","The thermal energy of a substance increases as its temperature decreases.","The thermal energy of a substance remains constant as its temperature decreases.","The thermal energy of a substance decreases as its temperature decreases.","The thermal energy of a substance has no relation to its temperature.","The thermal energy of a substance decreases as its temperature decreases.","As the temperature of a substance decreases, its thermal energy also decreases, as less heat energy is transferred to the substance.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-effect-of-03c5349c","Science G9: Analyzing information related to the movement - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-3-3 Explaining how matter and energy are transferred within an ecosystem, predicting the changes resulting from the extinction of one of its components, describing material cycles in the ecosystem, and inferring their role in supporting its sustainability.","2. Describing material cycles (water, carbon dioxide, and nitrogen) in an ecosystem and their transfer through biotic and abiotic components, and inferring their role in supporting sustainability.","What is the effect of increasing the surface area of a material on its rate of heat transfer?","The rate of heat transfer decreases as the surface area increases.","The rate of heat transfer remains constant as the surface area increases.","The rate of heat transfer increases as the surface area increases.","The rate of heat transfer has no relation to the surface area.","The rate of heat transfer increases as the surface area increases.","As the surface area of a material increases, its rate of heat transfer also increases, as more heat energy can be transferred through the increased surface area.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-term-energy-e213bcf1","Science G9: Analyzing information related to the movement - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-1-2 Analyzing information related to the movement of celestial bodies and their apparent and relative locations, and deducing the conditions prevailing in","2. Analyzing information related to the movement of celestial bodies across the sky to know their apparent and relative locations.","What is the term for the energy required to change the state of a substance from solid to liquid?","Latent heat of fusion is the energy required to change the state of a substance from solid to liquid.","Latent heat of vaporization is the energy required to change the state of a substance from liquid to gas.","Specific heat capacity is the energy required to change the temperature of a substance.","Heat capacity is the energy required to change the state of a substance.","Latent heat of fusion is the energy required to change the state of a substance from solid to liquid.","Latent heat of fusion is the energy required to change the state of a substance from solid to liquid, which is a constant value for a given substance.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-effect-of-0b94bc15","Science G9: Analyzing information related to the movement - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-4-2 Understanding the mechanism of heat transmission and conduction between objects, and measuring temperature.","2. Comparing the methods of transmission and conduction of thermal energy between objects.","What is the effect of increasing the temperature of a substance on its thermal energy?","The thermal energy of a substance decreases as its temperature increases.","The thermal energy of a substance remains constant as its temperature increases.","The thermal energy of a substance increases as its temperature increases.","The thermal energy of a substance has no relation to its temperature.","The thermal energy of a substance increases as its temperature increases.","As the temperature of a substance increases, its thermal energy also increases, as more heat energy is transferred to the substance.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-term-ability-e5d99d3d","Science G9: Analyzing information related to the movement - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-4-3Understanding specific heat and the factors affecting it.","2. Determining the factors affecting the body's absorption or loss of thermal energy.","What is the term for the ability of a material to retain heat energy?","Specific heat capacity is the ability of a material to absorb heat energy.","Thermal conductivity is the ability of a material to conduct heat energy.","Heat capacity is the ability of a material to retain heat energy.","Latent heat is the energy required to change the state of a substance.","Heat capacity is the ability of a material to retain heat energy.","Heat capacity is the ability of a material to retain heat energy, which depends on the material's mass and specific heat capacity.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-why-do-stars-1a9617c7","Science G9: Analyzing information related to the movement - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-1-2 Analyzing information related to the movement of celestial bodies and their apparent and relative locations, and deducing the conditions prevailing in","2. Analyzing information related to the movement of celestial bodies across the sky to know their apparent and relative locations.","Why do stars appear to have different colors in the sky?","Because of the Earth's rotation","Because of the Sun's gravity","Because of the atmosphere's refraction","Because of the star's temperature","Because of the star's temperature","The star's temperature causes the star to appear to have different colors in the sky.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-why-do-stars-8583977b","Science G9: Analyzing information related to the movement - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-1-2 Analyzing information related to the movement of celestial bodies and their apparent and relative locations, and deducing the conditions prevailing in","2. Analyzing information related to the movement of celestial bodies across the sky to know their apparent and relative locations.","Why do stars appear to twinkle in the sky?","Because of the Earth's rotation","Because of the Sun's gravity","Because of the atmosphere's refraction","Because of the star's size","Because of the atmosphere's refraction","The atmosphere's refraction causes the stars to appear to twinkle in the sky.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-term-apparent-3a5468b2","Science G9: Analyzing information related to the movement - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-1-2 Analyzing information related to the movement of celestial bodies and their apparent and relative locations, and deducing the conditions prevailing in","1. Describing the prevailing climatic conditions in some planets of the solar system.","What is the term for the apparent shift of a star or planet due to the Earth's rotation?","Parallax","Refraction","Aberration","Scintillation","Parallax","Parallax is the apparent shift of a star or planet due to the Earth's rotation.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-why-do-stars-0df8b5d2","Science G9: Analyzing information related to the movement - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-1-2 Analyzing information related to the movement of celestial bodies and their apparent and relative locations, and deducing the conditions prevailing in","2. Analyzing information related to the movement of celestial bodies across the sky to know their apparent and relative locations.","Why do stars appear to rise and set in the sky?","Because they are moving away from us","Because they are moving towards us","Because of the Earth's rotation","Because of the Sun's gravity","Because of the Earth's rotation","The Earth's rotation causes the stars to appear to rise and set in the sky.","/image-source/sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-why-do-stars-0df8b5d2.png","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-term-apparent-58088b16","Science G9: Analyzing information related to the movement - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-1-2 Analyzing information related to the movement of celestial bodies and their apparent and relative locations, and deducing the conditions prevailing in","2. Analyzing information related to the movement of celestial bodies across the sky to know their apparent and relative locations.","What is the term for the apparent path of a star or planet across the sky?","The ecliptic","The equator","The celestial equator","The horizon","The ecliptic","The ecliptic is the apparent path of the Sun, Moon, and planets across the sky.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-term-angle-afa7769c","Science G9: Analyzing information related to the movement - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-1-2 Analyzing information related to the movement of celestial bodies and their apparent and relative locations, and deducing the conditions prevailing in","2. Analyzing information related to the movement of celestial bodies across the sky to know their apparent and relative locations.","What is the term for the angle between the position of a star or planet and the position of the Sun?","Right ascension","Declination","Altitude","Azimuth","Right ascension","Right ascension is the angle between the position of a star or planet and the position of the Sun.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-why-do-planets-fc3fc1f9","Science G9: Analyzing information related to the movement - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-1-2 Analyzing information related to the movement of celestial bodies and their apparent and relative locations, and deducing the conditions prevailing in","2. Analyzing information related to the movement of celestial bodies across the sky to know their apparent and relative locations.","Why do planets appear to move in a retrograde motion?","Because they are moving away from us","Because they are moving towards us","Because of the Earth's rotation","Because of the Earth's orbit around the Sun","Because of the Earth's orbit around the Sun","The Earth's orbit around the Sun causes the planets to appear to move in a retrograde motion.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-why-do-planets-ba62fada","Science G9: Analyzing information related to the movement - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-1 Explaining the causes of climate change, its effects and associated","1. Determining the rates and locations of carbon in the Earth's layers and explaining the process by which it is cyclically transported.","Why do planets appear to change their brightness in the sky?","Because of the Earth's rotation","Because of the Sun's gravity","Because of the planet's size","Because of the planet's distance from the Sun","Because of the planet's distance from the Sun","The planet's distance from the Sun causes the planet to appear to change its brightness in the sky.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-term-apparent-71972bc2","Science G9: Analyzing information related to the movement - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-1-2 Analyzing information related to the movement of celestial bodies and their apparent and relative locations, and deducing the conditions prevailing in","2. Analyzing information related to the movement of celestial bodies across the sky to know their apparent and relative locations.","What is the term for the apparent path of the Sun across the sky?","The ecliptic","The equator","The celestial equator","The horizon","The ecliptic","The ecliptic is the apparent path of the Sun across the sky.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-term-distance-ffcd1c80","Science G9: Analyzing information related to the movement - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-1-2 Analyzing information related to the movement of celestial bodies and their apparent and relative locations, and deducing the conditions prevailing in","2. Analyzing information related to the movement of celestial bodies across the sky to know their apparent and relative locations.","What is the term for the distance between the Earth and the Sun?","Astronomical unit","Light year","Parsec","Kilometer","Astronomical unit","An astronomical unit is the average distance between the Earth and the Sun.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-thermal-energy-b338d596","Science G9: Clarifying the concept of thermal energy - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-4-2 Understanding the mechanism of heat transmission and conduction between objects, and measuring temperature.","2. Comparing the methods of transmission and conduction of thermal energy between objects.","What is thermal energy?","The total internal energy of a substance’s particles, including their kinetic and potential energy.","Energy transferred between objects solely by electrical current.","The energy stored only in chemical bonds.","Energy produced only by sunlight.","The total internal energy of a substance’s particles, including their kinetic and potential energy.","Thermal energy is the part of a system’s internal energy associated with the random motion and interactions of its particles. Heat is energy transferred because of a temperature difference.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-happens-to-molecules-c110403b","Science G9: Clarifying the concept of thermal energy - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-4-1 Clarifying the concept of thermal energy, its impact and its relationship to temperature.","2. Describing the relationship between thermal energy and temperature.","What happens to the molecules in a substance when it is heated?","They slow down and move closer together.","They speed up and move further apart.","They remain the same speed and distance apart.","They change direction and move in a circular motion.","They speed up and move further apart.","When a substance is heated, the molecules speed up and move further apart.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-relationship-thermal-3e0698e9","Science G9: Clarifying the concept of thermal energy - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-4-1 Clarifying the concept of thermal energy, its impact and its relationship to temperature.","2. Describing the relationship between thermal energy and temperature.","How are work and thermal energy related?","Work can transfer energy into or out of a system, changing its thermal energy.","Work and thermal energy are unrelated quantities.","Thermal energy can change only through electrical current.","Doing work always lowers a system’s temperature.","Work can transfer energy into or out of a system, changing its thermal energy.","Work is one mechanism for transferring energy. Work done on or by a system can change its internal and thermal energy.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-happens-to-thermal-10b0bd60","Science G9: Clarifying the concept of thermal energy - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-4-1 Clarifying the concept of thermal energy, its impact and its relationship to temperature.","2. Describing the relationship between thermal energy and temperature.","What generally happens to the temperature of a gas when it is compressed rapidly?","It increases because work is done on the gas.","It decreases because the particles stop moving.","It always remains exactly the same.","It becomes absolute zero.","It increases because work is done on the gas.","Rapid compression does work on a gas. With little time for heat to escape, its internal energy and temperature generally increase.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-an-example-39375c23","Science G9: Clarifying the concept of thermal energy - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-4-1 Clarifying the concept of thermal energy, its impact and its relationship to temperature.","2. Describing the relationship between thermal energy and temperature.","What is an example of thermal energy transfer in a real-world scenario?","A person walking across a room.","A car driving down the highway.","A pot of water boiling on a stove.","A person sitting in a chair.","A pot of water boiling on a stove.","A pot of water boiling on a stove is an example of thermal energy transfer in a real-world scenario.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-difference-thermal-901edd9c","Science G9: Clarifying the concept of thermal energy - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-4-1 Clarifying the concept of thermal energy, its impact and its relationship to temperature.","2. Describing the relationship between thermal energy and temperature.","What is the difference between thermal energy and heat?","Thermal energy is the energy that is transferred from one body to another, while heat is the energy that is stored in a substance.","Thermal energy is the energy that is stored in a substance, while heat is the energy that is transferred from one body to another.","Thermal energy and heat are the same thing.","Thermal energy is the energy that is transferred from one body to another, while heat is the energy that is transferred from one body to another due to a temperature difference.","Thermal energy is the energy that is stored in a substance, while heat is the energy that is transferred from one body to another.","Thermal energy is the energy that is stored in a substance, while heat is the energy that is transferred from one body to another due to a temperature difference.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-happens-to-thermal-9bf1ab4f","Science G9: Clarifying the concept of thermal energy - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-4-1 Clarifying the concept of thermal energy, its impact and its relationship to temperature.","2. Describing the relationship between thermal energy and temperature.","What happens to the thermal energy of a substance when it is cooled?","It increases and the substance gets hotter.","It decreases and the substance gets colder.","It remains the same and the substance stays the same temperature.","It increases and the substance gets colder.","It decreases and the substance gets colder.","When a substance is cooled, its thermal energy decreases and it gets colder.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-an-example-125a77c6","Science G9: Clarifying the concept of thermal energy - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-4-1 Clarifying the concept of thermal energy, its impact and its relationship to temperature.","2. Describing the relationship between thermal energy and temperature.","What is an example of thermal energy in a natural phenomenon?","A rainbow appearing in the sky.","A thunderstorm forming over a lake.","A river flowing through a valley.","A mountain range forming over millions of years.","A thunderstorm forming over a lake.","A thunderstorm forming over a lake is an example of thermal energy in a natural phenomenon.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-relationship-thermal-961b5be9","Science G9: Clarifying the concept of thermal energy - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-4-1 Clarifying the concept of thermal energy, its impact and its relationship to temperature.","2. Describing the relationship between thermal energy and temperature.","What is the relationship between thermal energy and temperature?","Thermal energy increases as temperature decreases.","Thermal energy decreases as temperature increases.","Thermal energy increases as temperature increases.","Thermal energy remains the same regardless of temperature.","Thermal energy increases as temperature increases.","Thermal energy increases as temperature increases.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-an-example-9e2cd3b6","Science G9: Clarifying the concept of thermal energy - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-4-1 Clarifying the concept of thermal energy, its impact and its relationship to temperature.","1. Explaining the concept of thermal energy. Giving examples of thermal energy and its effects on his daily life","What is an example of thermal energy in everyday life?","A car engine running on gasoline.","A light bulb turning on when you flip a switch.","A cup of hot coffee on a cold day.","A book sitting on a shelf.","A cup of hot coffee on a cold day.","A cup of hot coffee on a cold day is an example of thermal energy in everyday life.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-relationship-thermal-41a4a46c","Science G9: Clarifying the concept of thermal energy - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-4-1 Clarifying the concept of thermal energy, its impact and its relationship to temperature.","1. Explaining the concept of thermal energy. Giving examples of thermal energy and its effects on his daily life","What is the relationship between thermal energy and the temperature of a substance in thermal equilibrium?","Thermal energy is directly proportional to temperature.","Thermal energy is inversely proportional to temperature.","Thermal energy is independent of temperature.","Temperature has no effect on thermal energy.","Thermal energy is directly proportional to temperature.","In thermal equilibrium, thermal energy is directly proportional to temperature, as an increase in temperature results in an increase in thermal energy.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-thermal-energy-23f402b3","Science G9: Clarifying the concept of thermal energy - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-4-1 Clarifying the concept of thermal energy, its impact and its relationship to temperature.","2. Describing the relationship between thermal energy and temperature.","Is thermal energy a measure of the potential energy of a substance?","Yes, thermal energy is a measure of potential energy.","No, thermal energy is not a measure of potential energy.","Thermal energy is related to potential energy, but not a direct measure.","Potential energy has no relation to thermal energy.","No, thermal energy is not a measure of potential energy.","Thermal energy is not a direct measure of potential energy, but rather a measure of the total internal kinetic energy of the particles in a substance.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-thermal-energy-depend-d20b4b99","Science G9: Clarifying the concept of thermal energy - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-4-1 Clarifying the concept of thermal energy, its impact and its relationship to temperature.","1. Explaining the concept of thermal energy. Giving examples of thermal energy and its effects on his daily life","Does thermal energy depend on the specific heat capacity of a substance?","Yes, thermal energy depends on the specific heat capacity of a substance.","No, thermal energy does not depend on the specific heat capacity of a substance.","Thermal energy is independent of specific heat capacity.","Specific heat capacity has no effect on thermal energy.","No, thermal energy does not depend on the specific heat capacity of a substance.","Thermal energy is independent of the specific heat capacity of a substance, as it depends only on the temperature and mass.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-thermal-energy-63cafa04","Science G9: Clarifying the concept of thermal energy - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-4-1 Clarifying the concept of thermal energy, its impact and its relationship to temperature.","2. Describing the relationship between thermal energy and temperature.","Is thermal energy a form of kinetic energy?","Yes, thermal energy is a form of kinetic energy.","No, thermal energy is not a form of kinetic energy.","Thermal energy is related to kinetic energy, but not the same.","Kinetic energy has no relation to thermal energy.","Thermal energy is related to kinetic energy, but not the same.","Thermal energy includes energy associated with particle motion and interactions; it is related to, but not simply identical to, kinetic energy.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-happens-to-thermal-1935a8ba","Science G9: Clarifying the concept of thermal energy - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-4-1 Clarifying the concept of thermal energy, its impact and its relationship to temperature.","2. Describing the relationship between thermal energy and temperature.","What happens to the thermal energy of a substance when it is cooled?","The thermal energy increases.","The thermal energy remains the same.","The thermal energy decreases.","The thermal energy becomes negative.","The thermal energy decreases.","When a substance is cooled, the particles lose kinetic energy, resulting in a decrease in thermal energy.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-thermal-energy-90ee7031","Science G9: Clarifying the concept of thermal energy - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-4-1 Clarifying the concept of thermal energy, its impact and its relationship to temperature.","2. Describing the relationship between thermal energy and temperature.","Is thermal energy a measure of the temperature of a substance?","Yes, thermal energy is a measure of temperature.","No, thermal energy is not a measure of temperature.","Thermal energy is related to temperature, but not a direct measure.","Temperature has no relation to thermal energy.","No, thermal energy is not a measure of temperature.","Thermal energy is not a direct measure of temperature, but rather a measure of the total internal kinetic energy of the particles in a substance.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-thermal-energy-depend-98696257","Science G9: Clarifying the concept of thermal energy - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-4-1 Clarifying the concept of thermal energy, its impact and its relationship to temperature.","1. Explaining the concept of thermal energy. Giving examples of thermal energy and its effects on his daily life","Does thermal energy depend on the mass of a substance?","Yes, thermal energy depends on the mass of a substance.","No, thermal energy does not depend on the mass of a substance.","Thermal energy is independent of mass.","Mass has no effect on thermal energy.","No, thermal energy does not depend on the mass of a substance.","Thermal energy is independent of the mass of a substance, as it depends only on the temperature and specific heat capacity.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-relationship-thermal-c16866ce","Science G9: Clarifying the concept of thermal energy - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-4-1 Clarifying the concept of thermal energy, its impact and its relationship to temperature.","1. Explaining the concept of thermal energy. Giving examples of thermal energy and its effects on his daily life","What is the relationship between thermal energy and the motion of particles in a substance?","The motion of particles has no effect on thermal energy.","The motion of particles increases thermal energy.","The motion of particles decreases thermal energy.","The motion of particles is unrelated to thermal energy.","The motion of particles increases thermal energy.","Faster average particle motion means greater average kinetic energy and therefore greater thermal energy.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-thermal-energy-5596be94","Science G9: Clarifying the concept of thermal energy - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-4-1 Clarifying the concept of thermal energy, its impact and its relationship to temperature.","1. Explaining the concept of thermal energy. Giving examples of thermal energy and its effects on his daily life","Is thermal energy directly proportional to temperature?","Yes, thermal energy is directly proportional to temperature.","No, thermal energy is inversely proportional to temperature.","Thermal energy is not related to temperature.","Temperature has no effect on thermal energy.","Yes, thermal energy is directly proportional to temperature.","Thermal energy is directly proportional to temperature, as an increase in temperature results in an increase in thermal energy.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-happens-to-thermal-b62fdf18","Science G9: Clarifying the concept of thermal energy - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-4-1 Clarifying the concept of thermal energy, its impact and its relationship to temperature.","2. Describing the relationship between thermal energy and temperature.","What happens to the thermal energy of a substance when its temperature increases?","The thermal energy remains the same.","The thermal energy increases.","The thermal energy decreases.","The thermal energy becomes negative.","The thermal energy increases.","When a substance's temperature increases, the particles gain kinetic energy, resulting in an increase in thermal energy.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-thermal-energy-cf278c8b","Science G9: Clarifying the concept of thermal energy - Set 2 T1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-4-1 Clarifying the concept of thermal energy, its impact and its relationship to temperature.","2. Describing the relationship between thermal energy and temperature.","What is thermal energy?","The energy an object has due to its motion","The energy an object has due to its temperature","The total kinetic and potential energy of the particles in an object","The energy an object has due to its electrical charge","The total kinetic and potential energy of the particles in an object","Thermal energy is the total energy of the particles in an object, which includes both kinetic energy (the energy of motion) and potential energy (stored energy).","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-how-thermal-energy-6ff30208","Science G9: Clarifying the concept of thermal energy - Set 2 T1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-4-1 Clarifying the concept of thermal energy, its impact and its relationship to temperature.","2. Describing the relationship between thermal energy and temperature.","How does the thermal energy of an object change when its temperature is held constant?","The thermal energy increases","The thermal energy decreases","The thermal energy stays the same","The thermal energy becomes zero","The thermal energy stays the same","If the temperature of an object is held constant, the average kinetic energy of the particles remains the same, which means the thermal energy of the object also remains the same.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-happens-to-particles-718f63cc","Science G9: Clarifying the concept of thermal energy - Set 2 T1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-4-1 Clarifying the concept of thermal energy, its impact and its relationship to temperature.","2. Describing the relationship between thermal energy and temperature.","What happens to the particles in an object when its thermal energy increases?","The particles slow down and lose kinetic energy","The particles stay the same and do not change","The particles speed up and gain kinetic energy","The particles come to a complete stop","The particles speed up and gain kinetic energy","When the thermal energy of an object increases, the particles in the object gain kinetic energy and start moving faster, which is a direct result of the increased thermal energy.","/image-source/sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-happens-to-particles-718f63cc.png","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-of-objects-has-040694b5","Science G9: Clarifying the concept of thermal energy - Set 2 T1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-4-1 Clarifying the concept of thermal energy, its impact and its relationship to temperature.","2. Describing the relationship between thermal energy and temperature.","Which of the following objects has the highest thermal energy?","A cup of hot coffee","A cup of cold water","A block of ice","A frozen pizza","A cup of hot coffee","The cup of hot coffee has the highest thermal energy because it has the highest temperature, which means the particles in the coffee have the most kinetic energy.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-difference-thermal-8962499a","Science G9: Clarifying the concept of thermal energy - Set 2 T1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-4-1 Clarifying the concept of thermal energy, its impact and its relationship to temperature.","2. Describing the relationship between thermal energy and temperature.","What is the difference between thermal energy and temperature?","Thermal energy is the average kinetic energy of particles, while temperature is the total energy of the particles","Thermal energy is the total energy of the particles, while temperature is the average kinetic energy of the particles","Thermal energy and temperature are the same thing","Thermal energy is the energy of motion, while temperature is the energy of position","Thermal energy is the total energy of the particles, while temperature is the average kinetic energy of the particles","Thermal energy is the total energy of the particles in an object, which includes both kinetic energy (the energy of motion) and potential energy (stored energy), while temperature is a measure of the average kinetic energy of the particles.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-how-thermal-energy-f8eb8799","Science G9: Clarifying the concept of thermal energy - Set 2 T1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-4-1 Clarifying the concept of thermal energy, its impact and its relationship to temperature.","2. Describing the relationship between thermal energy and temperature.","How does the thermal energy of an object change when it is placed in a cold environment?","The thermal energy increases","The thermal energy stays the same","The thermal energy decreases","The thermal energy becomes zero","The thermal energy decreases","When an object is placed in a cold environment, energy is transferred away from the object, which decreases the kinetic energy of the particles and therefore decreases the object's thermal energy.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-relationship-temperature-bb646911","Science G9: Clarifying the concept of thermal energy - Set 2 T1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-4-1 Clarifying the concept of thermal energy, its impact and its relationship to temperature.","2. Describing the relationship between thermal energy and temperature.","What is the relationship between the temperature of an object and the motion of its particles?","As temperature increases, particle motion decreases","As temperature decreases, particle motion increases","As temperature increases, particle motion increases","Particle motion is not related to temperature","As temperature increases, particle motion increases","As the temperature of an object increases, the particles in the object gain kinetic energy and start moving faster, which is a direct relationship between temperature and particle motion.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-of-is-an-955bc89c","Science G9: Clarifying the concept of thermal energy - Set 2 T1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-4-1 Clarifying the concept of thermal energy, its impact and its relationship to temperature.","2. Describing the relationship between thermal energy and temperature.","Which of the following is an example of thermal energy transfer?","A book falling off a table","A hot cup of coffee warming a cold spoon","A balloon filling with air","A car moving down the road","A hot cup of coffee warming a cold spoon","Thermal energy transfer occurs when there is a temperature difference between two objects, causing energy to flow from the hotter object to the cooler object, which is what happens when a hot cup of coffee warms a cold spoon.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-happens-to-thermal-a0f2fbb0","Science G9: Clarifying the concept of thermal energy - Set 2 T1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-4-2 Understanding the mechanism of heat transmission and conduction between objects, and measuring temperature.","2. Comparing the methods of transmission and conduction of thermal energy between objects.","What happens to the thermal energy of an object when it is heated?","The thermal energy decreases","The thermal energy stays the same","The thermal energy increases","The thermal energy becomes zero","The thermal energy increases","When an object is heated, energy is transferred to the object, which increases the kinetic energy of the particles and therefore increases the object's thermal energy.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-how-temperature-of-698da97f","Science G9: Clarifying the concept of thermal energy - Set 2 T1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-4-1 Clarifying the concept of thermal energy, its impact and its relationship to temperature.","1. Explaining the concept of thermal energy. Giving examples of thermal energy and its effects on his daily life","How does the temperature of an object relate to its thermal energy?","As temperature increases, thermal energy decreases","As temperature decreases, thermal energy increases","As temperature increases, thermal energy increases","Temperature has no effect on thermal energy","As temperature increases, thermal energy increases","As the temperature of an object increases, the particles in the object gain kinetic energy and start moving faster, which increases the object's thermal energy.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-of-is-an-f11f62db","Science G9: Comparing between compounds and mixtures, classifying - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-2 Comparing between compounds and mixtures, classifying mixtures, suggesting appropriate methods to separate their components, and distinguishing between types of solutions and their components.","1. Comparing compounds and mixtures according to their chemical and physical properties.","Which of the following is an example of a colloid?","Saltwater","Milk","Oil and water","Sand and water","Milk","Milk is an example of a colloid because it is a heterogeneous mixture that has particles that are not uniformly distributed.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-difference-a-8dea7b3f","Science G9: Comparing between compounds and mixtures, classifying - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-2 Comparing between compounds and mixtures, classifying mixtures, suggesting appropriate methods to separate their components, and distinguishing between types of solutions and their components.","2. Classifying homogeneous and heterogeneous mixtures according to the nature of their components.","What is the difference between a solution and a mixture?","A solution is a type of mixture, while a mixture is a type of solution","A solution is a homogeneous mixture, while a mixture can be either homogeneous or heterogeneous","A solution is a type of compound, while a mixture is a type of element","A solution is a type of element, while a mixture is a type of compound","A solution is a homogeneous mixture, while a mixture can be either homogeneous or heterogeneous","A solution is a homogeneous mixture, while a mixture can be either homogeneous or heterogeneous.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-main-difference-54a7af43","Science G9: Comparing between compounds and mixtures, classifying - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-2 Comparing between compounds and mixtures, classifying mixtures, suggesting appropriate methods to separate their components, and distinguishing between types of solutions and their components.","2. Classifying homogeneous and heterogeneous mixtures according to the nature of their components.","What is the main difference between a homogeneous and a heterogeneous mixture?","A homogeneous mixture has the same composition throughout, while a heterogeneous mixture has different components that are not uniformly distributed","A homogeneous mixture has different components that are not uniformly distributed, while a heterogeneous mixture has the same composition throughout","A homogeneous mixture is a type of element, while a heterogeneous mixture is a type of compound","A homogeneous mixture is a type of compound, while a heterogeneous mixture is a type of element","A homogeneous mixture has the same composition throughout, while a heterogeneous mixture has different components that are not uniformly distributed","A homogeneous mixture has the same composition throughout, while a heterogeneous mixture has different components that are not uniformly distributed.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-type-of-mixture-009b79a6","Science G9: Comparing between compounds and mixtures, classifying - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-2 Comparing between compounds and mixtures, classifying mixtures, suggesting appropriate methods to separate their components, and distinguishing between types of solutions and their components.","2. Classifying homogeneous and heterogeneous mixtures according to the nature of their components.","What type of mixture is air?","A homogeneous mixture","A heterogeneous mixture","A compound","An element","A homogeneous mixture","Air is a homogeneous mixture because it has the same composition throughout, even though it is made up of different gases.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-of-is-an-14076648","Science G9: Comparing between compounds and mixtures, classifying - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-2 Comparing between compounds and mixtures, classifying mixtures, suggesting appropriate methods to separate their components, and distinguishing between types of solutions and their components.","1. Comparing compounds and mixtures according to their chemical and physical properties.","Which of the following is an example of a homogeneous mixture?","Saltwater","Oil and water","Sand and water","Mud and water","Saltwater","Saltwater is an example of a homogeneous mixture because it has the same composition throughout.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-purpose-of-8011fdbd","Science G9: Comparing between compounds and mixtures, classifying - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-2 Comparing between compounds and mixtures, classifying mixtures, suggesting appropriate methods to separate their components, and distinguishing between types of solutions and their components.","2. Classifying homogeneous and heterogeneous mixtures according to the nature of their components.","What is the purpose of classifying mixtures as homogeneous or heterogeneous?","To determine the type of solution","To determine the type of compound","To determine the appropriate method of separation","To determine the type of element","To determine the appropriate method of separation","The purpose of classifying mixtures as homogeneous or heterogeneous is to determine the appropriate method of separation.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-of-is-an-132a3a00","Science G9: Comparing between compounds and mixtures, classifying - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-2 Comparing between compounds and mixtures, classifying mixtures, suggesting appropriate methods to separate their components, and distinguishing between types of solutions and their components.","3. Identifying the component of the solution, and factorsaffecting it, distinguishing different types of solutions from everyday life, defines aqueous solutions, and explains why water is considered a universal solvent","Which of the following is an example of a heterogeneous mixture?","Sugar and water","Oil and water","Salt and water","Milk and water","Oil and water","Oil and water is an example of a heterogeneous mixture because it has different components that are not uniformly distributed.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-of-is-an-b97c697e","Science G9: Comparing between compounds and mixtures, classifying - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-2 Comparing between compounds and mixtures, classifying mixtures, suggesting appropriate methods to separate their components, and distinguishing between types of solutions and their components.","1. Comparing compounds and mixtures according to their chemical and physical properties.","Which of the following is an example of a heterogeneous mixture that can be separated by filtration?","Saltwater","Oil and water","Sand and water","Mud and water","Sand and water","Sand and water is an example of a heterogeneous mixture that can be separated by filtration.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-difference-a-b0f079d3","Science G9: Comparing between compounds and mixtures, classifying - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-2 Comparing between compounds and mixtures, classifying mixtures, suggesting appropriate methods to separate their components, and distinguishing between types of solutions and their components.","2. Classifying homogeneous and heterogeneous mixtures according to the nature of their components.","What is the difference between a colloid and a solution?","A colloid is a type of solution, while a solution is a type of colloid","A colloid is a heterogeneous mixture, while a solution is a homogeneous mixture","A colloid is a type of compound, while a solution is a type of element","A colloid is a type of element, while a solution is a type of compound","A colloid is a heterogeneous mixture, while a solution is a homogeneous mixture","A colloid is a heterogeneous mixture, while a solution is a homogeneous mixture.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-of-methods-is-32246a64","Science G9: Comparing between compounds and mixtures, classifying - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-2 Comparing between compounds and mixtures, classifying mixtures, suggesting appropriate methods to separate their components, and distinguishing between types of solutions and their components.","2. Classifying homogeneous and heterogeneous mixtures according to the nature of their components.","Which of the following methods is used to separate the components of a heterogeneous mixture?","Distillation","Filtration","Chromatography","All of the above","All of the above","All of the above methods can be used to separate the components of a heterogeneous mixture, depending on the specific mixture and its components.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-of-materials-is-a9b2d3b1","Science G9: Comparing between different types of materials - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-3-3Comparing between different types of materials in terms of their electrical conductivity.","1. Comparing the types of materials in terms of their electrical conductivity. Providing examples of conductive and insulating materials and their uses in daily life.","Which of the following materials is a good conductor of electricity?","Glass is a good conductor of electricity","Copper is a good conductor of electricity","Wood is a good conductor of electricity","Plastic is a good conductor of electricity","Copper is a good conductor of electricity","Copper is a good conductor of electricity and is often used in electrical wiring.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-of-materials-is-50963c30","Science G9: Comparing between different types of materials - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-3-3Comparing between different types of materials in terms of their electrical conductivity.","1. Comparing the types of materials in terms of their electrical conductivity. Providing examples of conductive and insulating materials and their uses in daily life.","Which of the following materials is a good insulator of electricity?","Wood is a good conductor of electricity","Wood is a good insulator of electricity","Plastic is a good conductor of electricity","Plastic is a good insulator of electricity","Wood is a good insulator of electricity","Wood is a good insulator of electricity and is often used in electrical applications.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-type-of-materials-4dff77f8","Science G9: Comparing between different types of materials - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-3-3Comparing between different types of materials in terms of their electrical conductivity.","1. Comparing the types of materials in terms of their electrical conductivity. Providing examples of conductive and insulating materials and their uses in daily life.","What type of materials allow electricity to flow through them easily?","Materials that are good conductors of heat are also good conductors of electricity","Materials that are good insulators of heat are also good insulators of electricity","Materials that allow electricity to flow through them easily are called conductors","Materials that prevent electricity from flowing through them are called insulators","Materials that allow electricity to flow through them easily are called conductors","Conductors are materials that allow electricity to flow through them easily, such as copper wire.","/image-source/sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-type-of-materials-4dff77f8.png","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-of-materials-is-9d7f2fe1","Science G9: Comparing between different types of materials - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-3-3Comparing between different types of materials in terms of their electrical conductivity.","1. Comparing the types of materials in terms of their electrical conductivity. Providing examples of conductive and insulating materials and their uses in daily life.","Which of the following materials is not a good conductor of electricity?","Copper is a good conductor of electricity","Wood is a good conductor of electricity","Glass is a good conductor of electricity","Plastic is a good conductor of electricity","Glass is a good conductor of electricity","Glass is not a good conductor of electricity and is often used as an insulator in electrical devices.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-of-materials-is-f269e030","Science G9: Comparing between different types of materials - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-3-3Comparing between different types of materials in terms of their electrical conductivity.","1. Comparing the types of materials in terms of their electrical conductivity. Providing examples of conductive and insulating materials and their uses in daily life.","Which of the following materials is commonly used as a conductor in electrical wiring?","Glass is commonly used as a conductor in electrical wiring","Copper is commonly used as a conductor in electrical wiring","Wood is commonly used as a conductor in electrical wiring","Plastic is commonly used as a conductor in electrical wiring","Copper is commonly used as a conductor in electrical wiring","Copper is commonly used as a conductor in electrical wiring due to its high conductivity.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-happens-to-flow-c3944dd5","Science G9: Comparing between different types of materials - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-3-3Comparing between different types of materials in terms of their electrical conductivity.","1. Comparing the types of materials in terms of their electrical conductivity. Providing examples of conductive and insulating materials and their uses in daily life.","What happens to the flow of electricity when an insulator is connected to a power source?","The flow of electricity increases","The flow of electricity remains the same","The flow of electricity decreases","The flow of electricity stops","The flow of electricity stops","When an insulator is connected to a power source, the flow of electricity stops and the device does not function.","/image-source/sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-happens-to-flow-c3944dd5.png","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-of-materials-is-579db6e8","Science G9: Comparing between different types of materials - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-3-3Comparing between different types of materials in terms of their electrical conductivity.","1. Comparing the types of materials in terms of their electrical conductivity. Providing examples of conductive and insulating materials and their uses in daily life.","Which of the following materials is a good insulator of heat?","Wood is a good conductor of heat","Wood is a good insulator of heat","Plastic is a good conductor of heat","Plastic is a good insulator of heat","Wood is a good insulator of heat","Wood is a good insulator of heat and is often used in building construction.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-purpose-of-c9c35568","Science G9: Comparing between different types of materials - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-3-3Comparing between different types of materials in terms of their electrical conductivity.","1. Comparing the types of materials in terms of their electrical conductivity. Providing examples of conductive and insulating materials and their uses in daily life.","What is the purpose of using conductive materials in electrical devices?","To increase the heat generated by electricity","To decrease the flow of electricity","To allow electricity to flow through them easily","To prevent electrical shock","To allow electricity to flow through them easily","Conductive materials are used to allow electricity to flow through them easily and to ensure proper functioning of electrical devices.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-happens-to-flow-afdea8b6","Science G9: Comparing between different types of materials - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-3-3Comparing between different types of materials in terms of their electrical conductivity.","1. Comparing the types of materials in terms of their electrical conductivity. Providing examples of conductive and insulating materials and their uses in daily life.","What happens to the flow of electricity when a conductor is connected to a power source?","The flow of electricity decreases","The flow of electricity remains the same","The flow of electricity increases","The flow of electricity stops","The flow of electricity increases","When a conductor is connected to a power source, the flow of electricity increases and allows the device to function.","/image-source/sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-happens-to-flow-afdea8b6.png","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-purpose-of-657d51c4","Science G9: Comparing between different types of materials - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-3-3Comparing between different types of materials in terms of their electrical conductivity.","1. Comparing the types of materials in terms of their electrical conductivity. Providing examples of conductive and insulating materials and their uses in daily life.","What is the purpose of using insulating materials in electrical devices?","To increase the flow of electricity","To decrease the flow of electricity","To prevent electrical shock","To increase the heat generated by electricity","To prevent electrical shock","Insulating materials are used to prevent electrical shock and to ensure safe handling of electrical devices.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-of-best-describes-9754bbeb","Science G9: Comparing between different types of materials - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-3-4 Interpreting the relationship between magnets and electric current and their role in designing devices that convert electrical energy into mechanical energy and vice versa.","2. listing its applied uses, and showing how an electric current generates a magnetic field.","Which of the following best describes the relationship between electric current and magnetic field in terms of energy transfer?","Electric current generates magnetic field, and magnetic field generates electric current","Electric current is converted into magnetic field energy","Magnetic field is converted into electric current energy","Electric current and magnetic field are unrelated in terms of energy transfer","Electric current is converted into magnetic field energy","When electric current flows through a wire, some of the electric energy is converted into magnetic field energy, illustrating the relationship between electric current and magnetic field in terms of energy transfer.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-effect-of-5d37b1a9","Science G9: Comparing between different types of materials - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-3-3Comparing between different types of materials in terms of their electrical conductivity.","1. Explaining the relationship between the electric current and the magnetic field, and deducing the factors that control it.","What is the effect of increasing the number of turns in a coil of wire on the magnetic field it produces when a current flows through it?","The magnetic field becomes weaker","The magnetic field remains the same","The magnetic field becomes stronger","The magnetic field direction changes","The magnetic field becomes stronger","Increasing the number of turns in a coil of wire increases the strength of the magnetic field it produces when a current flows through it, because each turn of the coil contributes to the overall magnetic field.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-happens-to-magnetic-526e1f7c","Science G9: Comparing between different types of materials - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-3-3Comparing between different types of materials in terms of their electrical conductivity.","1. Explaining the relationship between the electric current and the magnetic field, and deducing the factors that control it.","What happens to the magnetic field around a wire when the electric current flowing through it increases?","The magnetic field becomes weaker and less dense","The magnetic field remains the same and is unaffected by the current","The magnetic field becomes stronger and more dense","The magnetic field disappears completely","The magnetic field becomes stronger and more dense","As the electric current flowing through a wire increases, the magnetic field around the wire also becomes stronger and more dense due to the direct relationship between electric current and magnetic field strength.","/image-source/sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-happens-to-magnetic-526e1f7c.png","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-of-materials-is-6860d489","Science G9: Comparing between different types of materials - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-3-3Comparing between different types of materials in terms of their electrical conductivity.","1. Explaining the relationship between the electric current and the magnetic field, and deducing the factors that control it.","Which material is best for carrying electric current in a wire?","Copper","Rubber","Glass","Dry wood","Copper","Copper is a good electrical conductor, whereas rubber, glass, and dry wood are electrical insulators.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-relationship-direction-e1a4a302","Science G9: Comparing between different types of materials - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-3-3Comparing between different types of materials in terms of their electrical conductivity.","1. Explaining the relationship between the electric current and the magnetic field, and deducing the factors that control it.","What is the relationship between the direction of the electric current in a wire and the direction of the magnetic field it produces?","The magnetic field is in the same direction as the electric current","The magnetic field is in the opposite direction to the electric current","The magnetic field is perpendicular to the electric current","The magnetic field is parallel to the electric current but has no specific direction","The magnetic field is perpendicular to the electric current","The magnetic field produced by an electric current in a wire is perpendicular to the direction of the current, as described by the right-hand rule.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-factor-not-affect-3504ff18","Science G9: Comparing between different types of materials - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-3-3Comparing between different types of materials in terms of their electrical conductivity.","1. Explaining the relationship between the electric current and the magnetic field, and deducing the factors that control it.","Which factor does NOT affect the strength of the magnetic field produced by a current-carrying wire?","The material the wire is made of","The temperature of the wire","The direction of the electric current","The presence of other magnetic fields nearby","The presence of other magnetic fields nearby","The presence of other magnetic fields nearby can affect the overall magnetic field but does not directly affect the strength of the magnetic field produced by the current-carrying wire itself.","/image-source/sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-factor-not-affect-3504ff18.png","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-happens-to-magnetic-b549f975","Science G9: Comparing between different types of materials - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-3-3Comparing between different types of materials in terms of their electrical conductivity.","1. Explaining the relationship between the electric current and the magnetic field, and deducing the factors that control it.","What happens to the magnetic field around a coil of wire when the electric current flowing through it is reversed?","The magnetic field becomes stronger","The magnetic field becomes weaker","The magnetic field remains the same","The magnetic field reverses its direction","The magnetic field reverses its direction","Reversing the direction of the electric current in a coil of wire causes the magnetic field to reverse its direction as well, due to the direct relationship between the current direction and the magnetic field polarity.","/image-source/sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-happens-to-magnetic-b549f975.png","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-of-is-an-21d3078e","Science G9: Comparing between different types of materials - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-3-3Comparing between different types of materials in terms of their electrical conductivity.","1. Explaining the relationship between the electric current and the magnetic field, and deducing the factors that control it.","Which of the following is an example of a material that is a poor conductor of electricity and would produce a very weak magnetic field when a current flows through it?","Aluminum foil","Copper wire","Wooden plank","Metal pipe","Wooden plank","Wood is a poor conductor of electricity, meaning it has high resistance to electric current, and thus would produce a very weak magnetic field when a current flows through it.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-purpose-of-72d49ffa","Science G9: Comparing between different types of materials - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-3-3Comparing between different types of materials in terms of their electrical conductivity.","1. Explaining the relationship between the electric current and the magnetic field, and deducing the factors that control it.","What is the purpose of using a ferromagnetic core in an electromagnet?","To increase the resistance of the coil","To decrease the electric current flowing through the coil","To increase the strength of the magnetic field produced","To reduce the size of the electromagnet","To increase the strength of the magnetic field produced","A ferromagnetic core, such as iron, increases the strength of the magnetic field produced by an electromagnet because it can easily magnetize and thus concentrate the magnetic field lines.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-how-distance-wire-2ddb4278","Science G9: Comparing between different types of materials - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-3-3Comparing between different types of materials in terms of their electrical conductivity.","1. Explaining the relationship between the electric current and the magnetic field, and deducing the factors that control it.","How does the distance from the wire affect the strength of the magnetic field produced by a current-carrying wire?","The magnetic field strength increases with distance from the wire","The magnetic field strength remains constant regardless of distance","The magnetic field strength decreases with distance from the wire","The magnetic field strength becomes zero at a certain distance","The magnetic field strength decreases with distance from the wire","The strength of the magnetic field produced by a current-carrying wire decreases with increasing distance from the wire, following the inverse square law.","/image-source/sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-how-distance-wire-2ddb4278.png","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-main-advantage-56747b01","Science G9: Describing some of the methods - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-1-1 Describing some of the methods, techniques and tools used in exploring the","1. Explaining the means, techniques and tools used in space exploration and listing the most important space flights.","What is the main advantage of using space probes to explore the universe?","Space probes can travel faster than light, allowing for quicker exploration.","They can withstand extreme temperatures and conditions.","Space probes can collect data and send it back to Earth.","They are the only way to explore the universe without human intervention.","Space probes can collect data and send it back to Earth.","Space probes can collect data and send it back to Earth, allowing scientists to study the universe without being physically present.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-main-advantage-95c86d91","Science G9: Describing some of the methods - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-1-1 Describing some of the methods, techniques and tools used in exploring the","1. Explaining the means, techniques and tools used in space exploration and listing the most important space flights.","What is the main advantage of using computer simulations to study complex systems?","Computer simulations allow us to study complex systems in a controlled environment.","They provide high-resolution images of complex systems.","Computer simulations help us understand the Earth's internal structure.","They are the only way to study complex systems without being physically present.","Computer simulations allow us to study complex systems in a controlled environment.","Computer simulations allow us to study complex systems in a controlled environment, allowing scientists to test hypotheses and understand the underlying principles of complex phenomena.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-primary-method-133165cc","Science G9: Describing some of the methods - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-2-1 Classifying living organisms according to modern classification system based on their features and characteristics.","2. Comparing the main characteristics of living organisms.","What is the primary method used by ecologists to study the interactions between living organisms and their environment?","Surveys involve collecting data from a large number of people.","Experiments involve manipulating variables to test hypotheses.","Observations involve watching and recording the behavior of living organisms.","Classifications involve grouping living organisms based on their characteristics.","Observations involve watching and recording the behavior of living organisms.","Observations involve watching and recording the behavior of living organisms, allowing ecologists to understand the interactions between living organisms and their environment.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-primary-tool-2673ebcf","Science G9: Describing some of the methods - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-1-1 Describing some of the methods, techniques and tools used in exploring the","1. Explaining the means, techniques and tools used in space exploration and listing the most important space flights.","What is the primary tool used by geologists to study the Earth's surface?","Seismographs help us understand the Earth's internal structure.","Thermometers measure the temperature of the Earth's surface.","Topographic maps show the shape and features of the Earth's surface.","Barometers measure the air pressure on the Earth's surface.","Topographic maps show the shape and features of the Earth's surface.","Topographic maps show the shape and features of the Earth's surface, allowing geologists to understand the geological history and processes that have shaped the planet.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-main-advantage-70201534","Science G9: Describing some of the methods - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-1-1 Describing some of the methods, techniques and tools used in exploring the","1. Explaining the means, techniques and tools used in space exploration and listing the most important space flights.","What is the main advantage of using satellite imagery to study the Earth?","Satellite imagery provides high-resolution images of the Earth's surface.","It allows us to study the Earth's surface in real-time.","Satellite imagery helps us understand the Earth's internal structure.","It is the only way to study the Earth's surface without being physically present.","Satellite imagery provides high-resolution images of the Earth's surface.","Satellite imagery provides high-resolution images of the Earth's surface, allowing scientists to study the planet's features and changes in detail.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-primary-method-09d025b1","Science G9: Describing some of the methods - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-2-1 Classifying living organisms according to modern classification system based on their features and characteristics.","2. Comparing the main characteristics of living organisms.","What is the primary method used by biologists to study the behavior of living organisms?","Observations involve watching and recording the behavior of living organisms.","Experiments involve manipulating variables to test hypotheses.","Surveys involve collecting data from a large number of people.","Classifications involve grouping living organisms based on their characteristics.","Observations involve watching and recording the behavior of living organisms.","Observations involve watching and recording the behavior of living organisms, allowing biologists to understand their natural behavior and habits.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-main-advantage-192e8be7","Science G9: Describing some of the methods - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-1-1 Describing some of the methods, techniques and tools used in exploring the","1. Explaining the means, techniques and tools used in space exploration and listing the most important space flights.","What is the main advantage of using laboratory experiments to study the natural world?","Laboratory experiments allow us to study the natural world in a controlled environment.","They provide high-resolution images of the natural world.","Laboratory experiments help us understand the Earth's internal structure.","They are the only way to study the natural world without being physically present.","Laboratory experiments allow us to study the natural world in a controlled environment.","Laboratory experiments allow us to study the natural world in a controlled environment, allowing scientists to test hypotheses and understand the underlying principles of natural phenomena.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-primary-method-f3155f1e","Science G9: Describing some of the methods - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-1-1 Describing some of the methods, techniques and tools used in exploring the","3. Organizing and analyzing the data and information available on the vastness and magnitude of the universe, on the number of galaxies, their cosmic distances and paths, to provide evidence for them.","What is the primary method used by astronomers to study distant stars and galaxies?","Telescopes allow us to see distant objects in the universe.","Microscopes are used to study the structure of stars.","Binoculars are used for observing the moon.","Spectroscopes help us understand the composition of celestial objects.","Spectroscopes help us understand the composition of celestial objects.","Spectroscopes are used to analyze the light coming from distant stars and galaxies, allowing us to determine their composition and properties.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-main-advantage-fd7adf97","Science G9: Describing some of the methods - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-1-1 Describing some of the methods, techniques and tools used in exploring the","1. Explaining the means, techniques and tools used in space exploration and listing the most important space flights.","What is the main advantage of using remote sensing to study the Earth's surface?","Remote sensing allows us to study the Earth's surface in real-time.","It provides high-resolution images of the Earth's surface.","Remote sensing helps us understand the Earth's internal structure.","It is the only way to study the Earth's surface without being physically present.","It provides high-resolution images of the Earth's surface.","Remote sensing provides high-resolution images of the Earth's surface, allowing scientists to study the planet's features and changes in detail.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-primary-tool-c279e1bc","Science G9: Describing some of the methods - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-1-1 Describing some of the methods, techniques and tools used in exploring the","1. Explaining the means, techniques and tools used in space exploration and listing the most important space flights.","What is the primary tool used by meteorologists to study the weather?","Weather stations measure temperature and humidity.","Radar systems track the movement of weather systems.","Weather satellites provide images of the Earth's atmosphere.","Barometers measure air pressure.","Radar systems track the movement of weather systems.","Radar systems track the movement of weather systems, allowing meteorologists to predict weather patterns and forecast the weather.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-term-process-ee958c4e","Science G9: Describing some of the methods, techniques - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-1-1 Describing some of the methods, techniques and tools used in exploring the","1. Explaining the means, techniques and tools used in space exploration and listing the most important space flights.","What is the term for the process of launching a spacecraft into orbit around the Earth?","Spacecraft deployment","Orbital insertion","Launch and deployment","Spacecraft injection","Orbital insertion","Orbital insertion is the process of placing a spacecraft into orbit around the Earth, typically involving a series of complex maneuvers.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-of-space-agencies-d69f3c92","Science G9: Describing some of the methods, techniques - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-1-1 Describing some of the methods, techniques and tools used in exploring the","1. Explaining the means, techniques and tools used in space exploration and listing the most important space flights.","Which of the following space agencies was responsible for the International Space Station?","European Space Agency","National Aeronautics and Space Administration (NASA)","Russian Federal Space Agency","China National Space Administration","National Aeronautics and Space Administration (NASA)","The International Space Station is a collaborative project between multiple space agencies, including NASA, with the primary goal of conducting scientific research in microgravity.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-primary-purpose-3576e806","Science G9: Describing some of the methods, techniques - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-1-1 Describing some of the methods, techniques and tools used in exploring the","1. Explaining the means, techniques and tools used in space exploration and listing the most important space flights.","What is the primary purpose of a spacecraft's life support system?","To generate electricity for the spacecraft's systems","To provide a safe and stable environment for astronauts","To collect and analyze data from celestial bodies","To recycle air and water for the astronauts","To recycle air and water for the astronauts","A spacecraft's life support system is responsible for recycling air and water for the astronauts, as well as maintaining a safe and stable environment.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-of-spacecraft-is-38c9c56a","Science G9: Describing some of the methods, techniques - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-1-1 Describing some of the methods, techniques and tools used in exploring the","1. Explaining the means, techniques and tools used in space exploration and listing the most important space flights.","Which of the following spacecraft is known for being the first to travel to Pluto?","New Horizons","Voyager 1","Pioneer 10","Cassini-Huygens","New Horizons","The New Horizons spacecraft successfully flew by Pluto in 2015, providing the first close-up images and data about the dwarf planet.","/image-source/sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-of-spacecraft-is-38c9c56a.png","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-term-study-3cd0eaee","Science G9: Describing some of the methods, techniques - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-1-2 Analyzing information related to the movement of celestial bodies and their apparent and relative locations, and deducing the conditions prevailing in","1. Describing the prevailing climatic conditions in some planets of the solar system.","What is the term for the study of the movement of celestial bodies through space?","Astrodynamics","Spacecraft navigation","Planetary science","Astronomy","Astrodynamics","Astrodynamics is the study of the movement of celestial bodies through space, including the orbits of planets, moons, and spacecraft.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-of-space-missions-1670322c","Science G9: Describing some of the methods, techniques - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-1-1 Describing some of the methods, techniques and tools used in exploring the","1. Explaining the means, techniques and tools used in space exploration and listing the most important space flights.","Which of the following space missions was designed to study the Sun?","Voyager 1","Pioneer 10","Solar Dynamics Observatory","International Space Station","Solar Dynamics Observatory","The Solar Dynamics Observatory is a spacecraft designed to study the Sun's behavior and activity, providing valuable insights into solar physics.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-primary-function-f73f536f","Science G9: Describing some of the methods, techniques - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-1-1 Describing some of the methods, techniques and tools used in exploring the","1. Explaining the means, techniques and tools used in space exploration and listing the most important space flights.","What is the primary function of a spacecraft's communication system?","To transmit data back to Earth","To receive commands from Earth","To navigate through space","To generate power for the spacecraft","To transmit data back to Earth","A spacecraft's communication system is responsible for transmitting data back to Earth, allowing scientists to analyze and understand the spacecraft's findings.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-of-spacecraft-is-e3bd8a6c","Science G9: Describing some of the methods, techniques - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-1-1 Describing some of the methods, techniques and tools used in exploring the","1. Explaining the means, techniques and tools used in space exploration and listing the most important space flights.","Which of the following spacecraft is known for being the first to land on Mars?","Voyager 1","Pioneer 10","Mars Curiosity Rover","Cassini-Huygens","Mars Curiosity Rover","The Mars Curiosity Rover is a robotic spacecraft that successfully landed on Mars in 2012, marking a major milestone in space exploration.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-of-space-agencies-145f2e19","Science G9: Describing some of the methods, techniques - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-1-1 Describing some of the methods, techniques and tools used in exploring the","1. Explaining the means, techniques and tools used in space exploration and listing the most important space flights.","Which of the following space agencies was responsible for the Apollo 11 mission?","European Space Agency","National Aeronautics and Space Administration (NASA)","Russian Federal Space Agency","China National Space Administration","National Aeronautics and Space Administration (NASA)","The Apollo 11 mission was a historic spaceflight that successfully landed astronauts on the Moon, and it was conducted by NASA.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-primary-purpose-420e3918","Science G9: Describing some of the methods, techniques - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-1-1 Describing some of the methods, techniques and tools used in exploring the","1. Explaining the means, techniques and tools used in space exploration and listing the most important space flights.","What is the primary purpose of a spacecraft's propulsion system?","To generate electricity for the spacecraft's systems","To provide a safe and stable environment for astronauts","To change the spacecraft's trajectory and travel through space","To collect and analyze data from celestial bodies","To change the spacecraft's trajectory and travel through space","A spacecraft's propulsion system is responsible for changing its trajectory and traveling through space, allowing it to reach its destination.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-of-is-a-d23fe117","Science G9: Describing some of the methods, techniques - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-1-1 Describing some of the methods, techniques and tools used in exploring the","1. Explaining the means, techniques and tools used in space exploration and listing the most important space flights.","Which of the following is a benefit of using a microscope in scientific research?","It allows us to see only living things","It helps us to understand the structure of the Earth's crust","It enables us to observe tiny objects and organisms","It is used only in medical research","It enables us to observe tiny objects and organisms","A microscope allows us to magnify and observe tiny objects and organisms that are not visible to the naked eye, enabling us to gain a better understanding of their structure and behavior.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-of-is-a-c8a3ed98","Science G9: Describing some of the methods, techniques - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-1-1 Describing some of the methods, techniques and tools used in exploring the","2. Explaining one of the means of exploring the universe, illustrating its basic characteristics and its most prominent advantages.","Which of the following is a tool used in the study of earthquakes?","Seismograph","Barometer","Thermometer","Hygrometer","Seismograph","A seismograph is a device used to detect and record the vibrations caused by earthquakes, allowing us to study the characteristics of seismic waves and understand the underlying mechanisms of earthquakes.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-main-purpose-55ca20d7","Science G9: Describing some of the methods, techniques - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-1-1 Describing some of the methods, techniques and tools used in exploring the","1. Explaining the means, techniques and tools used in space exploration and listing the most important space flights.","What is the main purpose of using a pH meter in scientific research?","To measure the temperature of a substance","To determine the acidity or basicity of a solution","To detect the presence of a specific gas","To calculate the speed of a moving object","To determine the acidity or basicity of a solution","A pH meter is used to measure the acidity or basicity of a solution, allowing us to determine its pH level and understand its chemical properties.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-main-purpose-e61077a6","Science G9: Describing some of the methods, techniques - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-1-1 Describing some of the methods, techniques and tools used in exploring the","1. Explaining the means, techniques and tools used in space exploration and listing the most important space flights.","What is the main purpose of using a weather vane in meteorology?","To measure the temperature of the air","To detect the presence of a specific weather pattern","To indicate the direction of the wind","To predict the next solar eclipse","To indicate the direction of the wind","A weather vane is used to indicate the direction of the wind, allowing us to understand the movement of air masses and predict changes in the weather.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-of-is-a-73cd64e4","Science G9: Describing some of the methods, techniques - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-1-1 Describing some of the methods, techniques and tools used in exploring the","2. Explaining one of the means of exploring the universe, illustrating its basic characteristics and its most prominent advantages.","Which of the following is a tool used in the study of the Earth's magnetic field?","Magnetometer","Barometer","Thermometer","Seismograph","Magnetometer","A magnetometer is a device used to measure the strength and direction of the Earth's magnetic field, allowing us to study its characteristics and understand its role in navigation and geophysics.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-of-is-a-a3173af1","Science G9: Describing some of the methods, techniques - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-1-1 Describing some of the methods, techniques and tools used in exploring the","1. Explaining the means, techniques and tools used in space exploration and listing the most important space flights.","Which of the following is a technique used in the study of the Earth's atmosphere?","Spectroscopy","Radiocarbon dating","Geologic mapping","Paleomagnetism","Spectroscopy","Spectroscopy is the study of the interaction between matter and electromagnetic radiation, allowing us to analyze the composition and properties of the Earth's atmosphere and understand its role in the Earth's climate system.","/image-source/sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-of-is-a-a3173af1.png","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-primary-function-4b9caafe","Science G9: Describing some of the methods, techniques - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-1-1 Describing some of the methods, techniques and tools used in exploring the","1. Explaining the means, techniques and tools used in space exploration and listing the most important space flights.","What is the primary function of a GPS receiver in navigation?","To determine the altitude of a location","To calculate the speed of a moving object","To provide location information and navigation","To detect the presence of a specific gas","To provide location information and navigation","A GPS receiver uses a network of satellites to determine its location and provide navigation information, allowing us to accurately determine our position and navigate through unfamiliar terrain.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-of-is-a-2141c3b0","Science G9: Describing some of the methods, techniques - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-1-1 Describing some of the methods, techniques and tools used in exploring the","1. Explaining the means, techniques and tools used in space exploration and listing the most important space flights.","Which of the following is a technique used in the study of fossils?","Radiocarbon dating","Fossilization","Geologic mapping","Paleomagnetism","Fossilization","Fossilization is the process by which organic matter is preserved and transformed into a fossil, allowing us to study the history of life on Earth and understand the evolution of different species.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-primary-function-d0765130","Science G9: Describing some of the methods, techniques - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-1-1 Describing some of the methods, techniques and tools used in exploring the","1. Explaining the means, techniques and tools used in space exploration and listing the most important space flights.","What is the primary function of a spectrometer in scientific research?","To measure the temperature of a substance","To analyze the chemical composition of a sample","To detect the presence of a specific gas","To calculate the speed of a moving object","To analyze the chemical composition of a sample","A spectrometer is used to analyze the interaction between matter and electromagnetic radiation, allowing us to determine the chemical composition of a sample and identify its constituent elements.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-main-purpose-ebebc277","Science G9: Describing some of the methods, techniques - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-1-1 Describing some of the methods, techniques and tools used in exploring the","1. Explaining the means, techniques and tools used in space exploration and listing the most important space flights.","What is the main purpose of using a telescope in astronomy?","To study the weather on other planets","To observe distant stars and galaxies","To measure the temperature of the sun","To predict the next solar eclipse","To observe distant stars and galaxies","A telescope is used to collect and focus light from distant objects in space, allowing us to observe and study them in detail.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-of-methods-is-44b8f65b","Science G9: Describing some of the methods, techniques - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-1-1 Describing some of the methods, techniques and tools used in exploring the","3. Organizing and analyzing the data and information available on the vastness and magnitude of the universe, on the number of galaxies, their cosmic distances and paths, to provide evidence for them.","Which of the following methods is used to study the age of the universe?","Spectroscopy","Telescopes","Radio waves","Cosmic microwave background radiation","Cosmic microwave background radiation","The cosmic microwave background radiation is used to study the age of the universe by analyzing the leftover radiation from the Big Bang.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-main-purpose-7df2d095","Science G9: Describing some of the methods, techniques - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-1-1 Describing some of the methods, techniques and tools used in exploring the","3. Organizing and analyzing the data and information available on the vastness and magnitude of the universe, on the number of galaxies, their cosmic distances and paths, to provide evidence for them.","What is the main purpose of organizing and analyzing data on the universe?","To understand the formation of galaxies","To determine the age of the universe","To measure the speed of light","To calculate the number of stars in the Milky Way","To determine the age of the universe","Organizing and analyzing data on the universe helps scientists to understand its vastness and magnitude, which is essential for determining its age.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-of-methods-is-2e083f64","Science G9: Describing some of the methods, techniques - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-1-1 Describing some of the methods, techniques and tools used in exploring the","3. Organizing and analyzing the data and information available on the vastness and magnitude of the universe, on the number of galaxies, their cosmic distances and paths, to provide evidence for them.","Which of the following methods is used to measure the distance of galaxies from Earth?","Spectroscopy","Telescopes","Radio waves","Parallax method","Parallax method","The parallax method is used to measure the distance of galaxies from Earth by measuring the apparent shift of nearby stars against the background of more distant stars.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-term-used-09362cf1","Science G9: Describing some of the methods, techniques - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-1-1 Describing some of the methods, techniques and tools used in exploring the","3. Organizing and analyzing the data and information available on the vastness and magnitude of the universe, on the number of galaxies, their cosmic distances and paths, to provide evidence for them.","What is the term used to describe the path that galaxies follow in the universe?","Galactic plane","Cosmic web","Galaxy cluster","Galactic spiral","Cosmic web","The cosmic web is a network of galaxy filaments and voids that galaxies follow in the universe.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-of-tools-is-bede74e8","Science G9: Describing some of the methods, techniques - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-1-1 Describing some of the methods, techniques and tools used in exploring the","1. Explaining the means, techniques and tools used in space exploration and listing the most important space flights.","Which of the following tools is used to study the universe in different wavelengths of light?","Telescopes","Microscopes","Spectrometers","Oscilloscopes","Spectrometers","Spectrometers are used to study the universe in different wavelengths of light, allowing scientists to analyze the composition and properties of celestial objects.","/image-source/sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-of-tools-is-bede74e8.png","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-term-used-9d4449cb","Science G9: Describing some of the methods, techniques - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-1-1 Describing some of the methods, techniques and tools used in exploring the","3. Organizing and analyzing the data and information available on the vastness and magnitude of the universe, on the number of galaxies, their cosmic distances and paths, to provide evidence for them.","What is the term used to describe the number of galaxies in the observable universe?","Galaxy count","Cosmic density","Galaxy cluster","Galaxy population","Galaxy population","The galaxy population is the term used to describe the number of galaxies in the observable universe.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-of-methods-is-4e5ac7ad","Science G9: Describing some of the methods, techniques - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-1-1 Describing some of the methods, techniques and tools used in exploring the","3. Organizing and analyzing the data and information available on the vastness and magnitude of the universe, on the number of galaxies, their cosmic distances and paths, to provide evidence for them.","Which of the following methods is used to study the motion of galaxies in the universe?","Spectroscopy","Telescopes","Radio waves","Redshift method","Radio waves","Redshift measurements show that distant galaxies are generally moving away, supporting an expanding universe.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-term-used-9a3366d0","Science G9: Describing some of the methods, techniques - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-1-1 Describing some of the methods, techniques and tools used in exploring the","3. Organizing and analyzing the data and information available on the vastness and magnitude of the universe, on the number of galaxies, their cosmic distances and paths, to provide evidence for them.","What is the term used to describe the vast empty spaces between galaxies in the universe?","Galactic voids","Cosmic web","Galaxy clusters","Galactic filaments","Galactic voids","Galactic voids are the vast empty spaces between galaxies in the universe.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-of-tools-is-ff1b4ff9","Science G9: Describing some of the methods, techniques - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-1-1 Describing some of the methods, techniques and tools used in exploring the","1. Explaining the means, techniques and tools used in space exploration and listing the most important space flights.","Which of the following tools is used to study the properties of celestial objects in the universe?","Telescopes","Microscopes","Spectrometers","Oscilloscopes","Telescopes","Telescopes are used to study the properties of celestial objects in the universe, allowing scientists to analyze their composition, size, and distance.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-term-used-eb6fdf09","Science G9: Describing some of the methods, techniques - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-1-1 Describing some of the methods, techniques and tools used in exploring the","3. Organizing and analyzing the data and information available on the vastness and magnitude of the universe, on the number of galaxies, their cosmic distances and paths, to provide evidence for them.","What is the term used to describe the network of galaxy filaments and voids in the universe?","Cosmic web","Galactic plane","Galaxy cluster","Galactic spiral","Cosmic web","The cosmic web is a network of galaxy filaments and voids in the universe.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-biomass-ab20d13b","Science G9: Describing the concept of biomass and its sources - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-3-6 Describing the concept of biomass and its sources, and identifying its importance in biofuel production and reducing carbon emissions.","1. Explaining the concept of biomass and identifying its sources.","What is biomass?","A type of fossil fuel","Organic matter that comes from living organisms","A type of renewable energy source","A non-renewable resource","Organic matter that comes from living organisms","Biomass is organic matter that comes from living organisms, such as plants, animals, and microorganisms.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-type-of-energy-ca5cdcd4","Science G9: Describing the concept of biomass and its sources - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-3-6 Describing the concept of biomass and its sources, and identifying its importance in biofuel production and reducing carbon emissions.","1. Explaining the concept of biomass and identifying its sources.","What type of energy is biomass considered?","Non-renewable energy","Renewable energy","Nuclear energy","Fossil fuel energy","Renewable energy","Biomass is considered a renewable energy source because it comes from living organisms that can be replenished over time.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-are-some-examples-c5a7e1d8","Science G9: Describing the concept of biomass and its sources - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-3-6 Describing the concept of biomass and its sources, and identifying its importance in biofuel production and reducing carbon emissions.","3. Explaining the efforts of the Kingdom of Saudi Arabia to reduce carbon emissions and their impacts, and providing examples of these efforts.","What are some examples of biomass materials?","Coal, oil, and natural gas","Wood, crops, and waste","Water, wind, and solar energy","Minerals and metals","Wood, crops, and waste","Examples of biomass materials include wood, crops, and waste, which can be used to produce biofuels, heat, and electricity.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-one-benefit-20241f3d","Science G9: Describing the concept of biomass and its sources - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-3-6 Describing the concept of biomass and its sources, and identifying its importance in biofuel production and reducing carbon emissions.","1. Explaining the concept of biomass and identifying its sources.","What is one benefit of using biomass as a fuel source?","It produces more air pollution","It is more expensive than fossil fuels","It reduces greenhouse gas emissions","It is less efficient than fossil fuels","It reduces greenhouse gas emissions","One benefit of using biomass as a fuel source is that it can help reduce greenhouse gas emissions, which contribute to climate change.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-why-is-biomass-3be35741","Science G9: Describing the concept of biomass and its sources - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-3-6 Describing the concept of biomass and its sources, and identifying its importance in biofuel production and reducing carbon emissions.","1. Explaining the concept of biomass and identifying its sources.","Why is biomass important in reducing carbon emissions?","Because it releases more carbon dioxide into the atmosphere","Because it absorbs more carbon dioxide from the atmosphere","Because it is a non-renewable resource","Because it produces more greenhouse gases","Because it absorbs more carbon dioxide from the atmosphere","Biomass is important in reducing carbon emissions because it absorbs carbon dioxide from the atmosphere as it grows, which can help offset the amount of carbon dioxide released when it is burned or converted into biofuels.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-role-of-fdf33e32","Science G9: Describing the concept of biomass and its sources - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-3-6 Describing the concept of biomass and its sources, and identifying its importance in biofuel production and reducing carbon emissions.","1. Explaining the concept of biomass and identifying its sources.","What is the role of microorganisms in biomass production?","They absorb carbon dioxide from the atmosphere","They produce biofuels through fermentation","They release greenhouse gases into the atmosphere","They are a type of fossil fuel","They produce biofuels through fermentation","Microorganisms, such as bacteria and yeast, play a crucial role in biomass production by converting biomass into biofuels through fermentation.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-how-biomass-contribute-e7b1ad27","Science G9: Describing the concept of biomass and its sources - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-3-6 Describing the concept of biomass and its sources, and identifying its importance in biofuel production and reducing carbon emissions.","1. Explaining the concept of biomass and identifying its sources.","How does biomass contribute to a circular economy?","By producing more waste and pollution","By reducing the use of renewable energy sources","By promoting the use of sustainable and renewable resources","By increasing greenhouse gas emissions","By promoting the use of sustainable and renewable resources","Biomass contributes to a circular economy by promoting the use of sustainable and renewable resources, reducing waste, and minimizing the environmental impact of energy production.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-why-is-biomass-5dd8410f","Science G9: Describing the concept of biomass and its sources - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-3-6 Describing the concept of biomass and its sources, and identifying its importance in biofuel production and reducing carbon emissions.","1. Explaining the concept of biomass and identifying its sources.","Why is biomass a sustainable option for energy production?","Because it is a non-renewable resource","Because it produces more greenhouse gases","Because it can be replenished over time","Because it is more expensive than fossil fuels","Because it can be replenished over time","Biomass is a sustainable option for energy production because it can be replenished over time, making it a renewable energy source.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-one-way-f8853db4","Science G9: Describing the concept of biomass and its sources - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-3-6 Describing the concept of biomass and its sources, and identifying its importance in biofuel production and reducing carbon emissions.","1. Explaining the concept of biomass and identifying its sources.","What is one way that biomass is used?","To generate electricity in power plants","To produce biofuels for transportation","To manufacture plastics and other synthetic materials","To create pesticides and fertilizers","To produce biofuels for transportation","Biomass is used to produce biofuels, which can be used as a sustainable alternative to fossil fuels for transportation.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-are-some-common-8aa0a19c","Science G9: Describing the concept of biomass and its sources - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-3-6 Describing the concept of biomass and its sources, and identifying its importance in biofuel production and reducing carbon emissions.","1. Explaining the concept of biomass and identifying its sources.","What are some common sources of biomass?","Fossil fuels, nuclear power, and coal","Plants, animals, and microorganisms","Water, wind, and solar energy","Minerals and metals","Plants, animals, and microorganisms","Biomass comes from a variety of sources, including plants, animals, and microorganisms.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-process-of-0146ffab","Science G9: Describing the concept of biomass and - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-3-6 Describing the concept of biomass and its sources, and identifying its importance in biofuel production and reducing carbon emissions.","1. Explaining the concept of biomass and identifying its sources.","What is the process of converting biomass into energy called?","Biomass conversion","Biofuel production","Energy conversion","Renewable energy production","Biofuel production","The process of converting biomass into energy is called biofuel production.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-benefit-of-f43169d8","Science G9: Describing the concept of biomass and - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-3-6 Describing the concept of biomass and its sources, and identifying its importance in biofuel production and reducing carbon emissions.","1. Explaining the concept of biomass and identifying its sources.","What is the benefit of using biofuels made from biomass?","It increases greenhouse gas emissions","It has no effect on greenhouse gas emissions","It reduces greenhouse gas emissions","It is not a renewable energy source","It reduces greenhouse gas emissions","Using biofuels made from biomass can help reduce greenhouse gas emissions.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-biomass-9004c87a","Science G9: Describing the concept of biomass and - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-3-6 Describing the concept of biomass and its sources, and identifying its importance in biofuel production and reducing carbon emissions.","1. Explaining the concept of biomass and identifying its sources.","What is biomass?","A type of fossil fuel","Organic material from plants and animals","A type of renewable energy","A type of non-renewable energy","Organic material from plants and animals","Biomass is organic material from plants and animals that can be used as a source of energy.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-are-some-common-95236a3f","Science G9: Describing the concept of biomass and - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-3-6 Describing the concept of biomass and its sources, and identifying its importance in biofuel production and reducing carbon emissions.","1. Explaining the concept of biomass and identifying its sources.","What are some common sources of biomass?","Fossil fuels, coal, and natural gas","Wood, crops, and waste","Water and wind","Geothermal energy and nuclear power","Wood, crops, and waste","Common sources of biomass include wood, crops, and waste.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-are-some-examples-913969c3","Science G9: Describing the concept of biomass and - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-3-6 Describing the concept of biomass and its sources, and identifying its importance in biofuel production and reducing carbon emissions.","1. Explaining the concept of biomass and identifying its sources.","What are some examples of biomass that can be used as a source of energy?","Wood, crops, and waste","Fossil fuels, coal, and natural gas","Water and wind","Geothermal energy and nuclear power","Wood, crops, and waste","Examples of biomass that can be used as a source of energy include wood, crops, and waste.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-why-is-biomass-8b28d931","Science G9: Describing the concept of biomass and - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-3-6 Describing the concept of biomass and its sources, and identifying its importance in biofuel production and reducing carbon emissions.","1. Explaining the concept of biomass and identifying its sources.","Why is biomass important in biofuel production?","Because it is a non-renewable energy source","Because it is a cheap energy source","Because it can be converted into fuels like ethanol and biodiesel","Because it is a clean energy source","Because it can be converted into fuels like ethanol and biodiesel","Biomass is important in biofuel production because it can be converted into fuels like ethanol and biodiesel.","","",""
