"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-probability-of-87bf6a87","Science G9: Describing the development of genetics - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-4-1 Describing the development of genetics, and the use of Mendelian laws to explain the inheritance of genetic traits, and the probabilities of their appearance in different generations.","4. Distinguishing between homozygous and heterozygous genes, distinguish between homozygous genes organisms and non- homozygous organisms, and providing an example of each.","What is the probability of a trait being expressed if both parents are homozygous dominant?","25%","50%","75%","100%","100%","Two homozygous dominant parents can pass only the dominant allele, so all offspring are expected to show the dominant phenotype.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-term-combination-176544bb","Science G9: Describing the development of genetics - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-4-1 Describing the development of genetics, and the use of Mendelian laws to explain the inheritance of genetic traits, and the probabilities of their appearance in different generations.","4. Distinguishing between homozygous and heterozygous genes, distinguish between homozygous genes organisms and non- homozygous organisms, and providing an example of each.","What is the term for the combination of two different alleles in an individual?","Heterozygous","Homozygous dominant","Homozygous recessive","Genotype","Heterozygous","An individual with two different alleles for a gene is heterozygous.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-probability-of-a9f43200","Science G9: Describing the development of genetics - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-4-1 Describing the development of genetics, and the use of Mendelian laws to explain the inheritance of genetic traits, and the probabilities of their appearance in different generations.","4. Distinguishing between homozygous and heterozygous genes, distinguish between homozygous genes organisms and non- homozygous organisms, and providing an example of each.","What is the probability of a trait being expressed if one parent is homozygous dominant and the other is homozygous recessive?","25%","50%","75%","100%","50%","The probability of a trait being expressed is 50% when one parent is homozygous dominant and the other is homozygous recessive.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-term-passing-c7bdf329","Science G9: Describing the development of genetics - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-4-1 Describing the development of genetics, and the use of Mendelian laws to explain the inheritance of genetic traits, and the probabilities of their appearance in different generations.","1. Distinguishing the structure of the chromosome, and the relationship between its components. Explaining the concept of a gene, identifying its components and its location on the chromosome, and explains the occurrence of genetic mutation and its effects on making protein in the cell.","What is the term for the passing of traits from parents to offspring?","Inheritance","Mutation","Genetic drift","Gene expression","Inheritance","Inheritance is the passing of traits from parents to offspring.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-group-of-elements-872a2e96","Science G9: Describing the history of the periodic - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-7 Explaining how atoms bond with each other, identifying what is a chemical bond and how it is formed, and distinguishing between different types","2. Describes the states of the outer electron level (valence electrons) through the periodic chemical properties of elements within the same group.","Which group of elements is known for being highly reactive and readily gaining one electron to form a negative ion?","Alkali metals","Halogens","Noble gases","Transition metals","Halogens","The halogens are known for being highly reactive and readily gaining one electron to form a negative ion, which makes them highly reactive with metals and other substances.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-primary-use-169fb22d","Science G9: Describing the history of the periodic - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-5 Describing the history of the periodic table, explaining how the elements are organized in the periodic table, and the properties of the elements and their common uses.","5. Explaining the meaning of processed elements and catalyst, and providing examples for each.","What is the primary use of the element tungsten?","Making light bulb filaments","Manufacturing high-speed tools","Producing superconducting materials","Creating radiation shielding","Making light bulb filaments","Tungsten is a hard, dense metal that is often used in the manufacture of light bulb filaments due to its high melting point and resistance to corrosion.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-of-elements-is-8a444f96","Science G9: Describing the history of the periodic - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-7 Explaining how atoms bond with each other, identifying what is a chemical bond and how it is formed, and distinguishing between different types","2. Describes the states of the outer electron level (valence electrons) through the periodic chemical properties of elements within the same group.","Which of the following elements is an example of a representative element?","Copper","Silver","Carbon","Tin","Carbon","Carbon is an example of a representative element, which is a group of elements that have a full outer energy level and exhibit a wide range of chemical properties.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-term-elements-43d1c9cb","Science G9: Describing the history of the periodic - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-5 Describing the history of the periodic table, explaining how the elements are organized in the periodic table, and the properties of the elements and their common uses.","4. Recognizing the location of the representative elements, the transitional elements, and the inner transition (lanthanides and actinides) in the periodic table, based on their electronic composition, predicting their physical and chemical properties, and identifying some of their uses.","What is the term for the elements in the periodic table that are located at the bottom of the table and have partially filled f subshells?","Lanthanides","Actinides","Transition elements","Inner transition elements","Lanthanides","The lanthanides are a group of inner transition elements that have partially filled f subshells and are located at the bottom of the periodic table.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-primary-use-da61a92e","Science G9: Describing the history of the periodic - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-5 Describing the history of the periodic table, explaining how the elements are organized in the periodic table, and the properties of the elements and their common uses.","5. Explaining the meaning of processed elements and catalyst, and providing examples for each.","What is the primary use of the element titanium?","Making jewelry","Manufacturing aircraft and spacecraft parts","Producing pigments and dyes","Creating medical implants","Manufacturing aircraft and spacecraft parts","Titanium is a strong and lightweight metal that is often used in the manufacture of aircraft and spacecraft parts due to its high strength-to-weight ratio and resistance to corrosion.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-of-elements-is-275f2284","Science G9: Describing the history of the periodic - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-5 Describing the history of the periodic table, explaining how the elements are organized in the periodic table, and the properties of the elements and their common uses.","4. Recognizing the location of the representative elements, the transitional elements, and the inner transition (lanthanides and actinides) in the periodic table, based on their electronic composition, predicting their physical and chemical properties, and identifying some of their uses.","Which of the following elements is an example of a lanthanide?","Copper","Silver","Lanthanum","Tin","Lanthanum","Lanthanum is an example of a lanthanide, which is a group of inner transition elements that have partially filled f subshells.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-term-elements-f2eb7bd1","Science G9: Describing the history of the periodic - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-5 Describing the history of the periodic table, explaining how the elements are organized in the periodic table, and the properties of the elements and their common uses.","4. Recognizing the location of the representative elements, the transitional elements, and the inner transition (lanthanides and actinides) in the periodic table, based on their electronic composition, predicting their physical and chemical properties, and identifying some of their uses.","What is the term for the elements in the periodic table that are located in the middle of the table and have partially filled d subshells?","Representative elements","Transition elements","Inner transition elements","Noble gases","Transition elements","The transition elements are located in the middle of the periodic table and have partially filled d subshells, which gives them unique chemical properties.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-group-of-elements-a0244e8d","Science G9: Describing the history of the periodic - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-6 Comparing acids and bases according to their properties and uses, and their effect on reagents.","1. Describing how electrons are arranged within the atom, and its relationship to their position in the periodic table. Comparing the numbers of electrons in each energy levels, and determining the lowest and highest energy levels of an element.","Which group of elements is located on the far right side of the periodic table and is known for being unreactive?","Alkali metals","Noble gases","Halogens","Transition metals","Noble gases","The noble gases are located on the far right side of the periodic table and are known for being unreactive due to their full outer energy level.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-main-reason-7580ba83","Science G9: Describing the history of the periodic - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-5 Describing the history of the periodic table, explaining how the elements are organized in the periodic table, and the properties of the elements and their common uses.","4. Recognizing the location of the representative elements, the transitional elements, and the inner transition (lanthanides and actinides) in the periodic table, based on their electronic composition, predicting their physical and chemical properties, and identifying some of their uses.","What is the main reason for the organization of elements in the periodic table?","Their atomic mass increases from left to right and top to bottom","Their physical properties such as color and texture","Their electronic composition and the number of electrons in the outermost energy level","Their chemical reactivity with other elements","Their electronic composition and the number of electrons in the outermost energy level","The elements in the periodic table are organized based on their electronic composition, specifically the number of electrons in the outermost energy level, which determines their chemical properties.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-group-of-elements-58e93f1e","Science G9: Describing the history of the periodic - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-7 Explaining how atoms bond with each other, identifying what is a chemical bond and how it is formed, and distinguishing between different types","2. Describes the states of the outer electron level (valence electrons) through the periodic chemical properties of elements within the same group.","Which group of elements is known for being highly reactive and readily losing one electron to form a positive ion?","Alkali metals","Halogens","Noble gases","Transition metals","Alkali metals","The alkali metals are known for being highly reactive and readily losing one electron to form a positive ion, which makes them highly reactive with water and other substances.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-of-is-an-8ed5ad3f","Science G9: Describing the history of the periodic - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-4 Explaining the properties of liquids, comparing crystalline and amorphous solids, and describing the pattern of crystals in solids.","1. Explaining the contributions of scientists to the arrangement of the elements discovered in the periodic table and the history of its development leading to the modern periodic table.","Which of the following is an example of a processed element?","Oxygen found in the air","Iron extracted from iron ore","Helium found in natural gas deposits","Nitrogen in the atmosphere","Iron extracted from iron ore","Iron extracted from iron ore is an example of a processed element, as it has been refined and purified from its natural state.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-of-elements-is-f0a8a2f1","Science G9: Describing the history of the periodic - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-4 Explaining the properties of liquids, comparing crystalline and amorphous solids, and describing the pattern of crystals in solids.","1. Explaining the contributions of scientists to the arrangement of the elements discovered in the periodic table and the history of its development leading to the modern periodic table.","Which of the following elements is commonly used as a catalyst in the production of polyethylene?","Chromium","Molybdenum","Tungsten","Aluminum","Chromium","Chromium is commonly used as a catalyst in the production of polyethylene, as it helps to facilitate the polymerization reaction.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-main-purpose-7490ea8c","Science G9: Describing the history of the periodic - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-4 Explaining the properties of liquids, comparing crystalline and amorphous solids, and describing the pattern of crystals in solids.","1. Explaining the contributions of scientists to the arrangement of the elements discovered in the periodic table and the history of its development leading to the modern periodic table.","What is the main purpose of the periodic table?","To list all the elements in alphabetical order","To organize elements based on their properties and atomic mass","To show the chemical reactions between elements","To describe the history of chemistry","To organize elements based on their properties and atomic mass","The periodic table is used to organize elements based on their properties and atomic mass, allowing for the identification of patterns and relationships between elements.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-primary-difference-3bf01e53","Science G9: Describing the history of the periodic - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-4 Explaining the properties of liquids, comparing crystalline and amorphous solids, and describing the pattern of crystals in solids.","1. Explaining the contributions of scientists to the arrangement of the elements discovered in the periodic table and the history of its development leading to the modern periodic table.","What is the primary difference between a processed element and a raw element?","The processed element is more expensive to produce","The processed element has a higher atomic mass","The processed element has been refined and purified from its natural state","The processed element is more reactive than the raw element","The processed element has been refined and purified from its natural state","The primary difference between a processed element and a raw element is that the processed element has been refined and purified from its natural state, often through industrial processes.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-of-is-an-4f51eae0","Science G9: Describing the history of the periodic - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-4 Explaining the properties of liquids, comparing crystalline and amorphous solids, and describing the pattern of crystals in solids.","1. Explaining the contributions of scientists to the arrangement of the elements discovered in the periodic table and the history of its development leading to the modern periodic table.","Which of the following is an example of a common use of the element titanium?","As a catalyst in chemical reactions","As a component of high-strength alloys","As a pigment in paints and coatings","As a fuel source for nuclear reactors","As a component of high-strength alloys","Titanium is often used as a component of high-strength alloys, due to its high strength-to-weight ratio and corrosion resistance.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-term-elements-80f02107","Science G9: Describing the history of the periodic - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-4 Explaining the properties of liquids, comparing crystalline and amorphous solids, and describing the pattern of crystals in solids.","1. Explaining the contributions of scientists to the arrangement of the elements discovered in the periodic table and the history of its development leading to the modern periodic table.","What is the term for elements that have been refined and purified from their natural state?","Raw elements","Processed elements","Natural elements","Synthetic elements","Processed elements","Processed elements are elements that have been refined and purified from their natural state, often through industrial processes.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-of-elements-is-e910c13b","Science G9: Describing the history of the periodic - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-4 Explaining the properties of liquids, comparing crystalline and amorphous solids, and describing the pattern of crystals in solids.","1. Explaining the contributions of scientists to the arrangement of the elements discovered in the periodic table and the history of its development leading to the modern periodic table.","Which of the following elements is not a noble gas?","Neon","Argon","Oxygen","Krypton","Oxygen","Oxygen is not a noble gas, as it is a highly reactive element that readily forms compounds with other elements.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-primary-function-9cf337ca","Science G9: Describing the history of the periodic - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-4 Explaining the properties of liquids, comparing crystalline and amorphous solids, and describing the pattern of crystals in solids.","1. Explaining the contributions of scientists to the arrangement of the elements discovered in the periodic table and the history of its development leading to the modern periodic table.","What is the primary function of the periodic table in relation to the elements?","To predict the properties of unknown elements","To organize elements based on their properties and atomic mass","To describe the history of element discovery","To list the elements in order of their discovery","To organize elements based on their properties and atomic mass","The primary function of the periodic table is to organize elements based on their properties and atomic mass, allowing for the identification of patterns and relationships between elements.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-element-is-commonly-140f5074","Science G9: Describing the history of the periodic - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-4 Explaining the properties of liquids, comparing crystalline and amorphous solids, and describing the pattern of crystals in solids.","1. Explaining the contributions of scientists to the arrangement of the elements discovered in the periodic table and the history of its development leading to the modern periodic table.","Which element is commonly used as a catalyst in the production of ammonia?","Iron","Copper","Nickel","Platinum","Iron","Iron is commonly used as a catalyst in the Haber-Bosch process, which is used to produce ammonia on an industrial scale.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-a-catalyst-efed83da","Science G9: Describing the history of the periodic - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-9 Describing the speed of chemical reactions and identifying the factors affecting them","2. Determining the factors affecting the rate of a chemical reaction (concentration of reactants, concentration of reactants, pressure, temperature, catalyst).","What is a catalyst in a chemical reaction?","A substance that is consumed by the reaction","A substance that speeds up the reaction without being consumed","A substance that slows down the reaction","A substance that is produced by the reaction","A substance that speeds up the reaction without being consumed","A catalyst is a substance that speeds up a chemical reaction without being consumed or altered in the process.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-chemical-symbol-3089678c","Science G9: Describing the history of the periodic table - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-4 Explaining the properties of liquids, comparing crystalline and amorphous solids, and describing the pattern of crystals in solids.","1. Explaining the contributions of scientists to the arrangement of the elements discovered in the periodic table and the history of its development leading to the modern periodic table.","What is the chemical symbol for gold?","The chemical symbol for gold is Ag.","The chemical symbol for gold is Au.","The chemical symbol for gold is Hg.","The chemical symbol for gold is Pb.","The chemical symbol for gold is Au.","The chemical symbol for gold is Au.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-chemical-symbol-c553ff9f","Science G9: Describing the history of the periodic table - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-4 Explaining the properties of liquids, comparing crystalline and amorphous solids, and describing the pattern of crystals in solids.","1. Explaining the contributions of scientists to the arrangement of the elements discovered in the periodic table and the history of its development leading to the modern periodic table.","What is the chemical symbol for silver?","The chemical symbol for silver is Hg.","The chemical symbol for silver is Pb.","The chemical symbol for silver is Ag.","The chemical symbol for silver is Au.","The chemical symbol for silver is Ag.","The chemical symbol for silver is Ag.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-of-is-a-c246091e","Science G9: Describing the history of the periodic table - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-4 Explaining the properties of liquids, comparing crystalline and amorphous solids, and describing the pattern of crystals in solids.","1. Explaining the contributions of scientists to the arrangement of the elements discovered in the periodic table and the history of its development leading to the modern periodic table.","Which of the following is a non-metal?","Boron is a metal.","Nitrogen is a gas.","Phosphorus is a non-metal.","Sodium is a metal.","Phosphorus is a non-metal.","Phosphorus is a non-metal.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-chemical-symbol-b1da837e","Science G9: Describing the history of the periodic table - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-4 Explaining the properties of liquids, comparing crystalline and amorphous solids, and describing the pattern of crystals in solids.","1. Explaining the contributions of scientists to the arrangement of the elements discovered in the periodic table and the history of its development leading to the modern periodic table.","What is the chemical symbol for mercury?","The chemical symbol for mercury is Ag.","The chemical symbol for mercury is Au.","The chemical symbol for mercury is Hg.","The chemical symbol for mercury is Pb.","The chemical symbol for mercury is Hg.","The chemical symbol for mercury is Hg.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-of-is-a-ca0396c7","Science G9: Describing the history of the periodic table - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-4 Explaining the properties of liquids, comparing crystalline and amorphous solids, and describing the pattern of crystals in solids.","2. Explaining the properties of the elements in the periodic table sectors within the period and group, and lists the uses of the common elements around it.","Which of the following is a group in the periodic table?","A family is a group in the periodic table.","A group is a family in the periodic table.","A group is a horizontal row of elements.","A group is a vertical column of elements.","A group is a vertical column of elements.","A group is a vertical column of elements.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-chemical-symbol-5a5467fc","Science G9: Describing the history of the periodic table - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-4 Explaining the properties of liquids, comparing crystalline and amorphous solids, and describing the pattern of crystals in solids.","1. Explaining the contributions of scientists to the arrangement of the elements discovered in the periodic table and the history of its development leading to the modern periodic table.","What is the chemical symbol for lead?","The chemical symbol for lead is Ag.","The chemical symbol for lead is Au.","The chemical symbol for lead is Hg.","The chemical symbol for lead is Pb.","The chemical symbol for lead is Pb.","The chemical symbol for lead is Pb.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-of-is-a-5449f6c6","Science G9: Describing the history of the periodic table - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-4 Explaining the properties of liquids, comparing crystalline and amorphous solids, and describing the pattern of crystals in solids.","2. Explaining the properties of the elements in the periodic table sectors within the period and group, and lists the uses of the common elements around it.","Which of the following is a period in the periodic table?","A period is a horizontal row of elements.","A period is a vertical column of elements.","A period is a family in the periodic table.","A period is a group in the periodic table.","A period is a horizontal row of elements.","A period is a horizontal row of elements.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-element-key-93ea24d8","Science G9: Describing the history of the periodic table - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-4 Explaining the properties of liquids, comparing crystalline and amorphous solids, and describing the pattern of crystals in solids.","2. Explaining the properties of the elements in the periodic table sectors within the period and group, and lists the uses of the common elements around it.","What is the element key in the periodic table?","A horizontal row of elements is called a period.","A vertical column of elements is called a group.","A vertical column of elements is called a family.","A diagonal line of elements is called a series.","A vertical column of elements is called a group.","The element key in the periodic table is a vertical column of elements called a group.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-of-is-a-a7f098bc","Science G9: Describing the history of the periodic table - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-4 Explaining the properties of liquids, comparing crystalline and amorphous solids, and describing the pattern of crystals in solids.","1. Explaining the contributions of scientists to the arrangement of the elements discovered in the periodic table and the history of its development leading to the modern periodic table.","Which of the following is a metal?","Sulfur is a metal.","Copper is a metal.","Carbon is a non-metal.","Oxygen is a gas.","Copper is a metal.","Copper is a metal.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-of-is-a-ecee658c","Science G9: Describing the history of the periodic table - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-5 Describing the history of the periodic table, explaining how the elements are organized in the periodic table, and the properties of the elements and their common uses.","3. Explaining the element key, naming some chemical elements and knowing how to write their chemical symbols. Distinguishing between metals, non-metals and metalloids, and providing examples thereof.","Which of the following is a metalloid?","Boron is a metal.","Carbon is a non-metal.","Silicon is a metalloid.","Oxygen is a gas.","Silicon is a metalloid.","Silicon is a metalloid.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-why-is-cell-e2fb42a5","Science G9: Describing the main events in the - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-2 Describing the main events in the stages of the cell cycle and comparing between mitosis and meiosis.","5. Comparing mitosis and meiosis in terms of importance, stages, outcomes, and the types of cells in which each division occurs.","Why is cell division important in the cell cycle?","It allows cells to grow and mature before dividing.","It enables cells to replicate their DNA and prepare for cell division.","It causes cells to become dormant and stop growing.","It leads to cell death and replacement by new cells.","It enables cells to replicate their DNA and prepare for cell division.","Cell division is important because it enables cells to replicate their DNA and prepare for cell division.","/image-source/sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-why-is-cell-e2fb42a5.png","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-difference-active-0c1b7096","Science G9: Describing the main events in the - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-2 Describing the main events in the stages of the cell cycle and comparing between mitosis and meiosis.","2. Explaining the importance of interphase and cell division, describing the condition of cells during these stages, and distinguishing them from active cells.","What is the difference between active cells and cells during interphase?","Active cells are dormant and not growing, while cells during interphase are actively dividing.","Active cells are preparing for cell division, while cells during interphase are dormant and not growing.","Active cells are growing and maturing, while cells during interphase are preparing for cell division.","Active cells are dying and being replaced by new cells, while cells during interphase are growing and maturing.","Active cells are growing and maturing, while cells during interphase are preparing for cell division.","Active cells are growing and maturing, while cells during interphase are preparing for cell division.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-main-event-c9372cb1","Science G9: Describing the main events in the - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-2 Describing the main events in the stages of the cell cycle and comparing between mitosis and meiosis.","5. Comparing mitosis and meiosis in terms of importance, stages, outcomes, and the types of cells in which each division occurs.","What is the main event in the stage of meiosis?","The cell grows and matures.","The cell replicates its DNA.","The cell divides to produce four daughter cells.","The cell dies and is replaced by new cells.","The cell divides to produce four daughter cells.","The main event in meiosis is the cell dividing to produce four daughter cells.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-main-event-bae0e962","Science G9: Describing the main events in the - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-2 Describing the main events in the stages of the cell cycle and comparing between mitosis and meiosis.","1. Illustrating the two main stages of the cell cycle (interphase and cell division), describing the changes and events associated with each stage, identifying their duration, and providing examples.","What is the main event in the stage of mitosis?","The cell grows and matures.","The cell replicates its DNA.","The cell divides to produce two daughter cells.","The cell dies and is replaced by new cells.","The cell divides to produce two daughter cells.","The main event in mitosis is the cell dividing to produce two daughter cells.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-condition-of-e71a7849","Science G9: Describing the main events in the - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-2 Describing the main events in the stages of the cell cycle and comparing between mitosis and meiosis.","2. Explaining the importance of interphase and cell division, describing the condition of cells during these stages, and distinguishing them from active cells.","What is the condition of cells during cell division?","The cells are dormant and not growing.","The cells are actively dividing.","The cells are preparing for cell division by replicating their DNA.","The cells are dying and being replaced by new cells.","The cells are actively dividing.","During cell division, cells are actively dividing and producing two daughter cells.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-primary-function-b6f076e8","Science G9: Describing the main events in the - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-2 Describing the main events in the stages of the cell cycle and comparing between mitosis and meiosis.","5. Comparing mitosis and meiosis in terms of importance, stages, outcomes, and the types of cells in which each division occurs.","What is the primary function of cell division in the cell cycle?","The cell grows and matures before dividing.","The cell prepares for cell division by replicating its DNA.","The cell divides to produce two daughter cells.","The cell dies and is replaced by new cells.","The cell divides to produce two daughter cells.","Cell division is the process by which the cell divides to produce two daughter cells.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-why-is-interphase-c81eacd5","Science G9: Describing the main events in the - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-2 Describing the main events in the stages of the cell cycle and comparing between mitosis and meiosis.","5. Comparing mitosis and meiosis in terms of importance, stages, outcomes, and the types of cells in which each division occurs.","Why is interphase important in the cell cycle?","It allows cells to grow and mature before dividing.","It enables cells to replicate their DNA and prepare for cell division.","It causes cells to become dormant and stop growing.","It leads to cell death and replacement by new cells.","It enables cells to replicate their DNA and prepare for cell division.","Interphase is important because it allows cells to replicate their DNA and prepare for cell division.","/image-source/sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-why-is-interphase-c81eacd5.png","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-condition-of-81ee3a1c","Science G9: Describing the main events in the - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-2 Describing the main events in the stages of the cell cycle and comparing between mitosis and meiosis.","2. Explaining the importance of interphase and cell division, describing the condition of cells during these stages, and distinguishing them from active cells.","What is the condition of cells during interphase?","The cells are dormant and not growing.","The cells are actively dividing.","The cells are preparing for cell division by replicating their DNA.","The cells are dying and being replaced by new cells.","The cells are preparing for cell division by replicating their DNA.","During interphase, cells are preparing for cell division by replicating their DNA, growing, and maturing.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-main-difference-b6708a75","Science G9: Describing the main events in the - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-2 Describing the main events in the stages of the cell cycle and comparing between mitosis and meiosis.","4. Defining meiosis, describing its stages, comparing (using diagrams) what occurs during anaphase I and anaphase II in the division processes, and distinguishing their different forms.","What is the main difference between mitosis and meiosis?","Mitosis produces gametes, while meiosis produces somatic cells.","Mitosis produces somatic cells, while meiosis produces gametes.","Mitosis occurs in somatic cells, while meiosis occurs in gametes.","Mitosis occurs in gametes, while meiosis occurs in somatic cells.","Mitosis produces somatic cells, while meiosis produces gametes.","Mitosis produces somatic cells, while meiosis produces gametes.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-primary-function-1a8785c0","Science G9: Describing the main events in the - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-2 Describing the main events in the stages of the cell cycle and comparing between mitosis and meiosis.","2. Explaining the importance of interphase and cell division, describing the condition of cells during these stages, and distinguishing them from active cells.","What is the primary function of interphase in the cell cycle?","The cell prepares for cell division by replicating its DNA.","The cell grows and matures before dividing.","The cell becomes dormant and stops growing.","The cell dies and is replaced by new cells.","The cell prepares for cell division by replicating its DNA.","During interphase, the cell replicates its DNA, grows, and prepares for cell division.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-process-called-a1b621b3","Science G9: Describing the main events in the - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-2 Describing the main events in the stages of the cell cycle and comparing between mitosis and meiosis.","4. Defining meiosis, describing its stages, comparing (using diagrams) what occurs during anaphase I and anaphase II in the division processes, and distinguishing their different forms.","What is the process called when a cell divides into four daughter cells with half the number of chromosomes as the parent cell?","Mitosis","Meiosis","Photosynthesis","Respiration","Meiosis","Meiosis is the process of cell division that results in four daughter cells with half the number of chromosomes as the parent cell.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-final-stage-438323b1","Science G9: Describing the main events in the - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-2 Describing the main events in the stages of the cell cycle and comparing between mitosis and meiosis.","5. Comparing mitosis and meiosis in terms of importance, stages, outcomes, and the types of cells in which each division occurs.","What is the final stage of mitosis where the nuclear envelope reforms and the chromosomes uncoil?","Cytokinesis","Telophase","Prophase","Metaphase","Telophase","Telophase is the final stage of mitosis where the nuclear envelope reforms and the chromosomes uncoil.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-happens-to-chromosomes-f48b4a29","Science G9: Describing the main events in the - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-2 Describing the main events in the stages of the cell cycle and comparing between mitosis and meiosis.","4. Defining meiosis, describing its stages, comparing (using diagrams) what occurs during anaphase I and anaphase II in the division processes, and distinguishing their different forms.","What happens to the chromosomes during the metaphase stage of mitosis?","They move to the poles of the cell","They line up in the center of the cell","They begin to separate","They disappear","They line up in the center of the cell","During metaphase, the chromosomes line up in the center of the cell attached to the spindle fibers.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-stage-of-e2399cfd","Science G9: Describing the main events in the - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-2 Describing the main events in the stages of the cell cycle and comparing between mitosis and meiosis.","4. Defining meiosis, describing its stages, comparing (using diagrams) what occurs during anaphase I and anaphase II in the division processes, and distinguishing their different forms.","What is the stage of meiosis where the homologous chromosomes separate?","Meiosis I","Meiosis II","Prophase","Telophase","Meiosis I","Meiosis I is the stage of meiosis where the homologous chromosomes separate.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-first-stage-7d73e83c","Science G9: Describing the main events in the - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-2 Describing the main events in the stages of the cell cycle and comparing between mitosis and meiosis.","4. Defining meiosis, describing its stages, comparing (using diagrams) what occurs during anaphase I and anaphase II in the division processes, and distinguishing their different forms.","What is the first stage of mitosis where the chromatin condenses and the nuclear envelope breaks down?","Prophase","Metaphase","Anaphase","Telophase","Prophase","Prophase is the first stage of mitosis where the chromatin condenses and the nuclear envelope breaks down.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-process-called-63445ef5","Science G9: Describing the main events in the - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-2 Describing the main events in the stages of the cell cycle and comparing between mitosis and meiosis.","5. Comparing mitosis and meiosis in terms of importance, stages, outcomes, and the types of cells in which each division occurs.","What is the process called when a cell divides into two daughter cells with the same number of chromosomes as the parent cell?","Mitosis","Meiosis","Photosynthesis","Respiration","Mitosis","Mitosis is the process of cell division that results in two daughter cells with the same number of chromosomes as the parent cell.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-final-stage-0f2a0d76","Science G9: Describing the main events in the - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-2 Describing the main events in the stages of the cell cycle and comparing between mitosis and meiosis.","5. Comparing mitosis and meiosis in terms of importance, stages, outcomes, and the types of cells in which each division occurs.","What is the final stage of meiosis where the nuclear envelope reforms and the chromosomes uncoil?","Telophase","Cytokinesis","Prophase","Metaphase","Telophase","Telophase is the final stage of meiosis where the nuclear envelope reforms and the chromosomes uncoil.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-process-called-71466471","Science G9: Describing the main events in the - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-2 Describing the main events in the stages of the cell cycle and comparing between mitosis and meiosis.","1. Illustrating the two main stages of the cell cycle (interphase and cell division), describing the changes and events associated with each stage, identifying their duration, and providing examples.","What is the process called when a cell prepares for cell division by replicating its DNA?","Interphase","Mitosis","Meiosis","Cytokinesis","Interphase","Interphase is the stage of the cell cycle where the cell prepares for cell division by replicating its DNA.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-stage-of-c78bd4e5","Science G9: Describing the main events in the - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-2 Describing the main events in the stages of the cell cycle and comparing between mitosis and meiosis.","4. Defining meiosis, describing its stages, comparing (using diagrams) what occurs during anaphase I and anaphase II in the division processes, and distinguishing their different forms.","What is the stage of meiosis where the sister chromatids separate?","Meiosis I","Meiosis II","Prophase","Telophase","Meiosis II","Meiosis II is the stage of meiosis where the sister chromatids separate.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-process-called-348a14af","Science G9: Describing the main events in the - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-2 Describing the main events in the stages of the cell cycle and comparing between mitosis and meiosis.","4. Defining meiosis, describing its stages, comparing (using diagrams) what occurs during anaphase I and anaphase II in the division processes, and distinguishing their different forms.","What is the process called when a cell divides into four non-identical daughter cells?","Mitosis","Meiosis","Photosynthesis","Respiration","Meiosis","Meiosis is the process of cell division that results in four non-identical daughter cells.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-type-of-cell-1d0e83e4","Science G9: Describing the main events in the - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-2 Describing the main events in the stages of the cell cycle and comparing between mitosis and meiosis.","5. Comparing mitosis and meiosis in terms of importance, stages, outcomes, and the types of cells in which each division occurs.","Which type of cell division produces gametes?","Mitosis","Meiosis","Both mitosis and meiosis","Neither mitosis nor meiosis","Meiosis","Meiosis is the type of cell division that produces gametes, which are reproductive cells.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-term-process-8511c804","Science G9: Describing the main events in the - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-2 Describing the main events in the stages of the cell cycle and comparing between mitosis and meiosis.","4. Defining meiosis, describing its stages, comparing (using diagrams) what occurs during anaphase I and anaphase II in the division processes, and distinguishing their different forms.","What is the term for the process by which the sister chromatids are separated during cell division?","Chromatin condensation","Chromosome separation","Sister chromatid separation","Cellular division","Sister chromatid separation","Sister chromatid separation is the process by which the sister chromatids are separated during cell division, resulting in two identical sets of chromosomes.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-in-stage-of-773c58b0","Science G9: Describing the main events in the - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-2 Describing the main events in the stages of the cell cycle and comparing between mitosis and meiosis.","4. Defining meiosis, describing its stages, comparing (using diagrams) what occurs during anaphase I and anaphase II in the division processes, and distinguishing their different forms.","In which stage of meiosis does crossing over occur?","Prophase I","Metaphase I","Anaphase I","Telophase I","Prophase I","Crossing over occurs during prophase I of meiosis, where genetic material is exchanged between homologous chromosomes.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-term-process-0a72d35b","Science G9: Describing the main events in the - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-2 Describing the main events in the stages of the cell cycle and comparing between mitosis and meiosis.","4. Defining meiosis, describing its stages, comparing (using diagrams) what occurs during anaphase I and anaphase II in the division processes, and distinguishing their different forms.","What is the term for the process by which the number of chromosomes is reduced by half during meiosis?","Meiotic reduction","Genetic reduction","Chromosomal reduction","Haploidization","Meiotic reduction","Meiotic reduction is the reduction of chromosome number by half during meiosis, producing haploid gametes.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-main-difference-81c91a7d","Science G9: Describing the main events in the - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-2 Describing the main events in the stages of the cell cycle and comparing between mitosis and meiosis.","5. Comparing mitosis and meiosis in terms of importance, stages, outcomes, and the types of cells in which each division occurs.","What is the main difference between mitosis and meiosis in terms of the number of daughter cells produced?","Mitosis produces 2 daughter cells, while meiosis produces 4 daughter cells","Mitosis produces 4 daughter cells, while meiosis produces 2 daughter cells","Mitosis produces 1 daughter cell, while meiosis produces 2 daughter cells","Mitosis produces 2 daughter cells, while meiosis produces 1 daughter cell","Mitosis produces 2 daughter cells, while meiosis produces 4 daughter cells","Mitosis produces 2 daughter cells, while meiosis produces 4 daughter cells.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-primary-purpose-de009ce0","Science G9: Describing the main events in the - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-2 Describing the main events in the stages of the cell cycle and comparing between mitosis and meiosis.","5. Comparing mitosis and meiosis in terms of importance, stages, outcomes, and the types of cells in which each division occurs.","What is the primary purpose of mitosis in a cell?","To produce gametes for reproduction","To replace damaged or old cells","To increase the number of cells in the body","To repair damaged DNA","To increase the number of cells in the body","Mitosis is the process by which a cell divides into two daughter cells that are genetically identical to the parent cell, increasing the number of cells in the body.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-term-process-2d962e94","Science G9: Describing the main events in the - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-2 Describing the main events in the stages of the cell cycle and comparing between mitosis and meiosis.","5. Comparing mitosis and meiosis in terms of importance, stages, outcomes, and the types of cells in which each division occurs.","What is the term for the process by which the genetic material is duplicated before cell division?","DNA replication","Chromatin condensation","Chromosome separation","Cellular division","DNA replication","DNA replication is the process by which the genetic material is duplicated before cell division, resulting in two identical sets of chromosomes.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-type-of-cell-6def1ade","Science G9: Describing the main events in the - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-2 Describing the main events in the stages of the cell cycle and comparing between mitosis and meiosis.","5. Comparing mitosis and meiosis in terms of importance, stages, outcomes, and the types of cells in which each division occurs.","Which type of cell division occurs in somatic cells?","Mitosis","Meiosis","Both mitosis and meiosis","Neither mitosis nor meiosis","Mitosis","Mitosis is the type of cell division that occurs in somatic cells, which are non-reproductive cells.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-in-type-of-3d85b54e","Science G9: Describing the main events in the - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-2 Describing the main events in the stages of the cell cycle and comparing between mitosis and meiosis.","5. Comparing mitosis and meiosis in terms of importance, stages, outcomes, and the types of cells in which each division occurs.","In which type of cell division does crossing over occur?","Mitosis","Meiosis","Both mitosis and meiosis","Neither mitosis nor meiosis","Meiosis","Crossing over is a process that occurs during meiosis, where genetic material is exchanged between homologous chromosomes.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-term-process-13745374","Science G9: Describing the main events in the - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-2 Describing the main events in the stages of the cell cycle and comparing between mitosis and meiosis.","4. Defining meiosis, describing its stages, comparing (using diagrams) what occurs during anaphase I and anaphase II in the division processes, and distinguishing their different forms.","What is the term for the process by which the chromosomes line up at the center of the cell during cell division?","Chromatin condensation","Chromosome separation","Metaphase alignment","Cellular division","Metaphase alignment","Metaphase alignment is the process by which the chromosomes line up at the center of the cell during cell division, preparing for separation.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-purpose-of-bf0bb6e4","Science G9: Describing the main events in the stages of the cell cycle - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-2 Describing the main events in the stages of the cell cycle and comparing between mitosis and meiosis.","5. Comparing mitosis and meiosis in terms of importance, stages, outcomes, and the types of cells in which each division occurs.","What is the purpose of cell division in the cell cycle?","To produce energy for the cell.","To repair damaged DNA.","To prepare the cell for cell division.","To divide the cell into two daughter cells.","To divide the cell into two daughter cells.","Cell division is the process by which a cell divides into two or more daughter cells.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-condition-of-3db90935","Science G9: Describing the main events in the stages of the cell cycle - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-2 Describing the main events in the stages of the cell cycle and comparing between mitosis and meiosis.","2. Explaining the importance of interphase and cell division, describing the condition of cells during these stages, and distinguishing them from active cells.","What is the condition of cells during interphase?","The cells are dormant and do not grow.","The cells are active and preparing for cell division.","The cells are in a dormant state and do not grow.","The cells are in a dormant state and are not preparing for cell division.","The cells are active and preparing for cell division.","During interphase, cells are active and preparing for cell division.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-purpose-of-53af172d","Science G9: Describing the main events in the stages of the cell cycle - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-2 Describing the main events in the stages of the cell cycle and comparing between mitosis and meiosis.","1. Illustrating the two main stages of the cell cycle (interphase and cell division), describing the changes and events associated with each stage, identifying their duration, and providing examples.","What is the purpose of interphase in the cell cycle?","To prepare the cell for cell division.","To divide the cell into two daughter cells.","To repair damaged DNA.","To produce energy for the cell.","To prepare the cell for cell division.","Interphase is the stage of the cell cycle where the cell grows, replicates its DNA, and prepares for cell division.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-main-difference-3c5f72c7","Science G9: Describing the main events in the stages of the cell cycle - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-2 Describing the main events in the stages of the cell cycle and comparing between mitosis and meiosis.","4. Defining meiosis, describing its stages, comparing (using diagrams) what occurs during anaphase I and anaphase II in the division processes, and distinguishing their different forms.","What is the main difference between mitosis and meiosis?","Mitosis results in four daughter cells, while meiosis results in one daughter cell.","Mitosis results in one daughter cell, while meiosis results in four daughter cells.","Mitosis results in genetically identical daughter cells, while meiosis results in genetically unique daughter cells.","Mitosis results in genetically unique daughter cells, while meiosis results in genetically identical daughter cells.","Mitosis results in genetically identical daughter cells, while meiosis results in genetically unique daughter cells.","Mitosis results in genetically identical daughter cells, while meiosis results in genetically unique daughter cells.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-stage-of-cell-45d679a1","Science G9: Describing the main events in the stages of the cell cycle - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-2 Describing the main events in the stages of the cell cycle and comparing between mitosis and meiosis.","1. Illustrating the two main stages of the cell cycle (interphase and cell division), describing the changes and events associated with each stage, identifying their duration, and providing examples.","What stage of the cell cycle is characterized by the cell growing and preparing for cell division?","Interphase is the stage where the cell grows and prepares for cell division.","Interphase is the stage where the cell divides into two daughter cells.","Interphase is the stage where the cell is in a dormant state.","Interphase is the stage where the cell is in a dormant state and does not grow.","Interphase is the stage where the cell grows and prepares for cell division.","Interphase is the stage of the cell cycle where the cell grows, replicates its DNA, and prepares for cell division.","/image-source/sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-stage-of-cell-45d679a1.png","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-main-difference-5f23a64a","Science G9: Describing the main events in the stages of the cell cycle - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-2 Describing the main events in the stages of the cell cycle and comparing between mitosis and meiosis.","5. Comparing mitosis and meiosis in terms of importance, stages, outcomes, and the types of cells in which each division occurs.","What is the main difference between mitosis and meiosis in terms of genetic variation?","Mitosis produces genetically unique daughter cells, while meiosis produces genetically identical daughter cells.","Mitosis produces genetically identical daughter cells, while meiosis produces genetically unique daughter cells.","Mitosis produces one daughter cell, while meiosis produces four daughter cells.","Mitosis produces four daughter cells, while meiosis produces one daughter cell.","Mitosis produces genetically identical daughter cells, while meiosis produces genetically unique daughter cells.","Mitosis produces genetically identical daughter cells, while meiosis produces genetically unique daughter cells.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-condition-of-660b2121","Science G9: Describing the main events in the stages of the cell cycle - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-2 Describing the main events in the stages of the cell cycle and comparing between mitosis and meiosis.","2. Explaining the importance of interphase and cell division, describing the condition of cells during these stages, and distinguishing them from active cells.","What is the condition of cells during meiosis?","The cells are dormant and do not grow.","The cells are active and preparing for cell division.","The cells are in a dormant state and do not grow.","The cells are undergoing genetic recombination and producing genetically unique daughter cells.","The cells are undergoing genetic recombination and producing genetically unique daughter cells.","During meiosis, cells undergo genetic recombination and produce genetically unique daughter cells.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-importance-of-4bf0ae79","Science G9: Describing the main events in the stages of the cell cycle - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-2 Describing the main events in the stages of the cell cycle and comparing between mitosis and meiosis.","1. Illustrating the two main stages of the cell cycle (interphase and cell division), describing the changes and events associated with each stage, identifying their duration, and providing examples.","What is the importance of interphase in the cell cycle?","It allows the cell to produce energy for cell division.","It allows the cell to repair damaged DNA.","It allows the cell to prepare for cell division.","It allows the cell to divide into two daughter cells.","It allows the cell to prepare for cell division.","Interphase is the stage of the cell cycle where the cell grows, replicates its DNA, and prepares for cell division.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-main-difference-15918726","Science G9: Describing the main events in the stages of the cell cycle - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-2 Describing the main events in the stages of the cell cycle and comparing between mitosis and meiosis.","5. Comparing mitosis and meiosis in terms of importance, stages, outcomes, and the types of cells in which each division occurs.","What is the main difference between mitosis and meiosis in terms of the number of daughter cells produced?","Mitosis produces two daughter cells, while meiosis produces four daughter cells.","Mitosis produces four daughter cells, while meiosis produces two daughter cells.","Mitosis produces one daughter cell, while meiosis produces one daughter cell.","Mitosis and meiosis produce the same number of daughter cells.","Mitosis produces two daughter cells, while meiosis produces four daughter cells.","Mitosis produces two daughter cells, while meiosis produces four daughter cells.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-condition-of-2940ec09","Science G9: Describing the main events in the stages of the cell cycle - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-2 Describing the main events in the stages of the cell cycle and comparing between mitosis and meiosis.","2. Explaining the importance of interphase and cell division, describing the condition of cells during these stages, and distinguishing them from active cells.","What is the condition of cells during cell division?","The cells are dormant and do not grow.","The cells are active and preparing for cell division.","The cells are in a dormant state and do not grow.","The cells are dividing into two daughter cells.","The cells are dividing into two daughter cells.","During cell division, cells are actively dividing into two daughter cells.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-phase-of-6bbc0188","Science G9: Describing the main events in the stages of the cell cycle - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-2 Describing the main events in the stages of the cell cycle and comparing between mitosis and meiosis.","4. Defining meiosis, describing its stages, comparing (using diagrams) what occurs during anaphase I and anaphase II in the division processes, and distinguishing their different forms.","What is the phase of mitosis where the chromosomes line up at the metaphase plate?","Prophase is the phase of mitosis where the chromosomes line up at the metaphase plate.","Metaphase is the phase of mitosis where the chromosomes line up at the metaphase plate.","Anaphase is the phase of mitosis where the chromosomes line up at the metaphase plate.","Telophase is the phase of mitosis where the chromosomes line up at the metaphase plate.","Metaphase is the phase of mitosis where the chromosomes line up at the metaphase plate.","Metaphase is the phase of mitosis where the chromosomes line up at the metaphase plate.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-final-phase-76a5a38b","Science G9: Describing the main events in the stages of the cell cycle - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-2 Describing the main events in the stages of the cell cycle and comparing between mitosis and meiosis.","3. Defining mitosis and identifying its successive phases and the changes that occur in the cell during each phase.","What is the final phase of mitosis where the nuclear envelope reforms around each set of chromosomes?","Telophase is the final phase of mitosis.","Cytokinesis is the final phase of mitosis.","Prophase is the final phase of mitosis.","Metaphase is the final phase of mitosis.","Telophase is the final phase of mitosis.","Telophase is the final phase of mitosis where the nuclear envelope reforms around each set of chromosomes.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-process-called-5b134276","Science G9: Describing the main events in the stages of the cell cycle - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-2 Describing the main events in the stages of the cell cycle and comparing between mitosis and meiosis.","5. Comparing mitosis and meiosis in terms of importance, stages, outcomes, and the types of cells in which each division occurs.","What is the process called when a cell divides into two daughter cells that are genetically identical to the parent cell?","Mitosis is the process of cell division that results in four daughter cells.","Meiosis is the process of cell division that results in four daughter cells.","Mitosis is the process of cell division that results in two daughter cells.","Meiosis is the process of cell division that results in two daughter cells.","Mitosis is the process of cell division that results in two daughter cells.","Mitosis is the process of cell division that results in two daughter cells that are genetically identical to the parent cell.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-first-phase-70b13178","Science G9: Describing the main events in the stages of the cell cycle - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-2 Describing the main events in the stages of the cell cycle and comparing between mitosis and meiosis.","4. Defining meiosis, describing its stages, comparing (using diagrams) what occurs during anaphase I and anaphase II in the division processes, and distinguishing their different forms.","What is the first phase of mitosis where the chromatin condenses into visible chromosomes?","Interphase is the first phase of mitosis.","Prophase is the first phase of mitosis.","Metaphase is the first phase of mitosis.","Anaphase is the first phase of mitosis.","Prophase is the first phase of mitosis.","Prophase is the first phase of mitosis where the chromatin condenses into visible chromosomes.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-happens-to-sister-0c0564ac","Science G9: Describing the main events in the stages of the cell cycle - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-2 Describing the main events in the stages of the cell cycle and comparing between mitosis and meiosis.","4. Defining meiosis, describing its stages, comparing (using diagrams) what occurs during anaphase I and anaphase II in the division processes, and distinguishing their different forms.","What happens to the sister chromatids during anaphase of mitosis?","They move towards the center of the cell.","They move towards opposite poles of the cell.","They remain attached at the centromere.","They break apart at the centromere.","They break apart at the centromere.","During anaphase of mitosis, the sister chromatids break apart at the centromere and move towards opposite poles of the cell.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-term-process-1ebe0408","Science G9: Describing the main events in the stages of the cell cycle - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-2 Describing the main events in the stages of the cell cycle and comparing between mitosis and meiosis.","5. Comparing mitosis and meiosis in terms of importance, stages, outcomes, and the types of cells in which each division occurs.","What is the term for the process of cell division that results in four non-identical daughter cells?","Meiosis is the process of cell division that results in four identical daughter cells.","Mitosis is the process of cell division that results in four non-identical daughter cells.","Meiosis is the process of cell division that results in four non-identical daughter cells.","Mitosis is the process of cell division that results in two daughter cells.","Meiosis is the process of cell division that results in four non-identical daughter cells.","Meiosis is the process of cell division that results in four non-identical daughter cells.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-final-phase-7a319c08","Science G9: Describing the main events in the stages of the cell cycle - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-2 Describing the main events in the stages of the cell cycle and comparing between mitosis and meiosis.","5. Comparing mitosis and meiosis in terms of importance, stages, outcomes, and the types of cells in which each division occurs.","What is the final phase of meiosis where the cytoplasm divides and the daughter cells are separated?","Telophase is the final phase of meiosis.","Cytokinesis is the final phase of meiosis.","Prophase is the final phase of meiosis.","Metaphase is the final phase of meiosis.","Cytokinesis is the final phase of meiosis.","Cytokinesis is the final phase of meiosis where the cytoplasm divides and the daughter cells are separated.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-term-process-bc787d55","Science G9: Describing the main events in the stages of the cell cycle - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-2 Describing the main events in the stages of the cell cycle and comparing between mitosis and meiosis.","5. Comparing mitosis and meiosis in terms of importance, stages, outcomes, and the types of cells in which each division occurs.","What is the term for the process of cell division that results in four non-identical daughter cells, with genetic variation?","Meiosis is the process of cell division that results in four identical daughter cells.","Mitosis is the process of cell division that results in four non-identical daughter cells.","Meiosis is the process of cell division that results in four non-identical daughter cells with genetic variation.","Mitosis is the process of cell division that results in two daughter cells.","Meiosis is the process of cell division that results in four non-identical daughter cells with genetic variation.","Meiosis is the process of cell division that results in four non-identical daughter cells with genetic variation.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-phase-of-8a93d599","Science G9: Describing the main events in the stages of the cell cycle - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-2 Describing the main events in the stages of the cell cycle and comparing between mitosis and meiosis.","4. Defining meiosis, describing its stages, comparing (using diagrams) what occurs during anaphase I and anaphase II in the division processes, and distinguishing their different forms.","What is the phase of mitosis where the nuclear envelope breaks down?","Prophase is the phase of mitosis where the nuclear envelope breaks down.","Metaphase is the phase of mitosis where the nuclear envelope breaks down.","Anaphase is the phase of mitosis where the nuclear envelope breaks down.","Telophase is the phase of mitosis where the nuclear envelope breaks down.","Prophase is the phase of mitosis where the nuclear envelope breaks down.","Prophase is the phase of mitosis where the nuclear envelope breaks down.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-term-process-9ac70424","Science G9: Describing the main events in the stages of the cell cycle - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-2 Describing the main events in the stages of the cell cycle and comparing between mitosis and meiosis.","5. Comparing mitosis and meiosis in terms of importance, stages, outcomes, and the types of cells in which each division occurs.","What is the term for the process of cell division that results in two genetically identical daughter cells?","Meiosis is the process of cell division that results in four daughter cells.","Mitosis is the process of cell division that results in four non-identical daughter cells.","Mitosis is the process of cell division that results in two daughter cells.","Meiosis is the process of cell division that results in two daughter cells.","Mitosis is the process of cell division that results in two daughter cells.","Mitosis is the process of cell division that results in two genetically identical daughter cells.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-term-stage-9c6b292a","Science G9: Describing the main events in the stages of the cell cycle - Set 4","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-2 Describing the main events in the stages of the cell cycle and comparing between mitosis and meiosis.","4. Defining meiosis, describing its stages, comparing (using diagrams) what occurs during anaphase I and anaphase II in the division processes, and distinguishing their different forms.","What is the term for the stage of meiosis where homologous chromosomes separate?","Anaphase I","Anaphase II","Metaphase I","Telophase II","Anaphase I","Anaphase I is the stage of meiosis where homologous chromosomes separate.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-process-called-f6bf172b","Science G9: Describing the main events in the stages of the cell cycle - Set 4","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-2 Describing the main events in the stages of the cell cycle and comparing between mitosis and meiosis.","5. Comparing mitosis and meiosis in terms of importance, stages, outcomes, and the types of cells in which each division occurs.","What is the process called when a cell divides into two daughter cells with the same number of chromosomes as the parent cell?","Mitosis","Meiosis","Photosynthesis","Respiration","Mitosis","Mitosis is the process of cell division that results in two daughter cells with the same number of chromosomes as the parent cell.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-main-difference-400f438c","Science G9: Describing the main events in the stages of the cell cycle - Set 4","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-2 Describing the main events in the stages of the cell cycle and comparing between mitosis and meiosis.","4. Defining meiosis, describing its stages, comparing (using diagrams) what occurs during anaphase I and anaphase II in the division processes, and distinguishing their different forms.","What is the main difference between meiosis and mitosis?","Meiosis occurs in somatic cells, while mitosis occurs in gametes","Meiosis occurs in gametes, while mitosis occurs in somatic cells","Meiosis produces gametes with the same number of chromosomes as the parent cell, while mitosis produces somatic cells with the same number of chromosomes as the parent cell","Meiosis produces gametes with half the number of chromosomes as the parent cell, while mitosis produces somatic cells with the same number of chromosomes as the parent cell","Meiosis produces gametes with half the number of chromosomes as the parent cell, while mitosis produces somatic cells with the same number of chromosomes as the parent cell","Meiosis produces gametes with half the number of chromosomes as the parent cell, while mitosis produces somatic cells with the same number of chromosomes as the parent cell.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-term-stage-765674bf","Science G9: Describing the main events in the stages of the cell cycle - Set 4","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-2 Describing the main events in the stages of the cell cycle and comparing between mitosis and meiosis.","4. Defining meiosis, describing its stages, comparing (using diagrams) what occurs during anaphase I and anaphase II in the division processes, and distinguishing their different forms.","What is the term for the stage of meiosis where homologous chromosomes align at the metaphase plate?","Metaphase I","Metaphase II","Anaphase I","Telophase II","Metaphase I","Metaphase I is the stage of meiosis where homologous chromosomes align at the metaphase plate.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-result-of-46811d89","Science G9: Describing the main events in the stages of the cell cycle - Set 4","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-2 Describing the main events in the stages of the cell cycle and comparing between mitosis and meiosis.","4. Defining meiosis, describing its stages, comparing (using diagrams) what occurs during anaphase I and anaphase II in the division processes, and distinguishing their different forms.","What is the result of telophase II in the cell cycle?","Two daughter cells with half the number of chromosomes as the parent cell","Two daughter cells with the same number of chromosomes as the parent cell","One daughter cell with half the number of chromosomes as the parent cell","No change in the number of chromosomes","Two daughter cells with the same number of chromosomes as the parent cell","Telophase II results in two daughter cells with the same number of chromosomes as the parent cell, as sister chromatids separate.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-occurs-during-stage-85a814d0","Science G9: Describing the main events in the stages of the cell cycle - Set 4","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-2 Describing the main events in the stages of the cell cycle and comparing between mitosis and meiosis.","4. Defining meiosis, describing its stages, comparing (using diagrams) what occurs during anaphase I and anaphase II in the division processes, and distinguishing their different forms.","What occurs during the stage of meiosis called prophase I?","Homologous chromosomes align at the metaphase plate","Homologous chromosomes separate at the anaphase plate","Crossing over occurs between homologous chromosomes","No change occurs in the chromosomes","Crossing over occurs between homologous chromosomes","Crossing over occurs between homologous chromosomes during prophase I in meiosis.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-main-purpose-15704bf2","Science G9: Describing the main events in the stages of the cell cycle - Set 4","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-2 Describing the main events in the stages of the cell cycle and comparing between mitosis and meiosis.","4. Defining meiosis, describing its stages, comparing (using diagrams) what occurs during anaphase I and anaphase II in the division processes, and distinguishing their different forms.","What is the main purpose of meiosis?","To produce gametes with the same number of chromosomes as the parent cell","To produce gametes with half the number of chromosomes as the parent cell","To increase the genetic variation in offspring","To decrease the genetic variation in offspring","To produce gametes with half the number of chromosomes as the parent cell","Meiosis produces gametes with half the number of chromosomes as the parent cell, which is essential for sexual reproduction.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-result-of-95608a66","Science G9: Describing the main events in the stages of the cell cycle - Set 4","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-2 Describing the main events in the stages of the cell cycle and comparing between mitosis and meiosis.","4. Defining meiosis, describing its stages, comparing (using diagrams) what occurs during anaphase I and anaphase II in the division processes, and distinguishing their different forms.","What is the result of anaphase II in the cell cycle?","Two daughter cells with half the number of chromosomes as the parent cell","Two daughter cells with the same number of chromosomes as the parent cell","One daughter cell with half the number of chromosomes as the parent cell","No change in the number of chromosomes","Two daughter cells with the same number of chromosomes as the parent cell","Anaphase II results in two daughter cells with the same number of chromosomes as the parent cell, as sister chromatids separate.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-purpose-of-b32b32b9","Science G9: Describing the main events in the stages of the cell cycle - Set 4","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-2 Describing the main events in the stages of the cell cycle and comparing between mitosis and meiosis.","4. Defining meiosis, describing its stages, comparing (using diagrams) what occurs during anaphase I and anaphase II in the division processes, and distinguishing their different forms.","What is the purpose of crossing over during meiosis?","To increase the genetic variation in offspring","To decrease the genetic variation in offspring","To ensure that all offspring have the same genetic traits","To prevent genetic variation in offspring","To increase the genetic variation in offspring","Crossing over during meiosis increases genetic variation in offspring by shuffling genes between homologous chromosomes.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-main-difference-c0a0e571","Science G9: Describing the main events in the stages of the cell cycle - Set 4","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-2 Describing the main events in the stages of the cell cycle and comparing between mitosis and meiosis.","4. Defining meiosis, describing its stages, comparing (using diagrams) what occurs during anaphase I and anaphase II in the division processes, and distinguishing their different forms.","What is the main difference between anaphase I and anaphase II in the cell cycle?","Anaphase I occurs in mitosis, while anaphase II occurs in meiosis","Anaphase I occurs in meiosis, while anaphase II occurs in mitosis","Anaphase I and anaphase II occur in both mitosis and meiosis","Anaphase I and anaphase II are the same in both mitosis and meiosis","Anaphase I occurs in meiosis, while anaphase II occurs in mitosis","Anaphase I occurs in meiosis, while anaphase II occurs in mitosis. Anaphase I separates homologous chromosomes, while anaphase II separates sister chromatids.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-outcome-of-6ca8635f","Science G9: Describing the main events in the stages of the cell cycle - Set 5","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-2 Describing the main events in the stages of the cell cycle and comparing between mitosis and meiosis.","1. Illustrating the two main stages of the cell cycle (interphase and cell division), describing the changes and events associated with each stage, identifying their duration, and providing examples.","What is the outcome of meiosis?","Two daughter cells with the same number of chromosomes as the parent cell","Four daughter cells with the same number of chromosomes as the parent cell","Two daughter cells with half the number of chromosomes as the parent cell","Four daughter cells with half the number of chromosomes as the parent cell","Four daughter cells with half the number of chromosomes as the parent cell","Meiosis results in four daughter cells, each with half the number of chromosomes as the parent cell, which is important for sexual reproduction.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-type-of-cell-60b4ac5b","Science G9: Describing the main events in the stages of the cell cycle - Set 5","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-2 Describing the main events in the stages of the cell cycle and comparing between mitosis and meiosis.","5. Comparing mitosis and meiosis in terms of importance, stages, outcomes, and the types of cells in which each division occurs.","Which type of cell division is responsible for the production of gametes?","Mitosis","Meiosis","Binary fission","Endoreduplication","Meiosis","Meiosis is the type of cell division that results in the production of gametes, such as egg and sperm cells, which are necessary for sexual reproduction.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-purpose-of-4dc3fc24","Science G9: Describing the main events in the stages of the cell cycle - Set 5","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-2 Describing the main events in the stages of the cell cycle and comparing between mitosis and meiosis.","5. Comparing mitosis and meiosis in terms of importance, stages, outcomes, and the types of cells in which each division occurs.","What is the purpose of the spindle fibers in mitosis?","To replicate DNA","To synthesize proteins","To separate chromosomes","To repair damaged DNA","To separate chromosomes","The spindle fibers are responsible for separating the chromosomes during mitosis, ensuring that each daughter cell receives a complete set of chromosomes.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-stage-of-mitosis-70a89d8f","Science G9: Describing the main events in the stages of the cell cycle - Set 5","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-2 Describing the main events in the stages of the cell cycle and comparing between mitosis and meiosis.","4. Defining meiosis, describing its stages, comparing (using diagrams) what occurs during anaphase I and anaphase II in the division processes, and distinguishing their different forms.","Which stage of mitosis is characterized by the alignment of chromosomes at the center of the cell?","Prophase","Metaphase","Anaphase","Telophase","Metaphase","During metaphase, the chromosomes line up at the center of the cell, attached to the spindle fibers, in preparation for separation.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-in-type-of-c32ba4d3","Science G9: Describing the main events in the stages of the cell cycle - Set 5","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-2 Describing the main events in the stages of the cell cycle and comparing between mitosis and meiosis.","5. Comparing mitosis and meiosis in terms of importance, stages, outcomes, and the types of cells in which each division occurs.","In which type of cells does meiosis occur?","Somatic cells","Reproductive cells","Stem cells","Nerve cells","Reproductive cells","Meiosis occurs in reproductive cells, such as egg and sperm cells, to produce gametes with half the number of chromosomes as the parent cell.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-primary-difference-38cb02a4","Science G9: Describing the main events in the stages of the cell cycle - Set 5","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-2 Describing the main events in the stages of the cell cycle and comparing between mitosis and meiosis.","5. Comparing mitosis and meiosis in terms of importance, stages, outcomes, and the types of cells in which each division occurs.","What is the primary difference between mitosis and meiosis?","The number of cell divisions","The type of cells involved","The number of chromosomes in the daughter cells","The purpose of the cell division","The number of chromosomes in the daughter cells","Mitosis results in two daughter cells with the same number of chromosomes as the parent cell, while meiosis results in four daughter cells with half the number of chromosomes as the parent cell.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-stage-of-cell-68205bf9","Science G9: Describing the main events in the stages of the cell cycle - Set 5","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-2 Describing the main events in the stages of the cell cycle and comparing between mitosis and meiosis.","2. Explaining the importance of interphase and cell division, describing the condition of cells during these stages, and distinguishing them from active cells.","Which stage of the cell cycle is characterized by the replication of DNA?","Prophase","Metaphase","Anaphase","Interphase","Interphase","During interphase, the cell grows, replicates its DNA, and prepares for cell division.","/image-source/sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-stage-of-cell-68205bf9.png","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-main-purpose-aa1dc8d1","Science G9: Describing the main events in the stages of the cell cycle - Set 5","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-2 Describing the main events in the stages of the cell cycle and comparing between mitosis and meiosis.","4. Defining meiosis, describing its stages, comparing (using diagrams) what occurs during anaphase I and anaphase II in the division processes, and distinguishing their different forms.","What is the main purpose of mitosis in a cell?","To produce gametes for reproduction","To repair damaged tissues","To produce a new individual organism","To replace old or damaged cells","To replace old or damaged cells","The cell grows, replicates its DNA, and prepares for cell division.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-why-is-meiosis-9cdd63b0","Science G9: Describing the main events in the stages of the cell cycle - Set 5","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-2 Describing the main events in the stages of the cell cycle and comparing between mitosis and meiosis.","5. Comparing mitosis and meiosis in terms of importance, stages, outcomes, and the types of cells in which each division occurs.","Why is meiosis important for genetic diversity?","It produces genetically identical offspring","It increases the number of chromosomes in the offspring","It shuffles the genetic material to produce unique offspring","It decreases the number of chromosomes in the offspring","It shuffles the genetic material to produce unique offspring","Meiosis is important for genetic diversity because it shuffles the genetic material through crossing over and independent assortment, resulting in unique offspring with a mix of traits from the parents.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-result-of-73506a5f","Science G9: Describing the main events in the stages of the cell cycle - Set 5","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-2 Describing the main events in the stages of the cell cycle and comparing between mitosis and meiosis.","5. Comparing mitosis and meiosis in terms of importance, stages, outcomes, and the types of cells in which each division occurs.","What is the result of mitosis in a somatic cell?","Two daughter cells with half the number of chromosomes as the parent cell","Two daughter cells with the same number of chromosomes as the parent cell","Four daughter cells with the same number of chromosomes as the parent cell","Four daughter cells with half the number of chromosomes as the parent cell","Two daughter cells with the same number of chromosomes as the parent cell","Mitosis in a somatic cell results in two daughter cells that are genetically identical to the parent cell, with the same number of chromosomes.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-relationship-velocity-24b04c5e","Science G9: Describing the movement of a body based on the concepts - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-1 Describing the movement of a body based on the concepts of the main elements of movement and distinguishing between them.","3. Explaining the relationship between acceleration, velocity, displacement, time and direction of motion theoretically and mathematically.","What is the relationship between velocity and time?","Velocity is directly proportional to time","Velocity is inversely proportional to time","Velocity is not related to time","0.4 m/s","0.4 m/s","Using the formula v = u + at, where u = 20 m/s, a = -9.8 m/s^2 (gravity), and t = 2 s, the velocity is 20 - (9.8 * 2) = 0.4 m/s.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-an-object-moves-72bfbb5c","Science G9: Describing the movement of a body based on the concepts - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-1 Describing the movement of a body based on the concepts of the main elements of movement and distinguishing between them.","4. Identifying the circular motion, show the effect of the centripetal force on it, and provide an example of it.","An object moves 20 meters to the east and then 15 meters to the west. What is its total displacement?","35 meters","20 meters","5 meters","15 meters","5 meters","Total displacement is calculated by subtracting the distance traveled in the opposite direction from the distance traveled in the original direction, resulting in 20 meters - 15 meters = 5 meters.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-term-distance-7fa77c41","Science G9: Describing the movement of a body based on the concepts - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-1 Describing the movement of a body based on the concepts of the main elements of movement and distinguishing between them.","3. Explaining the relationship between acceleration, velocity, displacement, time and direction of motion theoretically and mathematically.","What is the term for the distance traveled by an object in a specific direction?","Displacement","Velocity","Speed","Acceleration","Displacement","Displacement refers to the distance traveled by an object in a specific direction, which can be different from the total distance traveled.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-a-car-travels-815a44b4","Science G9: Describing the movement of a body based on the concepts - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-1 Describing the movement of a body based on the concepts of the main elements of movement and distinguishing between them.","3. Explaining the relationship between acceleration, velocity, displacement, time and direction of motion theoretically and mathematically.","A car travels 50 meters in 5 seconds. What is its average velocity?","10 meters per second","5 meters per second","50 meters per second","25 meters per second","10 meters per second","Average velocity is calculated by dividing the total displacement by the total time taken, which in this case is 50 meters divided by 5 seconds, resulting in 10 meters per second.","/image-source/sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-a-car-travels-815a44b4.png","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-term-rate-07851735","Science G9: Describing the movement of a body based on the concepts - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-1 Describing the movement of a body based on the concepts of the main elements of movement and distinguishing between them.","3. Explaining the relationship between acceleration, velocity, displacement, time and direction of motion theoretically and mathematically.","What is the term for the rate of change of velocity?","Speed","Acceleration","Displacement","Velocity","Acceleration","Acceleration is the rate of change of velocity, which means it is the change in velocity over a given period of time.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-an-object-moves-de6bb74c","Science G9: Describing the movement of a body based on the concepts - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-1 Describing the movement of a body based on the concepts of the main elements of movement and distinguishing between them.","4. Identifying the circular motion, show the effect of the centripetal force on it, and provide an example of it.","An object moves in a circular path with a constant speed. What is its acceleration?","Zero","Constant","Increasing","Decreasing","Constant","Even though the object's speed is constant, it is still accelerating because its direction is changing as it moves in a circular path.","/image-source/sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-an-object-moves-de6bb74c.png","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-a-car-accelerates-90c494ab","Science G9: Describing the movement of a body based on the concepts - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-1 Describing the movement of a body based on the concepts of the main elements of movement and distinguishing between them.","4. Identifying the circular motion, show the effect of the centripetal force on it, and provide an example of it.","A car accelerates from rest to a velocity of 30 meters per second in 6 seconds. What is its average acceleration?","5 meters per second squared","10 meters per second squared","15 meters per second squared","20 meters per second squared","5 meters per second squared","Average acceleration is calculated by dividing the change in velocity by the time taken, which in this case is (30 meters per second - 0 meters per second) / 6 seconds = 5 meters per second squared.","/image-source/sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-a-car-accelerates-90c494ab.png","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-relationship-acceleration-a055a808","Science G9: Describing the movement of a body based on the concepts - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-1 Describing the movement of a body based on the concepts of the main elements of movement and distinguishing between them.","3. Explaining the relationship between acceleration, velocity, displacement, time and direction of motion theoretically and mathematically.","What is the relationship between acceleration and velocity?","Acceleration is the same as velocity","Acceleration is the rate of change of velocity","Acceleration is the opposite of velocity","Acceleration is not related to velocity","Acceleration is the rate of change of velocity","Acceleration is the rate of change of velocity, which means it is the change in velocity over a given period of time.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-term-change-1d47dd77","Science G9: Describing the movement of a body based on the concepts - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-1 Describing the movement of a body based on the concepts of the main elements of movement and distinguishing between them.","3. Explaining the relationship between acceleration, velocity, displacement, time and direction of motion theoretically and mathematically.","What is the term for the change in position of an object over time?","Displacement","Velocity","Acceleration","Motion","Displacement","Displacement refers to the change in position of an object over time, which can be measured in terms of distance and direction.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-a-ball-is-d287e196","Science G9: Describing the movement of a body based on the concepts - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-1 Describing the movement of a body based on the concepts of the main elements of movement and distinguishing between them.","4. Identifying the circular motion, show the effect of the centripetal force on it, and provide an example of it.","A ball is thrown upwards at an initial velocity of 20 meters per second. What is its velocity after 2 seconds, assuming a constant acceleration due to gravity of 9.8 meters per second squared?","20 meters per second","0 meters per second","38.8 meters per second","1.2 meters per second","0 meters per second","The ball's velocity will decrease due to gravity, and after 2 seconds, its velocity will be 20 meters per second - (9.8 meters per second squared * 2 seconds) = 0 meters per second.","/image-source/sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-a-ball-is-d287e196.png","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-relationship-speed-858f1cd5","Science G9: Describing the movement of a body based on the concepts - Set 4","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-1 Describing the movement of a body based on the concepts of the main elements of movement and distinguishing between them.","4. Identifying the circular motion, show the effect of the centripetal force on it, and provide an example of it.","What is the relationship between the speed of an object in circular motion and the radius of the circular path?","Speed increases with the radius","Speed decreases with the radius","Speed remains the same with the radius","Speed is independent of the radius","Speed decreases with the radius","The speed of an object in circular motion decreases with the radius of the circular path, as the force required to keep an object moving in a circular path decreases with increasing radius.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-effect-of-e4241046","Science G9: Describing the movement of a body based on the concepts - Set 4","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-1 Describing the movement of a body based on the concepts of the main elements of movement and distinguishing between them.","4. Identifying the circular motion, show the effect of the centripetal force on it, and provide an example of it.","What is the effect of increasing the mass of an object in circular motion on the centripetal force required?","The centripetal force decreases","The centripetal force increases","The centripetal force remains the same","The centripetal force becomes zero","The centripetal force increases","Increasing the mass of an object in circular motion requires an increase in the centripetal force to maintain its circular path.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-relationship-centripetal-55097c64","Science G9: Describing the movement of a body based on the concepts - Set 4","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-1 Describing the movement of a body based on the concepts of the main elements of movement and distinguishing between them.","4. Identifying the circular motion, show the effect of the centripetal force on it, and provide an example of it.","What is the relationship between centripetal force and the radius of a circular path?","Centripetal force increases with the radius","Centripetal force decreases with the radius","Centripetal force remains the same with the radius","Centripetal force is independent of the radius","Centripetal force decreases with the radius","Centripetal force decreases with the radius of a circular path, as the force required to keep an object moving in a circular path decreases with increasing radius.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-an-example-5a3b1de8","Science G9: Describing the movement of a body based on the concepts - Set 4","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-1 Describing the movement of a body based on the concepts of the main elements of movement and distinguishing between them.","4. Identifying the circular motion, show the effect of the centripetal force on it, and provide an example of it.","What is an example of circular motion in everyday life?","A car moving on a straight road","A ball rolling on the ground","A satellite orbiting the Earth","A person walking in a straight line","A satellite orbiting the Earth","A satellite orbiting the Earth is an example of circular motion, where the satellite moves in a circular path due to the gravitational force acting on it.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-force-keeps-66fa7748","Science G9: Describing the movement of a body based on the concepts - Set 4","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-1 Describing the movement of a body based on the concepts of the main elements of movement and distinguishing between them.","4. Identifying the circular motion, show the effect of the centripetal force on it, and provide an example of it.","What is the force that keeps an object moving in a circular path?","Centrifugal force","Centripetal force","Frictional force","Normal force","Centripetal force","Centripetal force is the force that acts towards the center of a circle, keeping an object moving in a circular path.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-type-of-motion-dacab814","Science G9: Describing the movement of a body based on the concepts - Set 4","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-3 Understanding the concept of friction force, its types, and how it affects the movement of objects.","2. Defining the force of friction, give examples of it, and explaining how friction affects motion.","What type of motion is characterized by an object moving in a circular path?","An object moving in a straight line","An object moving in a circular path","An object moving in a zigzag pattern","An object at rest","An object moving in a circular path","Circular motion is a type of motion where an object moves in a circular path due to a force acting towards the center of the circle.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-effect-of-70d83107","Science G9: Describing the movement of a body based on the concepts - Set 4","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-1 Describing the movement of a body based on the concepts of the main elements of movement and distinguishing between them.","4. Identifying the circular motion, show the effect of the centripetal force on it, and provide an example of it.","What is the effect of decreasing the centripetal force on an object in circular motion?","The object moves faster","The object moves slower","The object changes direction","The object falls towards the center","The object moves slower","Decreasing the centripetal force on an object in circular motion causes it to move slower, eventually leading to a loss of circular motion.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-happens-to-speed-33a43aa4","Science G9: Describing the movement of a body based on the concepts - Set 4","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-1 Describing the movement of a body based on the concepts of the main elements of movement and distinguishing between them.","4. Identifying the circular motion, show the effect of the centripetal force on it, and provide an example of it.","What happens to the speed of an object in circular motion if the centripetal force increases?","The speed decreases","The speed increases","The speed remains the same","The speed becomes zero","The speed increases","If the centripetal force increases, the speed of an object in circular motion also increases, allowing it to maintain its circular path.","/image-source/sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-happens-to-speed-33a43aa4.png","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-an-example-ee874f4c","Science G9: Describing the movement of a body based on the concepts - Set 4","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-1 Describing the movement of a body based on the concepts of the main elements of movement and distinguishing between them.","4. Identifying the circular motion, show the effect of the centripetal force on it, and provide an example of it.","What is an example of centripetal force in a real-world scenario?","A car moving on a straight road","A ball rolling on the ground","A satellite orbiting the Earth","A person walking in a circular path","A satellite orbiting the Earth","A satellite orbiting the Earth is an example of centripetal force, where the gravitational force acts towards the center of the Earth, keeping the satellite in a circular orbit.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-effect-of-911f64ef","Science G9: Describing the movement of a body based on the concepts - Set 4","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-1 Describing the movement of a body based on the concepts of the main elements of movement and distinguishing between them.","4. Identifying the circular motion, show the effect of the centripetal force on it, and provide an example of it.","What is the effect of decreasing the speed of an object in circular motion on the centripetal force required?","The centripetal force increases","The centripetal force decreases","The centripetal force remains the same","The centripetal force becomes zero","The centripetal force decreases","Decreasing the speed of an object in circular motion requires a decrease in the centripetal force to maintain its circular path.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-a-car-accelerates-bae0aa49","Science G9: Describing the movement of a body - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-1 Describing the movement of a body based on the concepts of the main elements of movement and distinguishing between them.","3. Explaining the relationship between acceleration, velocity, displacement, time and direction of motion theoretically and mathematically.","A car accelerates from 0 to 30 m/s in 10 seconds. What is its acceleration?","The car's acceleration is 3 m/s^2","The car's acceleration is 6 m/s^2","The car's acceleration is 9 m/s^2","The car's acceleration is 12 m/s^2","The car's acceleration is 6 m/s^2","To find the acceleration, we use the formula a = ?v / ?t, where a is acceleration, ?v is the change in velocity, and ?t is the time over which the change occurs. In this case, ?v = 30 m/s and ?t = 10 s, so a = 30 m/s / 10 s = 3 m/s^2.","/image-source/sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-a-car-accelerates-bae0aa49.png","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-a-car-accelerates-c8add662","Science G9: Describing the movement of a body - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-1 Describing the movement of a body based on the concepts of the main elements of movement and distinguishing between them.","3. Explaining the relationship between acceleration, velocity, displacement, time and direction of motion theoretically and mathematically.","A car accelerates from 20 m/s to 40 m/s in 5 seconds. What is its acceleration?","The car's acceleration is 4 m/s^2","The car's acceleration is 6 m/s^2","The car's acceleration is 8 m/s^2","The car's acceleration is 10 m/s^2","The car's acceleration is 6 m/s^2","To find the acceleration, we use the formula a = ?v / ?t, where a is acceleration, ?v is the change in velocity, and ?t is the time over which the change occurs. In this case, ?v = 40 m/s - 20 m/s = 20 m/s and ?t = 5 s, so a = 20 m/s / 5 s = 4 m/s^2.","/image-source/sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-a-car-accelerates-c8add662.png","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-a-ball-is-a0ed3052","Science G9: Describing the movement of a body - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-1 Describing the movement of a body based on the concepts of the main elements of movement and distinguishing between them.","2. Explaining the concept of acceleration for a moving body, and indicating the time of its occurrence. Calculating the value of positive and negative acceleration","A ball is thrown upwards with an initial velocity of 20 m/s. What is its acceleration?","The ball has a positive acceleration of 10 m/s^2","The ball has a negative acceleration of 10 m/s^2","The ball has a positive acceleration of 20 m/s^2","4 m/s^2","4 m/s^2","The ball is under the influence of gravity, which is a force that causes it to accelerate downwards. Since the ball is thrown upwards, its velocity is decreasing, so its acceleration is negative.","/image-source/sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-a-ball-is-a0ed3052.png","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-a-car-accelerates-23422319","Science G9: Describing the movement of a body - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-1 Describing the movement of a body based on the concepts of the main elements of movement and distinguishing between them.","3. Explaining the relationship between acceleration, velocity, displacement, time and direction of motion theoretically and mathematically.","A car accelerates from 0 to 60 km/h in 10 seconds. What is the acceleration of the car?","The car's acceleration is 6 km/h per second","The car's acceleration is 12 km/h per second","The car's acceleration is 60 km/h per second","The car's acceleration is 0.6 km/h per second","The car's acceleration is 12 km/h per second","To find the acceleration, we use the formula a = ?v / ?t, where a is acceleration, ?v is the change in velocity, and ?t is the time over which the change occurs. In this case, ?v = 60 km/h and ?t = 10 s, so a = 60 km/h / 10 s = 6 km/h/s.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-a-bicycle-is-2ab802b5","Science G9: Describing the movement of a body - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-1 Describing the movement of a body based on the concepts of the main elements of movement and distinguishing between them.","2. Explaining the concept of acceleration for a moving body, and indicating the time of its occurrence. Calculating the value of positive and negative acceleration","A bicycle is moving at a constant speed of 10 m/s. What is its acceleration?","The bicycle has a positive acceleration of 10 m/s^2","The bicycle has a negative acceleration of 10 m/s^2","The bicycle has no acceleration","The bicycle has a positive acceleration of 1 m/s^2","The bicycle has no acceleration","Acceleration is the rate of change of velocity. Since the bicycle is moving at a constant speed, its velocity is not changing, so its acceleration is zero.","/image-source/sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-a-bicycle-is-2ab802b5.png","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-a-person-is-b7f3bbcd","Science G9: Describing the movement of a body - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-1 Describing the movement of a body based on the concepts of the main elements of movement and distinguishing between them.","2. Explaining the concept of acceleration for a moving body, and indicating the time of its occurrence. Calculating the value of positive and negative acceleration","A person is running at a constant speed of 5 m/s. What is their acceleration?","The person has a positive acceleration of 5 m/s^2","The person has a negative acceleration of 5 m/s^2","The person has no acceleration","The person has a positive acceleration of 1 m/s^2","The person has no acceleration","Acceleration is the rate of change of velocity. Since the person is running at a constant speed, their velocity is not changing, so their acceleration is zero.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-a-car-accelerates-0f57df6e","Science G9: Describing the movement of a body - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-1 Describing the movement of a body based on the concepts of the main elements of movement and distinguishing between them.","3. Explaining the relationship between acceleration, velocity, displacement, time and direction of motion theoretically and mathematically.","A car accelerates from 0 to 50 m/s in 15 seconds. What is its acceleration?","The car's acceleration is 3.33 m/s^2","The car's acceleration is 6.67 m/s^2","The car's acceleration is 10 m/s^2","The car's acceleration is 12.5 m/s^2","The car's acceleration is 3.33 m/s^2","To find the acceleration, we use the formula a = ?v / ?t, where a is acceleration, ?v is the change in velocity, and ?t is the time over which the change occurs. In this case, ?v = 50 m/s and ?t = 15 s, so a = 50 m/s / 15 s = 3.33 m/s^2.","/image-source/sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-a-car-accelerates-0f57df6e.png","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-a-ball-is-c03f0c3f","Science G9: Describing the movement of a body - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-1 Describing the movement of a body based on the concepts of the main elements of movement and distinguishing between them.","2. Explaining the concept of acceleration for a moving body, and indicating the time of its occurrence. Calculating the value of positive and negative acceleration","A ball is thrown downwards with an initial velocity of 10 m/s. What is its acceleration?","The ball has a positive acceleration of 10 m/s^2","The ball has a negative acceleration of 10 m/s^2","The ball has a positive acceleration of 5 m/s^2","4 m/s^2","4 m/s^2","The ball is under the influence of gravity, which is a force that causes it to accelerate downwards. Since the ball is thrown downwards, its velocity is increasing, so its acceleration is positive.","/image-source/sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-a-ball-is-c03f0c3f.png","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-a-car-accelerates-4aa15559","Science G9: Describing the movement of a body - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-1 Describing the movement of a body based on the concepts of the main elements of movement and distinguishing between them.","3. Explaining the relationship between acceleration, velocity, displacement, time and direction of motion theoretically and mathematically.","A car accelerates from 40 m/s to 60 m/s in 5 seconds. What is its acceleration?","The car's acceleration is 4 m/s^2","The car's acceleration is 6 m/s^2","The car's acceleration is 8 m/s^2","The car's acceleration is 10 m/s^2","The car's acceleration is 6 m/s^2","To find the acceleration, we use the formula a = ?v / ?t, where a is acceleration, ?v is the change in velocity, and ?t is the time over which the change occurs. In this case, ?v = 60 m/s - 40 m/s = 20 m/s and ?t = 5 s, so a = 20 m/s / 5 s = 4 m/s^2.","/image-source/sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-a-car-accelerates-4aa15559.png","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-a-person-is-308903ac","Science G9: Describing the movement of a body - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-1 Describing the movement of a body based on the concepts of the main elements of movement and distinguishing between them.","2. Explaining the concept of acceleration for a moving body, and indicating the time of its occurrence. Calculating the value of positive and negative acceleration","A person is walking at a constant speed of 2 m/s. What is their acceleration?","The person has a positive acceleration of 2 m/s^2","The person has a negative acceleration of 2 m/s^2","The person has no acceleration","3 m/s^2","The person has no acceleration","An object moving with constant velocity has zero acceleration because its velocity is not changing.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-term-rate-7a3c2571","Science G9: Describing the movement of a body - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-1 Describing the movement of a body based on the concepts of the main elements of movement and distinguishing between them.","3. Explaining the relationship between acceleration, velocity, displacement, time and direction of motion theoretically and mathematically.","What is the term for the rate of change of speed?","Acceleration","Velocity","Speed","Displacement","Acceleration","Acceleration is the rate of change of speed, which means it is the change in speed per unit time.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-term-total-5ba7e5bc","Science G9: Describing the movement of a body - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-1 Describing the movement of a body based on the concepts of the main elements of movement and distinguishing between them.","3. Explaining the relationship between acceleration, velocity, displacement, time and direction of motion theoretically and mathematically.","What is the term for the total distance traveled by an object?","Displacement","Velocity","Acceleration","Distance","Distance","Distance is the total length of the path traveled by an object, regardless of the direction of motion.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-term-time-e88a4dc5","Science G9: Describing the movement of a body - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-1 Describing the movement of a body based on the concepts of the main elements of movement and distinguishing between them.","3. Explaining the relationship between acceleration, velocity, displacement, time and direction of motion theoretically and mathematically.","What is the term for the time it takes for an object to travel a certain distance?","Time","Speed","Distance","Velocity","Velocity","Time is the quantity used to describe how long an event or motion lasts.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-term-direction-d49afead","Science G9: Describing the movement of a body - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-1 Describing the movement of a body based on the concepts of the main elements of movement and distinguishing between them.","3. Explaining the relationship between acceleration, velocity, displacement, time and direction of motion theoretically and mathematically.","What is the term for the direction of motion of an object?","Velocity","Speed","Acceleration","Displacement","Velocity","Velocity is the speed of an object in a specific direction, which means it takes into account both the magnitude and direction of motion.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-term-speed-6b9746ec","Science G9: Describing the movement of a body - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-1 Describing the movement of a body based on the concepts of the main elements of movement and distinguishing between them.","3. Explaining the relationship between acceleration, velocity, displacement, time and direction of motion theoretically and mathematically.","What is the term for the speed of an object in a specific direction?","Velocity","Speed","Acceleration","Displacement","Velocity","Velocity is the speed of an object in a specific direction, which means it takes into account both the magnitude and direction of motion.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-term-total-5ae3b1f4","Science G9: Describing the movement of a body - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-1 Describing the movement of a body based on the concepts of the main elements of movement and distinguishing between them.","3. Explaining the relationship between acceleration, velocity, displacement, time and direction of motion theoretically and mathematically.","What is the term for the rate of change of velocity?","Acceleration","Speed","Displacement","Distance","Acceleration","Acceleration is the rate at which velocity changes with time.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-term-change-8ab3a479","Science G9: Describing the movement of a body - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-1 Describing the movement of a body based on the concepts of the main elements of movement and distinguishing between them.","3. Explaining the relationship between acceleration, velocity, displacement, time and direction of motion theoretically and mathematically.","What is the term for the change in position of an object?","Displacement","Velocity","Acceleration","Distance","Displacement","Displacement is the change in an object’s position from its initial position to its final position.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-term-rate-a38ccc3d","Science G9: Describing the movement of a body - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-1 Describing the movement of a body based on the concepts of the main elements of movement and distinguishing between them.","3. Explaining the relationship between acceleration, velocity, displacement, time and direction of motion theoretically and mathematically.","What is the term for the rate of change of displacement?","Acceleration","Velocity","Speed","Displacement","Velocity","Velocity is the rate of change of displacement with respect to time.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-term-distance-2242805b","Science G9: Describing the movement of a body - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-1 Describing the movement of a body based on the concepts of the main elements of movement and distinguishing between them.","3. Explaining the relationship between acceleration, velocity, displacement, time and direction of motion theoretically and mathematically.","What is the term for the distance traveled by an object in a specific direction?","Distance","Displacement","Velocity","Acceleration","Displacement","Displacement is the distance traveled by an object in a specific direction, which means it takes into account both the magnitude and direction of motion.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-term-rate-28e4a311","Science G9: Describing the movement of a body - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-1 Describing the movement of a body based on the concepts of the main elements of movement and distinguishing between them.","3. Explaining the relationship between acceleration, velocity, displacement, time and direction of motion theoretically and mathematically.","What is the term for the rate of change of velocity?","The speed of an object","The distance traveled by an object","The rate of change of velocity","The direction of motion","The rate of change of velocity","Acceleration is the rate of change of velocity, which means it is the change in velocity per unit time.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-an-example-c34a5cca","Science G9: Describing the movement of a body - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-1 Describing the movement of a body based on the concepts of the main elements of movement and distinguishing between them.","4. Identifying the circular motion, show the effect of the centripetal force on it, and provide an example of it.","What is an example of circular motion in everyday life?","A car driving in a straight line on the highway.","A ball rolling on the ground in a straight line.","A merry-go-round spinning around a central axis.","A person walking in a straight line down the street.","A merry-go-round spinning around a central axis.","A merry-go-round spinning around a central axis is an example of circular motion, where the force of centripetal force keeps the riders moving in a circular path.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-effect-of-04742522","Science G9: Describing the movement of a body - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-1 Describing the movement of a body based on the concepts of the main elements of movement and distinguishing between them.","4. Identifying the circular motion, show the effect of the centripetal force on it, and provide an example of it.","What is the effect of centripetal force on circular motion?","It slows down the object's motion.","It speeds up the object's motion.","It keeps the object moving in a circular path.","It causes the object to move in a straight line.","It keeps the object moving in a circular path.","Centripetal force keeps the object moving in a circular path, allowing it to maintain its curved trajectory.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-relationship-centripetal-efe5cf7e","Science G9: Describing the movement of a body - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-1 Describing the movement of a body based on the concepts of the main elements of movement and distinguishing between them.","4. Identifying the circular motion, show the effect of the centripetal force on it, and provide an example of it.","What is the relationship between centripetal force and circular motion?","Centripetal force causes circular motion.","Circular motion causes centripetal force.","Centripetal force is a result of circular motion.","Centripetal force is necessary for circular motion.","Centripetal force is necessary for circular motion.","Centripetal force is necessary for circular motion, as it provides the force required to keep the object moving in a curved path.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-happens-to-an-527d47f3","Science G9: Describing the movement of a body - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-1 Describing the movement of a body based on the concepts of the main elements of movement and distinguishing between them.","4. Identifying the circular motion, show the effect of the centripetal force on it, and provide an example of it.","What happens to an object if the centripetal force is removed?","It continues to move in a circular path.","It moves in a straight line.","It speeds up its motion.","It slows down its motion.","It moves in a straight line.","If the centripetal force is removed, the object will move in a straight line, as it no longer has the force required to maintain its circular motion.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-role-of-bdedb625","Science G9: Describing the movement of a body - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-1 Describing the movement of a body based on the concepts of the main elements of movement and distinguishing between them.","4. Identifying the circular motion, show the effect of the centripetal force on it, and provide an example of it.","What is the role of centripetal force in keeping an object on a circular path?","It pushes the object away from the center.","It pulls the object towards the center.","It slows down the object's motion.","Oscillatory motion involves changing speed as the object moves back and forth","Oscillatory motion involves changing speed as the object moves back and forth","Oscillatory motion is characterized by an object moving back and forth about a central position, involving changes in speed and direction.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-type-of-motion-61548654","Science G9: Describing the movement of a body - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-3 Understanding the concept of friction force, its types, and how it affects the movement of objects.","2. Defining the force of friction, give examples of it, and explaining how friction affects motion.","What type of motion is characterized by a constant speed and changing direction?","Linear motion is the movement of an object in a straight line.","Circular motion is the movement of an object in a curved path.","Oscillatory motion is the movement of an object back and forth around a central point.","Circular motion is the movement of an object in a circular path with a constant speed.","Oscillatory motion is the movement of an object back and forth around a central point.","Oscillatory motion is the movement of an object back and forth around a central point, characterized by a constant speed and changing direction.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-effect-of-a083a7c5","Science G9: Describing the movement of a body - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-1 Describing the movement of a body based on the concepts of the main elements of movement and distinguishing between them.","4. Identifying the circular motion, show the effect of the centripetal force on it, and provide an example of it.","What is the effect of increasing the speed of an object on its circular motion?","It decreases the centripetal force required.","It increases the centripetal force required.","It has no effect on the centripetal force required.","It decreases the radius of the circular path.","It increases the centripetal force required.","Increasing the speed of an object increases the centripetal force required to keep it moving in a circular path, as the force must be greater to maintain the object's curved trajectory.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-relationship-radius-a3ccabb4","Science G9: Describing the movement of a body - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-1 Describing the movement of a body based on the concepts of the main elements of movement and distinguishing between them.","4. Identifying the circular motion, show the effect of the centripetal force on it, and provide an example of it.","What is the relationship between the radius of a circular path and the centripetal force?","The radius of the circular path decreases with an increase in centripetal force.","The radius of the circular path increases with an increase in centripetal force.","The radius of the circular path has no effect on the centripetal force.","The centripetal force has no effect on the radius of the circular path.","The radius of the circular path increases with an increase in centripetal force.","The radius of the circular path increases with an increase in centripetal force, as the force must be greater to maintain the object's curved trajectory over a larger distance.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-force-keeps-cc8621c3","Science G9: Describing the movement of a body - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-1 Describing the movement of a body based on the concepts of the main elements of movement and distinguishing between them.","4. Identifying the circular motion, show the effect of the centripetal force on it, and provide an example of it.","What is the force that keeps an object moving in a circular path?","Centrifugal force is the force that pushes an object away from the center.","Centripetal force is the force that pulls an object towards the center.","Frictional force is the force that opposes motion between two surfaces.","Normal force is the force that acts perpendicular to a surface.","Centripetal force is the force that pulls an object towards the center.","Centripetal force is the force that pulls an object towards the center, keeping it moving in a circular path.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-type-of-motion-67420cbd","Science G9: Describing the movement of a body - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-1 Describing the movement of a body based on the concepts of the main elements of movement and distinguishing between them.","4. Identifying the circular motion, show the effect of the centripetal force on it, and provide an example of it.","What type of motion occurs when an object moves in a circular path?","Linear motion is the movement of an object in a straight line.","Circular motion is the movement of an object in a curved path.","Radial motion is the movement of an object towards or away from a central point.","Oscillatory motion is the movement of an object back and forth around a central point.","Circular motion is the movement of an object in a curved path.","Circular motion is the movement of an object in a curved path, such as a planet orbiting the sun.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-importance-of-d2e7501c","Science G9: Describing the natural cycles and identifying their causes - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-3-4 Describing types of aquatic and terrestrial ecosystems and the interactive relationships among their components.","1. Identifying the characteristics of a balanced ecosystem based on the availability of factors that maintain its balance (availability of clean water, recycling of nutrients, prevention of soil erosion, and diversity of living organisms).","What is the importance of the nutrient cycle in the local environment?","It helps to reduce the amount of soil erosion","It increases the amount of greenhouse gases in the atmosphere","It provides nutrients for plants to grow and develop","It reduces the amount of water in the oceans","It provides nutrients for plants to grow and develop","The nutrient cycle is important because it provides nutrients for plants to grow and develop, which is essential for their survival and for maintaining a healthy ecosystem.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-role-of-12822f30","Science G9: Describing the natural cycles and identifying their causes - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-3 Describing the natural cycles and identifying their causes and benefits.","1. Explaining how natural cycles occur in the local environment and determining their usefulness.","What is the role of decomposers in the nutrient cycle?","They produce nutrients through photosynthesis","They absorb nutrients from the soil","They break down dead organisms to release nutrients back into the soil","They remove nutrients from the soil","They break down dead organisms to release nutrients back into the soil","Decomposers, such as bacteria and fungi, play a crucial role in the nutrient cycle by breaking down dead organisms to release nutrients back into the soil, making them available for other living organisms.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-importance-of-05646f9d","Science G9: Describing the natural cycles and identifying their causes - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-3 Describing the natural cycles and identifying their causes and benefits.","1. Explaining how natural cycles occur in the local environment and determining their usefulness.","What is the importance of the oxygen cycle in the local environment?","It helps to reduce the amount of carbon dioxide in the atmosphere","It allows for the growth of plants and trees by providing them with oxygen","It provides oxygen for humans and other animals to breathe","It increases the amount of greenhouse gases in the atmosphere","It provides oxygen for humans and other animals to breathe","The oxygen cycle is important because it provides oxygen for humans and other animals to breathe, which is essential for their survival.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-how-rock-cycle-739ed0a8","Science G9: Describing the natural cycles and identifying their causes - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-3 Describing the natural cycles and identifying their causes and benefits.","1. Explaining how natural cycles occur in the local environment and determining their usefulness.","How does the rock cycle affect the local environment?","It helps to reduce the amount of soil erosion","It increases the amount of greenhouse gases in the atmosphere","It shapes the landscape and creates new landforms","It reduces the amount of water in the oceans","It shapes the landscape and creates new landforms","The rock cycle affects the local environment by shaping the landscape and creating new landforms, such as mountains and valleys, through the processes of weathering, erosion, and plate tectonics.","/image-source/sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-how-rock-cycle-739ed0a8.png","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-role-of-d93fec4f","Science G9: Describing the natural cycles and identifying their causes - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-3 Describing the natural cycles and identifying their causes and benefits.","1. Explaining how natural cycles occur in the local environment and determining their usefulness.","What is the role of plants in the carbon cycle?","They release carbon dioxide into the atmosphere through respiration","They absorb carbon dioxide from the atmosphere through photosynthesis","They reduce the amount of oxygen in the atmosphere","They increase the amount of greenhouse gases in the atmosphere","They absorb carbon dioxide from the atmosphere through photosynthesis","Plants play a crucial role in the carbon cycle by absorbing carbon dioxide from the atmosphere through photosynthesis, which helps to reduce the amount of carbon dioxide in the atmosphere.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-how-water-cycle-89ae93bb","Science G9: Describing the natural cycles and identifying their causes - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-3 Describing the natural cycles and identifying their causes and benefits.","1. Explaining how natural cycles occur in the local environment and determining their usefulness.","How does the water cycle affect the local environment?","It helps to reduce the amount of soil erosion","It increases the amount of greenhouse gases in the atmosphere","It provides water for humans and other animals to drink","It shapes the landscape and creates new landforms","It provides water for humans and other animals to drink","The water cycle affects the local environment by providing water for humans and other animals to drink, as well as for plants to grow, which is essential for their survival.","/image-source/sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-how-water-cycle-89ae93bb.png","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-how-do-humans-0a9b063a","Science G9: Describing the natural cycles and identifying their causes - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-3 Describing the natural cycles and identifying their causes and benefits.","1. Explaining how natural cycles occur in the local environment and determining their usefulness.","How do humans impact the natural cycles in the local environment?","By reducing the amount of greenhouse gases in the atmosphere","By increasing the amount of oxygen in the atmosphere","By releasing pollutants and waste that can disrupt the natural cycles","By reducing the amount of water in the oceans","By releasing pollutants and waste that can disrupt the natural cycles","Humans can impact the natural cycles in the local environment by releasing pollutants and waste that can disrupt the natural cycles, such as the water cycle, carbon cycle, and nutrient cycle.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-main-benefit-0e4234e2","Science G9: Describing the natural cycles and identifying their causes - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-3 Describing the natural cycles and identifying their causes and benefits.","1. Explaining how natural cycles occur in the local environment and determining their usefulness.","What is the main benefit of the carbon cycle in the local environment?","It helps to reduce the amount of oxygen in the atmosphere","It allows for the growth of plants and trees by providing them with carbon dioxide","It increases the amount of greenhouse gases in the atmosphere","It reduces the amount of water in the oceans","It allows for the growth of plants and trees by providing them with carbon dioxide","The main benefit of the carbon cycle is that it allows for the growth of plants and trees by providing them with carbon dioxide, which is essential for photosynthesis.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-result-of-5b0e4fb4","Science G9: Describing the natural cycles and identifying their causes - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-3 Describing the natural cycles and identifying their causes and benefits.","1. Explaining how natural cycles occur in the local environment and determining their usefulness.","What is the result of the nitrogen cycle in the local environment?","The reduction of nitrogen in the soil","The increase of oxygen in the atmosphere","The conversion of nitrogen into a form that can be used by plants","The decrease of carbon dioxide in the atmosphere","The conversion of nitrogen into a form that can be used by plants","The nitrogen cycle results in the conversion of nitrogen into a form that can be used by plants, such as ammonia or nitrate, which is essential for their growth and development.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-primary-cause-346bc2cb","Science G9: Describing the natural cycles and identifying their causes - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-3 Describing the natural cycles and identifying their causes and benefits.","1. Explaining how natural cycles occur in the local environment and determining their usefulness.","What is the primary cause of the water cycle in the local environment?","The movement of tectonic plates","The process of evaporation from oceans and lakes","The formation of mountains and valleys","The growth of plants and trees","The process of evaporation from oceans and lakes","The primary cause of the water cycle is the process of evaporation from oceans and lakes, which leads to the formation of clouds and eventually precipitation.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-term-rate-7bda2ad7","Science G9: Describing the speed of chemical reactions and identifying - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-9 Describing the speed of chemical reactions and identifying the factors affecting them","2. Determining the factors affecting the rate of a chemical reaction (concentration of reactants, concentration of reactants, pressure, temperature, catalyst).","What is the term for the rate at which a chemical reaction occurs?","The rate of reaction","The rate of change","The rate of velocity","The rate of speed","The rate of reaction","The rate of reaction is the term for the rate at which a chemical reaction occurs.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-term-number-5c60f168","Science G9: Describing the speed of chemical reactions and identifying - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-9 Describing the speed of chemical reactions and identifying the factors affecting them","2. Determining the factors affecting the rate of a chemical reaction (concentration of reactants, concentration of reactants, pressure, temperature, catalyst).","What is the term for the number of collisions between reactant molecules per unit time?","Collision frequency","Collision rate","Collision speed","Collision time","Collision frequency","Collision frequency is the term for the number of collisions between reactant molecules per unit time.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-effect-of-80a3a758","Science G9: Describing the speed of chemical reactions and identifying - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-9 Describing the speed of chemical reactions and identifying the factors affecting them","2. Determining the factors affecting the rate of a chemical reaction (concentration of reactants, concentration of reactants, pressure, temperature, catalyst).","What is the effect of increasing the concentration of reactants on the rate of a chemical reaction?","It decreases","It stays the same","It increases","It remains constant","It increases","The rate of a chemical reaction increases when the concentration of reactants increases.","","",""
