"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-role-of-ebc20392","Science G9: Describing types of aquatic and terrestrial ecosystems and - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-3-4 Describing types of aquatic and terrestrial ecosystems and the interactive relationships among their components.","1. Identifying the characteristics of a balanced ecosystem based on the availability of factors that maintain its balance (availability of clean water, recycling of nutrients, prevention of soil erosion, and diversity of living organisms).","What is the role of plants in a balanced ecosystem?","To pollute the environment and cause erosion","To recycle nutrients and maintain biodiversity","To only consume resources without giving back","To only produce waste without contributing to the ecosystem","To recycle nutrients and maintain biodiversity","Plants play a crucial role in recycling nutrients and maintaining biodiversity in a balanced ecosystem.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-effect-of-c62fc053","Science G9: Describing types of aquatic and terrestrial ecosystems and - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-3-4 Describing types of aquatic and terrestrial ecosystems and the interactive relationships among their components.","1. Identifying the characteristics of a balanced ecosystem based on the availability of factors that maintain its balance (availability of clean water, recycling of nutrients, prevention of soil erosion, and diversity of living organisms).","What is the effect of nutrient recycling on a balanced ecosystem?","It leads to the loss of nutrients and soil degradation","It has no impact on the ecosystem's balance","It maintains soil fertility and prevents nutrient loss","It increases the diversity of living organisms","It maintains soil fertility and prevents nutrient loss","Nutrient recycling maintains soil fertility and prevents nutrient loss, supporting the balance of a ecosystem.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-role-of-c04ed7cd","Science G9: Describing types of aquatic and terrestrial ecosystems and - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-3-4 Describing types of aquatic and terrestrial ecosystems and the interactive relationships among their components.","1. Identifying the characteristics of a balanced ecosystem based on the availability of factors that maintain its balance (availability of clean water, recycling of nutrients, prevention of soil erosion, and diversity of living organisms).","What is the role of diversity of living organisms in a balanced ecosystem?","It has no impact on the ecosystem's balance","It only supports the growth of plants","It supports the growth of plants and animals, and maintains the food chain","It only supports the growth of animals","It supports the growth of plants and animals, and maintains the food chain","Biodiversity is the variety of living organisms, genes, and ecosystems in an area.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-importance-of-cfaedaaa","Science G9: Describing types of aquatic and terrestrial ecosystems and - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-3-4 Describing types of aquatic and terrestrial ecosystems and the interactive relationships among their components.","1. Identifying the characteristics of a balanced ecosystem based on the availability of factors that maintain its balance (availability of clean water, recycling of nutrients, prevention of soil erosion, and diversity of living organisms).","What is the importance of clean water in a balanced ecosystem?","It has no impact on the ecosystem's balance","It only supports the growth of plants","It supports the growth of plants and animals, and maintains the water cycle","It only supports the growth of animals","It supports the growth of plants and animals, and maintains the water cycle","Clean water is essential for drinking, sanitation, agriculture, and healthy ecosystems.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-effect-of-181760d7","Science G9: Describing types of aquatic and terrestrial ecosystems and - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-3-4 Describing types of aquatic and terrestrial ecosystems and the interactive relationships among their components.","1. Identifying the characteristics of a balanced ecosystem based on the availability of factors that maintain its balance (availability of clean water, recycling of nutrients, prevention of soil erosion, and diversity of living organisms).","What is the effect of soil erosion on a balanced ecosystem?","It helps to maintain soil fertility and prevent nutrient loss","It has no impact on the ecosystem's balance","It leads to the loss of nutrients and soil degradation","It increases the diversity of living organisms","It leads to the loss of nutrients and soil degradation","Soil erosion removes fertile topsoil and can degrade habitats and ecosystems.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-role-of-29fb4872","Science G9: Describing types of aquatic and terrestrial ecosystems and - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-3-4 Describing types of aquatic and terrestrial ecosystems and the interactive relationships among their components.","1. Identifying the characteristics of a balanced ecosystem based on the availability of factors that maintain its balance (availability of clean water, recycling of nutrients, prevention of soil erosion, and diversity of living organisms).","What is the role of living organisms in a balanced ecosystem?","To pollute the environment and cause erosion","To recycle nutrients and maintain biodiversity","To only consume resources without giving back","To only produce waste without contributing to the ecosystem","To recycle nutrients and maintain biodiversity","Living organisms play a crucial role in recycling nutrients and maintaining biodiversity in a balanced ecosystem.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-necessary-a-7b481996","Science G9: Describing types of aquatic and terrestrial ecosystems and - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-3-4 Describing types of aquatic and terrestrial ecosystems and the interactive relationships among their components.","1. Identifying the characteristics of a balanced ecosystem based on the availability of factors that maintain its balance (availability of clean water, recycling of nutrients, prevention of soil erosion, and diversity of living organisms).","What is necessary for a balanced ecosystem?","Availability of clean water and recycling of nutrients","Prevention of soil erosion and diversity of living organisms","Only clean water and diversity of living organisms","Only recycling of nutrients and prevention of soil erosion","Availability of clean water and recycling of nutrients","A balanced ecosystem requires the availability of clean water and recycling of nutrients to maintain its balance.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-necessary-a-692c8ed9","Science G9: Describing types of aquatic and terrestrial ecosystems and - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-3-4 Describing types of aquatic and terrestrial ecosystems and the interactive relationships among their components.","1. Identifying the characteristics of a balanced ecosystem based on the availability of factors that maintain its balance (availability of clean water, recycling of nutrients, prevention of soil erosion, and diversity of living organisms).","What is necessary for a balanced aquatic ecosystem?","Availability of clean water and recycling of nutrients","Prevention of soil erosion and diversity of living organisms","Only clean water and diversity of living organisms","Only recycling of nutrients and prevention of soil erosion","Availability of clean water and recycling of nutrients","A balanced aquatic ecosystem requires the availability of clean water and recycling of nutrients to maintain its balance.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-effect-of-5941792f","Science G9: Describing types of aquatic and terrestrial ecosystems and - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-3-4 Describing types of aquatic and terrestrial ecosystems and the interactive relationships among their components.","1. Identifying the characteristics of a balanced ecosystem based on the availability of factors that maintain its balance (availability of clean water, recycling of nutrients, prevention of soil erosion, and diversity of living organisms).","What is the effect of human activities on a balanced ecosystem?","It helps to maintain soil fertility and prevent nutrient loss","It has no impact on the ecosystem's balance","It leads to the loss of nutrients and soil degradation","It increases the diversity of living organisms","It leads to the loss of nutrients and soil degradation","Human activities such as pollution, deforestation, and overuse of resources can damage ecosystems.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-importance-of-7ddc83f5","Science G9: Describing types of aquatic and terrestrial ecosystems and - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-3-4 Describing types of aquatic and terrestrial ecosystems and the interactive relationships among their components.","1. Identifying the characteristics of a balanced ecosystem based on the availability of factors that maintain its balance (availability of clean water, recycling of nutrients, prevention of soil erosion, and diversity of living organisms).","What is the importance of biodiversity in a balanced ecosystem?","It has no impact on the ecosystem's balance","It only supports the growth of plants","It supports the growth of plants and animals, and maintains the food chain","It only supports the growth of animals","It only supports the growth of animals","Biodiversity supports the growth of plants and animals, and maintains the food chain, making it essential for a balanced ecosystem.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-a-characteristic-0c52c88e","Science G9: Describing types of aquatic and terrestrial - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-3-4 Describing types of aquatic and terrestrial ecosystems and the interactive relationships among their components.","1. Describing aquatic and terrestrial ecosystems, identifying the characteristics of the biological populations that live within them, and explaining how they interact together to form a biological community.","What is a characteristic of a terrestrial ecosystem?","A forest is a type of aquatic ecosystem.","A forest is a type of terrestrial ecosystem.","A forest is a type of ecosystem that exists underwater.","A forest is a type of ecosystem that exists in space.","A forest is a type of terrestrial ecosystem.","A terrestrial ecosystem is a community of living and non-living components that exist on land, such as forests, grasslands, and deserts.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-an-example-7a849fc2","Science G9: Describing types of aquatic and terrestrial - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-3-4 Describing types of aquatic and terrestrial ecosystems and the interactive relationships among their components.","1. Describing aquatic and terrestrial ecosystems, identifying the characteristics of the biological populations that live within them, and explaining how they interact together to form a biological community.","What is an example of a biological population that lives in a freshwater ecosystem?","A population of humans living in a city.","A population of fish living in a river.","A population of trees living in a forest.","A population of bacteria living in a petri dish.","A population of fish living in a river.","A biological population is a group of individuals of the same species living in a specific area, such as a population of fish living in a river.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-role-of-2d1371f3","Science G9: Describing types of aquatic and terrestrial - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-3-4 Describing types of aquatic and terrestrial ecosystems and the interactive relationships among their components.","1. Identifying the characteristics of a balanced ecosystem based on the availability of factors that maintain its balance (availability of clean water, recycling of nutrients, prevention of soil erosion, and diversity of living organisms).","What is the role of decomposers in an ecosystem?","Decomposers are producers that make their own food.","Decomposers are consumers that eat other organisms.","Decomposers are organisms that break down dead organisms and recycle nutrients.","Decomposers are organisms that make their own food and eat other organisms.","Decomposers are organisms that break down dead organisms and recycle nutrients.","Decomposers, such as bacteria and fungi, play a crucial role in breaking down dead organisms and recycling nutrients in an ecosystem.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-a-characteristic-454f50f9","Science G9: Describing types of aquatic and terrestrial - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-3-4 Describing types of aquatic and terrestrial ecosystems and the interactive relationships among their components.","1. Describing aquatic and terrestrial ecosystems, identifying the characteristics of the biological populations that live within them, and explaining how they interact together to form a biological community.","What is a characteristic of a coral reef ecosystem?","A terrestrial desert ecosystem.","A freshwater river ecosystem with no coral.","An underwater ecosystem formed by coral and many associated organisms.","An ecosystem that exists in space.","An underwater ecosystem formed by coral and many associated organisms.","A coral reef is an aquatic ecosystem built by coral and inhabited by many fish, invertebrates, and other organisms.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-an-example-87cc1829","Science G9: Describing types of aquatic and terrestrial - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-3-4 Describing types of aquatic and terrestrial ecosystems and the interactive relationships among their components.","2. Describing the interactive relationships among living organisms in ecosystems (competition, predation, mutualism, and parasitism).","What is an example of a symbiotic relationship in an ecosystem?","A predator-prey relationship between a lion and a zebra.","A mutualistic relationship between a clownfish and a sea anemone.","A competitive relationship between two species of plants.","A commensal relationship between a bird and a tree.","A mutualistic relationship between a clownfish and a sea anemone.","A symbiotic relationship is a close and often long-term interaction between different species, such as a mutualistic relationship between a clownfish and a sea anemone.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-role-of-b01ad7d3","Science G9: Describing types of aquatic and terrestrial - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-3-4 Describing types of aquatic and terrestrial ecosystems and the interactive relationships among their components.","2. Describing the interactive relationships among living organisms in ecosystems (competition, predation, mutualism, and parasitism).","What is the role of producers in an ecosystem?","Producers are organisms that break down dead organisms and recycle nutrients.","Producers are organisms that make their own food through photosynthesis.","Producers are organisms that eat other organisms.","Producers are organisms that make their own food and break down dead organisms.","Producers are organisms that make their own food through photosynthesis.","Producers, such as plants and algae, play a crucial role in making their own food through photosynthesis and providing energy for other organisms in an ecosystem.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-an-example-fdd780d5","Science G9: Describing types of aquatic and terrestrial - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-3-4 Describing types of aquatic and terrestrial ecosystems and the interactive relationships among their components.","2. Describing the interactive relationships among living organisms in ecosystems (competition, predation, mutualism, and parasitism).","What is an example of a terrestrial ecosystem?","A coral reef is a type of terrestrial ecosystem.","A forest is a type of terrestrial ecosystem.","A desert is a type of aquatic ecosystem.","A grassland is a type of ecosystem that exists in space.","A forest is a type of terrestrial ecosystem.","A terrestrial ecosystem is a community of living and non-living components that exist on land, such as forests, grasslands, and deserts.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-role-of-613c8fd1","Science G9: Describing types of aquatic and terrestrial - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-3-3 Explaining how matter and energy are transferred within an ecosystem, predicting the changes resulting from the extinction of one of its components, describing material cycles in the ecosystem, and inferring their role in supporting its sustainability.","2. Describing material cycles (water, carbon dioxide, and nitrogen) in an ecosystem and their transfer through biotic and abiotic components, and inferring their role in supporting sustainability.","What is the role of consumers in an ecosystem?","Consumers are organisms that make their own food through photosynthesis.","Consumers are organisms that break down dead organisms and recycle nutrients.","Consumers are organisms that eat other organisms.","Consumers are organisms that make their own food and break down dead organisms.","Consumers are organisms that eat other organisms.","Consumers, such as animals and humans, play a crucial role in eating other organisms and transferring energy from one trophic level to another in an ecosystem.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-an-example-fb2096c4","Science G9: Describing types of aquatic and terrestrial - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-3-4 Describing types of aquatic and terrestrial ecosystems and the interactive relationships among their components.","2. Describing the interactive relationships among living organisms in ecosystems (competition, predation, mutualism, and parasitism).","What is an example of a symbiotic relationship between two species?","A predator-prey relationship between a lion and a zebra.","A mutualistic relationship between a clownfish and a sea anemone.","A competitive relationship between two species of plants.","A commensal relationship between a bird and a tree.","A mutualistic relationship between a clownfish and a sea anemone.","A symbiotic relationship is a close and often long-term interaction between different species, such as a mutualistic relationship between a clownfish and a sea anemone.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-role-of-2ee55697","Science G9: Describing types of aquatic and terrestrial - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-3-4 Describing types of aquatic and terrestrial ecosystems and the interactive relationships among their components.","1. Identifying the characteristics of a balanced ecosystem based on the availability of factors that maintain its balance (availability of clean water, recycling of nutrients, prevention of soil erosion, and diversity of living organisms).","What is the role of decomposers in a freshwater ecosystem?","Decomposers are producers that make their own food.","Decomposers are consumers that eat other organisms.","Decomposers are organisms that break down dead organisms and recycle nutrients.","Decomposers are organisms that make their own food and eat other organisms.","Decomposers are organisms that break down dead organisms and recycle nutrients.","Decomposers, such as bacteria and fungi, play a crucial role in breaking down dead organisms and recycling nutrients in a freshwater ecosystem.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-necessary-recycling-504b4600","Science G9: Describing types of aquatic and terrestrial - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-3-4 Describing types of aquatic and terrestrial ecosystems and the interactive relationships among their components.","1. Identifying the characteristics of a balanced ecosystem based on the availability of factors that maintain its balance (availability of clean water, recycling of nutrients, prevention of soil erosion, and diversity of living organisms).","What is necessary for the recycling of nutrients in a balanced ecosystem?","Only clean water and diversity of living organisms","Only prevention of soil erosion and availability of clean water","Availability of clean water and diversity of living organisms","Prevention of soil erosion and recycling of nutrients","Availability of clean water and diversity of living organisms","Availability of clean water and diversity of living organisms are essential for the recycling of nutrients in a balanced ecosystem.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-role-of-555800b4","Science G9: Describing types of aquatic and terrestrial - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-3-4 Describing types of aquatic and terrestrial ecosystems and the interactive relationships among their components.","1. Identifying the characteristics of a balanced ecosystem based on the availability of factors that maintain its balance (availability of clean water, recycling of nutrients, prevention of soil erosion, and diversity of living organisms).","What is the role of prevention of soil erosion in a balanced ecosystem?","To increase the rate of soil erosion","To decrease the recycling of nutrients","To maintain the balance of the ecosystem","To reduce the availability of clean water","To maintain the balance of the ecosystem","Prevention of soil erosion is essential for maintaining the balance of the ecosystem by reducing the risk of landslides and maintaining soil quality.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-effect-of-a2535a9e","Science G9: Describing types of aquatic and terrestrial - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-3-4 Describing types of aquatic and terrestrial ecosystems and the interactive relationships among their components.","1. Identifying the characteristics of a balanced ecosystem based on the availability of factors that maintain its balance (availability of clean water, recycling of nutrients, prevention of soil erosion, and diversity of living organisms).","What is the effect of a lack of clean water on a balanced ecosystem?","It increases the recycling of nutrients","It decreases the diversity of living organisms","It disrupts the balance of the ecosystem","It increases the rate of soil erosion","It disrupts the balance of the ecosystem","A lack of clean water can disrupt the balance of the ecosystem by reducing the availability of water for living organisms.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-importance-of-90f15466","Science G9: Describing types of aquatic and terrestrial - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-3-4 Describing types of aquatic and terrestrial ecosystems and the interactive relationships among their components.","1. Identifying the characteristics of a balanced ecosystem based on the availability of factors that maintain its balance (availability of clean water, recycling of nutrients, prevention of soil erosion, and diversity of living organisms).","What is the importance of recycling of nutrients in a balanced ecosystem?","It reduces the diversity of living organisms","It increases the rate of soil erosion","It maintains the balance of the ecosystem","It decreases the availability of clean water","It maintains the balance of the ecosystem","Recycling of nutrients is essential for maintaining the balance of the ecosystem by providing essential nutrients for living organisms.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-role-of-09556b05","Science G9: Describing types of aquatic and terrestrial - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-3-4 Describing types of aquatic and terrestrial ecosystems and the interactive relationships among their components.","1. Identifying the characteristics of a balanced ecosystem based on the availability of factors that maintain its balance (availability of clean water, recycling of nutrients, prevention of soil erosion, and diversity of living organisms).","What is the role of diversity of living organisms in a balanced ecosystem?","To reduce the availability of clean water","To increase the rate of soil erosion","To maintain the balance of the ecosystem","To decrease the recycling of nutrients","To maintain the balance of the ecosystem","Diversity of living organisms helps to maintain the balance of the ecosystem by providing different roles and functions.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-effect-of-ffb130cb","Science G9: Describing types of aquatic and terrestrial - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-3-4 Describing types of aquatic and terrestrial ecosystems and the interactive relationships among their components.","1. Identifying the characteristics of a balanced ecosystem based on the availability of factors that maintain its balance (availability of clean water, recycling of nutrients, prevention of soil erosion, and diversity of living organisms).","What is the effect of a decrease in diversity of living organisms on a balanced ecosystem?","It increases the availability of clean water","It increases the recycling of nutrients","It disrupts the balance of the ecosystem","It increases the rate of soil erosion","It disrupts the balance of the ecosystem","A decrease in diversity of living organisms can disrupt the balance of the ecosystem by reducing the availability of essential services and functions.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-effect-of-8dd7a8a7","Science G9: Describing types of aquatic and terrestrial - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-3-4 Describing types of aquatic and terrestrial ecosystems and the interactive relationships among their components.","1. Identifying the characteristics of a balanced ecosystem based on the availability of factors that maintain its balance (availability of clean water, recycling of nutrients, prevention of soil erosion, and diversity of living organisms).","What is the effect of soil erosion on a balanced ecosystem?","It increases the availability of clean water","It decreases the recycling of nutrients","It disrupts the balance of the ecosystem","It increases the diversity of living organisms","It disrupts the balance of the ecosystem","Soil erosion can disrupt the balance of the ecosystem by reducing the availability of nutrients and increasing the risk of landslides.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-necessary-a-a5918bbf","Science G9: Describing types of aquatic and terrestrial - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-3-4 Describing types of aquatic and terrestrial ecosystems and the interactive relationships among their components.","1. Identifying the characteristics of a balanced ecosystem based on the availability of factors that maintain its balance (availability of clean water, recycling of nutrients, prevention of soil erosion, and diversity of living organisms).","What is necessary for a balanced ecosystem?","Availability of clean water and recycling of nutrients","Prevention of soil erosion and diversity of living organisms","Only clean water and diversity of living organisms","Only recycling of nutrients and prevention of soil erosion","Availability of clean water and recycling of nutrients","Availability of clean water and recycling of nutrients are essential for maintaining a balanced ecosystem.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-role-of-d7be43f3","Science G9: Describing types of aquatic and terrestrial - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-3-4 Describing types of aquatic and terrestrial ecosystems and the interactive relationships among their components.","1. Identifying the characteristics of a balanced ecosystem based on the availability of factors that maintain its balance (availability of clean water, recycling of nutrients, prevention of soil erosion, and diversity of living organisms).","What is the role of availability of clean water in a balanced ecosystem?","To reduce the recycling of nutrients","To decrease the diversity of living organisms","To maintain the balance of the ecosystem","To increase the rate of soil erosion","To maintain the balance of the ecosystem","Availability of clean water is essential for maintaining the balance of the ecosystem by providing essential resources for living organisms.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-effect-of-e586955e","Science G9: Describing types of aquatic and terrestrial - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-3-4 Describing types of aquatic and terrestrial ecosystems and the interactive relationships among their components.","1. Identifying the characteristics of a balanced ecosystem based on the availability of factors that maintain its balance (availability of clean water, recycling of nutrients, prevention of soil erosion, and diversity of living organisms).","What is the effect of an increase in soil erosion on a balanced ecosystem?","It increases the recycling of nutrients","It decreases the diversity of living organisms","It disrupts the balance of the ecosystem","It increases the availability of clean water","It disrupts the balance of the ecosystem","An increase in soil erosion can disrupt the balance of the ecosystem by reducing the availability of nutrients and increasing the risk of landslides.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-an-example-4ef56d9a","Science G9: Describing types of aquatic and terrestrial - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-3-4 Describing types of aquatic and terrestrial ecosystems and the interactive relationships among their components.","2. Describing the interactive relationships among living organisms in ecosystems (competition, predation, mutualism, and parasitism).","What is an example of a relationship where one organism is harmed and the other is not necessarily harmed?","Mutualism is a relationship where both organisms benefit.","Competition occurs when organisms compete for resources.","Predation is a relationship where one organism benefits and the other is harmed.","Parasitism is a relationship where one organism benefits and the other is harmed.","Parasitism is a relationship where one organism benefits and the other is harmed.","Parasitism is a relationship where one organism benefits and the other is harmed, but the parasite is not necessarily harmed.","","",""
