"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-term-maximum-6c57ad73","Science G9: Determining the concept of solubility - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-3 Determining the concept of solubility, the rate of solubility in a solution, and deducing the factors affecting the solubility rate of a solute in","1. Defining the concept of solubility and the rate of dissolution in a solution graphically and describing the relationship between the solvent and the solute according to the concept of solubility.","What is the term for the maximum amount of solute that can dissolve in a given amount of solvent at a particular temperature?","Solubility","Saturation","Concentration","Dissolution","Solubility","Solubility is the term for the maximum amount of solute that can dissolve in a given amount of solvent at a particular temperature.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-primary-factor-13ef3923","Science G9: Determining the concept of solubility - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-3 Determining the concept of solubility, the rate of solubility in a solution, and deducing the factors affecting the solubility rate of a solute in","3. Inferring the factors affecting the rate of solubility of the solute in the solvent for different types of solutions.","What is the primary factor that affects the rate of solubility of a solute in a solvent?","The temperature of the solvent","The pressure of the surrounding environment","The surface area of the solute","The color of the solute","The temperature of the solvent","The rate of solubility is primarily affected by the temperature of the solvent, as higher temperatures provide more energy for the molecules to move and interact, increasing the solubility of the solute.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-term-measure-e1b29e7e","Science G9: Determining the concept of solubility - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-3 Determining the concept of solubility, the rate of solubility in a solution, and deducing the factors affecting the solubility rate of a solute in","1. Explaining the properties of liquids (viscosity, surface tension) according to the composition of the substance, the arrangement of its molecules, and the forces between them.","What is the term for the measure of a liquid's resistance to flow?","Viscosity","Surface tension","Density","Solubility","Viscosity","Viscosity is the measure of a liquid's resistance to flow, which is determined by the strength of the intermolecular forces between its molecules.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-of-factors-can-6e82fdce","Science G9: Determining the concept of solubility - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-3 Determining the concept of solubility, the rate of solubility in a solution, and deducing the factors affecting the solubility rate of a solute in","3. Inferring the factors affecting the rate of solubility of the solute in the solvent for different types of solutions.","Which of the following factors can increase the solubility of a solute in a solvent?","Decreasing the temperature of the solvent","Increasing the pressure of the surrounding environment","Increasing the surface area of the solute","Increasing the temperature of the solvent","Increasing the temperature of the solvent","Increasing the temperature of the solvent provides more energy for the molecules to move and interact, increasing the solubility of the solute.","/image-source/sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-of-factors-can-6e82fdce.png","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-term-attractive-4700e126","Science G9: Determining the concept of solubility - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-3 Determining the concept of solubility, the rate of solubility in a solution, and deducing the factors affecting the solubility rate of a solute in","1. Explaining the properties of liquids (viscosity, surface tension) according to the composition of the substance, the arrangement of its molecules, and the forces between them.","What is the term for the attractive forces between molecules at the surface of a liquid?","Viscosity","Surface tension","Adhesion","Cohesion","Surface tension","Surface tension is the term for the attractive forces between molecules at the surface of a liquid, which causes the liquid to behave as if it has an elastic skin at its surface.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-how-arrangement-of-fc20ac20","Science G9: Determining the concept of solubility - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-3 Determining the concept of solubility, the rate of solubility in a solution, and deducing the factors affecting the solubility rate of a solute in","1. Explaining the properties of liquids (viscosity, surface tension) according to the composition of the substance, the arrangement of its molecules, and the forces between them.","How does the arrangement of molecules in a liquid affect its viscosity?","The more ordered the molecules, the lower the viscosity","The more ordered the molecules, the higher the viscosity","The less ordered the molecules, the lower the viscosity","The less ordered the molecules, the higher the viscosity","The more ordered the molecules, the higher the viscosity","The more ordered the molecules in a liquid, the stronger the intermolecular forces between them, resulting in higher viscosity.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-happens-to-solubility-97d58016","Science G9: Determining the concept of solubility - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-3 Determining the concept of solubility, the rate of solubility in a solution, and deducing the factors affecting the solubility rate of a solute in","1. Explaining the properties of liquids (viscosity, surface tension) according to the composition of the substance, the arrangement of its molecules, and the forces between them.","What happens to the solubility of a gas in a liquid when the pressure of the surrounding environment increases?","The solubility of the gas decreases","The solubility of the gas remains the same","The solubility of the gas increases","The solubility of the gas becomes zero","The solubility of the gas increases","According to Henry's Law, the solubility of a gas in a liquid increases when the pressure of the surrounding environment increases.","/image-source/sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-happens-to-solubility-97d58016.png","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-how-surface-tension-9eaaffa8","Science G9: Determining the concept of solubility - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-3 Determining the concept of solubility, the rate of solubility in a solution, and deducing the factors affecting the solubility rate of a solute in","1. Explaining the properties of liquids (viscosity, surface tension) according to the composition of the substance, the arrangement of its molecules, and the forces between them.","How does the surface tension of a liquid affect its behavior?","It causes the liquid to spread out and wet the surrounding surface","It causes the liquid to form droplets and minimize its surface area","It causes the liquid to become more viscous","It causes the liquid to become less dense","It causes the liquid to form droplets and minimize its surface area","The surface tension of a liquid causes it to behave as if it has an elastic skin at its surface, resulting in the formation of droplets and the minimization of its surface area.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-term-process-8dfe0258","Science G9: Determining the concept of solubility - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-3 Determining the concept of solubility, the rate of solubility in a solution, and deducing the factors affecting the solubility rate of a solute in","1. Defining the concept of solubility and the rate of dissolution in a solution graphically and describing the relationship between the solvent and the solute according to the concept of solubility.","What is the term for the process by which a solute dissolves in a solvent?","Diffusion","Osmosis","Solubilization","Dissolution","Dissolution","Dissolution is the term for the process by which a solute dissolves in a solvent, resulting in the formation of a solution.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-how-viscosity-of-48cf5af7","Science G9: Determining the concept of solubility - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-3 Determining the concept of solubility, the rate of solubility in a solution, and deducing the factors affecting the solubility rate of a solute in","1. Explaining the properties of liquids (viscosity, surface tension) according to the composition of the substance, the arrangement of its molecules, and the forces between them.","How does the viscosity of a liquid affect its flow rate?","Higher viscosity results in faster flow rate","Higher viscosity results in slower flow rate","Lower viscosity results in faster flow rate","Lower viscosity results in slower flow rate","Higher viscosity results in slower flow rate","Higher viscosity results in slower flow rate, as the liquid's resistance to flow is greater.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-term-process-e57bf0ad","Science G9: Determining the concept of solubility - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-3 Determining the concept of solubility, the rate of solubility in a solution, and deducing the factors affecting the solubility rate of a solute in","1. Defining the concept of solubility and the rate of dissolution in a solution graphically and describing the relationship between the solvent and the solute according to the concept of solubility.","What is the term for the process by which a solid substance dissolves in a liquid solvent?","Diffusion.","Osmosis.","Dissolution.","Evaporation.","Dissolution.","Dissolution is the process in which a solute disperses in a solvent to form a solution.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-how-composition-of-8d294320","Science G9: Determining the concept of solubility - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-3 Determining the concept of solubility, the rate of solubility in a solution, and deducing the factors affecting the solubility rate of a solute in","2. Inferring the effect of temperature and compound composition on the solubility of a solution and explaining it.","How does the composition of a compound affect its solubility?","The composition of a compound has no effect on its solubility.","The composition of a compound increases its solubility.","The composition of a compound decreases its solubility.","The composition of a compound has a variable effect on its solubility.","The composition of a compound decreases its solubility.","The composition of a compound can affect its solubility, as different compounds have different properties that influence their ability to dissolve in a solvent.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-happens-to-solubility-e9f72445","Science G9: Determining the concept of solubility - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-3 Determining the concept of solubility, the rate of solubility in a solution, and deducing the factors affecting the solubility rate of a solute in","2. Inferring the effect of temperature and compound composition on the solubility of a solution and explaining it.","What happens to the solubility of a substance when the temperature of the solution is increased?","The solubility of the substance decreases.","The solubility of the substance increases.","The solubility of the substance remains the same.","The solubility of the substance becomes zero.","The solubility of the substance increases.","As the temperature of the solution increases, the particles of the solute gain kinetic energy and move faster, allowing them to dissolve more easily.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-term-maximum-27ac05c4","Science G9: Determining the concept of solubility - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-3 Determining the concept of solubility, the rate of solubility in a solution, and deducing the factors affecting the solubility rate of a solute in","1. Defining the concept of solubility and the rate of dissolution in a solution graphically and describing the relationship between the solvent and the solute according to the concept of solubility.","What is the term for the maximum amount of a substance that can dissolve in a given amount of solvent at a particular temperature?","Solubility limit.","Saturation point.","Dissolution rate.","Solvent capacity.","Saturation point.","The saturation point is the maximum amount of a substance that can dissolve in a given amount of solvent at a particular temperature.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-of-is-a-a5c47d6b","Science G9: Determining the concept of solubility - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-3 Determining the concept of solubility, the rate of solubility in a solution, and deducing the factors affecting the solubility rate of a solute in","2. Inferring the effect of temperature and compound composition on the solubility of a solution and explaining it.","Which of the following is a factor that affects the rate of solubility of a substance?","The size of the container.","The concentration of the solution.","The temperature of the solution.","The color of the substance.","The temperature of the solution.","Temperature is a factor that affects the rate of solubility of a substance, as it influences the kinetic energy of the particles.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-how-surface-area-7c5fbc16","Science G9: Determining the concept of solubility - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-3 Determining the concept of solubility, the rate of solubility in a solution, and deducing the factors affecting the solubility rate of a solute in","3. Inferring the factors affecting the rate of solubility of the solute in the solvent for different types of solutions.","How does the surface area of a substance affect its rate of solubility?","The surface area of a substance increases its rate of solubility.","The surface area of a substance decreases its rate of solubility.","The surface area of a substance has no effect on its rate of solubility.","The surface area of a substance increases its rate of solubility at high temperatures.","The surface area of a substance increases its rate of solubility.","The surface area of a substance can increase its rate of solubility, as a larger surface area provides more sites for the solvent particles to interact with the solute.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-term-ability-c1230538","Science G9: Determining the concept of solubility - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-3 Determining the concept of solubility, the rate of solubility in a solution, and deducing the factors affecting the solubility rate of a solute in","1. Defining the concept of solubility and the rate of dissolution in a solution graphically and describing the relationship between the solvent and the solute according to the concept of solubility.","What is the term for the ability of a substance to dissolve in a solvent?","Solubility.","Dissolution rate.","Solvent capacity.","Solvent ability.","Solubility.","Solubility is the ability of a substance to dissolve in a solvent.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-how-concentration-of-1c5ad273","Science G9: Determining the concept of solubility - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-3 Determining the concept of solubility, the rate of solubility in a solution, and deducing the factors affecting the solubility rate of a solute in","1. Defining the concept of solubility and the rate of dissolution in a solution graphically and describing the relationship between the solvent and the solute according to the concept of solubility.","How does the concentration of a solution affect the rate of solubility of a substance?","The concentration of a solution increases the rate of solubility of a substance.","The concentration of a solution decreases the rate of solubility of a substance.","The concentration of a solution has no effect on the rate of solubility of a substance.","The concentration of a solution increases the rate of solubility of a substance at high temperatures.","The concentration of a solution increases the rate of solubility of a substance.","The concentration of a solution can increase the rate of solubility of a substance, as the presence of more solvent particles can facilitate the dissolution process.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-term-amount-15054185","Science G9: Determining the concept of solubility - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-3 Determining the concept of solubility, the rate of solubility in a solution, and deducing the factors affecting the solubility rate of a solute in","1. Defining the concept of solubility and the rate of dissolution in a solution graphically and describing the relationship between the solvent and the solute according to the concept of solubility.","What is the term for the amount of a substance that dissolves in a given amount of solvent at a particular temperature?","Solubility limit.","Saturation point.","Dissolution rate.","Solubility rate.","Saturation point.","The saturation point is the amount of a substance that dissolves in a given amount of solvent at a particular temperature.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-how-pressure-of-bfadf2ee","Science G9: Determining the concept of solubility - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-3 Determining the concept of solubility, the rate of solubility in a solution, and deducing the factors affecting the solubility rate of a solute in","1. Explaining the properties of liquids (viscosity, surface tension) according to the composition of the substance, the arrangement of its molecules, and the forces between them.","How does the pressure of a gas affect its solubility in a liquid?","The pressure of a gas increases its solubility in a liquid.","The pressure of a gas decreases its solubility in a liquid.","The pressure of a gas has no effect on its solubility in a liquid.","The pressure of a gas increases its solubility in a liquid at high temperatures.","The pressure of a gas decreases its solubility in a liquid.","The pressure of a gas decreases its solubility in a liquid, as increased pressure causes the gas molecules to be forced out of the solution.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-of-properties-of-d1cd894f","Science G9: Determining the concept of solubility, the - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-3 Determining the concept of solubility, the rate of solubility in a solution, and deducing the factors affecting the solubility rate of a solute in","1. Explaining the properties of liquids (viscosity, surface tension) according to the composition of the substance, the arrangement of its molecules, and the forces between them.","Which of the following properties of a liquid is affected by the arrangement of its molecules?","Viscosity","Surface tension","Density","Color","Viscosity","The viscosity of a liquid is affected by the arrangement of its molecules. The stronger the intermolecular forces between the molecules, the higher the viscosity of the liquid.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-main-factor-60621a25","Science G9: Determining the concept of solubility, the - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-3 Determining the concept of solubility, the rate of solubility in a solution, and deducing the factors affecting the solubility rate of a solute in","2. Inferring the effect of temperature and compound composition on the solubility of a solution and explaining it.","What is the main factor that affects the rate of solubility of a solute in a solution?","The temperature of the solution","The pressure of the solution","The volume of the solution","The color of the solution","The temperature of the solution","The rate of solubility of a solute in a solution is mainly affected by the temperature of the solution. An increase in temperature generally increases the solubility of a solute.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-term-used-b74a8bdf","Science G9: Determining the concept of solubility, the - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-3 Determining the concept of solubility, the rate of solubility in a solution, and deducing the factors affecting the solubility rate of a solute in","1. Explaining the properties of liquids (viscosity, surface tension) according to the composition of the substance, the arrangement of its molecules, and the forces between them.","What is the term used to describe the amount of a substance that can dissolve in a given amount of solvent at a particular temperature?","Solubility rate","Solubility limit","Viscosity","Surface tension","Solubility limit","The term solubility limit refers to the maximum amount of a substance that can dissolve in a given amount of solvent at a particular temperature.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-of-is-an-681db144","Science G9: Determining the concept of solubility, the - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-3 Determining the concept of solubility, the rate of solubility in a solution, and deducing the factors affecting the solubility rate of a solute in","3. Inferring the factors affecting the rate of solubility of the solute in the solvent for different types of solutions.","Which of the following is an example of a factor that affects the solubility rate of a solute in a solution?","The shape of the container","The type of solvent used","The presence of other solutes","The color of the solute","The type of solvent used","The type of solvent used can affect the solubility rate of a solute in a solution. Different solvents have different properties that can affect the solubility of a solute.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-happens-to-solubility-db015f1b","Science G9: Determining the concept of solubility, the - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-3 Determining the concept of solubility, the rate of solubility in a solution, and deducing the factors affecting the solubility rate of a solute in","1. Explaining the properties of liquids (viscosity, surface tension) according to the composition of the substance, the arrangement of its molecules, and the forces between them.","What happens to the solubility of a gas in a liquid when the temperature is increased?","It increases","It decreases","It remains the same","It becomes zero","It decreases","When the temperature is increased, the solubility of a gas in a liquid generally decreases. This is because the increased kinetic energy of the gas molecules makes it more difficult for them to dissolve in the liquid.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-term-used-52747d18","Science G9: Determining the concept of solubility, the - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-3 Determining the concept of solubility, the rate of solubility in a solution, and deducing the factors affecting the solubility rate of a solute in","1. Explaining the properties of liquids (viscosity, surface tension) according to the composition of the substance, the arrangement of its molecules, and the forces between them.","What is the term used to describe the force that acts between the molecules of a liquid and the surface of the container?","Adhesion","Cohesion","Viscosity","Surface tension","Adhesion","The force that acts between the molecules of a liquid and the surface of the container is called adhesion.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-of-is-an-a47f00b2","Science G9: Determining the concept of solubility, the - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-3 Determining the concept of solubility, the rate of solubility in a solution, and deducing the factors affecting the solubility rate of a solute in","1. Explaining the properties of liquids (viscosity, surface tension) according to the composition of the substance, the arrangement of its molecules, and the forces between them.","Which of the following is an example of a substance with high surface tension?","Water","Alcohol","Gasoline","Mercury","Mercury","Mercury has a high surface tension due to the strong intermolecular forces between its molecules. This is why mercury forms droplets and has a convex meniscus when placed in a container.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-happens-to-viscosity-e612ac29","Science G9: Determining the concept of solubility, the - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-3 Determining the concept of solubility, the rate of solubility in a solution, and deducing the factors affecting the solubility rate of a solute in","1. Explaining the properties of liquids (viscosity, surface tension) according to the composition of the substance, the arrangement of its molecules, and the forces between them.","What happens to the viscosity of a liquid when its temperature is increased?","It increases","It decreases","It remains the same","It becomes zero","It decreases","When the temperature of a liquid is increased, its viscosity generally decreases. This is because the increased kinetic energy of the molecules makes it easier for them to flow past each other.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-of-factors-can-3504a5c3","Science G9: Determining the concept of solubility, the - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-3 Determining the concept of solubility, the rate of solubility in a solution, and deducing the factors affecting the solubility rate of a solute in","2. Inferring the effect of temperature and compound composition on the solubility of a solution and explaining it.","Which of the following factors can affect the solubility of a solute in a solution?","The pressure of the solution","The temperature of the solution","The presence of other solutes","All of the above","All of the above","All of the above factors can affect the solubility of a solute in a solution. The pressure, temperature, and presence of other solutes can all impact the solubility of a solute.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-term-used-f4c76f94","Science G9: Determining the concept of solubility, the - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-3 Determining the concept of solubility, the rate of solubility in a solution, and deducing the factors affecting the solubility rate of a solute in","3. Inferring the factors affecting the rate of solubility of the solute in the solvent for different types of solutions.","What is the term used to describe the process by which a solute dissolves in a solvent?","Dissolution","Solubilization","Precipitation","Crystallization","Dissolution","The term dissolution refers to the process by which a solute dissolves in a solvent. This process involves the breaking of intermolecular forces between the solute molecules and the formation of new forces between the solute and solvent molecules.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-happens-to-solubility-d4560fc9","Science G9: Determining the concept of solubility, the - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-3 Determining the concept of solubility, the rate of solubility in a solution, and deducing the factors affecting the solubility rate of a solute in","1. Explaining the properties of liquids (viscosity, surface tension) according to the composition of the substance, the arrangement of its molecules, and the forces between them.","What happens to the solubility of a substance when the composition of the solvent is changed?","The solubility of the substance remains the same","The solubility of the substance increases for all substances","The solubility of the substance decreases for all substances","The solubility of the substance changes depending on the intermolecular forces between the solute and solvent particles","The solubility of the substance changes depending on the intermolecular forces between the solute and solvent particles","The solubility of a substance can change when the composition of the solvent is changed, as the intermolecular forces between the solute and solvent particles can be affected, resulting in an increase or decrease in solubility.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-term-maximum-2e742de8","Science G9: Determining the concept of solubility, the - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-3 Determining the concept of solubility, the rate of solubility in a solution, and deducing the factors affecting the solubility rate of a solute in","1. Defining the concept of solubility and the rate of dissolution in a solution graphically and describing the relationship between the solvent and the solute according to the concept of solubility.","What is the term for the maximum amount of solute that can dissolve in a given amount of solvent at a particular temperature?","Solubility","Saturation point","Dissolution rate","Reaction rate","Solubility","Solubility is the maximum amount of solute that can dissolve in a given amount of solvent at a particular temperature.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-how-composition-of-6b1d8e8e","Science G9: Determining the concept of solubility, the - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-3 Determining the concept of solubility, the rate of solubility in a solution, and deducing the factors affecting the solubility rate of a solute in","2. Inferring the effect of temperature and compound composition on the solubility of a solution and explaining it.","How does the composition of a compound affect its solubility in a solution?","The composition of the compound has no effect on its solubility","The composition of the compound affects the intermolecular forces between the solute and solvent particles","The composition of the compound only affects the temperature of the solution","The composition of the compound only affects the amount of solvent present","The composition of the compound affects the intermolecular forces between the solute and solvent particles","The composition of the compound affects the intermolecular forces between the solute and solvent particles, which in turn affects the solubility of the compound, as different compounds have different types and strengths of intermolecular forces.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-happens-to-solubility-9d9f1ad9","Science G9: Determining the concept of solubility, the - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-3 Determining the concept of solubility, the rate of solubility in a solution, and deducing the factors affecting the solubility rate of a solute in","2. Inferring the effect of temperature and compound composition on the solubility of a solution and explaining it.","What happens to the solubility of a gas in a solution when the temperature is increased?","The solubility of the gas increases","The solubility of the gas remains the same","The solubility of the gas decreases","The solubility of the gas is unaffected by temperature","The solubility of the gas decreases","For gases, an increase in temperature results in a decrease in solubility, as the gas molecules gain kinetic energy and are able to escape the solution more easily.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-of-factors-can-b113ecc5","Science G9: Determining the concept of solubility, the - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-3 Determining the concept of solubility, the rate of solubility in a solution, and deducing the factors affecting the solubility rate of a solute in","2. Inferring the effect of temperature and compound composition on the solubility of a solution and explaining it.","Which of the following factors can affect the solubility rate of a solute in a solution?","Only the temperature of the solution","Only the composition of the solute","Both the temperature and composition of the solute","Neither the temperature nor composition of the solute","Both the temperature and composition of the solute","Both the temperature and composition of the solute can affect the solubility rate, as changes in temperature can increase or decrease the kinetic energy of the particles, and the composition of the solute can affect the intermolecular forces between the solute and solvent particles.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-happens-to-solubility-d5a5d06e","Science G9: Determining the concept of solubility, the - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-3 Determining the concept of solubility, the rate of solubility in a solution, and deducing the factors affecting the solubility rate of a solute in","2. Inferring the effect of temperature and compound composition on the solubility of a solution and explaining it.","What happens to the solubility of a substance when the temperature of the solution is increased?","The solubility of the substance decreases","The solubility of the substance remains the same","The solubility of the substance increases for most solids","The solubility of the substance increases for all substances","The solubility of the substance increases for most solids","For most solids, an increase in temperature results in an increase in solubility, as the particles gain kinetic energy and are able to move more freely, allowing more solute particles to dissolve.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-of-is-an-f892ae4c","Science G9: Determining the concept of solubility, the - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-3 Determining the concept of solubility, the rate of solubility in a solution, and deducing the factors affecting the solubility rate of a solute in","2. Inferring the effect of temperature and compound composition on the solubility of a solution and explaining it.","Which of the following is an example of a factor that affects the solubility rate of a solute in a solution?","The shape of the container holding the solution","The color of the solute","The pressure of the solution","The temperature of the solution","The temperature of the solution","The temperature of the solution is an example of a factor that affects the solubility rate of a solute, as changes in temperature can increase or decrease the kinetic energy of the particles, affecting the rate at which the solute dissolves.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-how-solubility-of-32a92491","Science G9: Determining the concept of solubility, the - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-3 Determining the concept of solubility, the rate of solubility in a solution, and deducing the factors affecting the solubility rate of a solute in","1. Explaining the properties of liquids (viscosity, surface tension) according to the composition of the substance, the arrangement of its molecules, and the forces between them.","How does the solubility of a substance change when the pressure of the solution is increased?","The solubility of the substance increases for all substances","The solubility of the substance decreases for all substances","The solubility of the substance remains the same for all substances","The solubility of the substance increases for gases and decreases for solids","The solubility of the substance increases for gases and decreases for solids","For gases, an increase in pressure results in an increase in solubility, as the gas molecules are forced into the solution, while for solids, an increase in pressure has little effect on solubility.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-term-process-9101ec42","Science G9: Determining the concept of solubility, the - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-3 Determining the concept of solubility, the rate of solubility in a solution, and deducing the factors affecting the solubility rate of a solute in","1. Defining the concept of solubility and the rate of dissolution in a solution graphically and describing the relationship between the solvent and the solute according to the concept of solubility.","What is the term for the process by which a solid, liquid, or gas dissolves in a solvent?","Dissolution","Solubilization","Saturation","Solubility","Dissolution","Dissolution is the term for the process by which a solid, liquid, or gas dissolves in a solvent, resulting in a solution.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-how-composition-of-6a0f5acb","Science G9: Determining the concept of solubility, the - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-3 Determining the concept of solubility, the rate of solubility in a solution, and deducing the factors affecting the solubility rate of a solute in","3. Inferring the factors affecting the rate of solubility of the solute in the solvent for different types of solutions.","How does the composition of a solute affect its solubility in a solution?","The composition of the solute has no effect on its solubility","The composition of the solute affects the pH of the solution","The composition of the solute affects the intermolecular forces between the solute and solvent particles","The composition of the solute affects the temperature of the solution","The composition of the solute affects the intermolecular forces between the solute and solvent particles","The composition of the solute affects the intermolecular forces between the solute and solvent particles, which in turn affects the solubility of the solute, as different solutes have different types and strengths of intermolecular forces.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-of-factors-can-681e7d1c","Science G9: Determining the concept of solubility, the - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-3 Determining the concept of solubility, the rate of solubility in a solution, and deducing the factors affecting the solubility rate of a solute in","3. Inferring the factors affecting the rate of solubility of the solute in the solvent for different types of solutions.","Which of the following factors can affect the rate of solubility of a solute in a solvent?","The color of the solute","The shape of the solute","The surface area of the solute","The smell of the solvent","The surface area of the solute","The surface area of the solute is a factor that can affect the rate of solubility, as increasing the surface area allows more of the solute to come into contact with the solvent at the same time.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-effect-of-00569449","Science G9: Determining the concept of solubility, the - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-3 Determining the concept of solubility, the rate of solubility in a solution, and deducing the factors affecting the solubility rate of a solute in","3. Inferring the factors affecting the rate of solubility of the solute in the solvent for different types of solutions.","What is the effect of stirring a solution on the rate of solubility of a solute in a solvent?","The rate of solubility decreases","The rate of solubility remains the same","The rate of solubility increases","The solute becomes insoluble","The rate of solubility increases","Stirring a solution can increase the rate of solubility, as it helps to distribute the solute particles throughout the solvent and increases the surface area of the solute in contact with the solvent.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-of-is-an-be1908f2","Science G9: Determining the concept of solubility, the - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-3 Determining the concept of solubility, the rate of solubility in a solution, and deducing the factors affecting the solubility rate of a solute in","3. Inferring the factors affecting the rate of solubility of the solute in the solvent for different types of solutions.","Which of the following is an example of a factor that can decrease the rate of solubility of a solute in a solvent?","Increasing the temperature of the solvent","Increasing the surface area of the solute","Decreasing the amount of solvent","Increasing the pressure of the surrounding environment","Decreasing the amount of solvent","Decreasing the amount of solvent can decrease the rate of solubility, as there are fewer solvent particles available to interact with the solute.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-happens-to-rate-456cfe25","Science G9: Determining the concept of solubility, the - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-3 Determining the concept of solubility, the rate of solubility in a solution, and deducing the factors affecting the solubility rate of a solute in","3. Inferring the factors affecting the rate of solubility of the solute in the solvent for different types of solutions.","What happens to the rate of solubility of a solute in a solvent when the amount of solvent is decreased?","The rate of solubility increases","The rate of solubility remains the same","The rate of solubility decreases","The solute becomes insoluble","The rate of solubility decreases","Decreasing the amount of solvent can decrease the rate of solubility, as there are fewer solvent particles available to interact with the solute.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-effect-of-cac81d71","Science G9: Determining the concept of solubility, the - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-3 Determining the concept of solubility, the rate of solubility in a solution, and deducing the factors affecting the solubility rate of a solute in","1. Explaining the properties of liquids (viscosity, surface tension) according to the composition of the substance, the arrangement of its molecules, and the forces between them.","What is the effect of increasing the pressure of the surrounding environment on the rate of solubility of a gas in a liquid?","The rate of solubility decreases","The rate of solubility remains the same","The rate of solubility increases","The gas becomes insoluble","The rate of solubility increases","Increasing the pressure of the surrounding environment can increase the rate of solubility of a gas in a liquid, as the increased pressure forces more gas molecules into the liquid.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-of-types-of-ddd6101b","Science G9: Determining the concept of solubility, the - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-3 Determining the concept of solubility, the rate of solubility in a solution, and deducing the factors affecting the solubility rate of a solute in","3. Inferring the factors affecting the rate of solubility of the solute in the solvent for different types of solutions.","Which of the following types of solutions is most likely to have a high rate of solubility?","A solution with a high concentration of solute","A solution with a low concentration of solute","A solution with a high temperature","A solution with a low surface area of solute","A solution with a high temperature","A solution with a high temperature is most likely to have a high rate of solubility, as the particles of the solvent have more energy and are moving faster, allowing them to interact with the solute more effectively.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-happens-to-rate-6e0e6980","Science G9: Determining the concept of solubility, the - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-3 Determining the concept of solubility, the rate of solubility in a solution, and deducing the factors affecting the solubility rate of a solute in","3. Inferring the factors affecting the rate of solubility of the solute in the solvent for different types of solutions.","What happens to the rate of solubility of a solute in a solvent when the temperature of the solvent is increased?","The rate of solubility decreases","The rate of solubility remains the same","The rate of solubility increases","The solute becomes insoluble","The rate of solubility increases","Increasing the temperature of the solvent generally increases the rate of solubility of a solute, as the particles of the solvent have more energy and are moving faster, allowing them to interact with the solute more effectively.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-of-is-an-a1513ebb","Science G9: Determining the concept of solubility, the - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-3 Determining the concept of solubility, the rate of solubility in a solution, and deducing the factors affecting the solubility rate of a solute in","3. Inferring the factors affecting the rate of solubility of the solute in the solvent for different types of solutions.","Which of the following is an example of a factor that can increase the rate of solubility of a solute in a solvent?","Decreasing the temperature of the solvent","Increasing the pressure of the surrounding environment","Increasing the surface area of the solute","Decreasing the amount of solvent","Increasing the surface area of the solute","Increasing the surface area of the solute can increase the rate of solubility, as it allows more of the solute to come into contact with the solvent at the same time.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-primary-factor-179ab43c","Science G9: Determining the concept of solubility, the - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-3 Determining the concept of solubility, the rate of solubility in a solution, and deducing the factors affecting the solubility rate of a solute in","3. Inferring the factors affecting the rate of solubility of the solute in the solvent for different types of solutions.","What is the primary factor that affects the rate of solubility of a solute in a solvent?","The temperature of the solvent","The pressure of the surrounding environment","The surface area of the solute","The color of the solute","The temperature of the solvent","The temperature of the solvent is a primary factor that affects the rate of solubility of a solute, as increasing the temperature generally increases the solubility of most substances.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-of-types-of-2bba968e","Science G9: Determining the concept of solubility, the - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-3 Determining the concept of solubility, the rate of solubility in a solution, and deducing the factors affecting the solubility rate of a solute in","3. Inferring the factors affecting the rate of solubility of the solute in the solvent for different types of solutions.","Which of the following types of solutions is most likely to have a low rate of solubility?","A solution with a high temperature","A solution with a low surface area of solute","A solution with a high concentration of solute","A solution with a low amount of solvent","A solution with a low surface area of solute","A solution with a low surface area of solute is most likely to have a low rate of solubility, as there is less solute in contact with the solvent at any given time.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-are-primary-sources-bd5ffff9","Science G9: Determining the sources of natural resources - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-3-2 Determining the sources of natural resources, ways of managing them, and the importance of preserving and developing them.","2. Predicting the effects and variables when relying entirely on non-renewable energy sources.","What are the primary sources of non-renewable energy?","Fossil fuels such as coal, oil, and natural gas","Solar and wind power","Hydroelectric power","Geothermal energy","Fossil fuels such as coal, oil, and natural gas","Fossil fuels are the primary sources of non-renewable energy, including coal, oil, and natural gas.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-are-consequences-of-7e230daf","Science G9: Determining the sources of natural resources - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-3-2 Determining the sources of natural resources, ways of managing them, and the importance of preserving and developing them.","2. Predicting the effects and variables when relying entirely on non-renewable energy sources.","What are the consequences of relying entirely on non-renewable energy sources for transportation?","Improved air quality and reduced greenhouse gas emissions","Increased energy independence and reduced reliance on fossil fuels","Accelerated climate change and environmental degradation","Reduced energy costs and increased efficiency","Accelerated climate change and environmental degradation","Relying entirely on non-renewable energy sources for transportation accelerates climate change and environmental degradation due to increased greenhouse gas emissions.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-why-is-it-fc3df9c3","Science G9: Determining the sources of natural resources - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-3-2 Determining the sources of natural resources, ways of managing them, and the importance of preserving and developing them.","2. Predicting the effects and variables when relying entirely on non-renewable energy sources.","Why is it crucial to manage non-renewable energy sources sustainably?","To reduce energy costs and increase efficiency","To improve air quality and public health","To preserve natural resources and mitigate climate change","To increase energy independence and reduce reliance on fossil fuels","To preserve natural resources and mitigate climate change","Managing non-renewable energy sources sustainably is crucial to preserve natural resources and mitigate climate change.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-are-effects-of-8beca7f5","Science G9: Determining the sources of natural resources - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-3-2 Determining the sources of natural resources, ways of managing them, and the importance of preserving and developing them.","2. Predicting the effects and variables when relying entirely on non-renewable energy sources.","What are the effects of non-renewable energy sources on the environment?","Reduced greenhouse gas emissions and improved air quality","Accelerated climate change and environmental degradation","Improved water quality and reduced pollution","Increased biodiversity and ecosystem health","Accelerated climate change and environmental degradation","Non-renewable energy sources accelerate climate change and environmental degradation due to increased greenhouse gas emissions.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-why-are-non-8484017f","Science G9: Determining the sources of natural resources - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-3-2 Determining the sources of natural resources, ways of managing them, and the importance of preserving and developing them.","2. Predicting the effects and variables when relying entirely on non-renewable energy sources.","Why are non-renewable energy sources considered a finite resource?","They are replenished quickly and are abundant","They take millions of years to form and are finite","They are not used by humans and are infinite","They are used by all living organisms and are renewable","They take millions of years to form and are finite","Non-renewable energy sources take millions of years to form and are finite, making them a finite resource.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-importance-of-63c55bb6","Science G9: Determining the sources of natural resources - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-3-2 Determining the sources of natural resources, ways of managing them, and the importance of preserving and developing them.","2. Predicting the effects and variables when relying entirely on non-renewable energy sources.","What is the importance of preserving non-renewable energy sources?","They are not used by humans","They are abundant and will last forever","They are essential for industrialization and economic growth","They are not necessary for energy production","They are essential for industrialization and economic growth","Preserving non-renewable energy sources is essential for industrialization and economic growth, as they are a vital component of modern energy production.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-are-main-effects-d9770b31","Science G9: Determining the sources of natural resources - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-3-2 Determining the sources of natural resources, ways of managing them, and the importance of preserving and developing them.","2. Predicting the effects and variables when relying entirely on non-renewable energy sources.","What are the main effects of relying entirely on non-renewable energy sources?","Reduced greenhouse gas emissions","Increased energy independence","Accelerated climate change and environmental degradation","Improved air quality","Accelerated climate change and environmental degradation","Relying entirely on non-renewable energy sources accelerates climate change and environmental degradation due to increased greenhouse gas emissions.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-why-are-non-801e16fd","Science G9: Determining the sources of natural resources - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-3-2 Determining the sources of natural resources, ways of managing them, and the importance of preserving and developing them.","2. Predicting the effects and variables when relying entirely on non-renewable energy sources.","Why are non-renewable energy sources considered unsustainable?","They are replenished quickly","They are abundant and will last forever","They take millions of years to form and are finite","They are not used by humans","They take millions of years to form and are finite","Non-renewable energy sources take millions of years to form and are finite, making them unsustainable.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-why-is-it-3feceb39","Science G9: Determining the sources of natural resources - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-3-2 Determining the sources of natural resources, ways of managing them, and the importance of preserving and developing them.","2. Predicting the effects and variables when relying entirely on non-renewable energy sources.","Why is it essential to develop alternative energy sources?","To reduce greenhouse gas emissions and mitigate climate change","To increase energy independence and reduce reliance on fossil fuels","To improve air quality and public health","To reduce energy costs and increase efficiency","To reduce greenhouse gas emissions and mitigate climate change","Developing alternative energy sources is essential to reduce greenhouse gas emissions and mitigate climate change.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-are-variables-affect-1e1daf75","Science G9: Determining the sources of natural resources - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-3-2 Determining the sources of natural resources, ways of managing them, and the importance of preserving and developing them.","2. Predicting the effects and variables when relying entirely on non-renewable energy sources.","What are the variables that affect the availability of non-renewable energy sources?","Population growth and energy demand","Technological advancements and efficiency improvements","Geological formations and resource extraction costs","Weather patterns and climate change","Population growth and energy demand","Population growth and energy demand are key variables that affect the availability of non-renewable energy sources.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-a-benefit-ef965e71","Science G9: Determining the sources of natural resources - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-3-2 Determining the sources of natural resources, ways of managing them, and the importance of preserving and developing them.","3. Proposing solutions and means to preserve natural resources and protect them from pollution and depletion","What is a benefit of sustainable agriculture practices?","Reducing the amount of food available for human consumption","Increasing the amount of pollution in the environment","Conserving water and reducing the use of chemical pesticides","Increasing the amount of greenhouse gases emitted","Conserving water and reducing the use of chemical pesticides","Sustainable agriculture practices can help conserve water and reduce the use of chemical pesticides, which can harm the environment and human health.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-why-is-it-64961833","Science G9: Determining the sources of natural resources - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-3-2 Determining the sources of natural resources, ways of managing them, and the importance of preserving and developing them.","3. Proposing solutions and means to preserve natural resources and protect them from pollution and depletion","Why is it important to reduce pollution in our oceans?","To increase the amount of marine life","To reduce the amount of oxygen in the atmosphere","To protect marine ecosystems and human health","To increase the production of greenhouse gases","To protect marine ecosystems and human health","Reducing pollution in our oceans is essential for protecting marine ecosystems and human health, as well as maintaining the overall health of our planet.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-a-benefit-3caeab1d","Science G9: Determining the sources of natural resources - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-3-2 Determining the sources of natural resources, ways of managing them, and the importance of preserving and developing them.","2. Predicting the effects and variables when relying entirely on non-renewable energy sources.","What is a benefit of using renewable energy sources?","Reducing the amount of greenhouse gases emitted","Increasing the amount of air pollution","Reducing the amount of water available for human consumption","Increasing the amount of waste generated","Reducing the amount of greenhouse gases emitted","Using renewable energy sources can help reduce the amount of greenhouse gases emitted, which contribute to climate change.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-main-reason-567a3245","Science G9: Determining the sources of natural resources - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-3-2 Determining the sources of natural resources, ways of managing them, and the importance of preserving and developing them.","3. Proposing solutions and means to preserve natural resources and protect them from pollution and depletion","What is the main reason why forests should be preserved?","To increase the amount of urban areas","To provide habitats for a variety of plant and animal species","To increase the production of greenhouse gases","To reduce the amount of oxygen in the atmosphere","To provide habitats for a variety of plant and animal species","Forests provide habitats for a variety of plant and animal species, and preserving them is essential for maintaining biodiversity.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-a-consequence-1447ed6c","Science G9: Determining the sources of natural resources - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-3-2 Determining the sources of natural resources, ways of managing them, and the importance of preserving and developing them.","3. Proposing solutions and means to preserve natural resources and protect them from pollution and depletion","What is a consequence of deforestation?","An increase in the amount of oxygen in the atmosphere","A decrease in the amount of greenhouse gases emitted","A loss of biodiversity and increased risk of soil erosion","An increase in the amount of freshwater available","A loss of biodiversity and increased risk of soil erosion","Deforestation can lead to a loss of biodiversity and increased risk of soil erosion, as well as contribute to climate change by releasing stored carbon into the atmosphere.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-a-way-e09722cd","Science G9: Determining the sources of natural resources - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-3-2 Determining the sources of natural resources, ways of managing them, and the importance of preserving and developing them.","3. Proposing solutions and means to preserve natural resources and protect them from pollution and depletion","What is a way to manage natural resources sustainably?","Overexploiting resources to meet short-term needs","Conserving and protecting resources for future generations","Reducing the amount of waste generated","Increasing the amount of pollution in the environment","Conserving and protecting resources for future generations","Conserving and protecting resources for future generations is essential for managing natural resources sustainably and ensuring their availability for years to come.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-why-is-it-c725a385","Science G9: Determining the sources of natural resources - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-3-2 Determining the sources of natural resources, ways of managing them, and the importance of preserving and developing them.","3. Proposing solutions and means to preserve natural resources and protect them from pollution and depletion","Why is it important to protect wildlife habitats?","To increase the amount of urban areas","To reduce the amount of biodiversity","To maintain ecosystem balance and support conservation efforts","To increase the amount of pollution in the environment","To maintain ecosystem balance and support conservation efforts","Protecting wildlife habitats is essential for maintaining ecosystem balance and supporting conservation efforts, which help preserve biodiversity and ensure the long-term health of ecosystems.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-a-way-3e30242e","Science G9: Determining the sources of natural resources - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-3-2 Determining the sources of natural resources, ways of managing them, and the importance of preserving and developing them.","3. Proposing solutions and means to preserve natural resources and protect them from pollution and depletion","What is a way to conserve water resources?","Using more water for irrigation","Reducing the use of plastic bottles","Fixing leaks and using low-flow appliances","Increasing the amount of wastewater released into rivers","Fixing leaks and using low-flow appliances","Fixing leaks and using low-flow appliances can help reduce water waste and conserve water resources.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-why-is-it-842570a6","Science G9: Determining the sources of natural resources - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-3-2 Determining the sources of natural resources, ways of managing them, and the importance of preserving and developing them.","3. Proposing solutions and means to preserve natural resources and protect them from pollution and depletion","Why is it important to protect and preserve natural resources?","To increase the amount of pollution in the environment","To reduce the amount of biodiversity","To ensure their availability for future generations and maintain ecosystem balance","To increase the amount of greenhouse gases emitted","To ensure their availability for future generations and maintain ecosystem balance","Protecting and preserving natural resources is essential for ensuring their availability for future generations and maintaining ecosystem balance, which is critical for supporting life on Earth.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-why-is-it-f8130a10","Science G9: Determining the sources of natural resources - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-3-2 Determining the sources of natural resources, ways of managing them, and the importance of preserving and developing them.","3. Proposing solutions and means to preserve natural resources and protect them from pollution and depletion","Why is it important to reduce waste generation?","To increase the amount of pollution in the environment","To reduce the amount of natural resources available","To conserve natural resources and reduce environmental harm","To increase the amount of greenhouse gases emitted","To conserve natural resources and reduce environmental harm","Reducing waste generation is essential for conserving natural resources and reducing environmental harm, as well as minimizing the amount of waste that ends up in landfills and oceans.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-type-of-energy-3543b3fe","Science G9: Distinguishing between the kinetic and potential energy - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-4-4 Distinguishing between the kinetic and potential energy of a body and the factors affecting them.","3. Calculating the kinetic and potential energy of objects, and deducing the relationship between them.","What type of energy does a moving car possess?","Potential energy due to its height","Kinetic energy due to its motion","Thermal energy due to its engine","Electromagnetic energy due to its headlights","Kinetic energy due to its motion","The moving car has kinetic energy because it is in motion, and its energy of motion is being maintained by the engine.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-type-of-energy-94391de7","Science G9: Distinguishing between the kinetic and potential energy - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-4-4 Distinguishing between the kinetic and potential energy of a body and the factors affecting them.","4. Predicting the relationship between the potential energy and the height of the object above the Earth's surface.","What type of energy does a ball possess when it is rolling down a hill?","Potential energy due to its height","Kinetic energy due to its motion","Thermal energy due to its temperature","Electromagnetic energy due to its radiation","Kinetic energy due to its motion","The ball has kinetic energy because it is in motion, and its energy of motion is being converted from potential energy as it rolls down the hill.","/image-source/sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-type-of-energy-94391de7.png","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-a-skateboarder-is-ce0afb4a","Science G9: Distinguishing between the kinetic and potential energy - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-4-4 Distinguishing between the kinetic and potential energy of a body and the factors affecting them.","3. Calculating the kinetic and potential energy of objects, and deducing the relationship between them.","A skateboarder is at the top of a ramp, what type of energy does the skateboarder have?","Kinetic energy due to their motion","Potential energy due to their height","Thermal energy due to their body heat","Electromagnetic energy due to their visibility","Potential energy due to their height","The skateboarder has potential energy because of their height at the top of the ramp, which will be converted into kinetic energy as they start moving down the ramp.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-happens-to-potential-2d52c3e0","Science G9: Distinguishing between the kinetic and potential energy - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-4-4 Distinguishing between the kinetic and potential energy of a body and the factors affecting them.","3. Calculating the kinetic and potential energy of objects, and deducing the relationship between them.","What happens to the potential energy of water as it flows over a waterfall?","It increases as it gains speed","It decreases as it loses height","It remains the same as it flows","It is converted into thermal energy","It decreases as it loses height","As the water flows over the waterfall, its potential energy decreases because it loses height, and this energy is converted into kinetic energy.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-a-bicycle-is-8cacb50a","Science G9: Distinguishing between the kinetic and potential energy - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-4-4 Distinguishing between the kinetic and potential energy of a body and the factors affecting them.","4. Predicting the relationship between the potential energy and the height of the object above the Earth's surface.","A bicycle is moving at a constant speed on a flat surface, what type of energy does it have?","Potential energy due to its height","Kinetic energy due to its motion","Thermal energy due to its friction","Electromagnetic energy due to its visibility","Kinetic energy due to its motion","The bicycle has kinetic energy because it is in motion, even though its speed is constant, and its energy of motion is being maintained by the rider's pedaling.","/image-source/sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-a-bicycle-is-8cacb50a.png","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-type-of-energy-83c9628b","Science G9: Distinguishing between the kinetic and potential energy - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-4-4 Distinguishing between the kinetic and potential energy of a body and the factors affecting them.","3. Calculating the kinetic and potential energy of objects, and deducing the relationship between them.","What type of energy does a compressed spring possess?","Kinetic energy due to its motion","Potential energy due to its compression","Thermal energy due to its temperature","Electromagnetic energy due to its radiation","Potential energy due to its compression","The compressed spring has potential energy because of its compressed state, which will be converted into kinetic energy when it is released.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-a-book-is-e04635ab","Science G9: Distinguishing between the kinetic and potential energy - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-4-4 Distinguishing between the kinetic and potential energy of a body and the factors affecting them.","4. Predicting the relationship between the potential energy and the height of the object above the Earth's surface.","A book is lying on a table, what type of energy does it have?","Kinetic energy due to its motion","Potential energy due to its height","Thermal energy due to its temperature","Electromagnetic energy due to its visibility","Potential energy due to its height","The book has potential energy because of its height above the ground, although it is not in motion, and this energy will be converted into kinetic energy if it falls off the table.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-happens-to-kinetic-38a44679","Science G9: Distinguishing between the kinetic and potential energy - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-4-4 Distinguishing between the kinetic and potential energy of a body and the factors affecting them.","3. Calculating the kinetic and potential energy of objects, and deducing the relationship between them.","What happens to the kinetic energy of a ball as it rolls up a hill?","It increases as it gains speed","It decreases as it loses speed","It remains the same as it rolls","It is converted into thermal energy","It decreases as it loses speed","As the ball rolls up the hill, its kinetic energy decreases because it loses speed, and this energy is converted into potential energy due to its increasing height.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-a-roller-coaster-13a1d9b2","Science G9: Distinguishing between the kinetic and potential energy - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-4-4 Distinguishing between the kinetic and potential energy of a body and the factors affecting them.","3. Calculating the kinetic and potential energy of objects, and deducing the relationship between them.","A roller coaster is at the top of a hill, what type of energy does it have?","Kinetic energy due to its motion","Potential energy due to its height","Thermal energy due to its temperature","Electromagnetic energy due to its visibility","Potential energy due to its height","The roller coaster has potential energy because of its height at the top of the hill, which will be converted into kinetic energy as it starts moving down the hill.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-a-pendulum-is-71581af3","Science G9: Distinguishing between the kinetic and potential energy - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-4-4 Distinguishing between the kinetic and potential energy of a body and the factors affecting them.","3. Calculating the kinetic and potential energy of objects, and deducing the relationship between them.","A pendulum is swinging back and forth, what type of energy does it have at the top of its swing?","Kinetic energy due to its motion","Potential energy due to its height","Thermal energy due to its temperature","Electromagnetic energy due to its radiation","Potential energy due to its height","The pendulum has potential energy at the top of its swing because of its height, which will be converted into kinetic energy as it starts moving downwards.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-how-speed-of-69bdc1e7","Science G9: Distinguishing between the kinetic and potential energy - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-4-4 Distinguishing between the kinetic and potential energy of a body and the factors affecting them.","2. Deducing the linear and non-linear relationship between kinetic energy and the mass and speed of the moving body and express it mathematically.","How does the speed of a moving body affect its kinetic energy?","The speed of the body has no effect on its kinetic energy.","The speed of the body is inversely proportional to its kinetic energy.","The speed of the body is directly proportional to its kinetic energy.","The speed of the body is directly proportional to the square of its kinetic energy.","The speed of the body is directly proportional to the square of its kinetic energy.","The speed of the body is directly proportional to the square of its kinetic energy, as shown by the equation KE = 0.5mv^2.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-effect-of-26d2b71a","Science G9: Distinguishing between the kinetic and potential energy - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-4-4 Distinguishing between the kinetic and potential energy of a body and the factors affecting them.","2. Deducing the linear and non-linear relationship between kinetic energy and the mass and speed of the moving body and express it mathematically.","What is the effect of doubling the speed of a moving body on its kinetic energy?","The kinetic energy remains the same.","The kinetic energy is halved.","The kinetic energy is doubled.","It doubles","It doubles","Kinetic energy is given by KE = 1/2 * m * v^2. Doubling the mass (m) while keeping velocity (v) constant results in doubling the kinetic energy.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-effect-of-e5d1b705","Science G9: Distinguishing between the kinetic and potential energy - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-4-4 Distinguishing between the kinetic and potential energy of a body and the factors affecting them.","2. Deducing the linear and non-linear relationship between kinetic energy and the mass and speed of the moving body and express it mathematically.","What is the effect of doubling the mass of a moving body on its kinetic energy?","The kinetic energy remains the same.","The kinetic energy is halved.","The kinetic energy is doubled.","The kinetic energy is quadrupled.","The kinetic energy is doubled.","Kinetic energy is KE = ½mv². If velocity stays constant and mass doubles, kinetic energy doubles.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-relationship-kinetic-4df26df4","Science G9: Distinguishing between the kinetic and potential energy - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-4-4 Distinguishing between the kinetic and potential energy of a body and the factors affecting them.","2. Deducing the linear and non-linear relationship between kinetic energy and the mass and speed of the moving body and express it mathematically.","What is the relationship between the kinetic energy of a moving body and its mass, when the speed of the body is constant?","Kinetic energy is directly proportional to the mass of the body.","Kinetic energy is inversely proportional to the mass of the body.","Kinetic energy is independent of the mass of the body.","Kinetic energy is directly proportional to the square of the mass of the body.","Kinetic energy is directly proportional to the mass of the body.","With speed held constant, KE = ½mv², so kinetic energy is directly proportional to mass.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-effect-of-75a58e75","Science G9: Distinguishing between the kinetic and potential energy - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-4-4 Distinguishing between the kinetic and potential energy of a body and the factors affecting them.","2. Deducing the linear and non-linear relationship between kinetic energy and the mass and speed of the moving body and express it mathematically.","What is the effect of increasing the speed of a moving body on its kinetic energy?","The kinetic energy remains the same.","The kinetic energy is halved.","The kinetic energy is doubled.","The kinetic energy is quadrupled.","The kinetic energy is quadrupled.","Increasing the speed of the body will quadruple its kinetic energy, as shown by the equation KE = 0.5mv^2.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-how-mass-of-b3acba37","Science G9: Distinguishing between the kinetic and potential energy - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-4-4 Distinguishing between the kinetic and potential energy of a body and the factors affecting them.","2. Deducing the linear and non-linear relationship between kinetic energy and the mass and speed of the moving body and express it mathematically.","How does the mass of a moving body affect its kinetic energy?","The mass of the body has no effect on its kinetic energy.","The mass of the body is inversely proportional to its kinetic energy.","The mass of the body is directly proportional to its kinetic energy.","The mass of the body is directly proportional to the square of its kinetic energy.","The mass of the body is directly proportional to its kinetic energy.","The mass of the body is directly proportional to its kinetic energy, as shown by the equation KE = 0.5mv^2.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-relationship-kinetic-05179a2c","Science G9: Distinguishing between the kinetic and potential energy - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-4-4 Distinguishing between the kinetic and potential energy of a body and the factors affecting them.","2. Deducing the linear and non-linear relationship between kinetic energy and the mass and speed of the moving body and express it mathematically.","What is the relationship between the kinetic energy of a moving body and its speed?","Kinetic energy is directly proportional to the square of the speed of the body.","Kinetic energy is directly proportional to the speed of the body.","Kinetic energy is inversely proportional to the speed of the body.","Kinetic energy is independent of the speed of the body.","Kinetic energy is directly proportional to the square of the speed of the body.","Kinetic energy is directly proportional to the square of the speed of the body, as shown by the equation KE = 0.5mv^2.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-equation-kinetic-e530fbb7","Science G9: Distinguishing between the kinetic and potential energy - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-4-4 Distinguishing between the kinetic and potential energy of a body and the factors affecting them.","2. Deducing the linear and non-linear relationship between kinetic energy and the mass and speed of the moving body and express it mathematically.","What is the equation for the kinetic energy of a moving body, when the mass of the body is 2 kg and the speed is 5 m/s?","KE = 0.5(2)(5)^2","KE = 0.5(2)(5)","KE = (2)(5)^2","KE = (2)(5)","KE = 0.5(2)(5)^2","The equation for the kinetic energy of a moving body is KE = 0.5(2)(5)^2.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-relationship-kinetic-cfacfa28","Science G9: Distinguishing between the kinetic and potential energy - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-4-4 Distinguishing between the kinetic and potential energy of a body and the factors affecting them.","2. Deducing the linear and non-linear relationship between kinetic energy and the mass and speed of the moving body and express it mathematically.","What is the relationship between the kinetic energy of a moving body and its speed, when the mass of the body is constant?","Kinetic energy is directly proportional to the speed of the body.","Kinetic energy is inversely proportional to the speed of the body.","Kinetic energy is independent of the speed of the body.","Kinetic energy is directly proportional to the square of the speed of the body.","Kinetic energy is directly proportional to the square of the speed of the body.","Kinetic energy is directly proportional to the square of the speed of the body, as shown by the equation KE = 0.5mv^2.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-equation-kinetic-cedc3674","Science G9: Distinguishing between the kinetic and potential energy - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-4-4 Distinguishing between the kinetic and potential energy of a body and the factors affecting them.","2. Deducing the linear and non-linear relationship between kinetic energy and the mass and speed of the moving body and express it mathematically.","What is the equation for the kinetic energy of a moving body?","KE = mv^2","KE = 0.5mv^2","KE = mv","KE = 0.5mv","KE = 0.5mv^2","The equation for the kinetic energy of a moving body is KE = 0.5mv^2.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-type-of-chemical-e550fe15","Science G9: Explaining how atoms bond with each - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-7 Explaining how atoms bond with each other, identifying what is a chemical bond and how it is formed, and distinguishing between different types","4. Explaining the concept of a chemical bond, comparing its different types (ionic, covalent, metallic, and polar), and describing how atoms are linked together by different chemical bonds to form compounds, using examples and illustrative models.","Which type of chemical bond is characterized by the transfer of electrons from one atom to another, resulting in the formation of ions with opposite charges?","Covalent bond","Ionic bond","Metallic bond","Polar bond","Ionic bond","An ionic bond is characterized by the transfer of electrons from one atom to another, resulting in the formation of ions with opposite charges that are attracted to each other.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-term-attractive-1550b6d8","Science G9: Explaining how atoms bond with each - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-7 Explaining how atoms bond with each other, identifying what is a chemical bond and how it is formed, and distinguishing between different types","5. Distinguishing between an ion, a molecule, a compound, and give examples for each one, explaining what is meant by a chemical formula, and its indication through various examples.","What is the term for the attractive force between two atoms that results from the interaction between their electrons?","Electronegativity","Electron affinity","Chemical bond","Intermolecular force","Chemical bond","A chemical bond is the attractive force between two atoms that results from the interaction between their electrons, resulting in the formation of a compound.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-of-is-an-2fc4296c","Science G9: Explaining how atoms bond with each - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-7 Explaining how atoms bond with each other, identifying what is a chemical bond and how it is formed, and distinguishing between different types","4. Explaining the concept of a chemical bond, comparing its different types (ionic, covalent, metallic, and polar), and describing how atoms are linked together by different chemical bonds to form compounds, using examples and illustrative models.","Which of the following is an example of a polar covalent bond?","The bond between two oxygen atoms in an oxygen molecule","The bond between a hydrogen atom and an oxygen atom in a water molecule","The bond between two hydrogen atoms in a hydrogen molecule","The bond between a sodium atom and a chlorine atom in a salt molecule","The bond between a hydrogen atom and an oxygen atom in a water molecule","The bond between a hydrogen atom and an oxygen atom in a water molecule is an example of a polar covalent bond, where the electrons are not shared equally between the atoms due to the difference in electronegativity.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-result-of-77365af2","Science G9: Explaining how atoms bond with each - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-7 Explaining how atoms bond with each other, identifying what is a chemical bond and how it is formed, and distinguishing between different types","2. Describes the states of the outer electron level (valence electrons) through the periodic chemical properties of elements within the same group.","What is the result of the formation of a chemical bond between two or more atoms?","The formation of a new element","The formation of a compound","The release of energy","The absorption of energy","The formation of a compound","The formation of a chemical bond between two or more atoms results in the formation of a compound, which is a substance with a fixed composition and properties that are different from those of its individual component atoms.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-type-of-chemical-1213c8af","Science G9: Explaining how atoms bond with each - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-7 Explaining how atoms bond with each other, identifying what is a chemical bond and how it is formed, and distinguishing between different types","4. Explaining the concept of a chemical bond, comparing its different types (ionic, covalent, metallic, and polar), and describing how atoms are linked together by different chemical bonds to form compounds, using examples and illustrative models.","Which type of chemical bond is typically found in metals?","Ionic bond","Covalent bond","Metallic bond","Polar bond","Metallic bond","A metallic bond is typically found in metals, where electrons are delocalized and free to move throughout the metal lattice, resulting in high electrical conductivity.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-main-difference-e8c51ee1","Science G9: Explaining how atoms bond with each - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-7 Explaining how atoms bond with each other, identifying what is a chemical bond and how it is formed, and distinguishing between different types","3. Illustrating the electronic distribution of a number of groups of the periodic table, and explaining the method of dot representation of electrons, and represent it for a number of elements.","What is the main difference between a polar covalent bond and a nonpolar covalent bond?","The number of electrons shared between atoms","The type of atoms involved in the bond","The distribution of electrons between atoms","The strength of the bond","The distribution of electrons between atoms","The main difference between a polar covalent bond and a nonpolar covalent bond is the distribution of electrons between atoms, with polar bonds having an unequal distribution of electrons and nonpolar bonds having an equal distribution.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-type-of-chemical-fb000230","Science G9: Explaining how atoms bond with each - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-7 Explaining how atoms bond with each other, identifying what is a chemical bond and how it is formed, and distinguishing between different types","4. Explaining the concept of a chemical bond, comparing its different types (ionic, covalent, metallic, and polar), and describing how atoms are linked together by different chemical bonds to form compounds, using examples and illustrative models.","Which type of chemical bond involves the sharing of one or more pairs of electrons between atoms?","Ionic bond","Covalent bond","Metallic bond","Hydrogen bond","Covalent bond","A covalent bond involves the sharing of one or more pairs of electrons between atoms, resulting in a strong and stable chemical bond.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-type-of-chemical-32b7ff75","Science G9: Explaining how atoms bond with each - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-7 Explaining how atoms bond with each other, identifying what is a chemical bond and how it is formed, and distinguishing between different types","4. Explaining the concept of a chemical bond, comparing its different types (ionic, covalent, metallic, and polar), and describing how atoms are linked together by different chemical bonds to form compounds, using examples and illustrative models.","What type of chemical bond is formed when one or more electrons are transferred from one atom to another?","Covalent bond","Ionic bond","Metallic bond","Polar bond","Ionic bond","An ionic bond is formed when one or more electrons are transferred from one atom to another, resulting in the formation of ions with opposite charges that are attracted to each other.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-a-chemical-b3169afd","Science G9: Explaining how atoms bond with each - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-7 Explaining how atoms bond with each other, identifying what is a chemical bond and how it is formed, and distinguishing between different types","4. Explaining the concept of a chemical bond, comparing its different types (ionic, covalent, metallic, and polar), and describing how atoms are linked together by different chemical bonds to form compounds, using examples and illustrative models.","What is a chemical bond?","A type of physical force that holds objects together","A type of attractive force that holds atoms together","A type of energy that is released during a chemical reaction","A type of matter that is found in all living things","A type of attractive force that holds atoms together","A chemical bond is a type of attractive force that holds atoms together, resulting in the formation of a chemical compound.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-main-difference-834d6e36","Science G9: Explaining how atoms bond with each - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-7 Explaining how atoms bond with each other, identifying what is a chemical bond and how it is formed, and distinguishing between different types","3. Illustrating the electronic distribution of a number of groups of the periodic table, and explaining the method of dot representation of electrons, and represent it for a number of elements.","What is the main difference between an ionic bond and a covalent bond?","The number of electrons shared between atoms","The type of atoms involved in the bond","The transfer of electrons between atoms","The strength of the bond","The transfer of electrons between atoms","The main difference between an ionic bond and a covalent bond is the transfer of electrons between atoms, with ionic bonds involving the transfer of electrons and covalent bonds involving the sharing of electrons.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-primary-role-491d82ae","Science G9: Explaining how matter and energy are - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-3-3 Explaining how matter and energy are transferred within an ecosystem, predicting the changes resulting from the extinction of one of its components, describing material cycles in the ecosystem, and inferring their role in supporting its sustainability.","2. Describing material cycles (water, carbon dioxide, and nitrogen) in an ecosystem and their transfer through biotic and abiotic components, and inferring their role in supporting sustainability.","What is the primary role of the water cycle in an ecosystem?","Water is stored in the atmosphere and does not move.","Water is absorbed by plants and used for photosynthesis.","Water is constantly cycled between the atmosphere, land, and oceans.","Water is only found in oceans and does not move elsewhere.","Water is constantly cycled between the atmosphere, land, and oceans.","The water cycle is the continuous process of water movement on, above, and below the surface of the Earth, involving evaporation, condensation, and precipitation.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-of-is-an-6216e8a2","Science G9: Explaining how matter and energy are - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-3-3 Explaining how matter and energy are transferred within an ecosystem, predicting the changes resulting from the extinction of one of its components, describing material cycles in the ecosystem, and inferring their role in supporting its sustainability.","2. Describing material cycles (water, carbon dioxide, and nitrogen) in an ecosystem and their transfer through biotic and abiotic components, and inferring their role in supporting sustainability.","Which of the following is an example of a biogeochemical cycle?","Water cycle","Carbon cycle","Nitrogen cycle","All of the above","All of the above","Biogeochemical cycles refer to the continuous movement of essential nutrients and resources through an ecosystem, including the water cycle, carbon cycle, and nitrogen cycle.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-role-of-8e90b43e","Science G9: Explaining how matter and energy are - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-3-3 Explaining how matter and energy are transferred within an ecosystem, predicting the changes resulting from the extinction of one of its components, describing material cycles in the ecosystem, and inferring their role in supporting its sustainability.","2. Describing material cycles (water, carbon dioxide, and nitrogen) in an ecosystem and their transfer through biotic and abiotic components, and inferring their role in supporting sustainability.","What is the role of the nitrogen cycle in an ecosystem?","The nitrogen cycle only involves the conversion of nitrogen gas to ammonia.","The nitrogen cycle involves the conversion of nitrogen gas to ammonia and back to nitrogen gas.","The nitrogen cycle involves the conversion of nitrogen gas to ammonia, which is then used by plants for growth.","The nitrogen cycle does not involve the conversion of nitrogen gas to ammonia.","The nitrogen cycle involves the conversion of nitrogen gas to ammonia, which is then used by plants for growth.","The nitrogen cycle involves the conversion of nitrogen gas to ammonia, which is then used by plants for growth, and the conversion of ammonia back to nitrogen gas through the process of denitrification.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-of-is-an-732d3096","Science G9: Explaining how matter and energy are - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-3-3 Explaining how matter and energy are transferred within an ecosystem, predicting the changes resulting from the extinction of one of its components, describing material cycles in the ecosystem, and inferring their role in supporting its sustainability.","2. Describing material cycles (water, carbon dioxide, and nitrogen) in an ecosystem and their transfer through biotic and abiotic components, and inferring their role in supporting sustainability.","Which is the largest active carbon sink among the choices below?","The oceans","The atmosphere","A desert road","An urban parking lot","The oceans","The oceans absorb and store a very large amount of carbon dioxide and are the largest active carbon sink among these choices.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-role-of-cc2614ed","Science G9: Explaining how matter and energy are - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-3-3 Explaining how matter and energy are transferred within an ecosystem, predicting the changes resulting from the extinction of one of its components, describing material cycles in the ecosystem, and inferring their role in supporting its sustainability.","2. Describing material cycles (water, carbon dioxide, and nitrogen) in an ecosystem and their transfer through biotic and abiotic components, and inferring their role in supporting sustainability.","What is the role of decomposers in an ecosystem?","Decomposers only break down dead plants.","Decomposers only break down dead animals.","Decomposers break down dead plants and animals, releasing nutrients back into the ecosystem.","Decomposers do not play a role in nutrient cycling.","Decomposers break down dead plants and animals, releasing nutrients back into the ecosystem.","Decomposers, such as bacteria and fungi, break down dead plants and animals, releasing nutrients back into the ecosystem, which are then available for other organisms to use.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-of-is-an-e1de4c8e","Science G9: Explaining how matter and energy are - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-3-3 Explaining how matter and energy are transferred within an ecosystem, predicting the changes resulting from the extinction of one of its components, describing material cycles in the ecosystem, and inferring their role in supporting its sustainability.","2. Describing material cycles (water, carbon dioxide, and nitrogen) in an ecosystem and their transfer through biotic and abiotic components, and inferring their role in supporting sustainability.","Which of the following is an example of a material cycle in an ecosystem?","Water cycle","Carbon cycle","Nitrogen cycle","All of the above","All of the above","Material cycles refer to the continuous movement of essential nutrients and resources through an ecosystem, including the water cycle, carbon cycle, and nitrogen cycle.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-role-of-0213a81a","Science G9: Explaining how matter and energy are - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-3-3 Explaining how matter and energy are transferred within an ecosystem, predicting the changes resulting from the extinction of one of its components, describing material cycles in the ecosystem, and inferring their role in supporting its sustainability.","2. Describing material cycles (water, carbon dioxide, and nitrogen) in an ecosystem and their transfer through biotic and abiotic components, and inferring their role in supporting sustainability.","What is the role of nitrogen in an ecosystem?","Nitrogen is only found in living organisms.","Nitrogen is released into the atmosphere through decomposition.","Nitrogen is absorbed by plants and used for growth.","Nitrogen is not essential for plant growth.","Nitrogen is absorbed by plants and used for growth.","Nitrogen is an essential nutrient for plant growth, and it is absorbed by plants through their roots and used to produce amino acids, which are the building blocks of proteins.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-of-is-an-9188665b","Science G9: Explaining how matter and energy are - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-3-4 Describing types of aquatic and terrestrial ecosystems and the interactive relationships among their components.","1. Identifying the characteristics of a balanced ecosystem based on the availability of factors that maintain its balance (availability of clean water, recycling of nutrients, prevention of soil erosion, and diversity of living organisms).","Which of the following is an example of a biotic component in an ecosystem?","Soil","Plants","Sunlight","Fungi","Plants","Biotic components are living organisms in an ecosystem, such as plants, animals, and microorganisms.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-of-is-an-4330bae1","Science G9: Explaining how matter and energy are - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-3-4 Describing types of aquatic and terrestrial ecosystems and the interactive relationships among their components.","1. Identifying the characteristics of a balanced ecosystem based on the availability of factors that maintain its balance (availability of clean water, recycling of nutrients, prevention of soil erosion, and diversity of living organisms).","Which of the following is an example of an abiotic component in an ecosystem?","Bacteria","Fungi","Plants","Rock","Rock","Abiotic components are non-living factors in an ecosystem, such as rocks, soil, water, and sunlight.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-process-by-02e49910","Science G9: Explaining how matter and energy are - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-3-3 Explaining how matter and energy are transferred within an ecosystem, predicting the changes resulting from the extinction of one of its components, describing material cycles in the ecosystem, and inferring their role in supporting its sustainability.","2. Describing material cycles (water, carbon dioxide, and nitrogen) in an ecosystem and their transfer through biotic and abiotic components, and inferring their role in supporting sustainability.","What is the process by which plants release oxygen into the atmosphere?","Respiration","Photosynthesis","Decomposition","Transpiration","Photosynthesis","Photosynthesis is the process by which plants, algae, and some bacteria convert light energy from the sun into chemical energy in the form of glucose, releasing oxygen as a byproduct.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-impact-of-20ab2981","Science G9: Explaining the causes of climate change - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-1 Explaining the causes of climate change, its effects and associated","2. Analyzing data to identify similarities and differences in temperatures in the surrounding area.","What is the impact of climate change on coral reefs?","They are becoming more diverse due to the increase in global temperatures","They are remaining stable due to the balance of ocean conditions","They are becoming less diverse due to the increase in global temperatures and ocean acidification","They are disappearing due to the decrease in global temperatures","They are becoming less diverse due to the increase in global temperatures and ocean acidification","Climate change is causing coral bleaching and ocean acidification, leading to a decline in coral reef diversity.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-impact-of-cbd97ee1","Science G9: Explaining the causes of climate change - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-1 Explaining the causes of climate change, its effects and associated","3. Providing evidence of the causes of climate change in some parts of the world and its future consequences.","What is the impact of climate change on global sea levels?","They are decreasing due to the melting of ice caps","They are remaining stable due to the balance of ocean currents","They are increasing due to the melting of glaciers and ice caps","They are fluctuating due to seasonal changes","They are increasing due to the melting of glaciers and ice caps","Climate change is causing the melting of glaciers and ice caps, leading to an increase in global sea levels.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-impact-of-0d329935","Science G9: Explaining the causes of climate change - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-1 Explaining the causes of climate change, its effects and associated","2. Analyzing data to identify similarities and differences in temperatures in the surrounding area.","What is the impact of climate change on human health?","It is having no impact on human health","It is leading to an increase in respiratory diseases due to poor air quality","It is leading to an increase in heat-related illnesses due to extreme temperatures","It is leading to an increase in water-borne diseases due to changing precipitation patterns","It is leading to an increase in heat-related illnesses due to extreme temperatures","Climate change is leading to an increase in heat-related illnesses and other health problems due to extreme temperatures and changing weather patterns.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-effect-of-f5e7bc45","Science G9: Explaining the causes of climate change - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-1 Explaining the causes of climate change, its effects and associated","2. Analyzing data to identify similarities and differences in temperatures in the surrounding area.","What is the effect of climate change on polar bears?","Their populations are increasing due to the availability of more food","Their habitats are expanding due to the melting of sea ice","Their populations are declining due to the loss of sea ice and hunting","Their behavior is changing due to the warmer temperatures","Their populations are declining due to the loss of sea ice and hunting","Climate change is causing the loss of sea ice, making it harder for polar bears to hunt and feed, leading to a decline in their populations.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-role-of-6ff2a5c1","Science G9: Explaining the causes of climate change - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-3-1 Tracking some of the changes that occurred to the Earth as a result of human activity and exploring the natural hazards that may occur on Earth and how to predict them.","2. Analyzing data and information about natural events on Earth to comparing them in terms of their effects, and to knowing the role of science in predicting them and limiting their damage.","What is the role of methane in climate change?","It is a greenhouse gas that contributes to climate change","It is a natural gas that is not related to climate change","It is a pollutant that has no impact on climate change","It is a byproduct of human activity that has no impact on climate change","It is a greenhouse gas that contributes to climate change","Methane is a potent greenhouse gas that contributes to climate change, particularly in the short-term.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-role-of-3b12a380","Science G9: Explaining the causes of climate change - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-1 Explaining the causes of climate change, its effects and associated","1. Determining the rates and locations of carbon in the Earth's layers and explaining the process by which it is cyclically transported.","What is the role of deforestation in climate change?","It releases oxygen into the atmosphere","It absorbs carbon dioxide from the atmosphere","It releases carbon dioxide and other greenhouse gases into the atmosphere","It has no impact on climate change","It releases carbon dioxide and other greenhouse gases into the atmosphere","Deforestation releases carbon dioxide and other greenhouse gases into the atmosphere, contributing to climate change.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-effect-of-20eccf8f","Science G9: Explaining the causes of climate change - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-1 Explaining the causes of climate change, its effects and associated","2. Analyzing data to identify similarities and differences in temperatures in the surrounding area.","What is the effect of climate change on weather patterns?","They are becoming more predictable due to the increase in global temperatures","They are becoming more extreme due to the increase in global temperatures","They are remaining the same due to the balance of atmospheric conditions","They are becoming less frequent due to the decrease in global temperatures","They are becoming more extreme due to the increase in global temperatures","Climate change is leading to more extreme weather events such as heatwaves, droughts, and heavy rainfall.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-main-cause-1c19ba33","Science G9: Explaining the causes of climate change - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-1 Explaining the causes of climate change, its effects and associated","3. Providing evidence of the causes of climate change in some parts of the world and its future consequences.","What is the main cause of the increase in ocean acidification?","The release of carbon dioxide from the ocean","The increase in global temperatures","The burning of fossil fuels","The melting of polar ice caps","The burning of fossil fuels","The burning of fossil fuels releases carbon dioxide into the atmosphere, which is then absorbed by the ocean, leading to ocean acidification.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-effect-of-e29b0d98","Science G9: Explaining the causes of climate change - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-1 Explaining the causes of climate change, its effects and associated","2. Analyzing data to identify similarities and differences in temperatures in the surrounding area.","What is the effect of climate change on agriculture?","It is becoming more productive due to the increase in global temperatures","It is remaining stable due to the balance of weather conditions","It is becoming less productive due to the increase in global temperatures and changing precipitation patterns","It is disappearing due to the decrease in global temperatures","It is becoming less productive due to the increase in global temperatures and changing precipitation patterns","Climate change is leading to changing precipitation patterns and increased temperatures, making it harder for crops to grow and leading to a decline in agricultural productivity.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-main-cause-e41c5985","Science G9: Explaining the causes of climate change - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-1 Explaining the causes of climate change, its effects and associated","3. Providing evidence of the causes of climate change in some parts of the world and its future consequences.","What is the main cause of the increase in global temperatures?","The burning of fossil fuels such as coal, oil, and natural gas","The release of greenhouse gases from landfills","The deforestation of large areas of the Amazon rainforest","The melting of polar ice caps due to seasonal changes","The burning of fossil fuels such as coal, oil, and natural gas","The burning of fossil fuels releases carbon dioxide and other greenhouse gases into the atmosphere, leading to an increase in global temperatures.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-layer-of-1481bef2","Science G9: Explaining the causes of stresses affecting - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-6 Explaining the causes of stresses affecting the rocks that make up the Earth’s interior, and describing the resulting effects.","1. Defining the theory of the earth's plates, their structure and components, listing their types (oceanic and continental), identifying the lithosphere, the plate and the fluid layers, and distinguishing the shape of each of them.","What is the layer of the Earth's interior that surrounds the core?","The crust","The mantle","The lithosphere","The hydrosphere","The mantle","The layer of the Earth's interior that surrounds the core is the mantle, which is a thick layer of hot, viscous rock.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-shape-of-9b28e390","Science G9: Explaining the causes of stresses affecting - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-6 Explaining the causes of stresses affecting the rocks that make up the Earth’s interior, and describing the resulting effects.","1. Defining the theory of the earth's plates, their structure and components, listing their types (oceanic and continental), identifying the lithosphere, the plate and the fluid layers, and distinguishing the shape of each of them.","What is the shape of the Earth's core?","Spherical","Elliptical","Cylindrical","Irregular","Spherical","The Earth's core is a sphere, which is a perfect ball shape.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-process-by-294e009c","Science G9: Explaining the causes of stresses affecting - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-6 Explaining the causes of stresses affecting the rocks that make up the Earth’s interior, and describing the resulting effects.","1. Defining the theory of the earth's plates, their structure and components, listing their types (oceanic and continental), identifying the lithosphere, the plate and the fluid layers, and distinguishing the shape of each of them.","What is the process by which the Earth's surface is shaped by the movement of plates?","Weathering","Erosion","Plate tectonics","Continental drift","Plate tectonics","Plate tectonics is the process by which the Earth's surface is shaped by the movement of plates.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-lithosphere-83dfc7a8","Science G9: Explaining the causes of stresses affecting - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-6 Explaining the causes of stresses affecting the rocks that make up the Earth’s interior, and describing the resulting effects.","1. Defining the theory of the earth's plates, their structure and components, listing their types (oceanic and continental), identifying the lithosphere, the plate and the fluid layers, and distinguishing the shape of each of them.","What is the lithosphere?","The outermost layer of the Earth's crust","The innermost layer of the Earth's core","The layer of the Earth's mantle that surrounds the core","The layer of the Earth's crust that is broken into plates","The outermost layer of the Earth's crust","The lithosphere is the outermost layer of the Earth's crust, which is broken into several large plates that move relative to each other.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-are-two-main-c0606b81","Science G9: Explaining the causes of stresses affecting - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-6 Explaining the causes of stresses affecting the rocks that make up the Earth’s interior, and describing the resulting effects.","1. Defining the theory of the earth's plates, their structure and components, listing their types (oceanic and continental), identifying the lithosphere, the plate and the fluid layers, and distinguishing the shape of each of them.","What are the two main types of tectonic plates?","Oceanic and continental plates","Continental and atmospheric plates","Oceanic and terrestrial plates","Continental and oceanic plates","Oceanic and continental plates","The two main types of tectonic plates are oceanic plates, which are denser and heavier, and continental plates, which are less dense and lighter.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-fluid-layer-5d06e7d7","Science G9: Explaining the causes of stresses affecting - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-6 Explaining the causes of stresses affecting the rocks that make up the Earth’s interior, and describing the resulting effects.","1. Defining the theory of the earth's plates, their structure and components, listing their types (oceanic and continental), identifying the lithosphere, the plate and the fluid layers, and distinguishing the shape of each of them.","What is the fluid layer of the Earth's interior?","The mantle","The outer core","The crust","The hydrosphere","The outer core","Earth’s outer core is a fluid layer composed mainly of molten iron and nickel.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-theory-explains-c9290758","Science G9: Explaining the causes of stresses affecting - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-6 Explaining the causes of stresses affecting the rocks that make up the Earth’s interior, and describing the resulting effects.","1. Defining the theory of the earth's plates, their structure and components, listing their types (oceanic and continental), identifying the lithosphere, the plate and the fluid layers, and distinguishing the shape of each of them.","What is the theory that explains the movement of the Earth's surface?","The theory of continental drift","The theory of plate tectonics","The theory of erosion","The theory of weathering","The theory of plate tectonics","The theory of plate tectonics explains how the Earth's surface is divided into moving plates that can cause earthquakes, volcanoes, and the creation of mountain ranges.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-layer-of-695c19ed","Science G9: Explaining the causes of stresses affecting - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-6 Explaining the causes of stresses affecting the rocks that make up the Earth’s interior, and describing the resulting effects.","1. Defining the theory of the earth's plates, their structure and components, listing their types (oceanic and continental), identifying the lithosphere, the plate and the fluid layers, and distinguishing the shape of each of them.","What is the layer of the Earth's interior that is broken into plates?","The core","The mantle","The crust","The lithosphere","The lithosphere","The lithosphere is the layer of the Earth's interior that is broken into plates.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-type-of-d825d7f0","Science G9: Explaining the causes of stresses affecting - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-6 Explaining the causes of stresses affecting the rocks that make up the Earth’s interior, and describing the resulting effects.","1. Defining the theory of the earth's plates, their structure and components, listing their types (oceanic and continental), identifying the lithosphere, the plate and the fluid layers, and distinguishing the shape of each of them.","What is the type of plate that is less dense and lighter?","Oceanic plate","Continental plate","Mantle plate","Crust plate","Continental plate","Continental plates are less dense and lighter than oceanic plates.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-process-by-c73bcfab","Science G9: Explaining the causes of stresses affecting - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-6 Explaining the causes of stresses affecting the rocks that make up the Earth’s interior, and describing the resulting effects.","1. Defining the theory of the earth's plates, their structure and components, listing their types (oceanic and continental), identifying the lithosphere, the plate and the fluid layers, and distinguishing the shape of each of them.","What is the process by which the Earth's surface is broken into plates?","Plate tectonics","Continental drift","Weathering","Erosion","Plate tectonics","Plate tectonics is the process by which the Earth's surface is broken into plates that move relative to each other.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-term-point-febd643e","Science G9: Explaining the causes of stresses affecting - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-6 Explaining the causes of stresses affecting the rocks that make up the Earth’s interior, and describing the resulting effects.","2. Defining earthquakes, seismic waves and their types, and defining the epicenter of the earthquake and distinguishing each of them through drawing.","What is the term for the point inside the Earth where an earthquake occurs?","Epicenter","Hypocenter","Focal point","Seismic station","Hypocenter","The hypocenter is the point inside the Earth where an earthquake occurs.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-type-of-seismic-55749f99","Science G9: Explaining the causes of stresses affecting - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-6 Explaining the causes of stresses affecting the rocks that make up the Earth’s interior, and describing the resulting effects.","2. Defining earthquakes, seismic waves and their types, and defining the epicenter of the earthquake and distinguishing each of them through drawing.","What type of seismic wave travels through the Earth's surface?","P-waves","S-waves","Surface waves","Body waves","Surface waves","Surface waves travel through the Earth's surface and are responsible for most of the damage caused by earthquakes.","/image-source/sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-type-of-seismic-55749f99.png","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-type-of-seismic-de696c5f","Science G9: Explaining the causes of stresses affecting - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-6 Explaining the causes of stresses affecting the rocks that make up the Earth’s interior, and describing the resulting effects.","2. Defining earthquakes, seismic waves and their types, and defining the epicenter of the earthquake and distinguishing each of them through drawing.","What type of seismic wave is responsible for most of the damage caused by earthquakes?","P-waves","S-waves","Surface waves","Body waves","Surface waves","Surface waves are responsible for most of the damage caused by earthquakes due to their ability to cause buildings to sway and collapse.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-term-point-a7ee2642","Science G9: Explaining the causes of stresses affecting - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-6 Explaining the causes of stresses affecting the rocks that make up the Earth’s interior, and describing the resulting effects.","2. Defining earthquakes, seismic waves and their types, and defining the epicenter of the earthquake and distinguishing each of them through drawing.","What is the term for the point on the Earth's surface where seismic waves are recorded?","Epicenter","Hypocenter","Seismic station","Focal point","Focal point","A focal point is the point on the Earth's surface where seismic waves are recorded.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-main-effect-344dc752","Science G9: Explaining the causes of stresses affecting - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-3-1 Tracking some of the changes that occurred to the Earth as a result of human activity and exploring the natural hazards that may occur on Earth and how to predict them.","2. Analyzing data and information about natural events on Earth to comparing them in terms of their effects, and to knowing the role of science in predicting them and limiting their damage.","What is the main effect of earthquakes on the Earth's surface?","Volcanic eruptions","Weathering and erosion","Landslides and soil liquefaction","Human activities","Landslides and soil liquefaction","Earthquakes can cause landslides and soil liquefaction, which can lead to significant damage and loss of life.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-main-cause-ef5da13b","Science G9: Explaining the causes of stresses affecting - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-6 Explaining the causes of stresses affecting the rocks that make up the Earth’s interior, and describing the resulting effects.","3. Explaining the phenomena resulting from earthquakes, explaining the reasons of their occurrence, showing their destructive effects, and explaining ways to be safe from them.","What is the main cause of earthquakes?","Movement of the Earth's crust due to plate tectonics","Volcanic eruptions","Weathering and erosion","Human activities","Movement of the Earth's crust due to plate tectonics","Earthquakes are primarily caused by the movement of the Earth's crust due to plate tectonics.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-point-on-a83b6fec","Science G9: Explaining the causes of stresses affecting - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-6 Explaining the causes of stresses affecting the rocks that make up the Earth’s interior, and describing the resulting effects.","2. Defining earthquakes, seismic waves and their types, and defining the epicenter of the earthquake and distinguishing each of them through drawing.","What is the point on the Earth's surface directly above the epicenter of an earthquake?","Epicenter","Hypocenter","Focal point","Seismic station","Epicenter","The epicenter is the point on the Earth's surface directly above the hypocenter of an earthquake.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-term-point-6836f277","Science G9: Explaining the causes of stresses affecting - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-6 Explaining the causes of stresses affecting the rocks that make up the Earth’s interior, and describing the resulting effects.","2. Defining earthquakes, seismic waves and their types, and defining the epicenter of the earthquake and distinguishing each of them through drawing.","What is the term for the point on the Earth's surface where seismic waves are detected?","Epicenter","Hypocenter","Seismic station","Focal point","Seismic station","A seismic station is the point on the Earth's surface where seismic waves are detected.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-term-movement-49c12114","Science G9: Explaining the causes of stresses affecting - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-6 Explaining the causes of stresses affecting the rocks that make up the Earth’s interior, and describing the resulting effects.","3. Explaining the phenomena resulting from earthquakes, explaining the reasons of their occurrence, showing their destructive effects, and explaining ways to be safe from them.","What is the term for the movement of the Earth's crust that causes earthquakes?","Plate tectonics","Volcanic eruptions","Weathering and erosion","Human activities","Plate tectonics","Plate tectonics is the movement of the Earth's crust that causes earthquakes.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-type-of-seismic-2758973b","Science G9: Explaining the causes of stresses affecting - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-6 Explaining the causes of stresses affecting the rocks that make up the Earth’s interior, and describing the resulting effects.","2. Defining earthquakes, seismic waves and their types, and defining the epicenter of the earthquake and distinguishing each of them through drawing.","What type of seismic wave travels through the Earth's interior?","P-waves","S-waves","Surface waves","Body waves","P-waves","P-waves travel through the Earth's interior and are the fastest type of seismic wave.","/image-source/sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-type-of-seismic-2758973b.png","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-why-is-it-e7b8623a","Science G9: Explaining the causes of stresses affecting - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-6 Explaining the causes of stresses affecting the rocks that make up the Earth’s interior, and describing the resulting effects.","3. Explaining the phenomena resulting from earthquakes, explaining the reasons of their occurrence, showing their destructive effects, and explaining ways to be safe from them.","Why is it essential to have an emergency plan during an earthquake?","Because of the beauty of the earthquake","Because of the noise of the earthquake","Because of the damage to buildings and infrastructure","Because it can help reduce the risk of injury or damage","Because it can help reduce the risk of injury or damage","Having an emergency plan during an earthquake can help reduce the risk of injury or damage by providing a clear plan of action.","/image-source/sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-why-is-it-e7b8623a.png","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-one-way-97b53d66","Science G9: Explaining the causes of stresses affecting - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-6 Explaining the causes of stresses affecting the rocks that make up the Earth’s interior, and describing the resulting effects.","3. Explaining the phenomena resulting from earthquakes, explaining the reasons of their occurrence, showing their destructive effects, and explaining ways to be safe from them.","What is one way to be safe from earthquakes?","By building structures that can withstand strong winds","By building structures that can withstand floods","By securing heavy objects and having an emergency plan","By ignoring the earthquake warning systems","By securing heavy objects and having an emergency plan","Securing heavy objects and having an emergency plan can help reduce the risk of injury or damage during an earthquake.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-why-do-earthquakes-958b7542","Science G9: Explaining the causes of stresses affecting - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-6 Explaining the causes of stresses affecting the rocks that make up the Earth’s interior, and describing the resulting effects.","3. Explaining the phenomena resulting from earthquakes, explaining the reasons of their occurrence, showing their destructive effects, and explaining ways to be safe from them.","Why do earthquakes often occur in certain regions?","Because of the presence of mountains","Because of the presence of oceans","Because of the movement of tectonic plates","Because of the climate","Because of the movement of tectonic plates","Earthquakes often occur in regions where tectonic plates are moving, such as near fault lines.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-happens-to-earth-66618c37","Science G9: Explaining the causes of stresses affecting - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-6 Explaining the causes of stresses affecting the rocks that make up the Earth’s interior, and describing the resulting effects.","3. Explaining the phenomena resulting from earthquakes, explaining the reasons of their occurrence, showing their destructive effects, and explaining ways to be safe from them.","What happens to the Earth's surface during an earthquake?","The Earth's surface becomes smoother","The Earth's surface becomes more rugged","The Earth's surface experiences sudden shaking and trembling","The Earth's surface remains unchanged","The Earth's surface experiences sudden shaking and trembling","During an earthquake, the Earth's surface experiences sudden shaking and trembling due to the release of energy.","/image-source/sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-happens-to-earth-66618c37.png","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-main-cause-a2d964f6","Science G9: Explaining the causes of stresses affecting - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-6 Explaining the causes of stresses affecting the rocks that make up the Earth’s interior, and describing the resulting effects.","3. Explaining the phenomena resulting from earthquakes, explaining the reasons of their occurrence, showing their destructive effects, and explaining ways to be safe from them.","What is the main cause of earthquakes?","Movement of the Earth's crust due to plate tectonics","Volcanic eruptions","Weathering and erosion","Human activities","Movement of the Earth's crust due to plate tectonics","Earthquakes are primarily caused by the movement of the Earth's crust due to plate tectonics.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-term-sudden-b4ba5b2c","Science G9: Explaining the causes of stresses affecting - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-6 Explaining the causes of stresses affecting the rocks that make up the Earth’s interior, and describing the resulting effects.","2. Defining earthquakes, seismic waves and their types, and defining the epicenter of the earthquake and distinguishing each of them through drawing.","What is the term for the sudden release of energy that causes an earthquake?","Seismic wave","Tectonic shift","Earthquake rupture","Ground tremor","Earthquake rupture","The sudden release of energy that causes an earthquake is called an earthquake rupture.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-why-are-earthquakes-4acaf6f4","Science G9: Explaining the causes of stresses affecting - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-6 Explaining the causes of stresses affecting the rocks that make up the Earth’s interior, and describing the resulting effects.","3. Explaining the phenomena resulting from earthquakes, explaining the reasons of their occurrence, showing their destructive effects, and explaining ways to be safe from them.","Why are earthquakes often destructive?","Because of the beauty of the earthquake","Because of the noise of the earthquake","Because of the damage to buildings and infrastructure","Because of the loss of life","Because of the damage to buildings and infrastructure","Earthquakes are often destructive because they can cause significant damage to buildings and infrastructure, leading to loss of life and property.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-one-way-fffbc9c5","Science G9: Explaining the causes of stresses affecting - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-6 Explaining the causes of stresses affecting the rocks that make up the Earth’s interior, and describing the resulting effects.","3. Explaining the phenomena resulting from earthquakes, explaining the reasons of their occurrence, showing their destructive effects, and explaining ways to be safe from them.","What is one way to prepare for an earthquake?","By having a first aid kit","By having a fire extinguisher","By having a emergency kit and a family emergency plan","By ignoring the earthquake warning systems","By having a emergency kit and a family emergency plan","Having a emergency kit and a family emergency plan can help you prepare for an earthquake and reduce the risk of injury or damage.","/image-source/sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-one-way-fffbc9c5.png","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-term-shaking-eb195b8b","Science G9: Explaining the causes of stresses affecting - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-6 Explaining the causes of stresses affecting the rocks that make up the Earth’s interior, and describing the resulting effects.","2. Defining earthquakes, seismic waves and their types, and defining the epicenter of the earthquake and distinguishing each of them through drawing.","What is the term for the shaking of the Earth's surface during an earthquake?","Seismic wave","Tectonic shift","Earthquake rupture","Ground tremor","Seismic wave","The shaking of the Earth's surface during an earthquake is called a seismic wave.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-one-way-7153ddda","Science G9: Explaining the causes of stresses affecting - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-6 Explaining the causes of stresses affecting the rocks that make up the Earth’s interior, and describing the resulting effects.","3. Explaining the phenomena resulting from earthquakes, explaining the reasons of their occurrence, showing their destructive effects, and explaining ways to be safe from them.","What is one way to reduce the risk of injury or damage during an earthquake?","By ignoring the earthquake warning systems","By not securing heavy objects","By not having an emergency kit","By securing heavy objects and having an emergency plan","By securing heavy objects and having an emergency plan","Securing heavy objects and having an emergency plan can help reduce the risk of injury or damage during an earthquake.","/image-source/sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-one-way-7153ddda.png","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-type-of-volcano-40abf107","Science G9: Explaining the causes of stresses affecting - Set 4","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-6 Explaining the causes of stresses affecting the rocks that make up the Earth’s interior, and describing the resulting effects.","4. Listing the different forms of volcanoes (shield, conical, compound, and fissure eruptions), identifying and distinguishing each of them, and providing an example.","What type of volcano produces long, narrow fissures in the Earth's surface?","Shield volcano.","Conical volcano.","Compound volcano.","Fissure eruption.","Fissure eruption.","Fissure eruptions produce long, narrow fissures in the Earth's surface, which can produce a range of lava flows and explosive eruptions.","/image-source/sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-type-of-volcano-40abf107.png","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-a-shield-97be00b5","Science G9: Explaining the causes of stresses affecting - Set 4","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-6 Explaining the causes of stresses affecting the rocks that make up the Earth’s interior, and describing the resulting effects.","4. Listing the different forms of volcanoes (shield, conical, compound, and fissure eruptions), identifying and distinguishing each of them, and providing an example.","What is a shield volcano?","A volcano that produces explosive eruptions and forms a steep-sided cone.","A volcano that is gently sloping and has a broad base, producing fluid lava flows.","A volcano that has multiple vents and produces a variety of eruption styles.","A volcano that produces long, narrow fissures in the Earth's surface.","A volcano that is gently sloping and has a broad base, producing fluid lava flows.","Shield volcanoes are characterized by their gently sloping shape and broad base, which is typical of volcanoes that produce fluid lava flows.","/image-source/sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-a-shield-97be00b5.png","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-main-characteristic-95a9319b","Science G9: Explaining the causes of stresses affecting - Set 4","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-6 Explaining the causes of stresses affecting the rocks that make up the Earth’s interior, and describing the resulting effects.","4. Listing the different forms of volcanoes (shield, conical, compound, and fissure eruptions), identifying and distinguishing each of them, and providing an example.","What is the main characteristic of a conical volcano?","A gently sloping shape and broad base.","A steep-sided shape and narrow base.","A single, large vent.","Multiple vents and varied eruption styles.","A steep-sided shape and narrow base.","Conical volcanoes are characterized by their steep-sided shape and narrow base, which is typical of volcanoes that produce explosive eruptions.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-a-conical-1dbf8826","Science G9: Explaining the causes of stresses affecting - Set 4","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-6 Explaining the causes of stresses affecting the rocks that make up the Earth’s interior, and describing the resulting effects.","4. Listing the different forms of volcanoes (shield, conical, compound, and fissure eruptions), identifying and distinguishing each of them, and providing an example.","What is a conical volcano?","A volcano that is gently sloping and has a broad base, producing fluid lava flows.","A volcano that has multiple vents and produces a variety of eruption styles.","A volcano that produces long, narrow fissures in the Earth's surface.","A volcano that is steep-sided and has a narrow base, producing explosive eruptions.","A volcano that is steep-sided and has a narrow base, producing explosive eruptions.","Conical volcanoes are characterized by their steep-sided shape and narrow base, which is typical of volcanoes that produce explosive eruptions.","/image-source/sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-a-conical-1dbf8826.png","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-a-compound-e86dafe0","Science G9: Explaining the causes of stresses affecting - Set 4","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-6 Explaining the causes of stresses affecting the rocks that make up the Earth’s interior, and describing the resulting effects.","4. Listing the different forms of volcanoes (shield, conical, compound, and fissure eruptions), identifying and distinguishing each of them, and providing an example.","What is a compound volcano?","A volcano that is a single, large vent.","A volcano that has multiple vents and produces a variety of eruption styles.","A volcano that produces long, narrow fissures in the Earth's surface.","A volcano that is gently sloping and has a broad base, producing fluid lava flows.","A volcano that has multiple vents and produces a variety of eruption styles.","Compound volcanoes are characterized by their multiple vents and varied eruption styles, which can produce a range of lava flows and explosive eruptions.","/image-source/sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-a-compound-e86dafe0.png","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-main-characteristic-ea1711e1","Science G9: Explaining the causes of stresses affecting - Set 4","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-6 Explaining the causes of stresses affecting the rocks that make up the Earth’s interior, and describing the resulting effects.","3. Explaining the phenomena resulting from earthquakes, explaining the reasons of their occurrence, showing their destructive effects, and explaining ways to be safe from them.","What is the main characteristic of a shield volcano?","A steep-sided shape and narrow base.","A gently sloping shape and broad base.","A single, large vent.","Multiple vents and varied eruption styles.","A gently sloping shape and broad base.","Shield volcanoes are characterized by their gently sloping shape and broad base, which is typical of volcanoes that produce fluid lava flows.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-a-fissure-c28a61b3","Science G9: Explaining the causes of stresses affecting - Set 4","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-6 Explaining the causes of stresses affecting the rocks that make up the Earth’s interior, and describing the resulting effects.","3. Explaining the phenomena resulting from earthquakes, explaining the reasons of their occurrence, showing their destructive effects, and explaining ways to be safe from them.","What is a fissure eruption?","A type of eruption that produces a single, large vent.","A type of eruption that produces long, narrow fissures in the Earth's surface.","A type of eruption that is characterized by a gentle slope and broad base.","A type of eruption that is characterized by a steep-sided shape and narrow base.","A type of eruption that produces long, narrow fissures in the Earth's surface.","Fissure eruptions produce long, narrow fissures in the Earth's surface, which can produce a range of lava flows and explosive eruptions.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-are-main-types-874910a0","Science G9: Explaining the causes of stresses affecting - Set 4","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-6 Explaining the causes of stresses affecting the rocks that make up the Earth’s interior, and describing the resulting effects.","4. Listing the different forms of volcanoes (shield, conical, compound, and fissure eruptions), identifying and distinguishing each of them, and providing an example.","What are the main types of volcanoes?","Volcanoes that are gently sloping and have a broad base, such as shield volcanoes.","Volcanoes that are steep-sided and have a narrow base, such as conical volcanoes.","Volcanoes that have multiple vents and produce a variety of eruption styles, such as compound volcanoes.","Volcanoes that produce long, narrow fissures in the Earth's surface, such as fissure eruptions.","Volcanoes that are gently sloping and have a broad base, such as shield volcanoes.","Shield volcanoes are characterized by their gently sloping shape and broad base, which is typical of volcanoes that produce fluid lava flows.","/image-source/sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-are-main-types-874910a0.png","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-main-characteristic-90064f6c","Science G9: Explaining the causes of stresses affecting - Set 4","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-6 Explaining the causes of stresses affecting the rocks that make up the Earth’s interior, and describing the resulting effects.","3. Explaining the phenomena resulting from earthquakes, explaining the reasons of their occurrence, showing their destructive effects, and explaining ways to be safe from them.","What is the main characteristic of a fissure eruption?","A single, large vent.","A gently sloping shape and broad base.","A steep-sided shape and narrow base.","Long, narrow fissures in the Earth's surface.","Long, narrow fissures in the Earth's surface.","Fissure eruptions produce long, narrow fissures in the Earth's surface, which can produce a range of lava flows and explosive eruptions.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-type-of-volcano-4370896c","Science G9: Explaining the causes of stresses affecting - Set 4","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-6 Explaining the causes of stresses affecting the rocks that make up the Earth’s interior, and describing the resulting effects.","4. Listing the different forms of volcanoes (shield, conical, compound, and fissure eruptions), identifying and distinguishing each of them, and providing an example.","What type of volcano has multiple vents and produces a variety of eruption styles?","Shield volcano.","Conical volcano.","Compound volcano.","Fissure eruption.","Compound volcano.","Compound volcanoes are characterized by their multiple vents and varied eruption styles, which can produce a range of lava flows and explosive eruptions.","/image-source/sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-type-of-volcano-4370896c.png","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-process-called-7e99d554","Science G9: Explaining the causes of stresses affecting the rocks that - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-7 Analyzing information and data related to the theory of plate motion and continental drift and predicting its results and benefits.","2. Explaining what is meant by moving plate boundaries, listing their types (convergence boundaries, divergence boundaries, and lateral or transformational boundaries), and distinguishing each type.","What is the process called when two tectonic plates move apart?","Continental drift","Plate convergence","Rifting","Subduction","Rifting","Rifting is the process of two tectonic plates moving apart, which can create new oceanic crust and lead to the formation of rift valleys.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-theory-explains-d250d691","Science G9: Explaining the causes of stresses affecting the rocks that - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-6 Explaining the causes of stresses affecting the rocks that make up the Earth’s interior, and describing the resulting effects.","1. Defining the theory of the earth's plates, their structure and components, listing their types (oceanic and continental), identifying the lithosphere, the plate and the fluid layers, and distinguishing the shape of each of them.","What is the theory that explains the movement of the Earth's surface?","The theory of continental drift","The theory of plate tectonics","The theory of erosion and deposition","The theory of weathering and landforms","The theory of plate tectonics","The theory of plate tectonics explains how the Earth's surface is divided into moving plates that can create and destroy landscapes.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-lithosphere-f2ef0f76","Science G9: Explaining the causes of stresses affecting the rocks that - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-6 Explaining the causes of stresses affecting the rocks that make up the Earth’s interior, and describing the resulting effects.","1. Defining the theory of the earth's plates, their structure and components, listing their types (oceanic and continental), identifying the lithosphere, the plate and the fluid layers, and distinguishing the shape of each of them.","What is the lithosphere?","The outermost layer of the Earth's crust","The innermost layer of the Earth's core","The layer of the Earth's mantle that surrounds the core","The layer of the Earth's atmosphere that surrounds the planet","The outermost layer of the Earth's crust","The lithosphere is the outermost layer of the Earth's crust, which includes the crust and the uppermost part of the mantle.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-are-two-main-475d7498","Science G9: Explaining the causes of stresses affecting the rocks that - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-6 Explaining the causes of stresses affecting the rocks that make up the Earth’s interior, and describing the resulting effects.","1. Defining the theory of the earth's plates, their structure and components, listing their types (oceanic and continental), identifying the lithosphere, the plate and the fluid layers, and distinguishing the shape of each of them.","What are the two main types of tectonic plates?","Oceanic and continental plates","Continental and atmospheric plates","Oceanic and terrestrial plates","Continental and aquatic plates","Oceanic and continental plates","The two main types of tectonic plates are oceanic and continental plates, which are distinguished by their composition and location.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-fluid-layer-95fc87a5","Science G9: Explaining the causes of stresses affecting the rocks that - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-6 Explaining the causes of stresses affecting the rocks that make up the Earth’s interior, and describing the resulting effects.","1. Defining the theory of the earth's plates, their structure and components, listing their types (oceanic and continental), identifying the lithosphere, the plate and the fluid layers, and distinguishing the shape of each of them.","What is the fluid layer of the Earth's interior?","The mantle","The outer core","The inner core","The crust","The outer core","The fluid layer of the Earth's interior is the outer core, which is a liquid layer of iron and nickel that surrounds the solid inner core.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-shape-of-64feb67b","Science G9: Explaining the causes of stresses affecting the rocks that - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-6 Explaining the causes of stresses affecting the rocks that make up the Earth’s interior, and describing the resulting effects.","1. Defining the theory of the earth's plates, their structure and components, listing their types (oceanic and continental), identifying the lithosphere, the plate and the fluid layers, and distinguishing the shape of each of them.","What is the shape of the Earth's mantle?","Spherical","Elliptical","Flat","Irregular","Spherical","The Earth's mantle is a large, spherical layer of hot, viscous rock that surrounds the core.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-boundary-two-18385ff4","Science G9: Explaining the causes of stresses affecting the rocks that - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-6 Explaining the causes of stresses affecting the rocks that make up the Earth’s interior, and describing the resulting effects.","3. Explaining the phenomena resulting from earthquakes, explaining the reasons of their occurrence, showing their destructive effects, and explaining ways to be safe from them.","What is the boundary between two tectonic plates called?","A fault line","A mountain range","A rift valley","A plate boundary","A plate boundary","A plate boundary is the boundary between two tectonic plates, where they interact and create geological features such as faults, mountain ranges, and rift valleys.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-process-called-5a82397c","Science G9: Explaining the causes of stresses affecting the rocks that - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-7 Analyzing information and data related to the theory of plate motion and continental drift and predicting its results and benefits.","2. Explaining what is meant by moving plate boundaries, listing their types (convergence boundaries, divergence boundaries, and lateral or transformational boundaries), and distinguishing each type.","What is the process called when two tectonic plates collide?","Continental drift","Plate convergence","Rifting","Subduction","Plate convergence","Plate convergence is the process of two tectonic plates colliding, which can create mountain ranges and lead to the formation of volcanoes.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-process-called-4464763b","Science G9: Explaining the causes of stresses affecting the rocks that - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-7 Analyzing information and data related to the theory of plate motion and continental drift and predicting its results and benefits.","2. Explaining what is meant by moving plate boundaries, listing their types (convergence boundaries, divergence boundaries, and lateral or transformational boundaries), and distinguishing each type.","What is the process called when a tectonic plate sinks into the Earth's mantle?","Continental drift","Plate convergence","Rifting","Subduction","Subduction","Subduction is the process of a tectonic plate sinking into the Earth's mantle, which can create deep-sea trenches and lead to the formation of volcanoes.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-layer-of-9028bc4e","Science G9: Explaining the causes of stresses affecting the rocks that - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-6 Explaining the causes of stresses affecting the rocks that make up the Earth’s interior, and describing the resulting effects.","1. Defining the theory of the earth's plates, their structure and components, listing their types (oceanic and continental), identifying the lithosphere, the plate and the fluid layers, and distinguishing the shape of each of them.","What is the layer of the Earth's interior that surrounds the core?","The mantle","The outer core","The inner core","The crust","The mantle","The mantle is the layer of the Earth's interior that surrounds the core, which is a large, spherical layer of hot, viscous rock.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-of-metamorphic-rocks-f22f5e9c","Science G9: Explaining the characteristics of different rocks - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-5 Explaining the characteristics of different rocks, their classification methods, and the cycle of their change from one type to another.","2. Comparing the types of metamorphic rocks to determine the similarities and differences in their characteristics.","Which of the following metamorphic rocks is formed from the alteration of quartz and feldspar minerals?","Marble","Slate","Gneiss","Granulite","Gneiss","Gneiss is a type of metamorphic rock that forms from the alteration of quartz and feldspar minerals, which are common in igneous and sedimentary rocks.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-of-metamorphic-rocks-f0b1d5da","Science G9: Explaining the characteristics of different rocks - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-5 Explaining the characteristics of different rocks, their classification methods, and the cycle of their change from one type to another.","2. Comparing the types of metamorphic rocks to determine the similarities and differences in their characteristics.","Which of the following metamorphic rocks is formed from the alteration of quartz minerals?","Marble","Slate","Gneiss","Quartzite","Quartzite","Quartzite is a type of metamorphic rock that forms from the alteration of quartz minerals, which are common in igneous and sedimentary rocks.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-primary-process-5436e34f","Science G9: Explaining the characteristics of different rocks - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-5 Explaining the characteristics of different rocks, their classification methods, and the cycle of their change from one type to another.","2. Comparing the types of metamorphic rocks to determine the similarities and differences in their characteristics.","What is the primary process that transforms sedimentary rocks into metamorphic rocks?","The rocks are subjected to high temperatures and pressures over a long period of time.","The rocks are exposed to water and wind erosion.","The rocks are melted and reformed into new minerals.","The rocks are affected by chemical reactions with surrounding rocks.","The rocks are subjected to high temperatures and pressures over a long period of time.","Metamorphic rocks are formed when sedimentary or igneous rocks are subjected to high temperatures and pressures, causing changes in their mineral composition and structure.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-of-metamorphic-rocks-44828fa7","Science G9: Explaining the characteristics of different rocks - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-5 Explaining the characteristics of different rocks, their classification methods, and the cycle of their change from one type to another.","2. Comparing the types of metamorphic rocks to determine the similarities and differences in their characteristics.","Which of the following metamorphic rocks is formed from the alteration of shale?","Marble","Slate","Gneiss","Schist","Slate","Slate is a type of metamorphic rock that forms from the alteration of shale, which is a type of sedimentary rock.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-primary-characteristic-2ea01b46","Science G9: Explaining the characteristics of different rocks - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-5 Explaining the characteristics of different rocks, their classification methods, and the cycle of their change from one type to another.","2. Comparing the types of metamorphic rocks to determine the similarities and differences in their characteristics.","What is the primary characteristic that distinguishes marble from other metamorphic rocks?","Its high density","Its high hardness","Its high calcium content","Its banded or foliated structure","Its high calcium content","Marble is a type of metamorphic rock that is characterized by its high calcium content, which is derived from the alteration of limestone or dolostone.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-of-metamorphic-rocks-ac5dd186","Science G9: Explaining the characteristics of different rocks - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-5 Explaining the characteristics of different rocks, their classification methods, and the cycle of their change from one type to another.","2. Comparing the types of metamorphic rocks to determine the similarities and differences in their characteristics.","Which of the following metamorphic rocks is formed from the alteration of mica minerals?","Gneiss","Schist","Phyllite","Quartzite","Schist","Schist is a type of metamorphic rock that forms from the alteration of mica minerals, which are common in igneous and sedimentary rocks.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-primary-process-66f64d29","Science G9: Explaining the characteristics of different rocks - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-5 Explaining the characteristics of different rocks, their classification methods, and the cycle of their change from one type to another.","2. Comparing the types of metamorphic rocks to determine the similarities and differences in their characteristics.","What is the primary process that transforms igneous rocks into metamorphic rocks?","The rocks are subjected to high temperatures and pressures over a long period of time.","The rocks are exposed to water and wind erosion.","The rocks are melted and reformed into new minerals.","The rocks are affected by chemical reactions with surrounding rocks.","The rocks are subjected to high temperatures and pressures over a long period of time.","Metamorphic rocks can form from the alteration of igneous rocks, which are subjected to high temperatures and pressures, causing changes in their mineral composition and structure.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-primary-characteristic-256b2fcf","Science G9: Explaining the characteristics of different rocks - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-5 Explaining the characteristics of different rocks, their classification methods, and the cycle of their change from one type to another.","2. Comparing the types of metamorphic rocks to determine the similarities and differences in their characteristics.","What is the primary characteristic that distinguishes gneiss from other metamorphic rocks?","Its high density","Its high hardness","Its banded or foliated structure","Its high calcium content","Its banded or foliated structure","Gneiss is a type of metamorphic rock that is characterized by its banded or foliated structure, which is formed from the alteration of igneous or sedimentary rocks.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-of-metamorphic-rocks-2cbe9630","Science G9: Explaining the characteristics of different rocks - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-5 Explaining the characteristics of different rocks, their classification methods, and the cycle of their change from one type to another.","2. Comparing the types of metamorphic rocks to determine the similarities and differences in their characteristics.","Which of the following metamorphic rocks is formed from the alteration of limestone?","Marble","Slate","Gneiss","Schist","Marble","Marble is a type of metamorphic rock that forms from the alteration of limestone, which is a type of sedimentary rock.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-primary-process-2a837d25","Science G9: Explaining the characteristics of different rocks - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-5 Explaining the characteristics of different rocks, their classification methods, and the cycle of their change from one type to another.","2. Comparing the types of metamorphic rocks to determine the similarities and differences in their characteristics.","What is the primary process that transforms sedimentary rocks into foliated metamorphic rocks?","The rocks are subjected to high temperatures and pressures over a long period of time.","The rocks are exposed to water and wind erosion.","The rocks are melted and reformed into new minerals.","The rocks are affected by chemical reactions with surrounding rocks.","The rocks are subjected to high temperatures and pressures over a long period of time.","Foliated metamorphic rocks, such as gneiss and schist, form from the alteration of sedimentary rocks, which are subjected to high temperatures and pressures, causing changes in their mineral composition and structure.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-process-of-73af5cbf","Science G9: Explaining the characteristics of different rocks - Set 1 T1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-5 Explaining the characteristics of different rocks, their classification methods, and the cycle of their change from one type to another.","4. Describes the rock cycle, the transformation of rocks from one type to another, and the processes and changes associated with it.","What is the process of rock erosion?","The process of rock formation","The process of rock weathering","The process of rock transformation","The process of rock removal","The process of rock removal","The process of rock erosion is the removal of rocks from one location to another through various geological processes such as water flow and wind action.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-difference-mechanical-23c1159e","Science G9: Explaining the characteristics of different rocks - Set 1 T1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-5 Explaining the characteristics of different rocks, their classification methods, and the cycle of their change from one type to another.","2. Comparing the types of metamorphic rocks to determine the similarities and differences in their characteristics.","What is the difference between mechanical and chemical weathering?","Mechanical weathering involves the breakdown of rocks through physical forces, while chemical weathering involves the breakdown of rocks through chemical reactions","Mechanical weathering involves the breakdown of rocks through chemical reactions, while chemical weathering involves the breakdown of rocks through physical forces","Mechanical weathering involves the breakdown of rocks through heat and cold, while chemical weathering involves the breakdown of rocks through water and ice","Mechanical weathering involves the breakdown of rocks through water and ice, while chemical weathering involves the breakdown of rocks through heat and cold","Mechanical weathering involves the breakdown of rocks through physical forces, while chemical weathering involves the breakdown of rocks through chemical reactions","Mechanical weathering involves the breakdown of rocks through physical forces, while chemical weathering involves the breakdown of rocks through chemical reactions.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-process-of-1d679ea3","Science G9: Explaining the characteristics of different rocks - Set 1 T1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-5 Explaining the characteristics of different rocks, their classification methods, and the cycle of their change from one type to another.","4. Describes the rock cycle, the transformation of rocks from one type to another, and the processes and changes associated with it.","What is the process of rock weathering?","The process of rock formation","The process of rock erosion","The process of rock transformation","The process of rock breakdown","The process of rock breakdown","The process of rock weathering is the breakdown of rocks into smaller particles through various geological processes such as mechanical weathering and chemical weathering.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-difference-sedimentary-0559eca6","Science G9: Explaining the characteristics of different rocks - Set 1 T1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-5 Explaining the characteristics of different rocks, their classification methods, and the cycle of their change from one type to another.","3. Explaining the characteristics of sedimentary rocks, the method of their formation, and the best way to classify them.","What is the difference between sedimentary and igneous rocks?","Sedimentary rocks are formed from the cooling of magma, while igneous rocks are formed from the compression of sediments","Sedimentary rocks are formed from the compression of sediments, while igneous rocks are formed from the cooling of magma","Sedimentary rocks are formed from the erosion of existing rocks, while igneous rocks are formed from the cooling of magma","Sedimentary rocks are formed from the erosion of existing rocks, while igneous rocks are formed from the compression of sediments","Sedimentary rocks are formed from the compression of sediments, while igneous rocks are formed from the cooling of magma","Sedimentary rocks are formed from the compression of sediments, while igneous rocks are formed from the cooling of magma.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-rock-cycle-081e0420","Science G9: Explaining the characteristics of different rocks - Set 1 T1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-5 Explaining the characteristics of different rocks, their classification methods, and the cycle of their change from one type to another.","4. Describes the rock cycle, the transformation of rocks from one type to another, and the processes and changes associated with it.","What is the rock cycle?","The process of rock formation","The process of rock weathering","The process of rock erosion","The process of rock transformation","The process of rock transformation","The rock cycle is the process of rock transformation, which involves the transformation of rocks from one type to another through various geological processes.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-process-of-5a2a49ee","Science G9: Explaining the characteristics of different rocks - Set 1 T1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-5 Explaining the characteristics of different rocks, their classification methods, and the cycle of their change from one type to another.","4. Describes the rock cycle, the transformation of rocks from one type to another, and the processes and changes associated with it.","What is the process of rock formation called?","Weathering and erosion","Plate tectonics","Rock cycle","Rock formation","Rock cycle","The process of rock formation is called the rock cycle, which involves the transformation of rocks from one type to another through various geological processes.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-a-reverse-837540d7","Science G9: Explaining the characteristics of different rocks - Set 1 T1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-6 Explaining the causes of stresses affecting the rocks that make up the Earth’s interior, and describing the resulting effects.","2. Defining earthquakes, seismic waves and their types, and defining the epicenter of the earthquake and distinguishing each of them through drawing.","What is a reverse fault?","A type of rock that forms from the cooling of magma","A type of fault where the Earth's crust is pulled apart","A type of fault where the Earth's crust is compressed","A type of fault where the Earth's crust is broken and pushed up","A type of fault where the Earth's crust is broken and pushed up","A reverse fault is a type of fault where the Earth's crust is broken and pushed up, resulting in an upward displacement of the Earth's surface.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-a-lateral-4c282404","Science G9: Explaining the characteristics of different rocks - Set 1 T1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-5 Explaining the characteristics of different rocks, their classification methods, and the cycle of their change from one type to another.","1. Defining the concept of crack, listing the types of cracks (normal, reverse, lateral, or slip), and distinguishing each type by drawing.","What is a lateral fault?","A type of rock that forms from the cooling of magma","A type of fault where the Earth's crust is pulled apart","A type of fault where the Earth's crust is broken and dropped down","A type of fault where the Earth's crust is broken and moved sideways","A type of fault where the Earth's crust is broken and moved sideways","A lateral fault is a type of fault where the Earth's crust is broken and moved sideways, resulting in a horizontal displacement of the Earth's surface.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-a-normal-b4a956c7","Science G9: Explaining the characteristics of different rocks - Set 1 T1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-5 Explaining the characteristics of different rocks, their classification methods, and the cycle of their change from one type to another.","1. Defining the concept of crack, listing the types of cracks (normal, reverse, lateral, or slip), and distinguishing each type by drawing.","What is a normal fault?","A type of rock that forms from the cooling of magma","A type of fault where the Earth's crust is pulled apart","A type of fault where the Earth's crust is compressed","A type of fault where the Earth's crust is broken and dropped down","A type of fault where the Earth's crust is broken and dropped down","A normal fault is a type of fault where the Earth's crust is broken and dropped down, resulting in a downward displacement of the Earth's surface.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-a-crack-fb8330c8","Science G9: Explaining the characteristics of different rocks - Set 1 T1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-5 Explaining the characteristics of different rocks, their classification methods, and the cycle of their change from one type to another.","3. Explaining the characteristics of sedimentary rocks, the method of their formation, and the best way to classify them.","What is a crack in a rock?","A line or fissure in a rock's surface","A type of mineral deposit","A process of rock formation","A type of rock texture","A line or fissure in a rock's surface","A crack in a rock is a line or fissure in its surface, which can be caused by various factors such as weathering or tectonic activity.","/image-source/sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-a-crack-fb8330c8.png","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-primary-method-b7fc8abf","Science G9: Explaining the characteristics of different rocks - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-5 Explaining the characteristics of different rocks, their classification methods, and the cycle of their change from one type to another.","3. Explaining the characteristics of sedimentary rocks, the method of their formation, and the best way to classify them.","What is the primary method of sedimentary rock formation?","Weathering and erosion of existing rocks","Magma cooling and solidifying","Pressure and heat from the Earth's crust","Volcanic eruptions and lava cooling","Weathering and erosion of existing rocks","Sedimentary rocks are formed through the weathering and erosion of existing rocks, which are then compacted and cemented together.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-how-do-sedimentary-d82505fc","Science G9: Explaining the characteristics of different rocks - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-5 Explaining the characteristics of different rocks, their classification methods, and the cycle of their change from one type to another.","3. Explaining the characteristics of sedimentary rocks, the method of their formation, and the best way to classify them.","How do sedimentary rocks contribute to the formation of new rocks?","Through the process of weathering and erosion","Through the process of plate tectonics","Through the process of metamorphism","Through the process of sedimentation","Through the process of sedimentation","Sedimentary rocks contribute to the formation of new rocks through the process of sedimentation, where existing rocks are broken down and reformed into new sedimentary rocks.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-of-is-a-df9ae754","Science G9: Explaining the characteristics of different rocks - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-5 Explaining the characteristics of different rocks, their classification methods, and the cycle of their change from one type to another.","3. Explaining the characteristics of sedimentary rocks, the method of their formation, and the best way to classify them.","Which of the following is a characteristic of clastic sedimentary rocks?","They are formed from the remains of plants and animals","They are composed of minerals that are not found in igneous rocks","They are formed from the cementation of sedimentary particles","They are always soft and porous","They are formed from the cementation of sedimentary particles","Clastic sedimentary rocks are formed from the cementation of sedimentary particles, such as sand and silt.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-process-called-b0c7a98a","Science G9: Explaining the characteristics of different rocks - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-5 Explaining the characteristics of different rocks, their classification methods, and the cycle of their change from one type to another.","3. Explaining the characteristics of sedimentary rocks, the method of their formation, and the best way to classify them.","What is the process called when sedimentary particles are cemented together?","Lithification","Compaction","Weathering","Erosion","Lithification","Lithification is the process of cementing sedimentary particles together to form a new sedimentary rock.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-how-do-sedimentary-fe3e7b8c","Science G9: Explaining the characteristics of different rocks - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-5 Explaining the characteristics of different rocks, their classification methods, and the cycle of their change from one type to another.","2. Comparing the types of metamorphic rocks to determine the similarities and differences in their characteristics.","How do sedimentary rocks differ from igneous rocks?","Sedimentary rocks are formed from the cooling and solidification of magma","Sedimentary rocks are composed of minerals that are not found in igneous rocks","Sedimentary rocks are formed from the remains of plants and animals","Sedimentary rocks are always hard and dense","Sedimentary rocks are composed of minerals that are not found in igneous rocks","Sedimentary rocks are often composed of minerals that are not found in igneous rocks, such as calcium carbonate and silica.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-result-of-23766afb","Science G9: Explaining the characteristics of different rocks - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-5 Explaining the characteristics of different rocks, their classification methods, and the cycle of their change from one type to another.","3. Explaining the characteristics of sedimentary rocks, the method of their formation, and the best way to classify them.","What is the result of the compaction of sedimentary particles?","The formation of a new igneous rock","The creation of a new sedimentary rock","The increase in the size of the sedimentary particles","The decrease in the porosity of the sedimentary rock","The decrease in the porosity of the sedimentary rock","The compaction of sedimentary particles results in a decrease in the porosity of the sedimentary rock, making it more dense and solid.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-of-is-an-26c5957c","Science G9: Explaining the characteristics of different rocks - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-5 Explaining the characteristics of different rocks, their classification methods, and the cycle of their change from one type to another.","3. Explaining the characteristics of sedimentary rocks, the method of their formation, and the best way to classify them.","Which of the following is an example of a sedimentary rock?","Granite","Basalt","Sandstone","Quartzite","Sandstone","Sandstone is a type of sedimentary rock that is formed from sand-sized particles of minerals and rock fragments.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-process-called-b8dcd3b9","Science G9: Explaining the characteristics of different rocks - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-5 Explaining the characteristics of different rocks, their classification methods, and the cycle of their change from one type to another.","2. Comparing the types of metamorphic rocks to determine the similarities and differences in their characteristics.","What is the process called when existing rocks are broken down into smaller particles?","Weathering","Erosion","Deposition","Plate tectonics","Weathering","Weathering is the process of breaking down existing rocks into smaller particles through exposure to wind, water, and ice.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-how-are-sedimentary-e5607fd6","Science G9: Explaining the characteristics of different rocks - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-5 Explaining the characteristics of different rocks, their classification methods, and the cycle of their change from one type to another.","3. Explaining the characteristics of sedimentary rocks, the method of their formation, and the best way to classify them.","How are sedimentary rocks typically classified?","Based on their texture and composition","Based on their color and hardness","Based on their origin and formation process","Based on their shape and size","Based on their origin and formation process","Sedimentary rocks are typically classified based on their origin and formation process, such as clastic, chemical, or organic sedimentary rocks.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-of-is-a-41a7f99e","Science G9: Explaining the characteristics of different rocks - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-5 Explaining the characteristics of different rocks, their classification methods, and the cycle of their change from one type to another.","3. Explaining the characteristics of sedimentary rocks, the method of their formation, and the best way to classify them.","Which of the following is a characteristic of sedimentary rocks?","They are formed from the cooling and solidification of magma","They are composed of minerals that are not found in igneous rocks","They are formed from the remains of plants and animals","They are always hard and dense","They are formed from the remains of plants and animals","Sedimentary rocks are often composed of the remains of plants and animals, such as sand, silt, and clay.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-primary-process-b4ee17c2","Science G9: Explaining the characteristics of different rocks - Set 2 T1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-5 Explaining the characteristics of different rocks, their classification methods, and the cycle of their change from one type to another.","2. Comparing the types of metamorphic rocks to determine the similarities and differences in their characteristics.","What is the primary process that transforms sedimentary rocks into metamorphic rocks?","The rocks are subjected to high temperatures and pressures over a long period of time.","The rocks are exposed to water and wind erosion.","The rocks are melted and reformed into new minerals.","The rocks are affected by chemical reactions with the surrounding environment.","The rocks are subjected to high temperatures and pressures over a long period of time.","Metamorphic rocks are formed when sedimentary or igneous rocks are subjected to high temperatures and pressures, causing changes in their mineral composition and structure.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-type-of-metamorphic-905c26ca","Science G9: Explaining the characteristics of different rocks - Set 2 T1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-5 Explaining the characteristics of different rocks, their classification methods, and the cycle of their change from one type to another.","2. Comparing the types of metamorphic rocks to determine the similarities and differences in their characteristics.","Which type of metamorphic rock is formed from the alteration of shale?","Marble","Slate","Gneiss","Schist","Slate","Slate is a type of metamorphic rock that is formed from the alteration of shale, which is a fine-grained sedimentary rock.","/image-source/sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-type-of-metamorphic-905c26ca.png","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-characteristic-feature-9f91f227","Science G9: Explaining the characteristics of different rocks - Set 2 T1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-5 Explaining the characteristics of different rocks, their classification methods, and the cycle of their change from one type to another.","2. Comparing the types of metamorphic rocks to determine the similarities and differences in their characteristics.","What is the characteristic feature of gneiss that distinguishes it from other metamorphic rocks?","Its banded or foliated structure","Its coarse-grained texture","Its high melting point","Its ability to conduct electricity","Its banded or foliated structure","Gneiss is a type of metamorphic rock that is characterized by its banded or foliated structure, which is formed by the alignment of minerals during the metamorphic process.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-type-of-metamorphic-ce6d6f4c","Science G9: Explaining the characteristics of different rocks - Set 2 T1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-5 Explaining the characteristics of different rocks, their classification methods, and the cycle of their change from one type to another.","2. Comparing the types of metamorphic rocks to determine the similarities and differences in their characteristics.","Which type of metamorphic rock is formed from the alteration of limestone?","Marble","Slate","Gneiss","Schist","Marble","Marble is a type of metamorphic rock that is formed from the alteration of limestone, which is a sedimentary rock composed of calcium carbonate.","/image-source/sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-type-of-metamorphic-ce6d6f4c.png","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-result-of-bcc34b00","Science G9: Explaining the characteristics of different rocks - Set 2 T1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-5 Explaining the characteristics of different rocks, their classification methods, and the cycle of their change from one type to another.","2. Comparing the types of metamorphic rocks to determine the similarities and differences in their characteristics.","What is the result of the metamorphic process on the mineral composition of rocks?","The minerals are destroyed and new minerals are formed.","The minerals are rearranged and new minerals are formed.","The minerals remain unchanged and the rock's texture changes.","The minerals are melted and reformed into new minerals.","The minerals are rearranged and new minerals are formed.","During the metamorphic process, the minerals in the rock are rearranged and new minerals are formed, resulting in changes to the rock's texture and composition.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-type-of-metamorphic-6623ca33","Science G9: Explaining the characteristics of different rocks - Set 2 T1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-5 Explaining the characteristics of different rocks, their classification methods, and the cycle of their change from one type to another.","2. Comparing the types of metamorphic rocks to determine the similarities and differences in their characteristics.","Which type of metamorphic rock is characterized by its flaky or layered structure?","Marble","Slate","Gneiss","Schist","Slate","Slate is a type of metamorphic rock that is characterized by its flaky or layered structure, which is formed by the alignment of minerals during the metamorphic process.","/image-source/sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-type-of-metamorphic-6623ca33.png","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-primary-factor-ced7b8ab","Science G9: Explaining the characteristics of different rocks - Set 2 T1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-9 Describing the speed of chemical reactions and identifying the factors affecting them","2. Determining the factors affecting the rate of a chemical reaction (concentration of reactants, concentration of reactants, pressure, temperature, catalyst).","What is the primary factor that determines the type of metamorphic rock that is formed?","The temperature and pressure conditions","The type of minerals present in the rock","The rate of metamorphism","The presence of water","The temperature and pressure conditions","The primary factor that determines the type of metamorphic rock that is formed is the temperature and pressure conditions under which the rock is metamorphosed.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-type-of-metamorphic-03fd5a7e","Science G9: Explaining the characteristics of different rocks - Set 2 T1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-5 Explaining the characteristics of different rocks, their classification methods, and the cycle of their change from one type to another.","2. Comparing the types of metamorphic rocks to determine the similarities and differences in their characteristics.","Which type of metamorphic rock is formed from the alteration of granite?","Gneiss","Schist","Marble","Slate","Gneiss","Gneiss is a type of metamorphic rock that is formed from the alteration of granite, which is a coarse-grained igneous rock.","/image-source/sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-type-of-metamorphic-03fd5a7e.png","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-result-of-be58ccdd","Science G9: Explaining the characteristics of different rocks - Set 2 T1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-5 Explaining the characteristics of different rocks, their classification methods, and the cycle of their change from one type to another.","2. Comparing the types of metamorphic rocks to determine the similarities and differences in their characteristics.","What is the result of the metamorphic process on the texture of rocks?","The texture becomes more coarse-grained.","The texture becomes more fine-grained.","The texture remains unchanged.","The texture becomes more irregular.","The texture becomes more coarse-grained.","During the metamorphic process, the texture of the rock becomes more coarse-grained as the minerals are rearranged and new minerals are formed.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-type-of-metamorphic-5c10df8a","Science G9: Explaining the characteristics of different rocks - Set 2 T1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-5 Explaining the characteristics of different rocks, their classification methods, and the cycle of their change from one type to another.","2. Comparing the types of metamorphic rocks to determine the similarities and differences in their characteristics.","Which type of metamorphic rock is formed from the alteration of basalt?","Gneiss","Schist","Marble","Slate","Gneiss","Gneiss is a type of metamorphic rock that is formed from the alteration of basalt, which is a fine-grained igneous rock.","/image-source/sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-type-of-metamorphic-5c10df8a.png","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-term-movement-1290d61e","Science G9: Explaining the characteristics of different rocks - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-5 Explaining the characteristics of different rocks, their classification methods, and the cycle of their change from one type to another.","2. Comparing the types of metamorphic rocks to determine the similarities and differences in their characteristics.","What is the term for the movement of the Earth's crust that can cause rocks to change?","Weathering","Erosion","Plate tectonics","Metamorphism","Plate tectonics","Plate tectonics is the movement of the Earth's crust that can cause rocks to change.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-type-of-rock-9c1623ea","Science G9: Explaining the characteristics of different rocks - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-5 Explaining the characteristics of different rocks, their classification methods, and the cycle of their change from one type to another.","2. Comparing the types of metamorphic rocks to determine the similarities and differences in their characteristics.","What type of rock is formed from the alteration of existing rocks by heat and pressure, resulting in a new rock with a different mineral composition?","Igneous rock","Sedimentary rock","Metamorphic rock","Foliated rock","Metamorphic rock","Metamorphic rock is formed from the alteration of existing rocks by heat and pressure, resulting in a new rock with a different mineral composition.","/image-source/sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-type-of-rock-9c1623ea.png","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-term-movement-49dea169","Science G9: Explaining the characteristics of different rocks - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-5 Explaining the characteristics of different rocks, their classification methods, and the cycle of their change from one type to another.","2. Comparing the types of metamorphic rocks to determine the similarities and differences in their characteristics.","What is the term for the movement of rocks and sediments from one location to another?","Weathering","Erosion","Plate tectonics","Deposition","Erosion","Erosion is the movement of rocks and sediments from one location to another.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-type-of-rock-cf2cabcf","Science G9: Explaining the characteristics of different rocks - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-5 Explaining the characteristics of different rocks, their classification methods, and the cycle of their change from one type to another.","2. Comparing the types of metamorphic rocks to determine the similarities and differences in their characteristics.","What type of rock is formed from the cooling and solidification of magma or lava beneath the Earth's surface?","Sedimentary rock","Igneous rock","Metamorphic rock","Foliated rock","Igneous rock","Igneous rock is formed from the cooling and solidification of magma or lava beneath the Earth's surface.","/image-source/sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-type-of-rock-cf2cabcf.png","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-type-of-rock-86f1bb27","Science G9: Explaining the characteristics of different rocks - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-5 Explaining the characteristics of different rocks, their classification methods, and the cycle of their change from one type to another.","2. Comparing the types of metamorphic rocks to determine the similarities and differences in their characteristics.","What type of rock is formed from the accumulation and compression of sediments?","Igneous rock","Sedimentary rock","Metamorphic rock","Foliated rock","Sedimentary rock","Sedimentary rock is formed from the accumulation and compression of sediments.","/image-source/sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-type-of-rock-86f1bb27.png","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-type-of-rock-6dc9de5f","Science G9: Explaining the characteristics of different rocks - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-5 Explaining the characteristics of different rocks, their classification methods, and the cycle of their change from one type to another.","2. Comparing the types of metamorphic rocks to determine the similarities and differences in their characteristics.","What type of rock is formed from the alteration of existing rocks by heat and pressure?","Igneous rock","Sedimentary rock","Metamorphic rock","Foliated rock","Metamorphic rock","Metamorphic rock is formed from the alteration of existing rocks by heat and pressure.","/image-source/sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-type-of-rock-6dc9de5f.png","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-process-by-861c3cbb","Science G9: Explaining the characteristics of different rocks - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-5 Explaining the characteristics of different rocks, their classification methods, and the cycle of their change from one type to another.","4. Describes the rock cycle, the transformation of rocks from one type to another, and the processes and changes associated with it.","What is the process by which existing rocks are changed by heat and pressure?","Weathering","Erosion","Metamorphism","Plate tectonics","Metamorphism","Metamorphism is the process by which existing rocks are changed by heat and pressure.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-process-by-4c697c72","Science G9: Explaining the characteristics of different rocks - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-5 Explaining the characteristics of different rocks, their classification methods, and the cycle of their change from one type to another.","2. Comparing the types of metamorphic rocks to determine the similarities and differences in their characteristics.","What is the process by which rocks are broken down into smaller particles?","Weathering","Erosion","Plate tectonics","Metamorphism","Weathering","Weathering is the process by which rocks are broken down into smaller particles.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-type-of-rock-7cc45e13","Science G9: Explaining the characteristics of different rocks - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-5 Explaining the characteristics of different rocks, their classification methods, and the cycle of their change from one type to another.","2. Comparing the types of metamorphic rocks to determine the similarities and differences in their characteristics.","What type of rock is formed from the cooling and solidification of magma or lava?","Sedimentary rock","Igneous rock","Metamorphic rock","Foliated rock","Igneous rock","Igneous rock is formed from the cooling and solidification of magma or lava.","/image-source/sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-type-of-rock-7cc45e13.png","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-process-by-aa62a5a5","Science G9: Explaining the characteristics of different rocks - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-5 Explaining the characteristics of different rocks, their classification methods, and the cycle of their change from one type to another.","4. Describes the rock cycle, the transformation of rocks from one type to another, and the processes and changes associated with it.","What is the process by which rocks are transformed from one type to another?","The rock cycle is a series of changes that occur over millions of years.","The rock cycle is a process that only occurs on Earth's surface.","The rock cycle is a type of chemical reaction.","The rock cycle is a type of physical change.","The rock cycle is a series of changes that occur over millions of years.","The rock cycle is a series of changes that occur over millions of years, including the transformation of igneous, sedimentary, and metamorphic rocks.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-of-is-a-50e2bf90","Science G9: Explaining the characteristics of different rocks - Set 3 T1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-5 Explaining the characteristics of different rocks, their classification methods, and the cycle of their change from one type to another.","3. Explaining the characteristics of sedimentary rocks, the method of their formation, and the best way to classify them.","Which of the following is a process that can lead to the alteration of sedimentary rocks?","Weathering and erosion","Plate tectonics and mountain building","Volcanic activity and lava flows","Metamorphism","Metamorphism","Metamorphism is a process that can lead to the alteration of sedimentary rocks, resulting in the formation of metamorphic rocks.","/image-source/sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-of-is-a-50e2bf90.png","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-process-by-43843a4d","Science G9: Explaining the characteristics of different rocks - Set 3 T1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-5 Explaining the characteristics of different rocks, their classification methods, and the cycle of their change from one type to another.","3. Explaining the characteristics of sedimentary rocks, the method of their formation, and the best way to classify them.","What is the process by which sediments are compressed and cemented together to form sedimentary rocks?","Diagenesis","Metamorphism","Weathering","Erosion","Diagenesis","Diagenesis is the process by which sediments are compressed and cemented together to form sedimentary rocks.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-of-is-a-554b0ef5","Science G9: Explaining the characteristics of different rocks - Set 3 T1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-5 Explaining the characteristics of different rocks, their classification methods, and the cycle of their change from one type to another.","3. Explaining the characteristics of sedimentary rocks, the method of their formation, and the best way to classify them.","Which of the following is a characteristic of clastic sedimentary rocks?","They are formed from the cooling and solidification of magma or lava","They are composed primarily of minerals such as quartz and feldspar","They are formed through the compression of existing rocks under high pressure","They are composed of fragments of other rocks","They are composed of fragments of other rocks","Clastic sedimentary rocks are composed of fragments of other rocks, which can include sand, silt, and clay particles.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-of-is-a-6b9a570a","Science G9: Explaining the characteristics of different rocks - Set 3 T1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-5 Explaining the characteristics of different rocks, their classification methods, and the cycle of their change from one type to another.","3. Explaining the characteristics of sedimentary rocks, the method of their formation, and the best way to classify them.","Which of the following is a characteristic of sedimentary rocks?","They are formed from the cooling and solidification of magma or lava","They are composed primarily of minerals such as quartz and feldspar","They are formed through the compression of existing rocks under high pressure","They are often found in layers or beds","They are often found in layers or beds","Sedimentary rocks are often found in layers or beds, which is a result of the deposition of sediments over time.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-how-do-sedimentary-69fd32d8","Science G9: Explaining the characteristics of different rocks - Set 3 T1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-5 Explaining the characteristics of different rocks, their classification methods, and the cycle of their change from one type to another.","2. Comparing the types of metamorphic rocks to determine the similarities and differences in their characteristics.","How do sedimentary rocks differ from igneous rocks?","Sedimentary rocks are formed from the cooling and solidification of magma or lava","Sedimentary rocks are composed primarily of minerals such as quartz and feldspar","Sedimentary rocks are formed through the compression of existing rocks under high pressure","Sedimentary rocks are formed from the accumulation and compression of sediments","Sedimentary rocks are formed from the accumulation and compression of sediments","Sedimentary rocks are formed from the accumulation and compression of sediments, whereas igneous rocks are formed from the cooling and solidification of magma or lava.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-an-example-23832b12","Science G9: Explaining the characteristics of different rocks - Set 3 T1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-5 Explaining the characteristics of different rocks, their classification methods, and the cycle of their change from one type to another.","3. Explaining the characteristics of sedimentary rocks, the method of their formation, and the best way to classify them.","What is an example of a chemical sedimentary rock?","Shale","Limestone","Sandstone","Gypsum","Limestone","Limestone is a type of chemical sedimentary rock that is formed from the precipitation of calcium carbonate from a solution.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-primary-method-ba8aa5df","Science G9: Explaining the characteristics of different rocks - Set 3 T1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-5 Explaining the characteristics of different rocks, their classification methods, and the cycle of their change from one type to another.","3. Explaining the characteristics of sedimentary rocks, the method of their formation, and the best way to classify them.","What is the primary method of sedimentary rock formation?","The cooling and solidification of magma or lava","The compression of existing rocks under high pressure","The cementation of sediments through mineral precipitation","The melting of ice and snow","The cementation of sediments through mineral precipitation","Sedimentary rocks are formed through the cementation of sediments, which can include mineral particles, organic matter, and other materials, through the process of mineral precipitation.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-how-are-sedimentary-e6e645f6","Science G9: Explaining the characteristics of different rocks - Set 3 T1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-5 Explaining the characteristics of different rocks, their classification methods, and the cycle of their change from one type to another.","3. Explaining the characteristics of sedimentary rocks, the method of their formation, and the best way to classify them.","How are sedimentary rocks typically classified?","Based on their texture and composition","Based on their color and shape","Based on their origin and formation process","Based on their hardness and density","Based on their origin and formation process","Sedimentary rocks are typically classified based on their origin and formation process, which can include clastic, chemical, or organic sedimentary rocks.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-an-example-37d19d0e","Science G9: Explaining the characteristics of different rocks - Set 3 T1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-5 Explaining the characteristics of different rocks, their classification methods, and the cycle of their change from one type to another.","3. Explaining the characteristics of sedimentary rocks, the method of their formation, and the best way to classify them.","What is an example of a sedimentary rock?","Granite","Basalt","Shale","Gypsum","Shale","Shale is a type of sedimentary rock that is formed from the compression of clay particles.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-of-is-a-3fed3357","Science G9: Explaining the characteristics of different rocks - Set 3 T1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-5 Explaining the characteristics of different rocks, their classification methods, and the cycle of their change from one type to another.","3. Explaining the characteristics of sedimentary rocks, the method of their formation, and the best way to classify them.","Which of the following is a process that can lead to the formation of sedimentary rocks?","Weathering and erosion","Plate tectonics and mountain building","Volcanic activity and lava flows","Atmospheric circulation and ocean currents","Weathering and erosion","Weathering and erosion can lead to the formation of sediments, which can then be compressed and cemented together to form sedimentary rocks.","/image-source/sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-of-is-a-3fed3357.png","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-term-change-5532ca7a","Science G9: Explaining the characteristics of different rocks - Set 4","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-5 Explaining the characteristics of different rocks, their classification methods, and the cycle of their change from one type to another.","3. Explaining the characteristics of sedimentary rocks, the method of their formation, and the best way to classify them.","What is the term for the change in the mineral composition and structure of rocks under high pressure and temperature conditions?","Metamorphic transformation","Igneous formation","Sedimentary deposition","Weathering and erosion","Metamorphic transformation","Metamorphic transformation is the change in the mineral composition and structure of rocks under high pressure and temperature conditions.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-process-called-b03fc0fc","Science G9: Explaining the characteristics of different rocks - Set 4","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-5 Explaining the characteristics of different rocks, their classification methods, and the cycle of their change from one type to another.","4. Describes the rock cycle, the transformation of rocks from one type to another, and the processes and changes associated with it.","What is the process called when rocks are carried away by natural forces, such as wind or water, and deposited in a new location?","Weathering and erosion","Sedimentation and deposition","Metamorphism and transformation","Lithification and compaction","Sedimentation and deposition","Sedimentation and deposition are the processes by which rocks are carried away by natural forces, such as wind or water, and deposited in a new location.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-type-of-rock-f56065fa","Science G9: Explaining the characteristics of different rocks - Set 4","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-5 Explaining the characteristics of different rocks, their classification methods, and the cycle of their change from one type to another.","2. Comparing the types of metamorphic rocks to determine the similarities and differences in their characteristics.","What type of rock can be formed from the transformation of igneous or sedimentary rocks under high pressure and temperature conditions?","Igneous rock","Sedimentary rock","Foliated rock","Metamorphic rock","Metamorphic rock","Metamorphic rock can be formed from the transformation of igneous or sedimentary rocks under high pressure and temperature conditions.","/image-source/sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-type-of-rock-f56065fa.png","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-term-process-302882ea","Science G9: Explaining the characteristics of different rocks - Set 4","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-5 Explaining the characteristics of different rocks, their classification methods, and the cycle of their change from one type to another.","2. Comparing the types of metamorphic rocks to determine the similarities and differences in their characteristics.","What is the term for the process by which sediments are compressed and cemented together to form a new sedimentary rock?","Lithification and sedimentation","Weathering and erosion","Metamorphism and foliation","Sedimentation and compaction","Lithification and sedimentation","Lithification and sedimentation are the processes by which sediments are compressed and cemented together to form a new sedimentary rock.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-type-of-rock-06fe892d","Science G9: Explaining the characteristics of different rocks - Set 4","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-5 Explaining the characteristics of different rocks, their classification methods, and the cycle of their change from one type to another.","2. Comparing the types of metamorphic rocks to determine the similarities and differences in their characteristics.","What type of rock is formed when sediments, such as sand and silt, are compressed and cemented together to form a new rock?","Igneous rock","Metamorphic rock","Sedimentary rock","Foliated rock","Sedimentary rock","Sedimentary rock is formed when sediments, such as sand and silt, are compressed and cemented together to form a new rock.","/image-source/sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-type-of-rock-06fe892d.png","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-process-called-768cd7f3","Science G9: Explaining the characteristics of different rocks - Set 4","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-5 Explaining the characteristics of different rocks, their classification methods, and the cycle of their change from one type to another.","2. Comparing the types of metamorphic rocks to determine the similarities and differences in their characteristics.","What is the process called when rocks are broken down into smaller particles through exposure to wind, water, and ice?","Sedimentation","Lithification","Metamorphism","Weathering","Weathering","Weathering is the process by which rocks are broken down into smaller particles through exposure to wind, water, and ice.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-type-of-rock-f9b6e081","Science G9: Explaining the characteristics of different rocks - Set 4","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-5 Explaining the characteristics of different rocks, their classification methods, and the cycle of their change from one type to another.","2. Comparing the types of metamorphic rocks to determine the similarities and differences in their characteristics.","What type of rock is formed when existing rocks are subjected to high temperatures and pressures, causing changes in their mineral composition and structure?","Sedimentary rock","Igneous rock","Foliated rock","Metamorphic rock","Metamorphic rock","Metamorphic rock is formed when existing rocks are subjected to high temperatures and pressures, causing changes in their mineral composition and structure.","/image-source/sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-type-of-rock-f9b6e081.png","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-term-continuous-848b816b","Science G9: Explaining the characteristics of different rocks - Set 4","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-5 Explaining the characteristics of different rocks, their classification methods, and the cycle of their change from one type to another.","2. Comparing the types of metamorphic rocks to determine the similarities and differences in their characteristics.","What is the term for the continuous process by which rocks are formed, transformed, and destroyed, resulting in the creation of new rocks?","Rock erosion","Rock sedimentation","Rock metamorphism","Rock cycle","Rock cycle","The rock cycle is the continuous process by which rocks are formed, transformed, and destroyed, resulting in the creation of new rocks.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-process-called-4c6805d0","Science G9: Explaining the characteristics of different rocks - Set 4","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-5 Explaining the characteristics of different rocks, their classification methods, and the cycle of their change from one type to another.","2. Comparing the types of metamorphic rocks to determine the similarities and differences in their characteristics.","What is the process called when magma from the Earth's interior cools and solidifies to form a new type of rock?","Weathering and erosion","Sedimentation and lithification","Igneous formation","Metamorphic transformation","Igneous formation","Igneous formation is the process by which magma from the Earth's interior cools and solidifies to form a new type of rock, known as igneous rock.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-type-of-rock-caa9b83c","Science G9: Explaining the characteristics of different rocks - Set 4","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-5 Explaining the characteristics of different rocks, their classification methods, and the cycle of their change from one type to another.","2. Comparing the types of metamorphic rocks to determine the similarities and differences in their characteristics.","What type of rock is formed when magma cools and solidifies on the Earth's surface, resulting in rocks with a glassy or fine-grained texture?","Intrusive igneous rock","Extrusive igneous rock","Sedimentary rock","Metamorphic rock","Extrusive igneous rock","Extrusive igneous rock is formed when magma cools and solidifies on the Earth's surface, resulting in rocks with a glassy or fine-grained texture.","/image-source/sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-type-of-rock-caa9b83c.png","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-purpose-of-fc841f58","Science G9: Explaining the concept of electric current and methods of - Set 4","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-3-1 Explaining the concept of electric current and methods of generating it in electrical circuits and its relationship to voltage and electrical resistance, and distinguishing between direct and alternating currents.","2. Describing the methods of generating electric current in electrical circuits","What is the purpose of a transformer in an electrical circuit?","To increase voltage","To decrease voltage","To increase current","To decrease current","To increase voltage","The purpose of a transformer is to increase or decrease voltage in an electrical circuit, allowing for efficient transmission of power over long distances.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-type-of-electric-f7c7ccd5","Science G9: Explaining the concept of electric current and methods of - Set 4","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-3-1 Explaining the concept of electric current and methods of generating it in electrical circuits and its relationship to voltage and electrical resistance, and distinguishing between direct and alternating currents.","2. Describing the methods of generating electric current in electrical circuits","What type of electric current flows in one direction only?","Electric current that flows in both directions","Electric current that flows in one direction only","Electric current that flows randomly","Electric current that flows in a circular motion","Electric current that flows in one direction only","Direct current flows in one direction only, whereas alternating current changes direction periodically.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-main-advantage-169f065f","Science G9: Explaining the concept of electric current and methods of - Set 4","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-3-1 Explaining the concept of electric current and methods of generating it in electrical circuits and its relationship to voltage and electrical resistance, and distinguishing between direct and alternating currents.","2. Describing the methods of generating electric current in electrical circuits","What is the main advantage of alternating current over direct current?","Alternating current flows faster than direct current","Alternating current is more expensive than direct current","Alternating current is more efficient than direct current","Alternating current is less efficient than direct current","Alternating current is more efficient than direct current","The main advantage of alternating current over direct current is its efficiency, as it can be transmitted over long distances with minimal loss of power.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-type-of-electric-bea35391","Science G9: Explaining the concept of electric current and methods of - Set 4","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-3-1 Explaining the concept of electric current and methods of generating it in electrical circuits and its relationship to voltage and electrical resistance, and distinguishing between direct and alternating currents.","2. Describing the methods of generating electric current in electrical circuits","What type of electric current is used in electronic devices?","Direct current","Alternating current","Pulsating current","Variable current","Direct current","Electronic devices typically use direct current, which flows in one direction through a circuit.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-primary-source-f420ef7e","Science G9: Explaining the concept of electric current and methods of - Set 4","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-3-1 Explaining the concept of electric current and methods of generating it in electrical circuits and its relationship to voltage and electrical resistance, and distinguishing between direct and alternating currents.","2. Describing the methods of generating electric current in electrical circuits","What is the primary source of alternating current?","Generators","Solar panels","Wind turbines","Hydroelectric power plants","Generators","Generators are the primary source of alternating current, as they produce a changing flow of electrons in both directions.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-main-difference-d3273b23","Science G9: Explaining the concept of electric current and methods of - Set 4","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-3-1 Explaining the concept of electric current and methods of generating it in electrical circuits and its relationship to voltage and electrical resistance, and distinguishing between direct and alternating currents.","2. Describing the methods of generating electric current in electrical circuits","What is the main difference between direct and alternating current?","Direct current flows faster than alternating current","Alternating current flows in both directions","Direct current flows in one direction only","Alternating current is more expensive than direct current","Direct current flows in one direction only","The main difference between direct and alternating current is that direct current flows in one direction only, whereas alternating current changes direction periodically.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-type-of-electric-dd0f0935","Science G9: Explaining the concept of electric current and methods of - Set 4","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-3-1 Explaining the concept of electric current and methods of generating it in electrical circuits and its relationship to voltage and electrical resistance, and distinguishing between direct and alternating currents.","2. Describing the methods of generating electric current in electrical circuits","What type of electric current is commonly used in households?","Direct current","Alternating current","Pulsating current","Variable current","Alternating current","Alternating current is commonly used in households due to its efficiency and ease of transmission over long distances.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-primary-source-0bec853e","Science G9: Explaining the concept of electric current and methods of - Set 4","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-3-1 Explaining the concept of electric current and methods of generating it in electrical circuits and its relationship to voltage and electrical resistance, and distinguishing between direct and alternating currents.","2. Describing the methods of generating electric current in electrical circuits","What is the primary source of direct current?","Batteries","Generators","Solar panels","Wind turbines","Batteries","Batteries are the primary source of direct current, as they produce a steady flow of electrons in one direction.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-type-of-electric-ce952265","Science G9: Explaining the concept of electric current and methods of - Set 4","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-3-1 Explaining the concept of electric current and methods of generating it in electrical circuits and its relationship to voltage and electrical resistance, and distinguishing between direct and alternating currents.","2. Describing the methods of generating electric current in electrical circuits","What type of electric current is used in power transmission lines?","Direct current","Alternating current","Pulsating current","Variable current","Alternating current","Alternating current is used in power transmission lines due to its efficiency and ease of transmission over long distances.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-relationship-voltage-7ae94789","Science G9: Explaining the concept of electric current and methods of - Set 4","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-3-1 Explaining the concept of electric current and methods of generating it in electrical circuits and its relationship to voltage and electrical resistance, and distinguishing between direct and alternating currents.","3. Determines the relationship between electric current, electric voltage, and electrical resistance, and calculates the value of each using Ohm’s law mathematically","What is the relationship between voltage and electric current?","Voltage increases electric current","Voltage decreases electric current","Voltage has no effect on electric current","Voltage and electric current are unrelated","Voltage increases electric current","For a circuit with constant resistance, Ohm's law states that I = V/R, so increasing the voltage increases the electric current.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-direction-of-63deec46","Science G9: Explaining the concept of electric current - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-3-1 Explaining the concept of electric current and methods of generating it in electrical circuits and its relationship to voltage and electrical resistance, and distinguishing between direct and alternating currents.","1. Explaining the formation of the electric force between charges theoretically and graphically, and describing its relationship to the electric field.","What is the direction of the electric field around a negative charge?","The electric field points towards the negative charge.","The electric field points away from the negative charge.","The electric field is perpendicular to the negative charge.","The electric field is parallel to the negative charge.","The electric field points towards the negative charge.","The electric field points towards a negative charge, as described by the direction of the force on a test charge.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-direction-of-87b4f145","Science G9: Explaining the concept of electric current - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-3-1 Explaining the concept of electric current and methods of generating it in electrical circuits and its relationship to voltage and electrical resistance, and distinguishing between direct and alternating currents.","1. Explaining the formation of the electric force between charges theoretically and graphically, and describing its relationship to the electric field.","What is the direction of the electric field around a positive charge?","The electric field points towards the positive charge.","The electric field points away from the positive charge.","The electric field is perpendicular to the positive charge.","The electric field is parallel to the positive charge.","The electric field points away from the positive charge.","The electric field points away from a positive charge, as described by the direction of the force on a test charge.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-relationship-voltage-7efb39a0","Science G9: Explaining the concept of electric current - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-3-1 Explaining the concept of electric current and methods of generating it in electrical circuits and its relationship to voltage and electrical resistance, and distinguishing between direct and alternating currents.","3. Determines the relationship between electric current, electric voltage, and electrical resistance, and calculates the value of each using Ohm’s law mathematically","What is the relationship between voltage and current in an electrical circuit?","The voltage is directly proportional to the current.","The voltage is inversely proportional to the current.","The voltage is equal to the current.","The voltage is independent of the current.","The voltage is inversely proportional to the current.","The voltage is inversely proportional to the current, as described by Ohm's Law.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-force-attracts-721b29b7","Science G9: Explaining the concept of electric current - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-3-1 Explaining the concept of electric current and methods of generating it in electrical circuits and its relationship to voltage and electrical resistance, and distinguishing between direct and alternating currents.","1. Explaining the formation of the electric force between charges theoretically and graphically, and describing its relationship to the electric field.","What is the force that attracts or repels charged particles?","The electric force is a type of gravitational force.","The electric force is a type of magnetic force.","The electric force is a type of force that arises between charged particles.","The electric force is a type of force that arises between magnets.","The electric force is a type of force that arises between charged particles.","The electric force is a type of force that arises between charged particles, such as protons and electrons.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-purpose-of-acadb1fc","Science G9: Explaining the concept of electric current - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-3-1 Explaining the concept of electric current and methods of generating it in electrical circuits and its relationship to voltage and electrical resistance, and distinguishing between direct and alternating currents.","3. Determines the relationship between electric current, electric voltage, and electrical resistance, and calculates the value of each using Ohm’s law mathematically","What is the purpose of a resistor in an electrical circuit?","To increase the voltage in the circuit.","To decrease the voltage in the circuit.","To increase the current in the circuit.","Voltage is directly proportional to current","Voltage is directly proportional to current","According to Ohm's Law, V = I * R, meaning voltage is directly proportional to current for a constant resistance.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-electric-field-d0a2f387","Science G9: Explaining the concept of electric current - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-3-1 Explaining the concept of electric current and methods of generating it in electrical circuits and its relationship to voltage and electrical resistance, and distinguishing between direct and alternating currents.","1. Explaining the formation of the electric force between charges theoretically and graphically, and describing its relationship to the electric field.","What is the electric field?","The electric field is a measure of the distance between charged particles.","The electric field is a measure of the force between charged particles.","The electric field is a region around a charged particle where the force can be detected.","The electric field is a measure of the temperature of a charged particle.","The electric field is a region around a charged particle where the force can be detected.","The electric field is a region around a charged particle where the force can be detected, and it is a measure of the strength of the electric force.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-relationship-electric-e3a267a6","Science G9: Explaining the concept of electric current - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-3-1 Explaining the concept of electric current and methods of generating it in electrical circuits and its relationship to voltage and electrical resistance, and distinguishing between direct and alternating currents.","1. Explaining the formation of the electric force between charges theoretically and graphically, and describing its relationship to the electric field.","What is the relationship between electric force and electric field?","The electric force is equal to the electric field.","The electric force is proportional to the electric field.","The electric force is inversely proportional to the electric field.","The electric force is independent of the electric field.","The electric force is proportional to the electric field.","The electric force is proportional to the electric field, as described by Coulomb's Law.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-difference-a-e3a9eea3","Science G9: Explaining the concept of electric current - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-3-1 Explaining the concept of electric current and methods of generating it in electrical circuits and its relationship to voltage and electrical resistance, and distinguishing between direct and alternating currents.","2. Describing the methods of generating electric current in electrical circuits","What is the difference between a series circuit and a parallel circuit?","A series circuit has multiple paths for the current to flow, while a parallel circuit has only one path for the current to flow.","A series circuit has only one path for the current to flow, while a parallel circuit has multiple paths for the current to flow.","A series circuit is used for high-voltage applications, while a parallel circuit is used for low-voltage applications.","A series circuit is used for low-voltage applications, while a parallel circuit is used for high-voltage applications.","A series circuit has only one path for the current to flow, while a parallel circuit has multiple paths for the current to flow.","A series circuit has only one path for the current to flow, while a parallel circuit has multiple paths for the current to flow.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-purpose-of-c636efb8","Science G9: Explaining the concept of electric current - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-3-1 Explaining the concept of electric current and methods of generating it in electrical circuits and its relationship to voltage and electrical resistance, and distinguishing between direct and alternating currents.","2. Describing the methods of generating electric current in electrical circuits","What is the purpose of a switch in an electrical circuit?","To increase the voltage in the circuit.","To decrease the voltage in the circuit.","To increase the current in the circuit.","To turn the circuit on or off.","To turn the circuit on or off.","A switch is used to turn the circuit on or off.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-difference-a-df6bd8d9","Science G9: Explaining the concept of electric current - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-3-1 Explaining the concept of electric current and methods of generating it in electrical circuits and its relationship to voltage and electrical resistance, and distinguishing between direct and alternating currents.","2. Describing the methods of generating electric current in electrical circuits","What is the difference between a direct current and an alternating current?","A direct current flows in one direction only, while an alternating current flows in both directions.","A direct current flows in both directions, while an alternating current flows in one direction only.","A direct current is a type of current that flows through a circuit, while an alternating current is a type of current that flows through a wire.","A direct current is a type of current that flows through a wire, while an alternating current is a type of current that flows through a circuit.","A direct current flows in one direction only, while an alternating current flows in both directions.","A direct current flows in one direction only, while an alternating current flows in both directions.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-process-called-81e60832","Science G9: Explaining the concept of electric current - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-3-1 Explaining the concept of electric current and methods of generating it in electrical circuits and its relationship to voltage and electrical resistance, and distinguishing between direct and alternating currents.","1. Explaining the formation of the electric force between charges theoretically and graphically, and describing its relationship to the electric field.","What is the process called when a coil of wire rotates within a magnetic field to produce an electric current?","Electromagnetic induction","Electrical conduction","Electrical resistance","Electrical capacitance","Electromagnetic induction","When a coil of wire rotates within a magnetic field, it induces an electric current through the process of electromagnetic induction.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-device-called-7cdc6cb0","Science G9: Explaining the concept of electric current - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-3-4 Interpreting the relationship between magnets and electric current and their role in designing devices that convert electrical energy into mechanical energy and vice versa.","2. listing its applied uses, and showing how an electric current generates a magnetic field.","What is the device called that converts electrical energy into mechanical energy?","Generator","Motor","Transformer","Battery","Motor","A motor is a device that converts electrical energy into mechanical energy, often used in applications like fans and pumps.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-process-called-4285bbc5","Science G9: Explaining the concept of electric current - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-3-1 Explaining the concept of electric current and methods of generating it in electrical circuits and its relationship to voltage and electrical resistance, and distinguishing between direct and alternating currents.","1. Explaining the formation of the electric force between charges theoretically and graphically, and describing its relationship to the electric field.","What is the process called when a coil of wire is stationary and a magnetic field is applied to produce a magnetic field?","Electromagnetic induction","Electrical conduction","Electrical resistance","Electromagnetic attraction","Electromagnetic induction","When a coil of wire is stationary and a magnetic field is applied, it induces a magnetic field through the process of electromagnetic induction.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-device-called-649c573c","Science G9: Explaining the concept of electric current - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-3-1 Explaining the concept of electric current and methods of generating it in electrical circuits and its relationship to voltage and electrical resistance, and distinguishing between direct and alternating currents.","2. Describing the methods of generating electric current in electrical circuits","What is the device called that steps up or steps down the voltage of an electrical current?","Generator","Motor","Transformer","Battery","Transformer","A transformer is a device that steps up or steps down the voltage of an electrical current, often used in power transmission and distribution.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-process-called-d23cf93f","Science G9: Explaining the concept of electric current - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-3-1 Explaining the concept of electric current and methods of generating it in electrical circuits and its relationship to voltage and electrical resistance, and distinguishing between direct and alternating currents.","1. Explaining the formation of the electric force between charges theoretically and graphically, and describing its relationship to the electric field.","What is the process called when a coil of wire is stationary and a magnetic field is applied to produce an electric current?","Electromagnetic induction","Electrical conduction","Electrical resistance","Electrical capacitance","Electromagnetic induction","When a coil of wire is stationary and a magnetic field is applied, it induces an electric current through the process of electromagnetic induction.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-device-called-6b8960cd","Science G9: Explaining the concept of electric current - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-3-4 Interpreting the relationship between magnets and electric current and their role in designing devices that convert electrical energy into mechanical energy and vice versa.","2. listing its applied uses, and showing how an electric current generates a magnetic field.","What is the device called that converts mechanical energy into electrical energy?","Generator","Motor","Transformer","Battery","Generator","A generator is a device that converts mechanical energy into electrical energy, often used in power plants to produce electricity.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-type-of-current-7565e44f","Science G9: Explaining the concept of electric current - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-3-1 Explaining the concept of electric current and methods of generating it in electrical circuits and its relationship to voltage and electrical resistance, and distinguishing between direct and alternating currents.","2. Describing the methods of generating electric current in electrical circuits","What type of current flows in one direction only and does not change direction?","Direct current","Alternating current","Static current","Pulsating current","Direct current","Direct current flows in one direction only and does not change direction, making it suitable for applications like powering small devices.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-process-called-ce375b19","Science G9: Explaining the concept of electric current - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-3-1 Explaining the concept of electric current and methods of generating it in electrical circuits and its relationship to voltage and electrical resistance, and distinguishing between direct and alternating currents.","2. Describing the methods of generating electric current in electrical circuits","What is the process called when a magnet rotates within a coil of wire to produce an electric current?","Electromagnetic induction","Electrical conduction","Electrical resistance","Electrical capacitance","Electromagnetic induction","When a magnet rotates within a coil of wire, it induces an electric current through the process of electromagnetic induction.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-primary-method-10f470fe","Science G9: Explaining the concept of electric current - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-3-1 Explaining the concept of electric current and methods of generating it in electrical circuits and its relationship to voltage and electrical resistance, and distinguishing between direct and alternating currents.","2. Describing the methods of generating electric current in electrical circuits","What is the primary method of generating electric current in a household?","Using a battery to convert chemical energy into electrical energy","Using a solar panel to convert sunlight into electrical energy","Using a dynamo to convert mechanical energy into electrical energy","Using a nuclear reactor to generate electricity","Using a battery to convert chemical energy into electrical energy","Households primarily use batteries or the electrical grid to generate electric current, which is often produced by power plants using chemical energy from batteries or mechanical energy from dynamos.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-type-of-current-bb459b6e","Science G9: Explaining the concept of electric current - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-3-1 Explaining the concept of electric current and methods of generating it in electrical circuits and its relationship to voltage and electrical resistance, and distinguishing between direct and alternating currents.","2. Describing the methods of generating electric current in electrical circuits","What type of current is commonly used in households and businesses?","Direct current","Alternating current","Static current","Pulsating current","Alternating current","Alternating current is commonly used in households and businesses due to its efficiency and ease of transmission over long distances.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-relationship-electric-108d4434","Science G9: Explaining the concept of electric current - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-3-1 Explaining the concept of electric current and methods of generating it in electrical circuits and its relationship to voltage and electrical resistance, and distinguishing between direct and alternating currents.","3. Determines the relationship between electric current, electric voltage, and electrical resistance, and calculates the value of each using Ohm’s law mathematically","What is the relationship between electric current, voltage, and resistance in an electrical circuit?","Current increases as voltage decreases and resistance increases","Current decreases as voltage increases and resistance decreases","Current increases as voltage increases and resistance decreases","Current decreases as voltage decreases and resistance decreases","Current increases as voltage increases and resistance decreases","According to Ohm's law, current increases as voltage increases and resistance decreases, as the equation I = V/R shows.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-unit-of-07c72ee5","Science G9: Explaining the concept of electric current - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-3-1 Explaining the concept of electric current and methods of generating it in electrical circuits and its relationship to voltage and electrical resistance, and distinguishing between direct and alternating currents.","2. Describing the methods of generating electric current in electrical circuits","What is the unit of measurement for electric current?","Volts","Amperes","Ohms","Watts","Amperes","The unit of measurement for electric current is amperes, often abbreviated as amps.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-electric-current-9d19fd0b","Science G9: Explaining the concept of electric current - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-3-1 Explaining the concept of electric current and methods of generating it in electrical circuits and its relationship to voltage and electrical resistance, and distinguishing between direct and alternating currents.","1. Explaining the formation of the electric force between charges theoretically and graphically, and describing its relationship to the electric field.","What is electric current in an electrical circuit?","The amount of energy used by a device","The flow of electrons through a conductor","The force that opposes the flow of electrons","The energy transferred from one point to another","The flow of electrons through a conductor","Electric current is the flow of electrons through a conductor, such as a wire, and is measured in amperes.","/image-source/sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-electric-current-9d19fd0b.png","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-happens-to-current-14aa85fb","Science G9: Explaining the concept of electric current - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-3-1 Explaining the concept of electric current and methods of generating it in electrical circuits and its relationship to voltage and electrical resistance, and distinguishing between direct and alternating currents.","3. Determines the relationship between electric current, electric voltage, and electrical resistance, and calculates the value of each using Ohm’s law mathematically","What happens to the current in a circuit if the voltage is doubled and the resistance remains the same?","The current decreases by half","The current remains the same","The current increases by half","The current doubles","The current doubles","According to Ohm's law, if the voltage is doubled and the resistance remains the same, the current will also double, as I = V/R.","/image-source/sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-happens-to-current-14aa85fb.png","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-type-of-current-62fe9840","Science G9: Explaining the concept of electric current - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-3-1 Explaining the concept of electric current and methods of generating it in electrical circuits and its relationship to voltage and electrical resistance, and distinguishing between direct and alternating currents.","1. Explaining the formation of the electric force between charges theoretically and graphically, and describing its relationship to the electric field.","What type of current flows in one direction only?","Alternating current","Direct current","Electromotive force","Resistance current","Direct current","Direct current, or DC, flows in one direction only, from positive to negative, whereas alternating current, or AC, periodically reverses direction.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-formula-to-d7e69519","Science G9: Explaining the concept of electric current - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-3-1 Explaining the concept of electric current and methods of generating it in electrical circuits and its relationship to voltage and electrical resistance, and distinguishing between direct and alternating currents.","3. Determines the relationship between electric current, electric voltage, and electrical resistance, and calculates the value of each using Ohm’s law mathematically","What is the formula to calculate electric current using Ohm's law?","I = V x R","I = V / R","I = R / V","I = V + R","I = V / R","The formula to calculate electric current using Ohm's law is I = V/R, where I is the current in amperes, V is the voltage in volts, and R is the resistance in ohms.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-effect-of-44ce34d1","Science G9: Explaining the concept of electric current - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-3-1 Explaining the concept of electric current and methods of generating it in electrical circuits and its relationship to voltage and electrical resistance, and distinguishing between direct and alternating currents.","3. Determines the relationship between electric current, electric voltage, and electrical resistance, and calculates the value of each using Ohm’s law mathematically","What is the effect of increasing the resistance in a circuit on the electric current?","The current increases","The current remains the same","The current decreases","The current becomes zero","The current decreases","According to Ohm's law, if the resistance in a circuit increases, the electric current will decrease, as I = V/R.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-type-of-current-14c3bc0c","Science G9: Explaining the concept of electric current - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-3-1 Explaining the concept of electric current and methods of generating it in electrical circuits and its relationship to voltage and electrical resistance, and distinguishing between direct and alternating currents.","2. Describing the methods of generating electric current in electrical circuits","What type of current is commonly used in households and businesses?","Direct current","Alternating current","Static current","Pulsating current","Alternating current","Alternating current is commonly used in households and businesses because it can be transmitted efficiently over long distances and easily transformed to different voltages.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-relationship-voltage-6d6b8442","Science G9: Explaining the concept of electric current - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-3-1 Explaining the concept of electric current and methods of generating it in electrical circuits and its relationship to voltage and electrical resistance, and distinguishing between direct and alternating currents.","3. Determines the relationship between electric current, electric voltage, and electrical resistance, and calculates the value of each using Ohm’s law mathematically","What is the relationship between voltage, current, and resistance in a circuit, as described by Ohm's law?","V = I + R","V = I x R","V = I / R","V = R / I","V = I x R","Ohm's law states that voltage, current, and resistance are related by the equation V = I x R, where V is the voltage in volts, I is the current in amperes, and R is the resistance in ohms.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-happens-to-voltage-76a0c48f","Science G9: Explaining the concept of electric current - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-3-1 Explaining the concept of electric current and methods of generating it in electrical circuits and its relationship to voltage and electrical resistance, and distinguishing between direct and alternating currents.","3. Determines the relationship between electric current, electric voltage, and electrical resistance, and calculates the value of each using Ohm’s law mathematically","What happens to the voltage in a circuit if the current and resistance are doubled?","The voltage decreases by half","The voltage remains the same","The voltage doubles","The voltage quadruples","The voltage quadruples","According to Ohm's law, V = I x R. If both current and resistance are doubled, the new voltage is (2I)(2R) = 4IR, so the voltage quadruples.","/image-source/sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-happens-to-voltage-76a0c48f.png","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-term-amount-89520d2d","Science G9: Explaining the development of the atomic model throughout - Set 4","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-1 Explaining the development of the atomic model throughout history, and understanding the structure and components of the atom.","4. Comparing alpha and beta particles, explaining the changes that occur in the nucleus upon emission of each of them, and their uses in life, explaining the concept of decay rate (half-life), and calculating the half-life of some isotopes.","What is the term for the amount of time it takes for a radioactive substance to decay to one-quarter of its original amount?","Half-life is the amount of time it takes for a substance to decay to one-quarter of its original amount.","The decay rate is the amount of time it takes for a substance to decay to one-quarter of its original amount.","Radioactive half-life is the amount of time it takes for a substance to decay to one-quarter of its original amount.","The time it takes for a substance to decay to one-quarter of its original amount is called the quarter-life.","The decay rate is the amount of time it takes for a substance to decay to one-quarter of its original amount.","The decay rate is the amount of time it takes for a substance to decay to one-quarter of its original amount.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-term-amount-cc93770b","Science G9: Explaining the development of the atomic model throughout - Set 4","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-1 Explaining the development of the atomic model throughout history, and understanding the structure and components of the atom.","4. Comparing alpha and beta particles, explaining the changes that occur in the nucleus upon emission of each of them, and their uses in life, explaining the concept of decay rate (half-life), and calculating the half-life of some isotopes.","What is the term for the amount of time it takes for a radioactive substance to lose half of its radioactivity?","The decay rate is the amount of time it takes for a substance to lose half of its radioactivity.","Radioactive half-life is the amount of time it takes for a substance to lose half of its radioactivity.","Half-life is the amount of time it takes for a substance to lose half of its radioactivity.","The time it takes for a substance to lose half of its radioactivity is called the half-life.","Half-life is the amount of time it takes for a substance to lose half of its radioactivity.","Half-life is the amount of time it takes for a substance to lose half of its radioactivity.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-term-rate-7ab021fd","Science G9: Explaining the development of the atomic model throughout - Set 4","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-1 Explaining the development of the atomic model throughout history, and understanding the structure and components of the atom.","4. Comparing alpha and beta particles, explaining the changes that occur in the nucleus upon emission of each of them, and their uses in life, explaining the concept of decay rate (half-life), and calculating the half-life of some isotopes.","What is the term for the rate at which a radioactive substance decays?","Half-life is the time it takes for a substance to become stable.","Half-life is the time it takes for half of the original amount of a substance to decay.","Half-life is the time it takes for a substance to become radioactive.","Half-life is the time it takes for a substance to become non-radioactive.","Half-life is the time it takes for half of the original amount of a substance to decay.","Half-life is the time it takes for half of the original amount of a substance to decay.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-primary-difference-dbc03167","Science G9: Explaining the development of the atomic model throughout - Set 4","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-1 Explaining the development of the atomic model throughout history, and understanding the structure and components of the atom.","4. Comparing alpha and beta particles, explaining the changes that occur in the nucleus upon emission of each of them, and their uses in life, explaining the concept of decay rate (half-life), and calculating the half-life of some isotopes.","What is the primary difference between alpha and beta particles?","Alpha particles have a higher mass than beta particles.","Beta particles have a higher mass than alpha particles.","Alpha particles are positively charged, while beta particles are negatively charged.","Alpha particles are negatively charged, while beta particles are positively charged.","Alpha particles are positively charged, while beta particles are negatively charged.","Alpha particles are positively charged, while beta particles are negatively charged.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-type-of-radiation-ca2ed456","Science G9: Explaining the development of the atomic model throughout - Set 4","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-1 Explaining the development of the atomic model throughout history, and understanding the structure and components of the atom.","2. Illustrating the components of the atom's nucleus (protons and neutrons) and their properties, describing the movement of electrons (electronic cloud) around the nucleus, and determining the number of protons, neutrons and electrons in the atom of the elements based on their atomic numbers.","What type of radiation is emitted when a neutron is converted into a proton and an electron?","Alpha particles are high-energy electrons.","Beta particles are high-energy protons.","Beta particles are high-energy electrons.","Alpha particles are high-energy protons.","Beta particles are high-energy electrons.","Beta particles are high-energy electrons that are emitted when a neutron is converted into a proton and an electron in the nucleus.","/image-source/sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-type-of-radiation-ca2ed456.png","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-term-process-6dd313ba","Science G9: Explaining the development of the atomic model throughout - Set 4","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-1 Explaining the development of the atomic model throughout history, and understanding the structure and components of the atom.","4. Comparing alpha and beta particles, explaining the changes that occur in the nucleus upon emission of each of them, and their uses in life, explaining the concept of decay rate (half-life), and calculating the half-life of some isotopes.","What is the term for the process by which a nucleus emits a high-energy electron?","Beta decay is the process by which a nucleus emits a high-energy electron.","Alpha decay is the process by which a nucleus emits a high-energy electron.","Radioactive decay is the process by which a nucleus emits a high-energy electron.","Nuclear decay is the process by which a nucleus emits a high-energy electron.","Beta decay is the process by which a nucleus emits a high-energy electron.","Beta decay is the process by which a nucleus emits a high-energy electron.","","",""
