"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-main-difference-a3be5bbf","Science G9: Explaining the properties of liquids, comparing - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-4 Explaining the properties of liquids, comparing crystalline and amorphous solids, and describing the pattern of crystals in solids.","2. Comparing crystalline and non-crystalline solids according to the organization and arrangement of their atoms.","What is the main difference between a metal and a nonmetal?","Metals are typically soft and nonmetals are typically hard","Metals are typically good at conducting electricity and nonmetals are not","Metals are typically found in nature and nonmetals are man-made","Metals are typically liquid at room temperature and nonmetals are solid","Metals are typically good at conducting electricity and nonmetals are not","Metals are typically good at conducting electricity, while nonmetals are not.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-pattern-of-d02ff2f5","Science G9: Explaining the properties of liquids, comparing - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-4 Explaining the properties of liquids, comparing crystalline and amorphous solids, and describing the pattern of crystals in solids.","2. Comparing crystalline and non-crystalline solids according to the organization and arrangement of their atoms.","What is the pattern of crystals in solids called?","Crystal lattice","Molecular structure","Atomic arrangement","Particle pattern","Crystal lattice","The crystal lattice is the regular, repeating pattern of particles in a crystalline solid.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-main-difference-0a0c969d","Science G9: Explaining the properties of liquids, comparing - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-4 Explaining the properties of liquids, comparing crystalline and amorphous solids, and describing the pattern of crystals in solids.","2. Comparing crystalline and non-crystalline solids according to the organization and arrangement of their atoms.","What is the main difference between crystalline and amorphous solids?","Crystalline solids have a random arrangement of particles, while amorphous solids have a regular arrangement","Crystalline solids have a regular arrangement of particles, while amorphous solids have a random arrangement","Crystalline solids are found only in nature, while amorphous solids are man-made","Crystalline solids are always harder than amorphous solids","Crystalline solids have a regular arrangement of particles, while amorphous solids have a random arrangement","Crystalline solids have a regular, repeating pattern of particles, while amorphous solids have a random arrangement of particles.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-of-solids-is-3a238e84","Science G9: Explaining the properties of liquids, comparing - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-4 Explaining the properties of liquids, comparing crystalline and amorphous solids, and describing the pattern of crystals in solids.","2. Comparing crystalline and non-crystalline solids according to the organization and arrangement of their atoms.","Which of the following solids is an example of an amorphous solid?","Salt","Sugar","Glass","Diamond","Glass","Glass is an example of an amorphous solid, which means it does not have a regular, repeating pattern of particles.","","",""
