"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-primary-characteristic-55e83301","Science G9: Explaining the properties of liquids - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-4 Explaining the properties of liquids, comparing crystalline and amorphous solids, and describing the pattern of crystals in solids.","2. Comparing crystalline and non-crystalline solids according to the organization and arrangement of their atoms.","What is the primary characteristic of a crystalline solid?","Crystalline solids have a random arrangement of particles.","Crystalline solids have a fixed arrangement of particles.","Crystalline solids have a fixed shape and volume.","Crystalline solids are liquids at room temperature.","Crystalline solids have a fixed arrangement of particles.","Crystalline solids have a fixed arrangement of particles.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-who-is-credited-b9a57961","Science G9: Explaining the properties of liquids - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-4 Explaining the properties of liquids, comparing crystalline and amorphous solids, and describing the pattern of crystals in solids.","1. Explaining the contributions of scientists to the arrangement of the elements discovered in the periodic table and the history of its development leading to the modern periodic table.","Who is credited with the development of the modern periodic table?","Dmitri Mendeleev and John Newlands","John Newlands and Henry Moseley","Dmitri Mendeleev and Henry Moseley","Henry Moseley and Marie Curie","Dmitri Mendeleev and John Newlands","Dmitri Mendeleev and John Newlands are credited with the development of the modern periodic table.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-primary-characteristic-a1824418","Science G9: Explaining the properties of liquids - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-4 Explaining the properties of liquids, comparing crystalline and amorphous solids, and describing the pattern of crystals in solids.","2. Comparing crystalline and non-crystalline solids according to the organization and arrangement of their atoms.","What is the primary characteristic of an amorphous solid?","Amorphous solids have a fixed shape and volume.","Amorphous solids have a fixed arrangement of particles.","Amorphous solids have a random arrangement of particles.","Amorphous solids are solids at room temperature.","Amorphous solids have a random arrangement of particles.","Amorphous solids have a random arrangement of particles.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-primary-difference-c415683b","Science G9: Explaining the properties of liquids - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-4 Explaining the properties of liquids, comparing crystalline and amorphous solids, and describing the pattern of crystals in solids.","2. Comparing crystalline and non-crystalline solids according to the organization and arrangement of their atoms.","What is the primary difference between crystalline and amorphous solids?","Crystalline solids have a fixed shape and volume, while amorphous solids do not.","Crystalline solids have a random arrangement of particles, while amorphous solids have a fixed arrangement.","Crystalline solids have a fixed arrangement of particles, while amorphous solids have a random arrangement.","Crystalline solids are liquids at room temperature, while amorphous solids are solids at room temperature.","Crystalline solids have a fixed arrangement of particles, while amorphous solids have a random arrangement.","Crystalline solids have a fixed arrangement of particles, while amorphous solids have a random arrangement.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-pattern-of-38bc7c2c","Science G9: Explaining the properties of liquids - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-4 Explaining the properties of liquids, comparing crystalline and amorphous solids, and describing the pattern of crystals in solids.","2. Comparing crystalline and non-crystalline solids according to the organization and arrangement of their atoms.","What is the pattern of crystals in solids?","The pattern of crystals in solids is random and irregular.","The pattern of crystals in solids is a repeating arrangement of particles.","The pattern of crystals in solids is a fixed and unchanging arrangement.","The pattern of crystals in solids is a mixture of crystalline and amorphous solids.","The pattern of crystals in solids is a repeating arrangement of particles.","The pattern of crystals in solids is a repeating arrangement of particles.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-contribution-of-1d2d06bd","Science G9: Explaining the properties of liquids - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-4 Explaining the properties of liquids, comparing crystalline and amorphous solids, and describing the pattern of crystals in solids.","1. Explaining the contributions of scientists to the arrangement of the elements discovered in the periodic table and the history of its development leading to the modern periodic table.","What is the contribution of Dmitri Mendeleev to the development of the periodic table?","Dmitri Mendeleev discovered the elements that are now included in the periodic table.","Dmitri Mendeleev developed the modern periodic table and predicted the existence of undiscovered elements.","Dmitri Mendeleev was the first scientist to arrange the elements in a logical and systematic way.","Dmitri Mendeleev was a chemist who worked on the development of the periodic table, but did not make significant contributions.","Dmitri Mendeleev developed the modern periodic table and predicted the existence of undiscovered elements.","Dmitri Mendeleev developed the modern periodic table and predicted the existence of undiscovered elements.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-primary-characteristic-fcbd90cf","Science G9: Explaining the properties of liquids - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-4 Explaining the properties of liquids, comparing crystalline and amorphous solids, and describing the pattern of crystals in solids.","2. Comparing crystalline and non-crystalline solids according to the organization and arrangement of their atoms.","What is the primary characteristic of a liquid?","Liquids have a fixed shape and volume.","Liquids have a fixed arrangement of particles.","Liquids have a random arrangement of particles and take the shape of their container.","Liquids are solids at room temperature.","Liquids have a random arrangement of particles and take the shape of their container.","Liquids have a random arrangement of particles and take the shape of their container.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-difference-a-2296569f","Science G9: Explaining the properties of liquids - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-4 Explaining the properties of liquids, comparing crystalline and amorphous solids, and describing the pattern of crystals in solids.","2. Comparing crystalline and non-crystalline solids according to the organization and arrangement of their atoms.","What is the difference between a crystalline solid and a liquid?","Crystalline solids have a fixed shape and volume, while liquids do not.","Crystalline solids have a random arrangement of particles, while liquids have a fixed arrangement.","Crystalline solids have a fixed arrangement of particles, while liquids have a random arrangement.","Crystalline solids are liquids at room temperature, while liquids are solids at room temperature.","Crystalline solids have a fixed shape and volume, while liquids do not.","Crystalline solids have a fixed shape and volume, while liquids do not.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-contribution-of-e852d561","Science G9: Explaining the properties of liquids - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-4 Explaining the properties of liquids, comparing crystalline and amorphous solids, and describing the pattern of crystals in solids.","1. Explaining the contributions of scientists to the arrangement of the elements discovered in the periodic table and the history of its development leading to the modern periodic table.","What is the contribution of John Newlands to the development of the periodic table?","John Newlands discovered the elements that are now included in the periodic table.","John Newlands developed the modern periodic table and predicted the existence of undiscovered elements.","John Newlands was the first scientist to arrange the elements in a logical and systematic way.","John Newlands was a chemist who worked on the development of the periodic table, but did not make significant contributions.","John Newlands was the first scientist to arrange the elements in a logical and systematic way.","John Newlands was the first scientist to arrange the elements in a logical and systematic way.","","",""
