"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-how-arrangement-of-3f3eb529","Science G9: Explaining the properties of liquids - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-4 Explaining the properties of liquids, comparing crystalline and amorphous solids, and describing the pattern of crystals in solids.","2. Comparing crystalline and non-crystalline solids according to the organization and arrangement of their atoms.","How does the arrangement of molecules in a crystalline solid affect its properties?","The arrangement of molecules has no effect on the properties of the solid","The arrangement of molecules affects the melting point and rigidity of the solid","The arrangement of molecules affects the color and texture of the solid","The arrangement of molecules affects the smell and taste of the solid","The arrangement of molecules affects the melting point and rigidity of the solid","In a crystalline solid, molecules are arranged in a regular repeating pattern. This orderly arrangement affects properties such as melting point and rigidity.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-term-repeating-7f6e1130","Science G9: Explaining the properties of liquids - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-4 Explaining the properties of liquids, comparing crystalline and amorphous solids, and describing the pattern of crystals in solids.","3. Describing the organization of molecules in crystalline solids by building models that describe their structure.","What is the term for the repeating pattern of molecules in a crystalline solid?","Crystal lattice","Molecular structure","Atomic arrangement","Random pattern","Crystal lattice","The crystal lattice is the term used to describe the repeating pattern of molecules in a crystalline solid.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-result-of-ed42f3c4","Science G9: Explaining the properties of liquids - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-4 Explaining the properties of liquids, comparing crystalline and amorphous solids, and describing the pattern of crystals in solids.","3. Describing the organization of molecules in crystalline solids by building models that describe their structure.","What is the result of the molecules in a crystalline solid being arranged in a regular, repeating pattern?","The solid has a low melting point and is less rigid","The solid has a high melting point and is more rigid","The solid is a liquid","The solid is a gas","The solid has a high melting point and is more rigid","The result of the molecules in a crystalline solid being arranged in a regular, repeating pattern is that the solid has a high melting point and is more rigid.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-term-process-8741164b","Science G9: Explaining the properties of liquids - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-4 Explaining the properties of liquids, comparing crystalline and amorphous solids, and describing the pattern of crystals in solids.","3. Describing the organization of molecules in crystalline solids by building models that describe their structure.","What is the term for the process by which a crystalline solid forms?","Crystallization","Melting","Boiling","Sublimation","Crystallization","Crystallization is the term used to describe the process by which a crystalline solid forms.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-type-of-solid-44cd3285","Science G9: Explaining the properties of liquids - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-4 Explaining the properties of liquids, comparing crystalline and amorphous solids, and describing the pattern of crystals in solids.","2. Comparing crystalline and non-crystalline solids according to the organization and arrangement of their atoms.","What type of solid has a highly ordered arrangement of molecules?","Amorphous solid","Crystalline solid","Liquid","Gas","Crystalline solid","A crystalline solid has a highly ordered arrangement of molecules, which is characterized by a regular, repeating pattern.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-how-do-molecules-95635a8b","Science G9: Explaining the properties of liquids - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-4 Explaining the properties of liquids, comparing crystalline and amorphous solids, and describing the pattern of crystals in solids.","3. Describing the organization of molecules in crystalline solids by building models that describe their structure.","How do the molecules in a crystalline solid interact with each other?","They do not interact with each other","They interact with each other through weak intermolecular forces","They interact with each other through strong intermolecular forces","They are not affected by intermolecular forces","They interact with each other through strong intermolecular forces","In a crystalline solid, the molecules interact with each other through strong intermolecular forces, which hold them in a fixed position.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-difference-a-0fb3ca0f","Science G9: Explaining the properties of liquids - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-4 Explaining the properties of liquids, comparing crystalline and amorphous solids, and describing the pattern of crystals in solids.","2. Comparing crystalline and non-crystalline solids according to the organization and arrangement of their atoms.","What is the difference between a crystalline solid and an amorphous solid?","A crystalline solid has a random arrangement of molecules, while an amorphous solid has a regular arrangement","A crystalline solid has a regular arrangement of molecules, while an amorphous solid has a random arrangement","A crystalline solid is a liquid, while an amorphous solid is a gas","A crystalline solid is a gas, while an amorphous solid is a liquid","A crystalline solid has a regular arrangement of molecules, while an amorphous solid has a random arrangement","A crystalline solid has a regular arrangement of molecules, while an amorphous solid has a random arrangement.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-main-characteristic-9dcd309e","Science G9: Explaining the properties of liquids - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-4 Explaining the properties of liquids, comparing crystalline and amorphous solids, and describing the pattern of crystals in solids.","3. Describing the organization of molecules in crystalline solids by building models that describe their structure.","What is the main characteristic of the molecules in a crystalline solid?","They are arranged in a random pattern","They are densely packed but have a lot of space between them","They are arranged in a regular, repeating pattern","They are always in a liquid state","They are arranged in a regular, repeating pattern","In a crystalline solid, the molecules are arranged in a regular, repeating pattern, which gives the solid its unique properties.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-how-do-properties-51744af6","Science G9: Explaining the properties of liquids - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-4 Explaining the properties of liquids, comparing crystalline and amorphous solids, and describing the pattern of crystals in solids.","2. Comparing crystalline and non-crystalline solids according to the organization and arrangement of their atoms.","How do the properties of a crystalline solid differ from those of an amorphous solid?","A crystalline solid has a higher melting point and is more rigid, while an amorphous solid has a lower melting point and is less rigid","A crystalline solid has a lower melting point and is less rigid, while an amorphous solid has a higher melting point and is more rigid","A crystalline solid is a liquid, while an amorphous solid is a gas","A crystalline solid is a gas, while an amorphous solid is a liquid","A crystalline solid has a higher melting point and is more rigid, while an amorphous solid has a lower melting point and is less rigid","A crystalline solid has a higher melting point and is more rigid, while an amorphous solid has a lower melting point and is less rigid.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-effect-of-bacf9275","Science G9: Identifying the characteristics of a balanced - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-3-5 Identifying the characteristics of a balanced ecosystem, analyzing the factors and variables that affect its balance, explaining the impact of human activities on it, predicting the consequences of its imbalance, and evaluating designed solutions to address local environmental problems and restore its balance.","2. Distinguishing between human and natural factors that affect ecosystem balance, and predicting the effects resulting from changes in one component of the ecosystem on the living organisms that inhabit it.","What is the effect of pollution on the balance of an ecosystem?","It has no effect.","It increases the diversity of species.","It decreases the diversity of species.","It has a neutral effect on the balance of species.","It decreases the diversity of species.","Pollution can decrease the diversity of species in an ecosystem, leading to an imbalance and potentially even extinctions.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-happens-to-population-77bc8572","Science G9: Identifying the characteristics of a balanced - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-3-5 Identifying the characteristics of a balanced ecosystem, analyzing the factors and variables that affect its balance, explaining the impact of human activities on it, predicting the consequences of its imbalance, and evaluating designed solutions to address local environmental problems and restore its balance.","2. Distinguishing between human and natural factors that affect ecosystem balance, and predicting the effects resulting from changes in one component of the ecosystem on the living organisms that inhabit it.","What happens to the population of a species when its natural predator is removed?","The population increases rapidly.","The population decreases rapidly.","The population remains the same.","The population fluctuates wildly.","The population decreases rapidly.","When a natural predator is removed, the population of its prey species can increase rapidly, leading to an imbalance in the ecosystem.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-main-characteristic-d49c23e1","Science G9: Identifying the characteristics of a balanced - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-3-4 Describing types of aquatic and terrestrial ecosystems and the interactive relationships among their components.","1. Identifying the characteristics of a balanced ecosystem based on the availability of factors that maintain its balance (availability of clean water, recycling of nutrients, prevention of soil erosion, and diversity of living organisms).","What is the main characteristic of a balanced ecosystem?","It has a diverse range of species.","It has no predators or prey.","It has no nutrient cycling.","It has no decomposition process.","It has a diverse range of species.","A balanced ecosystem has a diverse range of species, which helps to maintain its stability and resilience.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-role-of-1c090fdd","Science G9: Identifying the characteristics of a balanced - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-3-5 Identifying the characteristics of a balanced ecosystem, analyzing the factors and variables that affect its balance, explaining the impact of human activities on it, predicting the consequences of its imbalance, and evaluating designed solutions to address local environmental problems and restore its balance.","3. Analyzing data to identify the factors and variables that affect the efficiency of ecosystems.","What is the role of conservation efforts in maintaining the balance of an ecosystem?","It has no role.","It increases the diversity of species.","It decreases the diversity of species.","It helps to maintain the balance of species by protecting and preserving habitats.","It helps to maintain the balance of species by protecting and preserving habitats.","Conservation efforts, such as habitat preservation and species protection, can help to maintain the balance of an ecosystem and prevent extinctions.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-effect-of-fb8b7350","Science G9: Identifying the characteristics of a balanced - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-3-5 Identifying the characteristics of a balanced ecosystem, analyzing the factors and variables that affect its balance, explaining the impact of human activities on it, predicting the consequences of its imbalance, and evaluating designed solutions to address local environmental problems and restore its balance.","2. Distinguishing between human and natural factors that affect ecosystem balance, and predicting the effects resulting from changes in one component of the ecosystem on the living organisms that inhabit it.","What is the effect of invasive species on the balance of an ecosystem?","It has no effect.","It increases the diversity of species.","It decreases the diversity of species.","It has a neutral effect on the balance of species.","It decreases the diversity of species.","Invasive species can decrease the diversity of species in an ecosystem, leading to an imbalance and potentially even extinctions.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-role-of-8c8410ed","Science G9: Identifying the characteristics of a balanced - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-3-5 Identifying the characteristics of a balanced ecosystem, analyzing the factors and variables that affect its balance, explaining the impact of human activities on it, predicting the consequences of its imbalance, and evaluating designed solutions to address local environmental problems and restore its balance.","3. Analyzing data to identify the factors and variables that affect the efficiency of ecosystems.","What is the role of decomposition in maintaining the balance of an ecosystem?","It has no role.","It increases the population of species.","It decreases the population of species.","It helps to maintain the balance of species by breaking down organic matter.","It helps to maintain the balance of species by breaking down organic matter.","Decomposition is essential for maintaining the balance of an ecosystem, as it helps to break down organic matter and recycle nutrients.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-effect-of-8db1b994","Science G9: Identifying the characteristics of a balanced - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-3-5 Identifying the characteristics of a balanced ecosystem, analyzing the factors and variables that affect its balance, explaining the impact of human activities on it, predicting the consequences of its imbalance, and evaluating designed solutions to address local environmental problems and restore its balance.","2. Distinguishing between human and natural factors that affect ecosystem balance, and predicting the effects resulting from changes in one component of the ecosystem on the living organisms that inhabit it.","What is the effect of overhunting on the balance of an ecosystem?","It has no effect.","It increases the population of species.","It decreases the population of species.","It has a neutral effect on the balance of species.","It decreases the population of species.","Overhunting can decrease the population of a species, leading to an imbalance in the ecosystem and potentially even extinctions.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-role-of-330da5b4","Science G9: Identifying the characteristics of a balanced - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-3-5 Identifying the characteristics of a balanced ecosystem, analyzing the factors and variables that affect its balance, explaining the impact of human activities on it, predicting the consequences of its imbalance, and evaluating designed solutions to address local environmental problems and restore its balance.","3. Analyzing data to identify the factors and variables that affect the efficiency of ecosystems.","What is the role of human activities in affecting the balance of an ecosystem?","It has no role.","It increases the diversity of species.","It decreases the diversity of species.","It has a significant impact on the balance of species.","It has a significant impact on the balance of species.","Human activities, such as deforestation and pollution, can have a significant impact on the balance of an ecosystem, leading to an imbalance and potentially even extinctions.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-effect-of-33757dfd","Science G9: Identifying the characteristics of a balanced - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-3-5 Identifying the characteristics of a balanced ecosystem, analyzing the factors and variables that affect its balance, explaining the impact of human activities on it, predicting the consequences of its imbalance, and evaluating designed solutions to address local environmental problems and restore its balance.","2. Distinguishing between human and natural factors that affect ecosystem balance, and predicting the effects resulting from changes in one component of the ecosystem on the living organisms that inhabit it.","What is the effect of climate change on the balance of an ecosystem?","It has no effect.","It increases the diversity of species.","It decreases the diversity of species.","It has a neutral effect on the balance of species.","It decreases the diversity of species.","Climate change can decrease the diversity of species in an ecosystem, leading to an imbalance and potentially even extinctions.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-role-of-6d9480fe","Science G9: Identifying the characteristics of a balanced - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-3-3 Explaining how matter and energy are transferred within an ecosystem, predicting the changes resulting from the extinction of one of its components, describing material cycles in the ecosystem, and inferring their role in supporting its sustainability.","1. Explaining how matter is recycled and energy flows between biotic and abiotic components in food webs within an ecosystem, and predicting the impact of the extinction of a biotic component on ecosystem balance and stability.","What is the role of nutrient cycling in maintaining the balance of an ecosystem?","It has no role.","It increases the population of species.","It decreases the population of species.","It helps to maintain the balance of species by recycling nutrients.","It helps to maintain the balance of species by recycling nutrients.","Nutrient cycling is essential for maintaining the balance of an ecosystem, as it helps to recycle nutrients and maintain the health of species.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-role-of-c6195f1d","Science G9: Identifying the characteristics of a balanced - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-3-4 Describing types of aquatic and terrestrial ecosystems and the interactive relationships among their components.","1. Identifying the characteristics of a balanced ecosystem based on the availability of factors that maintain its balance (availability of clean water, recycling of nutrients, prevention of soil erosion, and diversity of living organisms).","What is the role of decomposers in an ecosystem?","They produce their own food through photosynthesis.","They break down dead plants and animals into nutrients.","They are the primary consumers of an ecosystem.","They are the top predators of an ecosystem.","They break down dead plants and animals into nutrients.","Decomposers, such as bacteria and fungi, break down dead plants and animals into nutrients, which are then used by other organisms.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-effect-of-019588cf","Science G9: Identifying the characteristics of a balanced - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-3-4 Describing types of aquatic and terrestrial ecosystems and the interactive relationships among their components.","1. Identifying the characteristics of a balanced ecosystem based on the availability of factors that maintain its balance (availability of clean water, recycling of nutrients, prevention of soil erosion, and diversity of living organisms).","What is the effect of overhunting on a population?","It increases the population size.","It has no effect on the population size.","It decreases the population size.","It stabilizes the population size.","It decreases the population size.","Overhunting can lead to a decrease in population size, as the number of individuals is reduced due to excessive hunting.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-role-of-83ab2297","Science G9: Identifying the characteristics of a balanced - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-3-5 Identifying the characteristics of a balanced ecosystem, analyzing the factors and variables that affect its balance, explaining the impact of human activities on it, predicting the consequences of its imbalance, and evaluating designed solutions to address local environmental problems and restore its balance.","3. Analyzing data to identify the factors and variables that affect the efficiency of ecosystems.","What is the role of humans in shaping ecosystem balance?","They have no impact on the ecosystem.","They only affect the population size of a single species.","They are essential for maintaining ecosystem balance.","They can disrupt the balance of the ecosystem.","They can disrupt the balance of the ecosystem.","Humans play a significant role in shaping ecosystem balance, either positively or negatively, through activities such as deforestation, pollution, and climate change.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-importance-of-291446a8","Science G9: Identifying the characteristics of a balanced - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-3-1 Explaining the impact of changes in biodiversity on the environment and humans at both local and global levels, and describing how the ability of species to adapt to diverse environmental conditions affects biodiversity.","2. Explaining the impact of species’ ability to adapt to diverse environmental conditions on biodiversity in different ecosystems.","What is the importance of conservation efforts in maintaining ecosystem balance?","They have no impact on the ecosystem.","They only affect the population size of a single species.","They are essential for maintaining ecosystem balance.","They only occur in certain ecosystems.","They are essential for maintaining ecosystem balance.","Conservation efforts, such as protecting habitats and reducing pollution, are essential for maintaining ecosystem balance and preserving biodiversity.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-impact-of-1d4a217f","Science G9: Identifying the characteristics of a balanced - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-3-4 Describing types of aquatic and terrestrial ecosystems and the interactive relationships among their components.","1. Identifying the characteristics of a balanced ecosystem based on the availability of factors that maintain its balance (availability of clean water, recycling of nutrients, prevention of soil erosion, and diversity of living organisms).","What is the impact of invasive species on a native ecosystem?","They have no impact on the ecosystem.","They increase the diversity of species.","They decrease the population size of native species.","They stabilize the population size of native species.","They decrease the population size of native species.","Invasive species can have a negative impact on a native ecosystem, decreasing the population size of native species and disrupting the balance of the ecosystem.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-importance-of-848bdf4f","Science G9: Identifying the characteristics of a balanced - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-3-4 Describing types of aquatic and terrestrial ecosystems and the interactive relationships among their components.","1. Identifying the characteristics of a balanced ecosystem based on the availability of factors that maintain its balance (availability of clean water, recycling of nutrients, prevention of soil erosion, and diversity of living organisms).","What is the importance of symbiotic relationships in an ecosystem?","They are rare and only occur in certain ecosystems.","They are essential for the survival of many species.","They have no impact on the ecosystem.","They are only found in aquatic ecosystems.","They are essential for the survival of many species.","Symbiotic relationships, such as mutualism and commensalism, are essential for the survival of many species and play a crucial role in maintaining the balance of an ecosystem.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-effect-of-5b03a6bf","Science G9: Identifying the characteristics of a balanced - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-3-4 Describing types of aquatic and terrestrial ecosystems and the interactive relationships among their components.","1. Identifying the characteristics of a balanced ecosystem based on the availability of factors that maintain its balance (availability of clean water, recycling of nutrients, prevention of soil erosion, and diversity of living organisms).","What is the effect of habitat destruction on an ecosystem?","It increases the population size of plants and animals.","It has no effect on the ecosystem.","It decreases the diversity of species.","It increases the efficiency of the ecosystem.","It decreases the diversity of species.","Habitat destruction can have a negative impact on an ecosystem, decreasing the diversity of species and disrupting the balance of the ecosystem.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-role-of-0ef33fda","Science G9: Identifying the characteristics of a balanced - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-3-4 Describing types of aquatic and terrestrial ecosystems and the interactive relationships among their components.","1. Identifying the characteristics of a balanced ecosystem based on the availability of factors that maintain its balance (availability of clean water, recycling of nutrients, prevention of soil erosion, and diversity of living organisms).","What is the role of climate in shaping an ecosystem?","It has no impact on the ecosystem.","It determines the types of plants and animals that can survive.","It only affects the temperature of the ecosystem.","It only affects the precipitation of the ecosystem.","It determines the types of plants and animals that can survive.","Climate plays a crucial role in shaping an ecosystem, determining the types of plants and animals that can survive and thrive in a particular environment.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-effect-of-6abcfe0d","Science G9: Identifying the characteristics of a balanced - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-3-4 Describing types of aquatic and terrestrial ecosystems and the interactive relationships among their components.","1. Identifying the characteristics of a balanced ecosystem based on the availability of factors that maintain its balance (availability of clean water, recycling of nutrients, prevention of soil erosion, and diversity of living organisms).","What is the effect of pollution on an ecosystem?","It increases the population of plants and animals.","It has no effect on the ecosystem.","It decreases the diversity of species.","It increases the efficiency of the ecosystem.","It increases the efficiency of the ecosystem.","Pollution can have a negative impact on an ecosystem, decreasing the diversity of species and disrupting the balance of the ecosystem.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-main-characteristic-85ccb764","Science G9: Identifying the characteristics of a balanced - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-3-4 Describing types of aquatic and terrestrial ecosystems and the interactive relationships among their components.","1. Identifying the characteristics of a balanced ecosystem based on the availability of factors that maintain its balance (availability of clean water, recycling of nutrients, prevention of soil erosion, and diversity of living organisms).","What is the main characteristic of a balanced ecosystem?","It has a stable population of plants and animals.","It has a constant water temperature.","It has a diverse range of species.","It has no predators or prey.","It has a diverse range of species.","A balanced ecosystem has a diverse range of species, which helps to maintain its stability and efficiency.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-effect-of-84988a3e","Science G9: Tracking some of the changes that - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-3-1 Tracking some of the changes that occurred to the Earth as a result of human activity and exploring the natural hazards that may occur on Earth and how to predict them.","2. Analyzing data and information about natural events on Earth to comparing them in terms of their effects, and to knowing the role of science in predicting them and limiting their damage.","What is the effect of overfishing on marine ecosystems?","Overfishing has no effect on marine ecosystems","Overfishing improves marine ecosystems","Overfishing can lead to the depletion of fish populations and damage to marine ecosystems","Overfishing is necessary for marine ecosystems","Overfishing can lead to the depletion of fish populations and damage to marine ecosystems","Overfishing can lead to the depletion of fish populations and damage to marine ecosystems, resulting in a loss of biodiversity and ecosystem function.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-main-cause-96740eed","Science G9: Tracking some of the changes that - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-3-1 Tracking some of the changes that occurred to the Earth as a result of human activity and exploring the natural hazards that may occur on Earth and how to predict them.","1. Describing the impact of human activity on the future of the Earth and predicting various and adverse changes in human life.","What is the main cause of climate change?","The burning of fossil fuels and deforestation","The melting of polar ice caps","The increase in global temperature","The decrease in greenhouse gases","The burning of fossil fuels and deforestation","The burning of fossil fuels and deforestation release large amounts of carbon dioxide and other greenhouse gases into the atmosphere, leading to global warming and climate change.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-impact-of-8ad8a5df","Science G9: Tracking some of the changes that - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-3-1 Tracking some of the changes that occurred to the Earth as a result of human activity and exploring the natural hazards that may occur on Earth and how to predict them.","1. Describing the impact of human activity on the future of the Earth and predicting various and adverse changes in human life.","What is the impact of pollution on water resources?","Pollution helps to maintain water quality","Pollution has no effect on water resources","Pollution can lead to the depletion of water resources","Pollution is necessary for aquatic life","Pollution can lead to the depletion of water resources","Pollution can lead to the contamination of water resources, making them unsafe for human consumption and aquatic life.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-effect-of-c7d05c30","Science G9: Tracking some of the changes that - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-3-1 Tracking some of the changes that occurred to the Earth as a result of human activity and exploring the natural hazards that may occur on Earth and how to predict them.","1. Describing the impact of human activity on the future of the Earth and predicting various and adverse changes in human life.","What is the effect of deforestation on biodiversity?","Deforestation increases biodiversity","Deforestation has no effect on biodiversity","Deforestation decreases biodiversity","Deforestation is necessary for biodiversity","Deforestation decreases biodiversity","Deforestation leads to the loss of habitats and the extinction of many plant and animal species, resulting in a decrease in biodiversity.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-main-cause-a53774ce","Science G9: Tracking some of the changes that - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-3-1 Tracking some of the changes that occurred to the Earth as a result of human activity and exploring the natural hazards that may occur on Earth and how to predict them.","1. Describing the impact of human activity on the future of the Earth and predicting various and adverse changes in human life.","What is the main cause of soil erosion?","Overgrazing","Deforestation","Urbanization","Climate change","Deforestation","Deforestation leads to the removal of tree roots, which hold the soil in place, making it more susceptible to erosion.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-impact-of-21d18263","Science G9: Tracking some of the changes that - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-3-1 Tracking some of the changes that occurred to the Earth as a result of human activity and exploring the natural hazards that may occur on Earth and how to predict them.","1. Describing the impact of human activity on the future of the Earth and predicting various and adverse changes in human life.","What is the impact of human activity on natural disasters?","Human activity reduces the frequency of natural disasters","Human activity has no effect on natural disasters","Human activity increases the frequency and severity of natural disasters","Human activity is necessary for natural disasters","Human activity increases the frequency and severity of natural disasters","Human activities such as deforestation, pollution, and climate change can increase the frequency and severity of natural disasters such as floods, landslides, and wildfires.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-effect-of-c06d9ca4","Science G9: Tracking some of the changes that - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-3-1 Tracking some of the changes that occurred to the Earth as a result of human activity and exploring the natural hazards that may occur on Earth and how to predict them.","1. Describing the impact of human activity on the future of the Earth and predicting various and adverse changes in human life.","What is the effect of pollution on human health?","Pollution has no effect on human health","Pollution improves human health","Pollution can lead to various health problems","Pollution is necessary for human health","Pollution can lead to various health problems","Pollution can lead to various health problems such as respiratory diseases, cancer, and neurological disorders.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-impact-of-c0f6cba6","Science G9: Tracking some of the changes that - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-3-1 Tracking some of the changes that occurred to the Earth as a result of human activity and exploring the natural hazards that may occur on Earth and how to predict them.","1. Describing the impact of human activity on the future of the Earth and predicting various and adverse changes in human life.","What is the impact of climate change on sea levels?","Climate change decreases sea levels","Climate change has no effect on sea levels","Climate change increases sea levels","Climate change is necessary for sea levels","Climate change increases sea levels","Climate change leads to the melting of polar ice caps and glaciers, resulting in an increase in sea levels.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-impact-of-2b808e53","Science G9: Tracking some of the changes that - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-3-1 Tracking some of the changes that occurred to the Earth as a result of human activity and exploring the natural hazards that may occur on Earth and how to predict them.","1. Describing the impact of human activity on the future of the Earth and predicting various and adverse changes in human life.","What is the impact of human activity on the water cycle?","Human activity has no effect on the water cycle","Human activity improves the water cycle","Human activity can disrupt the water cycle and lead to droughts and floods","Human activity is necessary for the water cycle","Human activity can disrupt the water cycle and lead to droughts and floods","Human activities such as deforestation, pollution, and climate change can disrupt the water cycle, leading to droughts and floods.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-effect-of-b1f471a7","Science G9: Tracking some of the changes that - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-3-1 Tracking some of the changes that occurred to the Earth as a result of human activity and exploring the natural hazards that may occur on Earth and how to predict them.","1. Describing the impact of human activity on the future of the Earth and predicting various and adverse changes in human life.","What is the effect of pollution on air quality?","Pollution has no effect on air quality","Pollution improves air quality","Pollution can lead to poor air quality and negative health effects","Pollution is necessary for air quality","Pollution can lead to poor air quality and negative health effects","Pollution can lead to poor air quality and negative health effects, such as respiratory diseases and cancer.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-impact-of-854e7a45","Science G9: Tracking some of the changes that - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-3-5 Identifying the characteristics of a balanced ecosystem, analyzing the factors and variables that affect its balance, explaining the impact of human activities on it, predicting the consequences of its imbalance, and evaluating designed solutions to address local environmental problems and restore its balance.","2. Distinguishing between human and natural factors that affect ecosystem balance, and predicting the effects resulting from changes in one component of the ecosystem on the living organisms that inhabit it.","What is the impact of pollution on the environment?","It helps to clean the air and water","It has no effect on the environment","It harms plants and animals and contaminates soil and water","It helps to reduce the effects of natural disasters","It harms plants and animals and contaminates soil and water","Pollution harms plants and animals and contaminates soil and water, affecting the entire ecosystem.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-main-cause-5d01e4c8","Science G9: Tracking some of the changes that - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-3-1 Tracking some of the changes that occurred to the Earth as a result of human activity and exploring the natural hazards that may occur on Earth and how to predict them.","1. Describing the impact of human activity on the future of the Earth and predicting various and adverse changes in human life.","What is the main cause of water pollution?","The increase in global temperature","The decrease in rainfall","The removal of vegetation and the use of heavy machinery","The release of pollutants into waterways","The release of pollutants into waterways","The release of pollutants into waterways, such as chemicals and sewage, can lead to water pollution.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-main-reason-daef24fc","Science G9: Tracking some of the changes that - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-3-1 Tracking some of the changes that occurred to the Earth as a result of human activity and exploring the natural hazards that may occur on Earth and how to predict them.","1. Describing the impact of human activity on the future of the Earth and predicting various and adverse changes in human life.","What is the main reason for the increase in natural disasters?","The increase in global temperature","The decrease in rainfall","The movement of tectonic plates","The removal of vegetation and the use of heavy machinery","The increase in global temperature","The increase in global temperature can lead to more frequent and severe natural disasters, such as hurricanes and wildfires.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-impact-of-974ce511","Science G9: Tracking some of the changes that - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-3-1 Tracking some of the changes that occurred to the Earth as a result of human activity and exploring the natural hazards that may occur on Earth and how to predict them.","1. Describing environmental changes and their negative effects on natural resources and tracks their occurrence over time.","What is the impact of human activity on the Earth's natural resources?","It helps to conserve and protect natural resources","It has no effect on natural resources","It leads to the depletion and degradation of natural resources","It helps to reduce the effects of natural disasters","It leads to the depletion and degradation of natural resources","Human activity can lead to the depletion and degradation of natural resources, such as water, air, and soil.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-main-cause-f36c1b5c","Science G9: Tracking some of the changes that - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-3-1 Tracking some of the changes that occurred to the Earth as a result of human activity and exploring the natural hazards that may occur on Earth and how to predict them.","1. Describing the impact of human activity on the future of the Earth and predicting various and adverse changes in human life.","What is the main cause of soil erosion?","The increase in global temperature","The decrease in rainfall","The removal of vegetation and the use of heavy machinery","The movement of tectonic plates","The removal of vegetation and the use of heavy machinery","The removal of vegetation and the use of heavy machinery can lead to soil erosion, as the soil is no longer protected by plant roots and is more susceptible to erosion.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-impact-of-85e96b9b","Science G9: Tracking some of the changes that - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-3-5 Identifying the characteristics of a balanced ecosystem, analyzing the factors and variables that affect its balance, explaining the impact of human activities on it, predicting the consequences of its imbalance, and evaluating designed solutions to address local environmental problems and restore its balance.","2. Distinguishing between human and natural factors that affect ecosystem balance, and predicting the effects resulting from changes in one component of the ecosystem on the living organisms that inhabit it.","What is the impact of deforestation on the environment?","It helps to reduce the effects of natural disasters","It has no effect on the environment","It harms plants and animals and contaminates soil and water","It helps to clean the air and water","It harms plants and animals and contaminates soil and water","Deforestation harms plants and animals and contaminates soil and water, affecting the entire ecosystem.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-main-reason-e7b073e6","Science G9: Tracking some of the changes that - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-3-1 Tracking some of the changes that occurred to the Earth as a result of human activity and exploring the natural hazards that may occur on Earth and how to predict them.","1. Describing the impact of human activity on the future of the Earth and predicting various and adverse changes in human life.","What is the main reason for the loss of biodiversity?","Habitat destruction and fragmentation","The introduction of non-native species","The increase in global temperature","The decrease in the Earth's atmosphere","Habitat destruction and fragmentation","Habitat destruction and fragmentation lead to the loss of habitats for many species, resulting in a decline in biodiversity.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-main-cause-02f0515b","Science G9: Tracking some of the changes that - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-3-1 Tracking some of the changes that occurred to the Earth as a result of human activity and exploring the natural hazards that may occur on Earth and how to predict them.","1. Describing the impact of human activity on the future of the Earth and predicting various and adverse changes in human life.","What is the main cause of climate change?","The burning of fossil fuels and deforestation","The increase in global temperature due to the sun","The movement of tectonic plates","The decrease in the Earth's atmosphere","The burning of fossil fuels and deforestation","The burning of fossil fuels and deforestation release large amounts of carbon dioxide into the atmosphere, contributing to climate change.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-impact-of-d22a7000","Science G9: Tracking some of the changes that - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-3-1 Explaining the impact of changes in biodiversity on the environment and humans at both local and global levels, and describing how the ability of species to adapt to diverse environmental conditions affects biodiversity.","2. Explaining the impact of species’ ability to adapt to diverse environmental conditions on biodiversity in different ecosystems.","What is the impact of climate change on the environment?","It has no effect on the environment","It helps to clean the air and water","It harms plants and animals and contaminates soil and water","It leads to the loss of biodiversity","It harms plants and animals and contaminates soil and water","Climate change can harm plants and animals and contaminate soil and water, affecting the entire ecosystem.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-impact-of-8b987499","Science G9: Tracking some of the changes that - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-3-1 Tracking some of the changes that occurred to the Earth as a result of human activity and exploring the natural hazards that may occur on Earth and how to predict them.","1. Describing the impact of human activity on the future of the Earth and predicting various and adverse changes in human life.","What is the impact of pollution on human health?","It has no effect on human health","It helps to improve human health","It harms human health and can lead to diseases","It has no impact on the environment","It harms human health and can lead to diseases","Pollution can harm human health and lead to diseases, such as respiratory problems and cancer.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-primary-way-0c2beaf6","Science G9: Tracking some of the changes that - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-3-1 Tracking some of the changes that occurred to the Earth as a result of human activity and exploring the natural hazards that may occur on Earth and how to predict them.","1. Describing the impact of human activity on the future of the Earth and predicting various and adverse changes in human life.","What is the primary way to prevent landslides?","Building dams to control water flow","Planting trees to stabilize soil","Constructing buildings on stable ground","Using explosives to clear rocks","Planting trees to stabilize soil","Planting trees helps to stabilize soil and prevent landslides by holding the soil in place with their roots.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-main-way-abd6c116","Science G9: Tracking some of the changes that - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-3-1 Tracking some of the changes that occurred to the Earth as a result of human activity and exploring the natural hazards that may occur on Earth and how to predict them.","1. Describing the impact of human activity on the future of the Earth and predicting various and adverse changes in human life.","What is the main way to prevent wildfires?","Building fire breaks to contain fires","Using fire retardants to extinguish fires","Creating fire-safe zones","Removing dry leaves and branches","Removing dry leaves and branches","Removing dry leaves and branches helps to prevent wildfires by reducing the amount of fuel available for fires to spread.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-natural-hazard-is-49939788","Science G9: Tracking some of the changes that - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-6 Explaining the causes of stresses affecting the rocks that make up the Earth’s interior, and describing the resulting effects.","2. Defining earthquakes, seismic waves and their types, and defining the epicenter of the earthquake and distinguishing each of them through drawing.","Which natural hazard is often caused by the movement of the ground?","Earthquake","Volcanic eruption","Hurricane","Tsunami","Earthquake","Earthquakes are often caused by the movement of the ground, resulting in seismic waves that can cause damage and loss of life.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-main-way-7bd68a8c","Science G9: Tracking some of the changes that - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-3-1 Tracking some of the changes that occurred to the Earth as a result of human activity and exploring the natural hazards that may occur on Earth and how to predict them.","1. Describing the impact of human activity on the future of the Earth and predicting various and adverse changes in human life.","What is the main way to prevent water pollution?","Building sewage treatment plants","Reducing waste and using recycling","Planting trees to absorb pollutants","Using chemicals to kill pollutants","Reducing waste and using recycling","Reducing waste and using recycling helps to prevent water pollution by minimizing the amount of waste that can contaminate water sources.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-natural-hazard-is-e518e0fb","Science G9: Tracking some of the changes that - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-3-1 Tracking some of the changes that occurred to the Earth as a result of human activity and exploring the natural hazards that may occur on Earth and how to predict them.","1. Describing the impact of human activity on the future of the Earth and predicting various and adverse changes in human life.","Which natural hazard is often caused by the movement of the ocean?","Hurricane","Tsunami","Earthquake","Volcanic eruption","Tsunami","Tsunamis are often caused by the movement of the ocean, resulting in large waves that can cause damage and loss of life.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-natural-hazard-is-2901d093","Science G9: Tracking some of the changes that - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-3-1 Tracking some of the changes that occurred to the Earth as a result of human activity and exploring the natural hazards that may occur on Earth and how to predict them.","1. Describing the impact of human activity on the future of the Earth and predicting various and adverse changes in human life.","Which natural hazard is often caused by the movement of the atmosphere?","Hurricane","Tornado","Earthquake","Volcanic eruption","Tornado","Tornadoes are often caused by the movement of the atmosphere, resulting in rotating columns of air that can cause damage and loss of life.","/image-source/sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-natural-hazard-is-2901d093.png","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-primary-way-5e630b8f","Science G9: Tracking some of the changes that - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-3-1 Tracking some of the changes that occurred to the Earth as a result of human activity and exploring the natural hazards that may occur on Earth and how to predict them.","1. Describing the impact of human activity on the future of the Earth and predicting various and adverse changes in human life.","What is the primary way to prevent soil pollution?","Using pesticides to kill pests","Planting trees to absorb pollutants","Building waste management facilities","Reducing waste and using recycling","Reducing waste and using recycling","Reducing waste and using recycling helps to prevent soil pollution by minimizing the amount of waste that can contaminate the soil.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-main-cause-1ef9e062","Science G9: Tracking some of the changes that - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-3-1 Tracking some of the changes that occurred to the Earth as a result of human activity and exploring the natural hazards that may occur on Earth and how to predict them.","1. Describing environmental changes and their negative effects on natural resources and tracks their occurrence over time.","What is the main cause of soil erosion?","Water flowing over the land","Human activities such as deforestation","Weathering and plate tectonics","Atmospheric pressure","Human activities such as deforestation","Human activities such as deforestation lead to soil erosion by removing tree roots that hold the soil in place.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-natural-hazard-is-3ffed98f","Science G9: Tracking some of the changes that - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-6 Explaining the causes of stresses affecting the rocks that make up the Earth’s interior, and describing the resulting effects.","2. Defining earthquakes, seismic waves and their types, and defining the epicenter of the earthquake and distinguishing each of them through drawing.","Which natural hazard is often caused by the movement of tectonic plates?","Hurricane","Tsunami","Earthquake","Volcanic eruption","Earthquake","Earthquakes are often caused by the movement of tectonic plates, resulting in seismic waves that can cause damage and loss of life.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-type-of-natural-68da5554","Science G9: Tracking some of the changes that - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-3-1 Tracking some of the changes that occurred to the Earth as a result of human activity and exploring the natural hazards that may occur on Earth and how to predict them.","1. Describing the impact of human activity on the future of the Earth and predicting various and adverse changes in human life.","Which type of natural hazard is often caused by the movement of water?","Flood","Drought","Wildfire","Tornado","Flood","Floods are often caused by the movement of water, such as heavy rainfall or storm surges, resulting in damage to property and loss of life.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-purpose-of-8537a49b","Science G9: Understanding how a chemical reaction occurs - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-8 Understanding how a chemical reaction occurs, expressing it in a balanced chemical equation based on the law of conservation of mass, and distinguishing chemical reactions according to the energy associated with","4. Distinguishing between an endothermic reaction and an exothermic reaction, providing examples of each, explaining how to express them in a chemical equation.","What is the purpose of using coefficients in a balanced chemical equation?","To indicate the state of matter of the reactants and products","To indicate the direction of the reaction","To balance the number of atoms of each element","To show the rate of the reaction","To balance the number of atoms of each element","Coefficients are used in a balanced chemical equation to balance the number of atoms of each element, ensuring that the law of conservation of mass is followed.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-type-of-chemical-c7976515","Science G9: Understanding how a chemical reaction occurs - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-8 Understanding how a chemical reaction occurs, expressing it in a balanced chemical equation based on the law of conservation of mass, and distinguishing chemical reactions according to the energy associated with","2. Describing a chemical reaction using a balanced verbal and symbolic chemical equation and apply the law of conservation of mass to different chemical reactions.","Which type of chemical reaction involves the breakdown of a single compound into two or more simpler substances?","Synthesis reaction","Decomposition reaction","Replacement reaction","Combustion reaction","Decomposition reaction","A decomposition reaction is a type of chemical reaction where a single compound breaks down into two or more simpler substances, such as 2H2O ? 2H2 + O2.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-difference-a-8c16ae23","Science G9: Understanding how a chemical reaction occurs - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-7 Explaining how atoms bond with each other, identifying what is a chemical bond and how it is formed, and distinguishing between different types","2. Describes the states of the outer electron level (valence electrons) through the periodic chemical properties of elements within the same group.","What is the difference between a reactant and a product in a chemical reaction?","A reactant is a substance that is formed during a reaction, while a product is a substance that is used up","A reactant is a substance that is used up during a reaction, while a product is a substance that is formed","A reactant and a product are the same thing","A reactant is a type of catalyst, while a product is a type of solvent","A reactant is a substance that is used up during a reaction, while a product is a substance that is formed","In a chemical reaction, a reactant is a substance that is used up or consumed, while a product is a substance that is formed or produced.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-of-is-an-023d0acf","Science G9: Understanding how a chemical reaction occurs - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-8 Understanding how a chemical reaction occurs, expressing it in a balanced chemical equation based on the law of conservation of mass, and distinguishing chemical reactions according to the energy associated with","4. Distinguishing between an endothermic reaction and an exothermic reaction, providing examples of each, explaining how to express them in a chemical equation.","Which of the following is an example of a balanced chemical equation?","2H2 + O2 → H2O","2H2 + O2 → 2H2O","H2 + O2 → H2O","H2 + 2O2 → H2O","2H2 + O2 → 2H2O","The equation 2H2 + O2 → 2H2O is balanced because it has the same number of atoms of each element on both the reactant and product sides.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-purpose-of-607aa16b","Science G9: Understanding how a chemical reaction occurs - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-8 Understanding how a chemical reaction occurs, expressing it in a balanced chemical equation based on the law of conservation of mass, and distinguishing chemical reactions according to the energy associated with","4. Distinguishing between an endothermic reaction and an exothermic reaction, providing examples of each, explaining how to express them in a chemical equation.","What is the purpose of balancing a chemical equation?","To show that the reaction is exothermic","To show that the reaction is endothermic","To ensure that the law of conservation of mass is followed","To determine the rate of the reaction","To ensure that the law of conservation of mass is followed","The purpose of balancing a chemical equation is to ensure that the law of conservation of mass is followed, meaning that the number of atoms of each element is the same on both the reactant and product sides.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-type-of-chemical-d746d14b","Science G9: Understanding how a chemical reaction occurs - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-7 Explaining how atoms bond with each other, identifying what is a chemical bond and how it is formed, and distinguishing between different types","5. Distinguishing between an ion, a molecule, a compound, and give examples for each one, explaining what is meant by a chemical formula, and its indication through various examples.","What type of chemical reaction involves the combination of two or more substances to form a new compound?","Synthesis reaction","Decomposition reaction","Replacement reaction","Combustion reaction","Synthesis reaction","A synthesis reaction is a type of chemical reaction where two or more substances combine to form a new compound, such as 2H2 + O2 → 2H2O.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-a-balanced-b3ad3c1b","Science G9: Understanding how a chemical reaction occurs - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-8 Understanding how a chemical reaction occurs, expressing it in a balanced chemical equation based on the law of conservation of mass, and distinguishing chemical reactions according to the energy associated with","4. Distinguishing between an endothermic reaction and an exothermic reaction, providing examples of each, explaining how to express them in a chemical equation.","What is a balanced chemical equation?","An equation that shows the reactants and products but does not account for the number of atoms","An equation that shows the reactants and products with the same number of atoms of each element on both sides","An equation that shows only the reactants","An equation that shows only the products","An equation that shows the reactants and products with the same number of atoms of each element on both sides","A balanced chemical equation is an equation that shows the reactants and products with the same number of atoms of each element on both sides, following the law of conservation of mass.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-law-of-16d06d61","Science G9: Understanding how a chemical reaction occurs - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-8 Understanding how a chemical reaction occurs, expressing it in a balanced chemical equation based on the law of conservation of mass, and distinguishing chemical reactions according to the energy associated with","2. Describing a chemical reaction using a balanced verbal and symbolic chemical equation and apply the law of conservation of mass to different chemical reactions.","What is the law of conservation of mass in a chemical reaction?","Matter is created during a chemical reaction","Matter is destroyed during a chemical reaction","The total mass of the reactants is equal to the total mass of the products","The total mass of the reactants is less than the total mass of the products","The total mass of the reactants is equal to the total mass of the products","The law of conservation of mass states that matter cannot be created or destroyed in a chemical reaction, so the total mass of the reactants must be equal to the total mass of the products.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-how-do-you-4b7f55a9","Science G9: Understanding how a chemical reaction occurs - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-8 Understanding how a chemical reaction occurs, expressing it in a balanced chemical equation based on the law of conservation of mass, and distinguishing chemical reactions according to the energy associated with","2. Describing a chemical reaction using a balanced verbal and symbolic chemical equation and apply the law of conservation of mass to different chemical reactions.","How do you know if a chemical equation is balanced?","If the number of atoms of each element is the same on both the reactant and product sides","If the number of atoms of each element is different on the reactant and product sides","If the equation has the same number of reactants and products","If the equation has a different number of reactants and products","If the number of atoms of each element is the same on both the reactant and product sides","A chemical equation is balanced if the number of atoms of each element is the same on both the reactant and product sides, following the law of conservation of mass.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-role-of-00df8da4","Science G9: Understanding how a chemical reaction occurs - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-8 Understanding how a chemical reaction occurs, expressing it in a balanced chemical equation based on the law of conservation of mass, and distinguishing chemical reactions according to the energy associated with","4. Distinguishing between an endothermic reaction and an exothermic reaction, providing examples of each, explaining how to express them in a chemical equation.","What is the role of energy in a chemical reaction?","Energy is always released during a chemical reaction","Energy is always absorbed during a chemical reaction","Energy can be either released or absorbed during a chemical reaction","Energy is not involved in chemical reactions","Energy can be either released or absorbed during a chemical reaction","In a chemical reaction, energy can be either released (exothermic) or absorbed (endothermic), depending on the specific reaction and the conditions under which it occurs.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-of-is-a-b3d00652","Science G9: Understanding how a chemical reaction occurs - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-8 Understanding how a chemical reaction occurs, expressing it in a balanced chemical equation based on the law of conservation of mass, and distinguishing chemical reactions according to the energy associated with","3. Listing the different forms of energy associated with chemical reactions (absorbed, released), and providing examples thereof.","Which of the following is a characteristic of an exothermic reaction?","Absorbs energy from the surroundings","Releases energy to the surroundings","Has a negative entropy change","Has a constant temperature","Releases energy to the surroundings","Releases energy to the surroundings is the correct answer because it describes a characteristic of an exothermic reaction, where energy is released to the surroundings, often in the form of heat or light.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-type-of-chemical-017c2335","Science G9: Understanding how a chemical reaction occurs - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-8 Understanding how a chemical reaction occurs, expressing it in a balanced chemical equation based on the law of conservation of mass, and distinguishing chemical reactions according to the energy associated with","2. Describing a chemical reaction using a balanced verbal and symbolic chemical equation and apply the law of conservation of mass to different chemical reactions.","Which type of chemical reaction releases energy to the surroundings?","Endothermic reaction","Exothermic reaction","Neutral reaction","Reversible reaction","Exothermic reaction","Exothermic reaction is the correct answer because it describes a chemical reaction that releases energy to the surroundings, often in the form of heat or light.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-term-energy-ae6ef29e","Science G9: Understanding how a chemical reaction occurs - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-8 Understanding how a chemical reaction occurs, expressing it in a balanced chemical equation based on the law of conservation of mass, and distinguishing chemical reactions according to the energy associated with","3. Listing the different forms of energy associated with chemical reactions (absorbed, released), and providing examples thereof.","What is the term for the energy released or absorbed during a chemical reaction?","Kinetic energy","Potential energy","Thermal energy","Chemical energy","Chemical energy","Chemical energy is the correct answer because it refers to the energy stored in the bonds of atoms and molecules, which is released or absorbed during a chemical reaction.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-type-of-chemical-fc63270b","Science G9: Understanding how a chemical reaction occurs - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-8 Understanding how a chemical reaction occurs, expressing it in a balanced chemical equation based on the law of conservation of mass, and distinguishing chemical reactions according to the energy associated with","4. Distinguishing between an endothermic reaction and an exothermic reaction, providing examples of each, explaining how to express them in a chemical equation.","Which type of chemical reaction has a positive entropy change?","Exothermic reaction","Endothermic reaction","Reversible reaction","Irreversible reaction","Endothermic reaction","Endothermic reaction is the correct answer because it often has a positive entropy change, as energy is absorbed from the surroundings and the system becomes more disordered.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-of-is-an-1156fc02","Science G9: Understanding how a chemical reaction occurs - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-8 Understanding how a chemical reaction occurs, expressing it in a balanced chemical equation based on the law of conservation of mass, and distinguishing chemical reactions according to the energy associated with","2. Describing a chemical reaction using a balanced verbal and symbolic chemical equation and apply the law of conservation of mass to different chemical reactions.","Which of the following is an example of a chemical reaction that absorbs energy from the surroundings?","Combustion of gasoline in a car engine","Photosynthesis in plants","Decomposition of organic matter","Respiration in humans","Photosynthesis in plants","Photosynthesis is the correct answer because it is an endothermic reaction that absorbs energy from sunlight to convert carbon dioxide and water into glucose and oxygen.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-type-of-energy-6b3ccc16","Science G9: Understanding how a chemical reaction occurs - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-8 Understanding how a chemical reaction occurs, expressing it in a balanced chemical equation based on the law of conservation of mass, and distinguishing chemical reactions according to the energy associated with","3. Listing the different forms of energy associated with chemical reactions (absorbed, released), and providing examples thereof.","What type of energy is associated with a chemical reaction that releases heat to the surroundings?","Electromagnetic energy","Thermal energy released","Chemical potential energy","Nuclear energy absorbed","Thermal energy released","Thermal energy released is the correct answer because it describes the type of energy associated with an exothermic reaction, where heat is released to the surroundings.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-an-example-557ea25f","Science G9: Understanding how a chemical reaction occurs - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-8 Understanding how a chemical reaction occurs, expressing it in a balanced chemical equation based on the law of conservation of mass, and distinguishing chemical reactions according to the energy associated with","3. Listing the different forms of energy associated with chemical reactions (absorbed, released), and providing examples thereof.","What is an example of a chemical reaction that releases energy in the form of light?","Combustion of wood","Decomposition of hydrogen peroxide","Respiration in humans","Glow stick reaction","Glow stick reaction","Glow stick reaction is the correct answer because it is a chemical reaction that releases energy in the form of light, which is an example of an exothermic reaction.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-of-chemical-reactions-49107ff2","Science G9: Understanding how a chemical reaction occurs - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-8 Understanding how a chemical reaction occurs, expressing it in a balanced chemical equation based on the law of conservation of mass, and distinguishing chemical reactions according to the energy associated with","2. Describing a chemical reaction using a balanced verbal and symbolic chemical equation and apply the law of conservation of mass to different chemical reactions.","Which of the following chemical reactions is an example of an endothermic reaction?","Combustion of gasoline","Decomposition of ammonia","Photosynthesis in plants","Respiration in humans","Photosynthesis in plants","Photosynthesis is the correct answer because it is an endothermic reaction that absorbs energy from sunlight to convert carbon dioxide and water into glucose and oxygen.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-type-of-energy-2ee94cba","Science G9: Understanding how a chemical reaction occurs - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-8 Understanding how a chemical reaction occurs, expressing it in a balanced chemical equation based on the law of conservation of mass, and distinguishing chemical reactions according to the energy associated with","3. Listing the different forms of energy associated with chemical reactions (absorbed, released), and providing examples thereof.","What type of energy is associated with the breaking of chemical bonds?","Chemical potential energy","Thermal energy","Electromagnetic energy","Kinetic energy","Chemical potential energy","Chemical potential energy is the correct answer because it refers to the energy stored in the bonds of atoms and molecules, which is released or absorbed during a chemical reaction.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-term-energy-a195637a","Science G9: Understanding how a chemical reaction occurs - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-9 Describing the speed of chemical reactions and identifying the factors affecting them","3. Explaining the concept of activation energy and its role in the rate of reaction and providing an example of it.","What is the term for the energy required to break the bonds of a molecule?","Activation energy","Chemical potential energy","Thermal energy","Bond energy","Activation energy","Activation energy is the correct answer because it refers to the energy required to break the bonds of a molecule and initiate a chemical reaction.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-an-exothermic-a38c8394","Science G9: Understanding how a chemical reaction occurs - Set 2 T1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-8 Understanding how a chemical reaction occurs, expressing it in a balanced chemical equation based on the law of conservation of mass, and distinguishing chemical reactions according to the energy associated with","3. Listing the different forms of energy associated with chemical reactions (absorbed, released), and providing examples thereof.","What is an exothermic reaction?","A reaction that absorbs heat energy from the surroundings.","A reaction that releases heat energy into the surroundings.","A reaction that does not involve the transfer of heat energy.","A reaction that releases light energy into the surroundings.","A reaction that releases heat energy into the surroundings.","An exothermic reaction is a reaction that releases heat energy into the surroundings.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-difference-a-0e3a83fa","Science G9: Understanding how a chemical reaction occurs - Set 2 T1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-5 Describing the history of the periodic table, explaining how the elements are organized in the periodic table, and the properties of the elements and their common uses.","4. Recognizing the location of the representative elements, the transitional elements, and the inner transition (lanthanides and actinides) in the periodic table, based on their electronic composition, predicting their physical and chemical properties, and identifying some of their uses.","What is the difference between a physical change and a chemical change?","A physical change is a change in the chemical composition of a substance, while a chemical change is a change in the physical state of a substance.","A physical change is a change in the physical state of a substance, while a chemical change is a change in the chemical composition of a substance.","A physical change is a change in the temperature of a substance, while a chemical change is a change in the color of a substance.","A physical change is a change in the color of a substance, while a chemical change is a change in the temperature of a substance.","A physical change is a change in the physical state of a substance, while a chemical change is a change in the chemical composition of a substance.","A physical change is a change in the physical state of a substance, while a chemical change is a change in the chemical composition of a substance.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-purpose-of-6de1c885","Science G9: Understanding how a chemical reaction occurs - Set 2 T1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-8 Understanding how a chemical reaction occurs, expressing it in a balanced chemical equation based on the law of conservation of mass, and distinguishing chemical reactions according to the energy associated with","2. Describing a chemical reaction using a balanced verbal and symbolic chemical equation and apply the law of conservation of mass to different chemical reactions.","What is the purpose of a chemical equation?","To show the reactants and products of a chemical reaction.","To show the energy changes that occur during a chemical reaction.","To show the physical changes that occur during a chemical reaction.","To show the chemical changes that occur during a chemical reaction.","To show the reactants and products of a chemical reaction.","The purpose of a chemical equation is to show the reactants and products of a chemical reaction.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-a-chemical-3e2d0954","Science G9: Understanding how a chemical reaction occurs - Set 2 T1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-9 Describing the speed of chemical reactions and identifying the factors affecting them","2. Determining the factors affecting the rate of a chemical reaction (concentration of reactants, concentration of reactants, pressure, temperature, catalyst).","What is a chemical reaction?","A change in the physical state of a substance, such as from solid to liquid.","A change in the chemical composition of a substance, such as the formation of a new substance.","A change in the temperature of a substance.","A change in the color of a substance.","A change in the chemical composition of a substance, such as the formation of a new substance.","A chemical reaction is a change in the chemical composition of a substance, such as the formation of a new substance.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-a-balanced-5505060c","Science G9: Understanding how a chemical reaction occurs - Set 2 T1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-7 Explaining how atoms bond with each other, identifying what is a chemical bond and how it is formed, and distinguishing between different types","3. Illustrating the electronic distribution of a number of groups of the periodic table, and explaining the method of dot representation of electrons, and represent it for a number of elements.","What is a balanced chemical equation?","A chemical equation that shows the reactants on the left side and the products on the right side, but does not show the correct number of atoms of each element.","A chemical equation that shows the correct number of atoms of each element on both the reactant and product sides.","A chemical equation that shows the reactants on the right side and the products on the left side.","A chemical equation that does not show the reactants or products.","A chemical equation that shows the correct number of atoms of each element on both the reactant and product sides.","A balanced chemical equation is a chemical equation that shows the correct number of atoms of each element on both the reactant and product sides.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-law-of-25ddcf33","Science G9: Understanding how a chemical reaction occurs - Set 2 T1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-8 Understanding how a chemical reaction occurs, expressing it in a balanced chemical equation based on the law of conservation of mass, and distinguishing chemical reactions according to the energy associated with","2. Describing a chemical reaction using a balanced verbal and symbolic chemical equation and apply the law of conservation of mass to different chemical reactions.","What is the law of conservation of mass?","The total mass of the reactants is always greater than the total mass of the products.","The total mass of the reactants is always equal to the total mass of the products.","The total mass of the products is always greater than the total mass of the reactants.","The total mass of the reactants and products is always different.","The total mass of the reactants is always equal to the total mass of the products.","The law of conservation of mass states that matter cannot be created or destroyed in a chemical reaction, so the total mass of the reactants is always equal to the total mass of the products.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-a-balanced-04af9a28","Science G9: Understanding how a chemical reaction occurs - Set 2 T1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-8 Understanding how a chemical reaction occurs, expressing it in a balanced chemical equation based on the law of conservation of mass, and distinguishing chemical reactions according to the energy associated with","2. Describing a chemical reaction using a balanced verbal and symbolic chemical equation and apply the law of conservation of mass to different chemical reactions.","What is a balanced verbal equation?","A verbal expression of a chemical reaction that does not show the correct number of atoms of each element.","A verbal expression of a chemical reaction that shows the correct number of atoms of each element.","A verbal expression of a chemical reaction that shows the reactants on the right side and the products on the left side.","A verbal expression of a chemical reaction that does not show the reactants or products.","A verbal expression of a chemical reaction that shows the correct number of atoms of each element.","A balanced verbal equation is a verbal expression of a chemical reaction that shows the correct number of atoms of each element.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-a-chemical-a5437354","Science G9: Understanding how a chemical reaction occurs - Set 2 T1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-8 Understanding how a chemical reaction occurs, expressing it in a balanced chemical equation based on the law of conservation of mass, and distinguishing chemical reactions according to the energy associated with","2. Describing a chemical reaction using a balanced verbal and symbolic chemical equation and apply the law of conservation of mass to different chemical reactions.","What is a chemical equation?","A written expression of a chemical reaction that shows the reactants and products.","A diagram of a chemical reaction that shows the reactants and products.","A table of the reactants and products of a chemical reaction.","A graph of the reactants and products of a chemical reaction.","A written expression of a chemical reaction that shows the reactants and products.","A chemical equation is a written expression of a chemical reaction that shows the reactants and products.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-law-of-d80f5f47","Science G9: Understanding how a chemical reaction occurs - Set 2 T1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-7 Explaining how atoms bond with each other, identifying what is a chemical bond and how it is formed, and distinguishing between different types","2. Describes the states of the outer electron level (valence electrons) through the periodic chemical properties of elements within the same group.","What is the law of definite proportions?","The law that states that a chemical compound always contains the same proportion of elements by mass.","The law that states that a chemical compound can contain different proportions of elements by mass.","The law that states that a chemical compound always contains a different proportion of elements by mass.","The law that states that a chemical compound does not contain any elements by mass.","The law that states that a chemical compound always contains the same proportion of elements by mass.","The law of definite proportions states that a chemical compound always contains the same proportion of elements by mass.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-an-endothermic-bcf6b4e8","Science G9: Understanding how a chemical reaction occurs - Set 2 T1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-8 Understanding how a chemical reaction occurs, expressing it in a balanced chemical equation based on the law of conservation of mass, and distinguishing chemical reactions according to the energy associated with","3. Listing the different forms of energy associated with chemical reactions (absorbed, released), and providing examples thereof.","What is an endothermic reaction?","A reaction that releases heat energy into the surroundings.","A reaction that absorbs heat energy from the surroundings.","A reaction that does not involve the transfer of heat energy.","A reaction that releases light energy into the surroundings.","A reaction that absorbs heat energy from the surroundings.","An endothermic reaction is a reaction that absorbs heat energy from the surroundings.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-term-energy-c1794a6f","Science G9: Understanding how a chemical reaction occurs - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-3-3 Explaining how matter and energy are transferred within an ecosystem, predicting the changes resulting from the extinction of one of its components, describing material cycles in the ecosystem, and inferring their role in supporting its sustainability.","2. Describing material cycles (water, carbon dioxide, and nitrogen) in an ecosystem and their transfer through biotic and abiotic components, and inferring their role in supporting sustainability.","What is the term for the energy transferred from one object to another through direct contact?","Convection","Radiation","Conduction","Chemical potential energy","Conduction","Conduction is the process of energy transfer through direct contact between objects.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-term-energy-5d4bb4bc","Science G9: Understanding how a chemical reaction occurs - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-4-4 Distinguishing between the kinetic and potential energy of a body and the factors affecting them.","3. Calculating the kinetic and potential energy of objects, and deducing the relationship between them.","What is the term for the energy stored in the chemical bonds of a substance?","Thermal energy","Kinetic energy","Potential energy","Chemical potential energy","Chemical potential energy","Chemical potential energy is the energy stored in the chemical bonds of a substance.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-type-of-chemical-49a82f7b","Science G9: Understanding how a chemical reaction occurs - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-8 Understanding how a chemical reaction occurs, expressing it in a balanced chemical equation based on the law of conservation of mass, and distinguishing chemical reactions according to the energy associated with","4. Distinguishing between an endothermic reaction and an exothermic reaction, providing examples of each, explaining how to express them in a chemical equation.","Which type of chemical reaction releases energy to the surroundings?","Endothermic reaction","Exothermic reaction","Neutral reaction","Reversible reaction","Exothermic reaction","An exothermic reaction is a chemical reaction that releases energy to the surroundings in the form of heat or light.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-type-of-energy-972d0045","Science G9: Understanding how a chemical reaction occurs - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-8 Understanding how a chemical reaction occurs, expressing it in a balanced chemical equation based on the law of conservation of mass, and distinguishing chemical reactions according to the energy associated with","3. Listing the different forms of energy associated with chemical reactions (absorbed, released), and providing examples thereof.","What type of energy is associated with a chemical reaction where heat is given off to the surroundings?","Energy absorbed from the surroundings","Energy released to the surroundings","Energy stored in the chemical bonds of the reactants","Energy transferred from one object to another through conduction","Energy released to the surroundings","In an exothermic reaction, energy is released to the surroundings in the form of heat.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-of-is-an-5c193252","Science G9: Understanding how a chemical reaction occurs - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-8 Understanding how a chemical reaction occurs, expressing it in a balanced chemical equation based on the law of conservation of mass, and distinguishing chemical reactions according to the energy associated with","2. Describing a chemical reaction using a balanced verbal and symbolic chemical equation and apply the law of conservation of mass to different chemical reactions.","Which of the following is an example of a chemical reaction that absorbs energy from the surroundings?","Combustion of gasoline in a car engine","Photosynthesis in plants","Decomposition of organic matter","Respiration in animals","Photosynthesis in plants","Photosynthesis is an endothermic reaction that absorbs energy from sunlight to convert carbon dioxide and water into glucose and oxygen.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-primary-source-5ecf9e1f","Science G9: Understanding how a chemical reaction occurs - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-8 Understanding how a chemical reaction occurs, expressing it in a balanced chemical equation based on the law of conservation of mass, and distinguishing chemical reactions according to the energy associated with","2. Describing a chemical reaction using a balanced verbal and symbolic chemical equation and apply the law of conservation of mass to different chemical reactions.","What is the primary source of energy for the chemical reaction of photosynthesis?","Chemical energy stored in glucose","Thermal energy from the surroundings","Electromagnetic radiation from the sun","Kinetic energy of moving objects","Electromagnetic radiation from the sun","Photosynthesis uses energy from sunlight to convert carbon dioxide and water into glucose and oxygen.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-of-is-an-f10872cf","Science G9: Understanding how a chemical reaction occurs - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-8 Understanding how a chemical reaction occurs, expressing it in a balanced chemical equation based on the law of conservation of mass, and distinguishing chemical reactions according to the energy associated with","3. Listing the different forms of energy associated with chemical reactions (absorbed, released), and providing examples thereof.","Which of the following is an example of a chemical reaction that releases energy to the surroundings?","Melting of ice","Boiling of water","Combustion of propane","Freezing of water","Combustion of propane","The combustion of propane is an exothermic reaction that releases energy to the surroundings in the form of heat and light.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-type-of-energy-2cc8e2e4","Science G9: Understanding how a chemical reaction occurs - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-4-3Understanding specific heat and the factors affecting it.","1. Comparing between the characteristics of the body's kinetic energy, potential energy, and the factors affecting each type. And provides real-world examples of conversion between the two","What type of energy is associated with the motion of particles in a substance?","Potential energy","Kinetic energy","Thermal energy","Electromagnetic energy","Kinetic energy","Kinetic energy is the energy associated with the motion of particles in a substance.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-of-chemical-reactions-51d9b8c8","Science G9: Understanding how a chemical reaction occurs - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-8 Understanding how a chemical reaction occurs, expressing it in a balanced chemical equation based on the law of conservation of mass, and distinguishing chemical reactions according to the energy associated with","2. Describing a chemical reaction using a balanced verbal and symbolic chemical equation and apply the law of conservation of mass to different chemical reactions.","Which of the following chemical reactions is an example of an endothermic process?","Burning of gasoline in a car engine","Decomposition of ammonia","Melting of ice","Respiration in animals","Melting of ice","The melting of ice is an endothermic process that absorbs energy from the surroundings to change the state of water from solid to liquid.","/image-source/sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-of-chemical-reactions-51d9b8c8.png","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-type-of-chemical-ed36ee06","Science G9: Understanding how a chemical reaction occurs - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-8 Understanding how a chemical reaction occurs, expressing it in a balanced chemical equation based on the law of conservation of mass, and distinguishing chemical reactions according to the energy associated with","4. Distinguishing between an endothermic reaction and an exothermic reaction, providing examples of each, explaining how to express them in a chemical equation.","Which type of chemical reaction absorbs energy from the surroundings and stores it in the chemical bonds of the products?","Exothermic reaction","Endothermic reaction","Reversible reaction","Irreversible reaction","Endothermic reaction","An endothermic reaction absorbs energy from the surroundings and stores it in the chemical bonds of the products.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-how-can-you-4411f6a7","Science G9: Understanding how a chemical reaction occurs - Set 4","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-8 Understanding how a chemical reaction occurs, expressing it in a balanced chemical equation based on the law of conservation of mass, and distinguishing chemical reactions according to the energy associated with","4. Distinguishing between an endothermic reaction and an exothermic reaction, providing examples of each, explaining how to express them in a chemical equation.","How can you represent an exothermic reaction in a chemical equation?","The reaction is represented by a minus sign in front of the energy term","The reaction is represented by a plus sign in front of the energy term","The reaction is represented by an arrow pointing to the products","The reaction is represented by an arrow pointing to the reactants","The reaction is represented by a plus sign in front of the energy term","An exothermic reaction is represented by a plus sign in front of the energy term in a chemical equation.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-type-of-chemical-0c4f6e2b","Science G9: Understanding how a chemical reaction occurs - Set 4","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-8 Understanding how a chemical reaction occurs, expressing it in a balanced chemical equation based on the law of conservation of mass, and distinguishing chemical reactions according to the energy associated with","3. Listing the different forms of energy associated with chemical reactions (absorbed, released), and providing examples thereof.","What type of chemical reaction absorbs heat energy from the surroundings?","A chemical reaction that releases heat energy into the surroundings","A chemical reaction that involves the transfer of electrons","A chemical reaction that involves the change of state from solid to liquid","A chemical reaction that absorbs heat energy from the surroundings","A chemical reaction that absorbs heat energy from the surroundings","An endothermic reaction is a type of chemical reaction that absorbs heat energy from the surroundings.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-type-of-chemical-02551fe3","Science G9: Understanding how a chemical reaction occurs - Set 4","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-8 Understanding how a chemical reaction occurs, expressing it in a balanced chemical equation based on the law of conservation of mass, and distinguishing chemical reactions according to the energy associated with","3. Listing the different forms of energy associated with chemical reactions (absorbed, released), and providing examples thereof.","What type of chemical reaction involves the transfer of electrons?","A chemical reaction that releases heat energy into the surroundings","A chemical reaction that absorbs heat energy from the surroundings","A chemical reaction that involves the change of state from solid to liquid","A chemical reaction that involves the transfer of electrons","A chemical reaction that involves the transfer of electrons","A chemical reaction that involves the transfer of electrons is an oxidation-reduction reaction.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-law-of-6b6a1375","Science G9: Understanding how a chemical reaction occurs - Set 4","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-8 Understanding how a chemical reaction occurs, expressing it in a balanced chemical equation based on the law of conservation of mass, and distinguishing chemical reactions according to the energy associated with","2. Describing a chemical reaction using a balanced verbal and symbolic chemical equation and apply the law of conservation of mass to different chemical reactions.","What is the law of conservation of mass in relation to chemical equations?","The law of conservation of mass states that matter is created in chemical equations","The law of conservation of mass states that matter is destroyed in chemical equations","The law of conservation of mass states that matter is neither created nor destroyed in chemical equations","The law of conservation of mass states that energy is created in chemical equations","The law of conservation of mass states that matter is neither created nor destroyed in chemical equations","The law of conservation of mass states that matter is neither created nor destroyed in chemical equations.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-how-can-you-1b16626e","Science G9: Understanding how a chemical reaction occurs - Set 4","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-8 Understanding how a chemical reaction occurs, expressing it in a balanced chemical equation based on the law of conservation of mass, and distinguishing chemical reactions according to the energy associated with","4. Distinguishing between an endothermic reaction and an exothermic reaction, providing examples of each, explaining how to express them in a chemical equation.","How can you represent an endothermic reaction in a chemical equation?","The reaction is represented by a plus sign in front of the energy term","The reaction is represented by a minus sign in front of the energy term","The reaction is represented by an arrow pointing to the products","The reaction is represented by an arrow pointing to the reactants","The reaction is represented by a plus sign in front of the energy term","An endothermic reaction is represented by a minus sign in front of the energy term in a chemical equation.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-type-of-chemical-3dffc03b","Science G9: Understanding how a chemical reaction occurs - Set 4","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-8 Understanding how a chemical reaction occurs, expressing it in a balanced chemical equation based on the law of conservation of mass, and distinguishing chemical reactions according to the energy associated with","3. Listing the different forms of energy associated with chemical reactions (absorbed, released), and providing examples thereof.","What type of chemical reaction releases heat energy into the surroundings?","A chemical reaction that absorbs heat energy from the surroundings","A chemical reaction that releases heat energy into the surroundings","A chemical reaction that involves the transfer of electrons","A chemical reaction that involves the change of state from solid to liquid","A chemical reaction that releases heat energy into the surroundings","An exothermic reaction is a type of chemical reaction that releases heat energy into the surroundings.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-an-example-944c1b2c","Science G9: Understanding how a chemical reaction occurs - Set 4","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-8 Understanding how a chemical reaction occurs, expressing it in a balanced chemical equation based on the law of conservation of mass, and distinguishing chemical reactions according to the energy associated with","4. Distinguishing between an endothermic reaction and an exothermic reaction, providing examples of each, explaining how to express them in a chemical equation.","What is an example of an exothermic reaction?","The reaction between sodium and water","The melting of ice","The combustion of gasoline","The reaction between calcium carbonate and hydrochloric acid","The combustion of gasoline","Endothermic reactions are represented by a plus sign because energy is absorbed from the surroundings.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-an-example-81805129","Science G9: Understanding how a chemical reaction occurs - Set 4","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-8 Understanding how a chemical reaction occurs, expressing it in a balanced chemical equation based on the law of conservation of mass, and distinguishing chemical reactions according to the energy associated with","4. Distinguishing between an endothermic reaction and an exothermic reaction, providing examples of each, explaining how to express them in a chemical equation.","What is an example of an endothermic reaction?","The combustion of gasoline","The reaction between sodium and water","The melting of ice","The reaction between calcium carbonate and hydrochloric acid","The melting of ice","The melting of ice is an example of an endothermic reaction.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-difference-an-8cba1b89","Science G9: Understanding how a chemical reaction occurs - Set 4","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-8 Understanding how a chemical reaction occurs, expressing it in a balanced chemical equation based on the law of conservation of mass, and distinguishing chemical reactions according to the energy associated with","4. Distinguishing between an endothermic reaction and an exothermic reaction, providing examples of each, explaining how to express them in a chemical equation.","What is the difference between an endothermic reaction and an exothermic reaction?","An endothermic reaction releases heat energy, while an exothermic reaction absorbs heat energy","An endothermic reaction absorbs heat energy, while an exothermic reaction releases heat energy","An endothermic reaction involves the transfer of electrons, while an exothermic reaction involves the change of state","An endothermic reaction involves the change of state, while an exothermic reaction involves the transfer of electrons","An endothermic reaction absorbs heat energy, while an exothermic reaction releases heat energy","An endothermic reaction absorbs heat energy, while an exothermic reaction releases heat energy.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-law-of-2cfe511f","Science G9: Understanding how a chemical reaction occurs - Set 4","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-8 Understanding how a chemical reaction occurs, expressing it in a balanced chemical equation based on the law of conservation of mass, and distinguishing chemical reactions according to the energy associated with","2. Describing a chemical reaction using a balanced verbal and symbolic chemical equation and apply the law of conservation of mass to different chemical reactions.","What is the law of conservation of mass in relation to chemical reactions?","The law of conservation of mass states that matter is created in chemical reactions","The law of conservation of mass states that matter is destroyed in chemical reactions","The law of conservation of mass states that matter is neither created nor destroyed in chemical reactions","The law of conservation of mass states that energy is created in chemical reactions","The law of conservation of mass states that matter is neither created nor destroyed in chemical reactions","The law of conservation of mass states that matter is neither created nor destroyed in chemical reactions.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-happens-to-acceleration-09750c5d","Science G9: Understanding Newton's second law theoretically and - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-5 Understanding Newton›s second law theoretically and graphically and determining the relationship between body acceleration and the factors affecting","5. Defining the concept of weight, differentiating between the weight and the mass. Calculating the weight of an object mathematically.","What happens to the acceleration of an object when the force acting on it is doubled and its mass is halved?","The acceleration decreases","The acceleration remains the same","The acceleration increases","The acceleration becomes four times greater","The acceleration becomes four times greater","According to Newton's second law, a = F/m. Doubling the force and halving the mass gives a new acceleration of 2F divided by m/2, which is 4F/m, so the acceleration becomes four times greater.","/image-source/sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-happens-to-acceleration-09750c5d.png","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-happens-to-acceleration-2979cfc7","Science G9: Understanding Newton's second law theoretically and - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-5 Understanding Newton›s second law theoretically and graphically and determining the relationship between body acceleration and the factors affecting","5. Defining the concept of weight, differentiating between the weight and the mass. Calculating the weight of an object mathematically.","What happens to the acceleration of an object when its mass is increased and the force acting on it remains constant?","The acceleration increases","The acceleration remains the same","The acceleration decreases","The acceleration becomes zero","The acceleration decreases","According to Newton's second law, if the mass of an object is increased and the force remains constant, the acceleration will decrease because force is divided by mass to get acceleration.","/image-source/sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-happens-to-acceleration-2979cfc7.png","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-a-force-of-082ab46d","Science G9: Understanding Newton's second law theoretically and - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-5 Understanding Newton›s second law theoretically and graphically and determining the relationship between body acceleration and the factors affecting","5. Defining the concept of weight, differentiating between the weight and the mass. Calculating the weight of an object mathematically.","A force of 10 N is applied to a 2 kg object. What is its acceleration?","The acceleration is 5 m/s^2","The acceleration is 10 m/s^2","The acceleration is 2 m/s^2","The acceleration is 20 m/s^2","The acceleration is 5 m/s^2","According to Newton's second law, force is equal to mass multiplied by acceleration. So, acceleration is equal to force divided by mass, which is 10 N / 2 kg = 5 m/s^2.","/image-source/sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-a-force-of-082ab46d.png","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-relationship-force-17c31f2a","Science G9: Understanding Newton's second law theoretically and - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-5 Understanding Newton›s second law theoretically and graphically and determining the relationship between body acceleration and the factors affecting","5. Defining the concept of weight, differentiating between the weight and the mass. Calculating the weight of an object mathematically.","What is the relationship between force and acceleration according to Newton's second law?","Force and acceleration are inversely proportional","Force and acceleration are directly proportional","Force and acceleration are not related","Force and acceleration are proportional to the square of the mass","Force and acceleration are directly proportional","Newton's second law states that force is equal to the mass of an object multiplied by its acceleration, so force and acceleration are directly proportional.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-if-mass-of-1b012be7","Science G9: Understanding Newton's second law theoretically and - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-5 Understanding Newton›s second law theoretically and graphically and determining the relationship between body acceleration and the factors affecting","5. Defining the concept of weight, differentiating between the weight and the mass. Calculating the weight of an object mathematically.","If the mass of an object is increased, what happens to its acceleration when a constant force is applied?","The acceleration increases","The acceleration decreases","The acceleration remains the same","The acceleration becomes zero","The acceleration decreases","According to Newton's second law, if the mass of an object is increased and the force remains constant, the acceleration will decrease because force is divided by mass to get acceleration.","/image-source/sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-if-mass-of-1b012be7.png","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-effect-of-b88f663c","Science G9: Understanding Newton's second law theoretically and - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-5 Understanding Newton›s second law theoretically and graphically and determining the relationship between body acceleration and the factors affecting","4. Defining the force of gravity and its effects on bodies and providing examples.","What is the effect of increasing the force on an object while keeping its mass constant?","The acceleration decreases","The acceleration remains the same","The acceleration increases","The object will not move","The acceleration increases","According to Newton's second law, if the force on an object is increased while keeping its mass constant, the acceleration will increase because force is directly proportional to acceleration.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-a-car-is-9be26428","Science G9: Understanding Newton's second law theoretically and - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-4 Understanding the concept of moment of inertia and reformulating Newton›s first law based on it.","2. Formulating Newton's first law according to the moment of inertia.","A car is moving with a constant velocity. What is the net force acting on the car?","A large force in the direction of motion","A small force in the direction of motion","A large force opposite to the direction of motion","Zero net force","Zero net force","When an object is moving with a constant velocity, the net force acting on it is zero, according to Newton's first law.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-a-bicycle-a-df38de9d","Science G9: Understanding Newton's second law theoretically and - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-5 Understanding Newton›s second law theoretically and graphically and determining the relationship between body acceleration and the factors affecting","3. Calculating the value of the acceleration of the body affected by the resultant force mathematically.","A bicycle and a truck have the same force applied to them. If the bicycle has a smaller mass than the truck, which one will have a greater acceleration?","The truck will have a greater acceleration","The bicycle will have a greater acceleration","Both will have the same acceleration","The acceleration is not related to mass","The bicycle will have a greater acceleration","According to Newton's second law, the bicycle will have a greater acceleration because it has a smaller mass, and force is divided by mass to get acceleration.","/image-source/sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-a-bicycle-a-df38de9d.png","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-relationship-mass-e01c9f38","Science G9: Understanding Newton's second law theoretically and - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-5 Understanding Newton›s second law theoretically and graphically and determining the relationship between body acceleration and the factors affecting","3. Calculating the value of the acceleration of the body affected by the resultant force mathematically.","What is the relationship between mass and acceleration when a constant force is applied?","Mass and acceleration are directly proportional","Mass and acceleration are inversely proportional","Mass and acceleration are not related","Mass and acceleration are proportional to the square of the force","Mass and acceleration are inversely proportional","According to Newton's second law, mass and acceleration are inversely proportional because force is divided by mass to get acceleration.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-if-two-objects-742bfa4b","Science G9: Understanding Newton's second law theoretically and - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-5 Understanding Newton›s second law theoretically and graphically and determining the relationship between body acceleration and the factors affecting","5. Defining the concept of weight, differentiating between the weight and the mass. Calculating the weight of an object mathematically.","If two objects have the same mass, which one will have a greater acceleration when a greater force is applied to it?","The object with the smaller force will have a greater acceleration","The object with the greater force will have a greater acceleration","Both objects will have the same acceleration","The acceleration is not related to force","The object with the greater force will have a greater acceleration","According to Newton's second law, the object with the greater force will have a greater acceleration because force is directly proportional to acceleration.","/image-source/sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-if-two-objects-742bfa4b.png","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-force-pulls-e43a3217","Science G9: Understanding Newton's second law theoretically and - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-3 Understanding the concept of friction force, its types, and how it affects the movement of objects.","3. Listing types of friction (static, sliding, and rolling), distinguishing each type, and providing examples thereof.","What is the force that pulls objects towards each other?","Normal force","Frictional force","Gravitational force","Magnetic force","Gravitational force","Gravitational force is the force that attracts objects with mass towards each other.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-effect-of-11d00f56","Science G9: Understanding Newton's second law theoretically and - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-5 Understanding Newton›s second law theoretically and graphically and determining the relationship between body acceleration and the factors affecting","2. Determining the relationship between the acceleration of the body and the factors affecting it, based on Newton's second law, theoretically and mathematically.","What is the effect of gravity on a falling object?","It slows down the object","It speeds up the object","It keeps the object at a constant velocity","It changes the direction of the object","It speeds up the object","Gravity pulls objects towards the ground, causing them to accelerate and speed up as they fall.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-happens-to-force-0a1fd748","Science G9: Understanding Newton's second law theoretically and - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-5 Understanding Newton›s second law theoretically and graphically and determining the relationship between body acceleration and the factors affecting","4. Defining the force of gravity and its effects on bodies and providing examples.","What happens to the force of gravity between two objects as the distance between them increases?","The force of gravity increases","The force of gravity remains the same","The force of gravity decreases","The force of gravity becomes zero","The force of gravity decreases","As the distance between two objects increases, the force of gravity between them decreases.","/image-source/sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-happens-to-force-0a1fd748.png","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-of-is-an-3d6182e8","Science G9: Understanding Newton's second law theoretically and - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-5 Understanding Newton›s second law theoretically and graphically and determining the relationship between body acceleration and the factors affecting","4. Defining the force of gravity and its effects on bodies and providing examples.","Which of the following is an example of the effect of gravity on a body?","A car moving at a constant velocity","A ball rolling up a hill","A rock falling towards the ground","A bicycle turning a corner","A rock falling towards the ground","Gravity is the force that pulls the rock towards the ground, causing it to fall.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-force-of-88d3d7be","Science G9: Understanding Newton's second law theoretically and - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-4-4 Distinguishing between the kinetic and potential energy of a body and the factors affecting them.","4. Predicting the relationship between the potential energy and the height of the object above the Earth's surface.","What is the force of gravity on an object at the surface of the Earth?","It depends on the object's velocity","It depends on the object's mass and the Earth's mass","It depends on the object's shape and size","It is always zero","It depends on the object's mass and the Earth's mass","The force of gravity on an object at the surface of the Earth depends on the object's mass and the Earth's mass.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-relationship-mass-5961a083","Science G9: Understanding Newton's second law theoretically and - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-5 Understanding Newton›s second law theoretically and graphically and determining the relationship between body acceleration and the factors affecting","5. Defining the concept of weight, differentiating between the weight and the mass. Calculating the weight of an object mathematically.","What is the relationship between mass and gravity?","Mass decreases as gravity increases","Mass increases as gravity decreases","Mass and gravity are unrelated","Mass and gravity are directly proportional","Mass and gravity are directly proportional","The more massive an object is, the stronger its gravitational pull will be.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-how-force-of-7f39d5ca","Science G9: Understanding Newton's second law theoretically and - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-5 Understanding Newton›s second law theoretically and graphically and determining the relationship between body acceleration and the factors affecting","4. Defining the force of gravity and its effects on bodies and providing examples.","How does the force of gravity affect the motion of a projectile?","It causes the projectile to move in a straight line","It causes the projectile to move in a circular path","It causes the projectile to accelerate downwards","It has no effect on the projectile's motion","It causes the projectile to accelerate downwards","The force of gravity pulls the projectile downwards, causing it to accelerate and follow a curved path.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-difference-mass-e8b1844f","Science G9: Understanding Newton's second law theoretically and - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-5 Understanding Newton›s second law theoretically and graphically and determining the relationship between body acceleration and the factors affecting","5. Defining the concept of weight, differentiating between the weight and the mass. Calculating the weight of an object mathematically.","What is the difference between mass and weight?","Mass is the force of gravity, while weight is the object's resistance to motion","Mass is the object's resistance to motion, while weight is the force of gravity","Mass is a measure of an object's amount of matter, while weight is the force of gravity acting on it","Mass and weight are the same thing","Mass is a measure of an object's amount of matter, while weight is the force of gravity acting on it","Mass is a measure of an object's amount of matter, while weight is the force of gravity acting on it.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-how-mass-of-3e7da16e","Science G9: Understanding Newton's second law theoretically and - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-5 Understanding Newton›s second law theoretically and graphically and determining the relationship between body acceleration and the factors affecting","4. Defining the force of gravity and its effects on bodies and providing examples.","How does the mass of an object affect its weight?","The mass of an object has no effect on its weight","The mass of an object is inversely proportional to its weight","The mass of an object is directly proportional to its weight","The mass of an object is equal to its weight","The mass of an object is directly proportional to its weight","The weight of an object is the force of gravity acting on it, and it is directly proportional to the object's mass.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-relationship-force-76561ef0","Science G9: Understanding Newton's second law theoretically and - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-5 Understanding Newton›s second law theoretically and graphically and determining the relationship between body acceleration and the factors affecting","4. Defining the force of gravity and its effects on bodies and providing examples.","What is the relationship between the force of gravity and the acceleration of an object?","The force of gravity is inversely proportional to the acceleration","The force of gravity is directly proportional to the acceleration","The force of gravity is unrelated to the acceleration","The force of gravity is equal to the acceleration","The force of gravity is directly proportional to the acceleration","According to Newton's second law, the force of gravity is directly proportional to the acceleration of an object.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-you-kick-a-22cd9aaa","Science G9: Understanding Newton's third law and calculating - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-6 Understanding Newton›s third law and calculating the value of mutual forces mathematically based on it.","1. Explaining the concept of electric current theoretically and by drawing.","When you kick a soccer ball, what force is exerted on the ball?","A forward force exerted by your foot on the ball","A backward force exerted by the ball on your foot","A forward force exerted by the ball on your foot","Normal force","Normal force","The hill exerts a normal force perpendicular to its surface on the skateboard.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-newton-s-1d56081c","Science G9: Understanding Newton's third law and calculating - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-6 Understanding Newton›s third law and calculating the value of mutual forces mathematically based on it.","1. Explaining the concept of electric current theoretically and by drawing.","What is Newton's third law of motion?","For every action, there is an equal and opposite reaction","For every action, there is a greater reaction","For every action, there is a lesser reaction","For every action, there is no reaction","For every action, there is an equal and opposite reaction","Newton's third law states that for every action, there is an equal and opposite reaction, which means that the forces exerted by two objects on each other are always equal in magnitude and opposite in direction.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-a-bicycle-accelerates-2552d484","Science G9: Understanding Newton's third law and calculating - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-6 Understanding Newton›s third law and calculating the value of mutual forces mathematically based on it.","1. Explaining the concept of electric current theoretically and by drawing.","A bicycle accelerates down the road. What force is exerted on the bicycle?","A forward force exerted by the road on the bicycle","A backward force exerted by the bicycle on the road","A forward force exerted by the bicycle on the road","No force is exerted on the bicycle","A forward force exerted by the road on the bicycle","According to Newton's third law, the force exerted by the road on the bicycle is equal and opposite to the force exerted by the bicycle on the road. Since the bicycle is accelerating forward, the road exerts a forward force on the bicycle, and the bicycle exerts a backward force on the road.","/image-source/sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-a-bicycle-accelerates-2552d484.png","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-you-throw-a-a5fc8366","Science G9: Understanding Newton's third law and calculating - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-6 Understanding Newton›s third law and calculating the value of mutual forces mathematically based on it.","1. Explaining the concept of electric current theoretically and by drawing.","When you throw a ball to a friend, what force is exerted on the ball?","A forward force exerted by your hand on the ball","A backward force exerted by the ball on your hand","A forward force exerted by the ball on your hand","No force is exerted on the ball","A forward force exerted by your hand on the ball","According to Newton's third law, the force exerted by your hand on the ball is equal and opposite to the force exerted by the ball on your hand. Since you are throwing the ball forward, your hand exerts a forward force on the ball, and the ball exerts a backward force on your hand.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-a-rocket-blasts-742b85ce","Science G9: Understanding Newton's third law and calculating - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-6 Understanding Newton›s third law and calculating the value of mutual forces mathematically based on it.","1. Explaining the concept of electric current theoretically and by drawing.","A rocket blasts off into space. What force is exerted on the rocket?","A downward force exerted by the Earth on the rocket","An upward force exerted by the exhaust gases on the rocket","A downward force exerted by the exhaust gases on the rocket","No force is exerted on the rocket","An upward force exerted by the exhaust gases on the rocket","According to Newton's third law, the force exerted by the exhaust gases on the rocket is equal and opposite to the force exerted by the rocket on the exhaust gases. Since the exhaust gases are expelled downward, the rocket exerts a downward force on the gases, and the gases exert an upward force on the rocket.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-you-push-on-e5f5e9cc","Science G9: Understanding Newton's third law and calculating - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-6 Understanding Newton›s third law and calculating the value of mutual forces mathematically based on it.","1. Explaining the concept of electric current theoretically and by drawing.","When you push on a wall, what happens to the force you exert?","The force you exert is absorbed by the wall","The force you exert is reflected back to you","The force you exert is equal and opposite to the force exerted by the wall on you","The force you exert is greater than the force exerted by the wall on you","The force you exert is equal and opposite to the force exerted by the wall on you","According to Newton's third law, the force you exert on the wall is equal and opposite to the force exerted by the wall on you. This means that the wall exerts an equal force on you in the opposite direction.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-you-pull-on-0b18cbe1","Science G9: Understanding Newton's third law and calculating - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-6 Understanding Newton›s third law and calculating the value of mutual forces mathematically based on it.","1. Explaining the concept of electric current theoretically and by drawing.","When you pull on a rope, what force is exerted on the rope?","A force exerted by the rope on you in the same direction as the pull","A force exerted by the rope on you in the opposite direction of the pull","A force exerted by you on the rope in the same direction as the pull","No force is exerted on the rope","A force exerted by you on the rope in the same direction as the pull","According to Newton's third law, the force exerted by you on the rope is equal and opposite to the force exerted by the rope on you. Since you are pulling on the rope, you exert a force on the rope in the same direction as the pull, and the rope exerts a force on you in the opposite direction.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-a-boat-moves-4dd9f9b1","Science G9: Understanding Newton's third law and calculating - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-6 Understanding Newton›s third law and calculating the value of mutual forces mathematically based on it.","1. Explaining the concept of electric current theoretically and by drawing.","A boat moves through the water. What force is exerted on the boat?","A forward force exerted by the water on the boat","A backward force exerted by the boat on the water","A forward force exerted by the boat on the water","No force is exerted on the boat","A forward force exerted by the water on the boat","According to Newton's third law, the force exerted by the water on the boat is equal and opposite to the force exerted by the boat on the water. Since the boat is moving forward, the water exerts a forward force on the boat, and the boat exerts a backward force on the water.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-a-skateboard-rolls-ecd36fcf","Science G9: Understanding Newton's third law and calculating - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-6 Understanding Newton›s third law and calculating the value of mutual forces mathematically based on it.","1. Explaining the concept of electric current theoretically and by drawing.","A skateboard rolls down a hill. What force is exerted on the skateboard?","A downward force exerted by the hill on the skateboard","An upward force exerted by the skateboard on the hill","A downward force exerted by the skateboard on the hill","No force is exerted on the skateboard","A downward force exerted by the hill on the skateboard","According to Newton's third law, the force exerted by the hill on the skateboard is equal and opposite to the force exerted by the skateboard on the hill. Since the skateboard is rolling down the hill, the hill exerts a downward force on the skateboard, and the skateboard exerts an upward force on the hill.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-a-car-accelerates-cf471b35","Science G9: Understanding Newton's third law and calculating - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-6 Understanding Newton›s third law and calculating the value of mutual forces mathematically based on it.","1. Explaining the concept of electric current theoretically and by drawing.","A car accelerates down the road. What force is exerted on the road?","A forward force exerted by the road on the car","A backward force exerted by the car on the road","A forward force exerted by the car on the road","No force is exerted on the road","A backward force exerted by the car on the road","According to Newton's third law, the force exerted by the car on the road is equal and opposite to the force exerted by the road on the car. Since the car is accelerating forward, the road exerts a forward force on the car, and the car exerts a backward force on the road.","/image-source/sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-a-car-accelerates-cf471b35.png","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-how-newton-s-f306fcd8","Science G9: Understanding Newton's third law and calculating - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-6 Understanding Newton›s third law and calculating the value of mutual forces mathematically based on it.","1. Explaining the concept of electric current theoretically and by drawing.","How does Newton's third law apply to the motion of a car?","The car exerts a force on the ground, and the ground exerts an equal and opposite force on the car","The car exerts a force on the air, and the air exerts an equal and opposite force on the car","The car exerts a force on the road, and the road exerts a lesser force on the car","The car exerts a force on the road, and the road exerts a greater force on the car","The car exerts a force on the ground, and the ground exerts an equal and opposite force on the car","According to Newton's third law, when a car is in motion, it exerts a force on the ground, and the ground exerts an equal and opposite force on the car. This is what propels the car forward, as the force exerted by the car on the ground is equal and opposite to the force exerted by the ground on the car.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-concept-of-fd1ba5ec","Science G9: Understanding Newton's third law and calculating - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-6 Understanding Newton›s third law and calculating the value of mutual forces mathematically based on it.","2. Formulating Newton's third law and giving practical daily life examples. Explaining some phenomena in light of it, such as weightlessness and free fall, theoretically and graphically","What is the concept of weightlessness?","The state of being in a location with no gravity","The state of being in a location with very strong gravity","The state of being in a location with equal and opposite forces","The state of being in a location with no forces acting on an object","The state of being in a location with equal and opposite forces","Weightlessness occurs when an object is in a state of free fall, where the force of gravity acting on the object is equal and opposite to the force exerted by the object on the ground. This results in a net force of zero, causing the object to feel weightless.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-newton-s-feb3ef13","Science G9: Understanding Newton's third law and calculating - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-2 Understanding the concept of momentum and the law of momentum conservation.","1. Stating Newton's first law of motion and providing examples thereof.","What is Newton's third law of motion?","For every action, there is an equal and opposite reaction","For every action, there is a greater reaction","For every action, there is a lesser reaction","For every action, there is no reaction","For every action, there is an equal and opposite reaction","Newton's third law states that for every action, there is an equal and opposite reaction. This means that when object A exerts a force on object B, object B will exert an equal and opposite force on object A.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-relationship-forces-d2b76844","Science G9: Understanding Newton's third law and calculating - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-6 Understanding Newton›s third law and calculating the value of mutual forces mathematically based on it.","1. Explaining the concept of electric current theoretically and by drawing.","What is the relationship between the forces exerted by two objects on each other?","The forces are equal and opposite","The forces are equal but in the same direction","The forces are unequal and opposite","The forces are unequal and in the same direction","The forces are equal and opposite","According to Newton's third law, the forces exerted by two objects on each other are equal and opposite. This means that the magnitude of the forces is the same, but the direction is opposite.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-an-example-2d4d7ee4","Science G9: Understanding Newton's third law and calculating - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-6 Understanding Newton›s third law and calculating the value of mutual forces mathematically based on it.","2. Formulating Newton's third law and giving practical daily life examples. Explaining some phenomena in light of it, such as weightlessness and free fall, theoretically and graphically","What is an example of Newton's third law in everyday life?","A car accelerating down the road","A person throwing a ball upwards","A rocket blasting off into space","All of the above","All of the above","All of the above options are examples of Newton's third law in everyday life. When a car accelerates, the ground exerts an equal and opposite force on the car. When a person throws a ball upwards, the ball exerts an equal and opposite force on the person's hand. When a rocket blasts off into space, the rocket exerts an equal and opposite force on the ground.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-concept-of-b28f88a3","Science G9: Understanding Newton's third law and calculating - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-6 Understanding Newton›s third law and calculating the value of mutual forces mathematically based on it.","1. Explaining the concept of electric current theoretically and by drawing.","What is the concept of free fall?","The state of being in a location with no gravity","The state of being in a location with very strong gravity","The state of an object falling towards the ground with no air resistance","The state of an object falling towards the ground with very strong air resistance","The state of an object falling towards the ground with no air resistance","Free fall occurs when an object is falling towards the ground with no air resistance, resulting in a constant acceleration due to gravity. This is an example of Newton's third law, where the force of gravity acting on the object is equal and opposite to the force exerted by the object on the ground.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-how-do-you-601960da","Science G9: Understanding Newton's third law and calculating - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-6 Understanding Newton›s third law and calculating the value of mutual forces mathematically based on it.","1. Explaining the concept of electric current theoretically and by drawing.","How do you calculate the value of mutual forces mathematically based on Newton's third law?","By using the formula F = ma","By using the formula F = -F","By using the formula F = (m1 * m2) / r^2","By using the formula F = 0","By using the formula F = -F","According to Newton's third law, the forces exerted by two objects on each other are equal and opposite. Mathematically, this can be represented by the formula F = -F, where F is the force exerted by one object on the other, and -F is the equal and opposite force exerted by the other object.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-an-example-2cd81bb1","Science G9: Understanding Newton's third law and calculating - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-6 Understanding Newton›s third law and calculating the value of mutual forces mathematically based on it.","2. Formulating Newton's third law and giving practical daily life examples. Explaining some phenomena in light of it, such as weightlessness and free fall, theoretically and graphically","What is an example of Newton's third law in a sports context?","A tennis player hitting a tennis ball","A basketball player shooting a basketball","A soccer player kicking a soccer ball","All of the above","All of the above","All of the above options are examples of Newton's third law in a sports context. When a tennis player hits a tennis ball, the ball exerts an equal and opposite force on the racket. When a basketball player shoots a basketball, the ball exerts an equal and opposite force on the player's hand. When a soccer player kicks a soccer ball, the ball exerts an equal and opposite force on the player's foot.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-how-newton-s-52ee5eba","Science G9: Understanding Newton's third law and calculating - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-2 Understanding the concept of momentum and the law of momentum conservation.","1. Stating Newton's first law of motion and providing examples thereof.","How does Newton's third law relate to the concept of friction?","Friction is a force that opposes motion, and Newton's third law states that for every action, there is an equal and opposite reaction","Friction is a force that opposes motion, and Newton's third law states that for every action, there is a greater reaction","Friction is a force that aids motion, and Newton's third law states that for every action, there is an equal and opposite reaction","Friction is a force that aids motion, and Newton's third law states that for every action, there is a lesser reaction","Friction is a force that opposes motion, and Newton's third law states that for every action, there is an equal and opposite reaction","Friction is a force that opposes motion, and according to Newton's third law, for every action, there is an equal and opposite reaction. This means that when an object is moving, the force of friction exerted by the surface on the object is equal and opposite to the force exerted by the object on the surface.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-difference-a-be8f98e3","Science G9: Understanding Newton's third law and calculating - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-6 Understanding Newton›s third law and calculating the value of mutual forces mathematically based on it.","1. Explaining the concept of electric current theoretically and by drawing.","What is the difference between an action force and a reaction force?","An action force is a force that causes motion, while a reaction force is a force that opposes motion","An action force is a force that opposes motion, while a reaction force is a force that causes motion","An action force is a force that causes motion, while a reaction force is a force that also causes motion","An action force is a force that opposes motion, while a reaction force is a force that also opposes motion","An action force is a force that causes motion, while a reaction force is a force that opposes motion","According to Newton's third law, an action force is a force that causes motion, while a reaction force is a force that opposes motion. The action force and reaction force are equal and opposite, meaning that they have the same magnitude but opposite directions.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-basic-unit-8b790ea1","Science G9: Understanding that the cell is the basic building unit of - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-1 Understanding that the cell is the basic building unit of living organisms, knowing some of the technologies that helped study them, and comparing single-celled and multi-cellular organisms.","3. Comparing unicellular and multicellular organisms and providing examples of each.","What is the basic unit of structure of living organisms?","A cell is the smallest unit of life","A molecule is the smallest unit of life","An organism is the smallest unit of life","A tissue is the smallest unit of life","A cell is the smallest unit of life","A cell is the smallest unit of life and the basic building block of all living organisms.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-main-purpose-a3f63980","Science G9: Understanding that the cell is the basic building unit of - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-1 Understanding that the cell is the basic building unit of living organisms, knowing some of the technologies that helped study them, and comparing single-celled and multi-cellular organisms.","1. Identifying the basic unit of structure of living organisms, stating the principles of the cell theory, recognizing the importance of technological tools (magnifying devices and microscopes), and explaining their role in identifying cells and their components.","What is the main purpose of using a microscope to study cells?","To see the entire cell","To see the components of the cell","To see the surrounding environment of the cell","To see the cell's DNA","To see the components of the cell","The main purpose of using a microscope to study cells is to see the components of the cell, such as the nucleus, mitochondria, and other organelles.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-main-difference-af886db3","Science G9: Understanding that the cell is the basic building unit of - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-1 Understanding that the cell is the basic building unit of living organisms, knowing some of the technologies that helped study them, and comparing single-celled and multi-cellular organisms.","3. Comparing unicellular and multicellular organisms and providing examples of each.","What is the main difference between a light microscope and an electron microscope?","A light microscope uses light, while an electron microscope uses electricity","A light microscope uses electricity, while an electron microscope uses light","A light microscope is more powerful than an electron microscope","An electron microscope is more powerful than a light microscope","An electron microscope is more powerful than a light microscope","The main difference between a light microscope and an electron microscope is that an electron microscope is more powerful than a light microscope, allowing us to see cells at a much smaller scale.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-main-advantage-377fc487","Science G9: Understanding that the cell is the basic building unit of - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-1 Understanding that the cell is the basic building unit of living organisms, knowing some of the technologies that helped study them, and comparing single-celled and multi-cellular organisms.","1. Identifying the basic unit of structure of living organisms, stating the principles of the cell theory, recognizing the importance of technological tools (magnifying devices and microscopes), and explaining their role in identifying cells and their components.","What is the main advantage of using a magnifying glass to study cells?","It is more expensive than a microscope","It is less expensive than a microscope","It is more difficult to use than a microscope","It allows us to see cells more clearly","It allows us to see cells more clearly","The main advantage of using a magnifying glass to study cells is that it allows us to see cells more clearly, although it is not as powerful as a microscope.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-are-three-main-d28e9dea","Science G9: Understanding that the cell is the basic building unit of - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-1 Understanding that the cell is the basic building unit of living organisms, knowing some of the technologies that helped study them, and comparing single-celled and multi-cellular organisms.","1. Identifying the basic unit of structure of living organisms, stating the principles of the cell theory, recognizing the importance of technological tools (magnifying devices and microscopes), and explaining their role in identifying cells and their components.","What are the three main principles of the cell theory?","All living things are made up of cells","Cells are the basic units of life","All cells come from pre-existing cells","Cells are the smallest units of life","All cells come from pre-existing cells","The three main principles of the cell theory are: 1) All living things are made up of cells, 2) Cells are the basic units of life, and 3) All cells come from pre-existing cells.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-main-difference-2395cfd8","Science G9: Understanding that the cell is the basic building unit of - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-1 Understanding that the cell is the basic building unit of living organisms, knowing some of the technologies that helped study them, and comparing single-celled and multi-cellular organisms.","3. Comparing unicellular and multicellular organisms and providing examples of each.","What is the main difference between single-celled and multi-celled organisms?","Single-celled organisms have a fixed shape","Multi-celled organisms have a fixed shape","Single-celled organisms have one cell, while multi-celled organisms have many cells","Multi-celled organisms have one cell, while single-celled organisms have many cells","Single-celled organisms have one cell, while multi-celled organisms have many cells","The main difference between single-celled and multi-celled organisms is that single-celled organisms have one cell, while multi-celled organisms have many cells.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-purpose-of-3f23b326","Science G9: Understanding that the cell is the basic building unit of - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-1 Understanding that the cell is the basic building unit of living organisms, knowing some of the technologies that helped study them, and comparing single-celled and multi-cellular organisms.","1. Identifying the basic unit of structure of living organisms, stating the principles of the cell theory, recognizing the importance of technological tools (magnifying devices and microscopes), and explaining their role in identifying cells and their components.","What is the purpose of a microscope in studying cells?","To see the entire cell","To see the components of the cell","To see the surrounding environment of the cell","To see the cell's DNA","To see the components of the cell","The purpose of a microscope in studying cells is to see the components of the cell, such as the nucleus, mitochondria, and other organelles.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-main-purpose-f29878cc","Science G9: Understanding that the cell is the basic building unit of - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-1 Understanding that the cell is the basic building unit of living organisms, knowing some of the technologies that helped study them, and comparing single-celled and multi-cellular organisms.","1. Identifying the basic unit of structure of living organisms, stating the principles of the cell theory, recognizing the importance of technological tools (magnifying devices and microscopes), and explaining their role in identifying cells and their components.","What is the main purpose of using a magnifying glass to study cells?","To see the entire cell","To see the components of the cell","To see the surrounding environment of the cell","To see the cell's DNA","To see the components of the cell","The main purpose of using a magnifying glass to study cells is to see the components of the cell, such as the nucleus, mitochondria, and other organelles.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-main-difference-a8eabf91","Science G9: Understanding that the cell is the basic building unit of - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-1 Understanding that the cell is the basic building unit of living organisms, knowing some of the technologies that helped study them, and comparing single-celled and multi-cellular organisms.","3. Comparing unicellular and multicellular organisms and providing examples of each.","What is the main difference between a compound microscope and a simple microscope?","A compound microscope uses light, while a simple microscope uses electricity","A compound microscope uses electricity, while a simple microscope uses light","A compound microscope is more powerful than a simple microscope","A simple microscope is more powerful than a compound microscope","A compound microscope is more powerful than a simple microscope","The main difference between a compound microscope and a simple microscope is that a compound microscope is more powerful than a simple microscope, allowing us to see cells at a much smaller scale.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-main-advantage-7dab41ca","Science G9: Understanding that the cell is the basic building unit of - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-1 Understanding that the cell is the basic building unit of living organisms, knowing some of the technologies that helped study them, and comparing single-celled and multi-cellular organisms.","1. Identifying the basic unit of structure of living organisms, stating the principles of the cell theory, recognizing the importance of technological tools (magnifying devices and microscopes), and explaining their role in identifying cells and their components.","What is the main advantage of using a microscope to study cells?","It is more expensive than a magnifying glass","It is less expensive than a magnifying glass","It is more difficult to use than a magnifying glass","It allows us to see cells more clearly","It allows us to see cells more clearly","The main advantage of using a microscope to study cells is that it allows us to see cells more clearly, although it is not as portable as a magnifying glass.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-main-difference-c779bd2a","Science G9: Understanding that the cell is the basic building unit of - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-1 Understanding that the cell is the basic building unit of living organisms, knowing some of the technologies that helped study them, and comparing single-celled and multi-cellular organisms.","3. Comparing unicellular and multicellular organisms and providing examples of each.","What is the main difference between single-celled and multi-celled organisms?","Single-celled organisms have a larger size than multi-celled organisms","Single-celled organisms have a more complex structure than multi-celled organisms","Single-celled organisms consist of one cell, while multi-celled organisms consist of many cells","Single-celled organisms are capable of movement, while multi-celled organisms are not","Single-celled organisms consist of one cell, while multi-celled organisms consist of many cells","Single-celled organisms, such as bacteria and protozoa, consist of one cell, while multi-celled organisms, such as plants and animals, consist of many cells that work together to form tissues and organs.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-organelle-is-responsible-db7bc08c","Science G9: Understanding that the cell is the basic building unit of - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-1 Understanding that the cell is the basic building unit of living organisms, knowing some of the technologies that helped study them, and comparing single-celled and multi-cellular organisms.","3. Comparing unicellular and multicellular organisms and providing examples of each.","Which organelle is responsible for synthesizing proteins for the cell?","Mitochondria","Chloroplast","Endoplasmic reticulum","Ribosomes","Ribosomes","Ribosomes are small organelles found throughout the cytoplasm where protein synthesis takes place. They read the genetic code from mRNA and assemble amino acids into proteins.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-main-function-1867235c","Science G9: Understanding that the cell is the basic building unit of - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-1 Understanding that the cell is the basic building unit of living organisms, knowing some of the technologies that helped study them, and comparing single-celled and multi-cellular organisms.","3. Comparing unicellular and multicellular organisms and providing examples of each.","What is the main function of the nucleus in a cell?","To synthesize proteins for the cell","To store genetic information in the form of DNA","To regulate the movement of materials in and out of the cell","To produce energy for the cell through cellular respiration","To store genetic information in the form of DNA","The nucleus is the control center of the cell where genetic information is stored in the form of DNA. It plays a crucial role in regulating cell growth, division, and function.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-primary-function-101535e8","Science G9: Understanding that the cell is the basic building unit of - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-1 Understanding that the cell is the basic building unit of living organisms, knowing some of the technologies that helped study them, and comparing single-celled and multi-cellular organisms.","3. Comparing unicellular and multicellular organisms and providing examples of each.","What is the primary function of the cell membrane?","To provide structural support to the cell","To regulate the movement of materials in and out of the cell","To produce energy for the cell through photosynthesis","To synthesize proteins for the cell","To regulate the movement of materials in and out of the cell","The cell membrane, also known as the plasma membrane, is a thin layer of lipid and protein molecules that surrounds the cell and regulates the movement of materials in and out of the cell.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-organelle-is-responsible-f85d65fc","Science G9: Understanding that the cell is the basic building unit of - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-1 Understanding that the cell is the basic building unit of living organisms, knowing some of the technologies that helped study them, and comparing single-celled and multi-cellular organisms.","3. Comparing unicellular and multicellular organisms and providing examples of each.","Which organelle is responsible for generating most of the cell's energy?","Mitochondria","Chloroplast","Nucleus","Endoplasmic reticulum","Mitochondria","Mitochondria are often referred to as the powerhouses of the cell because they generate most of the cell's energy through a process called cellular respiration.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-organelle-is-responsible-ae57e9a9","Science G9: Understanding that the cell is the basic building unit of - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-1 Understanding that the cell is the basic building unit of living organisms, knowing some of the technologies that helped study them, and comparing single-celled and multi-cellular organisms.","3. Comparing unicellular and multicellular organisms and providing examples of each.","Which organelle is responsible for storing genetic information in the form of DNA?","Mitochondria","Chloroplast","Nucleus","Endoplasmic reticulum","Nucleus","The nucleus is the control center of the cell where genetic information is stored in the form of DNA. It plays a crucial role in regulating cell growth, division, and function.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-main-function-ff45505a","Science G9: Understanding that the cell is the basic building unit of - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-1 Understanding that the cell is the basic building unit of living organisms, knowing some of the technologies that helped study them, and comparing single-celled and multi-cellular organisms.","3. Comparing unicellular and multicellular organisms and providing examples of each.","What is the main function of the Golgi apparatus in a cell?","To synthesize proteins for the cell","To store genetic information in the form of DNA","To regulate the movement of materials in and out of the cell","To modify, sort, and package proteins and lipids for transport","To modify, sort, and package proteins and lipids for transport","The Golgi apparatus is a complex organelle that modifies, sorts, and packages proteins and lipids for transport out of the cell. It is also involved in the synthesis of complex lipids and the modification of proteins.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-organelle-is-responsible-da28e7a7","Science G9: Understanding that the cell is the basic building unit of - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-1 Understanding that the cell is the basic building unit of living organisms, knowing some of the technologies that helped study them, and comparing single-celled and multi-cellular organisms.","3. Comparing unicellular and multicellular organisms and providing examples of each.","Which organelle is responsible for breaking down and recycling cellular waste?","Lysosome","Mitochondria","Chloroplast","Ribosome","Lysosome","Lysosomes are organelles found in animal cells that contain digestive enzymes. They break down and recycle cellular waste, such as proteins and lipids, and help to maintain cellular homeostasis.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-main-function-4a91cad6","Science G9: Understanding that the cell is the basic building unit of - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-1 Understanding that the cell is the basic building unit of living organisms, knowing some of the technologies that helped study them, and comparing single-celled and multi-cellular organisms.","3. Comparing unicellular and multicellular organisms and providing examples of each.","What is the main function of the endoplasmic reticulum in a cell?","To synthesize proteins for the cell","To store genetic information in the form of DNA","To regulate the movement of materials in and out of the cell","To synthesize lipids and proteins for the cell","To synthesize lipids and proteins for the cell","The endoplasmic reticulum is a network of membranous tubules and cisternae that is involved in the synthesis and transport of lipids and proteins. It is also involved in the regulation of the cell's calcium levels.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-organelle-is-responsible-1d8a9c45","Science G9: Understanding that the cell is the basic building unit of - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-1 Understanding that the cell is the basic building unit of living organisms, knowing some of the technologies that helped study them, and comparing single-celled and multi-cellular organisms.","3. Comparing unicellular and multicellular organisms and providing examples of each.","Which organelle is responsible for photosynthesis in plant cells?","Mitochondria","Chloroplast","Nucleus","Endoplasmic reticulum","Chloroplast","Chloroplasts are organelles found in plant cells where photosynthesis takes place. They contain the pigment chlorophyll, which absorbs light energy and converts it into chemical energy in the form of glucose.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-function-of-09222a69","Science G9: Understanding that the cell is the basic building unit of - Set 4","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-1 Understanding that the cell is the basic building unit of living organisms, knowing some of the technologies that helped study them, and comparing single-celled and multi-cellular organisms.","2. Describing cell structures, relating them to their specialized functions within the cell, and explaining how each structure contributes to overall cellular functions.","What is the function of the ribosomes in a cell?","To synthesize proteins for the cell","To store genetic information in the cell","To regulate the movement of materials in and out of the cell","To produce energy for the cell","To synthesize proteins for the cell","Ribosomes are responsible for translating messenger RNA into proteins, which are essential for the cell's structure and function.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-main-difference-00bc68aa","Science G9: Understanding that the cell is the basic building unit of - Set 4","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-2 Describing the main events in the stages of the cell cycle and comparing between mitosis and meiosis.","4. Defining meiosis, describing its stages, comparing (using diagrams) what occurs during anaphase I and anaphase II in the division processes, and distinguishing their different forms.","What is the main difference between mitosis and meiosis?","Mitosis results in the production of gametes, while meiosis results in the production of somatic cells","Mitosis results in the production of somatic cells, while meiosis results in the production of gametes","Mitosis involves the replication of DNA, while meiosis involves the separation of chromosomes","Mitosis involves the separation of chromosomes, while meiosis involves the replication of DNA","Mitosis results in the production of somatic cells, while meiosis results in the production of gametes","Mitosis results in the production of somatic cells, which are non-reproductive cells, while meiosis results in the production of gametes, which are reproductive cells.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-basic-building-99d03af7","Science G9: Understanding that the cell is the basic building unit of - Set 4","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-1 Understanding that the cell is the basic building unit of living organisms, knowing some of the technologies that helped study them, and comparing single-celled and multi-cellular organisms.","3. Comparing unicellular and multicellular organisms and providing examples of each.","What is the basic building unit of living organisms?","A cell is a type of molecule","A cell is the smallest unit of life that can function independently","A cell is a type of organism","A cell is a type of tissue","A cell is the smallest unit of life that can function independently","A cell is the smallest unit of life that can function independently and is the basic building unit of living organisms.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-function-of-74121062","Science G9: Understanding that the cell is the basic building unit of - Set 4","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-1 Understanding that the cell is the basic building unit of living organisms, knowing some of the technologies that helped study them, and comparing single-celled and multi-cellular organisms.","3. Comparing unicellular and multicellular organisms and providing examples of each.","What is the function of the chloroplasts in plant cells?","To synthesize proteins for the cell","To store genetic information in the cell","To regulate the movement of materials in and out of the cell","To produce energy for the cell through photosynthesis","To produce energy for the cell through photosynthesis","Chloroplasts are responsible for producing energy for the cell through photosynthesis, which involves the conversion of light energy into chemical energy.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-role-of-c1772247","Science G9: Understanding that the cell is the basic building unit of - Set 4","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-1 Understanding that the cell is the basic building unit of living organisms, knowing some of the technologies that helped study them, and comparing single-celled and multi-cellular organisms.","3. Comparing unicellular and multicellular organisms and providing examples of each.","What is the role of the lysosomes in a cell?","To synthesize proteins for the cell","To store genetic information in the cell","To regulate the movement of materials in and out of the cell","To break down and recycle cellular waste and foreign substances","To break down and recycle cellular waste and foreign substances","Lysosomes are responsible for breaking down and recycling cellular waste and foreign substances, and they also play a role in cellular digestion and recycling.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-main-difference-7c03a614","Science G9: Understanding that the cell is the basic building unit of - Set 4","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-1 Understanding that the cell is the basic building unit of living organisms, knowing some of the technologies that helped study them, and comparing single-celled and multi-cellular organisms.","3. Comparing unicellular and multicellular organisms and providing examples of each.","What is the main difference between autotrophic and heterotrophic organisms?","Autotrophic organisms produce their own food, while heterotrophic organisms do not","Autotrophic organisms do not produce their own food, while heterotrophic organisms do","Autotrophic organisms have a large amount of genetic material, while heterotrophic organisms have a small amount","Autotrophic organisms have a small amount of genetic material, while heterotrophic organisms have a large amount","Autotrophic organisms produce their own food, while heterotrophic organisms do not","Autotrophic organisms, such as plants and some bacteria, produce their own food through photosynthesis or chemosynthesis, while heterotrophic organisms, such as animals and fungi, cannot produce their own food and must consume other organisms or organic matter.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-main-difference-c01a2dc2","Science G9: Understanding that the cell is the basic building unit of - Set 4","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-1 Understanding that the cell is the basic building unit of living organisms, knowing some of the technologies that helped study them, and comparing single-celled and multi-cellular organisms.","3. Comparing unicellular and multicellular organisms and providing examples of each.","What is the main difference between prokaryotic and eukaryotic cells?","Prokaryotic cells have a true nucleus, while eukaryotic cells do not","Prokaryotic cells have a true nucleus, while eukaryotic cells have a false nucleus","Prokaryotic cells do not have a true nucleus, while eukaryotic cells do","Prokaryotic cells have a large amount of genetic material, while eukaryotic cells have a small amount","Prokaryotic cells do not have a true nucleus, while eukaryotic cells do","Prokaryotic cells do not have a true nucleus, while eukaryotic cells have a true nucleus and other membrane-bound organelles.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-main-difference-70e65fef","Science G9: Understanding that the cell is the basic building unit of - Set 4","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-1 Understanding that the cell is the basic building unit of living organisms, knowing some of the technologies that helped study them, and comparing single-celled and multi-cellular organisms.","2. Describing cell structures, relating them to their specialized functions within the cell, and explaining how each structure contributes to overall cellular functions.","What is the main difference between single-celled and multi-cellular organisms?","Single-celled organisms have more cells than multi-cellular organisms","Single-celled organisms have fewer cells than multi-cellular organisms","Single-celled organisms have cells that are similar in structure and function","Single-celled organisms have cells that are different in structure and function","Single-celled organisms have fewer cells than multi-cellular organisms","Single-celled organisms have fewer cells than multi-cellular organisms, and each cell performs multiple functions.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-role-of-79a53bbe","Science G9: Understanding that the cell is the basic building unit of - Set 4","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-1 Understanding that the cell is the basic building unit of living organisms, knowing some of the technologies that helped study them, and comparing single-celled and multi-cellular organisms.","3. Comparing unicellular and multicellular organisms and providing examples of each.","What is the role of the cell membrane in a cell?","To provide structural support to the cell","To regulate the movement of materials in and out of the cell","To produce energy for the cell","To store genetic information in the cell","To regulate the movement of materials in and out of the cell","The cell membrane regulates the movement of materials in and out of the cell and acts as a barrier to protect the cell's internal environment.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-function-of-abd25fa0","Science G9: Understanding that the cell is the basic building unit of - Set 4","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-1 Understanding that the cell is the basic building unit of living organisms, knowing some of the technologies that helped study them, and comparing single-celled and multi-cellular organisms.","2. Describing cell structures, relating them to their specialized functions within the cell, and explaining how each structure contributes to overall cellular functions.","What is the function of the mitochondria in a cell?","To synthesize proteins for the cell","To store genetic information in the cell","To produce energy for the cell through cellular respiration","To regulate the movement of materials in and out of the cell","To produce energy for the cell through cellular respiration","Mitochondria are the site of cellular respiration, where energy is produced for the cell through the breakdown of glucose.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-difference-a-c44348e2","Science G9: Understanding that the cell is the - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-1 Understanding that the cell is the basic building unit of living organisms, knowing some of the technologies that helped study them, and comparing single-celled and multi-cellular organisms.","2. Describing cell structures, relating them to their specialized functions within the cell, and explaining how each structure contributes to overall cellular functions.","What is the difference between a single-celled organism and a multi-celled organism?","A single-celled organism has one cell, while a multi-celled organism has many cells.","A single-celled organism is larger than a multi-celled organism.","A single-celled organism is more complex than a multi-celled organism.","A single-celled organism is less complex than a multi-celled organism.","A single-celled organism has one cell, while a multi-celled organism has many cells.","A single-celled organism has one cell, while a multi-celled organism has many cells, which allows for greater complexity and specialization of functions.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-basic-unit-8043523d","Science G9: Understanding that the cell is the - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-1 Understanding that the cell is the basic building unit of living organisms, knowing some of the technologies that helped study them, and comparing single-celled and multi-cellular organisms.","2. Describing cell structures, relating them to their specialized functions within the cell, and explaining how each structure contributes to overall cellular functions.","What is the basic unit of structure of living organisms?","A cell is the smallest unit of life that can function independently.","A cell is the largest unit of life that can function independently.","A cell is a part of a larger organism but not the basic unit of structure.","A cell is a type of molecule that makes up living organisms.","A cell is the smallest unit of life that can function independently.","A cell is the smallest unit of life that can function independently and is the basic unit of structure of living organisms.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-are-three-main-1d951863","Science G9: Understanding that the cell is the - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-1 Understanding that the cell is the basic building unit of living organisms, knowing some of the technologies that helped study them, and comparing single-celled and multi-cellular organisms.","1. Identifying the basic unit of structure of living organisms, stating the principles of the cell theory, recognizing the importance of technological tools (magnifying devices and microscopes), and explaining their role in identifying cells and their components.","What are the three main principles of the cell theory?","All living things are composed of cells.","Cells are the basic units of life.","All cells come from pre-existing cells.","Cells are the smallest units of life that can function independently.","All cells come from pre-existing cells.","The three main principles of the cell theory are: 1) All living things are composed of cells, 2) Cells are the basic units of life, and 3) All cells come from pre-existing cells.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-main-purpose-3ac8937e","Science G9: Understanding that the cell is the - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-1 Understanding that the cell is the basic building unit of living organisms, knowing some of the technologies that helped study them, and comparing single-celled and multi-cellular organisms.","1. Identifying the basic unit of structure of living organisms, stating the principles of the cell theory, recognizing the importance of technological tools (magnifying devices and microscopes), and explaining their role in identifying cells and their components.","What is the main purpose of a microscope in cell biology?","To study the structure of cells and their components.","To study the behavior of cells and their interactions.","To study the environment in which cells live.","To study the history of cell biology.","To study the structure of cells and their components.","The main purpose of a microscope in cell biology is to study the structure of cells and their components, allowing scientists to observe and understand the details of cellular organization.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-role-of-5f1362c9","Science G9: Understanding that the cell is the - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-1 Understanding that the cell is the basic building unit of living organisms, knowing some of the technologies that helped study them, and comparing single-celled and multi-cellular organisms.","1. Identifying the basic unit of structure of living organisms, stating the principles of the cell theory, recognizing the importance of technological tools (magnifying devices and microscopes), and explaining their role in identifying cells and their components.","What is the role of a magnifying glass in cell biology?","To study the structure of cells and their components.","To study the behavior of cells and their interactions.","To observe cells and their components at a larger scale.","To study the environment in which cells live.","To observe cells and their components at a larger scale.","A magnifying glass is used to observe cells and their components at a larger scale, allowing scientists to see the general structure and organization of cells.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-importance-of-5102d146","Science G9: Understanding that the cell is the - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-1 Understanding that the cell is the basic building unit of living organisms, knowing some of the technologies that helped study them, and comparing single-celled and multi-cellular organisms.","1. Identifying the basic unit of structure of living organisms, stating the principles of the cell theory, recognizing the importance of technological tools (magnifying devices and microscopes), and explaining their role in identifying cells and their components.","What is the importance of cell theory in biology?","It explains the structure and function of cells.","It explains the behavior of cells and their interactions.","It explains the diversity of life on Earth.","It explains the evolution of cells over time.","It explains the structure and function of cells.","Cell theory is important in biology because it explains the structure and function of cells, providing a fundamental understanding of the basic units of life.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-main-difference-024edbd0","Science G9: Understanding that the cell is the - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-1 Understanding that the cell is the basic building unit of living organisms, knowing some of the technologies that helped study them, and comparing single-celled and multi-cellular organisms.","3. Comparing unicellular and multicellular organisms and providing examples of each.","What is the main difference between prokaryotic and eukaryotic cells?","Prokaryotic cells have a nucleus, while eukaryotic cells do not.","Prokaryotic cells are larger than eukaryotic cells.","Prokaryotic cells are more complex than eukaryotic cells.","Prokaryotic cells lack a true nucleus, while eukaryotic cells have a true nucleus.","Prokaryotic cells lack a true nucleus, while eukaryotic cells have a true nucleus.","The main difference between prokaryotic and eukaryotic cells is that prokaryotic cells lack a true nucleus, while eukaryotic cells have a true nucleus, which allows for greater complexity and organization of cellular components.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-role-of-ae6fc1cc","Science G9: Understanding that the cell is the - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-1 Understanding that the cell is the basic building unit of living organisms, knowing some of the technologies that helped study them, and comparing single-celled and multi-cellular organisms.","1. Identifying the basic unit of structure of living organisms, stating the principles of the cell theory, recognizing the importance of technological tools (magnifying devices and microscopes), and explaining their role in identifying cells and their components.","What is the role of microscopes in the discovery of cells?","They allowed scientists to see cells for the first time.","They allowed scientists to study the behavior of cells and their interactions.","They allowed scientists to study the environment in which cells live.","They allowed scientists to study the history of cell biology.","They allowed scientists to see cells for the first time.","Microscopes played a crucial role in the discovery of cells, allowing scientists to see cells for the first time and observe their structure and organization.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-significance-of-b64247c9","Science G9: Understanding that the cell is the - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-1 Understanding that the cell is the basic building unit of living organisms, knowing some of the technologies that helped study them, and comparing single-celled and multi-cellular organisms.","4. Describing the vital activities and processes of the cell that are necessary for the continuation of life in living organisms","What is the significance of cell division in biology?","It allows cells to grow and repair themselves.","It allows cells to differentiate and specialize.","It allows cells to reproduce and pass on genetic information.","It allows cells to die and be replaced by new cells.","It allows cells to reproduce and pass on genetic information.","Cell division is significant in biology because it allows cells to reproduce and pass on genetic information, ensuring the continuation of life and the transmission of genetic traits.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-main-advantage-1ac8402c","Science G9: Understanding that the cell is the - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-1 Understanding that the cell is the basic building unit of living organisms, knowing some of the technologies that helped study them, and comparing single-celled and multi-cellular organisms.","1. Identifying the basic unit of structure of living organisms, stating the principles of the cell theory, recognizing the importance of technological tools (magnifying devices and microscopes), and explaining their role in identifying cells and their components.","What is the main advantage of using a microscope in cell biology?","It allows scientists to see cells and their components at a larger scale.","It allows scientists to study the behavior of cells and their interactions.","It allows scientists to study the environment in which cells live.","It allows scientists to study the history of cell biology.","It allows scientists to see cells and their components at a larger scale.","The main advantage of using a microscope in cell biology is that it allows scientists to see cells and their components at a larger scale, providing a detailed understanding of cellular structure and organization.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-function-of-1ad8a366","Science G9: Understanding that the cell is the - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-1 Understanding that the cell is the basic building unit of living organisms, knowing some of the technologies that helped study them, and comparing single-celled and multi-cellular organisms.","3. Comparing unicellular and multicellular organisms and providing examples of each.","What is the function of the mitochondria in a cell?","To synthesize proteins for the cell","To store genetic information for the cell","To produce energy for the cell through cellular respiration","To regulate the movement of materials in and out of the cell","To produce energy for the cell through cellular respiration","Mitochondria are organelles found in eukaryotic cells that produce energy for the cell through cellular respiration, a process that converts glucose into ATP.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-main-difference-e76050a6","Science G9: Understanding that the cell is the - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-1 Understanding that the cell is the basic building unit of living organisms, knowing some of the technologies that helped study them, and comparing single-celled and multi-cellular organisms.","2. Describing cell structures, relating them to their specialized functions within the cell, and explaining how each structure contributes to overall cellular functions.","What is the main difference between a single-celled organism and a multi-celled organism?","Single-celled organisms have a large amount of genetic material, while multi-celled organisms have a small amount","Single-celled organisms are able to move, while multi-celled organisms are not","Single-celled organisms lack a true nucleus, while multi-celled organisms have a true nucleus","Single-celled organisms are able to perform all functions on their own, while multi-celled organisms rely on specialized cells to perform functions","Single-celled organisms are able to perform all functions on their own, while multi-celled organisms rely on specialized cells to perform functions","Single-celled organisms, such as bacteria and protozoa, are able to perform all functions on their own, while multi-celled organisms, such as plants and animals, rely on specialized cells to perform functions.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-function-of-be4c8a24","Science G9: Understanding that the cell is the - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-1 Understanding that the cell is the basic building unit of living organisms, knowing some of the technologies that helped study them, and comparing single-celled and multi-cellular organisms.","3. Comparing unicellular and multicellular organisms and providing examples of each.","What is the function of the lysosomes in a cell?","To synthesize proteins for the cell","To store genetic information for the cell","To regulate the movement of materials in and out of the cell","To break down and recycle cellular waste and foreign substances","To break down and recycle cellular waste and foreign substances","Lysosomes are organelles found in cells that break down and recycle cellular waste and foreign substances by using enzymes to degrade and recycle cellular components.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-main-difference-5fcc148d","Science G9: Understanding that the cell is the - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-1 Understanding that the cell is the basic building unit of living organisms, knowing some of the technologies that helped study them, and comparing single-celled and multi-cellular organisms.","3. Comparing unicellular and multicellular organisms and providing examples of each.","What is the main difference between a plant cell and an animal cell?","Plant cells have a large amount of genetic material, while animal cells have a small amount","Plant cells lack a true nucleus, while animal cells have a true nucleus","Plant cells have a true nucleus, while animal cells do not","Plant cells have a cell wall, while animal cells do not","Plant cells have a cell wall, while animal cells do not","Plant cells, such as those found in plants, have a cell wall that provides structural support and protection, while animal cells, such as those found in animals, do not have a cell wall.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-main-difference-a3abaf84","Science G9: Understanding that the cell is the - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-1 Understanding that the cell is the basic building unit of living organisms, knowing some of the technologies that helped study them, and comparing single-celled and multi-cellular organisms.","3. Comparing unicellular and multicellular organisms and providing examples of each.","What is the main difference between a single-celled organism and a multi-celled organism in terms of reproduction?","Single-celled organisms often reproduce by binary fission, while many multi-celled organisms reproduce sexually using gametes.","Single-celled organisms reproduce only by meiosis, while multi-celled organisms reproduce only by binary fission.","Single-celled organisms cannot reproduce, while multi-celled organisms can reproduce.","Single-celled and multi-celled organisms reproduce in exactly the same way.","Single-celled organisms often reproduce by binary fission, while many multi-celled organisms reproduce sexually using gametes.","Many single-celled organisms reproduce by binary fission. Many multi-celled organisms reproduce sexually using gametes, which are produced by meiosis.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-function-of-91f699b1","Science G9: Understanding that the cell is the - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-1 Understanding that the cell is the basic building unit of living organisms, knowing some of the technologies that helped study them, and comparing single-celled and multi-cellular organisms.","3. Comparing unicellular and multicellular organisms and providing examples of each.","What is the function of the ribosomes in a cell?","To synthesize proteins for the cell","To store genetic information for the cell","To regulate the movement of materials in and out of the cell","To produce energy for the cell through cellular respiration","To synthesize proteins for the cell","Ribosomes are organelles found in cells that synthesize proteins for the cell by reading messenger RNA sequences and assembling amino acids into polypeptide chains.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-function-of-cfc7af49","Science G9: Understanding that the cell is the - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-1 Understanding that the cell is the basic building unit of living organisms, knowing some of the technologies that helped study them, and comparing single-celled and multi-cellular organisms.","3. Comparing unicellular and multicellular organisms and providing examples of each.","What is the function of the golgi apparatus in a cell?","To synthesize proteins for the cell","To store genetic information for the cell","To regulate the movement of materials in and out of the cell","To modify, package, and transport proteins and lipids synthesized by the endoplasmic reticulum","To modify, package, and transport proteins and lipids synthesized by the endoplasmic reticulum","The golgi apparatus is an organelle found in cells that modifies, packages, and transports proteins and lipids synthesized by the endoplasmic reticulum by adding carbohydrates and lipids to the molecules.","","",""
