"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-main-difference-4dcefc5b","Science G9: Understanding that the cell is the - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-1 Understanding that the cell is the basic building unit of living organisms, knowing some of the technologies that helped study them, and comparing single-celled and multi-cellular organisms.","3. Comparing unicellular and multicellular organisms and providing examples of each.","What is the main difference between unicellular and multicellular organisms?","Unicellular organisms have more cells than multicellular organisms","Unicellular organisms have fewer cells than multicellular organisms","Unicellular organisms are larger than multicellular organisms","Unicellular organisms are smaller than multicellular organisms","Unicellular organisms have fewer cells than multicellular organisms","Unicellular organisms have fewer cells than multicellular organisms, which are made up of many cells working together.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-of-organisms-is-afb7d03b","Science G9: Understanding that the cell is the - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-1 Understanding that the cell is the basic building unit of living organisms, knowing some of the technologies that helped study them, and comparing single-celled and multi-cellular organisms.","3. Comparing unicellular and multicellular organisms and providing examples of each.","Which of the following organisms is an example of a unicellular organism?","A human","An amoeba","A tree","A car","An amoeba","An amoeba is a single-celled organism that can live independently.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-basic-building-b3cf9fb0","Science G9: Understanding that the cell is the - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-1 Understanding that the cell is the basic building unit of living organisms, knowing some of the technologies that helped study them, and comparing single-celled and multi-cellular organisms.","3. Comparing unicellular and multicellular organisms and providing examples of each.","What is the basic building unit of living organisms?","A cell is a tiny building block of life","A cell is a type of animal","A cell is a type of plant","A cell is a type of rock","A cell is a tiny building block of life","A cell is the basic building unit of living organisms, and it is the smallest unit of life that can function independently.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-main-benefit-844fe547","Science G9: Understanding that the cell is the - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-1 Understanding that the cell is the basic building unit of living organisms, knowing some of the technologies that helped study them, and comparing single-celled and multi-cellular organisms.","3. Comparing unicellular and multicellular organisms and providing examples of each.","What is the main benefit of being a multicellular organism?","Being able to move quickly","Being able to reproduce more quickly","Being able to perform complex tasks","Being able to adapt to changing environments","Being able to perform complex tasks","The main benefit of being a multicellular organism is being able to perform complex tasks, such as movement, reproduction, and adaptation to changing environments.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-main-function-e09f6d36","Science G9: Understanding that the cell is the - Set 4","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-1 Understanding that the cell is the basic building unit of living organisms, knowing some of the technologies that helped study them, and comparing single-celled and multi-cellular organisms.","3. Comparing unicellular and multicellular organisms and providing examples of each.","What is the main function of the cell membrane?","To provide structural support to the cell","To control the movement of substances in and out of the cell","To produce energy for the cell","To synthesize proteins","To control the movement of substances in and out of the cell","The cell membrane, also known as the plasma membrane, is a thin layer that surrounds the cell and regulates the movement of substances in and out of the cell.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-main-difference-8cbd4f74","Science G9: Understanding that the cell is the - Set 4","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-1 Understanding that the cell is the basic building unit of living organisms, knowing some of the technologies that helped study them, and comparing single-celled and multi-cellular organisms.","3. Comparing unicellular and multicellular organisms and providing examples of each.","What is the main difference between prokaryotic and eukaryotic cells?","Prokaryotic cells have a larger size than eukaryotic cells","Eukaryotic cells have a more complex structure than prokaryotic cells","Prokaryotic cells lack a true nucleus","Prokaryotic cells are able to move on their own","Prokaryotic cells lack a true nucleus","Prokaryotic cells, such as bacteria, lack a true nucleus and are typically smaller in size than eukaryotic cells.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-process-by-053f48b0","Science G9: Understanding that the cell is the - Set 4","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-2 Describing the main events in the stages of the cell cycle and comparing between mitosis and meiosis.","4. Defining meiosis, describing its stages, comparing (using diagrams) what occurs during anaphase I and anaphase II in the division processes, and distinguishing their different forms.","What is the process by which cells synthesize proteins?","Photosynthesis","Respiration","Mitosis","Translation","Translation","Translation is the process by which cells synthesize proteins from the genetic information contained in the DNA.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-main-function-6cb60655","Science G9: Understanding that the cell is the - Set 4","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-1 Understanding that the cell is the basic building unit of living organisms, knowing some of the technologies that helped study them, and comparing single-celled and multi-cellular organisms.","2. Describing cell structures, relating them to their specialized functions within the cell, and explaining how each structure contributes to overall cellular functions.","What is the main function of the mitochondria in a cell?","To synthesize proteins","To store genetic information","To regulate cell growth","To produce energy for the cell","To produce energy for the cell","The mitochondria are the powerhouses of the cell and are responsible for producing energy for the cell through cellular respiration.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-process-by-d943fa53","Science G9: Understanding that the cell is the - Set 4","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-2 Describing the main events in the stages of the cell cycle and comparing between mitosis and meiosis.","4. Defining meiosis, describing its stages, comparing (using diagrams) what occurs during anaphase I and anaphase II in the division processes, and distinguishing their different forms.","What is the process by which cells produce energy from glucose?","Photosynthesis","Respiration","Mitosis","Meiosis","Respiration","Cellular respiration is the process by which cells produce energy from glucose in the form of ATP.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-process-by-f205b8db","Science G9: Understanding that the cell is the - Set 4","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-2 Describing the main events in the stages of the cell cycle and comparing between mitosis and meiosis.","4. Defining meiosis, describing its stages, comparing (using diagrams) what occurs during anaphase I and anaphase II in the division processes, and distinguishing their different forms.","What is the process by which cells divide to produce four daughter cells?","Mitosis","Meiosis","Photosynthesis","Respiration","Meiosis","Meiosis is the process by which a cell divides into four daughter cells that are genetically different from the parent cell.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-role-of-2fe20f52","Science G9: Understanding that the cell is the - Set 4","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-1 Understanding that the cell is the basic building unit of living organisms, knowing some of the technologies that helped study them, and comparing single-celled and multi-cellular organisms.","3. Comparing unicellular and multicellular organisms and providing examples of each.","What is the role of the nucleus in a cell?","To synthesize proteins","To store genetic information","To regulate cell growth","To control cell division","To store genetic information","The nucleus is the control center of a cell and contains the genetic information necessary for the cell to function properly.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-main-difference-863e58b9","Science G9: Understanding that the cell is the - Set 4","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-1 Understanding that the cell is the basic building unit of living organisms, knowing some of the technologies that helped study them, and comparing single-celled and multi-cellular organisms.","2. Describing cell structures, relating them to their specialized functions within the cell, and explaining how each structure contributes to overall cellular functions.","What is the main difference between single-celled and multi-celled organisms?","Single-celled organisms have a larger size than multi-celled organisms","Multi-celled organisms have a more complex structure than single-celled organisms","Single-celled organisms are able to move on their own","Single-celled organisms are unable to perform complex functions","Multi-celled organisms have a more complex structure than single-celled organisms","Single-celled organisms consist of one cell, while multi-celled organisms are made of many cells that can specialize for different functions.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-process-by-70e8ee2c","Science G9: Understanding that the cell is the - Set 4","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-2 Describing the main events in the stages of the cell cycle and comparing between mitosis and meiosis.","1. Illustrating the two main stages of the cell cycle (interphase and cell division), describing the changes and events associated with each stage, identifying their duration, and providing examples.","What is the process by which cells divide to produce two daughter cells?","Photosynthesis","Respiration","Mitosis","Meiosis","Mitosis","Mitosis is the process by which a cell divides into two daughter cells that are genetically identical to the parent cell.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-role-of-95c272cf","Science G9: Understanding that the cell is the - Set 4","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-1 Understanding that the cell is the basic building unit of living organisms, knowing some of the technologies that helped study them, and comparing single-celled and multi-cellular organisms.","3. Comparing unicellular and multicellular organisms and providing examples of each.","What is the role of the ribosomes in a cell?","To synthesize proteins","To store genetic information","To regulate cell growth","To control cell division","To synthesize proteins","Ribosomes are the site of protein synthesis in a cell and are responsible for translating the genetic information contained in the DNA into a protein.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-why-is-it-8dd432c5","Science G9: Understanding the concept of friction force - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-3 Understanding the concept of friction force, its types, and how it affects the movement of objects.","2. Defining the force of friction, give examples of it, and explaining how friction affects motion.","Why is it easier to slide a box across a smooth floor than a rough floor?","Because the smooth floor has more friction","Because the smooth floor has less friction","Because the rough floor has more gravity","Because the rough floor has less gravity","Because the smooth floor has less friction","It is easier to slide a box across a smooth floor because it has less friction, which means there is less opposition to the motion of the box.","/image-source/sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-why-is-it-8dd432c5.png","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-purpose-of-38c9a8e2","Science G9: Understanding the concept of friction force - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-3 Understanding the concept of friction force, its types, and how it affects the movement of objects.","2. Defining the force of friction, give examples of it, and explaining how friction affects motion.","What is the purpose of lubricants in reducing friction?","To increase the force of friction","To decrease the force of friction","To change the direction of friction","To eliminate friction altogether","To decrease the force of friction","Lubricants reduce friction by filling in the gaps between two surfaces, which decreases the force of friction and makes it easier for objects to move.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-of-is-an-aff053de","Science G9: Understanding the concept of friction force - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-3 Understanding the concept of friction force, its types, and how it affects the movement of objects.","2. Defining the force of friction, give examples of it, and explaining how friction affects motion.","Which of the following is an example of fluid friction?","A car driving on the road","A boat sailing through the water","A person walking on the ground","A bicycle riding on a smooth floor","A boat sailing through the water","Fluid friction occurs when an object moves through a fluid, such as water or air, which is why a boat sailing through the water is an example of fluid friction.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-how-force-of-8c94def2","Science G9: Understanding the concept of friction force - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-3 Understanding the concept of friction force, its types, and how it affects the movement of objects.","2. Defining the force of friction, give examples of it, and explaining how friction affects motion.","How does the force of friction affect the motion of a skateboard?","It increases the speed of the skateboard","It decreases the speed of the skateboard","It changes the direction of the skateboard","It has no effect on the skateboard","It decreases the speed of the skateboard","The force of friction decreases the speed of a skateboard by opposing its motion, which is why the skateboard eventually comes to rest.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-difference-static-3c89c462","Science G9: Understanding the concept of friction force - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-3 Understanding the concept of friction force, its types, and how it affects the movement of objects.","2. Defining the force of friction, give examples of it, and explaining how friction affects motion.","What is the difference between static and kinetic friction?","Static friction occurs when an object is moving, while kinetic friction occurs when an object is stationary","Static friction occurs when an object is stationary, while kinetic friction occurs when an object is moving","Static friction is stronger than kinetic friction","Kinetic friction is stronger than static friction","Static friction occurs when an object is stationary, while kinetic friction occurs when an object is moving","Static friction occurs when an object is stationary and a force is applied to it, while kinetic friction occurs when an object is moving and the force of friction opposes the motion.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-why-is-it-723542ca","Science G9: Understanding the concept of friction force - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-3 Understanding the concept of friction force, its types, and how it affects the movement of objects.","2. Defining the force of friction, give examples of it, and explaining how friction affects motion.","Why is it important to consider friction when designing machines and mechanisms?","Because friction increases the efficiency of machines","Because friction decreases the efficiency of machines","Because friction has no effect on machines","Because friction is only important in certain situations","Because friction decreases the efficiency of machines","Friction decreases the efficiency of machines by opposing motion and generating heat, which is why it is important to consider friction when designing machines and mechanisms.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-force-opposes-ffa3615f","Science G9: Understanding the concept of friction force - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-3 Understanding the concept of friction force, its types, and how it affects the movement of objects.","2. Defining the force of friction, give examples of it, and explaining how friction affects motion.","What is the force that opposes motion between two surfaces that are in contact?","Normal force","Friction force","Applied force","Gravity force","Friction force","Friction force is the force that opposes motion between two surfaces that are in contact, which is a fundamental concept in understanding the motion of objects.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-of-is-an-6f2ca96f","Science G9: Understanding the concept of friction force - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-3 Understanding the concept of friction force, its types, and how it affects the movement of objects.","3. Listing types of friction (static, sliding, and rolling), distinguishing each type, and providing examples thereof.","Which of the following is an example of static friction?","A car moving down the road","A person trying to push a heavy box","A ball rolling on the ground","A bicycle in motion","A person trying to push a heavy box","Static friction occurs when an object is stationary and a force is applied to it, but the object does not move, such as when a person tries to push a heavy box.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-type-of-friction-7ccc7a9e","Science G9: Understanding the concept of friction force - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-3 Understanding the concept of friction force, its types, and how it affects the movement of objects.","3. Listing types of friction (static, sliding, and rolling), distinguishing each type, and providing examples thereof.","What type of friction occurs when an object is moving and the force of friction opposes the motion?","Static friction","Kinetic friction","Rolling friction","Fluid friction","Kinetic friction","Kinetic friction occurs when an object is moving and the force of friction opposes the motion, which is also known as sliding friction.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-effect-of-fc1f7f5d","Science G9: Understanding the concept of friction force - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-3 Understanding the concept of friction force, its types, and how it affects the movement of objects.","2. Defining the force of friction, give examples of it, and explaining how friction affects motion.","What is the effect of friction on the motion of a rolling ball?","It increases the speed of the ball","It decreases the speed of the ball","It changes the direction of the ball","It has no effect on the ball","It decreases the speed of the ball","Friction decreases the speed of a rolling ball by opposing its motion, which is why the ball eventually comes to rest.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-an-example-37160c25","Science G9: Understanding the concept of friction force - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-3 Understanding the concept of friction force, its types, and how it affects the movement of objects.","2. Defining the force of friction, give examples of it, and explaining how friction affects motion.","What is an example of rolling friction in everyday life?","A car driving on a flat road","A bicycle rolling on a hill","A ball rolling on the ground","A wheel rolling on a track","A wheel rolling on a track","Rolling friction is the force that opposes the motion of a rolling object, such as a wheel rolling on a track.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-an-example-7ffd14be","Science G9: Understanding the concept of friction force - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-3 Understanding the concept of friction force, its types, and how it affects the movement of objects.","3. Listing types of friction (static, sliding, and rolling), distinguishing each type, and providing examples thereof.","What is an example of kinetic friction in everyday life?","A car rolling down a hill","A book sliding off a table","A person walking on a sidewalk","A car stopping at a red light","A person walking on a sidewalk","Kinetic friction is the force that opposes the motion of an object when it is moving, such as a person walking on a sidewalk.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-relationship-friction-3a68fd88","Science G9: Understanding the concept of friction force - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-3 Understanding the concept of friction force, its types, and how it affects the movement of objects.","2. Defining the force of friction, give examples of it, and explaining how friction affects motion.","What is the relationship between friction and motion?","Friction causes motion","Friction opposes motion","Friction has no effect on motion","Friction increases the speed of motion","Friction opposes motion","Friction opposes the motion of an object by creating a force that acts in the opposite direction.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-an-example-3f0b0598","Science G9: Understanding the concept of friction force - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-3 Understanding the concept of friction force, its types, and how it affects the movement of objects.","2. Defining the force of friction, give examples of it, and explaining how friction affects motion.","What is an example of fluid friction in everyday life?","A car driving through a tunnel","A boat sailing on a lake","A plane flying through the air","A submarine diving underwater","A boat sailing on a lake","Fluid friction is the force that opposes the motion of an object through a fluid, such as a boat sailing on a lake.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-type-of-friction-2fdd1922","Science G9: Understanding the concept of friction force - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-3 Understanding the concept of friction force, its types, and how it affects the movement of objects.","3. Listing types of friction (static, sliding, and rolling), distinguishing each type, and providing examples thereof.","What type of friction occurs when two surfaces are moving relative to each other?","Static friction","Kinetic friction","Rolling friction","Fluid friction","Kinetic friction","Kinetic friction occurs when two surfaces are moving relative to each other.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-force-opposes-402c4254","Science G9: Understanding the concept of friction force - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-3 Understanding the concept of friction force, its types, and how it affects the movement of objects.","2. Defining the force of friction, give examples of it, and explaining how friction affects motion.","What is the force that opposes the motion of an object when it is in contact with another surface?","The force that pushes objects apart","The force that pulls objects together","The force that opposes motion","The force that causes objects to stick together","The force that opposes motion","Friction is the force that opposes the motion of an object when it is in contact with another surface.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-effect-of-19155628","Science G9: Understanding the concept of friction force - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-3 Understanding the concept of friction force, its types, and how it affects the movement of objects.","2. Defining the force of friction, give examples of it, and explaining how friction affects motion.","What is the effect of friction on the motion of an object?","It increases the speed of the object","It decreases the speed of the object","It has no effect on the speed of the object","It increases the distance traveled by the object","It decreases the speed of the object","Friction decreases the speed of an object by opposing its motion.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-an-example-d0bde51f","Science G9: Understanding the concept of friction force - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-3 Understanding the concept of friction force, its types, and how it affects the movement of objects.","3. Listing types of friction (static, sliding, and rolling), distinguishing each type, and providing examples thereof.","What is an example of static friction in everyday life?","A car rolling down a hill","A book sliding off a table","A person trying to push a heavy box","A car stopping at a red light","A person trying to push a heavy box","Static friction is the force that prevents an object from moving when it is stationary, such as a person trying to push a heavy box.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-difference-static-fe9f8d35","Science G9: Understanding the concept of friction force - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-3 Understanding the concept of friction force, its types, and how it affects the movement of objects.","3. Listing types of friction (static, sliding, and rolling), distinguishing each type, and providing examples thereof.","What is the difference between static and kinetic friction?","Static friction is stronger than kinetic friction","Static friction is weaker than kinetic friction","Static friction and kinetic friction are the same","Static friction and kinetic friction are opposite forces","Static friction is stronger than kinetic friction","Static friction is stronger than kinetic friction because it is the force that prevents an object from moving when it is stationary.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-effect-of-0711096a","Science G9: Understanding the concept of friction force - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-3 Understanding the concept of friction force, its types, and how it affects the movement of objects.","2. Defining the force of friction, give examples of it, and explaining how friction affects motion.","What is the effect of friction on the distance traveled by an object?","It increases the distance traveled","It decreases the distance traveled","It has no effect on the distance traveled","It increases the speed of the object","It decreases the distance traveled","Friction decreases the distance traveled by an object by opposing its motion.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-type-of-friction-195c870e","Science G9: Understanding the concept of friction force - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-3 Understanding the concept of friction force, its types, and how it affects the movement of objects.","3. Listing types of friction (static, sliding, and rolling), distinguishing each type, and providing examples thereof.","Which type of friction is responsible for the resistance experienced by a car when it is driving on a road?","Static friction","Sliding friction","Rolling friction","Fluid friction","Rolling friction","Rolling friction is the type of friction that occurs when an object rolls on a surface, such as a car driving on a road. It is responsible for the resistance experienced by the car.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-type-of-friction-dfd65856","Science G9: Understanding the concept of friction force - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-3 Understanding the concept of friction force, its types, and how it affects the movement of objects.","3. Listing types of friction (static, sliding, and rolling), distinguishing each type, and providing examples thereof.","What type of friction occurs when an object is stationary and a force is applied to it, but it does not move?","Rolling friction","Sliding friction","Static friction","Fluid friction","Static friction","Static friction occurs when an object is stationary and a force is applied to it, but it does not move. This type of friction prevents the object from moving.","/image-source/sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-type-of-friction-dfd65856.png","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-type-of-friction-503e8c84","Science G9: Understanding the concept of friction force - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-3 Understanding the concept of friction force, its types, and how it affects the movement of objects.","3. Listing types of friction (static, sliding, and rolling), distinguishing each type, and providing examples thereof.","Which type of friction is responsible for the resistance experienced by a ball when it rolls on the ground?","Static friction","Sliding friction","Rolling friction","Air resistance","Rolling friction","Rolling friction is the type of friction that occurs when an object rolls on a surface, such as a ball rolling on the ground. It is responsible for the resistance experienced by the ball.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-type-of-friction-87402918","Science G9: Understanding the concept of friction force - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-3 Understanding the concept of friction force, its types, and how it affects the movement of objects.","3. Listing types of friction (static, sliding, and rolling), distinguishing each type, and providing examples thereof.","What type of friction occurs when two surfaces are sliding against each other?","Static friction","Rolling friction","Fluid friction","Sliding friction","Sliding friction","Sliding friction occurs when two surfaces are sliding against each other, such as when a block of wood is pushed across a floor.","/image-source/sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-type-of-friction-87402918.png","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-purpose-of-fcc01ebf","Science G9: Understanding the concept of friction force - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-3 Understanding the concept of friction force, its types, and how it affects the movement of objects.","2. Defining the force of friction, give examples of it, and explaining how friction affects motion.","What is the purpose of lubricants in reducing friction?","To increase the force of friction","To decrease the force of friction","To change the type of friction","To eliminate friction altogether","To decrease the force of friction","Lubricants are used to reduce friction by decreasing the force of friction between two surfaces. They work by filling in the gaps between the surfaces, reducing the amount of contact and therefore reducing the friction.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-of-is-an-94e42c87","Science G9: Understanding the concept of friction force - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-3 Understanding the concept of friction force, its types, and how it affects the movement of objects.","3. Listing types of friction (static, sliding, and rolling), distinguishing each type, and providing examples thereof.","Which of the following is an example of static friction?","A car rolling down a hill","A book sliding across a table","A person trying to push a heavy box that is not moving","A bicycle rolling on a flat surface","A person trying to push a heavy box that is not moving","Static friction is the type of friction that occurs when an object is stationary and a force is applied to it, but it does not move. A person trying to push a heavy box that is not moving is an example of static friction.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-main-difference-67438546","Science G9: Understanding the concept of friction force - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-3 Understanding the concept of friction force, its types, and how it affects the movement of objects.","3. Listing types of friction (static, sliding, and rolling), distinguishing each type, and providing examples thereof.","What is the main difference between static and sliding friction?","The amount of force applied","The speed of the object","The type of surface the object is on","Whether the object is moving or not","Whether the object is moving or not","The main difference between static and sliding friction is whether the object is moving or not. Static friction occurs when an object is stationary, while sliding friction occurs when an object is moving.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-type-of-friction-b98a904d","Science G9: Understanding the concept of friction force - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-3 Understanding the concept of friction force, its types, and how it affects the movement of objects.","3. Listing types of friction (static, sliding, and rolling), distinguishing each type, and providing examples thereof.","Which type of friction is typically the weakest?","Static friction","Sliding friction","Rolling friction","Fluid friction","Rolling friction","Rolling friction is typically the weakest type of friction. This is because the objects are rolling, which reduces the amount of contact between the surfaces and therefore reduces the friction.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-happens-to-force-21afd1ae","Science G9: Understanding the concept of friction force - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-3 Understanding the concept of friction force, its types, and how it affects the movement of objects.","3. Listing types of friction (static, sliding, and rolling), distinguishing each type, and providing examples thereof.","What happens to the force of friction when an object starts to roll?","It increases","It decreases","It stays the same","It becomes zero","It decreases","When an object starts to roll, the force of friction decreases. This is because rolling friction is typically weaker than sliding friction.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-of-is-an-322c4242","Science G9: Understanding the concept of friction force - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-3 Understanding the concept of friction force, its types, and how it affects the movement of objects.","3. Listing types of friction (static, sliding, and rolling), distinguishing each type, and providing examples thereof.","Which of the following is an example of rolling friction?","A block of wood sliding across a floor","A car driving on a road","A ball rolling on a flat surface","A person walking on a rough surface","A ball rolling on a flat surface","A ball rolling on a flat surface is an example of rolling friction. The ball is rolling, which means it is experiencing rolling friction.","/image-source/sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-of-is-an-322c4242.png","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-significance-of-48e3f65a","Science G9: Understanding the concept of moment of inertia and reformulating - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-4 Understanding the concept of moment of inertia and reformulating Newton›s first law based on it.","2. Formulating Newton's first law according to the moment of inertia.","What is the significance of moment of inertia in the context of Newton's first law?","It is unrelated to Newton's first law.","It is a measure of an object's mass.","It is a measure of an object's resistance to changes in its motion.","It is a measure of an object's energy.","It is a measure of an object's resistance to changes in its motion.","Moment of inertia is a measure of an object's resistance to changes in its motion, which is related to Newton's first law, also known as the law of inertia.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-moment-of-f5504759","Science G9: Understanding the concept of moment of inertia and reformulating - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-4 Understanding the concept of moment of inertia and reformulating Newton›s first law based on it.","2. Formulating Newton's first law according to the moment of inertia.","What is moment of inertia?","The resistance of an object to changes in its motion.","The force required to move an object.","The mass of an object multiplied by its distance from the axis of rotation.","The energy required to rotate an object.","The resistance of an object to changes in its motion.","Moment of inertia is the measure of an object's resistance to changes in its motion, especially when it's rotating.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-of-factors-affects-07834248","Science G9: Understanding the concept of moment of inertia and reformulating - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-4 Understanding the concept of moment of inertia and reformulating Newton›s first law based on it.","1. Clarifying the concept of moment of inertia. Giving examples of factors affecting inertia in daily life","Which of the following factors affects an object's moment of inertia?","Only its mass.","Only its distance from the axis of rotation.","Both its mass and distance from the axis of rotation.","Neither its mass nor distance from the axis of rotation.","Both its mass and distance from the axis of rotation.","An object's moment of inertia is affected by both its mass and its distance from the axis of rotation.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-of-objects-has-4a32ae93","Science G9: Understanding the concept of moment of inertia and reformulating - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-4 Understanding the concept of moment of inertia and reformulating Newton›s first law based on it.","2. Formulating Newton's first law according to the moment of inertia.","Which of the following objects has a higher moment of inertia?","A small, dense ball.","A large, light hoop.","A small, dense ball and a large, light hoop.","Neither of the above.","A large, light hoop.","A large, light hoop has a higher moment of inertia than a small, dense ball because it has more mass and is farther from the axis of rotation.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-of-objects-has-4ffce54e","Science G9: Understanding the concept of moment of inertia and reformulating - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-4 Understanding the concept of moment of inertia and reformulating Newton›s first law based on it.","2. Formulating Newton's first law according to the moment of inertia.","Which of the following objects has a lower moment of inertia?","A large, dense wheel.","A small, light hoop.","A large, dense wheel and a small, light hoop.","Neither of the above.","A small, light hoop.","A small, light hoop has a lower moment of inertia than a large, dense wheel because it has less mass and is closer to the axis of rotation.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-relationship-moment-0d48b3a8","Science G9: Understanding the concept of moment of inertia and reformulating - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-4 Understanding the concept of moment of inertia and reformulating Newton›s first law based on it.","2. Formulating Newton's first law according to the moment of inertia.","What is the relationship between moment of inertia and Newton's first law?","Moment of inertia is a measure of an object's mass.","Moment of inertia is a measure of an object's resistance to changes in its motion.","Newton's first law is a measure of an object's moment of inertia.","Newton's first law is unrelated to moment of inertia.","Moment of inertia is a measure of an object's resistance to changes in its motion.","Moment of inertia is a measure of an object's resistance to changes in its motion, which is related to Newton's first law, also known as the law of inertia.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-how-distance-of-dc6a5cca","Science G9: Understanding the concept of moment of inertia and reformulating - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-4 Understanding the concept of moment of inertia and reformulating Newton›s first law based on it.","1. Clarifying the concept of moment of inertia. Giving examples of factors affecting inertia in daily life","How does the distance of an object from the axis of rotation affect its moment of inertia?","It has no effect.","It increases the moment of inertia.","It decreases the moment of inertia.","It stays the same.","It increases the moment of inertia.","The distance of an object from the axis of rotation increases its moment of inertia, assuming the mass remains constant.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-an-example-24b03d35","Science G9: Understanding the concept of moment of inertia and reformulating - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-4 Understanding the concept of moment of inertia and reformulating Newton›s first law based on it.","1. Clarifying the concept of moment of inertia. Giving examples of factors affecting inertia in daily life","What is an example of a factor that affects an object's moment of inertia in daily life?","The force required to move an object.","The mass of an object.","The distance of an object from the axis of rotation.","The energy required to rotate an object.","The distance of an object from the axis of rotation.","The distance of an object from the axis of rotation is an example of a factor that affects an object's moment of inertia in daily life, such as a bicycle wheel.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-how-mass-of-17c0b28e","Science G9: Understanding the concept of moment of inertia and reformulating - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-4 Understanding the concept of moment of inertia and reformulating Newton›s first law based on it.","1. Clarifying the concept of moment of inertia. Giving examples of factors affecting inertia in daily life","How does the mass of an object affect its moment of inertia?","It has no effect.","It decreases the moment of inertia.","It increases the moment of inertia.","It stays the same.","It increases the moment of inertia.","The mass of an object increases its moment of inertia, assuming the distance from the axis of rotation remains constant.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-happens-to-an-61cf6f16","Science G9: Understanding the concept of moment of inertia and reformulating - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-4 Understanding the concept of moment of inertia and reformulating Newton›s first law based on it.","2. Formulating Newton's first law according to the moment of inertia.","What happens to an object's moment of inertia when its mass increases?","It decreases.","It stays the same.","It increases.","It becomes zero.","It increases.","An object's moment of inertia increases when its mass increases, assuming the distance from the axis of rotation remains constant.","/image-source/sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-happens-to-an-61cf6f16.png","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-significance-of-197f4c08","Science G9: Understanding the concept of moment of inertia and reformulating - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-4 Understanding the concept of moment of inertia and reformulating Newton›s first law based on it.","2. Formulating Newton's first law according to the moment of inertia.","What is the significance of the moment of inertia in the context of Newton's first law?","It contradicts Newton's first law.","It is unrelated to Newton's first law.","It is a reformulation of Newton's first law.","It is a measure of an object's mass.","It is a reformulation of Newton's first law.","The moment of inertia is used to reformulate Newton's first law, which states that an object at rest will remain at rest, and an object in motion will continue to move, unless acted upon by an external force.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-how-moment-of-c2b80ae6","Science G9: Understanding the concept of moment of inertia and reformulating - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-4 Understanding the concept of moment of inertia and reformulating Newton›s first law based on it.","2. Formulating Newton's first law according to the moment of inertia.","How does the moment of inertia relate to Newton's first law?","It contradicts Newton's first law.","It is unrelated to Newton's first law.","It is a reformulation of Newton's first law.","It is a measure of an object's mass.","It is a reformulation of Newton's first law.","The moment of inertia is used to reformulate Newton's first law, which states that an object at rest will remain at rest, and an object in motion will continue to move, unless acted upon by an external force.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-moment-of-52cad1bf","Science G9: Understanding the concept of moment of inertia and reformulating - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-4 Understanding the concept of moment of inertia and reformulating Newton›s first law based on it.","2. Formulating Newton's first law according to the moment of inertia.","What is the moment of inertia in physics?","The measure of an object's resistance to changes in its motion.","The measure of an object's mass.","The measure of an object's velocity.","The measure of an object's acceleration.","The measure of an object's resistance to changes in its motion.","The moment of inertia is a measure of an object's resistance to changes in its motion, which depends on its mass distribution.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-how-moment-of-28bb437e","Science G9: Understanding the concept of moment of inertia and reformulating - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-4-4 Distinguishing between the kinetic and potential energy of a body and the factors affecting them.","3. Calculating the kinetic and potential energy of objects, and deducing the relationship between them.","How does the moment of inertia affect the torque required to produce a given angular acceleration?","It decreases the torque required.","It increases the torque required.","It has no effect on the torque required.","It increases the object's potential energy.","It increases the torque required.","The moment of inertia affects the torque required to produce a given angular acceleration, with a larger moment of inertia requiring a greater torque to produce the same angular acceleration.","/image-source/sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-how-moment-of-28bb437e.png","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-effect-of-17b9e2b1","Science G9: Understanding the concept of moment of inertia and reformulating - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-4 Understanding the concept of moment of inertia and reformulating Newton›s first law based on it.","2. Formulating Newton's first law according to the moment of inertia.","What is the effect of increasing the moment of inertia on an object's rotational motion?","It increases the object's velocity.","It decreases the object's velocity.","It increases the object's resistance to changes in its motion.","It decreases the object's resistance to changes in its motion.","It increases the object's resistance to changes in its motion.","Increasing the moment of inertia of an object increases its resistance to changes in its rotational motion, making it more difficult to accelerate or decelerate.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-significance-of-84f4bd7e","Science G9: Understanding the concept of moment of inertia and reformulating - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-5 Understanding Newton›s second law theoretically and graphically and determining the relationship between body acceleration and the factors affecting","5. Defining the concept of weight, differentiating between the weight and the mass. Calculating the weight of an object mathematically.","What is the significance of the moment of inertia in rotational motion?","It determines the object's velocity.","It determines the object's acceleration.","It determines the object's resistance to changes in its motion.","It determines the object's mass.","It determines the object's resistance to changes in its motion.","The moment of inertia determines an object's resistance to changes in its rotational motion, which depends on its mass distribution.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-how-moment-of-1f02e564","Science G9: Understanding the concept of moment of inertia and reformulating - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-4 Understanding the concept of moment of inertia and reformulating Newton›s first law based on it.","1. Clarifying the concept of moment of inertia. Giving examples of factors affecting inertia in daily life","How does the moment of inertia affect an object's motion?","It increases the object's velocity.","It decreases the object's velocity.","It increases the object's resistance to changes in its motion.","It decreases the object's resistance to changes in its motion.","It increases the object's resistance to changes in its motion.","Rotational inertia is an object’s resistance to changes in its rotational motion.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-relationship-moment-a6006dfa","Science G9: Understanding the concept of moment of inertia and reformulating - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-4 Understanding the concept of moment of inertia and reformulating Newton›s first law based on it.","2. Formulating Newton's first law according to the moment of inertia.","What is the relationship between the moment of inertia and the torque applied to an object?","The moment of inertia is directly proportional to the torque.","The moment of inertia is inversely proportional to the torque.","The moment of inertia is unrelated to the torque.","The moment of inertia is a measure of the torque.","The moment of inertia is inversely proportional to the torque.","The moment of inertia is inversely proportional to the torque applied to an object, meaning that a larger moment of inertia requires a greater torque to produce the same angular acceleration.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-how-moment-of-eaf16fc6","Science G9: Understanding the concept of moment of inertia and reformulating - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-4-4 Distinguishing between the kinetic and potential energy of a body and the factors affecting them.","3. Calculating the kinetic and potential energy of objects, and deducing the relationship between them.","How does the moment of inertia affect an object's rotational kinetic energy?","It increases the object's rotational kinetic energy.","It decreases the object's rotational kinetic energy.","It has no effect on the object's rotational kinetic energy.","It increases the object's potential energy.","It increases the object's rotational kinetic energy.","The moment of inertia affects an object's rotational kinetic energy, with a larger moment of inertia resulting in a greater rotational kinetic energy for the same angular velocity.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-how-moment-of-92267fab","Science G9: Understanding the concept of moment of inertia and reformulating - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-4 Understanding the concept of moment of inertia and reformulating Newton›s first law based on it.","2. Formulating Newton's first law according to the moment of inertia.","How does the moment of inertia relate to the angular momentum of an object?","It is directly proportional to the angular momentum.","It is inversely proportional to the angular momentum.","It is unrelated to the angular momentum.","It is a measure of the angular momentum.","It is directly proportional to the angular momentum.","The moment of inertia is directly proportional to the angular momentum of an object, meaning that a larger moment of inertia results in a greater angular momentum for the same angular velocity.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-unit-of-0e5843c6","Science G9: Understanding the concept of moment of - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-4 Understanding the concept of moment of inertia and reformulating Newton›s first law based on it.","2. Formulating Newton's first law according to the moment of inertia.","What is the unit of measurement for the moment of inertia?","Kilograms per meter","Kilograms per second","Kilogram-meters squared","Newtons per meter","Kilogram-meters squared","The unit of measurement for the moment of inertia is kilogram-meters squared. This unit represents the amount of mass and the distance from the axis of rotation, which are the two factors that determine the moment of inertia.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-how-moment-of-84c4f130","Science G9: Understanding the concept of moment of - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-4 Understanding the concept of moment of inertia and reformulating Newton›s first law based on it.","1. Clarifying the concept of moment of inertia. Giving examples of factors affecting inertia in daily life","How does the moment of inertia relate to the concept of torque?","The moment of inertia is a measure of torque","The moment of inertia is not related to torque","The moment of inertia affects the amount of torque required to rotate an object","The moment of inertia is a measure of the force required to rotate an object","The moment of inertia affects the amount of torque required to rotate an object","The moment of inertia affects the amount of torque required to rotate an object. The greater the moment of inertia, the more torque is required to rotate the object, and the less the object will rotate.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-how-moment-of-5063df1a","Science G9: Understanding the concept of moment of - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-4 Understanding the concept of moment of inertia and reformulating Newton›s first law based on it.","1. Clarifying the concept of moment of inertia. Giving examples of factors affecting inertia in daily life","How does the moment of inertia affect the stability of a rotating object?","The moment of inertia has no effect on the stability of a rotating object","The moment of inertia decreases the stability of a rotating object","The moment of inertia increases the stability of a rotating object","The moment of inertia affects the stability of a rotating object, with a higher moment of inertia resulting in a lower stability","The moment of inertia increases the stability of a rotating object","The moment of inertia increases the stability of a rotating object. The greater the moment of inertia, the more resistant the object is to changes in its rotation, and the more stable it will be.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-moment-of-aac8fc5e","Science G9: Understanding the concept of moment of - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-4 Understanding the concept of moment of inertia and reformulating Newton›s first law based on it.","2. Formulating Newton's first law according to the moment of inertia.","What is the moment of inertia and how does it relate to Newton's first law?","The moment of inertia is a measure of an object's resistance to changes in its rotation and it affects how much force is required to change the object's state of motion","The moment of inertia is a measure of an object's mass and it determines the object's velocity","The moment of inertia is a measure of an object's velocity and it determines the object's acceleration","The moment of inertia is a measure of an object's acceleration and it determines the object's force","The moment of inertia is a measure of an object's resistance to changes in its rotation and it affects how much force is required to change the object's state of motion","The moment of inertia is a measure of an object's resistance to changes in its rotation, which is related to Newton's first law. According to Newton's first law, an object at rest will remain at rest, and an object in motion will continue to move with a constant velocity, unless acted upon by an external force. The moment of inertia affects how much force is required to change the object's state of motion.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-relationship-moment-d69484e3","Science G9: Understanding the concept of moment of - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-4-4 Distinguishing between the kinetic and potential energy of a body and the factors affecting them.","3. Calculating the kinetic and potential energy of objects, and deducing the relationship between them.","What is the relationship between the moment of inertia and the rotational kinetic energy of an object?","The moment of inertia is a measure of the rotational kinetic energy of an object","The moment of inertia is not related to the rotational kinetic energy of an object","The moment of inertia affects the rotational kinetic energy of an object, with a higher moment of inertia resulting in a lower rotational kinetic energy","The moment of inertia affects the rotational kinetic energy of an object, with a higher moment of inertia resulting in a higher rotational kinetic energy","The moment of inertia affects the rotational kinetic energy of an object, with a higher moment of inertia resulting in a higher rotational kinetic energy","The moment of inertia affects the rotational kinetic energy of an object. The greater the moment of inertia, the higher the rotational kinetic energy, and the more energy is required to rotate the object.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-how-distribution-of-c6fd2e0f","Science G9: Understanding the concept of moment of - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-4 Understanding the concept of moment of inertia and reformulating Newton›s first law based on it.","1. Clarifying the concept of moment of inertia. Giving examples of factors affecting inertia in daily life","How does the distribution of mass affect the moment of inertia of an object?","The distribution of mass has no effect on the moment of inertia of an object","The distribution of mass increases the moment of inertia of an object","The distribution of mass decreases the moment of inertia of an object","The distribution of mass affects the moment of inertia of an object, with more mass concentrated near the axis of rotation resulting in a lower moment of inertia","The distribution of mass affects the moment of inertia of an object, with more mass concentrated near the axis of rotation resulting in a lower moment of inertia","The distribution of mass affects the moment of inertia of an object. When more mass is concentrated near the axis of rotation, the moment of inertia is lower, and when more mass is concentrated farther from the axis of rotation, the moment of inertia is higher.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-relationship-moment-38d11e64","Science G9: Understanding the concept of moment of - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-4 Understanding the concept of moment of inertia and reformulating Newton›s first law based on it.","2. Formulating Newton's first law according to the moment of inertia.","What is the relationship between the moment of inertia and Newton's first law?","The moment of inertia is a measure of an object's mass and it is not related to Newton's first law","The moment of inertia is a measure of an object's velocity and it is not related to Newton's first law","The moment of inertia is a measure of an object's resistance to changes in its rotation and it affects how much force is required to change the object's state of motion, which is related to Newton's first law","The moment of inertia is a measure of an object's acceleration and it determines the object's force","The moment of inertia is a measure of an object's resistance to changes in its rotation and it affects how much force is required to change the object's state of motion, which is related to Newton's first law","The moment of inertia is a measure of an object's resistance to changes in its rotation, which is related to Newton's first law. According to Newton's first law, an object at rest will remain at rest, and an object in motion will continue to move with a constant velocity, unless acted upon by an external force. The moment of inertia affects how much force is required to change the object's state of motion.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-happens-to-moment-fe47fb9f","Science G9: Understanding the concept of moment of - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-4 Understanding the concept of moment of inertia and reformulating Newton›s first law based on it.","2. Formulating Newton's first law according to the moment of inertia.","What happens to the moment of inertia of an object when its mass is increased?","The moment of inertia decreases","The moment of inertia remains the same","The moment of inertia increases","The moment of inertia becomes zero","The moment of inertia increases","When the mass of an object is increased, the moment of inertia also increases. This is because the moment of inertia is directly proportional to the mass of the object.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-how-radius-of-3b6fe5a2","Science G9: Understanding the concept of moment of - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-4 Understanding the concept of moment of inertia and reformulating Newton›s first law based on it.","1. Clarifying the concept of moment of inertia. Giving examples of factors affecting inertia in daily life","How does the radius of rotation affect the moment of inertia of an object?","The radius of rotation has no effect on the moment of inertia of an object","The radius of rotation decreases the moment of inertia of an object","The radius of rotation increases the moment of inertia of an object","The radius of rotation affects the moment of inertia of an object, with a larger radius resulting in a higher moment of inertia","The radius of rotation affects the moment of inertia of an object, with a larger radius resulting in a higher moment of inertia","The radius of rotation affects the moment of inertia of an object. When the radius of rotation is larger, the moment of inertia is higher, and when the radius of rotation is smaller, the moment of inertia is lower.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-how-moment-of-1e0ecf04","Science G9: Understanding the concept of moment of - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-4 Understanding the concept of moment of inertia and reformulating Newton›s first law based on it.","1. Clarifying the concept of moment of inertia. Giving examples of factors affecting inertia in daily life","How does the moment of inertia affect the rotation of an object?","The moment of inertia increases the rotation of an object","The moment of inertia decreases the rotation of an object","The moment of inertia has no effect on the rotation of an object","The moment of inertia determines the resistance to changes in the rotation of an object","The moment of inertia determines the resistance to changes in the rotation of an object","The moment of inertia determines the resistance to changes in the rotation of an object. The greater the moment of inertia, the more resistance there is to changes in the rotation, and the less the object will rotate.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-newton-s-522901ac","Science G9: Understanding the concept of momentum and - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-2 Understanding the concept of momentum and the law of momentum conservation.","1. Stating Newton's first law of motion and providing examples thereof.","What is Newton's first law of motion?","An object at rest will remain at rest, and an object in motion will continue to move with a constant velocity, unless acted upon by an external force","An object in motion will always come to rest, and an object at rest will always remain at rest","The force applied to an object is equal to its mass times its acceleration","The momentum of an object is equal to its mass times its velocity","An object at rest will remain at rest, and an object in motion will continue to move with a constant velocity, unless acted upon by an external force","Newton's first law of motion states that an object at rest will remain at rest, and an object in motion will continue to move with a constant velocity, unless acted upon by an external force. This law is also known as the law of inertia.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-of-is-an-51dae391","Science G9: Understanding the concept of momentum and - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-2 Understanding the concept of momentum and the law of momentum conservation.","1. Stating Newton's first law of motion and providing examples thereof.","Which of the following is an example of Newton's first law of motion?","A car accelerating down the road","A ball rolling on a frictionless surface","A book falling off a table","A bicycle turning a corner","A ball rolling on a frictionless surface","A ball rolling on a frictionless surface is an example of Newton's first law of motion because it will continue to move with a constant velocity unless acted upon by an external force, such as friction.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-happens-to-an-5721c7f6","Science G9: Understanding the concept of momentum and - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-2 Understanding the concept of momentum and the law of momentum conservation.","1. Stating Newton's first law of motion and providing examples thereof.","What happens to an object when it is not acted upon by an external force?","It will accelerate","It will decelerate","It will remain at rest or continue to move with a constant velocity","It will change direction","It will remain at rest or continue to move with a constant velocity","According to Newton's first law of motion, an object will remain at rest or continue to move with a constant velocity when it is not acted upon by an external force.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-of-statements-is-e0db792f","Science G9: Understanding the concept of momentum and - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-2 Understanding the concept of momentum and the law of momentum conservation.","1. Stating Newton's first law of motion and providing examples thereof.","Which of the following statements is a consequence of Newton's first law of motion?","An object will always come to rest","An object will always be in motion","An object will resist changes in its motion","An object will always accelerate","An object will resist changes in its motion","Newton's first law of motion implies that an object will resist changes in its motion, which is a fundamental concept in understanding the behavior of objects in the physical world.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-term-tendency-587e6af5","Science G9: Understanding the concept of momentum and - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-2 Understanding the concept of momentum and the law of momentum conservation.","1. Stating Newton's first law of motion and providing examples thereof.","What is the term for the tendency of an object to resist changes in its motion?","Inertia","Momentum","Friction","Gravity","Inertia","Inertia is the term used to describe the tendency of an object to resist changes in its motion, which is a fundamental concept in Newton's first law of motion.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-of-is-not-5bdb80d5","Science G9: Understanding the concept of momentum and - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-2 Understanding the concept of momentum and the law of momentum conservation.","1. Stating Newton's first law of motion and providing examples thereof.","Which of the following is NOT an example of Newton's first law of motion?","A car accelerating down the road","A ball rolling on a frictionless surface","A book falling off a table","A bicycle turning a corner due to the force of friction","A car accelerating down the road","A car accelerating down the road is not an example of Newton's first law of motion because it is being acted upon by an external force, such as the engine or friction, which is causing it to accelerate.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-relationship-an-cbe6129e","Science G9: Understanding the concept of momentum and - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-2 Understanding the concept of momentum and the law of momentum conservation.","1. Stating Newton's first law of motion and providing examples thereof.","What is the relationship between an object's mass and its tendency to resist changes in its motion?","The more massive the object, the less it resists changes in its motion","The more massive the object, the more it resists changes in its motion","The mass of the object has no effect on its tendency to resist changes in its motion","The mass of the object is inversely proportional to its tendency to resist changes in its motion","The more massive the object, the more it resists changes in its motion","According to Newton's first law of motion, the more massive an object is, the more it will resist changes in its motion, which is a fundamental concept in understanding the behavior of objects in the physical world.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-of-statements-newton-9e922122","Science G9: Understanding the concept of momentum and - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-2 Understanding the concept of momentum and the law of momentum conservation.","1. Stating Newton's first law of motion and providing examples thereof.","Which of the following statements about Newton's first law of motion is FALSE?","An object at rest will remain at rest unless acted upon by an external force","An object in motion will continue to move with a constant velocity unless acted upon by an external force","All objects have a natural tendency to come to rest","The law of inertia applies to all objects in the universe","All objects have a natural tendency to come to rest","This statement is false because Newton's first law of motion states that an object will remain at rest or continue to move with a constant velocity unless acted upon by an external force, not that it has a natural tendency to come to rest.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-main-idea-a8773245","Science G9: Understanding the concept of momentum and - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-2 Understanding the concept of momentum and the law of momentum conservation.","1. Stating Newton's first law of motion and providing examples thereof.","What is the main idea of Newton's first law of motion?","That an object will always be in motion","That an object will always come to rest","That an object will resist changes in its motion","That an object will always accelerate","That an object will resist changes in its motion","The main idea of Newton's first law of motion is that an object will resist changes in its motion, which is a fundamental concept in understanding the behavior of objects in the physical world.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-of-is-a-0f8b9a9e","Science G9: Understanding the concept of momentum and - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-2 Understanding the concept of momentum and the law of momentum conservation.","1. Stating Newton's first law of motion and providing examples thereof.","Which situation best illustrates inertia, as described by Newton’s first law?","A passenger moves forward when a car stops suddenly, so a seat belt restrains the passenger.","A rocket accelerates because its engine produces a net force.","A ball falls because Earth exerts a gravitational force.","A magnet attracts an iron nail.","A passenger moves forward when a car stops suddenly, so a seat belt restrains the passenger.","Inertia is the tendency of an object to resist a change in its motion. A passenger’s body tends to keep moving forward when the car stops.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-law-of-b6e7d86b","Science G9: Understanding the concept of momentum and - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-2 Understanding the concept of momentum and the law of momentum conservation.","1. Stating Newton's first law of motion and providing examples thereof.","What is the law of conservation of momentum?","The total momentum of a closed system decreases over time","The total momentum of a closed system remains constant over time","The total momentum of a closed system increases over time","The total momentum of a closed system is lost over time","The total momentum of a closed system remains constant over time","The law of conservation of momentum states that the total momentum of a closed system remains constant over time, which means that the momentum before a collision or explosion is equal to the momentum after.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-a-car-is-4f31d454","Science G9: Understanding the concept of momentum and - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-2 Understanding the concept of momentum and the law of momentum conservation.","2. Calculating the magnitude of momentum mathematically for a moving body. Explaining the law of conservation of momentum theoretically and mathematically.","A car is traveling at a constant velocity, what is its momentum?","Its momentum is zero","Its momentum is decreasing","Its momentum is increasing","Its momentum is the product of its mass and velocity","Its momentum is the product of its mass and velocity","Momentum is defined as the product of an object's mass and velocity, so as long as the car is moving at a constant velocity, its momentum is the product of its mass and velocity.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-a-rocket-ship-4ead403b","Science G9: Understanding the concept of momentum and - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-2 Understanding the concept of momentum and the law of momentum conservation.","1. Stating Newton's first law of motion and providing examples thereof.","A rocket ship is moving through space, what happens to its momentum when it turns on its engines?","Its momentum decreases","Its momentum remains the same","Its momentum increases","Its momentum is lost","Its momentum increases","When the rocket ship turns on its engines, it expels hot gases out of the back, which increases its momentum in the forward direction.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-a-bowling-ball-2976d6f5","Science G9: Understanding the concept of momentum and - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-2 Understanding the concept of momentum and the law of momentum conservation.","1. Stating Newton's first law of motion and providing examples thereof.","A bowling ball and a tennis ball have the same velocity, which one has more momentum?","The tennis ball has more momentum","The bowling ball has more momentum","They have the same momentum","Momentum cannot be compared","The bowling ball has more momentum","Momentum is the product of an object's mass and velocity, and since the bowling ball has a greater mass than the tennis ball, it has more momentum.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-a-bullet-is-50c368cc","Science G9: Understanding the concept of momentum and - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-2 Understanding the concept of momentum and the law of momentum conservation.","1. Stating Newton's first law of motion and providing examples thereof.","A bullet is fired from a gun, what happens to the momentum of the gun?","The momentum of the gun increases","The momentum of the gun decreases","The momentum of the gun remains the same","The momentum of the gun is conserved","The momentum of the gun decreases","When the bullet is fired from the gun, the gun recoils in the opposite direction, resulting in a decrease in its momentum.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-a-hockey-puck-d387d920","Science G9: Understanding the concept of momentum and - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-2 Understanding the concept of momentum and the law of momentum conservation.","1. Stating Newton's first law of motion and providing examples thereof.","A hockey puck is moving across the ice, what happens to its momentum when it is struck by a hockey stick?","Its momentum decreases","Its momentum remains the same","Its momentum increases","Its momentum is lost","Its momentum increases","When the hockey puck is struck by the hockey stick, it gains momentum in the direction of the strike, resulting in an increase in its momentum.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-two-objects-collide-77c1019d","Science G9: Understanding the concept of momentum and - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-2 Understanding the concept of momentum and the law of momentum conservation.","1. Stating Newton's first law of motion and providing examples thereof.","Two objects collide in a perfectly elastic collision, what happens to their momentum?","Their momentum is lost","Their momentum is conserved","Their momentum increases","Their momentum decreases","Their momentum is conserved","In a perfectly elastic collision, both momentum and kinetic energy are conserved, which means that the total momentum of the objects before the collision is equal to the total momentum after the collision.","/image-source/sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-two-objects-collide-77c1019d.png","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-momentum-of-fca8c539","Science G9: Understanding the concept of momentum and - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-3 Understanding the concept of friction force, its types, and how it affects the movement of objects.","2. Defining the force of friction, give examples of it, and explaining how friction affects motion.","What is the momentum of an object?","The force that acts on an object","The energy of an object in motion","The product of an object's mass and velocity","The distance an object travels in a given time","The product of an object's mass and velocity","Momentum is defined as the product of an object's mass and velocity, which is a fundamental concept in physics.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-a-car-a-fc2a90fb","Science G9: Understanding the concept of momentum and - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-2 Understanding the concept of momentum and the law of momentum conservation.","1. Stating Newton's first law of motion and providing examples thereof.","A car and a truck collide and stick together, what happens to their momentum?","Their momentum increases","Their momentum decreases","Their momentum is conserved","Their momentum is lost","Their momentum is conserved","According to the law of conservation of momentum, the total momentum of a closed system remains constant over time, so the momentum of the car and truck is conserved.","/image-source/sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-a-car-a-fc2a90fb.png","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-two-cars-collide-0461fa8c","Science G9: Understanding the concept of momentum and - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-2 Understanding the concept of momentum and the law of momentum conservation.","1. Stating Newton's first law of motion and providing examples thereof.","Two cars collide and stick together, which of the following is true about their momentum?","Their momentum is lost","Their momentum is conserved","Their momentum increases","Their momentum decreases","Their momentum is conserved","According to the law of conservation of momentum, the total momentum of a closed system remains constant over time, so when the two cars collide and stick together, their momentum is conserved.","/image-source/sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-two-cars-collide-0461fa8c.png","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-formula-to-2c9a1e59","Science G9: Understanding the concept of momentum and the law of momentum - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-2 Understanding the concept of momentum and the law of momentum conservation.","2. Calculating the magnitude of momentum mathematically for a moving body. Explaining the law of conservation of momentum theoretically and mathematically.","What is the formula to calculate the magnitude of momentum for an object?","p = m * v","p = m / v","p = m * a","p = m - v","p = m * v","The formula for momentum is p = m * v, where p is momentum, m is mass, and v is velocity.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-happens-to-momentum-106bef8e","Science G9: Understanding the concept of momentum and the law of momentum - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-9 Describing the speed of chemical reactions and identifying the factors affecting them","1. Distinguishing between types of velocity theoretically and graphically by calculating the velocity value of a moving object.","What happens to the momentum of an object when its mass is doubled and velocity is halved?","The momentum remains the same.","The momentum increases by a factor of 2.","The momentum decreases by a factor of 2.","The momentum increases by a factor of 4.","The momentum remains the same.","Momentum is calculated as p = m x v. If the mass is doubled and the velocity is halved, the new momentum is (2m)(v/2) = mv, so the momentum remains the same.","/image-source/sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-happens-to-momentum-106bef8e.png","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-law-of-ed8e07a4","Science G9: Understanding the concept of momentum and the law of momentum - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-2 Understanding the concept of momentum and the law of momentum conservation.","1. Stating Newton's first law of motion and providing examples thereof.","What is the law of conservation of momentum?","The total momentum of a closed system remains constant over time.","The total momentum of a closed system increases over time.","The total momentum of a closed system decreases over time.","It remains the same","It remains the same","Momentum is p = m * v. If mass is doubled (2m) and velocity is halved (v/2), the new momentum is (2m) * (v/2) = m * v, which is the same as the original momentum.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-law-of-896760e3","Science G9: Understanding the concept of momentum and the law of momentum - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-2 Understanding the concept of momentum and the law of momentum conservation.","1. Stating Newton's first law of motion and providing examples thereof.","What is the law of conservation of momentum used in?","Only in physics.","Only in engineering.","In physics and engineering.","In all fields of science.","In physics and engineering.","The law of conservation of momentum is used in physics and engineering to understand the relationship between momentum and force in a closed system.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-relationship-momentum-754eae97","Science G9: Understanding the concept of momentum and the law of momentum - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-4-4 Distinguishing between the kinetic and potential energy of a body and the factors affecting them.","3. Calculating the kinetic and potential energy of objects, and deducing the relationship between them.","What is the relationship between the momentum of an object and its velocity?","Momentum is directly proportional to velocity.","Momentum is inversely proportional to velocity.","Momentum is independent of velocity.","Momentum is equal to velocity.","Momentum is directly proportional to velocity.","Momentum is calculated as p = m * v, so momentum is directly proportional to velocity.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-formula-to-55538c84","Science G9: Understanding the concept of momentum and the law of momentum - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-9 Describing the speed of chemical reactions and identifying the factors affecting them","1. Distinguishing between types of velocity theoretically and graphically by calculating the velocity value of a moving object.","What is the formula to calculate the momentum of an object in a circular motion?","p = m * v","p = m * r","p = m * v / r","p = m * r / v","p = m * v / r","For an object in circular motion, the momentum is calculated as p = m * v / r, where p is momentum, m is mass, v is velocity, and r is radius.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-difference-momentum-72a29a97","Science G9: Understanding the concept of momentum and the law of momentum - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-4-4 Distinguishing between the kinetic and potential energy of a body and the factors affecting them.","2. Deducing the linear and non-linear relationship between kinetic energy and the mass and speed of the moving body and express it mathematically.","How are momentum and kinetic energy calculated for an object of mass m moving at speed v?","Momentum is p = mv, while kinetic energy is KE = ½mv².","Momentum is p = ½mv², while kinetic energy is KE = mv.","Both momentum and kinetic energy equal mv.","Both quantities are independent of mass and speed.","Momentum is p = mv, while kinetic energy is KE = ½mv².","Momentum depends linearly on speed, p = mv, while kinetic energy depends on the square of speed, KE = ½mv².","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-law-of-8ec23cfb","Science G9: Understanding the concept of momentum and the law of momentum - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-2 Understanding the concept of momentum and the law of momentum conservation.","1. Stating Newton's first law of motion and providing examples thereof.","What is the law of conservation of momentum used for?","To calculate the momentum of an object.","To calculate the force acting on an object.","To determine the energy of an object.","To understand the relationship between momentum and force.","To understand the relationship between momentum and force.","The law of conservation of momentum is used to understand the relationship between momentum and force in a closed system.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-relationship-momentum-b14d3880","Science G9: Understanding the concept of momentum and the law of momentum - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-5 Understanding Newton›s second law theoretically and graphically and determining the relationship between body acceleration and the factors affecting","3. Calculating the value of the acceleration of the body affected by the resultant force mathematically.","What is the relationship between momentum and force?","Momentum is directly proportional to force.","Momentum is inversely proportional to force.","Momentum is independent of force.","Momentum is equal to force.","Momentum is inversely proportional to force.","Momentum is calculated as p = m * v, and force is calculated as F = m * a. Since a = ?v / ?t, momentum is inversely proportional to force.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-unit-of-7bcf1530","Science G9: Understanding the concept of momentum and the law of momentum - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-2 Understanding the concept of momentum and the law of momentum conservation.","1. Stating Newton's first law of motion and providing examples thereof.","What is the unit of measurement for momentum?","kg*m/s","kg/s","m/s","kg*m","kg*m/s","The unit of measurement for momentum is kg*m/s, where kg is mass and m/s is velocity.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-a-car-of-9f71be4d","Science G9: Understanding the concept of momentum and the law of momentum - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-9 Describing the speed of chemical reactions and identifying the factors affecting them","1. Distinguishing between types of velocity theoretically and graphically by calculating the velocity value of a moving object.","A car of mass 1500 kg is traveling at a speed of 25 m/s. What is its momentum?","37500 kg m/s","3750 kg m/s","375 kg m/s","3750 kg","37500 kg m/s","Momentum is calculated by multiplying the mass of an object by its velocity. In this case, the momentum of the car is 1500 kg x 25 m/s = 37500 kg m/s.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-relationship-momentum-40f23976","Science G9: Understanding the concept of momentum and the law of momentum - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-5 Understanding Newton›s second law theoretically and graphically and determining the relationship between body acceleration and the factors affecting","5. Defining the concept of weight, differentiating between the weight and the mass. Calculating the weight of an object mathematically.","What is the relationship between momentum and velocity?","Momentum is directly proportional to velocity","Momentum is inversely proportional to velocity","Momentum is independent of velocity","Momentum is equal to velocity","Momentum is directly proportional to velocity","Momentum is directly proportional to velocity, as it is calculated by multiplying the mass of an object by its velocity.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-an-example-78fb2a3e","Science G9: Understanding the concept of momentum and the law of momentum - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-2 Understanding the concept of momentum and the law of momentum conservation.","2. Calculating the magnitude of momentum mathematically for a moving body. Explaining the law of conservation of momentum theoretically and mathematically.","What is an example of a system in which the law of conservation of momentum is not applicable?","A car moving on a straight road","A ball bouncing off a wall","A person walking in a park","A system with an external force acting on it","A system with an external force acting on it","The law of conservation of momentum is not applicable to a system with an external force acting on it, as the external force can transfer momentum to or from the system.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-a-hockey-puck-0373d80e","Science G9: Understanding the concept of momentum and the law of momentum - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-9 Describing the speed of chemical reactions and identifying the factors affecting them","1. Distinguishing between types of velocity theoretically and graphically by calculating the velocity value of a moving object.","A hockey puck of mass 0.15 kg is traveling at a speed of 30 m/s. What is its momentum?","4.5 kg m/s","4.5 kg","0.15 kg m/s","0.15 kg","4.5 kg m/s","Momentum is calculated by multiplying the mass of an object by its velocity. In this case, the momentum of the hockey puck is 0.15 kg x 30 m/s = 4.5 kg m/s.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-happens-to-momentum-3ac8d5ff","Science G9: Understanding the concept of momentum and the law of momentum - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-2 Understanding the concept of momentum and the law of momentum conservation.","1. Stating Newton's first law of motion and providing examples thereof.","What happens to the momentum of a system when an external force acts on it?","The momentum increases","The momentum decreases","The momentum remains the same","The momentum becomes zero","The momentum decreases","When an external force acts on a system, its momentum changes. This is because the external force can transfer momentum to or from the system.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-a-tennis-ball-d6f5a06a","Science G9: Understanding the concept of momentum and the law of momentum - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-1-9 Describing the speed of chemical reactions and identifying the factors affecting them","1. Distinguishing between types of velocity theoretically and graphically by calculating the velocity value of a moving object.","A tennis ball of mass 0.06 kg is traveling at a speed of 25 m/s. What is its momentum?","1.5 kg m/s","1.5 kg","0.06 kg m/s","0.06 kg","1.5 kg m/s","Momentum is calculated by multiplying the mass of an object by its velocity. In this case, the momentum of the tennis ball is 0.06 kg x 25 m/s = 1.5 kg m/s.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-principle-of-76f77431","Science G9: Understanding the concept of momentum and the law of momentum - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-2 Understanding the concept of momentum and the law of momentum conservation.","1. Stating Newton's first law of motion and providing examples thereof.","What is the principle of conservation of momentum?","The total momentum of a system remains constant if no external forces act on it","The total energy of a system remains constant if no external forces act on it","The total mass of a system remains constant if no external forces act on it","The total velocity of a system remains constant if no external forces act on it","The total momentum of a system remains constant if no external forces act on it","The principle of conservation of momentum states that the total momentum of a closed system remains constant if no external forces act on it.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-an-example-5c34dce3","Science G9: Understanding the concept of momentum and the law of momentum - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-2 Understanding the concept of momentum and the law of momentum conservation.","1. Stating Newton's first law of motion and providing examples thereof.","What is an example of a closed system in terms of momentum?","A car moving on a straight road","A ball bouncing off a wall","Two objects colliding and then moving apart","A person walking in a park","Two objects colliding and then moving apart","A closed system is one in which no external forces act on it. An example of a closed system in terms of momentum is two objects colliding and then moving apart, as the total momentum of the system remains constant.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-happens-to-momentum-c5a03978","Science G9: Understanding the concept of momentum and the law of momentum - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-2 Understanding the concept of momentum and the law of momentum conservation.","1. Stating Newton's first law of motion and providing examples thereof.","What happens to the momentum of a system when no external forces act on it?","The momentum increases","The momentum decreases","The momentum remains the same","The momentum becomes zero","The momentum remains the same","According to the law of conservation of momentum, the total momentum of a closed system remains constant if no external forces act on it.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-momentum-in-ce3a7e58","Science G9: Understanding the concept of momentum and the law of momentum - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-2 Understanding the concept of momentum and the law of momentum conservation.","1. Stating Newton's first law of motion and providing examples thereof.","What is momentum in physics?","The force applied to an object","The mass of an object multiplied by its velocity","The distance an object travels in a given time","The energy of an object","The mass of an object multiplied by its velocity","Momentum is the product of an object's mass and velocity, and it is a measure of an object's tendency to keep moving in a straight line.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-of-systems-helps-ce1671bd","Science G9: Understanding the importance of integration of - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-3 Understanding the importance of integration of human body systems and how the structure of different organ relate to one another to maintain homeostasis and body health.","1. Identifying the components of the humanâ€™s body systems (circulatory, immune, digestive, respiratory, excretory, muscular, skeletal, nervous, hormonal, and reproductive) and their specific functions that support the functioning of the body.","Which of the following systems helps to maintain the body's energy balance?","Circulatory system","Respiratory system","Nervous system","Digestive system","Digestive system","The digestive system helps to maintain the body's energy balance by breaking down food into nutrients that can be used by the body for energy production.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-primary-function-0465435f","Science G9: Understanding the importance of integration of - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-3 Understanding the importance of integration of human body systems and how the structure of different organ relate to one another to maintain homeostasis and body health.","1. Identifying the components of the humanâ€™s body systems (circulatory, immune, digestive, respiratory, excretory, muscular, skeletal, nervous, hormonal, and reproductive) and their specific functions that support the functioning of the body.","What is the primary function of the immune system in maintaining homeostasis?","To produce hormones that regulate body functions","To control and coordinate body movements","To detect and eliminate pathogens and foreign substances","To produce digestive enzymes","To detect and eliminate pathogens and foreign substances","The immune system plays a crucial role in maintaining homeostasis by detecting and eliminating pathogens and foreign substances that can cause disease.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-of-systems-helps-0590ec28","Science G9: Understanding the importance of integration of - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-3 Understanding the importance of integration of human body systems and how the structure of different organ relate to one another to maintain homeostasis and body health.","1. Identifying the components of the humanâ€™s body systems (circulatory, immune, digestive, respiratory, excretory, muscular, skeletal, nervous, hormonal, and reproductive) and their specific functions that support the functioning of the body.","Which of the following systems helps to maintain the body's temperature regulation?","Nervous system","Circulatory system","Respiratory system","Integumentary system","Integumentary system","The integumentary system helps to maintain the body's temperature regulation by providing insulation and regulating heat loss through sweating.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-main-function-8c7be78b","Science G9: Understanding the importance of integration of - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-3 Understanding the importance of integration of human body systems and how the structure of different organ relate to one another to maintain homeostasis and body health.","2. Explaining how the systems interact and integrate in maintaining homeostasis health and safety of the body's balance.","What is the main function of the endocrine system in maintaining homeostasis?","To produce hormones that regulate body functions","To control and coordinate body movements","To detect changes in the body's internal environment and respond accordingly","To produce digestive enzymes","To produce hormones that regulate body functions","The endocrine system plays a crucial role in maintaining homeostasis by producing hormones that regulate body functions such as growth, development, and metabolism.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-of-systems-helps-2e8bf479","Science G9: Understanding the importance of integration of - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-3 Understanding the importance of integration of human body systems and how the structure of different organ relate to one another to maintain homeostasis and body health.","1. Identifying the components of the humanâ€™s body systems (circulatory, immune, digestive, respiratory, excretory, muscular, skeletal, nervous, hormonal, and reproductive) and their specific functions that support the functioning of the body.","Which of the following systems helps to maintain the body's fluid balance?","Circulatory system","Respiratory system","Nervous system","Urinary system","Urinary system","The urinary system helps to maintain the body's fluid balance by regulating the levels of water and electrolytes in the body through the process of urination.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-of-systems-helps-ee601bdc","Science G9: Understanding the importance of integration of - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-3 Understanding the importance of integration of human body systems and how the structure of different organ relate to one another to maintain homeostasis and body health.","1. Identifying the components of the humanâ€™s body systems (circulatory, immune, digestive, respiratory, excretory, muscular, skeletal, nervous, hormonal, and reproductive) and their specific functions that support the functioning of the body.","Which of the following systems helps to maintain the body's waste removal?","Circulatory system","Respiratory system","Nervous system","Excretory system","Excretory system","The excretory system helps to maintain the body's waste removal by eliminating waste products through the process of urination and defecation.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-main-function-6a61e335","Science G9: Understanding the importance of integration of - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-3 Understanding the importance of integration of human body systems and how the structure of different organ relate to one another to maintain homeostasis and body health.","1. Identifying the components of the humanâ€™s body systems (circulatory, immune, digestive, respiratory, excretory, muscular, skeletal, nervous, hormonal, and reproductive) and their specific functions that support the functioning of the body.","What is the main function of the nervous system in maintaining homeostasis?","To produce hormones that regulate body functions","To control and coordinate body movements","To detect changes in the body's internal environment and respond accordingly","To produce digestive enzymes","To detect changes in the body's internal environment and respond accordingly","The nervous system plays a crucial role in maintaining homeostasis by detecting changes in the body's internal environment and responding accordingly through the autonomic nervous system.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-of-systems-helps-40289e1b","Science G9: Understanding the importance of integration of - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-3 Understanding the importance of integration of human body systems and how the structure of different organ relate to one another to maintain homeostasis and body health.","1. Identifying the components of the humanâ€™s body systems (circulatory, immune, digestive, respiratory, excretory, muscular, skeletal, nervous, hormonal, and reproductive) and their specific functions that support the functioning of the body.","Which of the following systems helps to maintain the body's acid-base balance?","Circulatory system","Respiratory system","Nervous system","Digestive system","Respiratory system","The respiratory system helps to maintain the body's acid-base balance by regulating the levels of carbon dioxide and oxygen in the blood.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-primary-function-244454ff","Science G9: Understanding the importance of integration of - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-3 Understanding the importance of integration of human body systems and how the structure of different organ relate to one another to maintain homeostasis and body health.","1. Identifying the components of the humanâ€™s body systems (circulatory, immune, digestive, respiratory, excretory, muscular, skeletal, nervous, hormonal, and reproductive) and their specific functions that support the functioning of the body.","What is the primary function of the circulatory system in maintaining homeostasis?","To produce hormones that regulate body functions","To control and coordinate body movements","To transport oxygen and nutrients to cells and remove waste products","To produce digestive enzymes","To transport oxygen and nutrients to cells and remove waste products","The circulatory system plays a crucial role in maintaining homeostasis by transporting oxygen and nutrients to cells and removing waste products through the process of circulation.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-main-function-cfe0a9c5","Science G9: Understanding the importance of integration of - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-3 Understanding the importance of integration of human body systems and how the structure of different organ relate to one another to maintain homeostasis and body health.","1. Identifying the components of the humanâ€™s body systems (circulatory, immune, digestive, respiratory, excretory, muscular, skeletal, nervous, hormonal, and reproductive) and their specific functions that support the functioning of the body.","What is the main function of the muscular system in maintaining homeostasis?","To produce hormones that regulate body functions","To control and coordinate body movements","To maintain posture and support the body's structure","To produce digestive enzymes","To maintain posture and support the body's structure","The muscular system plays a crucial role in maintaining homeostasis by maintaining posture and supporting the body's structure through the contraction and relaxation of muscles.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-main-function-5aa06a62","Science G9: Understanding the importance of integration of - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-3 Understanding the importance of integration of human body systems and how the structure of different organ relate to one another to maintain homeostasis and body health.","2. Explaining how the systems interact and integrate in maintaining homeostasis health and safety of the body's balance.","What is the main function of the circulatory system in maintaining homeostasis?","To facilitate communication between different parts of the body","To regulate body temperature through sweating and shivering","To transport oxygen and nutrients to cells","To remove waste products from the body","To transport oxygen and nutrients to cells","The circulatory system plays a crucial role in maintaining homeostasis by transporting oxygen and nutrients to cells and removing waste products from the body.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-main-function-8bb0c6b2","Science G9: Understanding the importance of integration of - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-3 Understanding the importance of integration of human body systems and how the structure of different organ relate to one another to maintain homeostasis and body health.","2. Explaining how the systems interact and integrate in maintaining homeostasis health and safety of the body's balance.","What is the main function of the nervous system in maintaining homeostasis?","To control body temperature through sweating and shivering","To regulate blood sugar levels by releasing hormones","To facilitate communication between different parts of the body","To produce red blood cells in the bone marrow","To facilitate communication between different parts of the body","The nervous system plays a crucial role in maintaining homeostasis by facilitating communication between different parts of the body, allowing for the coordination of various bodily functions.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-organ-system-is-ed124044","Science G9: Understanding the importance of integration of - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-3 Understanding the importance of integration of human body systems and how the structure of different organ relate to one another to maintain homeostasis and body health.","1. Identifying the components of the humanâ€™s body systems (circulatory, immune, digestive, respiratory, excretory, muscular, skeletal, nervous, hormonal, and reproductive) and their specific functions that support the functioning of the body.","Which organ system is responsible for filtering waste and excess fluids from the blood?","Circulatory system","Respiratory system","Digestive system","Urinary system","Urinary system","The urinary system, also known as the excretory system, is responsible for filtering waste and excess fluids from the blood through the process of urination.","/image-source/sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-organ-system-is-ed124044.png","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-happens-to-body-3920ac4e","Science G9: Understanding the importance of integration of - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-3 Understanding the importance of integration of human body systems and how the structure of different organ relate to one another to maintain homeostasis and body health.","2. Explaining how the systems interact and integrate in maintaining homeostasis health and safety of the body's balance.","What happens to the body's pH levels if the kidneys are not functioning properly?","The pH levels remain stable","The pH levels become more acidic","The pH levels become more alkaline","The pH levels fluctuate wildly","The pH levels become more acidic","If the kidneys are not functioning properly, the body's pH levels can become more acidic, leading to a range of health problems.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-system-helps-to-aeac3804","Science G9: Understanding the importance of integration of - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-3 Understanding the importance of integration of human body systems and how the structure of different organ relate to one another to maintain homeostasis and body health.","1. Identifying the components of the humanâ€™s body systems (circulatory, immune, digestive, respiratory, excretory, muscular, skeletal, nervous, hormonal, and reproductive) and their specific functions that support the functioning of the body.","Which system helps to regulate body temperature through sweating and shivering in response to changes in the environment?","Nervous system","Circulatory system","Respiratory system","Integumentary system","Integumentary system","The integumentary system, which includes the skin, helps to regulate body temperature through sweating and shivering in response to changes in the environment.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-system-helps-to-bc276f35","Science G9: Understanding the importance of integration of - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-3 Understanding the importance of integration of human body systems and how the structure of different organ relate to one another to maintain homeostasis and body health.","1. Identifying the components of the humanâ€™s body systems (circulatory, immune, digestive, respiratory, excretory, muscular, skeletal, nervous, hormonal, and reproductive) and their specific functions that support the functioning of the body.","Which system helps to regulate body temperature through sweating and shivering?","Nervous system","Circulatory system","Respiratory system","Integumentary system","Integumentary system","The integumentary system, which includes the skin, helps to regulate body temperature through sweating and shivering.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-system-helps-to-7986b2a4","Science G9: Understanding the importance of integration of - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-3 Understanding the importance of integration of human body systems and how the structure of different organ relate to one another to maintain homeostasis and body health.","1. Identifying the components of the humanâ€™s body systems (circulatory, immune, digestive, respiratory, excretory, muscular, skeletal, nervous, hormonal, and reproductive) and their specific functions that support the functioning of the body.","Which system helps to remove waste products from the body?","Urinary system","Digestive system","Respiratory system","Integumentary system","Urinary system","The urinary system, also known as the excretory system, helps to remove waste products from the body through the process of urination.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-happens-to-body-5d6010b2","Science G9: Understanding the importance of integration of - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-3 Understanding the importance of integration of human body systems and how the structure of different organ relate to one another to maintain homeostasis and body health.","2. Explaining how the systems interact and integrate in maintaining homeostasis health and safety of the body's balance.","What happens to the body's blood pressure if the kidneys are not functioning properly?","The blood pressure remains stable","The blood pressure becomes lower","The blood pressure becomes higher","The blood pressure fluctuates wildly","The blood pressure becomes higher","If the kidneys are not functioning properly, the body's blood pressure can become higher, leading to a range of health problems.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-role-of-ac2c2fb4","Science G9: Understanding the importance of integration of - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-3 Understanding the importance of integration of human body systems and how the structure of different organ relate to one another to maintain homeostasis and body health.","2. Explaining how the systems interact and integrate in maintaining homeostasis health and safety of the body's balance.","What is the role of the liver in maintaining homeostasis?","To filter waste and excess fluids from the blood","To regulate blood sugar levels by releasing hormones","To store glycogen and release glucose as needed","To detoxify the body by breaking down toxins","To detoxify the body by breaking down toxins","The liver plays a crucial role in maintaining homeostasis by detoxifying the body through the breakdown of toxins.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-system-helps-to-2e88bd06","Science G9: Understanding the importance of integration of - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-1-1-3 Understanding the importance of integration of human body systems and how the structure of different organ relate to one another to maintain homeostasis and body health.","1. Identifying the components of the humanâ€™s body systems (circulatory, immune, digestive, respiratory, excretory, muscular, skeletal, nervous, hormonal, and reproductive) and their specific functions that support the functioning of the body.","Which system helps to regulate blood sugar levels by releasing hormones?","Nervous system","Circulatory system","Respiratory system","Endocrine system","Endocrine system","The endocrine system, which includes glands such as the pancreas, helps to regulate blood sugar levels by releasing hormones.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-of-is-a-05735f39","Science G9: Understanding the importance of the carbon cycle - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-2 Understanding the importance of the carbon cycle, its geological utility and describing the phenomena associated","2. Describing the phenomena associated with the process of carbon transport through the earth's layers and various materials.","Which of the following is a major mechanism by which carbon is transported through the Earth's biosphere?","Photosynthesis and respiration","Atmospheric circulation and ocean currents","Weathering and erosion","Volcanic activity and earthquakes","Photosynthesis and respiration","Photosynthesis and respiration are major mechanisms by which carbon is transported through the Earth's biosphere, as they drive the exchange of carbon between the atmosphere and the biosphere.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-of-is-a-7f1da97f","Science G9: Understanding the importance of the carbon cycle - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-2 Understanding the importance of the carbon cycle, its geological utility and describing the phenomena associated","2. Describing the phenomena associated with the process of carbon transport through the earth's layers and various materials.","Which of the following is a major pathway for carbon to move from the atmosphere to the Earth's interior?","Weathering of rocks and soil","Volcanic eruptions and earthquakes","Photosynthesis and plant growth","Atmospheric circulation and ocean currents","Volcanic eruptions and earthquakes","Volcanic eruptions and earthquakes are major pathways for carbon to move from the atmosphere to the Earth's interior, as they release carbon-rich magma and gases into the atmosphere.","/image-source/sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-of-is-a-7f1da97f.png","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-process-by-3fadef93","Science G9: Understanding the importance of the carbon cycle - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-2 Understanding the importance of the carbon cycle, its geological utility and describing the phenomena associated","2. Describing the phenomena associated with the process of carbon transport through the earth's layers and various materials.","What is the process by which carbon is released from the Earth's interior to the atmosphere?","Weathering and erosion","Volcanic activity and earthquakes","Photosynthesis and respiration","Atmospheric circulation and ocean currents","Volcanic activity and earthquakes","Volcanic activity and earthquakes release carbon from the Earth's interior to the atmosphere, often in the form of carbon dioxide and other gases.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-of-is-a-74b5e3a1","Science G9: Understanding the importance of the carbon cycle - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-2 Understanding the importance of the carbon cycle, its geological utility and describing the phenomena associated","2. Describing the phenomena associated with the process of carbon transport through the earth's layers and various materials.","Which of the following is a major factor in the transport of carbon through the Earth's mantle?","Plate tectonics and convection currents","Weathering and erosion","Photosynthesis and respiration","Atmospheric circulation and ocean currents","Plate tectonics and convection currents","Plate tectonics and convection currents are major factors in the transport of carbon through the Earth's mantle, as they drive the movement of tectonic plates and the circulation of hot, carbon-rich fluids.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-role-of-4cab0021","Science G9: Understanding the importance of the carbon cycle - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-2 Understanding the importance of the carbon cycle, its geological utility and describing the phenomena associated","2. Describing the phenomena associated with the process of carbon transport through the earth's layers and various materials.","What is the role of groundwater in the transport of carbon through the Earth's crust?","It acts as a major pathway for carbon to move from the atmosphere to the Earth's interior","It plays a minor role in the transport of carbon","It is not involved in the transport of carbon","It is a major source of carbon dioxide","It acts as a major pathway for carbon to move from the atmosphere to the Earth's interior","Groundwater acts as a major pathway for carbon to move from the atmosphere to the Earth's interior, as it can dissolve and transport carbon dioxide and other carbon-rich compounds.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-primary-mechanism-9f8ff06a","Science G9: Understanding the importance of the carbon cycle - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-2 Understanding the importance of the carbon cycle, its geological utility and describing the phenomena associated","2. Describing the phenomena associated with the process of carbon transport through the earth's layers and various materials.","What is the primary mechanism by which carbon is transported through the Earth's crust?","Plate tectonics and volcanic activity","Weathering and erosion","Atmospheric circulation and ocean currents","Photosynthesis and respiration","Plate tectonics and volcanic activity","Carbon is transported through the Earth's crust primarily through plate tectonics and volcanic activity, which involve the movement of the Earth's lithosphere and the release of carbon-rich magma.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-of-is-a-6d15e5fe","Science G9: Understanding the importance of the carbon cycle - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-2 Understanding the importance of the carbon cycle, its geological utility and describing the phenomena associated","2. Describing the phenomena associated with the process of carbon transport through the earth's layers and various materials.","Which of the following is a major mechanism by which carbon is transported through the Earth's oceans?","Photosynthesis and respiration","Atmospheric circulation and ocean currents","Weathering and erosion","Volcanic activity and earthquakes","Atmospheric circulation and ocean currents","Atmospheric circulation and ocean currents are major mechanisms by which carbon is transported through the Earth's oceans, as they drive the movement of carbon-rich waters and the exchange of carbon between the atmosphere and the ocean.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-role-of-37e70d76","Science G9: Understanding the importance of the carbon cycle - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-2 Understanding the importance of the carbon cycle, its geological utility and describing the phenomena associated","2. Describing the phenomena associated with the process of carbon transport through the earth's layers and various materials.","What is the role of plants in the transport of carbon through the Earth's crust?","They act as a major source of carbon dioxide","They play a minor role in the transport of carbon","They are not involved in the transport of carbon","They act as a major pathway for carbon to move from the atmosphere to the Earth's interior","They act as a major pathway for carbon to move from the atmosphere to the Earth's interior","Plants act as a major pathway for carbon to move from the atmosphere to the Earth's interior, as they absorb carbon dioxide through photosynthesis and store it in their biomass and soils.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-of-is-a-8abdc455","Science G9: Understanding the importance of the carbon cycle - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-2 Understanding the importance of the carbon cycle, its geological utility and describing the phenomena associated","2. Describing the phenomena associated with the process of carbon transport through the earth's layers and various materials.","Which of the following is a major factor in the transport of carbon through the Earth's atmosphere?","Photosynthesis and respiration","Atmospheric circulation and ocean currents","Weathering and erosion","Volcanic activity and earthquakes","Photosynthesis and respiration","Photosynthesis and respiration are major factors in the transport of carbon through the Earth's atmosphere, as they drive the exchange of carbon between the atmosphere and the biosphere.","/image-source/sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-of-is-a-8abdc455.png","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-role-of-2275ef48","Science G9: Understanding the importance of the carbon cycle - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-2 Understanding the importance of the carbon cycle, its geological utility and describing the phenomena associated","2. Describing the phenomena associated with the process of carbon transport through the earth's layers and various materials.","What is the role of the atmosphere in the transport of carbon through the Earth's crust?","It acts as a major pathway for carbon to move from the Earth's interior to the atmosphere","It plays a minor role in the transport of carbon","It is not involved in the transport of carbon","It is a major source of carbon dioxide","It acts as a major pathway for carbon to move from the Earth's interior to the atmosphere","The atmosphere acts as a major pathway for carbon to move from the Earth's interior to the atmosphere, as it can dissolve and transport carbon dioxide and other carbon-rich compounds.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-role-of-b36c0179","Science G9: Understanding the importance of the carbon - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-2 Understanding the importance of the carbon cycle, its geological utility and describing the phenomena associated","2. Describing the phenomena associated with the process of carbon transport through the earth's layers and various materials.","What is the role of the atmosphere in the carbon cycle?","To absorb and store carbon dioxide from the Earth's crust","To release carbon dioxide into the Earth's crust","To transport carbon through the Earth's layers","To have no impact on the carbon cycle","To absorb and store carbon dioxide from the Earth's crust","The atmosphere plays a crucial role in the carbon cycle by absorbing and storing carbon dioxide from the Earth's crust, which helps to regulate the Earth's climate.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-primary-method-531d50f1","Science G9: Understanding the importance of the carbon - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-2 Understanding the importance of the carbon cycle, its geological utility and describing the phenomena associated","2. Describing the phenomena associated with the process of carbon transport through the earth's layers and various materials.","What is the primary method by which carbon is transported from the atmosphere to the Earth's crust?","Photosynthesis and respiration","Weathering and erosion","Plate tectonics and volcanic activity","Human activities and industrial processes","Photosynthesis and respiration","Photosynthesis and respiration are the primary methods by which carbon is transported from the atmosphere to the Earth's crust, as plants absorb carbon dioxide through photosynthesis and release it through respiration.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-of-is-an-60b461f2","Science G9: Understanding the importance of the carbon - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-2 Understanding the importance of the carbon cycle, its geological utility and describing the phenomena associated","2. Describing the phenomena associated with the process of carbon transport through the earth's layers and various materials.","Which of the following is an example of a geological process that transports carbon through the Earth's layers?","Weathering of rocks and minerals","Formation of sedimentary rocks","Plate tectonics and mountain building","Volcanic eruptions and lava flows","Weathering of rocks and minerals","Weathering of rocks and minerals is an example of a geological process that transports carbon through the Earth's layers, as it breaks down rocks and releases carbon dioxide into the atmosphere.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-role-of-ab820798","Science G9: Understanding the importance of the carbon - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-2 Understanding the importance of the carbon cycle, its geological utility and describing the phenomena associated","2. Describing the phenomena associated with the process of carbon transport through the earth's layers and various materials.","What is the role of the ocean in the carbon cycle?","To absorb and store carbon dioxide from the atmosphere","To release carbon dioxide into the atmosphere","To transport carbon through the Earth's layers","To have no impact on the carbon cycle","To absorb and store carbon dioxide from the atmosphere","The ocean plays a crucial role in the carbon cycle by absorbing and storing carbon dioxide from the atmosphere, which helps to regulate the Earth's climate.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-of-is-an-4bf3499a","Science G9: Understanding the importance of the carbon - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-2 Understanding the importance of the carbon cycle, its geological utility and describing the phenomena associated","2. Describing the phenomena associated with the process of carbon transport through the earth's layers and various materials.","Which of the following is an example of a human activity that affects the carbon cycle?","Deforestation and land use changes","Industrial processes and fossil fuel burning","Agricultural practices and soil management","All of the above","All of the above","All of the above human activities affect the carbon cycle, as deforestation and land use changes release carbon stored in trees, industrial processes and fossil fuel burning release stored carbon into the atmosphere, and agricultural practices and soil management can affect the amount of carbon stored in soils.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-process-by-7fe7c341","Science G9: Understanding the importance of the carbon - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-2 Understanding the importance of the carbon cycle, its geological utility and describing the phenomena associated","2. Describing the phenomena associated with the process of carbon transport through the earth's layers and various materials.","What is the process by which carbon is transported through the Earth's crust and released into the atmosphere?","Weathering and erosion","Plate tectonics and volcanic activity","Human activities and industrial processes","Biological processes and decomposition","Weathering and erosion","Weathering and erosion are the processes by which carbon is transported through the Earth's crust and released into the atmosphere, as rocks and minerals are broken down and release carbon dioxide into the atmosphere.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-of-is-an-56cf99a6","Science G9: Understanding the importance of the carbon - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-2 Understanding the importance of the carbon cycle, its geological utility and describing the phenomena associated","2. Describing the phenomena associated with the process of carbon transport through the earth's layers and various materials.","Which of the following is an example of a natural process that transports carbon through the Earth's layers?","Weathering of rocks and minerals","Formation of sedimentary rocks","Plate tectonics and mountain building","Volcanic eruptions and lava flows","Weathering of rocks and minerals","Weathering of rocks and minerals is an example of a natural process that transports carbon through the Earth's layers, as it breaks down rocks and releases carbon dioxide into the atmosphere.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-of-is-an-12ff7d5d","Science G9: Understanding the importance of the carbon - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-2 Understanding the importance of the carbon cycle, its geological utility and describing the phenomena associated","2. Describing the phenomena associated with the process of carbon transport through the earth's layers and various materials.","Which of the following is an example of a geological process that affects the carbon cycle?","Weathering of rocks and minerals","Formation of sedimentary rocks","Plate tectonics and mountain building","Volcanic eruptions and lava flows","Weathering of rocks and minerals","Weathering of rocks and minerals is an example of a geological process that affects the carbon cycle, as it breaks down rocks and releases carbon dioxide into the atmosphere.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-process-by-ff25973f","Science G9: Understanding the importance of the carbon - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-2 Understanding the importance of the carbon cycle, its geological utility and describing the phenomena associated","2. Describing the phenomena associated with the process of carbon transport through the earth's layers and various materials.","What is the process by which carbon is transported from the Earth's crust to the atmosphere?","Weathering and erosion","Plate tectonics and volcanic activity","Human activities and industrial processes","Biological processes and decomposition","Weathering and erosion","Weathering and erosion are the processes by which carbon is transported from the Earth's crust to the atmosphere, as rocks and minerals are broken down and release carbon dioxide into the atmosphere.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-of-is-an-ac89da97","Science G9: Understanding the importance of the carbon - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-3-2-2 Understanding the importance of the carbon cycle, its geological utility and describing the phenomena associated","2. Describing the phenomena associated with the process of carbon transport through the earth's layers and various materials.","Which of the following is an example of a human activity that affects the carbon cycle?","Deforestation and land use changes","Industrial processes and fossil fuel burning","Agricultural practices and soil management","All of the above","All of the above","All of the above human activities affect the carbon cycle, as deforestation and land use changes release carbon stored in trees, industrial processes and fossil fuel burning release stored carbon into the atmosphere, and agricultural practices and soil management can affect the amount of carbon stored in soils.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-purpose-of-0cbf9bce","Science G9: Understanding the law of energy conservation during its - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-4-5 Understanding the law of energy conservation during its transformations and suggesting ways to generate energy.","2. Tracking a series of applications of technical or natural energy transformations and applying the law of conservation of energy in it.","What is the purpose of a wind turbine?","To generate electricity from fossil fuels","To harness the energy of the sun","To convert kinetic energy into electrical energy","To reduce the amount of energy used","To convert kinetic energy into electrical energy","The purpose of a wind turbine is to convert kinetic energy from the wind into electrical energy, which can be used to power homes and businesses.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-an-example-b4a1b7b2","Science G9: Understanding the law of energy conservation during its - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-4-5 Understanding the law of energy conservation during its transformations and suggesting ways to generate energy.","2. Tracking a series of applications of technical or natural energy transformations and applying the law of conservation of energy in it.","What is an example of a closed system?","A car engine","A solar panel","A bicycle wheel","A sealed container of gas","A sealed container of gas","A sealed container of gas is an example of a closed system, where energy cannot enter or leave the system.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-happens-to-energy-e11b9e53","Science G9: Understanding the law of energy conservation during its - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-4-5 Understanding the law of energy conservation during its transformations and suggesting ways to generate energy.","2. Tracking a series of applications of technical or natural energy transformations and applying the law of conservation of energy in it.","What happens to the energy in a closed system?","It increases over time.","It decreases over time.","It remains constant.","It disappears completely.","It remains constant.","In a closed system, the energy remains constant, meaning it does not increase or decrease over time.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-law-of-9f0c288a","Science G9: Understanding the law of energy conservation during its - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-4-5 Understanding the law of energy conservation during its transformations and suggesting ways to generate energy.","2. Tracking a series of applications of technical or natural energy transformations and applying the law of conservation of energy in it.","What is the law of energy conservation?","Energy can be created or destroyed.","Energy can be transferred from one form to another.","Energy is always lost in a transformation.","Energy remains constant in a closed system.","Energy remains constant in a closed system.","The law of energy conservation states that energy remains constant in a closed system, meaning it cannot be created or destroyed, only transformed from one form to another.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-primary-source-cfb9052f","Science G9: Understanding the law of energy conservation during its - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-4-5 Understanding the law of energy conservation during its transformations and suggesting ways to generate energy.","2. Tracking a series of applications of technical or natural energy transformations and applying the law of conservation of energy in it.","What is the primary source of energy for a hydroelectric power plant?","Fossil fuels","Nuclear reactions","Solar radiation","Gravitational potential energy","Gravitational potential energy","The primary source of energy for a hydroelectric power plant is the gravitational potential energy of water stored behind a dam.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-law-of-08aae6cb","Science G9: Understanding the law of energy conservation during its - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-4-5 Understanding the law of energy conservation during its transformations and suggesting ways to generate energy.","2. Tracking a series of applications of technical or natural energy transformations and applying the law of conservation of energy in it.","What is the law of conservation of energy used for?","To calculate the energy of a system","To predict the future of the universe","To understand the transformations of energy","To create new forms of energy","To understand the transformations of energy","The law of conservation of energy is used to understand the transformations of energy, helping us to predict how energy will change from one form to another.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-an-example-e3963cf1","Science G9: Understanding the law of energy conservation during its - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-4-5 Understanding the law of energy conservation during its transformations and suggesting ways to generate energy.","2. Tracking a series of applications of technical or natural energy transformations and applying the law of conservation of energy in it.","What is an example of energy transformation?","A car moving from 0 to 60 km/h","A light bulb turning on","A solar panel generating electricity","A person walking upstairs","A person walking upstairs","A person walking upstairs is an example of energy transformation, where chemical energy from food is converted into kinetic energy and potential energy.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-law-of-d500311d","Science G9: Understanding the law of energy conservation during its - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-2-2 Understanding the concept of momentum and the law of momentum conservation.","1. Stating Newton's first law of motion and providing examples thereof.","What is the law of conservation of energy related to?","The second law of thermodynamics","The first law of thermodynamics","The law of gravity","The law of motion","The first law of thermodynamics","The law of conservation of energy is related to the first law of thermodynamics, which states that energy cannot be created or destroyed, only transformed.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-an-example-dfb6bd3a","Science G9: Understanding the law of energy conservation during its - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-4-5 Understanding the law of energy conservation during its transformations and suggesting ways to generate energy.","2. Tracking a series of applications of technical or natural energy transformations and applying the law of conservation of energy in it.","What is an example of a natural energy transformation?","A car engine burning gasoline","A solar panel generating electricity","A waterfall flowing over rocks","A person walking upstairs","A waterfall flowing over rocks","A waterfall flowing over rocks is an example of a natural energy transformation, where gravitational potential energy is converted into kinetic energy and sound energy.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-law-of-2434d972","Science G9: Understanding the law of energy conservation during its - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-4-5 Understanding the law of energy conservation during its transformations and suggesting ways to generate energy.","1. Explaining the concept of sound wave theoretically and by drawing, Determining the type of sound wave (longitudinal or transversem, Describing the properties of sound waves (wavelength, frequency, amplitude) mathematically and graphically.","What is the law of conservation of energy used in?","Only mechanical systems","Only electrical systems","All types of systems","Only thermal systems","All types of systems","The law of conservation of energy is used in all types of systems, including mechanical, electrical, thermal, and more.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-material-would-be-e8cba15d","Science G9: Understanding the mechanism of heat transmission - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-4-2 Understanding the mechanism of heat transmission and conduction between objects, and measuring temperature.","2. Comparing the methods of transmission and conduction of thermal energy between objects.","Which material would be the best choice for the handle of a cooking pot?","Copper","Aluminum","Steel","Plastic","Plastic","Plastic is a poor conductor of heat, making it a good choice for the handle of a cooking pot as it will not transfer heat from the pot to your hand.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-primary-mechanism-6dccaddd","Science G9: Understanding the mechanism of heat transmission - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-4-2 Understanding the mechanism of heat transmission and conduction between objects, and measuring temperature.","2. Comparing the methods of transmission and conduction of thermal energy between objects.","What is the primary mechanism by which heat is transferred between objects in contact with each other?","Convection","Radiation","Conduction","Evaporation","Conduction","Conduction is the transfer of heat between objects in direct contact with each other.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-of-is-an-1951a69d","Science G9: Understanding the mechanism of heat transmission - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-4-2 Understanding the mechanism of heat transmission and conduction between objects, and measuring temperature.","2. Comparing the methods of transmission and conduction of thermal energy between objects.","Which of the following is an example of conduction?","A hot cup of coffee warming a nearby spoon","A fire heating a room through radiation","A pot of boiling water heating the air around it through convection","A person feeling the heat from a fire on their skin","A hot cup of coffee warming a nearby spoon","The hot coffee warms the spoon through direct contact, which is an example of conduction.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-term-transfer-add1c736","Science G9: Understanding the mechanism of heat transmission - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-4-2 Understanding the mechanism of heat transmission and conduction between objects, and measuring temperature.","2. Comparing the methods of transmission and conduction of thermal energy between objects.","What is the term for the transfer of heat between objects that are not in direct contact with each other?","Conduction","Convection","Radiation","Evaporation","Radiation","Radiation is the transfer of heat between objects through electromagnetic waves, and does not require direct contact between the objects.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-why-do-some-a4fcdc39","Science G9: Understanding the mechanism of heat transmission - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-4-2 Understanding the mechanism of heat transmission and conduction between objects, and measuring temperature.","2. Comparing the methods of transmission and conduction of thermal energy between objects.","Why do some cooking pots have handles made of materials with low thermal conductivity?","To make the pot more durable","To make the pot more conductive","To reduce the risk of burns when handling the pot","To make the pot more expensive","To reduce the risk of burns when handling the pot","Handles made of materials with low thermal conductivity, such as plastic or wood, reduce the risk of burns when handling a hot pot.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-how-thermal-conductivity-c23df48c","Science G9: Understanding the mechanism of heat transmission - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-4-3Understanding specific heat and the factors affecting it.","2. Determining the factors affecting the body's absorption or loss of thermal energy.","How does the thermal conductivity of a material affect its ability to conduct heat?","A high thermal conductivity means a material is a poor conductor of heat","A low thermal conductivity means a material is a good conductor of heat","A high thermal conductivity means a material is a good conductor of heat","A low thermal conductivity means a material is a poor conductor of heat","A high thermal conductivity means a material is a good conductor of heat","Materials with high thermal conductivity are good conductors of heat, while those with low thermal conductivity are poor conductors.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-happens-to-temperature-20b8bf62","Science G9: Understanding the mechanism of heat transmission - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-4-2 Understanding the mechanism of heat transmission and conduction between objects, and measuring temperature.","2. Comparing the methods of transmission and conduction of thermal energy between objects.","What happens to the temperature of a metal spoon when it is placed in a hot cup of coffee?","The temperature of the spoon decreases","The temperature of the spoon remains the same","The temperature of the spoon increases","The temperature of the spoon becomes absolute zero","The temperature of the spoon increases","The metal spoon conducts heat from the hot coffee, causing its temperature to rise.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-material-is-best-30d45e84","Science G9: Understanding the mechanism of heat transmission - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-4-2 Understanding the mechanism of heat transmission and conduction between objects, and measuring temperature.","2. Comparing the methods of transmission and conduction of thermal energy between objects.","Which material is the best conductor of heat?","Copper","Wood","Plastic","Glass","Copper","Copper is a metal with high thermal conductivity, making it an excellent conductor of heat.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-of-materials-is-6b6c543f","Science G9: Understanding the mechanism of heat transmission - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-4-2 Understanding the mechanism of heat transmission and conduction between objects, and measuring temperature.","2. Comparing the methods of transmission and conduction of thermal energy between objects.","Which of the following materials is a poor conductor of heat?","Aluminum","Steel","Wood","Copper","Wood","Wood is a poor conductor of heat due to its low thermal conductivity.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-why-do-people-08b76dd8","Science G9: Understanding the mechanism of heat transmission - Set 1","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-4-2 Understanding the mechanism of heat transmission and conduction between objects, and measuring temperature.","2. Comparing the methods of transmission and conduction of thermal energy between objects.","Why do people often use wooden spoons when cooking with hot liquids?","Wooden spoons are cheaper than metal spoons","Wooden spoons are better conductors of heat than metal spoons","Wooden spoons are poor conductors of heat and will not burn your hand","Wooden spoons are more durable than metal spoons","Wooden spoons are poor conductors of heat and will not burn your hand","Wooden spoons are poor conductors of heat, which means they will not transfer heat from the hot liquid to your hand, reducing the risk of burns.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-direction-of-e32f081d","Science G9: Understanding the relationship between the electric field - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-3-2 Understanding the relationship between the electric field and the electric and compares electric and magnetic fields using theoretical explanations and","4. Comparing between series and parallel connection in electrical circuits, theoretically and by drawing.","What is the direction of the magnetic field around a current-carrying wire?","The magnetic field lines radiate outward from the wire","The magnetic field lines radiate inward toward the wire","The magnetic field lines are parallel to the wire","The magnetic field lines form a circular pattern around the wire","The magnetic field lines form a circular pattern around the wire","The magnetic field lines form a circular pattern around a current-carrying wire, which is a key concept in understanding magnetic fields.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-direction-of-5b17c803","Science G9: Understanding the relationship between the electric field - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-3-2 Understanding the relationship between the electric field and the electric and compares electric and magnetic fields using theoretical explanations and","4. Comparing between series and parallel connection in electrical circuits, theoretically and by drawing.","What is the direction of the electric field around a positive charge?","The electric field lines radiate inward toward the charge","The electric field lines radiate outward from the charge","The electric field lines are parallel to the charge","The electric field lines are perpendicular to the charge","The electric field lines radiate outward from the charge","The electric field lines radiate outward from a positive charge, which is a fundamental concept in understanding electric fields.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-how-do-strength-2fd1e5be","Science G9: Understanding the relationship between the electric field - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-3-2 Understanding the relationship between the electric field and the electric and compares electric and magnetic fields using theoretical explanations and","2. Comparing the magnetic field and the electric field theoretically and by drawing.","How do the strength and direction of electric and magnetic fields change with distance?","The strength of both fields increases with distance","The strength of both fields decreases with distance","The strength of electric fields decreases with distance, while the strength of magnetic fields increases with distance","The strength of electric fields decreases with distance, while the strength of magnetic fields also decreases with distance","The strength of electric fields decreases with distance, while the strength of magnetic fields also decreases with distance","The strength of both electric and magnetic fields decreases with distance, which is an important concept in understanding how these fields interact with their surroundings.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-difference-electric-1b098ec9","Science G9: Understanding the relationship between the electric field - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-3-3Comparing between different types of materials in terms of their electrical conductivity.","1. Explaining the relationship between the electric current and the magnetic field, and deducing the factors that control it.","What is the difference between the electric field and the magnetic field in terms of their interaction with matter?","Electric fields interact with charged particles, while magnetic fields interact with neutral particles","Magnetic fields interact with charged particles, while electric fields interact with neutral particles","Electric fields interact with charged particles, while magnetic fields interact with moving charged particles","Magnetic fields interact with charged particles, while electric fields interact with stationary charged particles","Electric fields interact with charged particles, while magnetic fields interact with moving charged particles","Electric fields interact with charged particles, while magnetic fields interact with moving charged particles, which is a fundamental difference between the two types of fields.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-relationship-electric-b19993b8","Science G9: Understanding the relationship between the electric field - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-3-2 Understanding the relationship between the electric field and the electric and compares electric and magnetic fields using theoretical explanations and","2. Comparing the magnetic field and the electric field theoretically and by drawing.","What is the relationship between electric field strength and the distance from a charge?","The electric field strength increases with the square of the distance from the charge","The electric field strength decreases with the square of the distance from the charge","The electric field strength increases with the distance from the charge","The electric field strength decreases with the distance from the charge","The electric field strength decreases with the distance from the charge","The electric field strength decreases with the distance from the charge, which is a fundamental concept in understanding electric fields.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-main-difference-3c82de12","Science G9: Understanding the relationship between the electric field - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-3-2 Understanding the relationship between the electric field and the electric and compares electric and magnetic fields using theoretical explanations and","2. Comparing the magnetic field and the electric field theoretically and by drawing.","What is the main difference between electric and magnetic fields?","Electric fields are created by stationary charges, while magnetic fields are created by moving charges","Electric fields are created by moving charges, while magnetic fields are created by stationary charges","Electric fields are created by positive charges, while magnetic fields are created by negative charges","Electric fields are created by negative charges, while magnetic fields are created by positive charges","Electric fields are created by stationary charges, while magnetic fields are created by moving charges","Electric fields are created by stationary charges, while magnetic fields are created by moving charges, which is a fundamental difference between the two types of fields.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-relationship-electric-33e32085","Science G9: Understanding the relationship between the electric field - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-3-2 Understanding the relationship between the electric field and the electric and compares electric and magnetic fields using theoretical explanations and","2. Comparing the magnetic field and the electric field theoretically and by drawing.","What is the relationship between the electric field, magnetic field, and the speed of light?","The electric field and magnetic field are unrelated to the speed of light","The electric field is related to the speed of light, while the magnetic field is not","The magnetic field is related to the speed of light, while the electric field is not","The electric field and magnetic field are both related to the speed of light, as they form electromagnetic waves that travel at this speed","The electric field and magnetic field are both related to the speed of light, as they form electromagnetic waves that travel at this speed","The electric field and magnetic field are both related to the speed of light, as they form electromagnetic waves that travel at this speed, which is a fundamental concept in understanding the relationship between these fields and the behavior of light.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-how-do-electric-bc481d02","Science G9: Understanding the relationship between the electric field - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-3-2 Understanding the relationship between the electric field and the electric and compares electric and magnetic fields using theoretical explanations and","2. Comparing the magnetic field and the electric field theoretically and by drawing.","How do the electric and magnetic fields of a charged particle change when it is in motion?","The electric field increases, while the magnetic field decreases","The electric field decreases, while the magnetic field increases","The electric field remains the same, while the magnetic field increases","The electric field remains the same, while the magnetic field decreases","The electric field remains the same, while the magnetic field increases","The electric field remains the same, while the magnetic field increases when a charged particle is in motion, which is a key concept in understanding the relationship between electric and magnetic fields.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-how-do-electric-bf076728","Science G9: Understanding the relationship between the electric field - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-3-2 Understanding the relationship between the electric field and the electric and compares electric and magnetic fields using theoretical explanations and","2. Comparing the magnetic field and the electric field theoretically and by drawing.","How do electric and magnetic fields interact with each other?","Electric fields cancel out magnetic fields","Magnetic fields cancel out electric fields","Electric and magnetic fields are perpendicular to each other and can interact to form electromagnetic waves","Electric and magnetic fields are parallel to each other and do not interact","Electric and magnetic fields are perpendicular to each other and can interact to form electromagnetic waves","Electric and magnetic fields are perpendicular to each other and can interact to form electromagnetic waves, which is a key concept in understanding the relationship between the two fields.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-how-do-electric-a546d46a","Science G9: Understanding the relationship between the electric field - Set 2","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-3-2 Understanding the relationship between the electric field and the electric and compares electric and magnetic fields using theoretical explanations and","2. Comparing the magnetic field and the electric field theoretically and by drawing.","How do electric and magnetic fields compare in terms of their ability to exert forces on charged particles?","Electric fields exert forces on charged particles, while magnetic fields do not","Magnetic fields exert forces on charged particles, while electric fields do not","Both electric and magnetic fields exert forces on charged particles","Neither electric nor magnetic fields exert forces on charged particles","Both electric and magnetic fields exert forces on charged particles","Both electric and magnetic fields exert forces on charged particles, which is a key concept in understanding the relationship between these fields and charged particles.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-role-of-e222d962","Science G9: Understanding the relationship between the electric field - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-3-2 Understanding the relationship between the electric field and the electric and compares electric and magnetic fields using theoretical explanations and","3. Explaining the components and role of electrical circuits in energy transmission.","What is the role of a wire in an electrical circuit?","To insulate the circuit from the surroundings","To increase the resistance of the circuit","To allow the flow of electric current","To connect components of the circuit","To allow the flow of electric current","A wire is a conductor that allows electric current to flow through it, enabling the circuit to function properly.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-purpose-of-3e887935","Science G9: Understanding the relationship between the electric field - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-3-1 Explaining the concept of electric current and methods of generating it in electrical circuits and its relationship to voltage and electrical resistance, and distinguishing between direct and alternating currents.","2. Describing the methods of generating electric current in electrical circuits","What is the purpose of a transformer in an electrical circuit?","To increase the voltage of the circuit","To reduce the resistance of the circuit","To control the flow of electric current","To change the voltage of the circuit","To change the voltage of the circuit","A transformer is used to change the voltage of the circuit, either increasing or decreasing it, depending on the design of the transformer.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-purpose-of-43755fc7","Science G9: Understanding the relationship between the electric field - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-3-1 Explaining the concept of electric current and methods of generating it in electrical circuits and its relationship to voltage and electrical resistance, and distinguishing between direct and alternating currents.","2. Describing the methods of generating electric current in electrical circuits","What is the purpose of a circuit breaker in an electrical circuit?","To increase the voltage of the circuit","To reduce the resistance of the circuit","To control the flow of electric current","To protect the circuit from excessive current","To protect the circuit from excessive current","A circuit breaker is designed to automatically turn off the circuit when the current exceeds a certain limit, preventing damage to the circuit and its components.","","",""
"sci-g9-9-5-3-1-1-is-purpose-of-c528ccd2","Science G9: Understanding the relationship between the electric field - Set 3","Science","9","Assessment for Grade 9 Science","false","2027-01-05T21:00:00.000Z","9-5-2-3-1 Explaining the concept of electric current and methods of generating it in electrical circuits and its relationship to voltage and electrical resistance, and distinguishing between direct and alternating currents.","2. Describing the methods of generating electric current in electrical circuits","What is the purpose of a ground wire in an electrical circuit?","To increase the voltage of the circuit","To reduce the resistance of the circuit","To connect components of the circuit to the ground","To improve the efficiency of the circuit","To connect components of the circuit to the ground","A ground wire provides a safe path for electric current to flow to the ground, preventing shock and ensuring the circuit operates safely.","","",""
